window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"MSK Embryology & Limb Development","questions":[{"id":"sdl1_q01","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher removes the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) from a developing chick limb bud. Which of the following best describes the expected consequence?","choices":{"A":"Digit fusion develops along the hand plate, because apoptosis between the digital rays is suppressed when AER-derived FGF8 signals are absent from the distal limb mesenchyme.","B":"An ectopic limb bud forms on the contralateral side, because FGF10 released from the lateral plate mesoderm is redirected toward the opposite limb field without AER-mediated inhibition.","C":"The limb ceases outgrowth and fails to form structures distal to the level of removal, because the AER secretes FGF8 required for continued mesoderm proliferation in the progress zone.","D":"Mirror-image digit duplication occurs on the anterior limb border, because anterior mesenchyme is exposed to unopposed Sonic Hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity.","E":"The limb develops normally with intact proximal structures but shows shortened metacarpals, because only distal ossification depends on AER-derived FGF8 while proximal endochondral centers are autonomous."},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AER is located at the lateral-most rim of the limb bud and secretes FGF8 (and FGF4), which sustains undifferentiated mesenchymal proliferation in the underlying progress zone. Without the AER, outgrowth stops and only the proximal structures that had already formed are retained. Digit fusion (syndactyly) results from failed apoptosis between digital rays, not AER removal. Ectopic limb formation requires FGF10/FGF8 signaling to initiate a new bud, not a consequence of AER loss. Mirror-image digit duplication results from transplanting an extra ZPA anteriorly, not from AER removal. Isolated metacarpal shortening describes achondroplasia, not AER absence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AER = FGF8 source; if AER removed, limb truncated at that developmental stage — classic experimental manipulation tested in developmental biology. COMLEX: AER removal is a limb defect mechanism; connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (amelia = no AER/no FGF8 expressed).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q02","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) occupies the posterior mesenchyme of the developing limb bud and controls digit identity along the anterior-to-posterior axis. Which signaling molecule mediates this patterning function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8), which is secreted from the AER at the distal rim of the limb bud and maintains undifferentiated mesenchymal cells in the progress zone for continued outgrowth.","B":"Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), which is most highly concentrated near the posterior mesenchyme where the fifth digit forms and least concentrated anteriorly near the first digit.","C":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 (FGF10), which is released by lateral plate mesoderm to induce the overlying ectoderm to form the AER and initiate the initial phases of limb bud outgrowth.","D":"Wnt signaling protein, which is released by the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm to induce dermomyotome formation from the paraxial mesoderm somites bilaterally.","E":"Indian Hedgehog (IHH), which is secreted by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the epiphyseal growth plate to coordinate the rate of chondrocyte proliferation and longitudinal bone elongation."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), creating a posterior-to-anterior concentration gradient that assigns digit identity: highest Shh = fifth digit, lowest = first digit. FGF8 is the AER signal maintaining proximal-to-distal outgrowth. FGF10 is secreted by lateral plate mesoderm to initiate AER formation and limb bud outgrowth. Wnt induces dermomyotome differentiation from somites. Indian Hedgehog (IHH) is a growth plate regulator, not a limb patterning molecule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA = Shh gradient = digit identity (A-P axis); AER = FGF8 = proximal-to-distal outgrowth. These two structures are the key patterning signals tested. COMLEX: Connect to SDL 1 Obj 4 — transplanting ZPA anteriorly causes mirror-image polydactyly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q03","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During week 4 of embryogenesis, the paraxial mesoderm segments into somites. Sonic Hedgehog released by the notochord and ventral neural tube induces differentiation of the ventral portion of each somite into a specific structure. What does this ventral somite component become?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which migrates to surround the notochord and neural tube and differentiates into the vertebral column, ribs, and other axial skeletal elements.","B":"The dermomyotome, which is induced by Wnt signaling from the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm and gives rise to both the dermis of the skin and the skeletal muscle precursors.","C":"The myotome, which subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial portions under spinal nerve innervation and generates the intrinsic back muscles and the muscles of the trunk and limbs.","D":"The meningotome, which proliferates medial to the sclerotome under notochordal signaling and eventually differentiates into the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which lies peripheral to the paraxial mesoderm and splits into somatic and splanchnic layers to form the pelvic bones, limb bones, and body wall cavities."},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral portion of each somite becomes the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the notochord and ventral neural tube. The sclerotome forms the vertebral bodies, neural arches, ribs, and tendons. The dorsal portion becomes the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling, which further subdivides into the myotome (skeletal muscles) and dermatome (dermis). The meningotome is a subdivision of the sclerotome that forms the meninges, not the ventral somite itself. Lateral plate mesoderm is a separate compartment from paraxial mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paraxial mesoderm → somites → sclerotome (ventral, Shh) + dermomyotome (dorsal, Wnt). Sclerotome → vertebrae and ribs. Dermomyotome → myotome (muscle) and dermatome (dermis). COMLEX: Three MSK sources — paraxial mesoderm, neural crest cells (facial bones), and lateral plate mesoderm (limb/pelvic bones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis"},{"id":"sdl1_q04","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During vertebral column development, each sclerotome somite subdivides into cranial and caudal sub-segments that then undergo re-segmentation. Which of the following correctly describes this process and its anatomical result?","choices":{"A":"The caudal sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form a vertebral body, leaving the notochord remnant between segments to become the nucleus pulposus.","B":"The entire sclerotome ossifies as a single unit per segment, and the notochord degenerates completely without contributing to the intervertebral disc, leaving only loose mesenchyme between vertebrae.","C":"The cranial sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form paired vertebral bodies per spinal level, with sclerotome remnant forming the annulus fibrosus.","D":"The caudal sub-segment fuses with the ipsilateral dermomyotome to form the posterior elements of the vertebra, while the cranial sub-segment becomes the nucleus pulposus under FGF10 signaling.","E":"Adjacent sclerotome somites remain intact and separate without fusion, with the intervertebral disc cavity enlarging spontaneously under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the persisting notochord."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Re-segmentation is critical for vertebral column formation: the caudal portion of one sclerotome somite fuses with the cranial portion of the next inferior somite to build each vertebral body. This creates an offset so that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites. The notochord remnant trapped between the newly formed vertebral bodies persists as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral disc. The annulus fibrosus is derived from the sclerotome, not from the cranial sub-segment directly. Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, through which spinal nerve roots exit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults\"). Annulus fibrosus → from sclerotome. This is a classic embryology board question. COMLEX: Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, allowing spinal nerve exit — relevant to somatic dysfunction and radiculopathy discussions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q05","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the developing embryo, TBX genes play a key role in distinguishing which type of limb a limb bud will produce. Which of the following correctly matches the TBX gene to the limb field it specifies?","choices":{"A":"HOX genes establish whether a limb bud becomes an upper or lower extremity by controlling TBX5 and TBX4 expression at the level of the limb field within the lateral plate mesoderm.","B":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb field of lateral plate mesoderm specifying upper extremity identity, while TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying lower extremity identity.","C":"TBX4 is expressed in the upper limb field specifying shoulder and arm identity, while TBX5 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying hip and leg identity, with FGF10 as the common downstream effector.","D":"TBX genes specify anterior versus posterior limb identity within a single limb bud, working in concert with Sonic Hedgehog from the ZPA to assign digit positions along the hand or foot plate.","E":"Both TBX4 and TBX5 are co-expressed in both limb fields and only distinguish upper from lower limb identity through differential binding to the FGF10 promoter in the lateral plate mesoderm."},"correct":"B","explanation":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb lateral plate mesoderm and specifies upper extremity identity. TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field and specifies lower extremity identity. HOX genes determine position along the craniocaudal axis (where a limb forms), not which type of limb forms. The lecture slides confirm: HOX = \"where along the column,\" TBX = \"upper vs lower,\" with FGF10 as the shared primary growth stimulant. TBX genes do not control A/P digit patterning, which is the ZPA/Shh role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Limb bud gene hierarchy — HOX genes determine craniocaudal position; TBX5/TBX4 specify upper/lower limb identity; FGF10 initiates AER induction; AER (FGF8) drives outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns A/P axis. COMLEX: Upper limb bud forms at C5-T1; lower limb bud at L2-S3 levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q06","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A student is reviewing vertebral ossification for an upcoming exam. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary ossification centers of a typical vertebra and the secondary ossification centers that appear at puberty?","choices":{"A":"Primary centers: one in the centrum (vertebral body) and two in the neural arch (one on each side); secondary centers: tip of spinous process, tips of two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses at the superior and inferior vertebral body rim.","B":"Primary centers: three in the centrum, two in the pedicles, and two in the laminae; secondary centers: one in the spinous process appearing at puberty, completing ossification by age 25.","C":"Primary centers: one in the centrum and one in the neural arch fused midline; secondary centers: one annular epiphysis at the disc surface appearing at age 5 and fusing by early adulthood.","D":"Primary centers: one in the spinous process and two annular epiphyses; secondary centers: one in the centrum and two in the neural arch appearing at puberty after the posterior elements are complete.","E":"Primary centers: two in the centrum (anterior and posterior) and one in the neural arch; secondary centers: two in the transverse processes and one in the tip of the spinous process, all appearing before birth."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each typical vertebra has three primary ossification centers: one in the centrum (future vertebral body) and one on each side of the neural arch (two total). At puberty, five secondary ossification centers appear: one at the tip of the spinous process, one at each tip of the two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses (one superior, one inferior) at the rim of the vertebral body. The secondary centers fuse with the vertebral body and complete ossification during early adulthood. This pattern is important for understanding Scheuermann disease and stress-related vertebral injuries in adolescents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertebral ossification — 3 primary centers (centrum + 2 neural arch). Secondary centers appear at puberty. The primary centers are in the centrum and neural arch (course SDL 1 slides p18–19). COMLEX: Spina bifida occurs when the neural arch centers fail to fuse posteriorly — connects to SDL 1 Obj 4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q07","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The myotome subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial muscle groups during embryogenesis. Which of the following correctly distinguishes epaxial from hypaxial muscles and their innervation?","choices":{"A":"Epaxial muscles form the flexors of the upper and lower limbs and are innervated by anterior divisions of the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses, while hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic back extensors innervated by posterior rami.","B":"Epaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves, while hypaxial muscles form muscles of the anterior neck, trunk, limbs, and perineum and are innervated by anterior rami.","C":"Epaxial muscles derive from the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling and form the intercostal muscles, while hypaxial muscles derive from the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling and form the paraspinal muscles.","D":"Hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami, while epaxial muscles migrate into the limb buds to form flexor and extensor compartments innervated by the plexuses.","E":"Both epaxial and hypaxial muscles derive from the lateral plate mesoderm and are equally innervated by both anterior and posterior rami, depending on their final anatomical position in the body wall."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Epaxial muscles arise from the medial part of the myotome, remain dorsal, and form the intrinsic (erector spinae and deep back) muscles. They are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves. Hypaxial muscles arise from the lateral myotome, migrate ventrally and into limb buds, and form the muscles of the anterior neck, thorax, abdomen, limbs, and perineum. They are innervated by anterior rami, which become the cervical, brachial, intercostal, lumbar, and lumbosacral plexuses. Both groups arise from the myotome (paraxial mesoderm), not lateral plate mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epaxial = intrinsic back muscles, posterior rami; hypaxial = everything else (limbs, trunk, perineum), anterior rami. This explains why the intrinsic back muscles are supplied at every segmental level by posterior rami — each myotome level contributes one epaxial segment. COMLEX: Posterior rami somatic dysfunction affects epaxial muscles and is the basis for paraspinal muscle tender points and restricted spinal motion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q08","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During limb development, hypaxial myotome cells migrate into the limb bud and split into dorsal and ventral muscle masses. Which of the following correctly identifies the muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb and their nerve supply?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors of the upper limb and is innervated by branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus, including the median and ulnar nerves.","B":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the extensor muscles of the upper limb but is innervated by the anterior division of the brachial plexus, specifically the musculocutaneous and median nerve branches.","C":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms extensors innervated by the tibial and obturator nerves, which branch from the lumbosacral plexus to supply the posterior compartments of the lower limb.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass forms both flexor and extensor muscles, with each group innervated by opposite divisions of the brachial plexus based on their final anterior or posterior position in the limb.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms the extensor muscles and is innervated by branches of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, primarily the radial and axillary nerves."},"correct":"E","explanation":"During upper limb development, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior side of the developing limb and forms the extensor compartment (triceps, wrist extensors, finger extensors, intrinsic extensors). It is innervated by the posterior division of the brachial plexus — specifically the radial nerve (C5-T1) and axillary nerve (C5-C6). The ventral muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division via the median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves. The tibial and obturator nerves are lower limb nerves and have no role in upper limb innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal muscle mass = extensors = posterior division brachial plexus (radial + axillary). Ventral muscle mass = flexors/adductors = anterior division (median + ulnar + musculocutaneous). Lower limb: dorsal mass = extensors (now anterior after medial rotation), still supplied by posterior-division nerves (femoral, common fibular, gluteal); ventral mass = flexors/adductors, supplied by anterior-division nerves (tibial, obturator). COMLEX: Radial nerve palsy (wrist drop) from midshaft humerus fracture disrupts the dorsal muscle mass innervation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q09","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Skeletal muscle fibers develop through a defined sequence of cellular stages: myoblasts fuse into long, multinucleated myotubes whose nuclei are initially central. In the maturing fiber, what causes the nuclei to move from a central position to the periphery of the cell?","choices":{"A":"Satellite cells fuse with the surface of the myotube and pull the existing central nuclei outward toward the sarcolemma","B":"Early contractions against the developing tendons mechanically displace the nuclei toward the ends and edges of the fiber","C":"Accumulating myofilaments fill most of the cytoplasm of the maturing myotube and slowly push the nuclei to the periphery","D":"Contact with the motor nerve at the neuromuscular junction draws all of the nuclei toward the synaptic region of the fiber","E":"Apoptosis removes the central nuclei, leaving only peripheral nuclei that are then replenished by further myoblast fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During differentiation, myoblasts fuse to form long, multinucleated myotubes with centrally placed nuclei. As myofibrils (organized actin and myosin filaments) accumulate, they take up most of the cytoplasm and push the nuclei to the periphery; by the end of the third month cross-striations typical of skeletal muscle appear (Langman). Satellite cells are reserve stem cells lying outside the fiber and do not reposition existing nuclei. Neither early contraction nor motor innervation drives the shift, and the central nuclei are displaced, not eliminated by apoptosis. Clinically, centrally placed nuclei in postnatal muscle on biopsy point to a myopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myoblasts fuse → multinucleated myotube with central nuclei → myofilaments fill the cytoplasm and push the nuclei to the periphery. Centrally placed nuclei in mature muscle = myopathy (e.g., centronuclear myopathy, myotonic dystrophy). COMLEX: Cross-striations appear by the end of the third month of development (Langman).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q10","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has short stature with shortened limb bones relative to a normal-length trunk, a large cranium, and limited elbow extension. Spinal radiographs show a narrow interpedicular distance in the lumbar region. Genetic testing identifies a gain-of-function mutation that impairs chondrocyte proliferation at growth plates. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein), whose loss-of-function destabilizes chondrocytes during endochondral ossification and causes pseudoachondroplasia with more pronounced joint laxity and no foreshortening of facial bones.","B":"GHR (growth hormone receptor), whose loss-of-function reduces IGF-1 production and causes proportionate dwarfism affecting limb length, trunk height, and cranial dimensions equally without facial bone involvement.","C":"IHH (Indian Hedgehog), whose gain-of-function would overstimulate chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, causing disproportionate elongation of limb bones relative to the trunk rather than shortening.","D":"COL1A1 (type I collagen alpha-1), whose loss-of-function disrupts bone matrix production and causes osteogenesis imperfecta with blue sclerae, frequent fractures, and normal limb-to-trunk proportions.","E":"FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3), whose constitutive activation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates, causing short limbs with a relatively normal trunk and a large head."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Achondroplasia is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, which constitutively inhibits chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates (endochondral ossification). Because long bones grow via endochondral ossification, they are shortened. The skull, however, grows via intramembranous ossification and is unaffected, giving a characteristically large cranium relative to the limbs. More than 85% of cases are sporadic and associated with advanced paternal age. COMP mutations cause pseudoachondroplasia, which is clinically similar but has greater joint laxity and no facial bone foreshortening. GHR mutations cause proportionate dwarfism. IHH gain-of-function would increase, not decrease, bone length. COL1A1 mutations cause osteogenesis imperfecta, not disproportionate dwarfism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia = FGFR3 gain-of-function; autosomal dominant, full penetrance; homozygosity lethal; most common cause of short-limbed dwarfism; >85% sporadic; associated with advanced paternal age (FA). Endochondral ossification impaired, membranous ossification intact → big head, short limbs. COMLEX: Spinal stenosis is a common complication of achondroplasia (impaired neural arch endochondral growth narrows canal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q11","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn is noted on physical examination to have fusion of the second and third digits bilaterally, with soft tissue and bony elements joined from the metacarpophalangeal to the distal interphalangeal joints. The remainder of the examination is normal. Which embryologic process failed to occur?","choices":{"A":"Apoptosis between the digital rays of the hand plate, which normally eliminates the interdigital mesenchyme and carves out each individual finger as a distinct structure.","B":"Migration of lateral plate mesoderm cells into the limb bud to form the skeletal precursors of each digit, resulting in absent mesenchymal condensations between the affected digital rays.","C":"Re-segmentation of sclerotome somites to create the intervertebral foramina, a homologous process to digit separation that fails when HOX gene expression is disrupted during vertebral column formation.","D":"Expression of FGF8 by the apical ectodermal ridge, which normally induces apoptosis in the interdigital mesenchyme by withdrawing growth factor support from non-digital ray tissue.","E":"Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the zone of polarizing activity, which normally assigns unique digit identities by establishing an anterior-to-posterior Shh concentration gradient across the hand plate."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Syndactyly results from failure of apoptosis (programmed cell death) in the interdigital mesenchyme between the digital rays. Normally, the cells of the hand plate between each digital ray undergo apoptosis, separating the fingers by approximately day 52. When this apoptosis fails or is incomplete, the digits remain fused — a spectrum ranging from simple skin webbing to complete bony fusion. The AER supports growth zone proliferation but does not directly induce interdigital apoptosis. The ZPA assigns digit identity via Shh but is not responsible for digital separation. Polydactyly, not syndactyly, results from aberrant molecular signals causing extra digital ray formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndactyly = failed apoptosis between digital rays. Polydactyly = aberrant ZPA signals or excess digit ray formation. In bats, the absence of interdigital apoptosis is what gives them wings — a useful memory hook. COMLEX: Digital anomalies may accompany chromosomal syndromes (e.g., trisomy 21, VACTERL); examining digits is part of the newborn screen for syndromic malformations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q12","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An infant born in the 1960s has five-fingered hands attached directly to the shoulder girdles, with complete absence of the humerus, radius, and ulna bilaterally. The fingers are functional with mild motor deficit. The mother had taken a sedative during the first trimester. Which mechanism best explains the pattern of limb malformation?","choices":{"A":"Complete absence of FGF8 from the AER caused total failure of limb bud outgrowth, with the hands spared because digital ray condensations had formed before the teratogen was introduced.","B":"A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation selectively impaired chondrocyte proliferation in the long bone growth plates of the arm and forearm while sparing the hand bones, which undergo intramembranous ossification.","C":"Failure of the ZPA to express Sonic Hedgehog during A/P patterning caused loss of all proximal limb segments, with only the most distal Shh-independent digit structures surviving teratogen exposure.","D":"Disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical limb elongation period prevented proximal and intermediate segment formation, while the AER-maintained distal bud still produced functional hands.","E":"Premature apoptosis of the dorsal muscle mass in the upper limb bud caused secondary regression of the humeral and forearm skeletal elements, which require mechanical stimulation from muscle contraction for normal development."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phocomelia — hands and feet projecting directly from the trunk — is the classic presentation of thalidomide embryopathy, which was used as a sedative/antiemetic in the late 1950s and early 1960s. The mechanism involves disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical window of limb development (the fourth and fifth weeks of gestation are the most sensitive period for thalidomide limb defects, Langman Ch 12). Because HOX genes pattern the proximal-to-intermediate limb segments, their disruption prevents humerus, radius, and ulna from forming, while the more distal AER-driven hand bud continues developing. Total absence of the AER would cause complete amelia with no limb at all. FGFR3 mutations cause achondroplasia, not proximal limb segment aplasia. ZPA disruption affects digit identity, not proximal segment formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phocomelia = thalidomide; mechanism = HOX gene disruption during the critical limb period (weeks 4–5 most sensitive, Langman Ch 12). Amelia = no AER = no FGF8 = no limb at all. Meromelia = partial limb aplasia. COMLEX: Thalidomide is teratogenic during organogenesis (weeks 3-8); limb defects are most readily induced in weeks 4–5 — the window of sensitivity coincides with when women often do not yet know they are pregnant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida"},{"id":"sdl1_q13","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A developmental biology researcher transplants the zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) from the posterior mesenchyme of a chick limb bud to the anterior mesenchyme of the same limb bud. The developing limb subsequently forms six digits instead of five, with the extra digits arranged in a mirror-image pattern on the anterior side. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying this finding?","choices":{"A":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF8 into the anterior mesenchyme, overriding the AER signal and causing progress zone cells to produce additional digit condensations proportional to local FGF8 concentration.","B":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF10 into the anterior mesenchyme, initiating a new limb bud outgrowth event that recruits additional lateral plate mesoderm cells to form a supernumerary complete digit column.","C":"The transplanted ZPA releases Wnt signaling proteins into the anterior limb field, which redirect dermomyotome cells from the paraxial mesoderm into the limb bud to form extra mesenchymal digit condensations.","D":"The transplanted ZPA releases Indian Hedgehog, which normally functions in growth plate chondrocyte regulation but ectopically activates HOX gene expression in the anterior limb field causing polydactyly.","E":"The transplanted ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog in the anterior mesenchyme, creating a second posterior-to-anterior Shh gradient in opposition to the original gradient, which induces mirror-image digit duplication in the anterior limb field."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ZPA normally sits in the posterior mesenchyme and expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh) at high concentration near the fifth digit and low concentration near the first digit. When a second ZPA is placed anteriorly, it creates an opposing Shh gradient, so both the posterior and anterior ends of the limb now interpret themselves as \"posterior.\" The result is mirror-image digit duplication: posterior-type digits (another fifth and fourth digit) form on the anterior side in place of the thumb. This classic experiment established that Shh is the key digit-patterning signal. The ZPA does not express FGF8, FGF10, Wnt, or IHH as its primary signaling output. FGF10 originates from lateral plate mesoderm, and AER expresses FGF8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA transplant to anterior = mirror-image polydactyly = classic experimental proof that Shh controls A/P digit patterning. Removal of ZPA from medial hand = thumbs on both sides. High Shh concentration = digit 5 identity; low = digit 1. COMLEX: Polydactyly can result from ZPA signal overstimulation or aberrant molecular signals during hand plate formation (the hand plate forms after day 35, course SDL 1 text).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — polydactyly from aberrant ZPA/Shh signals; amelia from absent AER/FGF8; phocomelia from HOX gene disruption during limb elongation"},{"id":"sdl1_q14","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A developmental biologist implants beads that block Wnt signaling between the dorsal neural tube and the overlying surface ectoderm of a chick embryo at the somite stage. Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the notochord and ventral neural tube is left intact. Which somite derivative would be expected to fail to form?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, because its mesenchyme depends on dorsal Wnt signals to migrate around the notochord and lay down the cartilage model of the vertebrae","B":"The meningotome, because the dura, arachnoid, and pia arise from somite cells that require Wnt from the overlying ectoderm to separate from the sclerotome","C":"The nucleus pulposus, because notochordal cells trapped between vertebral bodies require Wnt from the dorsal neural tube to persist within each disc","D":"The dermomyotome, because dorsal Wnt induces it lateral to the sclerotome, so the dermis and the skeletal muscle precursors of the somite are lost","E":"The parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm, because Wnt from the dorsal neural tube is required for it to join the ectoderm and form the somatopleure of the limbs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two dorsoventral signals pattern each somite. Sonic Hedgehog from the notochord and ventral neural tube induces the sclerotome, while Wnt from the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm induces the dermomyotome lateral to it; the dermomyotome then splits into the myotome (skeletal muscle) and dermatome (dermis). Blocking Wnt with Shh intact therefore removes the dermomyotome. The sclerotome is the Shh-dependent ventral derivative, so it still forms. The meningotome lies medial to the sclerotome and forms the meninges; the course text does not tie it to dorsal Wnt. The nucleus pulposus comes from the notochord, not from a somite. The parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm is not a somite derivative at all; it forms the somatopleure that builds the body wall and limb buds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somite patterning: ventral Shh (notochord, floor plate) → sclerotome (vertebrae, ribs); dorsal Wnt → dermomyotome → myotome (skeletal muscle) + dermatome (dermis). COMLEX: Dermatomes and myotomes share a segmental origin with the spinal nerve that follows them, the basis of segmental somatic dysfunction findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — the myotome splits into epaxial (posterior rami) and hypaxial (anterior rami) muscle groups"},{"id":"sdl1_q15","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman presents with acute low back pain after lifting a heavy box at work. MRI shows a herniated intervertebral disc at L4-L5 compressing the L5 nerve root. A first-year medical student reviewing the case asks their attending which embryonic structure gives rise to the nucleus pulposus that is compressing the nerve root. Which of the following is the correct answer?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which forms the fibrous outer ring of the intervertebral disc (the annulus fibrosus) and whose partial failure of migration creates the gelatinous herniated material seen on MRI.","B":"The notochord, a midline signaling structure in the embryo whose remnant, trapped between the re-segmented vertebral bodies, persists as the gelatinous nucleus pulposus of each intervertebral disc.","C":"The myotome, which gives rise to the paraspinal and intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami and does not contribute structural material to the intervertebral disc or nucleus pulposus.","D":"The meningotome, which forms the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord and nerve roots; its remnant does not persist within the nucleus pulposus or the disc space.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which forms the pelvic and lower extremity bones and the body wall cavities and does not contribute to midline axial structures such as the vertebral bodies or intervertebral discs."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The notochord is the embryonic midline structure that induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate and signals the surrounding sclerotomes to form the vertebral column. During re-segmentation, the caudal sub-segment of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite, trapping a remnant of the notochord between the newly formed vertebral bodies. This notochord remnant differentiates into the nucleus pulposus — the gelatinous central portion of the intervertebral disc. The sclerotome forms the annulus fibrosus (the outer ring), not the nucleus pulposus. First Aid explicitly states: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults.\"","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA direct quote, very high yield). Annulus fibrosus → sclerotome. In disc herniation, the nucleus pulposus (notochord remnant) ruptures through the annulus fibrosus (sclerotome-derived). COMLEX: Disc herniation at L4-L5 affects the L5 nerve root (exits below L5 pedicle); this root innervates the hypaxial muscles of the big toe dorsiflexion and lateral leg sensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — vertebral column re-segmentation creates nucleus pulposus from notochord remnant; annulus fibrosus from sclerotome"},{"id":"sdl1_q16","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An embryology professor explains that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites rather than a single somite. A student asks why this re-segmentation arrangement exists. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of the re-segmentation process?","choices":{"A":"Re-segmentation distributes the notochord remnant evenly between each vertebral body, preventing compression of the spinal cord by ensuring the gelatinous disc material is centered in the intervertebral space.","B":"Re-segmentation allows Sonic Hedgehog from the notochord to diffuse uniformly across all vertebral segments, inducing symmetric ossification of the centrum, pedicles, and neural arches simultaneously.","C":"Re-segmentation is required for the primary ossification centers to appear concurrently in both the centrum and neural arch, because the two sub-segments provide the separate vascular territories needed for each center.","D":"Re-segmentation places the cleft that forms in the middle of each original somite between two vertebrae, where it becomes the intervertebral foramen through which spinal nerves exit to reach their myotomes","E":"Re-segmentation prevents the somites from fusing into a continuous bony column around the spinal cord by maintaining individual segmental identities through differential HOX gene expression gradients."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Re-segmentation is functionally critical because it ensures that the intervertebral foramen (the exit point for spinal nerves) sits adjacent to the middle of the original somite — not at the somite boundary. If vertebrae were simply derived from single somites without re-segmentation, spinal nerves would exit directly at the sclerotome-to-sclerotome boundary, which would be mechanically problematic. By re-segmenting, each vertebra straddles the junction of two somites, and the cleft in the middle of each original somite (the fissure of von Ebner) becomes the foramen through which the segmental nerve exits. This is why each spinal nerve serves the same-numbered myotome and dermatome. The notochord remnant distribution and ossification center vascularization are separate processes not dependent on re-segmentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Re-segmentation purpose = creates intervertebral foramina for spinal nerve exit. Each vertebra = caudal half of superior somite + cranial half of inferior somite. This is why each spinal nerve exits BELOW its named vertebra (except C nerves). COMLEX: Intervertebral foramina are the sites of nerve root compression in radiculopathy; somatic dysfunction of a vertebral unit affects the same segment that innervates that myotome and dermatome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — somite segmentation and HOX gene patterning establish vertebral identities before re-segmentation reorganizes them into the final vertebral column"},{"id":"sdl1_q17","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman at 20 weeks gestation has an ultrasound revealing a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine. The sac contains meninges filled with cerebrospinal fluid, but the spinal cord and nerve roots are not displaced and no neurologic deficits are identified. Maternal serum AFP was elevated on second-trimester screening. Which of the following correctly identifies this defect and its embryologic basis?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele, in which both CSF-filled meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through the posterior bony defect; neurologic deficits below the level of the lesion are invariably present due to cord involvement.","B":"Spina bifida occulta, a closed defect with no herniation of any meningeal or neural tissue; discovered incidentally on imaging and typically marked only by a tuft of hair or skin dimple with no sac formation.","C":"Meningocele, from failure of the spinous processes and laminae to close over the lumbar spinal cord; the sac contains only CSF-filled meninges because the neural tube closed normally and the cord stays in the canal.","D":"Rachischisis, the most severe form of spina bifida, in which neurulation completely fails and exposed neural tissue lies on the dorsal surface without any meningeal covering, sac, or skin closure.","E":"Encephalocele, occurring when the cranial neuropore fails to close by day 24 and allows brain parenchyma and meninges to herniate through an opening in the calvarium rather than the lumbar vertebral column."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meningocele is an open NTD in which the meninges (but not the neural tissue) herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. Because the spinal cord itself remains within the vertebral canal, neurologic function is typically preserved. This differs from myelomeningocele, where both meninges and spinal cord or cauda equina herniate, causing neurologic deficits below the lesion. Spina bifida occulta has no external sac; it is a subclinical bony defect. Rachischisis involves complete neurulation failure with no meningeal covering at all. Encephalocele involves the cranial, not lumbar, neuropore. Open NTDs are associated with elevated maternal serum AFP, because AFP leaks from the exposed neural tissue into the amniotic fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NTD classification — occulta (no herniation) → meningocele (meninges only, no deficits) → myelomeningocele (cord + meninges, has deficits) → rachischisis (most severe, no closure). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid supplementation before conception reduces NTD risk. COMLEX: SDL 8 covers spinal dysraphism in the pediatric context; Cranial neuropore closes ~day 24 and caudal neuropore ~day 27 (course text, SDL 1); caudal closure failure → spina bifida spectrum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — neural tube defects from failed caudal neuropore closure; folic acid prevents NTDs by supporting rapid cell division during neurulation"},{"id":"sdl1_q18","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A second-year medical student is reviewing the embryologic basis for the anatomical organization of limb muscles. She notes that the upper limb undergoes a 90-degree rotation by day 56 so that the elbow points posteriorly, while the lower limb rotates 90 degrees in the opposite direction. Which of the following correctly describes a consequence of the lower limb rotating opposite to the upper limb?","choices":{"A":"Both upper and lower limbs rotate in the same direction, which is why both the elbow and the knee point posteriorly in anatomical position and are both innervated by the posterior divisions of their respective plexuses.","B":"The lower limb rotation causes the dorsal muscle mass to remain posterior, keeping the knee extensors in the same relative position as the elbow extensors, making the lower limb innervation pattern identical to the upper limb.","C":"The opposite rotation of the lower limb causes the knee to point posteriorly just like the elbow, explaining why the quadriceps are innervated by the posterior division of the lumbosacral plexus rather than the femoral nerve.","D":"The lower limb rotation has no effect on muscle compartment arrangement because hypaxial muscle mass migration from the myotome determines final compartment position independently of any limb bud torsion.","E":"The lower limb extensors, which arise from the dorsal muscle mass, come to lie anteriorly after rotation, while the flexors lie posteriorly — the opposite of the upper limb, where dorsal muscle mass extensors remain posterior."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The upper limb rotates so that the thumb points laterally and the elbow points posteriorly, placing the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) posteriorly — innervated by posterior brachial plexus division (radial/axillary). The lower limb rotates in the opposite direction so that the big toe is medial and the knee points anteriorly. This places the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) anteriorly — the quadriceps and pretibial muscles. This is why the \"extensors\" of the lower limb are on the front and still innervated by a posterior-division nerve (the femoral nerve), even though they now lie on the front of the limb. The lower limb flexors (hamstrings, calf) remain posterior, innervated by the tibial nerve (anterior division). Timing differs by source: the course text places the rotation by day 56, and Langman (Ch 12) places it in the seventh week, with each limb turning about 90 degrees.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upper limb: dorsal mass = posterior extensors = radial nerve (posterior division). Lower limb: dorsal mass = anterior extensors = femoral nerve (posterior division; the compartment moved, the division did not). Opposite rotation = inverted compartment positions. COMLEX: This explains dermatome \"barber pole\" arrangement on the lower limb — skin innervation twists due to limb rotation, producing spiral dermatomal patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — dorsal and ventral muscle mass innervation by posterior vs anterior plexus divisions; limb rotation determines final compartment position"},{"id":"sdl1_q19","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 22-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. Imaging reveals a midshaft humerus fracture. On examination he cannot extend the wrist, cannot extend the fingers, and has diminished sensation over the dorsum of the hand and lateral forearm. Which embryologic muscle mass is denervated by this injury and what is its developmental origin?","choices":{"A":"The ventral muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates anteriorly to form the flexor and adductor compartment and is innervated by the median and ulnar nerves, branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus.","B":"The epaxial muscle compartment, which derives from the medial myotome and forms the intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami; its injury causes paraspinal atrophy rather than wrist drop or dorsal hand sensory loss.","C":"The hypaxial prevertebral division, which forms muscles along the anterior vertebral column and is innervated by anterior rami; injury to this group causes neck flexion weakness rather than distal upper extremity motor deficit.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates posteriorly to form the extensor compartment and is innervated by the radial nerve, a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the lower limb, which forms the extensor compartment of the thigh and leg innervated by the femoral and common fibular nerves; this group is unrelated to upper extremity function."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial nerve palsy from a midshaft humerus fracture is the classic presentation of \"wrist drop\" with associated loss of finger extension and diminished dorsal hand sensation. The radial nerve is a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, which innervates all muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb. During embryogenesis, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior compartment as the limb rotates and differentiates into all extensor muscles of the arm, forearm, and hand. The ventral muscle mass forms flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division (median, ulnar, musculocutaneous nerves). Epaxial muscles are intrinsic back muscles and are not affected by a peripheral nerve injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus fracture = radial nerve injury = wrist drop + loss of finger extension + diminished lateral forearm/dorsal hand sensation (from superficial radial nerve). Radial nerve = posterior division = dorsal muscle mass embryologic origin. COMLEX: \"Saturday night palsy\" = radial nerve compression at the spiral groove (same nerve, different mechanism); somatic dysfunction at C7-C8 vertebral levels may reproduce radial nerve symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — dorsal muscle mass = posterior division brachial plexus; AER and somite myotome migration together establish limb muscle compartments"},{"id":"sdl1_q20","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman develops difficulty bringing her right thigh toward the midline after a pelvic lymph node dissection. Examination shows weak hip adduction and reduced sensation over a small area of the medial thigh; knee extension and hip abduction are normal. From which embryologic muscle mass did the weakened muscles develop?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal muscle mass of the lower limb, which migrated along the posterior side of the developing limb and is supplied by the gluteal, femoral, and common fibular nerves","B":"The ventral muscle mass of the lower limb, which migrated along the anterior side of the developing limb to form flexors and adductors on the obturator and tibial nerves","C":"The epaxial division of the myotome, which stayed close to the vertebral column to form the deep back muscles supplied segmentally by the posterior rami of the spinal nerves","D":"The prevertebral hypaxial division, which remained along the anterior surface of the lumbar vertebrae to form the psoas group supplied directly by the lumbar anterior rami","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm of the somatopleure, which formed the limb musculature in place without migration and receives its supply from the lumbosacral plexus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weak adduction with medial thigh numbness after pelvic surgery points to the obturator nerve, which supplies the adductor compartment. In the course text, hypaxial cells entering each limb bud split into a dorsal mass that forms extensors and a ventral mass that forms flexors and adductors; in the lower limb the ventral mass is supplied by the obturator and tibial nerves. The dorsal mass of the lower limb takes the gluteal, femoral, and common fibular nerves, and knee extension (femoral) and hip abduction (superior gluteal) are spared. Epaxial muscles are the intrinsic back muscles on posterior rami. The prevertebral hypaxial division forms muscles along the front of the vertebrae and would not explain an adductor deficit. Lateral plate mesoderm forms the limb bones and connective tissue; limb muscle comes from migrating myotome cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lower limb dorsal mass = extensors (gluteal, femoral, common fibular nerves); ventral mass = flexors/adductors (obturator, tibial nerves). Obturator nerve injury → weak adduction + medial thigh sensory loss. COMLEX: Obturator nerve injury is a known complication of pelvic lymph node dissection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lower limb rotation brings the dorsal-mass extensors to the anterior thigh"},{"id":"sdl1_q21","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher studying fetal skeletal muscle development collects biopsies at different gestational ages. She observes that the myotubes have begun producing myofilaments but their nuclei remain centrally located. By the end of the third month, cross-striations appear. She further notes that fetal movement begins around the third month of development. Which developmental event is most closely associated with the onset of fetal movement at this time?","choices":{"A":"Completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of fetal joint capsules by about the third month of development, allowing myotubes with forming myofilaments to move the joints for the first time.","B":"The first appearance of myofilaments in the early myotubes, which immediately generates sufficient contractile force for fetal movement before synovial joint cavities have fully formed or filled with fluid.","C":"The peripheral migration of muscle fiber nuclei as myofibrils accumulate, which is a histological marker of fiber maturation rather than the event that allows the fetal joints to be mobilized in the third month.","D":"The innervation of the myotome by anterior rami of the spinal cord at four weeks of development, which enables immediate coordinated muscle contractions upon completion of neurulation and axon outgrowth.","E":"The formation of secondary ossification centers in the epiphyses during the first two years of postnatal life, which allows load-bearing and initiates the muscle contractions required for coordinated movement."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fetal movement begins around the third month of intrauterine life (approximately 12 weeks), which correlates with the completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of joint capsules — an event that occurs by the end of the third month. Before this, joint cavities exist anatomically but the synovial layer has not yet begun secreting adequate fluid for smooth joint movement. Myofilaments appear earlier, but the ability to actually mobilize joints requires the mechanical prerequisite of functional synovial joints. Peripheral migration of the nuclei is a histological marker of fiber maturation, not the event that makes joint movement possible. Motor axons reach the myotome early, but coordinated movement requires both mature neuromuscular junctions and functional joints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial joint development is complete by the third month, enabling fetal movement. Oligohydramnios limits fetal movement and can cause contractures — e.g., talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) from restricted joint mobilization. COMLEX: Restricted fetal movement is a documented cause of positional deformities; this connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (clubfoot from oligohydramnios) and SDL 8 (DDH from restricted hip movement).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — synovial joint development from loose mesenchyme by the 3rd month enables the functional use of muscles that were formed from myotome differentiation"},{"id":"sdl1_q22","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for short stature. He has shortened extremities relative to a normal-length trunk, a prominent forehead, midface hypoplasia, and trident-shaped hands. His parents are of normal stature. Lumbar spine radiographs show decreased interpedicular distance. Neurological examination is normal. Which of the following best explains why this child is at risk for spinal cord compression?","choices":{"A":"Increased growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary stimulates IGF-1-driven vertebral body growth asymmetrically, causing anterior osteophyte formation that narrows the spinal canal from the front.","B":"A COMP gene mutation disrupts chondrocyte survival during endochondral ossification, causing early disc degeneration and progressive posterior disc herniation that compresses the cord at multiple levels.","C":"Premature estrogen rise relative to testosterone during early childhood accelerates closure of vertebral growth plates, shortening vertebral bodies and narrowing the spinal canal before age five.","D":"The gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates, including those that contribute to vertebral body height and the growth of the neural arches, producing a small spinal canal that can compress the cord.","E":"Failure of secondary ossification centers to appear in the annular epiphyses at puberty results in incomplete vertebral height and a congenitally narrow canal that worsens progressively during adolescence."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Achondroplasia from FGFR3 gain-of-function inhibits endochondral ossification at all growth plates, including those of the vertebral bodies and neural arches. In the lumbar spine, this produces a characteristically narrow interpedicular distance (the distance between the pedicles on AP imaging), creating a congenitally small spinal canal. Even minor disc pathology or osteophyte formation in adulthood can cause cord or cauda equina compression in these patients. This explains why spinal stenosis is a significant adult complication of achondroplasia. Growth hormone excess (gigantism/acromegaly) causes increased bone size via IGF-1 and would not cause a congenitally narrow canal. COMP mutations produce pseudoachondroplasia with joint laxity as the prominent feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia complications: (1) spinal stenosis from narrow canal; (2) sleep apnea from foramen magnum narrowing; (3) intelligence normal (membranous ossification unaffected). FGFR3 = constitutive activation = inhibits chondrocytes (gain-of-function paradox). COMLEX: Foramen magnum stenosis in achondroplasia can cause sudden death in infancy from cervical cord compression — an urgent pediatric complication requiring decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — endochondral ossification in growth plates driven by chondrocyte proliferation; FGFR3 normally limits growth plate activity, so gain-of-function causes premature closure"},{"id":"sdl1_q23","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve stays on her usual oral anticoagulant through the first trimester. Her newborn has a hypoplastic nasal bridge, short limbs, and stippled epiphyses on radiographs. Which statement best explains these findings and the drug choice that would have avoided them?","choices":{"A":"Warfarin crosses the placenta and disrupts fetal bone and cartilage formation; heparin, which does not cross the placenta, is the anticoagulant used in pregnancy","B":"Heparin crosses the placenta and causes bleeding into the fetal growth plates; warfarin, which does not cross the placenta, is the anticoagulant used in pregnancy","C":"Warfarin blocks FGF8 release from the apical ectodermal ridge, and stopping it at the end of the first trimester would have restored normal limb outgrowth","D":"The drug acted in the first two weeks after fertilization, when the embryo is most prone to organ-specific skeletal defects from a maternal medication","E":"A new gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation explains the short limbs, because drugs taken during organogenesis do not disturb fetal bone or cartilage growth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists warfarin among the teratogens that affect the musculoskeletal system (\"abnormal/reduced bone growth\", SDL 1 abnormal-development slides), and First Aid (p632) gives the full pattern: bone and cartilage deformities such as stippled epiphyses and nasal and limb hypoplasia, plus optic nerve atrophy and cerebral hemorrhage. Heparin does not cross the placenta, so it is the anticoagulant used during pregnancy (FA p632); saying heparin crosses and warfarin does not reverses the placental transfer. FGF8 from the apical ectodermal ridge drives limb outgrowth, and its loss causes amelia (course SDL 1 text); no source ties warfarin to that pathway. Exposure before implantation has an all-or-none effect, and the embryo is most susceptible to structural defects during organogenesis, before week 8 (FA p632). A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation causes achondroplasia, but it does not account for stippled epiphyses and nasal hypoplasia after a known drug exposure, and teratogens clearly can affect bone, as warfarin shows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warfarin embryopathy = bone and cartilage deformities (stippled epiphyses, nasal and limb hypoplasia), optic nerve atrophy, cerebral hemorrhage; use heparin in pregnancy because it does not cross the placenta. Before implantation = all-or-none; organogenesis (before week 8) = structural defects. COMLEX: Other musculoskeletal teratogens on the course slides: valproic acid and NSAIDs (spina bifida), leflunomide (limb and vertebral malformations), clindamycin (musculoskeletal hypoplasia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — teratogens act during organogenesis; warfarin, valproic acid, leflunomide, NSAIDs and clindamycin are the course's MSK teratogens"},{"id":"sdl1_q24","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn male has a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine at the L3-L4 level. The sac contains meninges, CSF, and displaced spinal cord with nerve roots. Lower extremity motor and sensory deficits are present below L3, and bladder and bowel function are absent. The mother had taken no prenatal vitamins. Maternal AFP was elevated on second-trimester screening. Which of the following best describes the embryologic event that failed and the preventable risk factor?","choices":{"A":"Failure of cranial neuropore closure by day 24 caused the neural tube defect; folate deficiency is the risk factor, but the resulting defect is anencephaly with absent forebrain rather than a lumbar spinal cord anomaly.","B":"Failed re-segmentation of the lumbar sclerotome somites produced fused vertebrae that compressed the spinal cord; iron deficiency anemia is the preventable risk factor associated with this form of spinal dysraphism.","C":"Failure of caudal neuropore closure by day 27 allowed sclerotomal mesenchyme to be excluded from the posterior arch, producing a myelomeningocele; folate deficiency is the preventable maternal risk factor.","D":"Failure of apoptosis in the lumbar interdigital mesenchyme caused soft tissue bridging over the developing neural tube, preventing normal spinous process formation and creating the fluid-filled posterior sac.","E":"Failed secondary ossification center development in the lumbar vertebral annular epiphyses allowed spinal cord herniation during the pubertal growth spurt; vitamin D deficiency is the relevant preventable risk factor."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Myelomeningocele is a severe open form of spinal dysraphism (only rachischisis is more severe), occurring when both meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. The embryologic failure is incomplete closure of the caudal neuropore by approximately day 27 of development, which prevents the sclerotomal mesenchyme from migrating posterior to the neural tube to form the spinous processes and laminae. The exposed, unfused neural tissue and meninges herniate posteriorly. Maternal folate deficiency during the periconception period is the primary preventable risk factor; supplementation with 400 mcg/day starting at least 1 month before conception (Langman Ch 18) reduces NTD risk significantly. Cranial neuropore closure failure causes anencephaly. Re-segmentation failure causes vertebral fusion, not NTD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelomeningocele = meninges + neural tissue herniate; neurologic deficits below the level. Caudal neuropore closes day 27; cranial closes day 24 (cranial closes first). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid: supplement 1 month prior to conception (FA). COMLEX: Myelomeningocele at L3-L4 → paraplegia, neurogenic bladder/bowel; management includes early surgical closure to prevent infection and further neural damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — caudal neuropore closure by day 27 is required for sclerotomal mesenchyme to form posterior vertebral elements; failure prevents arch formation and exposes neural tube"}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"Knee Pain: Diagnostic Workflow & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl4_q01","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating knee pain uses a 5-step sequential diagnostic workflow. The first step is to determine whether pain is related to trauma. Which of the following correctly identifies the FIFTH and final step in this knee pain diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Determining whether the pain is related to acute trauma or a recent sports-related mechanism","B":"Ruling out a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis, malignancy, or fracture","C":"Assessing whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","D":"Evaluating whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or referred pain is responsible","E":"Determining whether the pain is localized and identifying the specific pain generator"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 5-step knee pain diagnostic workflow proceeds: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized and, if so, what structure is the pain generator? Identifying the specific anatomic pain generator is the final step that guides targeted treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A structured diagnostic workflow is essential — always rule out dangerous causes (infection, fracture, tumor) before assuming OA. COMLEX: Osteopathic palpatory exam can help localize the pain generator at step 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q02","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male presents with acute knee pain and swelling after falling from a ladder at work. He has point tenderness over the tibial plateau and cannot bear full weight. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step should the physician prioritize FIRST?","choices":{"A":"Proceeding directly to the arthritis differential and ordering an X-ray series for OA staging","B":"Evaluating for nerve pathology such as radiculopathy as the most likely cause of acute swelling","C":"Ruling out dangerous medical conditions including fracture, dislocation, or vascular injury","D":"Identifying the localized pain generator by palpating individual knee compartments systematically","E":"Initiating conservative management with PRICE therapy before completing the diagnostic workflow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Step 2 of the knee pain workflow — ruling out dangerous medical conditions — takes priority when trauma is identified in step 1. Fracture, dislocation, vascular injury, and compartment syndrome must be excluded before advancing to the arthritis or nerve pathology steps. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator assessment before ruling out these conditions is an error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules guide X-ray ordering after knee trauma (age >55, inability to bear weight, isolated patella tenderness, or fibular head tenderness). COMLEX: Always document neurovascular status distal to a suspected fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q03","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral knee pain worsening with activity and stiffness lasting 15 minutes in the morning. She has no trauma, no fever, and normal neurological exam. The physician has excluded dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. Which step in the knee pain diagnostic workflow should be evaluated NEXT?","choices":{"A":"Returning to step 1 to re-evaluate for an unrecognized traumatic mechanism of injury","B":"Evaluating whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","C":"Skipping to step 5 and identifying the localized pain generator without further workup","D":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize the meniscal and ligamentous anatomy in detail","E":"Referring to neurology to rule out lumbar radiculopathy as the source of bilateral knee pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding dangerous conditions (step 2) and nerve pathology (step 4), the 5-step workflow directs the clinician to step 3: evaluating for OA or another arthritis. The clinical features — bilateral distribution, activity-related worsening, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and no trauma — are classic for OA and fit perfectly within this step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA clinical criteria include age ≥45, usage-related pain in one or few joints, and morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction of the knee may accompany OA and can be treated with OMT as adjunctive therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q04","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female soccer player plants her foot and receives a direct lateral blow to her knee, creating a valgus force. She immediately collapses with a \"pop,\" develops hemarthrosis within hours, and has medial joint line tenderness, positive anterior drawer, and positive valgus stress test with laxity at 30 degrees. This presentation is MOST consistent with which combination of injuries?","choices":{"A":"PCL, LCL, and lateral meniscus — the unhappy triad variant from a posterior hyperflexion mechanism","B":"ACL, PCL, and lateral meniscus — multiligamentous injury from a combined valgus and rotational force","C":"ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus — the classic unhappy triad from lateral valgus force on a planted foot","D":"MCL, LCL, and popliteal ligament — posterolateral corner injury from combined varus and hyperextension","E":"Patellar tendon, ACL, and medial collateral ligament — extensor mechanism disruption from deceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The unhappy triad consists of ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus injury caused by a lateral valgus force with the foot planted — the exact mechanism described. The anterior drawer sign indicates ACL disruption, valgus stress laxity confirms MCL injury, and medial joint line tenderness suggests medial meniscal involvement. First Aid notes that lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial in conjunction with ACL+MCL injury, but the classic teaching triad remains ACL+MCL+medial meniscus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus (classic); First Aid notes lateral meniscus is more frequently co-injured. Lachman test (knee at 30°) is more sensitive than anterior drawer for ACL. COMLEX: Valgus force is the classic mechanism for this CP8 knee injury pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q05","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old rugby player falls directly onto his flexed right knee with his ankle plantarflexed. He develops posterolateral knee pain and swelling. Examination reveals a positive posterior sag sign and 8mm of posterior tibial glide on the posterior drawer test at 90 degrees of knee flexion. Which ligament is most likely injured and what is its primary function?","choices":{"A":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which resists anterior translation of the tibia on the femur","B":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which resists posterior translation of the tibia on the femur","C":"Medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus stress and medial compartment widening","D":"Lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus stress and posterolateral corner instability","E":"Popliteofibular ligament, which resists external tibial rotation and posterior subluxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PCL resists posterior tibial translation on the femur. The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall onto a flexed knee with a plantarflexed ankle, or a dashboard injury (anterior force to the proximal tibia with the knee flexed). Positive posterior sag sign and posterior drawer test confirm PCL disruption. The mechanism and findings rule out ACL (which would show anterior drawer/Lachman), MCL (valgus stress), and LCL (varus stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL mnemonic — medial femoral condyle to POSTERIOR tibia. PCL injury: posterior sag + posterior drawer at 90°. MOI: fall on flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle OR dashboard injury (MVA). COMLEX: PCL rarely injured in isolation; assess for posterolateral corner co-injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q06","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old football player sustains a valgus blow to the knee. Examination shows medial joint tenderness, laxity of 7mm on valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion, and a firm endpoint. There is no laxity at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. Which MCL injury grade does this represent, and what structures are most likely intact?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1 injury with laxity under 5mm and a solid endpoint, indicating microscopic fiber disruption only","B":"Grade 3 injury with laxity over 10mm and no perceptible endpoint, indicating complete MCL rupture","C":"Grade 4 injury with complete bony avulsion from the medial femoral condyle requiring urgent surgery","D":"Grade 2 injury, 5-9mm laxity with a firm endpoint: a partial MCL tear, deep capsule and ACL intact","E":"Grade 3 injury with laxity at both 0 and 30 degrees, indicating complete MCL and ACL co-disruption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MCL grading: Grade 1 = <5mm laxity, solid endpoint; Grade 2 = 5-9mm laxity, firm endpoint (partial tear); Grade 3 = ≥10mm laxity, no endpoint (complete rupture). Laxity at 30° isolates the superficial MCL. Laxity at 0° indicates deep capsular and ACL involvement as well. This patient has 7mm laxity with a firm endpoint = Grade 2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCL valgus stress test at 30° = superficial MCL; laxity at 0° adds deep MCL and possibly ACL involvement. Grade 2 treated conservatively. Pellegrini-Stieda lesion = chronic MCL calcification on X-ray from prior injury. COMLEX: The course slides make two \"most\" statements: the ACL slide calls the ACL \"the most injured knee ligament\" (SDL 4 p4), and the MCL slide says ligamentous injuries are 40% of all knee injuries and \"MCL is the most common\" (p17; 7.9% of all knee injuries; high-risk sports football, hockey, rugby, wrestling). Moore (Ch 7) says only that ligament sprain is the most common knee injury in contact sports.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q07","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male basketball player twists his planted knee during a game, immediately feeling pain at the medial joint line followed by intermittent locking and giving-out episodes. McMurray test performed with external rotation and valgus force at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and medial pain. Which meniscus is injured and how does the McMurray test confirm this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral meniscus, because internal rotation with varus force stresses the lateral compartment reproducing lateral symptoms","B":"Posterior horn of the lateral meniscus, because external rotation with flexion compresses the posterolateral space","C":"Both menisci equally, because the McMurray test is non-specific and stresses medial and lateral simultaneously","D":"Anterior horn of the medial meniscus, because valgus stress preferentially loads the anterior attachment site","E":"Medial meniscus, because external rotation with valgus force stresses the medial compartment and reproduces medial pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The McMurray test mnemonic: LIME — Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus = Medial meniscus. External rotation stresses the medial compartment; a click or pain with this maneuver implicates the medial meniscus. Medial meniscal tears are more common than lateral (medial > lateral), occurring at 61/100,000 in the general population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: McMurray LIME mnemonic — Internal+varus=Lateral, External+valgus=Medial. Locking = bucket-handle tear. Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving-out) are indications for surgical referral. COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q08","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following patient profiles is MOST consistent with a degenerative meniscal tear rather than a traumatic meniscal tear?","choices":{"A":"A 16-year-old female soccer player who develops acute swelling and locking after a contact pivot injury","B":"A 55-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis who develops medial pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair","C":"A 22-year-old wrestler who sustains acute medial joint line pain after a takedown with rotational force","D":"A 30-year-old marathon runner who develops sudden lateral pain after a varus twisting fall on a trail","E":"A 19-year-old basketball player with prior ACL reconstruction who develops locking with pivoting drills"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (typically over 40) with pre-existing arthritis, often with minimal or no significant trauma. Risk factors include male gender, age >40, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis. Traumatic tears occur in younger individuals through acute twisting mechanisms. The 55-year-old with OA and minimal-trauma onset fits the degenerative profile exactly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tears = horizontal cleavage pattern, older patients, OA background, minimal trauma. Traumatic tears = vertical/longitudinal (bucket-handle), younger athletes, acute mechanism. COMLEX: OMT is not appropriate for acute bucket-handle tears causing mechanical locking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q09","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male has been managed conservatively for 8 weeks for a meniscal tear with PRICE therapy and physical therapy. He continues to experience his knee locking during daily activities, cannot fully extend the joint, and reports recurrent giving-out episodes. Which of the following findings BEST justifies surgical referral at this point?","choices":{"A":"A positive Thessaly maneuver in the clinic without any subjective sense of instability or locking during activities","B":"Mild medial joint line tenderness reproducible with deep squatting but no loss of full range of motion","C":"Persistent true locking and giving-out that have not responded to an adequate trial of conservative treatment","D":"MRI showing a Grade 1 signal change within the posterior horn without surface disruption or extension","E":"Occasional clicking with knee flexion beyond 90 degrees without effusion or functional limitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgical referral for meniscal tears is indicated when: (1) mechanical symptoms persist (locking, giving-out), (2) multiligamentous pathology is present, or (3) conservative measures have failed. True locking — inability to fully extend the knee — typically indicates a displaced bucket-handle tear requiring arthroscopic intervention. A positive provocative test without mechanical symptoms alone does not meet the threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications = mechanical symptoms (locking/giving-out), multiligamentous instability, or failure of conservative care. True locking = bucket-handle tear. Initial management = PRICE + PT + avoidance of deep squatting and pivoting. COMLEX: Role of regenerative injections (PRP) is being studied for degenerative meniscal tears.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q10","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old soccer player sustains a pivoting (internal rotation) injury and develops acute hemarthrosis. AP X-ray shows a small avulsed bone fragment at the anterolateral margin of the proximal tibia, just below the lateral tibial plateau. Which fracture does this represent and what associated injury is strongly implied?","choices":{"A":"Pellegrini-Stieda lesion, a calcification at the medial femoral condyle indicating prior chronic MCL injury","B":"Arcuate sign, a small fibular head avulsion indicating injury to the posterolateral corner structures","C":"Osgood-Schlatter lesion, fragmentation at the tibial tubercle from patellar tendon traction in adolescents","D":"Segond fracture, an anterolateral ligament avulsion from the lateral tibia that strongly implies an ACL tear","E":"Tibial plateau impaction fracture, indicating articular surface depression from axial loading of the lateral condyle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Segond fracture is an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament (ALL) from the lateral tibial plateau. It appears as a small osseous fragment lateral to the tibial plateau on AP X-ray and is highly specific for concurrent ACL tear — so much so that a Segond fracture is considered pathognomonic for ACL disruption until proven otherwise. The arcuate sign is a posterolateral corner fracture of the fibular head. Pellegrini-Stieda = chronic MCL calcification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = ACL tear until proven otherwise. X-ray finding: small bony fragment lateral to proximal tibia on AP view. COMLEX: All traumatic knee injuries require neurovascular assessment distal to the injury before imaging workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q11","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Knee Rules, which of the following patient presentations mandates knee radiography after acute trauma?","choices":{"A":"Inability to bear weight for four steps, age over 55 years, or isolated tenderness at the fibular head or patella","B":"Any knee pain occurring after a sports injury regardless of the ability to ambulate after the event","C":"Presence of joint effusion or hemarthrosis regardless of weight-bearing status at the time of presentation","D":"Positive Lachman test suggesting ligamentous laxity in the absence of bony point tenderness","E":"Prior history of knee surgery or previous fracture regardless of current mechanism of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ottawa Knee Rules indicate X-ray if: (1) age ≥55, (2) isolated tenderness at the fibular head, (3) isolated patellar tenderness with no other bony tenderness, (4) inability to flex to 90°, or (5) inability to bear weight (4 steps) both immediately and in the ED. Effusion alone, prior surgery, and ligamentous laxity without the above criteria do not mandate imaging per the Ottawa Rules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules — validated decision rule to reduce unnecessary knee X-rays after trauma. COMLEX: These rules apply to adults; pediatric knee trauma generally warrants lower threshold for imaging given growth plate vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q12","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male sustains a tibial plateau fracture during a football game. X-ray confirms the fracture but cannot characterize potential soft tissue injuries. Which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for evaluating concurrent meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption, and chondral injury in this setting?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the knee, which provides the best characterization of articular cartilage and synovial tissue","B":"Bone scan, which detects areas of increased metabolic activity corresponding to ligamentous microtrauma","C":"Diagnostic arthroscopy, which is the definitive assessment but carries procedural risk and is not first-line","D":"Weight-bearing X-ray series, which adequately characterizes all soft tissue injuries around the fracture site","E":"MRI, with superior soft tissue contrast for meniscal, ligamentous, and cartilage injury not seen on X-ray"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for evaluating soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligament disruption, and cartilage damage — that accompany knee fractures. CT excels at defining bony architecture and articular depression but is inferior to MRI for soft tissue. Bone scan is not used for acute soft tissue evaluation. Arthroscopy remains definitive but invasive; MRI guides surgical planning first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = study of choice for meniscal tears and ligament injuries. After tibial plateau fracture, MRI changes surgical plan in a high proportion of cases by revealing meniscal and ligamentous co-injuries. COMLEX: Always image before manipulating a post-fracture knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q13","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellofemoral syndrome (PFS), commonly called \"runner's knee,\" is one of the most prevalent knee complaints in outpatient practice. Which of the following correctly describes the proportion of all knee problems that PFS represents in a typical outpatient clinic setting?","choices":{"A":"Fewer than 5% of all knee problems, primarily seen in elderly sedentary patients with significant comorbidities","B":"Approximately 8-10% of all knee problems, similar in incidence to lateral meniscal tears in athletes","C":"More than 60% of all knee problems, making it the most common musculoskeletal complaint overall","D":"Between 25 and 40% of all knee problems seen in outpatient clinical settings","E":"Exactly 15% of all knee problems, documented exclusively in female distance runners under age 30"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PFS accounts for 25-40% of all knee problems seen in clinic, making it one of the most common knee complaints in outpatient practice. Its true incidence is unknown due to under-reporting and variable diagnostic criteria. It can be traumatic (fall on flexed knee) or atraumatic (overuse). It is NOT exclusively seen in runners and affects all activity levels and ages.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS is extremely common; also called \"chondromalacia patella.\" Key clinical finding: pain with patellofemoral compression test and single-leg squat. Biomechanical drivers = dynamic valgus, quad weakness, hip weakness. COMLEX: PFS often has a component of somatic dysfunction of the patellofemoral articulation amenable to soft tissue OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q14","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, prolonged sitting with knees bent, and single-leg squatting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Dynamic valgus is observed on single-leg squat. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the patellofemoral joint to reduce inflammatory-driven cartilage pain","B":"Immediate surgical referral for arthroscopic debridement of patellar articular cartilage surface changes","C":"Complete activity restriction for 3 months with cast immobilization to allow cartilage surface recovery","D":"Oral opioid analgesics as a bridge therapy while awaiting specialist evaluation for surgical management","E":"Physical therapy for quadriceps and posterior hip strengthening and hamstring flexibility to correct maltracking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Physical therapy is the cornerstone first-line treatment for PFS. The program targets: (1) quadriceps strengthening for patellar stabilization, (2) posterior hip strengthening (gluteus medius/maximus) to reduce dynamic valgus, and (3) hamstring flexibility. Dynamic valgus is a key biomechanical driver of patellofemoral maltracking and increased contact pressure. Surgery and injections are not first-line for PFS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS first-line = PT with quad + hip strengthening. No role for surgery without prior PT failure. Dynamic valgus from hip abductor weakness is the dominant biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellar taping (McConnell technique) may provide short-term symptom relief as an adjunct to PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q15","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male recreational cyclist develops anterior knee pain reproducible with the patellofemoral compression test and aggravated by prolonged knee flexion (the \"movie sign\"). Which mechanism BEST explains the pathophysiology of pain in patellofemoral syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Synovitis from repetitive friction between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral epicondyle during cycling","B":"Abnormal patellar cartilage contact pressure against the femoral trochlear groove caused by patellar maltracking","C":"Posterior knee capsule inflammation from excessive tibial internal rotation during the power phase of cycling","D":"Tibial stress fracture from repetitive axial load producing referred anterior knee pain from periosteal irritation","E":"Avascular necrosis of the patellar apex from stress-related disruption of blood supply during high-cadence cycling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PFS results from abnormal patellofemoral contact pressure caused by patellar maltracking — the patella does not glide smoothly through the trochlear groove. Increased contact pressure on articular cartilage produces pain, particularly with activities that increase patellofemoral loading (stairs, squatting, prolonged sitting = \"movie sign\"). The other choices describe separate entities: IT band syndrome, popliteal pathology, tibial stress fracture, and AVN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Movie sign\" = pain after prolonged sitting with knees flexed (patellofemoral contact pressure increases with knee flexion). Dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and amplifies contact pressure. COMLEX: PFS in runners often presents with bilateral symptoms unlike meniscal or ligamentous injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver"},{"id":"sdl4_q16","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old tile installer presents with fluctuant, erythematous swelling directly overlying the patella after years of kneeling on hard surfaces. He has low-grade fever. Aspiration reveals cloudy, purulent fluid and the Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which is the MOST appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Compression wrap and elevation alone without aspiration or antibiotics, since bursitis resolves with PRICE therapy","B":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the prepatellar bursa to reduce acute inflammation and prevent recurrence","C":"Colchicine therapy for presumed gout since urate crystals can cause purulent-appearing fluid and elevated WBC","D":"Immediate surgical bursectomy without prior antibiotic therapy given the occupational exposure and purulent aspirate","E":"Aspiration of the bursal fluid combined with systemic antibiotics targeting Staphylococcus aureus for septic prepatellar bursitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Septic prepatellar bursitis (positive Gram stain, fever, purulent aspirate) requires aspiration plus systemic antibiotics. Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus aureus, the most common causative organism. Corticosteroid injection into a septic bursa is contraindicated. Colchicine is for gout (urate crystals on aspirate, no organisms). Bursectomy is reserved for recurrent or refractory cases, not first-line. PRICE alone is insufficient for septic bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepatellar bursitis ddx — septic (fever, erythema, organisms) vs. gout (urate crystals) vs. mechanical (kneeling exposure, no fever). Septic = aspirate + antibiotics. \"Housemaid's knee\" = classic name for prepatellar bursitis from chronic kneeling. COMLEX: Distinguish intra-articular from extra-articular fluid collection before injecting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q17","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old female volleyball player sustains her first lateral patellar dislocation after a non-contact planting and pivoting maneuver. Reduction is achieved in the emergency department. MRI confirms disruption of the medial patellofemoral ligament without osteochondral fracture. She has no prior history of patellar instability. What is the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical MPFL reconstruction regardless of dislocation episode history or recurrence risk","B":"Long-leg cast immobilization for 10 weeks before any rehabilitation or weightbearing is permitted","C":"PRICE therapy, physical therapy for quadriceps and hip strengthening, and a patellar stabilizing brace","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce hemarthrosis followed by early return to sport","E":"Lateral retinacular release as the primary surgical intervention for first-episode lateral dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First-episode patellar dislocation without osteochondral fracture or predisposing anatomic factors is managed conservatively with PRICE, PT (quad/hip strengthening), and patellar bracing. Surgical MPFL reconstruction is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when predisposing anatomic factors (trochlear dysplasia, high TT-TG distance) are present. Lateral retinacular release alone does not address the MPFL deficiency and is not standard first-line care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint to lateral patellar displacement. MRI is the key imaging study post-reduction. Surgery is considered for recurrent dislocation or anatomic predisposition. COMLEX: Patellar instability is a CP8 joint dislocation condition; ensure documentation of ligamentous laxity as a predisposing factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q18","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When evaluating a patient with recurrent patellar subluxation using MRI, which structure is the PRIMARY imaging target for assessing the integrity of the medial patellar restraint system?","choices":{"A":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which acts as the primary medial stabilizer of the patellofemoral articulation","B":"Quadriceps tendon, which is assessed for longitudinal splitting and partial tearing in recurrent instability","C":"Lateral collateral ligament, which when torn allows unrestricted medial-to-lateral patellar displacement","D":"Medial patellofemoral ligament, which is the primary passive restraint preventing lateral displacement of the patella","E":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which indirectly stabilizes the patella by controlling tibial rotation at the knee"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary passive restraint against lateral patellar displacement, and the course lists MRI of MPFL integrity as the imaging step for patellar subluxation (SDL 4 slide p57). On MRI, MPFL injury appears as signal change or discontinuity along its course from the medial femoral condyle to the superomedial patella. PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps tendon do not serve as primary medial patellar restraints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL runs from medial femoral epicondyle to superomedial patella. MPFL completely torn in over 50% of patellar dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics). Merchant view (X-ray) = sunrise view for patellofemoral alignment. COMLEX: Ligamentous laxity as a systemic condition predisposes to recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint"},{"id":"sdl4_q19","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is best described pathophysiologically as which of the following structures?","choices":{"A":"A true synovial cyst arising from a congenital remnant of the embryonic knee joint capsule in adults","B":"A ganglion cyst forming from periarticular connective tissue without communication to the joint space","C":"A hematoma that organizes into a posterior fluid collection after posterior cruciate ligament rupture","D":"A lipoma within the popliteal fossa that compresses neurovascular structures during knee flexion","E":"A gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis that frequently communicates with the synovial cavity of the knee joint"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is a gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis — specifically a fluid collection within the bursa located between the medial gastrocnemius and semimembranosus tendons. In adults it typically communicates with the synovial cavity, allowing joint fluid to accumulate in the bursa when intra-articular pressure increases. This distinguishes it from a true ganglion cyst (no synovial communication) or a hematoma (post-traumatic).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = popliteal fluid in gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa, communicating with synovial space in adults. Causes include OA, RA, and meniscal tears. COMLEX: Rupture of a Baker's cyst can mimic DVT (\"pseudothrombophlebitis\") — ultrasound differentiates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q20","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Popliteal cysts demonstrate a bimodal age distribution with two distinct incidence peaks. Which of the following correctly identifies BOTH peak age ranges and their corresponding pathophysiological contexts?","choices":{"A":"Ages 1-3 years in infants due to congenital joint laxity, and ages 50-60 due to post-arthroplasty bursitis","B":"Ages 12-18 in adolescent athletes due to meniscal overuse, and ages 40-55 due to early inflammatory arthritis","C":"Ages 20-35 in young adults from acute knee ligament injuries, and ages 65-80 from post-surgical adhesions","D":"Ages 4-7 in children with non-communicating cysts, and ages 35-70 in adults with degenerative joint disease","E":"Ages 8-12 in school-age children from sports overuse, and ages 60-75 from end-stage knee osteoarthritis only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Popliteal cysts have two incidence peaks: (1) ages 4-7, where the cyst typically does NOT communicate with the joint space (different pathoanatomy than adults) and frequently resolves spontaneously; (2) ages 35-70, where the cyst is associated with underlying intra-articular pathology (OA, RA, meniscal tears) and communicates with the synovial space. Treatment differs significantly between the two groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Children (4-7) — non-communicating, often resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or aspiration. Adults (35-70) — communicating, address underlying pathology. Baker's cyst rupture mimics DVT with calf pseudothrombophlebitis. COMLEX: Ultrasound is useful to guide aspiration and confirm communication with joint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q21","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old male with advanced knee osteoarthritis presents with a large posterior knee mass and restricted flexion. Ultrasound confirms a popliteal cyst communicating with the synovial space. He has undergone two prior aspirations that resolved the cyst temporarily, but it returned within 3 months each time. Which represents the MOST effective long-term treatment strategy?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying osteoarthritis, since cyst recurrence reflects persistent intra-articular pathology","B":"Surgically excising the cyst wall through a posterior popliteal approach as the definitive long-term treatment","C":"Performing repeated aspiration every 3 months indefinitely to prevent symptomatic recurrence of the cyst","D":"Prescribing custom orthotics to reduce tibial loading and allow the cyst to reabsorb through offloading","E":"Aspirating the cyst with concurrent corticosteroid injection as the definitive non-surgical management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Popliteal cysts in adults recur after aspiration because the underlying cause — excess synovial fluid production from OA, RA, or meniscal tears — is not addressed. The cyst is a consequence, not a primary pathology. Effective treatment requires addressing the root cause. If OA is the driver, management follows OA guidelines (exercise, NSAIDs, IACS, eventually TKA). Aspirating the cyst without treating the source leads to predictable recurrence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = manifestation of intra-articular pathology. Treat the cause (OA, RA, meniscal tear). Aspiration alone = temporizing. COMLEX: This principle mirrors osteopathic philosophy — treat the primary dysfunction, not the compensatory finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q22","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the simplest clinical diagnostic criteria for knee osteoarthritis, a diagnosis can be established when a patient has persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, age of 45 years or older, and which additional criterion?","choices":{"A":"Elevated ESR above 40 mm/hr with synovial biopsy showing fibrocartilaginous pannus formation","B":"Morning stiffness lasting 30 minutes or less, without features of systemic inflammatory arthritis","C":"Positive rheumatoid factor with symmetric small joint involvement and periarticular osteopenia","D":"Positive ANA titer with malar rash, photosensitivity, and constitutional symptoms","E":"X-ray showing periarticular erosions with uniform joint space loss and cortical destruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The simplest OA diagnostic criteria are: (1) persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, (2) age ≥45 years, and (3) morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. This triad establishes the clinical diagnosis without requiring imaging or laboratory studies. Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SLE, vasculitis) is distinguished by morning stiffness > 1 hour (FA 2025; Bootcamp gives ~>30 min for RA), positive serology, systemic features, and erosive changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA is a CLINICAL diagnosis — morning stiffness ≤30 min vs. RA (>1 hour). Usage-related pain = mechanical (worse with activity, better with rest). COMLEX: OA is the most common form of arthritis; CP8 named musculoskeletal condition for COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q23","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines for non-surgical management of knee osteoarthritis, which of the following is classified as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus strength across ALL patient comorbidity profiles?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection as monotherapy without a concurrent structured exercise program","B":"Non-selective oral NSAIDs as the primary first-line treatment across all patients regardless of GI or CV history","C":"COX-2 selective inhibitors combined with intra-articular hyaluronic acid as universal first-line pharmacotherapy","D":"Duloxetine as the first-line agent in patients with bilateral knee OA given its central sensitization mechanism","E":"Structured land-based exercise programs combined with arthritis patient education as the foundation of OA management"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OARSI guidelines designate structured land-based exercise (strengthening, cardio, balance/neuromuscular training, OR mind-body exercise such as Tai Chi or yoga) combined with arthritis education as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus and strength of evidence. This applies regardless of comorbidities. Pharmacologic options are layered based on comorbidity profile (GI, CV, frailty, widespread pain/depression).","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI core = exercise + education. Topical NSAIDs preferred for GI comorbidity. IACS for symptomatic flares. IAHA conditionally recommended. COMLEX: Exercise is the most universally recommended intervention — OMT can complement structured exercise programs for OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q24","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is considered the GOLD STANDARD for the initial diagnosis and staging of knee osteoarthritis, and which radiographic features are most diagnostically useful?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing X-ray, showing joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and subchondral cysts","B":"MRI of the knee, showing synovial thickening and early cartilage signal changes on T2-weighted fluid-sensitive sequences","C":"Ultrasound of the knee, showing hypoechoic effusion and osteophyte shadowing with dynamic joint assessment","D":"CT arthrogram, showing articular cartilage thinning and full-thickness chondral defects with bony detail","E":"DEXA scan, showing periarticular bone density reduction and cortical thinning in the affected compartments"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging. Key findings: joint space narrowing (most important — reflects cartilage loss), osteophytes (bony growths at joint margins), subchondral sclerosis (increased bone density beneath cartilage), and subchondral cysts. Weight-bearing is essential — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing. MRI is reserved for complex cases or when soft tissue evaluation is needed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray triad = joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis/cysts. Weight-bearing is critical. MRI is NOT first-line for OA. Merchant view assesses patellofemoral compartment alignment. COMLEX: X-ray finding of bone-on-bone OA indicates TKA may be appropriate when conservative measures fail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging"},{"id":"sdl4_q25","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old male presents with chronic knee pain. The physician begins the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow by obtaining a thorough history including pain characteristics, prior knee injuries, and family history. What is the CORRECT next step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize cartilage and synovial pathology before completing physical examination","B":"Performing a directed exam with range of motion, palpation for bony prominences, effusion, tenderness, crepitus","C":"Obtaining synovial fluid analysis to differentiate inflammatory from non-inflammatory arthritis before imaging","D":"Prescribing a therapeutic NSAID trial to confirm an inflammatory pain pattern before further workup","E":"Ordering nuclear medicine bone scan to detect early periarticular metabolic activity before X-ray"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The knee arthritis diagnostic workflow proceeds: history → physical examination → imaging. After history, the next step is physical examination including ROM, palpation for bony prominences and effusion, assessment of tenderness, and crepitus on motion. Only after completing history and exam does imaging follow, beginning with weight-bearing X-ray. MRI is not the first imaging study ordered in the arthritis workflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow = history → PE → weight-bearing X-ray → MRI (only if complex). Physical exam hallmarks of OA: crepitus, bony enlargement, restricted ROM, effusion. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam can identify somatic dysfunction contributing to painful gait adaptation in OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q26","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has medial knee pain on stairs and squatting. A weight-bearing AP view taken with the knee extended shows a preserved medial joint space. The physician suspects that the cartilage loss lies on the posterior femoral condyle and the tibia. Which additional radiograph is most likely to reveal the arthritis?","choices":{"A":"A non-weight-bearing lateral view, because removing load lets the femur drop back and shows posterior condylar wear","B":"A Merchant view, because peering beneath the patella shows wear between the posterior femur and the tibial plateau","C":"A weight-bearing flexed-knee PA Rosenberg view, which rotates the worn posterior condyle opposite the bare tibia","D":"A non-weight-bearing AP view, because the relaxed joint opens and outlines the cartilage defect more clearly than standing","E":"Bilateral oblique views, because angling the beam profiles the posterior condyles that the AP view superimposes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course reading describes a common pattern in which cartilage is lost on the tibia and the posterior femoral condyle while the anterior femur keeps cartilage. On the extended weight-bearing AP the joint space still looks open. The weight-bearing flexed-knee PA view (Rosenberg or notch view) rotates the posterior bald spot opposite the bare tibia and reveals bone-on-bone contact. The lateral view gives the least helpful information of all the views. The Merchant view assesses the patellofemoral joint, not the tibiofemoral contact. A non-weight-bearing AP can make even a bone-on-bone joint look open because the surfaces relax apart. Oblique views belong to the emergency fracture series and are not the view used for this wear pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthritis X-ray series: weight-bearing AP, weight-bearing flexed PA (Rosenberg), Merchant, lateral. Non-weight-bearing films underestimate joint space loss. COMLEX: Order the arthritis series, not the emergency \"knee series,\" for office OA evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — Merchant (sunrise) view for patellofemoral alignment"},{"id":"sdl4_q27","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, in which clinical scenarios is MRI specifically indicated beyond the standard weight-bearing X-ray evaluation?","choices":{"A":"When the case is complex, soft tissue injury must be excluded, or cartilage status will change management","B":"As the routine initial imaging study for all patients over 60 presenting with knee pain and morning stiffness","C":"When the patient has classic OA risk factors and X-ray already demonstrates joint space narrowing bilaterally","D":"For annual monitoring of disease progression in all patients with established knee OA on stable treatment","E":"Only in patients under 40 presenting with unexplained monoarticular arthritis without X-ray changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, MRI is indicated when: (1) the case is complex and X-ray is insufficient, (2) soft tissue injuries (meniscal, ligamentous) need to be ruled out, or (3) cartilage integrity assessment will change the clinical management plan. MRI is NOT the routine first imaging study — weight-bearing X-ray serves that role. It is also not indicated for routine monitoring of stable OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI is powerful but not cost-effective for routine OA monitoring. Reserve for complexity, pre-surgical planning, or when diagnosis is uncertain. Ultrasound can assess effusion and guide injection but is not the gold standard for OA diagnosis. COMLEX: Imaging should guide — not replace — the clinical examination and history in the OA workflow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q28","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with BMI of 34 presents with 4 months of right knee pain that is diffuse, worsening with prolonged standing, and associated with morning stiffness lasting 15 minutes. He denies trauma, fever, or radicular leg pain. Neurovascular exam is normal. X-ray shows medial compartment joint space narrowing. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, the physician determines that trauma, dangerous conditions, and nerve pathology have been excluded. Which of the following BEST represents the appropriate next diagnostic conclusion?","choices":{"A":"Peroneal nerve palsy should be evaluated given the occupational exposure to prolonged standing positions","B":"ACL tear should be considered given the diffuse knee pain and positive biomechanical stress on the medial compartment","C":"Osteoarthritis is the most likely cause, so proceed with an OA-directed assessment and OARSI-guided management","D":"Femoral nerve entrapment should be ruled out given the anterior thigh and medial knee pain distribution pattern","E":"Popliteal artery entrapment should be considered given the BMI-related vascular risk and posterior knee symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1), dangerous conditions (step 2), and nerve pathology (step 4), step 3 of the workflow — OA or arthritis — becomes the focus. The clinical presentation (age, BMI, diffuse pain worse with activity, stiffness ≤30 min) combined with X-ray showing medial compartment narrowing is diagnostic of knee OA. Proceeding with OARSI-guided management (exercise, education, pharmacotherapy) is appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The knee pain workflow is a structured clinical heuristic — use it to prevent premature closure. OA is the most common cause of chronic knee pain in patients over 45. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction often accompanies OA and may be an adjunctive treatment target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/collateral injuries present after trauma; the workflow step 2 dangerous-condition screen must precede advancing to OA assessment in atraumatic chronic knee pain"},{"id":"sdl4_q29","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female marathon runner presents with left anterior knee pain that worsens with downhill running and stairs but resolves with rest. She has no trauma history, no joint line tenderness, no effusion, and no neurological deficits. The physician excludes dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. On single-leg squat, dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which anatomic pain generator is identified in step 5?","choices":{"A":"The patellofemoral joint, where dynamic valgus raises contact pressure during downhill impact loading","B":"The medial collateral ligament, which sustains chronic valgus stress from pronation during forefoot strike","C":"The posterior cruciate ligament, which bears eccentric load during downhill deceleration maneuvers","D":"The medial tibial plateau, which accumulates stress fracture from high-mileage cumulative impact loading","E":"The popliteal fossa bursa, which becomes inflamed from repetitive posterior compression during running"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Step 5 of the knee pain workflow identifies the specific pain generator. Dynamic valgus, anterior pain worse with patellofemoral loading (stairs, downhill), and positive patellofemoral compression test localize the pain to the patellofemoral joint. Downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure due to greater knee flexion angle during impact, amplifying pain in PFS. This finding guides targeted PT (hip and quad strengthening to correct valgus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Downhill running = greatest patellofemoral contact pressure (greater knee flexion angle). Single-leg squat reveals dynamic valgus as the biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellofemoral syndrome is a named CP8 musculoskeletal condition amenable to structured rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome identified as pain generator at step 5; downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure proportionally with knee flexion angle"},{"id":"sdl4_q30","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old male with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with right knee pain, erythema, and fever for 3 days. He has marked warmth and swelling of the entire joint. CRP is 85 mg/L and WBC is 16,000/mL. He denies trauma. Using the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step is MOST critical to prioritize immediately in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for osteoarthritis as the cause given his age and the presence of joint swelling","B":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic arthritis","C":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to determine whether OA or bursitis is responsible","D":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma assessment since a missed fall in a diabetic patient could explain findings","E":"Step 2 — determining whether a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis is responsible for the presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fever, erythema, elevated inflammatory markers, and monoarticular swelling in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient demand immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous medical condition — specifically septic arthritis, which requires urgent arthrocentesis, culture, and antibiotics. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator steps without ruling out septic arthritis would be a critical clinical error in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Septic arthritis is a medical emergency — joint destruction occurs within days. Synovial fluid WBC >50,000/mL with >90% PMNs is highly suggestive. Never inject a joint with suspected infection. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major risk factor for septic arthritis and septic bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — septic arthritis is a dangerous condition excluded in step 2 of the workflow before attributing knee pain to OA or structural causes"},{"id":"sdl4_q31","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old soccer player has two injuries from separate seasons. A complete (grade III) MCL tear from a valgus tackle healed with 2 weeks in an immobilizer followed by a hinged brace and strengthening. A complete ACL tear from a noncontact cutting injury this season still shows no continuity on MRI after 4 months of rehabilitation, and he has recurrent giving-out. Which feature best explains why the two complete tears behaved differently?","choices":{"A":"The ACL is loaded in tension throughout knee flexion, whereas the MCL is unloaded in full extension, so the MCL stumps can rest and reattach","B":"The MCL is extra-articular with vessels inserting along its length, whereas the ACL spans the joint space with poor blood supply and cannot heal","C":"Synovial fluid contains collagenase that digests torn MCL fibers less than ACL fibers, because the MCL is coated by the medial meniscus","D":"The ACL has a single bundle that retracts fully once ruptured, whereas the MCL has anteromedial and posterolateral bundles that bridge the gap","E":"The MCL receives sensory nerve fibers that trigger protective bracing reflexes, whereas the ACL lacks innervation and is reinjured during healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The practical office orthopedics reading for this SDL explains that the collateral ligaments are extra-articular, surrounded by vascular soft tissue, with blood vessels inserting along their whole length, so nearly all collateral injuries, even complete grade III tears, heal without surgery. The cruciates span the joint space with no vessels inserting along their length, so complete tears do not heal and ligament-deficient, unstable knees usually need graft reconstruction. Constant tension through flexion is not the reason given; the issue is blood supply. Synovial collagenase protecting the MCL is not described, and the MCL is not coated by the meniscus. The anteromedial and posterolateral bundles belong to the ACL, not the MCL. Differences in sensory innervation are not the explanation offered for healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collateral ligaments (MCL, LCL) are extra-articular and well vascularized → heal with bracing; cruciates (ACL, PCL) are intra-articular with poor blood supply → complete tears do not heal and need a graft. COMLEX: ACL deficiency → instability → meniscal tears → early arthritis, which is why active patients are offered reconstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — ACL instability lets the femoral condyles strike the posterior meniscal horns and tear them"},{"id":"sdl4_q32","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male rugby player falls onto his flexed knee with ankle plantarflexed during a tackle, developing posterolateral knee pain. Posterior sag sign is positive. Posterior drawer test shows 8mm of posterior glide at 90 degrees. MRI confirms a mid-substance PCL tear without bony avulsion and no associated ligamentous injury. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical PCL repair regardless of grade or associated injury status given the acute hemarthrosis","B":"Arthroscopic PCL reconstruction within 48 hours to prevent posterior instability and tibial sag progression","C":"Lateral collateral ligament reconstruction since LCL co-injury invariably accompanies PCL disruption patterns","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce acute hemarthrosis before initiating surgical planning","E":"Non-operative bracing and physical therapy, given an isolated mid-substance PCL tear without bony avulsion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isolated mid-substance PCL tears (without bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or severe instability >Grade 3) are managed non-operatively with bracing and PT. The literature supports good outcomes with conservative care for isolated PCL tears. Surgical indications include: bony avulsion fragment amenable to fixation, multiligamentous instability (combined with ACL/posterolateral corner), or Grade 3 tears with functional limitations not responding to PT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL isolated tears = non-operative (brace + PT). Surgical indications = bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or Grade 3 with functional limitation. PCL grade: Grade 1 (<5mm), Grade 2 (5-10mm), Grade 3 (>10mm). COMLEX: PCL injury is less common than ACL (PCL is the strongest knee ligament).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — PCL avulsion from posterior tibia is the bony avulsion pattern that converts a non-operative PCL tear into a surgical candidate; mid-substance tears without avulsion are managed conservatively"},{"id":"sdl4_q33","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old female presents after a varus stress injury to her right knee during recreational martial arts. Exam shows tenderness along the lateral knee, varus thrust gait, and laxity at 30 degrees of knee flexion with a solid endpoint at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. MRI reveals a Grade 2 LCL tear without posterolateral corner injury or other ligamentous disruption. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate management for this isolated injury?","choices":{"A":"Conservative care with a hinged knee brace and physical therapy, as indicated for an isolated Grade 2 LCL tear","B":"Immediate surgical LCL reconstruction because all LCL injuries above Grade 1 require operative stabilization","C":"Arthroscopic lateral retinacular release to reduce varus alignment forces on the lateral compartment ligaments","D":"Total knee arthroplasty consultation given the severity of lateral compartment laxity demonstrated at 30 degrees","E":"Aspiration of the lateral joint and corticosteroid injection to reduce inflammation and prevent chronic laxity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated LCL Grade 2 tears (5-10mm laxity, solid endpoint, no posterolateral corner involvement) are managed conservatively with bracing and PT. Surgical referral for LCL injury is indicated for isolated but unstable injuries, mid-portion tears, complete tears, persistent injury at 4-6 weeks, or associated posterolateral corner injuries. Laxity only at 30° with solid endpoint at 0° and no PLC co-injury = conservative management appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCL is the 2nd least common knee ligament injury (8% of knee injuries); 87% involve multiligamentous co-injuries. LCL surgical indications = isolated unstable, complete, mid-portion, persistent, or associated injuries. LCL runs from lateral femoral epicondyle to fibular head. COMLEX: Varus thrust gait is a key clinical sign of LCL/PLC insufficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — LCL injury assessment uses varus stress at 0° and 30°; laxity at 0° implies combined ACL or PCL involvement and elevates surgical threshold beyond isolated Grade 2 LCL"},{"id":"sdl4_q34","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis develops acute medial joint line pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair. He describes no significant trauma. MRI reveals a horizontal cleavage tear of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus. Which type of meniscal tear does this most likely represent, and what is the key distinguishing feature?","choices":{"A":"A traumatic bucket-handle tear requiring urgent arthroscopic repair given the mechanical symptoms on exam","B":"A lateral meniscal root tear from rotational stress with tibial external rotation during the rising motion","C":"An osteochondritis dissecans fragment causing a loose body that mimics a meniscal tear on MRI signal","D":"A degenerative tear: horizontal cleavage in an older patient with pre-existing OA and a minimal mechanism","E":"A parameniscal cyst arising from a traumatic vertical meniscal tear in a young athlete with acute pivot injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (>40) with pre-existing arthritis, typically presenting with minimal or no significant trauma. The horizontal cleavage pattern on MRI is characteristic of degenerative tears. They differ from traumatic tears (vertical, longitudinal, bucket-handle patterns in younger athletes with acute mechanisms). Risk factors include OA, male gender, and age. Treatment is typically conservative initially unless mechanical symptoms develop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tear = horizontal cleavage, older patient, OA background. Traumatic tear = vertical/bucket-handle, younger athlete, acute twist. MRI tear grades: 1 = intrasubstance, 2 = reaching surface, 3 = full-thickness. COMLEX: Degenerative tears in OA may not benefit from surgery beyond conservative care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — degenerative meniscal tears occur in the context of OA; risk factors include age >40, male gender, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis; minimal trauma is the distinguishing historical feature"},{"id":"sdl4_q35","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female basketball player twists her planted knee, hears a \"pop,\" and develops lateral joint line pain and swelling. McMurray test performed with internal rotation and a varus force applied at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and lateral pain. Which meniscus is most likely injured, and what confirms this finding?","choices":{"A":"The medial meniscus, confirmed by the positive external rotation with valgus force reproducing medial pain","B":"The lateral meniscus, confirmed by internal rotation with varus force reproducing lateral click and pain","C":"Both menisci, since internal rotation stresses both compartments equally during the McMurray maneuver","D":"The posterior cruciate ligament, producing pain from posterior capsular impingement during knee flexion","E":"The anterior cruciate ligament, with referred lateral pain from the joint capsule and synovial irritation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"McMurray test mnemonic LIME: Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus force = Medial meniscus. A click with internal rotation and varus stress implicates the lateral meniscus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common than medial (medial > lateral), but occur with rotational and pivot mechanisms. The lateral meniscus is less firmly attached to the capsule than the medial meniscus, making it more mobile but still susceptible to traumatic tears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LIME mnemonic — lateral meniscus = internal + varus; medial = external + valgus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common but often associated with ACL tears (lateral meniscus is more commonly co-injured with ACL than medial). COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 knee condition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial when ACL tears occur; the classic unhappy triad teaching (medial meniscus) reflects historical teaching rather than current evidence"},{"id":"sdl4_q36","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has medial knee pain without locking. MRI is reviewed on T2-weighted sagittal images. The posterior horn of the medial meniscus, normally a solid black triangle, contains a central bright signal that stays inside the meniscus and does not reach its superior or inferior surface. How should this finding be interpreted?","choices":{"A":"Myxoid degenerative change, a normal age-related finding that is not a tear because the bright central signal never reaches the meniscal surface","B":"A horizontal cleavage tear of the posterior horn that needs arthroscopic partial meniscectomy because the signal splits the meniscus into two leaves","C":"A peripheral vertical tear in the vascular red zone that should be repaired with sutures because it lies where the blood supply supports healing","D":"A bucket-handle tear with a displaced inner fragment that explains mechanical symptoms and needs urgent referral before the fragment becomes fixed","E":"Meniscal calcification from calcium pyrophosphate deposition, which appears bright on T2 imaging and points to pseudogout as the pain generator"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On T2-weighted sagittal MRI each meniscal horn is a solid black triangle and joint fluid is white. A tear is called only when the white signal penetrates through the meniscal surface. With age the meniscus normally accumulates central fluid, called intrasubstance myxoid degenerative change, which stays inside the tissue and does not mean the meniscus is torn. A horizontal cleavage tear is a degenerative tear, but by definition its signal reaches the surface. A peripheral vertical tear suitable for repair would also reach the surface and lie at the outer rim, not centrally. A bucket-handle tear shows a displaced fragment, not an intact triangle with central signal. Meniscal calcification is a plain X-ray finding listed in the course, and calcification is dark rather than bright on MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meniscal tear on MRI = high signal that reaches an articular surface; intrasubstance signal that does not reach the surface = myxoid degeneration (not a tear). COMLEX: In patients over 70, asymptomatic meniscal tears are common, so an MRI tear may not be the pain generator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — degenerative meniscal change accompanies knee osteoarthritis in older patients"},{"id":"sdl4_q37","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after his knee struck the dashboard in a car crash. The knee is swollen, he cannot bear weight, and the patellar region is tender. Which plain radiograph set is most appropriate for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing AP, flexed-knee PA, and Merchant views, because standing images under load reveal the joint space narrowing that marks injury","B":"A single standing flexed-knee PA notch view, because flexion rotates the posterior femoral condyle into profile where fractures are seen","C":"No radiographs, with MRI as the first study, because MRI is more sensitive for fractures and also shows ligament and meniscal injury","D":"The standard knee series of a non-weight-bearing AP, a lateral, and two oblique views, designed for injured patients who cannot stand","E":"A single weight-bearing AP view of both knees, because comparing the injured side with the opposite knee shows subtle fracture lines"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ottawa criteria are met (inability to bear weight, patellar tenderness), so radiographs are needed. The standard knee series of a non-weight-bearing AP, a lateral, and a pair of obliques was designed for emergency patients who cannot bear weight and have limited motion, and it is well suited to finding fractures. The weight-bearing AP, flexed PA (Rosenberg), and Merchant set is the arthritis series for office patients and cannot be taken in someone who cannot stand. A lone flexed PA view is likewise an arthritis view for posterior condylar wear. MRI picks up fractures and soft-tissue injury, but after significant trauma the course says an X-ray should be taken to rule out a fracture first. A standing bilateral AP is impractical in this patient and is used to judge cartilage space, not fracture lines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute knee trauma → standard (non-weight-bearing) knee series to rule out fracture; suspected arthritis → weight-bearing AP + flexed PA + Merchant. COMLEX: Ottawa knee rules decide who needs films; the type of film depends on whether the question is fracture or cartilage loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — knee fractures: mechanism, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl4_q38","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old female with severe osteoporosis falls from standing height and presents with acute knee pain and inability to bear weight. X-ray is equivocal. CT confirms a displaced lateral tibial plateau fracture with articular depression. To complete the fracture assessment and guide surgical planning, which additional imaging study is MOST appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear medicine bone scan to detect additional occult lower extremity fractures missed on CT scanning","B":"DEXA scan to quantify bone mineral density and urgently guide osteoporosis pharmacotherapy initiation","C":"MRI of the knee to evaluate concurrent meniscal, ligamentous, and chondral injury that will alter surgery","D":"Fluoroscopic stress views to demonstrate the degree of articular depression under valgus load application","E":"Ultrasound of the popliteal fossa to assess hemarthrosis volume and patellar tendon integrity dynamically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After CT characterizes the tibial plateau fracture bony architecture, MRI is the most appropriate next study to evaluate concurrent soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption (MCL, ACL, PCL), and chondral damage. These injuries frequently accompany tibial plateau fractures (on MRI, 91% have lateral meniscus pathology and 44% have medial meniscal tears, per the SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2) and directly influence surgical approach and outcomes. MRI findings change the operative plan in a high proportion of plateau fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau fractures = high association with meniscal (lateral 91%, medial 44% on MRI) and ligamentous co-injuries. CT = bony detail; MRI = soft tissue detail; both needed for complex fractures. COMLEX: Assess neurovascular status (peroneal nerve, popliteal artery) before any imaging in high-energy fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — MRI is indicated when soft tissue injuries need evaluation beyond what X-ray or CT provides; the knee arthritis and fracture workflows share this MRI decision-point principle"},{"id":"sdl4_q39","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 18-year-old male basketball player sustains a direct blow to his flexed knee with his ankle in plantarflexion during a fall. He cannot ambulate. X-ray shows a small posterior tibial avulsion fracture. Which structure is responsible for this avulsion pattern, and which clinical test corresponds to disruption of this structure?","choices":{"A":"PCL avulsion from its posterior tibial attachment; a positive posterior drawer test and posterior sag sign confirm the PCL injury","B":"ACL avulsion from the anterior tibial spine; positive Lachman test and anterior drawer confirm anterior cruciate disruption","C":"LCL avulsion from the fibular head; positive varus stress test at 30 degrees confirms lateral collateral ligament disruption","D":"Patellar tendon avulsion from the tibial tubercle; inability to actively extend the knee confirms extensor mechanism disruption","E":"Medial meniscal root avulsion from the tibial plateau; positive McMurray with external rotation confirms medial meniscal disruption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall directly onto a flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle (or MVA dashboard injury). PCL inserts on the posterior tibia; avulsion fractures occur at this insertion. The posterior sag sign (tibia sags posteriorly relative to femur with knee at 90°) and posterior drawer test (posterior tibial glide at 90° flexion) are the diagnostic exam findings. An anterior tibial spine avulsion would implicate the ACL with positive Lachman/anterior drawer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL avulsion fracture = small bony fragment posterior to the tibia on lateral X-ray. Mechanism: flexed knee + plantarflexed ankle impact (fall or dashboard). PCL = medial femoral condyle to posterior tibia (LAMP mnemonic). COMLEX: Bony avulsion PCL injuries are surgically repaired; mid-substance tears are typically managed non-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — PCL resists posterior tibial translation; the fall-on-flexed-knee with plantarflexed ankle is the prototypical PCL mechanism producing posterior sag and posterior drawer positive findings"},{"id":"sdl4_q40","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, single-leg squatting, and prolonged sitting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Video gait analysis shows dynamic valgus collapse during the stance phase of running. Which biomechanical abnormality MOST directly drives patellofemoral maltracking in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Tibial torsion causing excessive internal tibial rotation during heel strike and increasing patellofemoral compressive force","B":"Weakness of hip abductors and external rotators causing dynamic knee valgus that increases lateral patellar tracking forces","C":"Shortened Achilles tendon reducing ankle dorsiflexion and causing forefoot strike mechanics with knee flexion compensation","D":"Isolated quadriceps atrophy causing reduced extensor mechanism tension and inferior patellar tilt during loading","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint causing lateral compartment loading and increasing lateral tibial plateau stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hip abductor and external rotator weakness is the primary biomechanical driver of dynamic valgus in runners. Weakness of the gluteus medius causes the hip to adduct and internally rotate during single-leg stance, which forces the knee into valgus alignment. This valgus position increases the lateral force on the patella, causing maltracking and elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT targeting posterior hip strengthening directly addresses this mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS biomechanical chain — hip abductor weakness → dynamic valgus → lateral patellar tracking → elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT must include gluteus medius and external rotator strengthening, not just quad work. COMLEX: OMT targeting hip external rotator dysfunction can complement PFS rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — hip abductor/external rotator weakness drives dynamic valgus; physical therapy must address hip strengthening as the root cause, not only quadriceps strengthening around the knee"},{"id":"sdl4_q41","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male soccer player is diagnosed with patellofemoral syndrome after 6 weeks of anterior knee pain. His coach suggests he undergo arthroscopic debridement of the patella. Which of the following represents the LEAST appropriate treatment strategy for this patient at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Physical therapy targeting quadriceps strengthening and hamstring flexibility to improve patellar tracking mechanics","B":"Hip strengthening exercises focusing on the gluteus medius and external rotators to reduce dynamic valgus forces","C":"Immediate arthroscopic debridement of the patellar cartilage without first completing supervised physical therapy","D":"Activity modification to reduce high-impact loading while maintaining fitness with low-impact cross-training","E":"Patellar taping and stabilizing bracing techniques to improve patellofemoral alignment during rehabilitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgery for PFS is not indicated without an adequate prior trial of conservative management (physical therapy, activity modification, bracing). At 6 weeks without PT, the patient has not exhausted first-line treatments. PT targeting quadriceps and hip strengthening with biomechanical correction is the evidence-based first step. Surgical debridement is reserved for cases with documented chondromalacia refractory to extensive conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS treatment algorithm = PT first (quad + hip strengthening + biomechanics) → bracing/taping → activity modification → role of injections uncertain → surgery only for refractory documented chondromalacia. COMLEX: Conservative management must be exhausted before surgical referral in PFS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis and knee pain both reinforce conservative-first management before imaging-guided or surgical interventions"},{"id":"sdl4_q42","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female runner has patellofemoral syndrome confirmed by positive patellofemoral compression test. On single-leg squat assessment, moderate dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Which mechanism BEST explains why single-leg squatting reproducibly worsens her anterior knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendon elongation from eccentric quadriceps loading concentrates stress at the distal patellar pole","B":"Medial meniscal compression from tibial internal rotation during the deep phase of the single-leg squat","C":"Femoral nerve stretch from hip flexion causing referred anterior thigh and anterior knee discomfort","D":"Lateral retinacular tightness pulling the patella medially during varus knee alignment at the squat midpoint","E":"Dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and raises contact pressure against the lateral trochlea"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During single-leg squatting, dynamic valgus collapse — from hip abductor weakness — causes the knee to move medially over the foot, creating a valgus moment that lateralizes the pull on the patella. This increases lateral tracking of the patella against the trochlear groove, amplifying patellofemoral contact pressure. Single-leg squat is the most sensitive clinical movement for detecting dynamic valgus and reproducing PFS symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Single-leg squat = the clinical test of choice for detecting dynamic valgus in PFS. Patellofemoral compression forces are high during eccentric quadriceps contraction on a bent knee (course SDL 4 reading, Practical Office Orthopedics). COMLEX: Clinical video analysis during single-leg squat is a practical screening tool for biomechanical PFS contributors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and patellofemoral contact pressure; single-leg squat is the functional test that makes this biomechanical driver clinically visible"},{"id":"sdl4_q43","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who works as a carpet layer presents with 2 days of painful swelling directly over the patella. He has a history of recurrent podagra. He is afebrile, and there is no drainage or skin break. Ultrasound shows a distended prepatellar bursa. Which management best fits the course approach?","choices":{"A":"Surgical bursectomy within 48 hours, because crystal-laden bursal tissue will keep reforming the effusion until it is excised","B":"Empiric intravenous vancomycin without aspiration, because any acute prepatellar swelling in a laborer is septic until proven otherwise","C":"Intrabursal corticosteroid injection without aspiration, because steroid settles crystal inflammation and avoids a needle tract infection","D":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration with Gram stain and crystal analysis, then colchicine if urate crystals confirm a gouty bursitis","E":"Allopurinol started during the flare, because lowering serum urate quickly dissolves the crystals and decompresses the bursa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists urate crystals beside Gram stain in the laboratory work-up of prepatellar bursitis, uses ultrasound to guide aspiration, and treats with aspiration plus colchicine when gout is considered (antibiotics when infection is considered), along with PRICE, temporary immobilization, and a compression wrap. A man with recurrent podagra and no fever fits a gouty bursitis, and aspiration both relieves the bursa and separates crystal from infection. Bursectomy is not part of initial management. Empiric vancomycin without fluid analysis skips the step that tells infection from gout. Steroid injection without aspiration is not the course approach and would be unsafe before infection is excluded. Starting urate-lowering therapy during an acute flare does not treat the flare and can prolong it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepatellar bursitis work-up = aspirate → Gram stain + crystals; septic → anti-staph antibiotics; gout → colchicine. Gout = negatively birefringent needle-shaped urate crystals. COMLEX: Kneeling occupations (\"housemaid's knee\") drive prepatellar bursitis; aspiration is diagnostic and therapeutic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects by body system when choosing anti-inflammatory therapy"},{"id":"sdl4_q44","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old female basketball player has had three lateral patellar dislocations over 18 months. After the first episode she completed a full course of physical therapy for quadriceps and hip strengthening and has worn a patellar stabilizing brace for sports. MRI after the latest episode shows a torn medial patellofemoral ligament without an osteochondral fragment. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Repeat PRICE therapy and restart the same physical therapy program, because further strengthening will restore the torn ligament","B":"Referral for surgical stabilization, because the instability has recurred despite completed therapy and bracing for the first episode","C":"Long-term knee immobilization in extension for 12 weeks, because rigid protection lets the medial patellofemoral ligament scar in place","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection, because inflammation of the medial retinaculum drives repeated lateral patellar translation","E":"A lateral heel wedge orthotic, because shifting ground reaction forces medially reduces the lateral pull of the quadriceps on the patella"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course treats patellar subluxation and dislocation first with PRICE, physical therapy, and bracing, and reserves surgery for recurrent instability. This patient has had three dislocations despite completing that program, so surgical referral is next. Repeating the same failed program does not address recurrence, and physical therapy strengthens muscle rather than restoring a torn ligament. Prolonged immobilization in extension is not part of the course plan and causes stiffness and atrophy. Corticosteroid injection treats inflammation, not mechanical instability. A lateral heel wedge is used for medial knee loading problems and is not a treatment for patellar instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial patellofemoral ligament = primary passive restraint to lateral patellar displacement. First-time dislocation → conservative care; recurrent instability → surgery. COMLEX: Weak quadriceps and ligamentous laxity are listed mechanisms; adolescents have the highest incidence (29/100,000).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 6 — patellar dislocation presentation and reduction"},{"id":"sdl4_q45","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the evaluation of a patient with suspected recurrent patellar subluxation, which plain radiographic view is MOST appropriate for assessing patellofemoral alignment and lateral patellar tilt?","choices":{"A":"Merchant (sunrise) view, imaging the patellofemoral joint tangentially to show patellar tilt and alignment","B":"Weight-bearing AP view, which demonstrates tibiofemoral joint space and is the gold standard for OA staging","C":"Oblique lateral view, which demonstrates the arcuate sign indicating posterolateral corner injury pattern","D":"Standard lateral view, which shows the Pellegrini-Stieda lesion at the medial femoral condyle attachment","E":"Tunnel view (notch view), which is used to evaluate the intercondylar notch for osteochondritis dissecans"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Merchant view (sunrise or subpatellar view) is the dedicated radiographic view for patellofemoral assessment. The X-ray beam is directed inferosuperiorly along the axis of the femur, providing a tangential view of the patellofemoral joint. It demonstrates patellar tilt, trochlear morphology, and patellar subluxation. The course reading (Practical Office Orthopedics) uses this view to detect patellofemoral (bone-on-bone) arthritis. The AP and lateral views do not adequately assess the patellofemoral compartment in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Merchant view = sunrise view = subpatellar view. Used for: patellar subluxation, PFS assessment, patellofemoral OA (bone-on-bone on Merchant = patellofemoral arthritis). COMLEX: X-ray alignment views guide surgical planning for tibial tubercle osteotomy in recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — Merchant view is part of the knee arthritis imaging protocol for assessing the patellofemoral compartment; the knee arthritis workflow includes standard AP, lateral, and Merchant views"},{"id":"sdl4_q46","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing knee osteoarthritis has a large posterior knee mass confirmed on ultrasound as a popliteal cyst. Over the past month she has developed numbness of the sole of the foot and difficulty rising onto her toes. Dorsiflexion and sensation over the dorsum of the foot are normal. Which nerve is the cyst most likely compressing?","choices":{"A":"The common fibular nerve, which wraps the fibular neck and supplies foot dorsiflexion, eversion, and sensation over the dorsum of the foot","B":"The femoral nerve, which runs through the posterior knee to supply the quadriceps and sensation over the anteromedial leg and medial foot","C":"The saphenous nerve, which passes behind the medial femoral condyle and supplies the plantar skin and the long toe flexors of the foot","D":"The tibial nerve, which runs through the popliteal fossa to supply the plantarflexors, the long toe flexors, and the skin of the sole","E":"The obturator nerve, which descends behind the knee with the popliteal artery to supply the calf muscles and the skin of the heel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A popliteal (Baker) cyst sits in the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa in the popliteal fossa. First Aid lists a Baker cyst as a proximal cause of tibial nerve injury: the tibial nerve supplies the triceps surae and foot flexors and the skin of the sole, so the patient cannot stand on her toes and has plantar numbness. The common fibular nerve is lateral at the fibular neck and would cause foot drop and dorsal foot numbness, which she does not have. The femoral nerve is an anterior thigh nerve that does not run through the popliteal fossa. The saphenous nerve is sensory to the medial leg and has no plantar or motor role. The obturator nerve supplies the thigh adductors and does not descend behind the knee.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial nerve (L4-S3): plantarflexion, inversion, toe flexion, sole sensation; proximal lesion from knee trauma or Baker cyst; distal lesion = tarsal tunnel. \"Tibial Inverts and Plantarflexes; can't stand on TIPtoes.\" COMLEX: Baker cysts can also rupture and mimic DVT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — lower extremity nerve entrapments of the leg and foot"},{"id":"sdl4_q47","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is incidentally found to have a 2cm popliteal cyst on knee ultrasound during evaluation of mild knee stiffness after a minor fall. He has no constitutional symptoms, no fever, and no prior knee pathology. Ultrasound shows no communication with the synovial space. Which is the MOST appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical excision because pediatric popliteal cysts always carry risk of malignant transformation","B":"Aspiration combined with corticosteroid injection to permanently collapse the cyst in young children","C":"Watchful waiting or aspiration, since pediatric cysts rarely communicate and often resolve on their own","D":"MRI with gadolinium contrast to rule out a vascular malformation or synovial sarcoma in this age group","E":"Total knee arthroscopy to assess the posterior compartment and cauterize the cyst at its point of origin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pediatric popliteal cysts (peak incidence ages 4-7) have different pathoanatomy from adult cysts — they typically do NOT communicate with the synovial space, arise from the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa independently, and frequently resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or simple aspiration. They are not associated with intra-articular pathology the way adult cysts are, and there is no indication for aggressive surgical or injection treatment in the absence of concerning features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric (4-7) vs. adult (35-70) Baker's cysts have different pathoanatomy and treatment. Pediatric = non-communicating, benign, often resolve spontaneously. Adult = communicating, treat underlying joint pathology. COMLEX: Watchful waiting is appropriate for asymptomatic pediatric popliteal cysts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — pediatric popliteal cysts arise from different pathoanatomy than adult cysts; they typically lack synovial communication and resolve spontaneously, in contrast to adult cysts driven by intra-articular disease"},{"id":"sdl4_q48","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis develops acute calf pain and swelling mimicking a deep vein thrombosis. Doppler ultrasound shows no venous thrombus but reveals a ruptured popliteal cyst with free fluid tracking into the posterior calf. Which term BEST describes this complication and its underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"True DVT, because popliteal cyst compression of the popliteal vein causes secondary venous thrombosis formation","B":"Gastrocnemius muscle avulsion, caused by sudden knee hyperextension during the rupture event itself","C":"Compartment syndrome, caused by arterial embolization from synovial shedding producing distal calf ischemia","D":"Septic thrombophlebitis, caused by bacterial seeding from the ruptured cyst resulting in polymicrobial cellulitis","E":"Pseudothrombophlebitis, in which a ruptured cyst releases synovial fluid into the calf muscles and mimics DVT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rupture of a popliteal cyst causes \"pseudothrombophlebitis\" — synovial fluid tracks inferiorly into the calf, producing acute pain, swelling, and erythema that mimics DVT. Doppler ultrasound demonstrates the cyst rupture and absence of venous thrombus, distinguishing it from true DVT. This is a recognized complication of Baker's cysts in patients with RA or OA who have large fluid collections. Management involves treating the underlying joint disease rather than anticoagulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ruptured Baker's cyst = pseudothrombophlebitis. Ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice — shows free fluid tracking into calf with no venous thrombus. D-dimer may be falsely elevated. COMLEX: RA and OA are the most common underlying conditions driving large popliteal cysts that can rupture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — popliteal cyst rupture causes pseudothrombophlebitis; ultrasound differentiates this from true DVT and guides the decision against anticoagulation in this setting"},{"id":"sdl4_q49","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with symptomatic knee osteoarthritis had a myocardial infarction last year and has heart failure. He does structured land-based exercise and has used a topical NSAID for 2 months with inadequate relief. Using the OARSI comorbidity columns, which pharmacologic option is listed as a conditional Level 1B choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection of the knee","B":"Oral celecoxib at the lowest effective daily dose","C":"Oral naproxen combined with a proton pump inhibitor","D":"Oral ibuprofen taken with food for short pain flares","E":"Oral duloxetine titrated to a daily maintenance dose"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OARSI sorts knee OA recommendations by comorbidity profile. Arthritis education and structured land-based exercise are core for everyone, and topical NSAIDs are Level 1A for patients with no comorbidity, GI comorbidity, or cardiovascular comorbidity. In the cardiovascular column, the conditional Level 1B pharmacologic options are intra-articular corticosteroid (IACS) and intra-articular hyaluronic acid (IAHA); oral NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors are not listed for this profile. Celecoxib appears in the no-comorbidity, GI, and widespread pain columns, but not in the cardiovascular column. Naproxen with a PPI is a choice for patients without comorbidity, and the PPI protects the stomach, not the heart. Ibuprofen is a non-selective oral NSAID, also absent from the cardiovascular column. Duloxetine is listed only for the widespread pain or depression profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oral NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors raise cardiovascular risk (COX-2 → less prostacyclin, more thromboxane). OARSI cardiovascular profile: topical NSAIDs (1A), IACS or IAHA (1B). COMLEX: Match the OA drug to the comorbidity column before prescribing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 5 — COX-2 selective inhibitor cardiovascular adverse effects"},{"id":"sdl4_q50","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with knee osteoarthritis and a history of peptic ulcer disease controlled on PPI therapy requires pharmacologic treatment for moderate pain. He also has stable hypertension. According to OARSI guidelines, which pharmacologic option is MOST appropriate as the preferred initial choice for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Non-selective systemic NSAIDs as monotherapy given the anti-inflammatory benefit and cost-effectiveness","B":"COX-2 selective inhibitors alone without gastroprotection, given their lower GI ulcer risk than non-selective NSAIDs","C":"Oral opioid analgesics as first-line therapy given the contraindication to both selective and non-selective NSAIDs","D":"Topical NSAIDs, the preferred initial agent for patients with GI comorbidities such as peptic ulcer disease","E":"Aspirin monotherapy given its combined anti-inflammatory and cardiovascular protective dual mechanism of action"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OARSI guidelines specifically recommend topical NSAIDs as the preferred pharmacologic option for patients with GI comorbidities (peptic ulcer disease). Topical NSAIDs provide local anti-inflammatory effect with minimal systemic absorption, significantly reducing GI risk compared to oral NSAIDs. Systemic NSAIDs (selective or non-selective) carry GI bleeding risk even with PPI use. Opioids are not first-line for OA per OARSI. Aspirin is not recommended as primary OA pharmacotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI GI comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first. CV comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first (avoid systemic non-selective). Topical NSAIDs: diclofenac gel/patch has strong evidence for knee OA. COMLEX: Avoid systemic NSAIDs in patients with peptic ulcer disease even with PPI coverage when topical options are available.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — OARSI pharmacologic stratification by comorbidity: GI comorbidity = topical NSAIDs preferred; this reflects the risk-benefit calculation of minimizing systemic drug exposure in high-risk patients"},{"id":"sdl4_q51","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician wants to document baseline physical function in a 64-year-old woman newly diagnosed with knee osteoarthritis, so that response to an exercise program can be tracked. Which assessment is one of the performance-based tests OARSI recommends?","choices":{"A":"Kellgren-Lawrence grading of a weight-bearing AP radiograph","B":"A Lachman test repeated at each visit to track stability","C":"A 30-second chair stand test counting full sit-to-stands","D":"Serial knee joint aspiration to measure synovial fluid volume","E":"Goniometry of passive knee flexion with the patient supine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"OARSI recommends a set of performance-based functional tests for hip and knee osteoarthritis and publishes demonstration videos: the 30-second chair stand test, the 40-meter fast-paced walk test, a stair climb test, the timed up and go test, and the 6-minute walk test. The chair stand test measures what the patient can do and can be repeated to track response to exercise. Kellgren-Lawrence grading is a radiographic structure score, not a functional test. The Lachman test assesses the ACL, not osteoarthritis function. Serial aspiration is invasive and is not a functional measure. Passive goniometry measures range of motion, not functional performance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA is a clinical diagnosis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, morning stiffness ≤30 minutes); OARSI functional tests: 30-second chair stand, 40-m fast walk, stair climb, timed up and go, 6-minute walk. COMLEX: Objective function testing guides exercise prescription and documents improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — goals and measurable outcomes in a physical therapy prescription"},{"id":"sdl4_q52","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has 8 weeks of medial joint line pain. Medial joint line tenderness, medial pain with deep flexion, and a medial McMurray test are all positive. He asks to go straight to arthroscopy for a suspected meniscal tear. According to the course reading, what should be obtained before arthroscopy is considered?","choices":{"A":"Serum ESR and CRP, because meniscal pain in men over 50 is usually an early sign of inflammatory arthritis rather than a tear","B":"An MRI with gadolinium, because contrast is needed to show the tear before any arthroscopy and must precede plain films","C":"A diagnostic aspiration, because blood in the joint fluid confirms a peripheral tear in the vascular zone that can be repaired","D":"A weight-bearing arthritis series of X-rays, because medial bone-on-bone arthritis can produce all three positive meniscal signs","E":"A bone scan, because increased uptake in the medial tibial plateau separates a meniscal tear from a stress fracture of the tibia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The reading recommends the weight-bearing arthritis X-ray series in any patient over 40 with suspected meniscal tear. Medial bone-on-bone arthritis gives medial joint line pain, tenderness, pain with deep flexion, and a positive McMurray test, so history and exam alone can mislabel arthritis as a meniscal tear. Arthroscopy has little utility in arthritis and gives poor results next to exposed bone, so arthritis must be ruled out first. ESR and CRP do not separate a tear from osteoarthritis. MRI is not required before arthroscopy in a classic case and does not need gadolinium for meniscal tears. Aspiration is not part of this work-up. A bone scan is used for stress fractures and is not the step described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-rays (gold standard); MRI only for complex cases or cartilage questions. COMLEX: Rule out arthritis in at-risk patients (over 40) before meniscal surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — degenerative versus traumatic meniscal tears"},{"id":"sdl4_q53","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic knee pain, swelling, and stiffness. The physician notes that these complaints are common to many knee conditions. When she lies supine and squeezes her legs together, her ankles touch well before her knees. Which interpretation of this physical finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A severe varus deformity, the exam finding most specific for knee arthritis, from medial compartment cartilage wear","B":"A severe valgus deformity from lateral compartment wear, which brings the knees together before the ankles","C":"A normal finding, since mild angular deformities are common and do not point to cartilage loss in any compartment","D":"Medial collateral ligament laxity, which lets the tibia drift away from the midline when the legs are adducted","E":"Patellofemoral maltracking, which rotates the tibia outward and separates the knees during adduction of the hips"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course reading notes that swelling, stiffness, tenderness, and warmth are not specific for arthritis; the one exam finding that is very specific for knee arthritis is a severe angular deformity. In the supine squeeze test, normal knees and ankles touch at about the same time. If the ankles touch first, the knees are bowed (varus), which comes from medial compartment wear; if the knees touch first, the deformity is valgus from lateral wear. Valgus is the opposite pattern. Mild deformities can be normal, but a clear gap at the knees with the ankles touching is a severe varus pattern. MCL laxity would open the medial joint under valgus stress, not create a fixed bowleg. Patellofemoral maltracking does not cause a coronal-plane bowleg deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial compartment OA → varus (bowleg); lateral compartment OA → valgus (knock-knee). Severe angular deformity is the most specific exam sign of knee arthritis. COMLEX: Supine leg-squeeze test is a quick office screen before weight-bearing X-rays.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee osteoarthritis clinical criteria and OARSI management"},{"id":"sdl4_q54","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician completing the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow reaches the imaging step. The patient is a 65-year-old male with classic clinical OA features and pain that is moderate in severity. According to the knee arthritis workflow, which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for initial evaluation of knee arthritis?","choices":{"A":"MRI, because it provides the most sensitive evaluation of articular cartilage and synovial tissue integrity","B":"Ultrasound, because it provides real-time dynamic assessment of effusion and allows immediate guided aspiration","C":"Weight-bearing X-ray, which shows joint space narrowing under physiological load and is the OA gold standard","D":"CT arthrogram, because it provides superior cartilage mapping and bony architectural detail for OA staging","E":"Radionuclide bone scan, because it identifies early periarticular metabolic changes preceding X-ray abnormalities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for initial knee OA imaging. It is essential that imaging be performed weight-bearing — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing by allowing the femur and tibia to separate. Standard knee OA X-ray series includes AP weight-bearing (for tibiofemoral joint space), lateral, and Merchant view (for patellofemoral compartment). MRI is not the gold standard for OA; it is reserved for complex presentations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weight-bearing X-ray = OA gold standard. Always specify \"weight-bearing\" when ordering — non-weight-bearing AP is inadequate for OA assessment. Merchant view adds patellofemoral compartment evaluation. COMLEX: Appropriate imaging selection is a core clinical reasoning skill tested in COMLEX Level 1 and Level 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging; it is the definitive imaging step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow before MRI or other advanced imaging is considered"}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"Hip Pain: Diagnostic Workflow & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl5_q01","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating a patient with hip pain applies the 5-step diagnostic workflow. After confirming no trauma in step 1, what does step 2 direct the physician to assess?","choices":{"A":"Whether osteoarthritis or another inflammatory arthritis is the most likely cause of the pain","B":"Whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is responsible","C":"Whether the pain is localized and, if so, identifying the specific anatomic hip pain generator","D":"Whether a dangerous condition such as infection, malignancy, or fracture is the underlying cause","E":"Whether the pain is related to overuse activities such as running or repetitive hip flexion exercises"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hip pain diagnostic workflow mirrors the knee pain framework: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous medical condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized — what is the pain generator? Step 2 always screens for dangerous conditions — septic hip, fracture, psoas abscess, or malignancy — before advancing to the arthritis or nerve steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain diagnostic workflow = same 5-step structure as knee pain. Critical point: hip X-rays are almost always indicated to augment H&P for hip pain. COMLEX: In children presenting with knee pain, always include hip X-ray — hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip) commonly refers to the knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q02","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old male presents with medial knee pain and a slight limp but no knee trauma. The physician completes the knee pain diagnostic workflow and finds no knee pathology. Which additional imaging step is MOST critical before concluding the evaluation?","choices":{"A":"Ordering knee MRI to rule out occult growth plate injury in the medial femoral condyle region","B":"Ordering a bone scan to rule out malignancy at the distal femoral epiphysis and proximal tibia","C":"Obtaining hip X-rays, since hip pathology in children often refers pain to the thigh and knee","D":"Performing nerve conduction studies to evaluate for saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee","E":"Ordering bilateral knee X-rays with comparison views to assess for physeal growth plate abnormality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hip pain workflow explicitly states that hip X-rays should almost always be used to augment history and physical, including in children presenting with knee pain. Hip pathology (Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, SCFE, septic hip) frequently manifests as ipsilateral thigh or knee pain rather than hip pain in children, making this a critical clinical pearl. Missing hip pathology by focusing only on the knee is a recognized diagnostic error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic boards trap — child with knee pain without knee pathology = always X-ray the hip. SCFE and LCP both present with knee or thigh pain. SCFE: obese adolescent, \"ice cream scoop\" X-ray appearance. LCP: age 4-9, insidious limp. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a CP8 named musculoskeletal presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q03","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which of the following BEST distinguishes the approach to hip pain from that of knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Hip X-rays almost always augment the history and physical, even when the pain does not localize to the hip","B":"The hip pain workflow uses only 3 steps, while the knee pain workflow requires a 5-step sequential process","C":"Nerve pathology is assessed before dangerous conditions in the hip workflow, unlike the knee workflow order","D":"The hip workflow begins with imaging before the history because X-rays are always required for hip pain","E":"Arthritis is evaluated in the final step of the hip workflow, whereas it is assessed in step 3 for knee pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hip pain workflow uses the same 5-step sequential framework as the knee pain workflow. The key distinguishing feature is that hip X-rays are almost always used to augment the history and physical in hip evaluations — a stronger imaging recommendation than for knee pain. This is because hip pathology (fracture, OA, AVN, SCFE, septic joint) frequently requires radiographic confirmation even when the history and exam are incomplete or atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both knee and hip workflows use the same 5-step structure. Hip-specific addition: X-ray is almost always ordered as a routine augment — not just when Ottawa-like criteria are met. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam complements the hip diagnostic workflow by identifying somatic dysfunction patterns consistent with specific hip pathologies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q04","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old male presents with a deep aching pain around the hip socket, worsening with both active and passive motion, and progressive restriction of internal rotation followed by external rotation. He has no radicular pain. Which of the following risk factors is MOST associated with hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Male gender over age 40, NSAID use, and sedentary lifestyle are the primary risk factors for hip OA","B":"Autoimmune disease, positive rheumatoid factor, and prolonged corticosteroid use are the key risk factors","C":"Prior hip trauma, high-impact activity, obesity, and a family history of arthritis are all risk factors","D":"Tobacco use, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia are the most strongly associated risk factors for hip OA","E":"History of gout, hyperuricemia, and metabolic syndrome are the dominant risk factors for hip OA development"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hip OA risk factors are multifactorial and include: prior trauma or fracture or dislocation, history of high-impact activities, obesity, family history, and metabolic conditions. The clinical presentation features a deep aching pain around the hip socket, stiffness, and restricted range of motion — internal rotation is lost first, followed by external rotation in end-stage disease. Both active and passive motion reproduce pain, distinguishing hip OA from extra-articular causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA = deep ache around socket; IR lost first (then ER). Active and passive motion both painful = intra-articular source. Hip OA can refer to thigh or knee without hip pain. COMLEX: Hip OA is a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition; OMT can address somatic dysfunction of the hip as an adjunct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q05","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old female with hip osteoarthritis presents with predominantly thigh and knee pain with no groin complaint. Knee examination is normal. Which of the following BEST explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Hip pathology frequently refers pain to the thigh and knee without groin pain, so the hip must be evaluated","B":"The knee pain is from a separate degenerative meniscal tear that has developed simultaneously with the hip OA","C":"The thigh pain represents L2-L3 radiculopathy, which should be investigated with lumbar MRI before hip imaging","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment is the most likely cause of medial thigh pain in patients with hip osteoarthritis","E":"The patient has patellofemoral syndrome bilaterally causing knee pain secondary to the altered hip mechanics"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hip OA can present with thigh and/or knee pain without actual hip or groin pain — a clinically important pitfall. The hip joint refers pain through the obturator nerve and capsular innervation to the medial thigh and knee, which can mislead clinicians into focusing on the knee rather than the hip. This is why the hip diagnostic workflow recommends X-raying the hip in children with knee pain, and why a normal knee exam should prompt hip evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain pattern = medial thigh and knee. Clinical pearl: knee pain with normal knee exam = X-ray the hip. COMLEX: Hip OA is a common cause of knee pain misdiagnosis; osteopathic structural exam should include hip mobility assessment in all patients with knee complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q06","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with hip OA is found to have progressive loss of range of motion. Which pattern of motion restriction is MOST characteristic of advanced hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"External rotation is the first motion restricted, followed by internal rotation loss as a late finding in OA","B":"Internal rotation is lost first with progression, and external rotation restriction follows in end stage","C":"Flexion is the first and predominant motion loss, while rotation is preserved until the very late stages","D":"Abduction is preferentially restricted early and is always the first clinical sign of hip OA progression","E":"Extension loss occurs earliest and is the most reliable indicator of early hip OA on clinical examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hip OA, internal rotation (IR) is characteristically the first motion restricted as the joint capsule and surrounding tissues contract. As disease progresses, external rotation (ER) also becomes restricted, particularly in end-stage OA. Both active and passive hip motion provoke pain in intra-articular pathology — this distinguishes hip OA from extra-articular causes such as greater trochanteric tendinopathy, where passive motion is usually preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA motion loss pattern: IR first → then ER (end stage). Active AND passive motion painful = intra-articular. Passive motion preserved = extra-articular. COMLEX: Restricted hip IR on passive exam is a somatic dysfunction finding amenable to OMT (muscle energy, articulatory technique).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q07","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female marathon runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter, worsening with single-leg squat and associated with dynamic knee valgus. She was referred with a diagnosis of \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" What does current evidence suggest is the ACTUAL underlying pathology in most cases?","choices":{"A":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy is the primary pathology in most cases of so-called greater trochanteric bursitis","B":"Iliotibial band friction syndrome is the primary cause of lateral hip pain in runners, not tendinopathy","C":"Hip labral tear is the underlying cause of lateral hip pain in runners who present with dynamic valgus","D":"Snapping hip syndrome from iliopsoas impingement is the correct diagnosis in most cases of lateral hip pain","E":"True bursitis of the trochanteric bursa from direct pressure is the diagnosis in the majority of runners"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Current evidence demonstrates that \"greater trochanteric bursitis\" is a misnomer — the primary pathology is gluteus medius tendinopathy in the vast majority of cases. The presentation is lateral greater trochanter pain worsening with single-leg squat, running, and activities requiring repetitive hip flexion/extension. Dynamic knee valgus (from gluteus medius weakness) is both a biomechanical driver and a clinical finding. Treatment targets the tendinopathy and underlying biomechanics, not bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Greater trochanteric bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy. Lateral GT pain + single-leg squat pain + dynamic valgus = tendinopathy pattern. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness causes Trendelenburg gait and is a key somatic dysfunction pattern in the hip-pelvis region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q08","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female runner with confirmed gluteus medius tendinopathy fails conservative treatment with rest and physical therapy. Which of the following represents a RECOGNIZED escalation treatment option for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Surgical bursectomy is always the next step after physical therapy fails in any form of hip tendinopathy","B":"Oral opioid analgesics combined with long-leg immobilization is the recommended escalation for tendinopathy","C":"Total hip arthroplasty is the only definitive treatment for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy at the hip","D":"Corticosteroid injection, tenotomy, regenerative injection (PRP), or nitroglycerin patches are all options","E":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the hip joint capsule is the preferred escalation for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After first-line biomechanical adjustment (PT, gait retraining, strengthening), refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy can be treated with: corticosteroid injection (for acute flare), tenotomy (for recalcitrant cases), regenerative injection (PRP), or nitroglycerin patches (shown to improve tendinopathy healing). These options address the tendinopathic tissue directly. Surgical bursectomy is not indicated when the pathology is tendinopathic rather than true bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinopathy treatment ladder = biomechanics → CSI → regeneratives/NTG patches → tenotomy. NTG patches (nitroglycerin) have evidence for tendinopathy healing via nitric oxide signaling. COMLEX: OMT addressing hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction can reduce the biomechanical load driving tendinopathy recurrence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q09","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old female dancer describes an audible \"snapping\" sensation at the hip with certain movements. She has no pain with the snapping at rest but mild discomfort during repetitive activity. Which is the MOST common anatomic source of snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Avulsion of the gluteus medius from the greater trochanter producing an audible click during hip rotation","B":"Femoral head subluxation from a torn hip labrum producing a reproducible snap during hip flexion maneuvers","C":"Greater trochanteric bursitis producing a palpable fluid wave as the bursa shifts over the trochanteric ridge","D":"Osteochondral loose body impinging within the joint space producing a mechanical snap during hip extension","E":"The iliotibial band over the trochanter (external) or iliopsoas over the iliopectineal eminence (internal)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans) has two main anatomic sources: (1) external snapping — the iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter with hip flexion/extension; (2) internal snapping — the iliopsoas tendon snapping over the iliopectineal eminence with hip flexion. A third form involves intra-articular loose bodies, but this is less common. External snapping is the most common and is often painless; treatment focuses on IT band and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Snapping hip = coxa saltans. External = IT band over greater trochanter. Internal = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence. Most snapping is benign and painless. COMLEX: Snapping hip from IT band tightness responds to hip-pelvis OMT addressing somatic dysfunction of the iliotibial band insertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q10","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old female falls from standing height and presents with right groin pain, inability to bear weight, and the leg appearing shortened and internally rotated. Which hip fracture pattern is MOST consistent with this presentation, and what blood supply concern is associated with it?","choices":{"A":"Femoral neck fracture, which carries the highest AVN risk from disruption of the medial circumflex femoral artery","B":"Intertrochanteric fracture, which carries the highest AVN risk due to complete disruption of the femoral neck vasculature","C":"Subtrochanteric fracture, which is most commonly seen in elderly women and carries a low AVN risk overall","D":"Acetabular fracture, which disrupts the joint capsule but rarely affects blood supply to the femoral head","E":"Femoral shaft fracture, which presents with internal rotation and shortening but carries minimal AVN risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Femoral neck fractures carry the highest risk of AVN because the blood supply to the femoral head — primarily via the medial circumflex femoral artery — traverses the femoral neck and is disrupted by the fracture. Intertrochanteric fractures have better blood flow (below the neck) but greater displacement risk. The course slides list groin pain, decreased ROM, an internally rotated leg, and possible shortening as the presentation of hip fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Femoral neck = poor blood supply → high AVN risk. Medial circumflex femoral artery = primary blood supply to femoral head. 1/3 of hip fractures progress to THA. FA: hip fractures can lead to AVN and nonunion (femoral neck). COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly female = high COMLEX yield with osteoporosis management component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q11","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old male motorcyclist presents after a high-speed MVC with the right hip flexed and internally rotated, with the femoral head palpable posteriorly. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST common, and which nerve is MOST at risk?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation is most common; the femoral nerve is most at risk causing quadriceps weakness and knee extension deficit","B":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the obturator nerve is most at risk causing medial thigh adductor weakness","C":"Central fracture-dislocation is most common; the pudendal nerve is most at risk from acetabular fragment displacement","D":"Superior hip dislocation is most common; the superior gluteal nerve is most at risk causing abductor weakness and Trendelenburg gait","E":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the sciatic nerve is most at risk and can cause foot drop and posterior thigh sensory loss if injured"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation is the most common type (85% of hip dislocations per the course reading, SDL 25 CDT Orthopedics; about 90% per Bootcamp), typically caused by high-energy trauma (MVA dashboard mechanism). The femoral head dislocates posteriorly and superiorly, putting the sciatic nerve at direct risk of injury. Sciatic nerve injury causes foot drop (peroneal division) and hamstring weakness, along with posterior thigh and below-knee sensory loss. Urgent reduction is essential to reduce sciatic nerve and femoral head ischemia risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation — leg flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop = peroneal division). FA: inferior gluteal nerve injury = posterior hip dislocation = difficulty rising from seated/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation = CP8 named joint dislocation; requires urgent reduction, preferably within the first few hours, to minimize AVN risk (SDL 25 reading).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q12","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Regarding hip fracture epidemiology, which of the following correctly identifies the fracture type MOST common in women aged 65 and older, and the fracture subtype with a BIMODAL age distribution?","choices":{"A":"Femoral shaft fractures are most common in women over 65; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","B":"Acetabular fractures are most common in elderly women; intertrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution","C":"Femoral neck fractures are most common in elderly women; acetabular fractures have a bimodal distribution","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures are most common in elderly women; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","E":"Intertrochanteric and femoral neck are most common in elderly women; subtrochanteric fractures are bimodal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In women aged 65 and older, intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common hip fracture types. Femoral neck fractures occur 3x more commonly in women than men. Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution — a younger peak at ages 20-40 (high-energy trauma) and an older peak above age 60 (low-energy osteoporotic fractures). Overall hip fracture incidence is 100-315 per 100,000 population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture types by age — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck. Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. Risk factors: age, female, osteoporosis, low BMI <22, endocrine disease. COMLEX: Hip fracture prevention via osteoporosis screening and fall prevention is a key preventive medicine objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q13","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male with SLE who has been on long-term corticosteroids presents with left groin pain at rest and with weight bearing. X-ray is normal. MRI shows characteristic signal changes in the femoral head. Which of the following BEST describes the ARCO stage classification for this patient?","choices":{"A":"ARCO Stage II — subchondral fracture with crescent sign visible on plain X-ray indicating early collapse","B":"ARCO Stage III — femoral head depression greater than 2mm on X-ray representing established collapse","C":"ARCO Stage IV — joint space narrowing and degenerative acetabular changes consistent with end-stage OA","D":"ARCO Stage I — normal radiograph with abnormal MRI findings, a pre-collapse stage visible only on MRI","E":"ARCO Stage II — sclerosis and osteolysis visible on X-ray without crescent sign or femoral head collapse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ARCO staging for AVN: Stage I = normal X-ray with abnormal MRI (bone marrow edema, early ischemic signal); Stage II = no crescent sign, X-ray shows sclerosis/osteolysis/osteoporosis; Stage III = subchondral fracture with crescent sign and femoral head flattening (IIIA ≤2mm, IIIB >2mm depression); Stage IV = OA changes with joint space narrowing and acetabular degeneration. This patient has normal X-ray + abnormal MRI = Stage I.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI sensitivity for AVN = 100% (Stage I detectable on MRI when X-ray is normal). ARCO Stage I = pre-collapse = opportunity for joint-preserving treatment. FA: AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS. Most common site = femoral head (watershed from medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: AVN is a CP8 named hip condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q14","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly lists the risk factors associated with avascular necrosis of the femoral head using the First Aid mnemonic?","choices":{"A":"Calcium deficiency, Anemia, Smoking, Tuberculosis, Statin use, Bisphosphonate use, Lupus, Exercise excess","B":"Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, the Bends, Legg-Calvé-Perthes, Gaucher, and SCFE (CASTS Bend LEGS)","C":"Crohn disease, ACE inhibitors, Sepsis, Thrombus, Spondylosis, Bariatric surgery, Long-term NSAID use","D":"Corticosteroids, Aspirin, Septic arthritis, Thyroid disease, Systemic sclerosis, Bisphosphonate use, Lactose intolerance, Edema","E":"Calcium oxalate, Alcohol, Smoking, Thrombocytopenia, Septicemia, Beta-blocker use, Liver cirrhosis, Embolism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The First Aid mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS captures the key AVN associations: glucoCorticoids (most common drug-related cause), chronic Alcohol overuse, Sickle cell disease, Trauma, SLE, \"the Bends\" (caisson/decompression disease), Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (idiopathic childhood AVN), Gaucher disease, and SCFE (slipped capital femoral epiphysis). Corticosteroid use and alcohol are the two most common reversible risk factors in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CASTS Bend LEGS = canonical AVN mnemonic (First Aid). Corticosteroids + alcohol = most common drug-related causes. Sickle cell = AVN in multiple sites. Medial circumflex femoral artery = blood supply at risk. COMLEX: AVN occurs in all joints but femoral head is most clinically tested for boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q15","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with bilateral groin pain that is worse at night and at rest. X-ray shows flattening of the right femoral head and subchondral fracture with a crescent sign. Left femoral head shows sclerosis without collapse. Which imaging modality has 100% sensitivity for detecting early AVN before X-ray changes appear?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the pelvis, which is superior to MRI for detecting early bone marrow ischemia in AVN staging","B":"Bone scan with technetium, which is the gold standard imaging for early AVN detection before collapse occurs","C":"Ultrasound of the hip joint, which shows early joint effusion and subchondral signal change in pre-collapse AVN","D":"Plain X-ray with comparison views, which is reliable for detecting Stage I and Stage II AVN with high sensitivity","E":"MRI, which has near 100% sensitivity for detecting femoral head AVN even at Stage I when plain X-rays are completely normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI has near 100% sensitivity for detecting AVN even in Stage I, when X-rays are completely normal. The characteristic finding is a \"double line sign\" on T2-weighted images — a low-signal outer band and high-signal inner band at the necrotic zone interface. CT is better than X-ray but inferior to MRI. Plain X-ray only becomes positive at Stage II or later. Early detection is critical because joint-preserving interventions are most effective before collapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for early AVN (Stage I — normal X-ray). X-ray: crescent sign = subchondral fracture = Stage III. ARCO Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. FA: AVN of femoral head = watershed area (medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: Early detection enables core decompression before Stage III collapse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q16","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male with Crohn disease presents with right hip pain, fever, and pain that worsens with passive hip extension. Lab shows WBC 15,000 and ESR 88. CT reveals a focal hypodense lesion within the iliopsoas muscle plane. Which organism is MOST commonly implicated in psoas abscess?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, particularly in primary (hematogenous) cases","B":"Escherichia coli is the most common organism since psoas abscess is predominantly a GI-origin infection","C":"Candida albicans is the most common organism in immunocompromised patients with psoas abscess overall","D":"Streptococcus pyogenes is the most common organism in psoas abscess from Crohn disease-related spread","E":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most common organism causing psoas abscess in all geographic settings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, especially in primary (hematogenous) cases. Secondary psoas abscess from Crohn disease may also involve S. aureus or mixed flora (including gram-negative organisms from the GI tract). Tuberculosis causes psoas abscess in endemic regions (Pott disease spreading from vertebral osteomyelitis), but is not the global leading cause. Key diagnostic features: psoas sign — pain with passive hip extension — is a classic finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess = infection in iliopsoas compartment; S. aureus most common. FA: also associated with vertebral osteomyelitis (Pott disease), Crohn disease, pyelonephritis. Psoas sign = hip extension exacerbates lower abdominal/hip pain. CT = 90% sensitive (lower if <6 days of symptoms). TX: drainage + IV antibiotics. COMLEX: Psoas abscess is a CP8 dangerous hip condition that mimics hip OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q17","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with fever of 38.9°C, refusal to bear weight on the left leg, ESR of 52 mm/h, and WBC of 13,500. CRP is 2.8 mg/dL. Using the modified Kocher criteria, what is the estimated probability of septic hip arthritis?","choices":{"A":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 40% probability, which requires watchful waiting with repeat labs","B":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 3% probability, which is managed with outpatient antibiotics alone","C":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 93% probability, requiring urgent surgical lavage consultation","D":"A score of 4 criteria, plus an elevated CRP >2.0 mg/dL, gives approximately 99.6% probability of septic hip","E":"A score of 3 criteria gives approximately 93% probability since CRP is not included in the Kocher criteria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modified Kocher criteria include: (1) non-weight bearing, (2) ESR >40 mm/h, (3) temperature >101.3°F (38.5°C), (4) WBC >12,000, plus CRP >2.0 mg/dL as an add-on criterion. Risk stratification: 0 criteria = <0.2%, 1 = 3%, 2 = 40%, 3 = 93%, 4 = 99.6%. This patient meets all four classic criteria (non-weight bearing, fever 38.9°C, ESR 52, WBC 13,500) and also has CRP 2.8 mg/dL; a score of 4 = 99.6% probability (course slide). Urgent surgical aspiration/lavage and IV antibiotics are required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modified Kocher criteria = 4 classic criteria (+ CRP >2.0 mg/dL add-on); score 4 = 99.6% septic arthritis risk. Treatment = surgical aspiration/lavage + IV antibiotics. Key pitfall: distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal ESR/WBC, post-viral). COMLEX: Septic hip is a medical emergency in children — delay in treatment leads to femoral head destruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q18","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl presents with a 3-day history of left hip pain and reluctance to walk. Temperature is 37.1°C (afebrile), ESR is 22 mm/h, WBC is 8,200. She had a URI 2 weeks ago. Hip ultrasound shows a small effusion. Modified Kocher score is 0. Which diagnosis is MOST likely and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Septic arthritis, which must always be excluded with surgical aspiration regardless of the Kocher score result","B":"Transient synovitis, managed with NSAIDs and activity restriction since a Kocher score of 0 is <0.2% risk","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, which presents acutely with fever and joint effusion in children after a viral illness","D":"Psoas abscess, which should be excluded with CT imaging given the hip pain and recent URI history in a child","E":"SCFE, which presents acutely with effusion and restricted hip motion in young children after viral illness onset"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in children, often preceded by an upper respiratory infection a few days to 2 weeks earlier (SDL 9 p3 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). It is distinguished from septic arthritis by the absence of fever, normal or mildly elevated inflammatory markers, and a low Kocher score. Kocher score 0 = <0.2% probability of septic arthritis. Management is conservative: NSAIDs + activity restriction + close follow-up. The effusion is resorbed within 3 to 7 days, with no long-term sequelae (same reading).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transient synovitis vs. septic hip — key differentiator = Kocher criteria. Transient synovitis: afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, post-viral. Septic hip: fever, elevated inflammatory markers, ill-appearing child. Both can cause limp and hip effusion. COMLEX: Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in school-age children.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)"},{"id":"sdl5_q19","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is compressed in meralgia paresthetica, and what is the characteristic distribution of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"The femoral nerve is compressed, producing anterior thigh pain with weakness of knee extension and absent patellar reflex","B":"The obturator nerve is compressed, producing medial thigh pain and weakness of hip adduction bilaterally","C":"The sciatic nerve is compressed, producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and diminished Achilles reflex","D":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN) is compressed, producing pain, tingling, and burning paresthesias over the anterolateral thigh","E":"The ilioinguinal nerve is compressed, producing medial inguinal and scrotal pain without any thigh paresthesias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica is caused by compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN), a purely sensory nerve that exits the pelvis near the anterior superior iliac spine and passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament. It produces tingling, numbness, and burning pain in the anterolateral thigh — no motor deficit (it is purely sensory). The LFCN does not supply the medial thigh, groin, or below the knee, distinguishing it from femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN compression = anterolateral thigh paresthesias only, NO motor deficit. FA: causes = tight clothing, obesity, pregnancy; tingling/numbness/burning in anterolateral thigh. Median age = 50. COMLEX: LFCN runs beneath/through inguinal ligament near ASIS; palpation at this point may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q20","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with BMI of 38 and poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with burning and tingling over the anterior and lateral right thigh for 6 weeks. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness. Sensation is diminished over the anterolateral thigh only. Deep tendon reflexes are intact bilaterally. Which risk factor MOST significantly increases his risk for meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Male gender is the primary risk factor since meralgia paresthetica predominantly affects males over age 45","B":"Diabetes mellitus, which increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics","C":"Hypertension is the most significant risk factor through microvascular disease affecting nerve conduction velocity","D":"Hyperlipidemia is the primary risk factor through nerve sheath lipid deposition causing external compression","E":"Chronic NSAID use is the most significant risk factor through peripheral nerve demyelination over time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics — making it the most significant listed risk factor in this patient. The mechanism involves peripheral nerve susceptibility to compression from metabolic neuropathy. Other risk factors include obesity, older age (median age 50), pregnancy (increased abdominal pressure), ascites, tight belts/waistbands, prolonged pressure, and long-distance activities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica risk factors = obesity, older age (median 50), diabetes (7x risk), pregnancy, ascites, tight clothing. Clinical diagnosis — no EMG required unless diagnosis is uncertain. COMLEX: Anterior ASIS-area palpation may reproduce LFCN symptoms; OMT can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q21","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester develops burning pain and numbness over the anterolateral right thigh. Exam confirms LFCN distribution sensory loss without motor deficit. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying cause: reassurance that symptoms typically resolve postpartum once the pressure eases","B":"Prescribing anticonvulsants such as gabapentin or pregabalin as the primary first-line pharmacologic treatment","C":"Referring to anesthesia for nerve block injection at the anterior superior iliac spine immediately","D":"Ordering EMG and nerve conduction studies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment","E":"Placing the patient in a corset brace to decompress the inguinal ligament and reduce LFCN compression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica treatment begins by addressing the underlying cause. In pregnancy, increased abdominal pressure compresses the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. Symptoms typically resolve spontaneously after delivery as pressure decreases — expectant management and reassurance are appropriate first-line treatment. For recalcitrant cases (e.g., non-pregnant patients not responding to conservative measures), anticonvulsants (gabapentin, pregabalin) or anesthesia consultation for nerve block are escalation options.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat the cause first (weight loss, pregnancy delivery, remove tight clothing). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants or anesthesia consult. Clinical diagnosis — treatment does not require imaging or EMG. COMLEX: Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica resolves postpartum; OMT is safe in pregnancy and may address contributing pelvic somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q22","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female with type 2 diabetes presents with 3 days of right hip pain, fever of 38.7°C, and inability to bear weight. She appears ill. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step should be prioritized FIRST and what immediate action follows?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for hip osteoarthritis, since elderly diabetic patients with hip pain most likely have OA","B":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to differentiate greater trochanteric tendinopathy from joint disease","C":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic joint in elderly patients","D":"Step 2 — ruling out a dangerous condition with CBC, ESR, CRP, hip X-ray, and aspiration if an effusion is found","E":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma screen since a fall that was not reported is the most common cause of hip pain in elderly diabetics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, inability to bear weight, and an ill appearance in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient mandate immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous condition — specifically septic hip arthritis. Labs (CBC, ESR, CRP, blood cultures) and hip imaging are obtained, and the hip is aspirated urgently, because fluid analysis is the key to diagnosis. The hip diagnostic workflow explicitly requires dangerous conditions to be excluded before proceeding to OA or nerve pathology steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain + fever + non-weight-bearing + elevated labs = septic hip until proven otherwise. The modified Kocher criteria were derived in children; at any age, aspiration and fluid analysis make the diagnosis. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major immunocompromising risk factor for septic arthritis; septic hip requires surgical lavage + IV antibiotics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — septic hip must be ruled out in step 2 of the hip pain workflow; modified Kocher criteria guide the urgency of aspiration and surgical consultation in febrile patients with hip pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q23","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male presents with his mother reporting medial knee pain and a limp for 2 weeks. He denies trauma and has a normal knee examination. Which step in the hip pain diagnostic workflow is MOST critical to apply for this patient, and what imaging must be obtained?","choices":{"A":"The hip workflow applies; hip X-rays are essential because LCP and septic hip often refer pain to the thigh and knee","B":"The knee pain workflow should be completed first with MRI of the knee before considering any hip evaluation","C":"Lumbar spine MRI should be ordered first since L2-L3 radiculopathy is the most common cause of knee pain in children","D":"No additional imaging is needed since a normal knee exam in a child confirms there is no significant pathology","E":"Bilateral knee X-rays should be obtained to compare growth plates before expanding the evaluation to the hip"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A child with knee pain and a normal knee examination demands hip evaluation — this is a high-yield clinical pearl explicitly stated in the course hip pain workflow. Hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip, hip OA) frequently refers pain to the medial thigh and knee in children via obturator nerve distribution. Hip X-rays are almost always indicated. Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (ages 4-9, more common in males) and SCFE (obese adolescents) must both be excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Child with knee pain + normal knee exam = ALWAYS X-ray the hip. LCP = age 4-9, male predominance 4:1, insidious limp. SCFE = obese adolescent, ice cream scoop appearance on frog-leg lateral X-ray. Both are CP8 named COMLEX pediatric hip conditions. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a classic board trap with knee as the presenting complaint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fractures and pediatric hip pathologies (LCP, SCFE) frequently refer pain to the knee; the hip workflow's emphasis on hip X-ray in children with knee pain is driven by this referral pattern"},{"id":"sdl5_q24","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male presents with right hip pain for 4 months. History reveals no trauma. He denies fever. Neurological exam is normal. Hip X-ray shows mild joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step has the physician reached in determining the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Step 5 — the specific pain generator has been confirmed as the hip labrum based on the X-ray findings","B":"Step 3 — arthritis is the most likely cause, after trauma and dangerous conditions were excluded","C":"Step 4 — nerve pathology must now be ruled out before the OA diagnosis can be confirmed on X-ray","D":"Step 2 — a dangerous condition such as septic arthritis must be ruled out before X-ray findings are interpreted","E":"Step 1 — the history of possible missed trauma must be re-explored before attributing the findings to OA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1) and dangerous conditions (step 2), and with a normal neurological exam excluding nerve pathology (step 4), finding classic X-ray features of OA — joint space narrowing and osteophytes — places this physician at step 3 of the hip pain workflow: determining that OA is the most likely cause. Importantly, hip X-ray is almost always used to augment the H&P in the hip workflow, as it was here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip workflow integrates X-ray as a routine diagnostic augment — not just ordered after all H&P steps are complete. OA X-ray findings: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis. COMLEX: The hip pain workflow is identical in structure to the knee pain workflow; both follow the 5-step framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — hip OA presenting with joint space narrowing and osteophytes represents step 3 of the workflow; X-ray is almost always used to augment the hip pain diagnostic evaluation at this stage"},{"id":"sdl5_q25","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female presents with progressive right hip pain that she describes as a \"deep ache\" around the hip socket. She also reports right medial knee aching over the past year without trauma. Hip examination reveals markedly restricted internal rotation and moderate restriction of external rotation. Knee exam is entirely normal. What BEST explains the knee pain in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Concurrent medial compartment knee OA, which commonly develops simultaneously with ipsilateral hip OA in older women","B":"Degenerative medial meniscal tear, which is a predictable secondary complication of altered gait from hip OA","C":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee from altered biomechanics secondary to hip abductor dysfunction","D":"Hip OA referring pain to the medial thigh and knee through obturator nerve distribution, without intrinsic knee pathology","E":"Patellar subluxation from hip external rotator tightness causing lateral patellar tracking during ambulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hip OA can cause thigh and knee pain without any hip or groin pain — a recognized clinical pitfall. The hip joint's capsular innervation includes the obturator nerve (L2-L4), which also supplies the medial knee, explaining the referral pattern. The findings of restricted IR (first lost) and restricted ER (more advanced) confirm intra-articular hip pathology. A completely normal knee exam in the context of progressive hip OA strongly supports referred pain rather than intrinsic knee disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain = thigh and medial knee via obturator nerve. Always evaluate the hip when knee exam is normal in older patients with knee pain. COMLEX: In osteopathic practice, restricted hip IR on structural exam is a key finding that guides hip OMT and differentiates hip from knee pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the hip pain workflow specifically flags knee pain in children and thigh pain as potential hip referral patterns; this applies to adults with hip OA referring through obturator nerve distribution"},{"id":"sdl5_q26","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man reports 8 months of left \"hip pain.\" When asked to point with one finger, he indicates the groin. The pain is worse when he walks or turns in bed but is not worse when he lies on his left side, and the lateral thigh is not tender. Which feature of the history most supports hip osteoarthritis over greater trochanteric pain?","choices":{"A":"Pain located over the lateral upper thigh that worsens when he lies on the affected side, because the trochanter is compressed at night","B":"Pain spanning the joint and muscle along the whole limb, because hip arthritis spreads along the tensor fascia lata to the lateral knee","C":"Pain felt in the groin and worsened by moving the joint, with no increase when lying on that side, because true hip pain localizes there","D":"Pain in the buttock that radiates down the posterior thigh, because hip capsule distension irritates the sciatic nerve as it exits the pelvis","E":"Pain that is burning and brought on by light touch of the anterior thigh, because the capsule shares its supply with the lateral cutaneous nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The history chapter assigned for this SDL notes that pain from true hip pathology is felt in the groin, and that hip osteoarthritis usually radiates to the groin and is worsened by moving the joint. Patients often call lateral thigh pain \"hip\" pain, but lateral pain that is worse when lying on the affected side at night points to trochanteric pain (now understood mostly as gluteus medius tendinopathy). Pain along a whole limb spanning joint and muscle suggests a vascular or nerve root process, not osteoarthritis. Buttock pain radiating down the posterior thigh suggests sciatica or piriformis syndrome. Burning pain provoked by light touch of the anterolateral thigh is neuropathic, as in meralgia paresthetica.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip joint pain → groin (and may refer to thigh/knee via obturator nerve); lateral thigh pain worse lying on it → greater trochanteric pain syndrome. COMLEX: Have the patient point to the pain; location sorts articular from soft-tissue and neuropathic causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — gluteus medius tendinopathy behind so-called trochanteric bursitis"},{"id":"sdl5_q27","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old retired warehouse worker has right hip pain. Which pattern of modifying factors most supports osteoarthritis rather than rheumatoid arthritis as the cause of his hip pain?","choices":{"A":"Pain greatest on waking with prolonged morning stiffness that eases as the day goes on","B":"Pain brought on by movement and worse after prolonged use of the joint, easing with rest","C":"Pain present at rest and through the night with a warm, swollen joint and early erosions","D":"Pain triggered by light touch of the thigh with burning that does not change with motion","E":"Pain that improves with continued use and warm-up, then recurs after the activity ends"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The history chapter for this SDL sorts hip pain by modifying factors: joint pain present with movement suggests the effusion of osteoarthritis, and pain worse with prolonged use of the joint is listed under osteoarthritis. It also notes that repeated minor trauma, such as jumping off trucks and platforms at work, contributes to hip osteoarthritis. Pain greatest in the morning with stiffness, and pain present at rest, point to inflammatory disease such as rheumatoid arthritis, where synovial destruction can happen early. Burning pain provoked by light touch suggests neuropathic pain such as an entrapment neuropathy. Pain that improves with use and recurs after activity is the pattern listed for a hip adductor strain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = pain with use, relieved by rest, brief stiffness (≤30 min); RA = morning stiffness >1 hour, pain at rest, symmetric synovitis. COMLEX: Occupational history (repetitive impact loading) is a hip OA risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — clinical criteria for osteoarthritis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, stiffness ≤30 min)"},{"id":"sdl5_q28","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female long-distance runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter for 3 months, worsening with running, single-leg squat, and stair climbing. Passive hip range of motion is fully preserved and pain-free. MRI shows increased signal at the gluteus medius tendon insertion on the greater trochanter. What is the MOST accurate diagnosis and first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"True greater trochanteric bursitis; first-line treatment is intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection for bursitis","B":"Hip labral tear; first-line treatment is arthroscopic labral repair given the lateral hip pain in a runner","C":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy; first-line treatment is gait retraining and hip abductor strengthening to correct the load","D":"IT band friction syndrome; first-line treatment is foam rolling and stretching of the iliotibial band insertionally","E":"Hip OA; first-line treatment follows OARSI guidelines with structured land-based exercise and education as core care"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI showing signal change at the gluteus medius tendon insertion — not the bursa — confirms gluteus medius tendinopathy, the actual pathology underlying most cases of so-called \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" Preserved passive hip ROM distinguishes this extra-articular tendinopathy from intra-articular hip OA (which restricts passive motion). First-line treatment is biomechanical correction: gait retraining (reduce hip adduction during stance), hip abductor strengthening, and load management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy in most cases. MRI confirms tendon pathology. Passive ROM preserved = extra-articular. Treatment: biomechanics first; escalation options = CSI, regenerative injection (PRP), NTG patches, tenotomy. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness = Trendelenburg pattern; OMT addresses hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction driving tendinopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — lateral hip pain with preserved passive ROM represents step 5 of the hip pain workflow: the localized pain generator is the gluteus medius tendon, not the intra-articular hip joint"},{"id":"sdl5_q29","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has lateral upper thigh pain that radiates down the lateral thigh and is worse when she lies on that side at night. The pain does not reach the groin. She reports that one leg is noticeably longer than the other, and years ago similar pain went away after she added a lift to the shoe of the shorter leg. Which intervention is most directly suggested by her history?","choices":{"A":"Total hip arthroplasty, because lateral thigh pain at night in an older woman marks end-stage osteoarthritis of the joint","B":"Core decompression of the femoral head, because night pain with a limb length change signals femoral head collapse","C":"Open reduction and internal fixation, because limb shortening with lateral pain points to an occult femoral neck fracture","D":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve block, because lateral thigh pain worse at night is from nerve compression at the ligament","E":"Correcting the leg length discrepancy with a shoe lift, because the discrepancy can drive her greater trochanteric pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral upper thigh pain that radiates down the thigh, spares the groin, and worsens when lying on the affected side is the classic trochanteric presentation described in the history chapter's case. That case notes trochanteric pain may be caused by a leg length discrepancy and improve when the discrepancy is corrected, which fits her earlier response to a shoe lift. True hip osteoarthritis radiates to the groin and worsens with joint motion, so arthroplasty is not indicated. Core decompression treats pre-collapse avascular necrosis, and she has no AVN risk factors or groin pain. A femoral neck fracture presents with groin pain and inability to bear weight after trauma. Meralgia paresthetica causes burning anterolateral thigh paresthesias, not pain over the trochanter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater trochanteric pain syndrome is mostly gluteus medius tendinopathy; first-line care is biomechanical correction (gait retraining, abductor strengthening, leg length correction). COMLEX: Leg length discrepancy and pelvic obliquity are treatable biomechanical contributors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — prescribing footwear and orthotic changes based on biomechanics"},{"id":"sdl5_q30","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female ballet dancer describes an audible, painless snap at the left hip that occurs reproducibly when she swings her leg forward during pliés. She has no pain at rest and no mechanical locking. Which structure MOST likely causes this external snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter as the hip moves from extension to flexion, producing an external audible snap","B":"The iliopsoas tendon flipping over the iliopectineal eminence, producing an internal deep groin snap during hip flexion","C":"A hip labral tear causing intermittent femoral head subluxation with an audible click during hip rotation maneuvers","D":"A loose osteochondral body within the hip joint impinging during range of motion with reproducible snapping","E":"Greater trochanteric bursitis causing fluid displacement over the trochanteric prominence with hip motion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"External snapping hip syndrome occurs when the iliotibial band (or posterior edge of the gluteus maximus aponeurosis) snaps over the greater trochanter with hip flexion and extension. It produces a reproducible lateral snap or click that is audible and often palpable over the greater trochanter. Internal snapping hip involves the iliopsoas tendon over the iliopectineal eminence (anterior/deep groin snap). External snapping is the most common form; it is usually painless and benign, managed conservatively with IT band stretching and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: External snapping hip = IT band over greater trochanter = lateral audible snap. Internal snapping hip = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence = anterior deep groin snap. Intra-articular snapping = loose body or labral tear = painful mechanical click. COMLEX: Painless snapping hip without impingement is managed conservatively; OMT can address IT band and hip flexor somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — snapping hip (coxa saltans) is classified as external (IT band over greater trochanter) or internal (iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence); the lateral location of snapping distinguishes external from internal types"},{"id":"sdl5_q31","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A primary care physician is reviewing hip fracture prevention for an 80-year-old woman with osteoporosis. Besides her age, sex, and osteoporosis, which additional patient factor is listed in the course as a risk factor for hip fracture?","choices":{"A":"A body mass index above 30, because a heavier body raises the energy of a fall","B":"Male sex after age 65, because men have a smaller femoral neck cross section","C":"A low body mass index below 22, with little soft tissue padding over the hip","D":"Regular moderate exercise, because more time upright exposes her to falls","E":"Hypothyroidism on replacement, because thyroxine slows osteoblast activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists increasing age, female sex, osteoporosis, and low BMI (under 22) as hip fracture risk factors, with associations including endocrine disease (hyperthyroidism, diabetes), cardiovascular disease, and low socioeconomic status. A thin patient has less padding over the trochanter and often lower bone mass. A high BMI is not the listed risk factor. Female, not male, sex is the listed risk; femoral neck fractures are three times more common in women. Regular exercise is not a listed risk factor. The endocrine association named is hyperthyroidism (excess thyroid hormone accelerates bone turnover), not treated hypothyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture risk = age, female sex, osteoporosis, low BMI (<22); associations include hyperthyroidism, diabetes, CV disease. COMLEX: About one third of hip fractures go on to total hip arthroplasty; fall prevention and bone health are core primary care interventions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — hormones of bone remodeling, including thyroid hormone effects on bone turnover"},{"id":"sdl5_q32","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the ED after a collision. He is found to have his right hip held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Neurovascular assessment shows diminished sensation over the posterior thigh and lateral leg. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST likely, and what is the urgency of reduction?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation with femoral nerve injury; reduction can be delayed up to 24 hours safely without AVN risk","B":"Central fracture-dislocation with pudendal nerve injury; emergent CT must be completed before any reduction attempt","C":"Superior dislocation with superior gluteal nerve injury; urgent arthroscopic reduction is the preferred technique","D":"Posterior hip dislocation with peroneal nerve injury only; sciatic involvement is not possible in posterior dislocations","E":"Posterior hip dislocation with sciatic nerve injury; reduce urgently, as soon as possible, to limit AVN and ischemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation — the most common type — presents with the hip in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. The sciatic nerve lies posterior to the joint and is at risk of stretch injury, causing posterior thigh sensory loss (semitendinosus, semimembranosus territory) and lateral leg sensory loss (common peroneal component producing risk of foot drop). Reduction should be achieved as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours after injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics), to minimize both AVN risk (from femoral head ischemia) and sciatic nerve stretch injury. Emergent closed reduction under sedation is standard.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = hip flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop from peroneal division). Reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, to limit AVN. FA: inferior gluteal nerve at risk in posterior dislocation = difficulty rising from chair/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Hip dislocation is a CP8 named joint dislocation requiring urgent reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — posterior hip dislocation is a traumatic cause of femoral head AVN; urgency of reduction (within 6 hours) is driven by the same vascular disruption mechanism as femoral neck fracture-related AVN"},{"id":"sdl5_q33","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male with a high-velocity skiing injury presents with right hip pain and X-ray showing a subtrochanteric femur fracture. Which of the following BEST describes the epidemiology of subtrochanteric fractures?","choices":{"A":"Subtrochanteric fractures are the most common hip fracture type in women over 65 and have no bimodal distribution","B":"Subtrochanteric fractures are seen exclusively in elderly women over age 75 and are always associated with osteoporosis","C":"Subtrochanteric fractures are bimodal, peaking at ages 20-40 (high-energy trauma) and over 60 (osteoporotic falls)","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures occur predominantly in male athletes aged 15-25 from high-impact sports participation","E":"Subtrochanteric fractures are a pediatric injury seen exclusively in Salter-Harris physeal injury patterns under age 15"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution: a younger peak at ages 20-40 caused by high-energy trauma (MVA, falls from height, sports injuries) and an older peak above age 60 caused by osteoporotic low-energy falls. This is in contrast to intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures, which predominantly occur in elderly women. Intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common types in women over 65, with femoral neck occurring 3x more often in women than men.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture epidemiology by type — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck (most common). Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly = CP8 fracture; prevention requires fall risk assessment and osteoporosis treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution reflecting two distinct injury mechanisms: high-energy trauma in young adults and osteoporotic low-energy falls in the elderly population"},{"id":"sdl5_q34","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl who plays competitive soccer has 2 months of pain under the ball of the foot that is worse with running. There is tenderness over the head of the second metatarsal. Radiographs show flattening and sclerosis of the second metatarsal head. The course groups this condition with other forms of avascular necrosis in children. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Köhler disease, an osteochondrosis of the tarsal navicular that most often affects children aged 4 to 7","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, an osteochondrosis of the femoral head that most often affects ages 4 to 9","C":"Freiberg infarction, an osteochondrosis of the 2nd, 3rd, or 4th metatarsal, especially at 11 to 17","D":"Sever disease, a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus at the Achilles insertion in active children","E":"Morton neuroma, a perineural fibrosis of the third common digital nerve between the metatarsal heads"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course defines osteochondrosis as AVN in children and lists three types: Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease of the hip (ages 4 to 9), Köhler disease of the tarsal navicular (ages 4 to 7), and Freiberg infarction of the second, third, or fourth metatarsal (any age, especially 11 to 17). A teenager with second metatarsal head pain, flattening, and sclerosis has Freiberg infarction. Köhler disease affects the navicular in younger children. Legg-Calvé-Perthes involves the femoral head and presents with a limp and hip, thigh, or knee pain. Sever disease is a traction apophysitis at the heel, not osteonecrosis of a metatarsal. Morton neuroma is a nerve lesion with burning forefoot pain and normal bone on radiographs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteochondroses (pediatric AVN): Legg-Calvé-Perthes = femoral head (4–9 y), Köhler = navicular (4–7 y), Freiberg = 2nd–4th metatarsal head (11–17 y). COMLEX: AVN is ischemic death of marrow and bone; preserving the joint is the treatment goal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, the hip osteochondrosis of childhood"},{"id":"sdl5_q35","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male with sickle cell disease presents with severe bilateral groin pain, worse at night and with weight bearing. X-ray shows sclerosis in the right femoral head without crescent sign and a crescent sign with 3mm femoral head depression in the left. Which ARCO stages correspond to each hip, and which hip requires more urgent surgical consultation?","choices":{"A":"Right hip = Stage I (normal X-ray); left hip = Stage II (sclerosis); neither requires urgent surgical evaluation","B":"Right hip = Stage III (crescent sign); left hip = Stage IV (OA changes); both require total hip arthroplasty now","C":"Right hip = Stage IV (OA changes); left hip = Stage IIIA (depression ≤2mm); right hip is more urgent for arthroplasty","D":"Right hip = Stage II (sclerosis); left hip = Stage I (MRI only); surgical consultation is not yet warranted for either hip","E":"Right hip = Stage II (sclerosis); left hip = Stage IIIB (depression >2mm); the collapsed left hip is more urgent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ARCO staging: Stage II = X-ray shows sclerosis, osteolysis, or osteoporosis without crescent sign or collapse (right hip here); Stage IIIA = femoral head depression ≤2mm; Stage IIIB = depression >2mm (left hip with 3mm depression). The left hip at Stage IIIB has established structural collapse and requires urgent surgical evaluation for core decompression, osteotomy, or arthroplasty depending on extent. The right hip at Stage II is pre-collapse and eligible for joint-preserving procedures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARCO staging key cutpoints — Stage II = X-ray changes without crescent/collapse. Stage IIIA = ≤2mm depression. Stage IIIB = >2mm depression. Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. Sickle cell disease is a major cause of bilateral AVN. COMLEX: Pain at rest and nocturnal pain are characteristic of AVN, distinguishing it from OA (activity-related pain).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — ARCO staging guides treatment decisions in AVN; Stage I-II = joint-preserving (core decompression); Stage III-IV = reconstruction/arthroplasty; sickle cell disease causes bilateral AVN through vascular sludging in the femoral head watershed"},{"id":"sdl5_q36","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female is diagnosed with ARCO Stage II AVN of the femoral head secondary to chronic alcohol use. No femoral head collapse has occurred. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate conservative management options for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Immediate total hip arthroplasty since Stage II AVN always progresses to collapse and arthroplasty is the only cure","B":"Activity reduction, analgesics or NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, and physical therapy to delay femoral head collapse","C":"Systemic corticosteroid therapy to reduce inflammation in the femoral head and halt ischemic progression","D":"Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection into the hip as the primary treatment for Stage II femoral head AVN","E":"Complete hip immobilization with non-weight-bearing cast for 12 weeks as the definitive conservative treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conservative management of AVN at Stage II (pre-collapse) focuses on: (1) activity reduction to minimize femoral head loading, (2) analgesics/NSAIDs for pain, (3) bisphosphonates (reduce osteoclast activity and may slow progression), and (4) physical therapy to maintain function. No clearly superior treatment plan exists, but the goal is joint preservation. Investigational options include anticoagulants, statins, ESWT, and hyperbaric oxygen. Surgery (core decompression, osteotomy, arthroplasty) is reserved for progressive or end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN conservative management = activity reduction + NSAIDs + bisphosphonates + PT. No treatment reverses established necrosis — goal is joint preservation. Core decompression = surgical option for Stage I-II (pre-collapse). Stage III-IV = osteotomy or arthroplasty. COMLEX: Alcohol use disorder is a major modifiable AVN risk factor; counseling and cessation are part of management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fracture and dislocation are traumatic causes of femoral head AVN; alcohol-induced AVN shares the same endpoint of progressive femoral head ischemia and collapse requiring staged joint-preserving or reconstructive interventions"},{"id":"sdl5_q37","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has 3 days of right flank and anterior hip pain, fever, a limp, and pain with resisted hip flexion. WBC and CRP are elevated, and blood cultures are drawn. A contrast CT of the abdomen and pelvis shows no collection. Why should a psoas abscess still not be excluded?","choices":{"A":"CT cannot image the retroperitoneum, so the iliopsoas compartment must instead be examined with plain radiographs of the pelvis","B":"CT detects secondary abscesses from bowel disease, so a hematogenous abscess in a diabetic patient requires MRI","C":"Contrast enhancement masks fluid in the iliopsoas, so the CT must be repeated without contrast to show the abscess cavity","D":"Psoas abscess is a clinical diagnosis, so imaging has no role and drainage is planned from the examination findings","E":"CT is about 90% sensitive overall but falls to about 33% sensitive when symptoms have lasted less than 6 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course gives CT a sensitivity of about 90% for psoas abscess, but only about 33% when symptoms have lasted less than 6 days, so an early negative CT does not exclude the diagnosis in a patient with fever, flank and hip pain, a limp, and pain with resisted hip flexion. Diabetes is a listed risk factor for primary (hematogenous) abscess. CT does image the retroperitoneum, and plain films are not the test for an abscess. CT is not limited to secondary abscesses. Contrast does not mask the collection; contrast CT is the standard study. Imaging plays a central role; the course pairs CBC, ESR, CRP, LFTs, and blood cultures with CT, and treatment is incision and drainage with IV antibiotics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess: fever, flank/hip pain, limp, pain with resisted hip flexion (psoas sign); S. aureus most common; CT ~90% sensitive (≈33% if <6 days). COMLEX: Primary risk factors include diabetes, renal failure, HIV/immunosuppression, IV drug use, epidural anesthesia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — red-flag back pain from spinal infection"},{"id":"sdl5_q38","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 4 days of right hip pain, fever of 39.1°C, and refusal to bear weight. ESR is 65 mm/h, WBC is 14,200, and CRP is 3.2 mg/dL. He is ill-appearing. Modified Kocher score is 4-5. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Outpatient NSAID therapy and repeat Kocher score assessment in 48 hours to monitor for improvement or decline","B":"Urgent surgical aspiration and lavage of the hip joint with intravenous antibiotics for septic arthritis","C":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration for fluid analysis without lavage, followed by oral antibiotics if WBC >100,000","D":"Watchful waiting with IV hydration and bed rest while awaiting blood culture results before starting antibiotics","E":"MRI of the hip to confirm effusion before any aspiration or antibiotic therapy is initiated in this child"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Modified Kocher score 4-5 = 99.6% probability of septic hip arthritis. This is a surgical emergency — delay in treatment leads to rapid femoral head cartilage destruction and permanent disability. Management requires: (1) surgical aspiration/lavage of the joint, and (2) IV antibiotics (anti-staphylococcal coverage first-line). Delayed aspiration or conservative treatment alone is inappropriate at this Kocher score. The key pitfall is distinguishing this from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, Kocher score 0-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kocher score 4 = 99.6% septic arthritis = surgical emergency. Treatment = surgical lavage + IV antibiotics. Contrast with transient synovitis (Kocher score 0 = <0.2% risk). COMLEX: Septic hip is a named CP8 pediatric musculoskeletal emergency; joint destruction occurs within days without surgical intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — modified Kocher criteria are the validated decision tool for septic hip vs. transient synovitis in children; score of 4-5 mandates urgent surgical aspiration/lavage regardless of imaging findings"},{"id":"sdl5_q39","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin presents with fever, right flank pain, and a limp. He has no gastrointestinal, urinary, spinal, or prior hip surgery history. CT shows a collection within the right iliopsoas compartment. How is this abscess classified, and why is this compartment prone to infection?","choices":{"A":"Secondary abscess from contiguous spread, because injected drug enters the psoas directly from a nearby femoral vein","B":"Secondary abscess from vertebral osteomyelitis, because injection drug use seeds the disc before the muscle is involved","C":"Primary abscess of hematogenous origin, because the muscle has rich blood flow and lies near retroperitoneal lymphatics","D":"Primary abscess from Crohn disease, because occult transmural ileitis is the usual source in young patients without history","E":"Tuberculous cold abscess, because injection drug use classically reactivates spinal tuberculosis that tracks along the psoas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides psoas abscess into primary, which is hematogenous or lymphatic in origin, and secondary, from direct spread from an adjacent structure (vertebrae, infected hip arthroplasty, GI tract especially Crohn disease, GU tract, aorta). IV drug use is a listed primary risk factor, along with renal failure, HIV or immunosuppression, diabetes, epidural anesthesia, and trauma with hematoma. The compartment is vulnerable because of its very rich blood flow and its closeness to retroperitoneal lymphatics. Spread from a femoral injection site or from vertebral osteomyelitis would be contiguous, and he has no spinal or adjacent source. Crohn disease causes secondary, not primary, abscesses. Tuberculous cold abscess is not the course classification and is not tied to injection drug use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary psoas abscess = hematogenous/lymphatic (IVDU, HIV, diabetes, renal failure); secondary = contiguous spread (Crohn, vertebral osteomyelitis, GU, infected THA, aorta). Most common organism S. aureus. COMLEX: Treatment = incision/drainage + IV antibiotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — dangerous conditions (infection) screened in step 2 of the hip pain workflow"},{"id":"sdl5_q40","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old male with BMI of 42 and type 2 diabetes presents with a 3-month history of burning pain and numbness over the right anterior and lateral thigh. He works as a long-haul truck driver and wears a tight tool belt. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness, intact patellar reflex, and decreased sensation over the anterolateral thigh only. Which diagnosis BEST explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"L3 radiculopathy from lumbar disc herniation, producing anterior thigh pain with quadriceps weakness and reduced patellar reflex","B":"Meralgia paresthetica from LFCN compression by the tool belt and obesity, with purely sensory symptoms","C":"Femoral neuropathy from diabetic amyotrophy, producing proximal leg pain and weakness with reduced patellar reflex","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment producing medial thigh adductor weakness and medial thigh sensory loss","E":"Sciatic nerve compression producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and reduced Achilles tendon reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica (LFCN compression) presents with pure sensory symptoms — burning, tingling, numbness over the anterolateral thigh — without any motor deficit or reflex change. The LFCN is a purely sensory nerve. Key distinguishing features: no motor weakness (eliminates radiculopathy and femoral/obturator neuropathy), intact reflexes, and sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh. Risk factors include obesity, DM (7x risk), tight belts, and prolonged sitting/pressure — all present here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensory only, NO motor, intact reflexes. Distinguish from L3 radiculopathy (quadriceps weakness, reduced patellar reflex), femoral neuropathy (motor deficit + reflex loss). FA: LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensation; causes = obesity, pregnancy, tight clothing. COMLEX: LFCN passes under/through inguinal ligament at ASIS; local palpation at ASIS may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica is differentiated from lumbar radiculopathy by the absence of motor deficit and preserved deep tendon reflexes; sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh confirms LFCN rather than L2-L3 root involvement"},{"id":"sdl5_q41","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old female in her 32nd week of pregnancy develops burning and tingling over the right anterolateral thigh that began 3 weeks ago. Motor exam is normal. Patellar reflex is intact. She finds the symptoms most bothersome at night. Which is the MOST accurate explanation for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"L3-L4 disc herniation from pregnancy-related lumbar hyperlordosis causing radiculopathy into the anterior thigh","B":"Femoral nerve stretch from increased pelvic tilt during pregnancy causing quadriceps weakness and anterior thigh pain","C":"Obturator nerve entrapment from pelvic widening during pregnancy causing medial thigh burning and adductor weakness","D":"Piriformis syndrome from pelvic floor changes during pregnancy compressing the sciatic nerve in the gluteal region","E":"Gravid uterus pressure compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament (meralgia paresthetica), resolving postpartum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica results from increased abdominal pressure (gravid uterus) and altered pelvic mechanics compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. It presents with burning and tingling in the anterolateral thigh without motor deficit — characteristic of pure LFCN sensory compression. It typically resolves spontaneously within weeks to months after delivery as abdominal pressure decreases. Management is expectant with reassurance; positioning changes (avoiding prolonged sitting) may help.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy = major risk factor for meralgia paresthetica via increased abdominal pressure. Pure sensory (anterolateral thigh), NO motor deficit. Management = reassurance + symptom modification; resolves postpartum. COMLEX: OMT is safe in pregnancy and can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to LFCN compression during pregnancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — pregnancy causes meralgia paresthetica through increased abdominal pressure compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament; this is the same mechanism as obesity and ascites, both of which increase intra-abdominal pressure"},{"id":"sdl5_q42","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with obesity-related meralgia paresthetica fails to improve after weight loss counseling over 3 months and lifestyle modification. He has persistent 7/10 burning pain over the anterolateral thigh limiting work and sleep. Which is the MOST appropriate escalation of treatment for refractory meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar epidural steroid injection targeting the L2-L3 level to reduce radicular inflammation around the LFCN origin","B":"Intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection to reduce local inflammation compressing the LFCN at the joint margin","C":"Anticonvulsants such as gabapentin or pregabalin, or anesthesia referral for an LFCN block at the inguinal ligament","D":"Surgical hip arthroplasty to decompress the LFCN by relieving hip joint capsule tension on the surrounding nerves","E":"Systemic oral corticosteroid taper for 6 weeks to reduce perineural inflammation around the compressed LFCN"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For refractory meralgia paresthetica (failed conservative management including treating the underlying cause), escalation options include: (1) anticonvulsants — gabapentin or pregabalin — for neuropathic pain management, and (2) anesthesia consultation for nerve block injection at the inguinal ligament level (LFCN block). Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rarely needed. Lumbar epidural and hip injections target the wrong anatomic structure. The course slides explicitly list anticonvulsants and anesthesia consultation as the treatment options for recalcitrant cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat cause first (weight loss, remove compression). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants (gabapentin/pregabalin) or anesthesia consult for LFCN block. Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rare. COMLEX: LFCN block at ASIS is a targeted intervention that confirms diagnosis and provides therapeutic benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — anticonvulsant medications (gabapentin, pregabalin) are the recognized pharmacologic escalation for refractory meralgia paresthetica when treating the underlying cause fails to resolve the neuropathic pain"}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"Leg, Ankle & Foot Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl7_q01","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is the primary site of pathology in medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS)?","choices":{"A":"Posteromedial tibial periosteum, subjected to repetitive traction from posterior compartment muscles including the tibialis posterior, soleus, and flexor digitorum longus","B":"Anterior tibial cortex, stressed by axial compressive loading from repetitive heel strike impact forces during high-mileage training programs in competitive distance runners","C":"Lateral tibial cortex, subjected to shear stress from excessive ankle pronation and supination cycling forces during push-off in runners with biomechanical malalignment","D":"Medial tibial endosteum, injured by elevated intramedullary pressure from increased bone marrow vascularity during rapid progression of aerobic training load in athletes","E":"Posterior tibial diaphysis, compressed by increased quadriceps and hamstring co-activation forces generating axial tibial stress during high-intensity interval training"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The primary pathological site in MTSS is the posteromedial tibial periosteum, which is subjected to repetitive traction from the origins of the posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL). The anterior tibial cortex is more associated with anterior tibial stress fractures, while endosteal pathology suggests deeper marrow injury. Identifying the periosteum as the site explains why overpronation — which increases traction on the posteromedial periosteum — is the strongest modifiable risk factor.","boardPrep":"MTSS = traction periostitis at origins of posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL) on posteromedial tibia. Steps: repetitive activity → posterior compartment overuse → traction periostitis → bone remodeling → fracture if no rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q02","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone scan finding best differentiates medial tibial stress syndrome from a tibial stress fracture?","choices":{"A":"Focal, intense, well-demarcated area of increased uptake at a single point on the tibial cortex, indicating a stress fracture at that specific discrete cortical location","B":"Completely normal bone scan bilaterally with no areas of increased uptake despite significant clinical symptoms and prolonged posteromedial tibial tenderness on examination","C":"Patchy, diffuse areas of increased uptake spanning several centimeters along the posteromedial tibial cortex without any single focal hot spot on the delayed-phase images","D":"Increased uptake in the anterior tibial compartment bilaterally reflecting tibialis anterior muscle inflammation from overuse training in a distance runner during peak mileage","E":"Focal uptake at the tibial metaphysis bilaterally suggesting growth plate stress injury rather than periosteal or cortical bone involvement in the skeletally immature athlete"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key differentiator between MTSS and tibial stress fracture on bone scan is the distribution of tracer uptake: MTSS produces patchy, diffuse uptake spanning centimeters along the posteromedial cortex, while a stress fracture shows a focal, intense single-point hot spot at the fracture site. Both conditions can be bilateral, so symmetry is not the distinguishing feature. A normal scan does not separate the two: the bone scan is often negative in MTSS, so only the uptake pattern (diffuse vs focal) distinguishes them.","boardPrep":"MTSS bone scan: patchy diffuse uptake. Stress fracture: focal intense hot spot at one site. MRI: periosteal fluid, bone marrow signal in MTSS. X-ray often negative in MTSS; may show periosteal scalloping or cortical thickening late.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q03","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for medial tibial stress syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Increased external hip rotation beyond 15 degrees measured during gait analysis in female distance runners with a history of recurrent lower extremity overuse injuries","B":"Decreased lumbar spine bone mineral density measured by DEXA scan in collegiate athletes with amenorrhea from relative energy deficiency in sport during competitive season","C":"Use of minimalist footwear with a heel-to-toe drop below 4mm in athletes who transition abruptly from traditional cushioned running shoes during high-mileage training","D":"Increased tibial torsion angle above 20 degrees measured on CT scanogram of the lower extremity in military recruits during the first weeks of intensive basic training","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial tibial periosteum from the posterior compartment muscles during the loading phase of gait"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial periosteum from posterior compartment muscle origins during the loading phase of gait. External hip rotation, bone mineral density, footwear style, and tibial torsion are proposed risk factors but have weaker or less consistent evidence than subtalar overpronation in the published literature on MTSS.","boardPrep":"MTSS: the only confirmed ('Yes') risk factor on the course slide is overpronation; training errors, footwear, surface, fatigue, flexibility, low BMD and hypermobile subtalar joint are 'maybe'. Male military recruits: ↑ hip internal and external rotation, less calf girth. Females 3× more likely to get MTSS than males.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor"},{"id":"sdl7_q04","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of ankle sprains?","choices":{"A":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral sprain cases seen clinically","B":"Medial ankle sprains are the most common type, occurring at 2.7 per 1000 athlete exposures and frequently requiring surgical repair of the deltoid ligament complex","C":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprains account for 40-60% of all ankle injuries and most cases require orthopedic surgical fixation to restore distal tibiofibular joint stability","D":"Ankle sprains affect males more than females with an overall incidence of 7 per 1000 person-years in male athletes compared to female counterparts in all age groups","E":"Lateral ankle sprains carry a 45% recurrence rate and invariably progress to chronic ankle instability requiring surgical ligament reconstruction within 2 years of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral cases. Medial sprains are rare (0.06/1000 athlete exposures), not the most common type. Syndesmotic sprains account for only 0.38/1000 — not 40-60%. Ankle sprains occur more frequently in females than males, and the recurrence rate is approximately 11.9%, not 45%.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprains: 2 million/year in US. Lateral 70-90%; ATFL injured in 73% of lateral cases. Females > males. Children/adolescents > adults. Medial: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures. Syndesmotic: 0.38/1000. Recurrence rate 11.9%.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q05","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces a lateral ankle sprain with isolated ATFL injury?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion combined with internal rotation of the tibia over a fixed foot, stressing the anterior talofibular ligament during a rapid deceleration maneuver in sport","B":"Eversion combined with external rotation of the ankle while planting the foot, stressing the calcaneofibular and deltoid ligament complex in a multiligamentous injury","C":"Plantarflexion combined with tibial external rotation stressing the anterior tibiofibular ligament and the interosseous membrane of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis","D":"Inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, placing the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and making it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading","E":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation stressing the deltoid ligament medially and the anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis simultaneously"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprain most commonly occurs with inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, which orients the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and makes it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading. Dorsiflexion with external rotation stresses the syndesmosis; dorsiflexion with internal rotation is not the lateral-sprain mechanism (the ATFL is lax in dorsiflexion). Eversion with external rotation injures the deltoid complex, not the lateral ligaments.","boardPrep":"Lateral ankle sprain mechanism: inversion with plantarflexion. Order of injury: ATFL first (73% isolated) → CFL → PTFL. Medial sprain: eversion and dorsiflexion. Syndesmotic: dorsiflexion + external rotation. Anterior drawer tests ATFL; talar tilt tests CFL; squeeze and external rotation tests syndesmosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q06","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Ankle Rules, which criterion mandates ankle radiograph in a patient presenting with ankle pain?","choices":{"A":"Anterior ankle tenderness at the ATFL with visible swelling and ecchymosis, regardless of whether the patient can bear weight on the injured extremity at time of evaluation","B":"Midfoot tenderness at the cuboid or base of the 5th metatarsal with inability to bear weight, which triggers foot radiographs rather than standard ankle view series","C":"Posterior medial malleolus tenderness over the distal 6cm, posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps immediately after injury","D":"Lateral ankle tenderness anterior to the ATFL with a positive anterior drawer test indicating ligament laxity but no point tenderness over the malleoli on palpation","E":"Visible ankle swelling appearing within 2 hours of injury with complete inability to complete active range of motion in plantarflexion or dorsiflexion upon examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ottawa Ankle Rules mandate radiographs when there is posterior medial malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Anterior ATFL tenderness and visible swelling alone do NOT trigger ankle X-rays under the Ottawa rules. Midfoot tenderness at the navicular or 5th metatarsal base triggers foot radiographs, not ankle views. Positive anterior drawer without malleolar tenderness does not mandate imaging.","boardPrep":"Ottawa Ankle Rules — ankle X-ray if: (1) posterior lateral malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), (2) posterior medial malleolus tenderness, OR (3) inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Foot X-ray if: navicular tenderness OR 5th metatarsal base tenderness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral 70-90% (ATFL in 73%); inversion with plantarflexion; Ottawa rules guide imaging; grades 1-3 by ligament integrity"},{"id":"sdl7_q07","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces an acute gastrocnemius strain at the medial musculotendinous junction?","choices":{"A":"Sustained plantarflexion against resistance during repetitive uphill running causing concentric overload and progressive degenerative change at the medial gastrocnemius origin","B":"Rapid deceleration with the knee extended causing simultaneous hamstring and gastrocnemius strain with diffuse posterior knee pain in middle-aged recreational athletes","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading during prolonged downhill running causing gradual degenerative microtrauma at the Achilles tendon myotendinous junction proximal to insertion","D":"Sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, causing eccentric overstretch of the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous unit during push-off","E":"Direct blunt contusion to the posterior calf from contact during a team sport causing a localized intramuscular hematoma within the lateral gastrocnemius muscle belly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gastrocnemius medial head strain (tennis leg) classically occurs with sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, producing eccentric overstretch at the musculotendinous junction during push-off. Sustained plantarflexion against resistance causes concentric, not eccentric, overload. Rapid deceleration with the knee extended more commonly stresses the hamstrings. Direct blunt contusion causes intramuscular hematoma at the point of impact, not at the medial MTJ.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain (tennis leg): sudden acceleration from stationary position with ankle dorsiflexed (lunging in tennis or squash). Eccentric overstretch; injury at transition from eccentric to isometric contraction. Medial head most commonly injured. Classic: sudden pop, \"hit in the calf\" sensation, tenderness at medial MTJ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q08","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical finding distinguishes a Grade III gastrocnemius strain from a Grade II injury?","choices":{"A":"Severe posterior calf pain during passive dorsiflexion that prevents the patient from completing full range of motion assessment during the clinical physical examination","B":"Positive Thompson test with a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction indicating complete rupture of the medial gastrocnemius with surgical repair required","C":"Significant ecchymosis tracking distally to the heel within 24 hours of injury due to gravitational spread of blood along the posterior leg fascial planes after trauma","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg calf raise on the affected side within the first 72 hours of the injury due to pain and weakness of the posterior calf compartment","E":"Loss of active plantarflexion strength measured against gravity with the ankle held at 20 degrees of dorsiflexion, distinguishing Grade II from Grade I partial tear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Grade III gastrocnemius strain is defined by complete rupture of the medial head, producing a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction. Grade II has active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM but Thompson is negative and no palpable defect exists. Ecchymosis and inability to calf raise occur in both Grade II and III and therefore do not distinguish the grades from each other.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grades: Grade I — sharp pain, can calf raise, returns in 10-12 days. Grade II — unable to continue activity, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, returns in 16-21 days. Grade III — immediate severe pain, positive Thompson test, palpable defect at MTJ, surgical repair, returns in 6 months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q09","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which progressive rehabilitation sequence is most appropriate within the first 24 hours of a gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Begin concentric calf strengthening exercises, progressing to weighted calf raises and then eccentric calf lowering exercises as pain allows for full functional restoration","B":"Apply a full leg cast in equinus for 6 weeks with strict non-weight bearing to protect the myotendinous junction from any mechanical loading during the acute healing phase","C":"Administer a corticosteroid injection directly into the musculotendinous junction under ultrasound guidance to reduce the acute inflammatory response within 24 hours","D":"Proceed with surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius head regardless of clinical grade determination on initial physical examination findings","E":"Apply deep tissue massage and myofascial release techniques directly over the site of the muscle tear within the first 24 hours to limit early scar tissue formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation begins within 24 hours with concentric calf strengthening, then progressive loading with weight, then eccentric calf lowering as pain allows. Full equinus casting is reserved only for Grade III complete rupture after surgical repair. Corticosteroid injection and surgery are not indicated for Grade I or Grade II strains. The progression from concentric to eccentric loading is the established rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching. Strengthening within 24h: (1) concentric, (2) add weight, (3) eccentric calf lowering. Low-impact cardio maintained throughout. Myofascial techniques when pain-free. Surgery only for Grade III complete rupture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q10","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of clinical findings is most consistent with Grade 2 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Normal medial longitudinal arch with pain and swelling of the tendon; patient is able to complete a single-leg heel raise without significant difficulty or pain on examination","B":"Fixed subtalar joint deformity with rigid pes planus that cannot be passively corrected to neutral; requires orthopedic referral for surgical bony reconstruction procedure","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial tendon swelling with preserved normal arch height; patient completes heel raise without weakness or pain on repeat standardized physical exam","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg heel raise with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction deformity that is passively correctable to neutral subtalar and hindfoot alignment","E":"Complete tendon rupture confirmed on MRI with total collapse of medial arch requiring urgent orthopedic consultation for posterior tibial tendon surgical reconstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Grade 2 PTTD is defined by the inability to perform a single-leg heel raise combined with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction that is passively correctable to neutral hindfoot alignment. Grade 1 has tendon pain and swelling but preserved heel raise ability. Grade 3 has a fixed subtalar deformity that cannot be passively corrected to neutral. Grade 4 involves valgus tibiotalar tilt within the mortise, requiring surgical reconstruction.","boardPrep":"PTTD grading: Grade 1 — pain/swelling, able to single-leg heel raise, no deformity, conservative care. Grade 2 — CANNOT single-leg heel raise, flexible pes planus + forefoot abduction, same conservative treatment as Grade 1. Grade 3 — Grade 2 + fixed subtalar joint → ortho referral for reconstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q11","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test is the hallmark clinical assessment for posterior tibial tendon dysfunction across all grades?","choices":{"A":"Weakness or inability to perform a single-leg heel raise on the affected side, indicating insufficient posterior tibial tendon function to supinate the hindfoot during rise","B":"Positive anterior drawer sign at the ankle indicating concurrent anterior talofibular ligament laxity contributing to chronic lateral ankle instability in the affected patient","C":"Tenderness over the lateral calcaneus worsening with inversion stress testing, indicating peroneal tendon subluxation or coexisting lateral ankle ligament pathology","D":"Decreased passive subtalar inversion range of motion below 10 degrees indicating early degenerative arthritic changes in the subtalar joint complex on examination","E":"Medial ankle ecchymosis with a palpable gap at the posterior tibial musculotendinous junction indicating acute complete rupture of the posterior tibial tendon recently"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The single-leg heel raise is the hallmark clinical test for PTTD across all grades, directly assessing the posterior tibial tendon ability to supinate the hindfoot and maintain the medial arch during heel rise. The anterior drawer tests the ATFL, not the posterior tibial tendon. Subtalar inversion ROM reflects joint mobility, not tendon function specifically. A palpable gap with medial ecchymosis would indicate acute complete tendon rupture, not the chronic progressive course of typical PTTD.","boardPrep":"PTTD hallmark: single-leg heel raise failure. The posterior tibial tendon inverts the hindfoot and maintains the medial arch during heel rise. Too-many-toes sign (hindfoot valgus, forefoot abduction viewed from behind) is classic for Grade 2+. Prevalence 3.3-10%; increases after age 40; usually unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q12","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which management strategy is most appropriate as the primary initial treatment for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Platelet-rich plasma injection directly into the posterior tibial tendon sheath under ultrasound guidance as the preferred first-line regenerative intervention for Grade 1","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the posterior tibial tendon sheath as the primary evidence-based first-line management approach for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction","C":"Nitroglycerin patch applied over the medial ankle as the first-line adjunctive treatment for all grades of posterior tibial tendinopathy based on strong evidence","D":"Extracorporeal shockwave therapy targeting the tendon insertion as the evidence-based preferred non-surgical option for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction management","E":"Activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics for pain control, and supervised physical therapy with eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grade 1 PTTD is managed conservatively with activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, and supervised physical therapy including eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening. PRP and corticosteroid injections are adjunctive options with limited evidence and are not first-line for Grade 1. Casting is reserved for more advanced cases. Nitroglycerin patches have limited and inconsistent evidence and are not the primary recommended treatment.","boardPrep":"PTTD management: Grade 1 and Grade 2 — activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, physical therapy. Grade 3 — orthopedic referral for surgical reconstruction. Adjunctive options: corticosteroid injections (caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections, nitroglycerin patches (all limited evidence). Eccentric PT is cornerstone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint"},{"id":"sdl7_q13","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes insertional from mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy based on anatomic location?","choices":{"A":"Mid-portion tendinopathy involves the paratenon sheath exclusively and responds to eccentric loading programs regardless of the stage or chronicity of symptoms in the patient","B":"Insertional tendinopathy occurs exclusively in older sedentary patients and is entirely unrelated to activity level, sport participation, or mechanical loading forces","C":"Insertional tendinopathy affects the tendon-bone junction at the calcaneal enthesis, while mid-portion disease occurs in the hypovascular tendon body above the insertion","D":"Mid-portion tendinopathy causes calcaneal bone spurring at the insertion requiring surgical excision, while insertional disease has no associated bony calcaneal changes","E":"Insertional tendinopathy exclusively affects competitive athletes while mid-portion tendinopathy is limited to recreational runners with poor foot and ankle biomechanics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy occurs at the calcaneal enthesis where the tendon attaches to bone, while mid-portion tendinopathy occurs in the hypovascular tendon body proximal to the insertion — the most common site of tendinopathy. The mid-portion involves the tendon substance and paratenon, not the paratenon exclusively. Insertional tendinopathy is not limited to older sedentary patients, and bone spurring (Haglund deformity) is associated with the insertional type, not mid-portion disease.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy: insertional = at calcaneal enthesis (tendon-bone junction), associated with bone spur or Haglund deformity. Mid-portion = tendon body proximal to the insertion (hypovascular zone) (most common site). Both worsen with increased activity. Tendinopathy umbrella includes tendinitis, tendinosis, paratendinitis, enthesopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q14","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of factors is confirmed as independently associated with increased risk of Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Female sex with estrogen-related collagen laxity, vegetarian diet with reduced collagen precursors, and hyperpronation of the subtalar joint as the primary risk factors","B":"High weekly mileage above 50 miles per week, running exclusively on hard concrete surfaces, and consuming a high-fat diet as the primary confirmed risk factors identified","C":"Reduced ankle dorsiflexion ROM below 10 degrees measured non-weight bearing as the single most important confirmed primary risk factor for Achilles tendinopathy in athletes","D":"Male sex, increasing age, prior history of tendinopathy, overweight status, and poor running mechanics with excessive supination, each confirmed as an independent risk factor","E":"Leg-length discrepancy above 2cm measured by scanogram combined with arch height index below 0.30 as the most strongly confirmed combination of risk factors for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Confirmed risk factors for Achilles tendinopathy include male sex, increasing age, prior tendinopathy history, overweight status, and poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion). Female sex and estrogen-related laxity are not the primary confirmed risk factors — males have higher incidence. High mileage and hard surfaces are proposed risk factors but are less consistently confirmed than the biomechanical and demographic factors listed.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy risk factors: prior tendinopathy, poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion), worn footwear, weak plantarflexion, leg-length discrepancy, increased age, male sex, overweight. Incidence: competitive athletes 24%, recreational runners 6-17% of injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q15","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical therapy intervention is considered the first-line rehabilitation for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Concentric calf strengthening only in a completely pain-free range with no eccentric component permitted until 12 full weeks after symptom onset has been reached","B":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading protocol with progressive resistance increase, establishing controlled tendon loading to promote collagen remodeling and pain reduction","C":"Isokinetic strengthening using a dynamometer at 60 degrees per second as the preferred physical therapy approach validated for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy treatment","D":"Ultrasound-guided corticosteroid injection into the paratenon sheath followed by 6 weeks of complete relative rest and passive gentle Achilles tendon stretching exercises","E":"Static gastrosoleus stretching for 30 seconds three times daily combined with daily nitroglycerin patch application as the current evidence-based standard protocol for tendinopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line PT for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy, applying controlled tensile load that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction and promotes collagen remodeling. Concentric-only exercises lack the specific mechanical stimulus that drives matrix remodeling in tendinopathy. Corticosteroid injection carries significant tendon rupture risk and has uncertain long-term benefit. Static stretching and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive at best and are not first-line evidence-based treatments.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy PT: eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line. Perform heel drop off a step: rise on two, lower on one. Gastrosoleus mobilization and activity modification are complementary. Corticosteroid injection: uncertain benefit, rupture risk. Percutaneous tenotomy and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl7_q16","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomic description correctly identifies the location of the retrocalcaneal bursa?","choices":{"A":"The superficial subcutaneous bursa located posterior to the Achilles tendon between the skin and the tendon at the heel level, irritated by shoe counter pressure on activity","B":"The medial calcaneal bursa located at the plantar-medial calcaneal prominence adjacent to the plantar fascia attachment on the inferior calcaneal surface with weight bearing","C":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion","D":"The subtalar bursa located within the tarsal sinus between the talus and calcaneus adjacent to the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament on the lateral aspect of the foot","E":"The tibiotalar bursa located anteriorly between the tibial plafond and the talar dome within the ankle mortise, irritated by repetitive dorsiflexion impingement in athletes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion. The superficial calcaneal bursa lies between the Achilles and overlying skin and is irritated by shoe counter pressure. The medial calcaneal and subtalar bursae are distinct structures at different anatomic locations. The tibiotalar bursa is anterior within the ankle mortise and is not the retrocalcaneal bursa.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursa: between the calcaneus and deep surface of Achilles tendon. Distinct from superficial calcaneal bursa (between Achilles and skin). Often coexists with Achilles tendinosis and calcaneal enthesopathy. One of 3 most common bursitis locations. More common in adults; males = females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q17","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding is most characteristic of retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Pain with resisted plantarflexion and maximum tenderness at the mid-portion of the Achilles tendon 4cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion on direct palpation of the tendon","B":"Posterior heel pain with tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon insertion, worsening with passive ankle dorsiflexion examination","C":"Inferomedial heel pain that is most severe with the first morning steps and improves significantly after walking for approximately 10 minutes of activity on the affected foot","D":"Lateral heel tenderness with swelling that worsens with ankle inversion stress testing and resolves completely when the ankle is held in passive dorsiflexion by the examiner","E":"Diffuse posterior ankle swelling with pain on forced plantarflexion at end range indicating posterior impingement between the calcaneus and the posterior tibial plafond"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis characteristically produces posterior calcaneal tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just deep to the Achilles tendon, worsened by passive dorsiflexion which compresses the bursa. Mid-tendon tenderness 4cm proximal suggests mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy. Inferomedial heel pain worst with first morning steps is the classic presentation of plantar fasciitis, not bursitis. Lateral heel pain with inversion stress indicates lateral ankle ligament or peroneal tendon pathology.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: posterior calcaneal pain deep to Achilles, worse with dorsiflexion (bursa compressed). Often confused with plantar fasciitis (inferior heel, worse first steps). Highly associated with Achilles tendinosis. Treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching, corticosteroid injection, surgery rarely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q18","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions is most appropriate as the initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain control, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure, and Achilles tendon stretching program","B":"Immediate corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa under ultrasound guidance followed by 2 weeks of strict non-weight bearing on crutches for the patient","C":"Surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as the primary treatment given the poor long-term outcomes reported with conservative management for retrocalcaneal bursitis","D":"Platelet-rich plasma injection combined with prolotherapy injections into the bursa and surrounding tendon as the initial regenerative treatment approach at first visit","E":"Oral corticosteroid taper for 10 days combined with iontophoresis applied directly over the posterior heel as the standard evidence-based initial management protocol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis includes cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure on the bursa, and Achilles tendon stretching. Immediate corticosteroid injection is not first-line — it is reserved for cases refractory to conservative care. Surgery is rarely needed. PRP and prolotherapy have insufficient evidence to serve as initial treatment. Oral corticosteroid taper is not standard management for localized bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification (reduce heel counter pressure), Achilles stretching. If refractory: corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not into Achilles tendon). Surgery rarely indicated. Distinguish from superficial calcaneal bursitis (between skin and Achilles — caused by shoe rubbing).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q19","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease affects females at age 8 years and males at age 6 years, with unilateral involvement accounting for approximately 90% of all affected pediatric patients","B":"The incidence is 10 per 1000 pediatric patients, and unilateral presentation occurs in approximately 90% of all cases presenting in skeletally immature young athletes","C":"Sever disease predominantly affects adolescents over age 14 years and resolves spontaneously within 6 weeks without any specific treatment or activity restriction","D":"Males and females are equally affected with a peak onset between ages 12 and 14 years and predominantly unilateral involvement in the dominant extremity in most cases","E":"Males develop at a peak age of 8 years, females at 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately two-thirds of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease peaks in males at age 8 years and females at age 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately 2/3 of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients. Females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation, not later. The incidence is not 10/1000. Sever disease does NOT predominantly affect adolescents over 14 — it resolves as the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity, which occurs by the early teenage years.","boardPrep":"Sever disease: males peak at 8yr, females peak at 6yr (females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation). Males > females. Bilateral in 2/3. Incidence 3.7/1000. Duration: 3-4 years until skeletal maturity. Posteroinferior calcaneus growth plate at Achilles insertion is the site of injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q20","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pathophysiologic mechanism is responsible for calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis of the calcaneal epiphysis from disruption of the nutrient artery blood supply to the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate cartilage during athletic activity","B":"Repetitive traction stress from the Achilles tendon on the calcaneal apophysis growth plate during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes during development","C":"Acute complete fracture through the calcaneal apophyseal growth plate from a single episode of forceful ground impact during competitive athletic activity in youth athletes","D":"Inflammatory bursitis of the retrocalcaneal bursa causing secondary irritation of the adjacent calcaneal growth plate cartilage in young skeletally immature pediatric athletes","E":"Repetitive compressive stress fracture of the calcaneal body from excessive impact loading during running causing subchondral bone insufficiency injury in growing athletes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sever disease is caused by repetitive traction from the Achilles tendon on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate (apophysis) during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes. It is NOT avascular necrosis — that term applies to Legg-Calve-Perthes disease of the femoral head. It is not an acute fracture through the apophysis, nor isolated bursitis. The diagnosis is traction apophysitis, mechanically analogous to Osgood-Schlatter disease at the tibial tubercle but occurring at the calcaneal apophysis.","boardPrep":"Sever disease pathophysiology: calcaneal apophysis is the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate at the Achilles insertion. Repetitive Achilles traction on the immature apophysis during running and jumping → apophysitis (traction injury, NOT avascular necrosis — that is Legg-Calve-Perthes). Clinical diagnosis; X-ray not required but may show fragmentation of apophysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q21","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions represents appropriate initial management of Sever disease in a skeletally immature athlete?","choices":{"A":"Activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain control, and footwear modification with adequate heel support","B":"Immediate casting in equinus position for 6 weeks followed by a gradual return to sport over 3 months under supervised physical therapist rehabilitation program","C":"Surgical removal of the apophyseal ossification center when conservative measures fail to improve symptoms within 2 weeks of initial conservative treatment initiation","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the calcaneal apophysis under ultrasound guidance combined with complete cessation of all athletic activity for 3 months minimum","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the apophyseal growth plate combined with extracorporeal shockwave therapy as the preferred first-line treatment for all cases"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sever disease management begins with activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain, and footwear modification. Surgery is NEVER indicated for Sever disease, which is self-limited. Casting in equinus is not the initial approach. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at a pediatric growth plate. PRP is not an appropriate first-line therapy in pediatric growth plate conditions. The goal is symptom control with maintenance of activity as tolerated.","boardPrep":"Sever disease treatment: activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles stretching, cold compresses, NSAIDs, PT. Recalcitrant cases: short-term immobilization. Self-limited 3-4 years. Surgery NOT indicated. Corticosteroid injection NOT appropriate in pediatric growth plate conditions. Return to sport as symptoms allow with activity modification.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years"},{"id":"sdl7_q22","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical presentation is most specific for common peroneal nerve injury at the level of the fibular head?","choices":{"A":"Burning and shooting pain radiating from the lateral knee to the dorsum of the foot and toe web spaces, with possible ankle dorsiflexion weakness or foot drop","B":"Decreased sensation over the dorsolateral foot and sinus tarsi with tenderness to percussion at the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus without any weakness","C":"Burning superficial pain with decreased sensation at the anterolateral shin and a palpable fascial defect at the crural fascia just lateral to the fibula distally","D":"Medial knee pain with numbness extending to the medial calf without any foot weakness, from saphenous nerve compression at the medial knee fascial level on examination","E":"Burning pain along the medial calf with numbness at the medial knee and no identifiable motor deficits, consistent with saphenous nerve entrapment at the fascia level"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head produces burning and shooting pain from the lateral knee to the dorsal foot and web spaces, with possible foot drop from motor involvement (ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension weakness). Saphenous nerve entrapment causes medial knee and calf pain without any foot weakness. Superficial peroneal entrapment at the crural fascia produces only sensory symptoms (dorsolateral foot numbness) without motor deficit. Deep peroneal compression causes first web space numbness only.","boardPrep":"Common peroneal nerve: wraps around fibular neck at the fibular head. Injury from: cycling (hip flexion pressure), leg crossing, braces, casts, direct trauma. Motor: foot drop (ankle dorsiflexion, toe extension). Sensory: dorsum of foot, first through fourth web spaces, lateral lower leg. Tinel at fibular head. Most common cause of foot drop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q23","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Where is the superficial peroneal nerve most commonly entrapped, and which symptom pattern does it produce?","choices":{"A":"At the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus where the nerve exits the fascia, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot","B":"At the fibular head where the nerve wraps around the lateral fibular neck, producing foot drop and toe web space numbness from direct pressure at the lateral knee level","C":"At the medial knee where the nerve passes through the fascia between the sartorius and gracilis tendons, producing medial lower leg paresthesias and medial knee pain","D":"At the tarsal tunnel posterior to the medial malleolus beneath the flexor retinaculum, producing plantar foot burning, numbness, and arch pain with prolonged standing","E":"At the anterior ankle beneath the extensor retinaculum, producing first web space numbness and pain with active dorsiflexion testing of the anterior compartment musculature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superficial peroneal nerve exits the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus and is most commonly entrapped at this fascial exit point, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. It does NOT wrap around the fibular head — that is the common peroneal nerve. Superficial peroneal entrapment is PURELY SENSORY distally, distinguishing it from common peroneal injury which causes foot drop. Tarsal tunnel involves the tibial nerve posterior to the medial malleolus.","boardPrep":"Superficial peroneal nerve: entrapment at crural fascia proximal to lateral malleolus. Sports: bodybuilding, horse racing, running, soccer, tennis. Symptoms: burning superficial pain, decreased sensation at sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. Signs: Tinel at fascia, palpable fascial defect. Purely sensory distally — no foot drop (distinguishes from common peroneal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q24","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which diagnostic study is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis and localize the level and severity of lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the lower extremity with dedicated nerve protocol to evaluate for intraneural ganglion cysts or perineural fibrosis mechanically compressing the nerve trunk along its course","B":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to localize the level of nerve dysfunction and quantify whether the injury is primarily axonal or demyelinating in nature","C":"Ultrasound-guided injection of local anesthetic adjacent to the suspected nerve as the gold standard confirmatory test for definitively confirming peripheral nerve entrapment","D":"CT myelogram of the lumbar spine to exclude a radicular origin at the L4-S1 nerve roots as the source of lower extremity sensory and motor symptoms and changes","E":"Plain radiograph of the fibula to identify bony cortical abnormality at the fibular head contributing to common peroneal nerve compression and foot drop in the patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography (NCS/EMG) are the definitive tests for peripheral nerve entrapment, localizing the level of dysfunction and characterizing whether injury is primarily axonal (amplitude reduction) or demyelinating (conduction velocity slowing). MRI with nerve protocol detects structural causes but does not provide electrophysiological characterization. Ultrasound-guided local anesthetic injection is diagnostic-therapeutic but not the gold standard for nerve injury characterization. CT myelogram evaluates the spinal canal for radicular pathology.","boardPrep":"Lower extremity nerve injuries: confirm with NCS and EMG to localize level and characterize injury (axonal vs demyelinating). Saphenous nerve: purely sensory — pain/numbness medial knee and calf, NO motor deficits. Common peroneal: motor (foot drop) + sensory. Superficial peroneal: mainly sensory (dorsolateral foot). Conservative: gait analysis, neuromodulators, TENS, PT, anti-inflammatories. Invasive: steroid injection, hydrodissection, surgical release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q25","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female cross-country runner has 3 weeks of bilateral lower leg pain. Pain is diffuse along the posteromedial tibia, worsens with activity, and is present the following morning. She increased mileage from 30 to 50 miles/week over 2 weeks. Exam shows diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness without focal point tenderness. X-rays are negative. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral tibial stress fractures from acute mileage increase, requiring immediate non-weight bearing and orthopedic referral for management with focal point tenderness expected","B":"Bilateral exertional compartment syndrome causing elevated posterior compartment pressures with exercise, requiring compartment pressure measurement at rest and with exertion","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial vein deep venous thrombosis presenting as bilateral lower extremity pain in a young female athlete with increased training demands and activity level","D":"Bilateral periosteal hematomas from direct contact injuries during team cross-country training sessions causing localized posteromedial tibial tenderness and soft tissue swelling","E":"Medial tibial stress syndrome from repetitive posterior compartment traction periostitis triggered by rapid mileage increase and likely subtalar overpronation mechanics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This clinical picture — diffuse posteromedial tibial pain bilaterally, worsening with activity and present the following morning, triggered by rapid mileage increase in a young female runner with negative X-rays — is classic MTSS. Bilateral tibial stress fractures would show focal point tenderness and require imaging confirmation. Exertional compartment syndrome symptoms occur during exercise and require compartment pressure measurement at rest and post-exercise. DVT and periosteal hematoma from contact do not fit this activity-related pattern.","boardPrep":"MTSS hallmark: diffuse (NOT focal) posteromedial tibial pain at junction of proximal 2/3 to distal 1/3. Pain decreases with warm-up, athlete can complete session, recurs after exercise and the following morning. Key differentiator from stress fracture: diffuse vs focal tenderness. Bone scan differentiates: MTSS = patchy diffuse vs stress fracture = focal hot spot.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hip overuse injuries: repetitive mechanical loading exceeding tissue adaptation capacity is the shared pathophysiologic basis of all overuse syndromes including MTSS; apply the 10% rule to prevent all overuse injuries"},{"id":"sdl7_q26","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old volleyball player has a grade 2 lateral ankle sprain. After PRICE therapy and a few days on crutches, her physician fits an ankle stirrup brace rather than a cast and starts early motion exercises. Which statement best explains why selective motion in a stirrup brace is preferred over rigid casting for healing the torn ligament?","choices":{"A":"The brace allows flexion and extension but blocks inversion and eversion, and ligaments healed under this motion lay down collagen parallel to their long axis","B":"The brace blocks flexion and extension but allows inversion and eversion, so the torn lateral ligaments are moved through their normal arc and stretched back to full length","C":"The brace locks the ankle in plantarflexion, which shortens the anterior talofibular ligament and brings the torn ends together faster than a neutral cast can achieve","D":"The brace compresses the joint capsule, which raises intra-articular pressure and drives synovial fluid into the ligament to nourish its avascular midsubstance","E":"The brace is cheaper and easier to remove for bathing, but cast immobilization produces stronger healed tissue and remains the preferred option when cost is not a concern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading for this SDL explains that the stirrup brace allows ankle flexion and extension, which prevents stiffness, while blocking inversion and eversion, which would disturb healing of the torn lateral and medial ligaments. In the animal work it cites, ligaments allowed flexion-extension motion healed with collagen fibers parallel to the long axis of the ligament and were stronger and less likely to rerupture, whereas cast-immobilized ligaments laid down randomly oriented collagen. A brace that blocks flexion-extension and allows inversion-eversion reverses the design and would stress the injured ligaments. Locking the ankle in plantarflexion places the ATFL in its most vulnerable position, the position in which it tears. Raising intra-articular pressure to nourish the ligament is not the mechanism described. Casting is reserved for patients with severe pain and does not produce stronger healed tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Healing ligament under controlled tensile load → collagen aligns along the line of stress; rigid immobilization → disorganized collagen. Lateral sprain: grade 2 = brief non-weight bearing, brief immobilization, rehabilitation. COMLEX: Early proprioceptive and balance exercises reduce recurrent sprains.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — ligament composition as dense regular collagen aligned with tensile load"},{"id":"sdl7_q27","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female marathon runner recovers from MTSS after activity modification. Bilateral subtalar overpronation is confirmed on exam. She asks which modification most urgently reduces recurrence risk given the evidence base for MTSS risk factors. Which counseling point is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Transition entirely to barefoot running to eliminate heel strike and normalize posterior compartment loading mechanics during all future training and competition activities","B":"Increase training cadence above 180 steps per minute as the single most effective evidence-based intervention for reducing the risk of MTSS recurrence in distance runners","C":"Obtain custom orthotics proven by a randomized controlled trial to reduce MTSS recurrence by 60% in female runners with confirmed subtalar overpronation on physical exam","D":"Address subtalar overpronation with appropriate motion-control footwear or custom orthotics, as overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS","E":"Permanently reduce weekly mileage by 50% below the level at which symptoms developed, as mileage reduction is the only proven strategy for preventing future MTSS episodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, so addressing it with motion-control footwear or custom orthotics is the best-supported prevention counseling given this runner's confirmed subtalar overpronation. Barefoot running is not evidence-based for MTSS prevention and may worsen the condition. Increased cadence has theoretical benefit but is not confirmed as the most effective intervention. Permanent 50% mileage reduction is unsupported — the 10% weekly increase rule is the appropriate training modification.","boardPrep":"MTSS prevention: address overpronation (strongest confirmed evidence). Apply 10% rule for mileage increase. Footwear modification for gait abnormalities. Cross training maintains fitness during recovery. Return to play: 2 weeks to 4 months with 10% weekly mileage rule. Surgery (superficial posterior compartment release): 70% success rate for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: overpronation is the strongest confirmed modifiable risk factor; training errors such as rapid mileage increase and changes in terrain are probable contributors with less consistent evidence than biomechanical factors"},{"id":"sdl7_q28","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male basketball player rolls his right ankle during practice. He has lateral ankle pain, ecchymosis, and is able to bear weight with pain. Anterior drawer test is mildly positive; talar tilt is negative. Ottawa ankle rules are negative. Which sprain grade and initial management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain with incomplete ATFL tear; treat with PRICE therapy, brief non-weight bearing, short immobilization period, and early progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 1 lateral ankle sprain with microtears only; treat with PRICE and immediate full activity return without any immobilization or bracing because no instability is present","C":"Grade 3 lateral ankle sprain with complete ligament rupture; treat with surgery and 6 weeks of non-weight bearing in a full cast before initiating any rehabilitation program","D":"Syndesmotic ankle sprain requiring orthopedic referral for syndesmotic fixation and 8 weeks of non-weight bearing based on mechanism regardless of examination findings","E":"Medial ankle sprain of the deltoid complex requiring MRI confirmation and non-weight bearing before initiating any weight-bearing activity or rehabilitation for this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A mildly positive anterior drawer (ATFL partially torn) with negative talar tilt (CFL intact), ecchymosis, and ability to bear weight with pain best fits Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain. Grade 1 has no instability on anterior drawer. Grade 3 has complete ligament rupture with strongly positive stress testing and often inability to bear weight. Ottawa rules were negative, excluding clinically significant fracture. Syndesmotic or medial sprains do not match the mechanism or this exam pattern.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprain grading: Grade 1 — stretching/microtears, minimal pain, no instability, no immobilization, early rehab. Grade 2 — incomplete tear, moderate swelling/ecchymosis, mild anterior drawer, brief NWB + immobilization, rehab. Grade 3 — complete tear, marked swelling in 1-2h, cannot bear weight, positive stress test, immobilization + rehab.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 7 — Ankle joint dislocations: severe ankle ligament disruption with dislocation requires immediate orthopedic evaluation; ankle sprains without dislocation are graded 1-3 based on ligament integrity and functional instability"},{"id":"sdl7_q29","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female soccer player sustains an ankle injury when her foot is planted and her leg externally rotates while the ankle dorsiflexes. Pain is above the ankle at the distal tibiofibular junction. The squeeze test is positive. No lateral ankle ligament tenderness is present. Which structure is most likely injured?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament and calcaneofibular ligament complex from classic inversion with plantarflexion mechanism causing the most common lateral ankle sprain pattern","B":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis, injured by dorsiflexion and external rotation mechanism producing a high ankle sprain above the ankle mortise","C":"Deltoid ligament complex from eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism causing medial ankle pain, often associated with avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus in athletes","D":"Posterior talofibular ligament from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior ankle pain and positive posterior drawer sign on physical examination testing","E":"Calcaneofibular ligament in isolation from pure inversion mechanism without plantarflexion, producing lateral ankle pain and a positive talar tilt test on examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation of the leg over a planted foot, producing pain at the distal tibiofibular junction with a positive squeeze test but no lateral ligament tenderness, is pathognomonic of a syndesmotic (high) ankle sprain of the anterior tibiofibular ligament. Lateral ligament injuries (ATFL/CFL) occur with inversion-plantarflexion. Deltoid injury occurs with eversion. PTFL injury from severe inversion causes posterior ankle pain, not proximal tibiofibular pain. Always palpate the proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve.","boardPrep":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprain: mechanism = dorsiflexion + external rotation. Structures: anterior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL), posterior tibiofibular ligament (PITFL), interosseous membrane. Tests: squeeze test (compress fibula at mid-calf, pain at distal tibiofibular joint), external rotation test. Harder to diagnose, longer recovery than lateral sprain. Always palpate proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Maisonneuve fracture = syndesmotic disruption + proximal fibula fracture from external rotation; always palpate proximal fibula when syndesmotic sprain is suspected on examination after ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl7_q30","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female gymnast has medial ankle pain after landing from a dismount with her foot in eversion. X-ray shows an avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus. Which ligament injury is most likely responsible for the avulsion fracture mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament tear from inversion mechanism causing lateral ligament disruption and lateral ankle instability without medial ankle or malleolus involvement","B":"Calcaneofibular ligament rupture from combined inversion and plantarflexion during the landing, producing lateral ankle swelling and a positive talar tilt test on examination","C":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis torn from dorsiflexion and external rotation producing proximal pain above the ankle mortise after the gymnastics dismount","D":"Deltoid ligament complex injury from an eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism, with traction from the deltoid ligament avulsing the tip of the medial malleolus at its attachment","E":"Posterior talofibular ligament avulsion from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior lateral ankle pain and chronic instability after the gymnastics landing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eversion combined with dorsiflexion stresses the deltoid ligament complex, which can avulse the medial malleolus at its bony attachment. The deltoid is the medial stabilizer of the ankle and resists eversion forces. ATFL injuries from inversion and CFL injuries from inversion-plantarflexion do not produce medial malleolus avulsion. Syndesmotic injury from external rotation produces proximal pain at the tibiofibular junction, not medial malleolus avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Medial ankle sprain: mechanism = eversion and dorsiflexion. Deltoid complex injury: often with multiligamentous injury or medial malleolus avulsion fracture. Medial sprain incidence: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures (much rarer than lateral 0.93/1000). Treatment: PRICE, immobilization, non-weight bearing, orthopedic consult. Ottawa: medial malleolus posterior tenderness = X-ray required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: medial malleolus fracture from deltoid traction is a component of bimalleolar or trimalleolar fracture patterns in ankle injuries with eversion mechanism; Ottawa rules mandate imaging when medial malleolus tenderness is present"},{"id":"sdl7_q31","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male tennis player feels a sudden pop in his posterior left calf while lunging for a ball. He reports feeling \"hit in the leg.\" Tenderness is at the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous junction. Dorsiflexion reproduces pain. No palpable defect is found. Thompson test is negative. He could not continue playing. Active plantarflexion against resistance is painful, and passive dorsiflexion is reduced compared with the other side. Which grade and expected return-to-sport timeline is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 gastrocnemius strain with incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion ROM, and return to sport in 16 to 21 days with progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 3 gastrocnemius strain with complete rupture confirmed by Thompson positivity and palpable defect, requiring surgical repair with return 6 months after operative intervention","C":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain with microtears causing mild calf pain on unilateral calf raise only, with expected return to sport in 10 to 12 days with conservative management","D":"Achilles tendon rupture with positive Thompson sign and palpable gap at the tendon, requiring urgent orthopedic evaluation for either surgical repair or conservative casting","E":"Deep venous thrombosis of the posterior tibial vein mimicking gastrocnemius strain, requiring Doppler ultrasound and therapeutic anticoagulation rather than sport rehabilitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Negative Thompson test and absent palpable defect with active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM confirm Grade 2 (incomplete tear), with expected return to sport in 16-21 days. Grade 3 requires a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the MTJ. Grade 1 produces only pain with single-leg calf raise with no loss of dorsiflexion. Achilles tendon rupture has a gap at the tendon proper (not the MTJ) with a positive Thompson test. DVT should be excluded but has a distinct clinical course.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grade 2 vs 3: Grade 2 = incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, Thompson negative, no palpable defect, RTS 16-21 days. Grade 3 = complete rupture, Thompson positive, palpable defect at MTJ, surgery, RTS 6 months. Achilles rupture: gap at tendon itself (not MTJ), Thompson positive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Thompson test distinguishes Achilles rupture (positive = no plantarflexion squeeze response) from gastrocnemius strain (Thompson negative); palpable defect location at the MTJ (gastrocnemius) vs tendon itself (Achilles) is the key anatomic distinguishing feature"},{"id":"sdl7_q32","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female masters runner has a sudden tearing sensation in her right calf while accelerating from a stop. Ultrasound shows a 2cm partial tear of the medial gastrocnemius head at the musculotendinous junction. She has pain with weight bearing and reduced dorsiflexion. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius given the 2cm partial tear size confirmed on musculoskeletal ultrasound imaging assessment","B":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the partial tear under ultrasound guidance as the current standard first-line treatment based on strong randomized controlled trial evidence","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the tear site under ultrasound guidance to reduce acute inflammation and facilitate earlier return to running activity in this masters athlete","D":"Equinus casting for 4 weeks in full plantarflexion to protect the healing musculotendinous junction from mechanical stretch forces during the acute tissue repair phase","E":"Ice, crutches for weight bearing assistance, heel lift to offload the calf, and progressive rehabilitation beginning with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening program"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grade 2 (incomplete) gastrocnemius strain from a 2cm partial tear on ultrasound is managed conservatively with ice, crutches for weight-bearing assistance, heel lift, and progressive rehabilitation starting with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening. Surgery is not indicated for partial tears — only Grade III complete rupture warrants surgical exploration. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at an acute muscle tear site. Equinus casting is reserved for post-surgical Grade III repair management.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain Grade II conservative management: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching, strengthening within 24h (concentric → add weight → eccentric calf lowering), low-impact cardio, myofascial techniques when pain-free, biomechanical correction. MSK ultrasound helpful for grading >Grade 1. Surgery reserved for Grade III only.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric strengthening is a core modality for tendon and muscle injuries; progressive loading from concentric to eccentric is the standard rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries after gastrocnemius strain"},{"id":"sdl7_q33","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male soccer player has a Grade I gastrocnemius strain with pain on unilateral calf raise. He asks when he can return to full competitive soccer. Which return-to-sport timeline is most appropriate for Grade I gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Return in 16 to 21 days following rehabilitation with progressive concentric and eccentric calf strengthening, as appropriate for Grade II gastrocnemius strain management","B":"Return in 6 months following surgical repair and sport-specific rehabilitation as appropriate for Grade III complete gastrocnemius rupture with complete defect at the MTJ","C":"Return in 10 to 12 days following ice, relative rest, heel lift, and progressive strengthening once pain on unilateral calf raise has fully resolved with exercise","D":"Return in 4 to 6 weeks contingent upon achieving a pain-free single-leg calf raise and full ankle dorsiflexion range of motion compared to the uninjured contralateral side","E":"Return in 3 to 5 days following acute swelling resolution because Grade I strains involve only microtears and do not require any significant time away from competitive sport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain (microtears only, pain on single-leg calf raise) has an expected return-to-sport of 10-12 days once the athlete can perform a pain-free calf raise. The 16-21 day timeline applies to Grade 2. The 6-month timeline applies to Grade 3 post-surgery. A 4-6 week timeline is more consistent with Grade 2 criteria. A 3-5 day timeline is insufficient even for a Grade 1 strain and risks reinjury.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain return-to-sport timelines: Grade I = 10-12 days (pain-free calf raise), Grade II = 16-21 days (pain-free active plantarflexion, full dorsiflexion), Grade III = 6 months post-surgery. Always compare to contralateral side. Sport-specific activity progression before full return to competitive play.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: Grade I returns in 10-12 days, Grade II in 16-21 days, Grade III in 6 months post-surgery; these timelines assume progressive pain-free rehabilitation with restoration of strength and ROM before sport clearance"},{"id":"sdl7_q34","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has had medial ankle pain for 2 years and a collapsed arch that now feels stiff. Standing, her heel sits in valgus and she cannot rise onto the ball of that foot. When the examiner tries to swing the hindfoot back to neutral, it will not move because the subtalar joint is rigid. How should her posterior tibial tendon dysfunction be graded and managed?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1, because the tendon is still painful and swollen; treat with activity modification, analgesics, arch support, and a supervised physical therapy program","B":"Grade 2, because the heel raise fails with pes planus and forefoot abduction; treat with the same arch support, analgesics, and therapy used for grade 1 disease","C":"Grade 2, because a rigid subtalar joint is expected once the arch collapses; treat with a molded arch support so the foot conforms and the arch is restored","D":"Grade 3, because grade 2 findings now come with a fixed subtalar joint; refer to an orthopedic foot and ankle surgeon, since the rigid deformity needs surgery","E":"Grade 3, because she is older than 60 with symptoms beyond 1 year; treat with a corticosteroid injection into the tendon sheath and a period of casting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A rigid subtalar joint is the feature that defines the most advanced grade in the course scheme: grade 3 is grade 2 disease plus a fixed subtalar joint, and it is referred to an orthopedic foot and ankle surgeon. The office orthopedics reading adds that an untreated collapsed arch becomes rigid and cannot be corrected without surgery. Grade 1 is a painful, swollen tendon with a preserved heel raise and no deformity. Grade 2 adds a failed heel raise with flatfoot and forefoot abduction but remains flexible, and it is treated like grade 1 with activity modification, analgesics, arch support, and physical therapy. An arch support works while the foot can still conform to it. Grading does not depend on age or symptom duration, and corticosteroid injection is an adjunct, not the plan for a fixed deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) inverts the foot and supports the medial arch; failure → adult-acquired flatfoot. PTTD grade 3 = fixed subtalar joint → surgical referral. COMLEX: Flexible deformity → orthotic/UCBL brace + inversion strengthening; rigid → surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — orthotic and footwear prescription for foot alignment problems"},{"id":"sdl7_q35","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has several months of atraumatic medial arch pain on the right. He is examined standing in shorts with shoes and socks removed, and the examiner looks at both heels from behind. Which finding seen from this position most supports posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"The right heel tilts medially and the forefoot adducts, so fewer lateral toes are visible on the right than on the left when the feet are viewed from behind","B":"Both heels sit vertically with a high medial arch, so the toes are hidden behind the heels, the expected appearance of a tibialis posterior tendon tear","C":"The right heel tilts laterally and the foot rotates externally, so more lateral toes are visible on the right than on the left, the too many toes sign","D":"A firm swelling at the posterosuperior right calcaneus lateral to the Achilles tendon becomes red where the heel counter of the shoe rubs it","E":"The right calf shows a palpable gap at the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous junction, and squeezing the calf fails to plantarflex the foot"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading says that in a patient over 50 with atraumatic medial arch pain, PTTD is the most likely diagnosis. Viewed from behind while standing, the collapsed arch lets the heel tilt laterally and the foot rotate externally, so more lateral toes are visible on the affected side, the too many toes sign. The reading pairs this with the single-leg heel rise, which is nearly impossible with PTTD. A medially tilted heel with forefoot adduction and a high arch describes a cavovarus foot, the opposite pattern. A painful bump at the posterosuperior calcaneus rubbed by the heel counter is a posterior heel problem related to retrocalcaneal irritation, not PTTD. A gap at the musculotendinous junction with a failed calf squeeze describes a grade III gastrocnemius rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTTD = tendon swells and cannot glide → arch collapses, heel goes into valgus, forefoot abducts → \"too many toes\" sign + failed single-leg heel rise. COMLEX: Examine standing from behind with shoes off; risk rises after age 40 and disease is usually unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastrocnemius strain grading with the Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q36","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old female with posterior tibial tendinitis asks about nitroglycerin patches after seeing them mentioned as a treatment option online. Which statement best reflects current evidence for nitroglycerin patches in tendinopathy management?","choices":{"A":"Nitroglycerin patches are proven in multiple high-quality randomized controlled trials to significantly accelerate tendon collagen synthesis and reduce pain scores reliably","B":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive therapy option for tendinopathy management but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent overall","C":"Nitroglycerin patches are strictly contraindicated in tendinopathy because nitric oxide increases local vasodilation and paradoxically worsens inflammatory pain over time","D":"Nitroglycerin patches are the recommended first-line adjunctive therapy in all major orthopedic and sports medicine tendinopathy clinical practice guidelines worldwide","E":"Nitroglycerin patches are reserved exclusively for post-surgical tendon repair failure and should not be used for any conservative tendinopathy management in any patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive option for tendinopathy but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent — they are listed in the course slides with acknowledged uncertainty, not as first-line therapy. They are not proven by multiple high-quality RCTs. They are not contraindicated (they have been studied in this indication). They are not endorsed as first-line by all major guidelines. The established first-line treatments for tendinopathy are eccentric physical therapy and orthotics.","boardPrep":"PTTD adjunctive therapies: corticosteroid injections (use caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections/PRP (investigational), nitroglycerin patches (limited/mixed evidence). The course slides list all three as options without strong endorsement. For boards: eccentric PT and orthotics = established first-line. The adjunctive therapies have limited evidence and are used selectively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 6 — Analgesics: topical agents including nitroglycerin patches, topical NSAIDs, and local anesthetics may have roles in musculoskeletal pain management; evidence quality varies significantly by agent and specific clinical indication"},{"id":"sdl7_q37","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old recreational runner with Achilles tendinopathy says his worst pain comes with the first few steps out of bed in the morning and eases as he keeps walking. His physician recommends a night splint along with calf stretches. Which explanation best accounts for both the morning pain and the benefit of the splint?","choices":{"A":"Overnight immobility lets inflammatory cells settle in the paratenon, and the splint compresses the tendon sheath so the cells are pushed back into the circulation by morning","B":"The ankle dorsiflexes during sleep and overstretches the tendon all night, and the splint holds the foot in plantarflexion so that the tendon can rest in a shortened position","C":"Morning pain comes from retrocalcaneal bursal fluid that collects overnight, and the splint squeezes the bursa so the fluid drains before the patient bears weight","D":"Morning pain reflects calcaneal apophyseal stress that peaks with the first weight bearing of the day, and the splint protects the growth plate during sleep","E":"The foot drifts into plantarflexion during sleep, so the Achilles shortens and is abruptly stretched on the first step; the splint keeps a gentle stretch on it overnight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading explains that during sleep the foot drifts into plantarflexion, relaxing and shortening the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia. With the first step, these structures are stretched abruptly, which is painful when they are inflamed and lack compliance; this is why Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis both hurt most on the first steps of the day. A night splint keeps a gentle stretch across the tendon during the night. Inflammatory cells being pushed into the circulation is not the mechanism. The foot does not dorsiflex during sleep, and holding the ankle in plantarflexion would worsen the morning stretch. Retrocalcaneal bursitis is a separate diagnosis, and the splint does not drain a bursa. Calcaneal apophysitis is a condition of skeletally immature children, not a 41-year-old.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-step pain = Achilles tendinopathy or plantar fasciitis (structures shorten overnight in plantarflexion). Treatment: calf stretches, night splints, NSAIDs, eccentric loading; avoid corticosteroid injection near the Achilles (rupture risk). COMLEX: Gastrosoleus mobilization and correction of running mechanics address the load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — retrocalcaneal bursitis often confused with plantar fasciitis"},{"id":"sdl7_q38","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female distance runner with mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy is prescribed an eccentric loading protocol. She asks why eccentric exercises are specifically recommended over concentric-only exercises for tendinopathy rehabilitation. Which mechanism best explains the therapeutic benefit?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric loading acutely elevates local glucocorticoid levels within tendon tissue, suppressing inflammatory cytokine production and reducing tendon pain signaling pathways","B":"Eccentric loading applies mechanical tension that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis, fiber reorganization, and tendon matrix remodeling","C":"Eccentric loading immediately relieves pain by activating descending inhibitory spinal pain control pathways activated by high-intensity mechanical input during the exercise","D":"Eccentric loading compresses and reduces the size of the retrocalcaneal bursa by mechanically displacing fluid, reducing bursitis-related pain components during activity","E":"Eccentric loading relaxes the paratenon sheath surrounding the Achilles tendon, reducing adhesion formation between the tendon and paratenon during plantarflexion movements"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric loading of the Achilles tendon stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis and reorganization of the disorganized tendon matrix in tendinopathy. This is the established mechanism underlying the Alfredson eccentric protocol. Eccentric loading does not acutely elevate local glucocorticoids. It does not work through descending pain inhibitory pathways. It does not compress the retrocalcaneal bursa or relax the paratenon sheath mechanically.","boardPrep":"Eccentric calf loading (Alfredson protocol): stand on step, raise on two feet, lower on single affected foot. Mechanism: mechanotransduction in tenocytes → collagen synthesis and fiber reorganization in the tendon matrix. Mid-portion responds better to eccentric loading; insertional may worsen with full ROM eccentric (avoid dorsiflexion past neutral in insertional disease). Multiple RCTs support eccentric loading as first-line PT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric exercise involves muscle-tendon unit contraction while lengthening; eccentric calf lowering places controlled tensile load on the Achilles, stimulating collagen remodeling in tendinopathy through tenocyte mechanotransduction"},{"id":"sdl7_q39","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old male with chronic mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy has had 3 months of eccentric exercises with incomplete improvement. He asks whether corticosteroid injection would speed his recovery. Which response best reflects current evidence regarding corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection is the established gold standard first-line treatment for Achilles tendinopathy and should be offered before any physical therapy is attempted for this patient","B":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the Achilles tendon substance is safe and recommended before attempting any further physical therapy or rehabilitation program","C":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture with intratendinous injection","D":"Corticosteroid injection is beneficial only for insertional Achilles tendinopathy into the retrocalcaneal bursa and is absolutely contraindicated for mid-portion disease","E":"Corticosteroid injection is absolutely contraindicated for all forms of tendinopathy and may only be used after complete tendon rupture and surgical repair has fully healed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture, particularly with intratendinous injection. The evidence for corticosteroid injection in tendinopathy is uncertain. It is not the gold standard first-line treatment — eccentric loading is. Intratendinous injection is specifically dangerous. Corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not the tendon) is appropriate for retrocalcaneal bursitis, but not for mid-portion tendinopathy.","boardPrep":"Corticosteroid injection in Achilles tendinopathy: NOT first-line. Risks include tendon rupture, especially intratendinous injection. Peritendinous injection (into paratenon) carries lower risk. Evidence: may provide short-term pain relief but inferior long-term outcomes vs eccentric loading. The evidence is uncertain. Contrast with retrocalcaneal bursitis: corticosteroid injection into the bursa is an appropriate adjunctive option.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs and corticosteroids: systemic and local corticosteroid effects include tendon collagen weakening and increased rupture risk; intratendinous injection carries the highest risk of subsequent Achilles tendon rupture"},{"id":"sdl7_q40","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old female runner has posterior heel pain worse with dorsiflexion. Tenderness is at the posterior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon with local swelling. No plantar heel tenderness is present. She was previously treated for plantar fasciitis without success. Which finding most reliably distinguishes retrocalcaneal bursitis from plantar fasciitis?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis produces lateral calcaneal tenderness worse with inversion stress while plantar fasciitis produces medial calcaneal tenderness at the plantar fascia enthesis","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles worsened by dorsiflexion, while plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst on first steps","C":"Plantar fasciitis causes pain at the Achilles musculotendinous junction while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at the plantar fascial calcaneal attachment point","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at night while plantar fasciitis is exclusively a first-morning-step (post-static dyskinesia) symptom and not a nocturnal complaint","E":"Plantar fasciitis causes posterior ankle swelling anterior to the Achilles tendon while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes diffuse inferior heel pain with prolonged weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles tendon worsened by dorsiflexion (which compresses the bursa between the calcaneus and the deep Achilles surface). Plantar fasciitis causes inferior/medial heel pain worst with first morning steps (post-static dyskinesia). These two conditions are frequently confused because both involve the heel, but the location of tenderness (posterior deep vs inferior) and the aggravating movement (dorsiflexion vs initial weight-bearing) clearly distinguish them.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis vs plantar fasciitis: retrocalcaneal = posterior calcaneus deep to Achilles, dorsiflexion worsens (compresses bursa); plantar fasciitis = inferior/medial heel, worse first steps in morning (post-static dyskinesia), point tenderness at plantar fascia origin. Course slides: retrocalcaneal bursitis \"often confused with plantar fasciitis.\" Dorsiflexion worsening = key distinguishing feature for bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy; distinguish from plantar fasciitis (inferior heel), Achilles tendinopathy (tendon mid-substance or insertion), and superficial calcaneal bursitis (skin-tendon interface irritated by shoe counter)"},{"id":"sdl7_q41","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male has retrocalcaneal bursitis confirmed on ultrasound. After 3 months of cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching, he has only partial improvement. Which is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Proceed directly to surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as surgery is the definitive treatment and should be offered after any conservative management failure","B":"Begin a 2-week oral prednisone taper combined with 6 weeks of complete non-weight bearing immobilization to reduce residual bursal inflammation via systemic corticosteroid","C":"Administer platelet-rich plasma injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as the current evidence-based second-line treatment endorsed by all major orthopaedic specialty societies","D":"Repeat diagnostic ultrasound to reassess the bursa size and vascularity before initiating any additional therapeutic intervention for this patient with refractory bursitis","E":"Offer corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as an adjunctive treatment option for refractory cases that have failed the initial conservative management program"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After failure of initial conservative management (cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching), the appropriate next step for retrocalcaneal bursitis is corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa — not into the Achilles tendon. Surgery is rarely indicated and not the next step after only 3 months of conservative management. Oral corticosteroid taper is not the standard approach for localized bursitis. PRP is not endorsed as the current second-line standard for retrocalcaneal bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis management ladder: (1) Cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching. (2) Corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa (appropriate, lower rupture risk than intratendinous). (3) Surgery — rarely indicated. Key: injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory bursitis; injection INTO the Achilles tendon is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching are first-line; corticosteroid injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory cases; surgery is rarely needed and reserved for truly refractory cases"},{"id":"sdl7_q42","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female triathlete is diagnosed with retrocalcaneal bursitis. Ultrasound also shows concurrent insertional Achilles tendinopathy at the same site. She asks whether the two conditions are related. Which statement best reflects the pathophysiologic relationship between retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis and insertional Achilles tendinopathy have entirely distinct and unrelated pathophysiologic mechanisms with no consistent clinical association between them","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis invariably develops as a late complication of insertional Achilles tendinopathy and only occurs after tendinopathy has been present for several years","C":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus, as the bursa is mechanically stressed by the same disease process","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis always precedes Achilles tendinopathy and must be fully resolved before initiating any Achilles tendinopathy rehabilitation treatment in athletes","E":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy exclusively affects athletes over age 60 while retrocalcaneal bursitis exclusively affects younger athletes under 30 years of age in sports"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slides state that retrocalcaneal bursitis is \"highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus\" — the same mechanical forces that stress the Achilles tendon also compress the adjacent bursa between the thickened tendon and the calcaneus (or Haglund deformity). They do not have entirely unrelated mechanisms. The two conditions coexist frequently from early in the disease course and should be treated simultaneously with a unified rehabilitation approach.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: the course slide states \"inflammation highly associated with Achilles tendinosis, enthesopathy of the calcaneus.\" The bursa sits between calcaneus and Achilles — when the Achilles is thickened (tendinosis) or the calcaneus is irregular (enthesopathy/Haglund deformity), bursal compression increases. Treat both simultaneously. Haglund deformity = posterosuperior calcaneal prominence causing increased bursal compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional type involves the calcaneal enthesis; the retrocalcaneal bursa lies immediately adjacent to this enthesis and is compressed by the same Achilles forces, explaining their frequent coexistence on ultrasound or MRI"},{"id":"sdl7_q43","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy who plays travel soccer has had Sever disease for 5 months. He has followed activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, cold compresses, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, footwear modification, and a course of physical therapy, but heel pain still stops him from running. Which is the next step in management for this recalcitrant case?","choices":{"A":"Surgical excision of the calcaneal apophysis to remove the painful ossification center so that the Achilles tendon can reattach directly to mature calcaneal bone","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the apophysis at the Achilles insertion to settle the traction inflammation so that the boy can return to full practice this season","C":"Percutaneous tenotomy of the distal Achilles tendon to lengthen the tendon and reduce the tensile load it places on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate","D":"A period of immobilization to unload the apophysis from repetitive Achilles traction, the course option listed for recalcitrant cases of calcaneal apophysitis","E":"Permanent withdrawal from running sports until the apophysis fuses, since continued play leads to growth arrest and a shortened calcaneus in most children"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists activity reduction, footwear modification, heel cushioning, cold compresses, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, and physical therapy as treatment, and immobilization for recalcitrant cases. Sever disease is a traction apophysitis that lasts 3 to 4 years and resolves when the apophysis fuses, so unloading the growth plate is the escalation. Surgical excision of the apophysis is not a treatment for this self-limited condition. Injecting steroid at a pediatric tendon insertion is not part of the course plan. Achilles tenotomy is a procedure for chronic adult tendinopathy, not for a child's apophysis. Permanent withdrawal from sport is unnecessary because the condition resolves at skeletal maturity, and growth arrest is not a described outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sever disease = traction apophysitis of the posteroinferior calcaneus at the Achilles insertion; self-limited, lasts 3–4 years, resolves with apophyseal fusion. COMLEX: Conservative care first; immobilization for recalcitrant cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — other traction apophysitides of the growing athlete"},{"id":"sdl7_q44","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old female gymnast has bilateral Sever disease. Her parents ask about the expected course and long-term prognosis. Which statement is most accurate regarding the natural history of Sever disease?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease requires surgical resection of the apophyseal ossification center to achieve pain-free return to gymnastics and prevent long-term skeletal deformity of the calcaneus","B":"Sever disease resolves within 2 weeks with complete rest and will not recur upon returning to gymnastics if heel cushioning is consistently worn during all athletic activity","C":"Sever disease is a permanent condition that persists throughout adulthood and requires lifelong footwear modification and Achilles stretching to manage symptoms indefinitely","D":"Sever disease will inevitably progress to calcaneal apophyseal stress fracture requiring surgery if the child participates in any form of physical activity during treatment","E":"Sever disease is a self-limited condition that typically lasts 3 to 4 years until the calcaneal apophysis fuses with skeletal maturity, after which symptoms fully resolve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease is self-limited, resolving when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity — a process that takes approximately 3-4 years after onset. Surgery is NEVER indicated because the condition resolves completely without intervention at maturity, leaving no permanent deformity or sequelae. It does not resolve in 2 weeks with rest, nor does it persist permanently into adulthood. Progression to stress fracture is not inevitable with appropriate activity modification and symptom management.","boardPrep":"Sever disease prognosis: self-limited, resolves when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity (3-4 years). No permanent deformity or long-term sequelae. Surgery NOT indicated. Contrast: Legg-Calve-Perthes (AVN, can cause permanent hip deformity) vs Sever (resolves fully at maturity). Osgood-Schlatter is also self-limited at skeletal maturity. Sever disease = purely traction apophysitis with benign prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children is a distinct condition with potential for permanent deformity; unlike LCP, Sever disease resolves completely without sequelae once the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity"},{"id":"sdl7_q45","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has several months of heel pain that started gradually and is worse during and after running practice. Her mother notes the pain is clearly worse on days she wears thin-soled flats or cleats and better in cushioned sneakers. There is tenderness at the posteroinferior calcaneus. Which statement best links this history to the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Pain worse in cleats points to a calcaneal stress fracture, because studs concentrate force on the bone and produce a focal break in the cortex","B":"Pain worse in flat shoes points to plantar fasciitis, because thin soles let the fascia overstretch with each step and pull on the medial tubercle","C":"Insidious, chronic heel pain worse with activity and with footwear lacking heel cushioning is the course presentation of calcaneal apophysitis","D":"Pain linked to shoe type points to retrocalcaneal bursitis, because a rigid heel counter compresses the bursa between the tendon and the bone","E":"Pain worse in thin soles points to tarsal tunnel syndrome, because the flattened arch stretches the tibial nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes Sever disease as insidious in onset, chronic, worse with activity, and worse with footwear lacking heel cushioning; heel cushioning and footwear modification are part of treatment. Tenderness at the posteroinferior calcaneus, the site of the apophysis at the Achilles insertion, fits. Plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst with the first steps of the day. A calcaneal stress fracture would follow a training change and show focal bony tenderness; footwear sensitivity of this kind is the listed feature of apophysitis. Retrocalcaneal bursitis is more common in adults and causes posterosuperior pain worse with dorsiflexion. Tarsal tunnel syndrome is a neuropathic, burning plantar pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sever disease: boys ~8 y, girls ~6 y, 2/3 bilateral; repetitive Achilles traction with running and jumping. COMLEX: Heel cups and cushioned footwear are simple first-line interventions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — how footwear does and does not relate to running injuries"},{"id":"sdl7_q46","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old runner has 6 months of exertional lateral leg pain that begins partway through each run and fades with rest. Exertional compartment syndrome was suspected, but pre- and post-exercise compartment pressure testing is normal. Burning pain radiates to the dorsum of the foot after long runs. According to the course, how should the physician interpret the normal pressures?","choices":{"A":"Normal pressures exclude any structural cause, so the pain should be managed as a functional disorder with reassurance and a gradual return to mileage","B":"Normal pressures point to medial tibial stress syndrome, so a bone scan should be ordered to look for diffuse uptake along the posteromedial tibia","C":"Normal pressures confirm early exertional compartment syndrome, so fasciotomy of the lateral compartment should be scheduled before nerve damage","D":"Normal pressures point to popliteal artery entrapment, so ankle-brachial indices with the foot in forced plantarflexion are the next step","E":"A lower extremity nerve injury should be considered, since it is suspected when the picture fits compartment syndrome but pressures are normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course introduces lower extremity nerve injuries as the diagnosis to consider when the presentation is consistent with exertional compartment syndrome but compartment pressures are normal. The nerves typically involved are the common peroneal, superficial peroneal, and saphenous, and burning pain radiating to the dorsum of the foot suggests a peroneal nerve source; nerve conduction studies and EMG localize the lesion. Normal pressures do not exclude a structural cause. MTSS causes diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness, not lateral burning pain radiating to the foot. Normal pressures argue against, not for, compartment syndrome, so fasciotomy is not indicated. Popliteal artery entrapment is not part of the course differential for this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Common peroneal nerve wraps the fibular neck → foot drop and dorsal foot numbness; superficial peroneal → lateral leg/dorsal foot sensation and eversion. COMLEX: ECS-like leg pain with normal pressures → think nerve entrapment, confirm with NCS/EMG.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — lumbar radiculopathy as another neurogenic source of leg pain"},{"id":"sdl7_q47","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old soccer player has superficial peroneal nerve entrapment at the crural fascia above the lateral malleolus. Over 4 months she has completed running gait analysis, physical therapy, anti-inflammatories, TENS, and a trial of a neuromodulator, and burning pain over the dorsolateral foot persists. Which option does the course list as an invasive next step?","choices":{"A":"Fasciotomy of all four leg compartments, because entrapment of the nerve reflects a chronic rise in compartment pressure that a release will relieve","B":"Transfer of the peroneus longus tendon, because entrapment weakens eversion and a transfer restores the lateral pull that stabilizes the ankle","C":"Ultrasound-guided hydrodissection of the nerve at its fascial exit point, with steroid injection or surgical release as the other invasive options","D":"Below-knee cast immobilization for 6 weeks, because rest of the ankle allows the nerve to remyelinate at the site where it pierces the fascia","E":"Amputation of the sensory branch at the ankle, because cutting the distal nerve stops the burning pain without causing any loss of sensation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides treatment of lower extremity nerve injuries into conservative care (running gait analysis, neuromodulators, TENS, physical therapy, anti-inflammatories) and invasive care (steroid injection, hydrodissection, surgical release). After failed conservative care, hydrodissection of the nerve at its exit through the crural fascia is a listed invasive option. A four-compartment fasciotomy treats compartment syndrome, which is not present. Superficial peroneal entrapment is a sensory problem in this patient with no weakness, so a tendon transfer has no role. Casting is not a listed treatment and would cause stiffness. Cutting the nerve would cause numbness in its distribution and is not a course option.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superficial peroneal nerve pierces the crural fascia ~proximal to the lateral malleolus; entrapment → burning dorsolateral foot and sinus tarsi numbness ± palpable fascial defect. COMLEX: Escalate from gait retraining and neuromodulators to injection, hydrodissection, or surgical release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 6 — other analgesics used for neuropathic and musculoskeletal pain"},{"id":"sdl7_q48","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male runner is diagnosed with saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee. He has pain and numbness in the medial knee region extending to the medial calf. He denies foot drop or ankle weakness. Which statement most accurately characterizes the saphenous nerve and its clinical features?","choices":{"A":"Saphenous nerve injury causes foot drop and decreased sensation over the lateral dorsum of the foot from compression of the nerve at the lateral fibular head on examination","B":"Saphenous nerve injury causes burning pain in the lateral ankle and dorsolateral foot without any motor deficits due to its purely sensory function along the lateral lower leg","C":"Saphenous nerve injury causes complete loss of ankle plantarflexion strength from gastrocnemius and soleus denervation as the nerve innervates the posterior calf compartment","D":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and calf with no motor deficits because it is a purely sensory nerve branch","E":"Saphenous nerve injury causes wrist drop from compression at the medial elbow, as the saphenous nerve is the lower extremity equivalent of the radial nerve in the upper extremity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The saphenous nerve is the terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve and is PURELY SENSORY — it carries no motor fibers. Entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and medial calf without any motor deficit, distinguishing it from the common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) and differentiating it from other lower extremity entrapment neuropathies. Foot drop, plantarflexion weakness, and lateral foot symptoms are all inconsistent with saphenous nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Saphenous nerve: terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve. Location: entrapment at medial knee fascia (between sartorius and gracilis). PURELY SENSORY — no motor deficit (key board fact). Symptoms: pain and numbness at medial knee and medial calf. Compare: common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) vs superficial peroneal (sensory, dorsolateral foot) vs saphenous (purely sensory, medial knee/calf, NO motor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Upper extremity nerve injuries: axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves each carry both motor and sensory fibers; the saphenous nerve is purely sensory (femoral nerve branch) with no motor component, making it analogous to the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm in the upper extremity"}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"Pediatric MSK Conditions","questions":[{"id":"sdl8_q01","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology and recommended screening strategy for developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?","choices":{"A":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40%; bilateral in 37%; left > right; screening recommended at birth and every wellness visit until walking","B":"DDH occurs in 1 per 10,000 live births; mild instability in 10%; bilateral in 75%; right > left; screening recommended only once at birth by specialized pediatric orthopedics","C":"DDH occurs in 10-15 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 60%; right > left; screening with ultrasound is recommended for all infants between 2 and 4 months regardless of exam findings","D":"DDH occurs in 1 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 5%; unilateral in 90% of cases; screening is recommended only when clinical risk factors are identified at birth","E":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 70% of cases; right > left side; screening is deferred until 6 months of age because physical exam before that age is unreliable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40% of neonates (most resolve spontaneously); bilateral in 37%; left > right (due to left occiput anterior fetal positioning in most deliveries). Screening is recommended at birth and at every wellness visit until walking. Sources differ on incidence: the course slide gives 3-5 per 1000, while the CDT Orthopedics reading (SDL 9 p3) gives about 1 in 1000 live births in whites. The incidence is not 1 per 10,000 or 10-15 per 1000. Bilateral in only 37%, not 70%. Right is not more common than left.","boardPrep":"DDH epidemiology: 3-5/1000 live births. Mild instability: 40% (most resolve). Bilateral: 37%. Left > right (likely from left occiput anterior fetal position). Risk factors: female sex, breech position, family history, first-born, oligohydramnios. Screen at birth and every wellness visit until walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q02","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates the Ortolani maneuver from the Barlow maneuver in DDH examination?","choices":{"A":"Ortolani dislocates an unstable hip by applying posterior force with the hip adducted; Barlow reduces a dislocated hip by abducting while applying anterior force on the femoral head","B":"Ortolani reduces a dislocated hip (a clunk indicates the femoral head slipping back into the acetabulum); Barlow dislocates an unstable hip (posteriorly displaceable during the test)","C":"Ortolani tests hip abductor muscle strength in infants over 6 months; Barlow tests hip flexor tightness in infants under 3 months, both requiring radiographic confirmation of findings","D":"Ortolani is a dynamic ultrasound measurement of the acetabular angle; Barlow is a static clinical test that evaluates femoral head position relative to the acetabulum at rest","E":"Ortolani is performed with the hip in extension and external rotation; Barlow is performed with the hip in flexion and adduction to detect posterior hip joint laxity in infants"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ortolani is the REDUCTION maneuver: with the hip flexed and abducted while lifting the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk indicates the dislocated femoral head slipping BACK INTO the acetabulum. Barlow is the DISLOCATION maneuver: adduction while applying posterior force causes the unstable femoral head to slip OUT. The descriptions are not reversed. Both are performed with the hip flexed at 90 degrees. Ortolani does not involve ultrasound measurement or hip extension.","boardPrep":"DDH physical exam: Ortolani = reduction maneuver (hip flexed 90°, abduct while lifting femoral head anteriorly; positive = clunk of femoral head reducing into acetabulum). Barlow = dislocation maneuver (hip flexed 90°, adduct while applying posterior force; positive = femoral head dislocating out posteriorly). Also: Galeazzi test (functional femoral shortening with hips flexed), Klisic test (>3 months), Trendelenburg gait (ambulatory).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q03","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is recommended for the initial workup of developmental dysplasia of the hip, and what determines which modality is chosen?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the hip is the gold standard imaging modality for all ages regardless of the infant's age because it provides superior visualization of cartilaginous and bony hip anatomy","B":"Plain radiographs are preferred at all ages because they provide the most accurate measurement of the acetabular index and femoral head ossification center position in infants","C":"CT scan of the pelvis with radiation shielding is the preferred initial imaging modality to measure the acetabular index and confirm femoral head position in all ages","D":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age (cartilaginous femoral head not visible on X-ray); radiographs are preferred after 4 months when ossification centers appear on imaging","E":"Ultrasound should only be used after 6 months of age once the femoral head begins to ossify; radiographs with the hip in abduction are preferred for the first 6 months of life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age because the femoral head is cartilaginous and cannot be seen on plain X-ray. After 4 months, when the femoral head ossification center appears, plain radiographs become more informative and are preferred. MRI requires sedation and is reserved for complex cases, not first-line imaging. CT scan involves radiation and is not first-line. Ultrasound should NOT be deferred until 6 months — the 4-month threshold is the standard transition point.","boardPrep":"DDH imaging: ultrasound preferred before 4 months (femoral head is cartilaginous and not yet ossified — not visible on plain X-ray). Radiographs after 4 months when ossification centers appear. Hip ultrasound evaluates alpha angle (acetabular coverage) and femoral head position dynamically. Plain X-ray evaluates Shenton line, acetabular index, center-edge angle in older infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q04","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which molecular defect is responsible for the majority of osteogenesis imperfecta cases and what is the inheritance pattern of classical OI?","choices":{"A":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases; classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant","B":"Defects in Type 3 collagen synthesis account for 85% of OI cases; OI is exclusively autosomal recessive and the recessive form tends to be milder than the dominant form","C":"Defects in osteoclast function causing excessive bone resorption account for 85% of OI cases; OI is autosomal dominant with complete penetrance in all affected families","D":"Defects in bone matrix mineralization related to alkaline phosphatase deficiency account for 85% of OI cases; all forms of OI are autosomal recessive with variable expressivity","E":"Defects in fibrillin-1 causing abnormal connective tissue cross-linking account for 85% of OI cases; inheritance is X-linked recessive with severe phenotype in affected males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases (COL1A1 and COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant. Type V (classic) or type III (vascular subtype) collagen defects cause Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, not OI. Osteoclast dysfunction underlies osteopetrosis. Alkaline phosphatase deficiency causes hypophosphatasia. Fibrillin-1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome. OI is not X-linked.","boardPrep":"OI molecular basis: Type 1 collagen defects in 85% (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI = autosomal dominant. Autosomal recessive forms (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex: LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB) = more severe. Osteoblast function and bone matrix mineralization defects account for remaining cases. Type 1 collagen is the primary structural protein of bone, skin, and tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q05","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Sillence type of osteogenesis imperfecta is associated with perinatal lethality and the most severe skeletal deformity?","choices":{"A":"Sillence Type I is the mildest form with blue sclerae, presenile hearing loss, and recurrent childhood fractures, but results in perinatal death in 30% of affected newborns","B":"Sillence Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, and short stature, and causes perinatal death in 50% of affected newborns","C":"Sillence Type II is the perinatal lethal form, characterized by severe bone fragility with multiple fractures and deformities at birth that are incompatible with survival","D":"Sillence Type III is the progressive deforming form, always lethal in the perinatal period due to thoracic kyphoscoliosis and respiratory insufficiency from rib cage deformity","E":"Sillence Type V has calcifying interosseous membranes and hyperplastic callus formation after fractures, and is lethal in the perinatal period from cardiac involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sillence Type II is perinatal lethal, with multiple fractures in utero, accordion rib deformities, and marked bone fragility incompatible with survival beyond the neonatal period. Type I is the mildest form — patients survive with recurrent childhood fractures and blue sclerae. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type — patients survive but with progressive deformity and often wheelchair dependence. Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae. None of the other types are perinatal lethal as a rule.","boardPrep":"Sillence OI Classification: Type I (mild) — blue sclerae, recurrent fractures, presenile hearing loss (30-60%), least severe, normal stature. Type II (perinatal lethal) — multiple fractures in utero, accordion ribs, incompatible with life. Type III (progressive deforming) — most severe non-lethal; short stature; scleral hue varies; dentinogenesis imperfecta and hearing loss common. Type IV (moderate) — white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta. Classic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q06","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pharmacologic therapy is used in osteogenesis imperfecta and where in the skeleton does it exert the greatest benefit?","choices":{"A":"Teriparatide (PTH analog) is the primary bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit in cortical bone at long bone diaphyses rather than in trabecular bone","B":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate) are used in OI treatment and are more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular bone) than for cortical long bones in children with OI","C":"Denosumab is the preferred first-line agent for OI and exerts equal beneficial effects on cortical and trabecular bone, reducing fracture rates in all skeletal compartments equally","D":"High-dose calcium and vitamin D supplementation are the established first-line pharmacologic treatments for OI, exerting their greatest benefit at the cortical long bone level","E":"Romosozumab is the recommended bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit at cortical long bone diaphyses rather than at the vertebral trabecular bone level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate) reduce fracture rates and improve vertebral morphology in OI, with greater benefit demonstrated for trabecular (vertebral) bone than for cortical long bone diaphyses. Prolonged use may paradoxically weaken long bone material properties and increase nonunion risk. Bisphosphonates do NOT correct the underlying collagen defect. They do not provide equal benefit to all skeletal compartments. They are not the only required therapy — multidisciplinary care is essential.","boardPrep":"OI management: multidisciplinary approach — PT, bone-targeted therapies, surgical intervention, dental care, hearing/growth/cardiac monitoring. Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate): reduce fracture rate, improve vertebral shape (trabecular bone benefit), may weaken long bone material properties with prolonged use. Bisphosphonates more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular) than cortical long bones. Bisphosphonates reduce scoliosis incidence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q07","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The CAVE mnemonic describes the four components of talipes equinovarus. Which answer correctly identifies all four components?","choices":{"A":"Calcaneus (hindfoot in excessive dorsiflexion), Adduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted outward), Extension (ankle held in maximal dorsiflexion at rest)","B":"Contracture (of the Achilles tendon), Articular (joint deformity), Varus (foot inverted), Extension (limited plantarflexion caused by shortened anterior compartment muscles)","C":"Cavus (high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (foot inverted), Equinus (ankle held in plantarflexion with dorsiflexion limited by a tight Achilles tendon)","D":"Cavus (abnormally high lateral arch), Abduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted), Equinus (ankle held in excessive plantarflexion from intrinsic muscle shortening)","E":"Curvature (of the spine), Adduction (hip adduction contracture), Varus (knee varus deformity), Extension (limited knee flexion from posterior capsular fibrosis and contracture)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The CAVE mnemonic for clubfoot describes: Cavus (high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (hindfoot inverted), Equinus (limited ankle dorsiflexion). Calcaneus means excessive dorsiflexion — the opposite of equinus. Valgus means eversion — the opposite of varus. Abduction means lateral deviation — the opposite of adduction. The correction order in Ponseti casting follows the CAVE mnemonic from distal to proximal.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus = abnormally high medial arch (midfoot); Adduction = forefoot angled medially (forefoot/midfoot); Varus = foot inverted (hindfoot); Equinus = limited dorsiflexion (hindfoot/ankle). Mnemonic: CAVE = correction order from distal to proximal in Ponseti casting. The hindfoot is in varus and equinus; forefoot/midfoot in cavus and adductus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q08","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the Ponseti method of clubfoot treatment and its expected outcomes?","choices":{"A":"The Ponseti method involves daily casting changes to achieve rapid correction of all four CAVE components simultaneously, followed by immediate full weight-bearing without bracing","B":"The Ponseti method involves a single-stage surgical correction of all four CAVE components in the operating room, followed by 6 weeks of casting and then functional rehabilitation","C":"The Ponseti method involves the French functional method using physiotherapy and taping; Achilles tenotomy is avoided; bracing is not required; outcomes equal surgical release","D":"The Ponseti method involves 2-3 serial casts for positional clubfoot only; idiopathic and syndromic clubfoot always require primary surgical release for acceptable long-term outcomes","E":"The Ponseti method involves weekly serial casting in CAVE order, usually 5-10 casts; Achilles tenotomy in approximately 90% of cases; excellent outcomes at 40 years of follow-up"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Ponseti method uses weekly serial casting to correct deformities in CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus), requiring 5-10 casts; Achilles tenotomy is performed in approximately 90% of cases to correct residual equinus; a foot abduction brace (FAB) follows: 22 hours a day for 3 months, then at night until age 3-5 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading), or 1 to 4 years of bracing per CDT Orthopedics (SDL 9 p3). The method does NOT use daily cast changes. It does NOT begin with a foot abduction brace. It has replaced extensive soft tissue release surgery as the gold standard with excellent 40-year follow-up outcomes.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method: standard treatment for idiopathic clubfoot. Weekly serial casts correcting CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus). 5-10 casts typically required. Achilles tenotomy performed in ~90% of cases (to correct residual equinus). Foot abduction brace (FAB, Dennis-Brown bar): 22 h/day for 3 months, then nights until age 3-5 years (Nelson); CDT Orthopedics: 1-4 years of bracing. Excellent results at 40-year follow-up. Gold standard — preferred over extensive soft tissue release surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q09","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which characteristic best differentiates positional (postural) clubfoot from idiopathic congenital clubfoot?","choices":{"A":"Positional clubfoot always requires surgical correction because it is rigid and cannot be corrected with serial casting; idiopathic clubfoot is flexible and resolves with stretching only","B":"Positional clubfoot is associated with chromosomal anomalies and neuromuscular syndromes such as arthrogryposis; idiopathic clubfoot has no genetic component and is always unilateral","C":"Positional clubfoot is flexible (passively correctable), from intrauterine crowding; idiopathic clubfoot is rigid (not passively correctable) from intrinsic structural abnormality","D":"Positional clubfoot shows all four CAVE components on examination; idiopathic clubfoot shows only equinus and varus without cavus or adductus as the deformity is less complete","E":"Positional clubfoot is more common in males by a 4:1 ratio; idiopathic clubfoot is more common in females and requires immediate surgical repair in the first week of life"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Positional (postural) clubfoot is FLEXIBLE — passively correctable to neutral dorsiflexion and eversion — caused by intrauterine crowding, and treated with parental stretching with excellent prognosis. Idiopathic clubfoot is RIGID — not passively correctable — and requires Ponseti serial casting. The descriptions are not reversed. Positional clubfoot does not require surgery. Syndromic clubfoot (associated with chromosomal or neuromuscular disorders) is rigid and more treatment-resistant. Clubfoot overall has a 2:1 male predominance; the 4:1 figure in the distractor is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot types: Idiopathic (80%) — isolated, rigid, intrinsic structural/vascular foot abnormality, requires Ponseti casting. Syndromic — rigid, associated with chromosomal/neuromuscular conditions (trisomy 18, myelodysplasia, arthrogryposis), more resistant to treatment. Positional — flexible (passively correctable), caused by inadequate intrauterine space, resolves with stretching. KEY: rigid vs flexible determines treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q10","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes metatarsus adductus and its relationship to other pediatric musculoskeletal conditions?","choices":{"A":"Metatarsus adductus is the second most common congenital foot deformity after clubfoot; it has no association with DDH; affects children from 2 to 5 years and is more common in males","B":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in singleton pregnancies; it has a strong association with spinal dysraphism in affected children","C":"Metatarsus adductus is more common in females than males by a 3:1 ratio; it occurs in 7 per 1000 live births in Caucasian infants and always requires surgical correction by age 2 years","D":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins; some studies show an association with DDH; affects infants typically under 1 year of age","E":"Metatarsus adductus is equally common in all populations; it has no known association with DDH; it is caused exclusively by tibial torsion rather than forefoot adduction deformity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins (intrauterine crowding); some studies show an association with DDH (shared etiology of intrauterine molding); and it typically presents in infants under 1 year. It is not second to clubfoot in incidence. The reported association is with DDH, not with spinal dysraphism. It is not exclusively caused by tibial torsion — forefoot adduction is the primary deformity with a neutral hindfoot.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity (incidence difficult to determine due to mild cases). More common in twins (intrauterine crowding). Associated with DDH (both caused by intrauterine molding). Usually in infants <1 year. Causes: intrauterine molding, anatomic variant. Foot: forefoot adduction, hindfoot neutral. Heel bisection line: normally through 2nd web space; in metatarsus adductus, line passes lateral to 2nd web space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q11","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding on a bisection line drawn along the heel is most characteristic of metatarsus adductus?","choices":{"A":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 1st toe, indicating severe forefoot abduction and hindfoot valgus consistent with a rocker-bottom flatfoot deformity of the foot","B":"The bisection of the heel falls between the 3rd and 4th toes, indicating severe midfoot abduction with hindfoot varus consistent with a rigid clubfoot deformity in the infant","C":"The bisection of the heel falls through the center of the 2nd toe, which is the normal finding; deviation to the 1st web space indicates mild metatarsus adductus in newborns","D":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 2nd web space, indicating the normal alignment of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot in a newborn infant during examination","E":"The bisection of the heel falls lateral to the 2nd web space, indicating medial deviation of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot which is maintained in neutral alignment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In metatarsus adductus, the bisection of the heel longitudinally falls LATERAL to the 2nd web space, because the forefoot is deviated medially (adducted) relative to the neutral hindfoot. The normal bisection passes through the 2nd web space. A line medial to the 1st toe would represent severe forefoot abduction (opposite deformity). A line between the 3rd and 4th toes would suggest a more severe varus pattern. Medial to the 2nd web space is the normal finding, not metatarsus adductus.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus exam: bisect the heel longitudinally — normal line passes through 2nd web space. In metatarsus adductus: bisection line falls LATERAL to 2nd web space (forefoot is deviated medially). Hindfoot is neutral (distinguish from clubfoot where hindfoot is also in varus and equinus). Assess flexibility: gentle abduction pressure — flexible = correctable (good prognosis); rigid = requires more aggressive treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q12","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which treatment approach for metatarsus adductus is based on the rigidity of the deformity?","choices":{"A":"All cases of metatarsus adductus require immediate serial casting from birth regardless of flexibility; surgery is performed if casting fails after 6 months of conservative treatment","B":"Flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical metatarsal osteotomy at age 6 months; rigid metatarsus adductus resolves spontaneously with walking without any treatment","C":"Flexible deformity is treated with stretching and parental instruction; recalcitrant cases require serial casting; surgery is rarely indicated and reserved for severe persistent cases","D":"Rigid metatarsus adductus is treated with stretching and reverse-last shoes; flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical correction to prevent in-toe gait in adulthood","E":"Metatarsus adductus always resolves spontaneously without any intervention by age 3 years; no treatment is recommended for any degree of flexibility or rigidity of the deformity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treatment of metatarsus adductus is based on deformity flexibility: flexible deformity is treated with parental stretching exercises at each diaper change; recalcitrant cases receive serial casting; surgery is rare and reserved for severe rigid persistent deformity. All cases do NOT require immediate casting regardless of flexibility. Rigid deformity does not resolve spontaneously with walking. Most mild-to-moderate flexible cases resolve with stretching alone, and the treatment escalation is stepwise.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment: (1) Stretching — gentle forefoot abduction stretching by parents at each diaper change for flexible deformity; (2) Casting — for recalcitrant flexible and mild-moderate rigid cases; (3) Surgery — rarely indicated, for severe rigid persistent deformity usually after age 4. Most mild-moderate cases resolve with stretching. The course slide states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q13","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) from spina bifida occulta (closed spinal dysraphism)?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele opens both the vertebral column and the skin, exposing the meninges and spinal cord; spina bifida occulta has a vertebral defect with intact skin and no exposed neural tissue","B":"Myelomeningocele has an opening only in the skin without vertebral defect; spina bifida occulta has an opening in the vertebral column with meninges exposed but skin is intact over the defect","C":"Myelomeningocele is the milder form with only meningeal protrusion through the vertebral defect without cord involvement; spina bifida occulta involves complete cord exposure and paralysis","D":"Myelomeningocele involves only cerebrospinal fluid leakage through a skin defect without spinal cord exposure; spina bifida occulta involves full cord herniation with meningeal protrusion","E":"Myelomeningocele has no vertebral or skin defect but causes cerebellar herniation below the foramen magnum; spina bifida occulta is defined by herniation through both vertebral and skin defects"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) has an opening in both the vertebral column and the skin with meninges and spinal cord exposed — obvious at birth, requiring immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) has a vertebral defect without skin opening; neural tissue is not exposed and there may only be skin stigmata. The two conditions are not reversed. Meningocele is intermediate — meninges herniate through the vertebral defect but the spinal cord itself is not exposed. Chiari II is a complication of myelomeningocele, not a subtype of spinal dysraphism.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism types: Myelomeningocele (open/spina bifida aperta) = opening in vertebral column AND skin; meninges and spinal cord are exposed; obvious at birth; requires immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) = vertebral column defect WITHOUT skin opening; neural tissue not exposed; may have skin stigmata (tuft of hair, dimple, hemangioma); often incidental finding. Meningocele = meninges herniate through vertebral defect but cord not involved (between the two).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q14","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which etiologic factors are most strongly associated with the development of neural tube defects including spinal dysraphism?","choices":{"A":"Maternal diabetes mellitus and obesity are the only confirmed causes of neural tube defects; dietary folate intake has no proven relationship to neural tube defect prevalence or severity","B":"Retinoid (vitamin A derivative) exposure and alcohol consumption are the only confirmed environmental causes; folic acid supplementation has no effect on neural tube defect incidence","C":"Maternal hypothyroidism and gestational hypertension are the most common preventable causes; folate supplementation is recommended only if there is a previous affected pregnancy","D":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic drug) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes","E":"Paternal smoking and advanced paternal age are the most strongly implicated etiologic factors; maternal nutritional deficiencies have not been shown to increase neural tube defect risk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes of neural tube defects. Folic acid supplementation (400 mcg/day) prevents 50-70% of NTDs (Langman Ch 18). Maternal diabetes and obesity are risk factors but are not the only confirmed causes. Retinoids are teratogenic, but the claim that folate has no effect is false. Paternal factors are not the primary established NTD risk factors.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism etiology: majority are isolated with multifactorial origin. Key causes: (1) Folic acid deficiency — folic acid 400 mcg/day periconceptionally prevents 50-70% of NTDs (Langman Ch 18). (2) Valproate (antiepileptic) — first-trimester exposure carries a 1-2% risk of NTDs (Katzung). Also: chromosomal disorders, other anticonvulsants (carbamazepine). Epidemiology: 1:2875 live births US; Hispanic highest rate (3.80:10,000); AA 2.73:10,000; Caucasian 3.09:10,000.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q15","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which complication of myelomeningocele is characterized by herniation of the cerebellar tonsils and brainstem through the foramen magnum?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome involves downward herniation of the cerebellum through the foramen magnum and causes progressive lower extremity weakness and bladder dysfunction","B":"Hydromyelia involves herniation of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and is the most common complication of all types of spinal dysraphism including spina bifida occulta","C":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is characteristic of myelomeningocele","D":"Syringomyelia involves cerebrospinal fluid accumulation within the brainstem causing compression of cranial nerve nuclei and herniation of brainstem tissue into the foramen magnum","E":"Basilar invagination involves downward herniation of the cerebellar vermis through the foramen magnum and is the most frequent complication of myelomeningocele in neonates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is a near-universal complication of myelomeningocele. It causes CSF flow obstruction, contributing to hydrocephalus. Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but does NOT involve foramen magnum herniation. Hydromyelia is an intramedullary CSF cyst. Syringomyelia is intramedullary fluid. Basilar invagination is upward (odontoid migration), not downward cerebellar herniation.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Chiari II malformation (almost universal in MMC — herniation of cerebellum/brainstem through foramen magnum; causes hydrocephalus, breathing problems, swallowing difficulties). Hydrocephalus (requires VP shunt in most). Tethered cord. Hydromyelia. Seizures. Learning disabilities. Urinary/bowel dysfunction. Skin ulcers. Orthopedic problems (paralysis level-dependent). Prenatal diagnosis: elevated maternal AFP + ultrasound (lemon sign/banana sign on fetal head).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q16","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-week-old female infant, firstborn, delivered vaginally in breech presentation, is examined at her first wellness visit. On hip examination, when the hip is flexed 90° and abducted while the examiner lifts the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk is felt. Which finding is this and what does it indicate?","choices":{"A":"Positive Ortolani sign indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum; this infant requires ultrasound evaluation and pediatric orthopedic referral","B":"Positive Barlow sign indicating that the examiner has dislocated an unstable femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabulum; this is normal in newborns and requires no further workup","C":"Positive Galeazzi sign indicating functional shortening of the femur caused by a higher resting position of the femoral head on the affected side with the hips in a flexed position","D":"Positive Klisic test indicating that the line connecting the greater trochanter and the anterior superior iliac spine points above the umbilicus due to proximal femoral displacement","E":"Positive Thomas test indicating a hip flexion contracture from intrauterine positioning; this resolves spontaneously and does not require imaging or orthopedic referral in newborns"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lifting the femoral head anteriorly while abducting the hip with a palpable clunk is the Ortolani sign, indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum. Barlow involves adduction with posterior force (dislocation maneuver). Galeazzi tests for functional femoral shortening (unequal knee heights). Klisic tests for proximal femoral displacement via a palpated line. Thomas test assesses hip flexion contracture. This infant with multiple DDH risk factors (female, firstborn, breech) requires ultrasound and pediatric orthopedic referral.","boardPrep":"DDH risk factors: female sex, firstborn, breech presentation, family history, oligohydramnios. Ortolani = REDUCTION of a dislocated hip (positive = clunk). Barlow = DISLOCATION of an unstable hip (positive = hip subluxes/dislocates). Both performed with hip flexed 90°. Positive exam → ultrasound if <4 months, X-ray if >4 months → pediatric orthopedics referral. Pavlik harness is first-line treatment for <6 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — SCFE: both SCFE and DDH are high-yield pediatric hip conditions; DDH presents in infancy with instability; SCFE presents in adolescence with hip/knee pain and limited internal rotation; both require urgent orthopedic referral to prevent avascular necrosis"},{"id":"sdl8_q17","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl born breech is examined at a wellness visit. With the infant supine and both hips and knees flexed so that the feet rest side by side on the table, the left knee sits lower than the right. Which interpretation of this finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A positive Ortolani sign, showing that a dislocated left femoral head has slipped back into the acetabulum with a palpable clunk","B":"A positive Barlow sign, showing that the left femoral head can be pushed posteriorly out of the acetabulum under gentle adduction","C":"A positive Klisic test, the line from the anterior iliac spine to the trochanter passing below the umbilicus in an older infant","D":"A normal finding in breech infants, since physiologic hip flexion contractures make knee heights unequal for the first 6 months","E":"A positive Galeazzi test, a functional shortening of the left femur listed among DDH findings in infants under 3 months"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With both hips and knees flexed and the feet together, a lower knee on one side is the Galeazzi sign, which the course lists for infants younger than 3 months with DDH as functional shortening of the femur, alongside hip instability on testing and asymmetric leg creases. A displaced femoral head sits more posteriorly, so the thigh looks shorter. Ortolani and Barlow are dynamic maneuvers that reduce or dislocate the hip with a palpable clunk; they are not judged by knee height. The Klisic test is listed for infants older than 3 months. Unequal knee heights are not a normal breech finding and, with a breech history, call for DDH work-up (ultrasound before 4 months) and pediatric orthopedic referral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DDH exam by age: <3 months = Barlow/Ortolani instability, asymmetric creases, Galeazzi; >3 months = limited abduction, femoral shortening, Klisic; walking = Trendelenburg gait, limp, hyperlordosis. COMLEX: Risk factors include breech, female sex, firstborn; left > right.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip X-rays in the pediatric hip pain workflow"},{"id":"sdl8_q18","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-month-old boy began walking at 14 months. His parents report he has an unusual gait. Exam shows a Trendelenburg gait, prominent right greater trochanter, shortened right leg, and hyperlordosis. Ortolani and Barlow tests are negative at this age. Which diagnosis is most likely and what is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease presenting with avascular necrosis of the femoral head; initiate non-weight bearing immediately and order MRI to assess the degree of femoral head involvement","B":"Developmental dysplasia of the hip presenting in an ambulatory toddler; order an anteroposterior pelvis radiograph and refer urgently to pediatric orthopedics for evaluation","C":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis causing a valgus deformity of the femoral head; obtain radiographs in the frog-leg lateral view and refer to orthopedics for immediate surgical pinning","D":"Cerebral palsy causing spastic hip adductor contracture and gait abnormality; refer to neurology for EMG testing and initiate physical therapy with botulinum toxin injection","E":"Normal developmental gait variation in a toddler; reassure the parents that hyperlordosis and mild leg length discrepancy are expected findings in newly ambulatory children at this age"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trendelenburg gait, prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, and hyperlordosis in a 16-month-old walking toddler are classic signs of undiagnosed ambulatory DDH. Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers become negative in ambulatory children because the hip capsule tightens. LCP (avascular necrosis) presents between ages 4-9 with hip pain (course slides SDL 5 p27, SDL 10 p24), not Trendelenburg gait from birth. SCFE affects adolescents with hip and knee pain and limited internal rotation. Normal developmental variation does not explain a shortened leg with prominent greater trochanter.","boardPrep":"DDH in ambulatory children: Ortolani/Barlow are negative once the hip capsule tightens. Presentation: Trendelenburg gait (positive Trendelenburg sign = hip abductor weakness on affected side), prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, hyperlordosis, out-toeing, toe-heel walking. The course slides list these as ambulatory DDH findings. Imaging: plain X-ray. Undiagnosed DDH → early OA, chronic pain. Early referral to Pediatric Orthopedics is emphasized in SDL 8.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: AVN of the femoral head in children 4-8yr with insidious hip pain and antalgic gait; distinguishes from DDH by age of presentation and radiographic findings of femoral head fragmentation rather than acetabular dysplasia"},{"id":"sdl8_q19","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl presents with multiple long bone fractures following minimal trauma. Her sclerae appear distinctly blue. She has small, discolored teeth. Her mother reports she has had 6 fractures in her lifetime. Her father also had multiple childhood fractures. Skeletal survey shows osteopenia with cortical thinning. Which diagnosis and Sillence type best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type II (perinatal lethal), characterized by multiple in-utero fractures and accordion rib deformity; survival beyond the neonatal period essentially never occurs","B":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type III (progressive deforming), characterized by the most severe non-lethal course; sclerae vary from blue to white; most affected individuals are wheelchair-bound","C":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type I (mild), characterized by blue sclerae, recurrent childhood fractures with minimal trauma, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant inheritance","D":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type IV (moderately severe), characterized by white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, short stature, and autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","E":"Child abuse (non-accidental trauma), which must be excluded before diagnosing any skeletal dysplasia; blue sclerae and dental abnormalities are not features of osteogenesis imperfecta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multiple long bone fractures from minimal trauma combined with blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant family history in a 3-year-old = OI Type I (mild). Type II is perinatal lethal — this 3-year-old child is alive. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type with patients often wheelchair-bound; sclerae vary. Type IV has white sclerae and is moderately severe. Child abuse must always be excluded, but blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and diffuse osteopenia are strongly suggestive of OI.","boardPrep":"OI Type I (mild): blue sclerae + recurrent fractures in childhood + presenile hearing loss (30-60%). Family history often positive (AD). Dentinogenesis imperfecta in some. Normal or near-normal stature. KEY diagnostic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness. OI vs NAT: OI can mimic child abuse; blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and osteopenia help distinguish. Always consider OI when fractures are unexplained or disproportionate to trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Pediatric vs adult bone: pediatric bone has greater porosity, periosteal vascularity, and growth plate; OI disrupts collagen-based bone matrix globally; Salter-Harris fractures in OI are complicated by the underlying bone fragility and abnormal collagen scaffold"},{"id":"sdl8_q20","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A genetics counselor explains the inheritance of osteogenesis imperfecta to a family. The affected child has a new (de novo) pathogenic variant in COL1A1. The parents are unaffected. Which statement is most accurate regarding OI inheritance?","choices":{"A":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant with common de novo mutations; recessive forms involve the collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation pathway and tend to be more severe","B":"OI is exclusively autosomal recessive; both parents must carry one pathogenic variant to have an affected child; the recessive form is always milder than autosomal dominant variants","C":"OI is X-linked recessive; only males are severely affected; female carriers have mild bone fragility; the COL1A1 gene is located on the X chromosome and encodes alpha-1 collagen chains","D":"OI follows a codominant inheritance pattern; all forms have equal severity regardless of the number of pathogenic variants inherited; de novo mutations are extremely rare in OI","E":"OI is mitochondrial in inheritance; transmission is exclusively maternal; paternal contribution has no effect on OI severity or phenotype in any of the known molecular subtypes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant; de novo COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations are common, explaining why an affected child can have two unaffected parents. Autosomal recessive OI forms (LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB mutations affecting collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation) tend to produce more severe phenotypes. OI is NOT exclusively autosomal recessive. It is NOT X-linked recessive — males and females are equally affected by classical forms. It is NOT mitochondrial in inheritance.","boardPrep":"OI genetics: Classical OI (Types I-IV) = autosomal dominant (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). De novo mutations common — unaffected parents can have affected child. Autosomal recessive forms = LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex) → more severe phenotype. Type V = autosomal dominant (IFITM5). Parental mosaicism can cause recurrence risk even when parents appear unaffected. Genetic counseling is essential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens and abnormal MSK development: genetic mutations (AD, AR) cause intrinsic developmental abnormalities; OI results from dominant negative or haploinsufficiency mechanisms in Type 1 collagen synthesis analogous to how teratogens disrupt normal developmental signaling"},{"id":"sdl8_q21","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with osteogenesis imperfecta type III uses a wheelchair, has severe kyphoscoliosis and vertebral compression, and is followed by a multidisciplinary team. His parents ask which organ system accounts for most of the serious morbidity and mortality in OI. Which answer is best supported by the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Renal, because hypercalciuria from rapid bone turnover produces nephrocalcinosis and chronic kidney disease by adolescence","B":"Hepatic, because abnormal type I collagen in the liver capsule leads to progressive portal fibrosis and early cirrhosis","C":"Hematologic, because marrow space is crowded out by fracture callus, producing pancytopenia and recurrent sepsis","D":"Gastrointestinal, because weak collagen in the bowel wall causes recurrent spontaneous perforation in childhood","E":"Cardiopulmonary, with recurrent pneumonias and declining lung function in childhood and cor pulmonale in adults"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The OI chapter assigned for this SDL states that the morbidity and mortality of OI are cardiopulmonary: recurrent pneumonias and declining pulmonary function in childhood and cor pulmonale in adults. A small, deformed thorax and kyphoscoliosis restrict ventilation, and the course slides also list respiratory insufficiency, cardiac valve insufficiency, and aortic dilatation, which is why the cardiovascular system is monitored. Renal stone disease is not the listed cause. A hepatic, hematologic, or gastrointestinal process is not described as the main source of morbidity in OI. The chapter lists neurologic complications such as basilar invagination as additional, less common problems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OI = defective type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2, mostly AD) → fragile bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss, dentinogenesis imperfecta; death mainly from cardiopulmonary failure. COMLEX: Multidisciplinary care with PT, bone-targeted therapy, dental, hearing, growth, and cardiovascular monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — type I collagen in bone, tendon, and ligament"},{"id":"sdl8_q22","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn male is delivered at term and found to have bilateral rigid clubfoot on examination. The foot shows high medial arch, forefoot adduction, hindfoot inversion, and limited ankle dorsiflexion. The deformity is not passively correctable. Which is the most appropriate first step in management?","choices":{"A":"Schedule immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) in the first week of life to achieve complete correction before the tissues become too fibrotic","B":"Apply a rigid foot abduction brace (Dennis-Brown bar) immediately at birth without prior casting, worn 22 hours per day for 4 years to correct all deformity components","C":"Observe without treatment for 3-6 months to determine whether the deformity will resolve spontaneously, as most rigid clubfoot cases achieve spontaneous correction by 6 months","D":"Initiate the Ponseti method with serial casting beginning within the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes for 5-10 weeks","E":"Perform genetic testing and karyotype analysis before initiating any orthopedic treatment, as all rigid clubfoot requires syndromic workup to guide the appropriate treatment plan"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rigid bilateral idiopathic clubfoot requires Ponseti serial casting beginning in the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes. Immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) is the historical approach that Ponseti has replaced as the standard of care. Foot abduction brace alone without prior casting is incorrect — the brace is applied AFTER casting. Rigid clubfoot will NOT resolve spontaneously. Genetic testing is not required before initiating treatment for isolated bilateral clubfoot.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot management: Ponseti method is the standard first-line treatment for idiopathic clubfoot — start EARLY (first week of life). Serial casting in CAVE correction order (cavus first → adductus → varus → equinus). 5-10 weekly casts. Achilles tenotomy in ~90% to correct residual equinus. Foot abduction brace (FAB/Dennis-Brown) 22 h/day for 3 months, then at night until age 3-5 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading). NEVER start with FAB alone. Early surgery is NOT first-line; Ponseti method has replaced extensive soft tissue release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — Fracture nomenclature and casting: serial casting and cast immobilization are orthopedic techniques applied across multiple pediatric MSK conditions; Ponseti casting for clubfoot uses the same principles of controlled mechanical force over sequential intervals to achieve progressive correction"},{"id":"sdl8_q23","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-week-old with idiopathic clubfoot has completed 8 serial Ponseti casts. The cavus, adductus, and varus components are fully corrected, but 20 degrees of equinus deformity persists. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy to correct the residual equinus deformity; this is needed in approximately 90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases treated with Ponseti casting","B":"Apply a posterior night splint with the foot in maximum dorsiflexion for 3 months to stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the residual equinus without any surgical intervention","C":"Continue serial casting for an additional 10 weeks with the foot in progressive dorsiflexion to achieve correction of residual equinus without any tenotomy or surgical procedure","D":"Proceed with open surgical posteromedial release to address the residual equinus as this component always requires surgical correction when casting has not fully achieved correction","E":"Fit the foot abduction brace immediately without addressing the residual equinus, as the brace will passively stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the equinus over time alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Residual equinus after correction of cavus, adductus, and varus with casting is treated with percutaneous Achilles tenotomy in approximately 90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases. A posterior splint alone cannot overcome a contracted Achilles tendon. Additional casting cannot achieve what the tenotomy provides for residual equinus. Open posteromedial release is not indicated when only equinus persists — targeted percutaneous tenotomy is the appropriate procedure. The foot abduction brace cannot passively correct an unresolved equinus deformity.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method — Achilles tenotomy: performed in ~90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases when residual equinus persists after casting is complete (cavus, adductus, and varus corrected). Percutaneous tenotomy under local anesthesia in clinic or OR. Final long leg cast worn for 3-4 weeks post-tenotomy (Nelson) to allow healing in corrected position. Then → foot abduction brace. Tenotomy avoids the need for extensive open soft tissue surgery. Long-term outcomes are excellent at 40-year follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the Achilles tendon is the most powerful plantarflexor; in clubfoot, a contracted Achilles is the primary cause of residual equinus; percutaneous tenotomy achieves correction that serial casting alone cannot overcome in most cases"},{"id":"sdl8_q24","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy with idiopathic clubfoot finished Ponseti casting and had a percutaneous heel cord tenotomy, followed by a long leg cast for 3 weeks. The foot is now fully corrected. His parents ask what happens next and what most affects whether the deformity comes back. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"No further treatment is needed once correction is complete, because the tenotomy permanently lengthens the heel cord and holds the foot in place","B":"An abduction brace worn 22 hours a day for 3 months, then at night until age 3 to 5; poor brace compliance is the main cause of recurrence","C":"Weekly casting continues until walking age, because the foot relapses unless it stays casted until weight bearing remodels the tarsal bones","D":"A posteromedial surgical release is scheduled at 12 months, because Ponseti correction is temporary and most feet need release to stay aligned","E":"Annual radiographs guide whether bracing is needed, because talocalcaneal parallelism on films predicts which corrected feet will relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the clubfoot chapter for this SDL, Ponseti casting and tenotomy are followed by a long leg cast for 3 to 4 weeks, then an abduction brace (bars and shoes) worn 22 hours a day for 3 months and at night until age 3 to 5. Compliance with bracing is essential because recurrence is common if the brace is not worn as recommended. Correction is not maintained without bracing. Casting is not continued until walking age. Surgical release is reserved for feet that fail minimally invasive treatment or for rigid syndromic and neuromuscular clubfeet, and the Ponseti results are excellent at up to 40 years. Radiographs are not recommended for idiopathic clubfoot and have not been shown to predict relapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clubfoot (CAVE: cavus, adductus, varus, equinus): Ponseti weekly casts (5–10) → Achilles tenotomy (~90%) → abduction bracing; up to 20% need tibialis anterior transfer for dynamic supination. COMLEX: Examine the spine for occult dysraphism in every infant with clubfoot.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — clubfoot is common in myelodysplasia, so inspect the spine"},{"id":"sdl8_q25","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old infant is noted to have bilateral medial deviation of the forefoot during a well-child visit. The hindfoot is in neutral alignment. On examination, both feet are fully correctable to neutral with gentle lateral abduction pressure. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Apply bilateral serial casts immediately regardless of flexibility, as all metatarsus adductus deformities require casting to prevent in-toe gait and shoe wear problems in childhood","B":"Refer immediately to pediatric orthopedics for surgical metatarsal osteotomy to correct the flexible forefoot adduction before 6 months of age to prevent long-term gait abnormality","C":"Order bilateral lower extremity radiographs to quantify the metatarsus adductus angle before initiating any treatment or counseling the family about prognosis or management","D":"Apply bilateral reverse-last shoes immediately to reverse the forefoot adduction passively over 6 months, as parental stretching alone is insufficient for all metatarsus adductus cases","E":"Teach parents forefoot abduction stretching exercises at each diaper change; flexible metatarsus adductus has an excellent prognosis and typically resolves without further intervention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bilateral flexible metatarsus adductus (fully correctable with gentle lateral pressure) in a 4-month-old is treated with parental forefoot abduction stretching at each diaper change; most cases resolve without further intervention. Serial casting is not indicated for completely flexible deformity at this age. Surgery is not indicated for flexible deformity. Radiographs are not required before initiating treatment for a clinical diagnosis of flexible metatarsus adductus. Reverse-last shoes may be an option but parental stretching is the appropriate first step.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment algorithm: Flexible (passively correctable) → stretching by parents at every diaper change; most resolve spontaneously. Rigid (not correctable) → serial casting. Symptomatic residual deformity → surgery (rare), generally delayed until age 4-6 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading). The course slide states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\" Reassurance is key for flexible cases; excellent prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Talipes equinovarus: rigid clubfoot also begins management with serial casting (Ponseti method); the key principle is that deformity flexibility determines treatment; flexible metatarsus adductus (stretching) vs rigid clubfoot (Ponseti casting) exemplifies this principle"},{"id":"sdl8_q26","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-month-old has an inward-curving left foot. The examiner notes that the midfoot and hindfoot are normal, ankle dorsiflexion is full, and the subtalar joint moves normally. Which additional finding on the plantar view best fits metatarsus adductus rather than clubfoot?","choices":{"A":"A rigid equinus with the heel held high and a deep posterior crease, so the ankle cannot be brought to neutral dorsiflexion","B":"A heel in fixed varus with a cavus arch and a medial crease, so the sole faces inward and the foot cannot be passively everted","C":"A rocker-bottom sole with the talus palpable plantarly and the forefoot dorsiflexed, the shape of a congenital vertical talus","D":"A bean-shaped foot with a convex lateral border and a prominent fifth metatarsal base, while the hindfoot remains neutral","E":"A foot held in dorsiflexion and eversion with its dorsum touching the anterolateral leg, the appearance of calcaneovalgus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson chapter for this SDL describes metatarsus adductus as adduction of the forefoot with a normal midfoot and hindfoot: the lateral border of the foot is convex, the base of the fifth metatarsal is prominent, the plantar view is bean-shaped, and ankle and subtalar motion are normal. Fixed equinus and hindfoot varus with cavus are components of clubfoot (CAVE), which this infant lacks. A rocker-bottom sole with dorsal dislocation of the midfoot and fixed equinus describes congenital vertical talus. A dorsiflexed, everted foot touching the anterolateral leg is calcaneovalgus, another molding deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metatarsus adductus = most common congenital foot deformity; forefoot adducted, hindfoot neutral; heel bisector falls lateral to the second toe; bilateral in ~50%. COMLEX: Intrauterine molding → always examine the hips (some studies link MA with DDH).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — clubfoot CAVE deformity for comparison"},{"id":"sdl8_q27","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-month-old walking child has metatarsus adductus that corrects only to neutral, not into abduction, with passive pressure. Parents have done office-taught stretching for several months, and they have tried putting the shoes on the opposite feet, with little change in the heel bisector. Which is the next nonoperative option described in the course reading before serial casting?","choices":{"A":"Observation without treatment, because a deformity that corrects to neutral will resolve on its own once walking begins","B":"Reverse-last shoes worn full time, about 22 hours a day, with the heel bisector line reassessed after 4 to 6 months","C":"Midfoot osteotomy now, because walking children have lost the remodeling potential that nonoperative care needs","D":"A night splint holding the foot in adduction, because positioning the forefoot medially relaxes the tight lateral structures","E":"Botulinum toxin to the abductor hallucis, because a dynamic great toe deformity is the main driver of the adducted forefoot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Nelson chapter bases metatarsus adductus treatment on rigidity. Feet that overcorrect into abduction can be observed; feet that correct only to neutral get stretching, and in a walking child the shoes can be reversed. If that fails, reverse-last shoes that hold the foot abducted are worn full time (22 hours a day) and the foot is rechecked in 4 to 6 months; if there is no improvement, serial casts are considered. Observation is for feet that overcorrect into abduction. Surgery is reserved for symptomatic residual deformity and is generally delayed until 4 to 6 years of age. A splint holding the foot in adduction would reinforce the deformity. A dynamic hallux varus usually improves spontaneously and needs no active treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metatarsus adductus ladder: observe (overcorrects) → stretching/reversed shoes (to neutral) → reverse-last shoes → serial casting (best before 8 months) → surgery rarely, at 4–6 years. COMLEX: Track the heel bisector line to monitor improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — footwear prescription principles"},{"id":"sdl8_q28","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pregnant woman at 16 weeks of gestation has elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) on routine screening. Subsequent targeted ultrasound is performed. Which condition is elevated AFP most commonly associated with in this context and what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Elevated AFP is specifically associated with closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) where neural tissue is not exposed; MRI of the maternal spine is the recommended next step","B":"Elevated AFP is exclusively associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21); amniocentesis for karyotype is the recommended next step when AFP is elevated on routine prenatal screening","C":"Elevated AFP is associated with open neural tube defects including myelomeningocele; targeted ultrasound to look for a spinal defect and Chiari II malformation findings is next","D":"Elevated AFP is associated with placental insufficiency and intrauterine growth restriction; umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry is the recommended next step for elevated AFP levels","E":"Elevated AFP is a normal finding in pregnancy and does not require any additional workup unless it is elevated more than 10 multiples of the median for the gestational age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elevated maternal serum AFP is a biochemical marker for OPEN neural tube defects including myelomeningocele — the exposed neural tissue allows AFP to leak into the amniotic fluid and then into maternal serum. The next step is targeted ultrasound looking for lemon sign, banana sign, and absent cisterna magna. Closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) does NOT cause elevated AFP because skin is intact. Down syndrome is associated with LOW AFP (not elevated). Placental insufficiency does not specifically elevate AFP.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism prenatal diagnosis: Elevated maternal serum AFP (MSAFP) → open neural tube defects (open NTDs expose neural tissue and AFP leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal serum). Targeted ultrasound: \"lemon sign\" (scalloping of frontal bones), \"banana sign\" (cerebellar compression/Chiari II), absence of normal cisterna magna. Amniocentesis: amniotic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase confirm open NTD. Closed dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) = AFP is NORMAL (skin is intact, no leakage).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens: valproate and folic acid deficiency cause neural tube defects; elevated AFP is the biochemical marker for open NTDs on prenatal screening; folic acid supplementation 400-800mcg periconceptionally reduces NTD risk by 50-70%"},{"id":"sdl8_q29","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn with myelomeningocele at the L3 level undergoes surgical closure within 24 hours of birth. At 2 weeks of age, she develops a rapidly enlarging head circumference, irritability, and a bulging fontanelle. Which complication is most likely responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome causing progressive lower extremity weakness and urinary retention from re-tethering of the spinal cord at the surgical repair site in the neonatal period","B":"Wound dehiscence at the surgical closure site allowing CSF leakage and ascending bacterial meningitis causing fever, bulging fontanelle, and progressive neurological deterioration","C":"Basilar invagination compressing the brainstem from upward migration of the odontoid process through the foramen magnum causing acute obstructive hydrocephalus and apnea","D":"Syringomyelia with progressive intramedullary cyst expansion causing the head enlargement by compressing the aqueduct of Sylvius and disrupting normal cerebrospinal fluid dynamics","E":"Hydrocephalus from obstructed CSF flow due to the associated Chiari II malformation; most children with myelomeningocele develop it and need a ventriculoperitoneal shunt"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Progressive head enlargement with bulging fontanelle after myelomeningocele surgical closure = hydrocephalus from Chiari II-related obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum, requiring ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement. Hydrocephalus is commonly associated with myelomeningocele (CDT Orthopedics; course slide p36 lists it as a complication). Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but not enlarging head circumference. Wound dehiscence with meningitis would cause fever and sepsis. Basilar invagination is upward odontoid migration. Syringomyelia is an intramedullary cord cyst.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Hydrocephalus is common — caused by Chiari II obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum. Presentation: enlarging head, bulging fontanelle, sunset sign (downward gaze), vomiting, irritability. Treatment: ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt. Chiari II malformation: downward herniation of cerebellum and brainstem through foramen magnum — hallmark of myelomeningocele (almost universal). Also: tethered cord, hydromyelia, seizures, urinary dysfunction, orthopedic problems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: CSF flow obstruction is a shared mechanism in some spinal conditions; in myelomeningocele, Chiari II obstructs CSF at the foramen magnum causing hydrocephalus; in spinal stenosis, CSF is compressed at the lumbar level causing cauda equina syndrome"},{"id":"sdl8_q30","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with a repaired lumbar myelomeningocele has weakness of the calf muscles on both sides. Over the past year her right foot has come to rest dorsiflexed and everted, and she bears weight on her heel. Which mechanism best explains this foot deformity?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine molding from oligohydramnios, the cause of the newborn calcaneovalgus foot that resolves within the first weeks","B":"Neuromuscular imbalance with weak or paralyzed gastrocsoleus, so unopposed dorsiflexors pull the foot into calcaneovalgus","C":"Folate deficiency in mid-childhood, which softens the calcaneus and lets body weight press the hindfoot into dorsiflexion","D":"Tethering of the cord that lengthens the Achilles tendon directly, so plantarflexion fails even though the calf is innervated","E":"Chiari II compression of the brainstem, which produces a spastic dorsiflexed foot through upper motor neuron involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Orthopedic problems are a listed complication of spinal dysraphism. The Nelson foot chapter for this SDL notes that a calcaneovalgus deformity in an older child typically reflects a neuromuscular imbalance with weakness or paralysis of the gastrocsoleus, as in myelomeningocele; with the plantarflexors weak, the dorsiflexors pull the foot into dorsiflexion and eversion. Intrauterine molding causes the newborn calcaneovalgus foot, which resolves early and would not appear at age 6. Folate deficiency is a periconceptional risk factor for neural tube defects, not a cause of childhood foot deformity. Tethered cord causes progressive neurologic loss, not direct tendon lengthening. Chiari II is a hindbrain malformation linked to hydrocephalus and does not explain a lower motor neuron calf weakness pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelomeningocele (open NTD, ↑ AFP) complications: Chiari II, hydrocephalus, tethered cord, hydromyelia, urinary/GI problems, skin ulcers, orthopedic deformities (clubfoot, calcaneovalgus). COMLEX: Deficits depend on lesion level; care is multidisciplinary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — neural tube defects and folate as the preventable risk factor"}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"Lower Extremity Fractures","questions":[{"id":"sdl9_q01","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The region of a long bone located between the epiphysis and the diaphysis, containing predominantly cancellous bone, a rich vascular network, and the growth plate in skeletally immature individuals, is best described as which anatomical zone?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, the flared zone between the physis and diaphysis that is most metabolically active, most vascular, and the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding in children","B":"The epiphysis, the bony terminus at each end of a long bone that is covered by hyaline articular cartilage and separated from the metaphysis by the cartilaginous growth plate","C":"The diaphysis, the elongated tubular shaft of a long bone composed predominantly of dense cortical bone surrounding the central medullary canal filled with yellow marrow in adults","D":"The periosteum, the fibrous vascular membrane covering the outer surface of cortical bone that contains osteoprogenitor cells responsible for appositional bone growth and fracture healing","E":"The endosteum, the thin cellular layer lining the inner medullary canal and trabecular surfaces that contains osteoclasts and osteoblasts involved in bone remodeling and marrow maintenance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis is the flared zone adjacent to the physis, rich in cancellous bone and blood flow. Its sluggish capillary sinusoids and lack of phagocytic lining cells make it the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. The epiphysis forms the articular end; the diaphysis is the compact cortical shaft; the periosteum and endosteum are membranous coverings rather than discrete anatomical zones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metaphysis = most vascular zone = osteomyelitis in kids. Diaphysis = yellow marrow in adults. COMLEX: Growth plate at metaphyseal-epiphyseal junction is the weakest point in pediatric bone under stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q02","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In skeletally mature adults, the medullary canal of the femoral diaphysis is occupied predominantly by which type of tissue, and what does this represent compared to the composition found during fetal and early childhood development?","choices":{"A":"Red marrow containing hematopoietic precursor cells, representing the persistence of fetal marrow composition throughout adult life in order to meet the high metabolic demand of erythropoiesis in large bones","B":"Yellow marrow composed primarily of adipocytes, representing the normal age-related conversion from hematopoietically active red marrow that occupied the diaphysis during fetal development and early childhood","C":"Periosteal fibrous tissue that migrates inward from the cortical surface to fill the medullary space as endosteal remodeling reduces the diameter of the medullary canal with advancing age","D":"Cartilaginous matrix derived from retained physeal remnants that persists within the medullary canal after growth plate closure and gradually undergoes endochondral ossification in older adults","E":"Dense cortical bone that fills the medullary canal as endosteal apposition exceeds resorption over decades, progressively reducing marrow volume and increasing bone stiffness in older adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Red marrow progressively converts to yellow marrow during childhood, proceeding from distal to proximal and from peripheral to axial skeleton. By adulthood, only the axial skeleton and proximal femur and humerus retain significant red marrow. Reconversion of yellow to red marrow occurs in severe anemia, hematologic malignancy, and physiologic stress. Fetal and neonatal bones contain exclusively red marrow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red to yellow marrow conversion: distal to proximal, peripheral to axial. Red marrow persists in: vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, proximal femur/humerus. COMLEX: Yellow marrow reconversion in hemolytic anemia — boards favorite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q03","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The structural unit of compact cortical bone consists of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central vascular channel, together forming a cylindrical unit aligned with the long axis of the bone. What is this unit called?","choices":{"A":"The trabecula, a thin rod or plate of cancellous bone forming an interconnected lattice oriented along lines of mechanical stress in the metaphysis and epiphysis of long bones","B":"The lacuna, the small ovoid cavity within mineralized bone matrix that houses an individual osteocyte and connects to adjacent lacunae via a network of microscopic canalicular channels","C":"The Volkmann canal, a channel that runs oblique or perpendicular to the bone axis connecting adjacent Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteal and endosteal surfaces","D":"The canaliculus, a submicroscopic channel extending between adjacent osteocyte lacunae that allows cytoplasmic process contact and nutrient diffusion throughout the mineralized matrix","E":"The osteon, also called the Haversian system, in which concentric bony lamellae surround a central Haversian canal containing arterioles, venules, and unmyelinated nerve fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon is the basic structural unit of compact bone. Each osteon consists of concentric lamellae surrounding a central Haversian canal (Pawlina Ch 8). Volkmann canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals and connect them to the periosteum. Lacunae house osteocytes; canaliculi connect adjacent lacunae. Trabeculae are the structural units of cancellous bone, not compact bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteon = Haversian system. Concentric lamellae around central vascular canal. Osteocytes in lacunae communicate via canaliculi. COMLEX: Cortical bone remodeling disrupts osteons; cutting cones of osteoclasts tunnel through existing osteons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q04","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A long-bone fracture in which the fracture line wraps in a helical pattern around the shaft is characterized by which mechanism and what is the appropriate fracture nomenclature?","choices":{"A":"A spiral fracture caused by a torsional force applied along the long axis of the bone, in which the fracture propagates along a helical path of maximal shear stress","B":"A transverse fracture caused by a direct bending force perpendicular to the bone axis, in which the fracture line crosses the diaphysis at approximately 90 degrees to the long axis","C":"An oblique fracture caused by combined axial loading and angular force, in which the fracture line runs diagonally across the shaft at roughly 45 degrees to the long axis","D":"A comminuted fracture caused by high-energy direct impact, in which three or more fracture fragments are produced at a single level and the fracture cannot be reduced to two main fragments","E":"A segmental fracture caused by two separate high-energy impacts at different levels, in which an isolated middle bone segment is created between two distinct fracture lines"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spiral fractures result from torsional or rotational forces. The helical fracture line follows the plane of maximal shear stress around the bone. Transverse fractures result from three-point bending. Oblique fractures result from combined loading. Comminuted fractures have three or more fragments. Segmental fractures have two separate fracture levels isolating a middle fragment. All of these terms are part of standard fracture nomenclature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spiral fracture = torsion. Classic boards scenario: spiral tibial fracture in a toddler from a twisting fall — \"toddler fracture.\" Spiral humerus fracture from arm-twisting is also a child abuse red flag. COMLEX: Document mechanism; mechanism predicts fracture pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q05","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When describing a fracture radiographically, which term specifically refers to the directional relationship between the distal bone fragment and the proximal fragment, expressed as the direction the apex of the angle formed by the two fragments points?","choices":{"A":"Displacement, which refers to the lateral translation of one fragment relative to the other measured in millimeters or as a percentage of the bone shaft diameter at the fracture site","B":"Shortening, which refers to the longitudinal overlap of fracture fragments resulting in loss of overall bone length measured in centimeters on full-length standing or traction radiographs","C":"Rotation, which refers to axial twisting of one fracture fragment relative to the other around the long axis of the bone and may not be visible on standard two-view radiographs","D":"Angulation, which is reported as the direction the apex of the fracture deformity points — for example, apex-volar or apex-dorsal — and determines the direction of corrective manipulation required for reduction","E":"Comminution, which refers to the presence of three or more bone fragments at the fracture site typically resulting from high-energy trauma such as motor vehicle collision or fall from height"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Angulation describes the angle formed between fracture fragments and is reported by the direction the apex points. Apex-volar means the distal fragment is tilted dorsally, as in a Colles fracture. Displacement describes translational shift; shortening describes longitudinal overlap; rotation describes axial twist. All four descriptors are used together for complete fracture characterization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complete fracture description requires: bone name, fracture type, location, displacement, angulation, rotation, shortening, open vs closed. COMLEX: Reduction technique is guided by reversal of the deforming force — knowing angulation direction determines how to apply corrective force.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q06","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fracture is classified as open when which defining criterion is met, and why does this classification significantly alter the clinical management approach?","choices":{"A":"A fracture is open when the fracture line extends into the adjacent joint space, creating an intra-articular injury that requires anatomic reduction to restore joint surface congruity and prevent arthritis","B":"A fracture is open when the periosteum is disrupted by the fracture, exposing the cortical bone surface and increasing the risk of delayed union due to loss of the periosteal osteoprogenitor cell layer","C":"A fracture is open when the fracture communicates with the external environment through a wound in the overlying skin, requiring emergent surgical debridement and antibiotics to prevent osteomyelitis","D":"A fracture is open when the bone fragments are widely displaced with greater than 50 percent translational shift relative to the bone shaft diameter, indicating high-energy mechanism and ligamentous disruption","E":"A fracture is open when marrow fat is released into the circulation, producing a fat embolism syndrome characterized by hypoxemia, petechiae, and confusion in the days following a long-bone fracture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An open fracture communicates with the external environment, introducing contamination risk and dramatically increasing the likelihood of osteomyelitis. Management requires emergent operative debridement, irrigation, and IV antibiotics begun immediately (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Gustilo-Anderson classification grades open fractures I through IIIC by wound size and soft tissue injury. Intra-articular extension is a separate descriptor; periosteal disruption occurs in most displaced fractures; fat embolism is a systemic complication, not the classification criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture = skin breach + fracture communication. Gustilo I: wound under 1 cm. Gustilo II: 1-10 cm. Gustilo III: over 10 cm or periosteal stripping. IV antibiotics begun immediately, debridement in OR. COMLEX: Osteomyelitis complication of open fracture — Staph aureus is most common organism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q07","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of pediatric bone best explains why a bending force produces an incomplete greenstick fracture in a child but a complete transverse fracture in an adult subjected to the same force?","choices":{"A":"Pediatric bone has a thicker periosteum with a denser network of Sharpey fibers anchoring it to the cortex, generating mechanical resistance that halts fracture propagation across the bone before complete cortical failure","B":"Pediatric bone has a higher ratio of organic collagen matrix to inorganic mineral content, giving it greater plasticity and the ability to absorb energy by bending before the tension-side cortex ultimately fails","C":"Pediatric bone has a lower cortical bone mineral density due to ongoing endochondral ossification at the physis, reducing the stiffness of the diaphysis and allowing elastic bending without catastrophic failure","D":"Pediatric bone is supplied by a denser network of Haversian canals than adult bone, distributing stress over more vascular channels and preventing the stress concentration required to propagate a complete fracture line","E":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing microcracks to be repaired before they can coalesce into a macroscopic fracture line, which slows the progression from incomplete to complete fracture"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio than adult bone, giving it greater plasticity and energy absorption capacity. During a bending force, the tension cortex fails first while the compression cortex bends but remains intact, producing the classic greenstick pattern. Adult bone is stiffer and more brittle due to higher mineral content, causing both cortices to fail nearly simultaneously under the same force.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick = pediatric incomplete fracture from bending. Tension side breaks, compression side bends. Treat with reduction and casting. COMLEX: Pediatric bone material properties also explain torus/buckle fractures at the metaphysis from axial loading — different mechanism than greenstick.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q08","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy falls and sustains a distal radius fracture. Radiograph shows cortical buckling on the dorsal surface of the distal radius metaphysis with no cortical disruption on the volar surface. This fracture pattern is best described as which type and from which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture caused by a bending force that fractures the tension cortex while the compression cortex remains intact, characterized by a visible cortical break on one side with intact cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"A Salter-Harris Type I fracture caused by shear forces through the distal radial physis, characterized by widening of the growth plate and physeal tenderness without a discrete cortical fracture line on standard radiographs","C":"A pathologic fracture caused by cortical thinning from a bone cyst or fibrous dysplasia lesion within the distal radial metaphysis, showing a cortical bulge due to the underlying lytic lesion rather than acute traumatic buckling","D":"A plastic deformation injury caused by the bone bending beyond its elastic limit without any cortical disruption, producing a visible bow to the entire metaphyseal segment rather than the localized cortical bulge seen in a buckle fracture","E":"A torus fracture, also called a buckle fracture, caused by axial compressive loading at the metaphysis that causes the cortex to buckle outward without fully fracturing, analogous to the buckling of a metal torus ring under compression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A torus fracture results from axial compressive loading at the metaphysis. The cortex buckles outward on the compressive side without completing a fracture line. It is mechanically stable and typically treated with a short arm cast for 3 to 4 weeks. Greenstick fractures show cortical disruption on the tension side; Salter-Harris fractures involve the physis; plastic deformation shows global bowing without cortical buckling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus/buckle fracture = metaphyseal axial compression = cortical buckling. Stable, cast for 3-4 weeks, excellent prognosis. COMLEX: Distinguish from greenstick: torus = compression side buckle, no cortical break; greenstick = tension side cortical break with intact compression cortex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q09","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Pediatric long bone fractures are managed with less stringent alignment requirements than adult fractures in part because of the remodeling potential of growing bone. What is the primary mechanism driving this remodeling?","choices":{"A":"Intramembranous ossification at the fracture site activates dormant osteoprogenitor cells within the periosteum, producing a hypertrophic callus large enough to bridge angular deformities without requiring bony realignment","B":"Endochondral ossification at the fracture gap deposits a cartilaginous template that gradually mineralizes and reshapes under mechanical loading, correcting angular deformities through callus remodeling alone","C":"Secondary Haversian remodeling by cutting cone osteoclasts tunnels through the fracture callus and deposits new lamellar bone in the direction of loading, correcting angular deformities at the microscopic level","D":"Physeal-driven longitudinal bone growth combined with periosteal appositional remodeling guided by Wolff's law, which corrects angular deformities over months to years in proportion to the child's remaining growth potential","E":"Increased osteoclastic activity stimulated by parathyroid hormone surge after fracture resorbs the malunited callus, followed by osteoblastic refilling along functional loading lines to restore normal alignment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Remodeling of fracture malunion in children is driven by two mechanisms: physeal growth, which progressively shifts bone away from the angular deformity as the bone elongates, and periosteal appositional remodeling under Wolff's law, which adds bone on the concave side and resorbs on the convex side. Greatest remodeling occurs in young children with maximal remaining growth and in deformities in the plane of joint motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling potential is greatest when: young age with growth remaining, fracture near a rapidly growing physis, and deformity is in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. COMLEX: Varus/valgus deformities remodel better than rotational deformities — rotation does not reliably correct with growth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q10","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl falls off her bicycle. Wrist X-ray shows a fracture line extending through the distal radial physis with a small triangular cortical fragment attached at the metaphyseal corner. The epiphysis is displaced but not split. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and what is its prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture type, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis creating the Thurston-Holland corner fragment, and prognosis for normal growth is generally favorable","B":"Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture passes entirely through the physis without involving the metaphysis or epiphysis, the radiograph appears nearly normal, and diagnosis is made clinically by tenderness directly over the physis","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture extends through the physis into the epiphysis and enters the joint, creating an intra-articular fracture that requires anatomic reduction to restore articular congruity","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis, producing the highest risk of bony physeal bridge formation among the fracture types visible on initial radiograph","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush injury of the germinal physis from axial compression that appears normal on initial radiograph and is diagnosed retrospectively when premature growth arrest becomes evident months later"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Thurston-Holland fragment — a metaphyseal corner fragment — is the hallmark of Salter-Harris Type II fracture. Type II is the most common physeal fracture and has a generally good prognosis because the germinal zone of the physis is spared. Type I is a pure physeal separation; Types III and IV involve the epiphysis and carry greater risk of growth disturbance; Type V is a crush injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type II = most common = metaphyseal corner fragment = Thurston-Holland sign. Mnemonic SALTR: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Type V is the RAREST and WORST — often missed initially. COMLEX: CP8 pediatric named condition; follow-up to monitor for growth arrest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q11","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Salter-Harris fracture type carries the worst long-term prognosis for normal bone growth, often presents with a normal or near-normal initial radiograph, and is definitively diagnosed only retrospectively after growth arrest becomes evident on follow-up imaging?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type IV, which extends through the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis in a single oblique line, is visible on initial radiograph, has the highest surgical failure risk from bony bridge formation, but can be prevented with anatomic fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush or compression injury of the germinal zone of the physis that disrupts physeal chondrocytes without producing a visible fracture line, leading to premature physeal closure and growth arrest","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, which extends through the physis into the epiphysis creating an intra-articular fracture, is visible on initial radiograph, requires anatomic reduction, and carries moderate growth disturbance risk","D":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation with near-normal radiograph, occurs most commonly in neonates and young children, and generally has a favorable prognosis when recognized early and gently reduced","E":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture with a visible metaphyseal corner fragment, has a generally favorable prognosis because the germinal zone is spared by the fracture exiting through the metaphysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a compression or crush injury of the physis that damages the germinal and proliferative chondrocyte layers without producing a fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is diagnosed retrospectively when growth arrest or angular deformity is noted at follow-up. Type IV carries the highest surgical risk of bridge formation among radiographically visible types, but Type V has the worst overall prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush of physis = normal X-ray = diagnosed retrospectively = worst prognosis. Type IV = crosses all three layers = needs fixation to prevent physeal bridge. COMLEX: Physeal fractures need follow-up growth monitoring with contralateral comparison X-rays.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q12","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male soccer player inverts his ankle. Radiograph shows a fracture line extending from the distal tibial physis vertically downward through the lateral epiphysis into the ankle joint, with no metaphyseal fragment visible. Which Salter-Harris type is this and what management principle is critical?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III, an epiphyseal fracture with intra-articular extension, requiring anatomic reduction of the articular surface to restore joint congruity and prevent post-traumatic arthritis at the ankle","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis producing a corner fragment rather than splitting the epiphysis, generally managed with closed reduction and casting without strict articular alignment requirements","C":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation without epiphyseal or metaphyseal bony fragment, managed with immobilization alone since the articular surface is not involved and growth plate anatomy is preserved","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis simultaneously, requiring anatomic fixation to prevent a bony bridge across the physis that would cause angular growth arrest","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a compression injury to the physis with no fracture line visible on plain radiograph, diagnosed only after growth arrest appears on follow-up and not presenting acutely with an epiphyseal fracture line"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type III fractures extend from the physis downward through the epiphysis into the joint. This pattern is common at the distal tibia in adolescents and at the distal femur. The key management principle is anatomic reduction of the articular surface, typically requiring CT to assess reduction adequacy. Failure to achieve anatomic reduction leads to post-traumatic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type III = physis + epiphysis = intra-articular. CT scan to assess reduction. Needs anatomic reduction to prevent arthritis. Classic boards: triplane fracture of the distal tibia in adolescents is a variant combining Types II and III. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk from asymmetric physeal closure causing angular deformity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q13","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old cyclist is struck by a car bumper at knee height while riding. Radiograph of the knee shows depression of the lateral tibial plateau. Which imaging finding, if present on the lateral knee radiograph, would be pathognomonic for an intra-articular fracture communicating with the joint space?","choices":{"A":"A joint effusion seen as increased soft tissue density in the suprapatellar recess on the lateral radiograph, reflecting hemarthrosis that has accumulated within the knee joint after the plateau injury","B":"Cortical disruption of the tibial plateau surface seen as a step-off deformity on the AP radiograph, reflecting depression of the lateral articular surface by the femoral condyle at the time of impact","C":"Periosteal new bone formation along the proximal tibial metaphysis seen as a thin periosteal layer on the AP radiograph, reflecting periosteal elevation by hematoma extending from the fracture","D":"A lipohemarthrosis seen as a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch on a horizontal-beam lateral radiograph, as medullary fat enters the joint through the intra-articular fracture site","E":"Valgus alignment of the tibial plateau on a weight-bearing stress radiograph, reflecting collapse of the lateral compartment under axial loading through the depressed articular fragment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lipohemarthrosis is the pathognomonic finding of intra-articular fracture. On a cross-table lateral radiograph, fat from the bone marrow layers above blood in the joint, creating a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch. A simple effusion has no fat-fluid level and occurs with ligament injuries as well as fractures. A step-off shows a plateau fracture but not joint communication, periosteal new bone is a later healing response rather than an acute sign, and valgus collapse on stress views shows compartment depression, not marrow fat in the joint. Lipohemarthrosis confirms that the fracture extends into the joint, even when the fracture itself is subtle on other views.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipohemarthrosis on lateral knee X-ray = intra-articular fracture = pathognomonic. Requires horizontal-beam lateral. Also seen with occult tibial plateau fractures in elderly. COMLEX: Get MRI if X-ray shows effusion but no visible fracture — occult tibial plateau fractures are common in osteoporotic patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q14","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trauma lecture compares distal femur fractures in adults and children. According to the course, which statement best describes the typical mechanism of injury in each group?","choices":{"A":"Both groups usually sustain distal femur fractures from low-energy falls, because the thin metaphyseal cortex fails at the same low load","B":"Adults usually sustain them from high-energy trauma such as car crashes, whereas children usually fracture from simple falls at play","C":"Both groups usually sustain them from high-energy trauma, because the distal femur is the strongest metaphysis in the skeleton","D":"Adults usually fracture from repetitive running loads, whereas children usually fracture from direct blows during contact sports","E":"Adults usually sustain them from low-energy mechanisms such as a fall, whereas children usually need high-energy trauma to break"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists distal femur fractures as 3 to 6% of adult fractures, more common in females, with a low-energy mechanism such as a fall in adults and a high-energy mechanism in children. In older adults, osteoporotic metaphyseal bone breaks with modest force, while the healthy, more elastic bone of a child needs a large force to break at this level. Saying both groups fracture with low energy ignores the high-energy mechanism in children. Reversing the pattern (high energy in adults, falls in children) contradicts the course. A high-energy mechanism is not typical in adults. Repetitive running loads describe stress fractures, not the distal femur fractures discussed here. Management in both groups is PRICE, immobilization, and surgical consultation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee fractures: tibial plateau (proximal tibia; females >51, osteoporosis; watch for compartment syndrome), patella (direct blow; surgery if >2 mm step-off, >3 mm displacement, or extensor disruption), distal femur (adults low energy, children high energy). COMLEX: A high-energy distal femur fracture in a child should prompt a search for other injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — bone mineral loss and fracture risk in older adults"},{"id":"sdl9_q15","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old falls directly onto his patella. Radiograph shows a transverse patellar fracture with 5 mm of articular step-off and the patient is unable to perform an active straight-leg raise. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immobilization in a cylinder cast or knee immobilizer in full extension for 8 weeks followed by progressive quadriceps strengthening, allowing the fragments to unite without operative soft tissue disruption","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation using tension band wiring or cannulated screws to restore articular congruity and repair the extensor mechanism, followed by early range-of-motion rehabilitation","C":"Aspiration of the hemarthrosis and application of a functional knee brace locked in extension, followed by gradual unlocking of the brace over 6 weeks as quadriceps control and healing progress","D":"Patellectomy with repair of the quadriceps tendon directly to the patellar tendon stump, followed by extensor mechanism rehabilitation to restore active knee extension and straight-leg raise","E":"Urgent total knee arthroplasty with patellar resurfacing to address both the acute fracture and the patellar articular surface damage in a single definitive operative procedure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indications for patellar fracture are articular step-off greater than 2 mm or fragment separation greater than 3 mm, and disruption of the extensor mechanism demonstrated by inability to perform a straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring converts tensile forces into compressive forces across the fracture during knee flexion. Non-displaced fractures with an intact extensor mechanism are managed non-operatively in extension, but casting or a locked brace cannot restore a 5 mm step-off or a disrupted extensor mechanism. Patellectomy is reserved for severely comminuted fractures that cannot be fixed and permanently reduces quadriceps mechanical advantage, and arthroplasty is not a treatment for an isolated traumatic patellar fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar fracture surgical indications: articular step-off over 2 mm, separation over 3 mm, or failed straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring = compression across fracture with knee movement. COMLEX: Test extensor mechanism integrity at every knee fracture exam — straight-leg raise is the key clinical test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q16","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The tibia is more frequently involved in open fractures than any other long bone. Which anatomical feature is most responsible for this predisposition?","choices":{"A":"The tibial diaphysis has a relatively thin cortex compared to other long bones, causing the bone to fracture into sharp pointed fragments that more readily perforate surrounding soft tissues during high-energy injury","B":"The tibia bears the majority of axial load in the lower extremity, resulting in high-energy fracture patterns with greater displacement that tend to produce larger soft tissue injuries than weight-bearing bones elsewhere","C":"The tibial nutrient artery enters at the posterior cortex, creating a stress concentration point at the posterior diaphysis that predisposes to spiral fractures propagating toward the subcutaneous anterior surface","D":"The anteromedial tibial surface is subcutaneous throughout its entire length with minimal overlying soft tissue, making it highly susceptible to skin perforation when fracture fragments displace medially","E":"The interosseous membrane between tibia and fibula restricts outward periosteal expansion after fracture, directing displaced fragments toward the medial subcutaneous surface rather than allowing dissipation into deeper compartments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anteromedial border of the tibia is subcutaneous from the tibial tubercle to the medial malleolus, covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat. This minimal soft tissue coverage means that even relatively small amounts of fracture displacement can produce skin perforation. Combined with its exposed position, this explains why the tibia has the highest open fracture rate of any long bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibia = most common open fracture = subcutaneous anteromedial surface. Gustilo-Anderson classification I-IIIC. COMLEX: Open fracture management — IV antibiotics begun immediately; emergent irrigation and debridement within 6 hours is the accepted standard, though the evidence for that window is sparse (SDL 25 reading). Cephalosporin for all grades; add gram-negative coverage for Grade II/III.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q17","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture while skiing. Five hours after a splint is applied, he reports pain rated 9 out of 10 that is not relieved by opioids, and has pain with passive dorsiflexion of his toes. The compartments feel tense to palpation. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent four-compartment fasciotomy of the leg, since disproportionate pain and pain with passive muscle stretch are the key clinical signs of compartment syndrome","B":"Measurement of compartment pressures using a pressure monitor before proceeding, with fasciotomy reserved for patients whose absolute compartment pressure exceeds 50 mmHg","C":"Bivalving and spreading the splint down to skin level to reduce external compression, followed by reassessment of pain, passive stretch, and compartment firmness in 2 hours","D":"Elevation of the extremity well above the level of the heart to reduce venous engorgement and lower compartment pressure, together with ice and continued opioid analgesia","E":"Emergent MRI of the leg to confirm muscle ischemia and edema within the anterior and deep posterior compartments before proceeding to operative fasciotomy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis. Classic features are pain out of proportion to injury and pain with passive stretch of the muscles within the affected compartment. When clinical signs are unequivocal, emergent fasciotomy should not be delayed for pressure measurement. An absolute-pressure cutoff of 50 mmHg is too high and delays decompression. Bivalving the splint relieves external pressure but does not treat established compartment syndrome, elevating the limb above the heart lowers the arteriovenous gradient and worsens perfusion (keep it at heart level), and MRI only delays a clinical diagnosis. If pressure measurement is used, fasciotomy is indicated when the delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) is less than or equal to 30 mmHg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome = 5 Ps (pain, pallor, paresthesia, paralysis, pulselessness — late). Pain with passive stretch is earliest and most reliable sign. Delta-P threshold for fasciotomy: diastolic BP minus compartment pressure less than or equal to 30 mmHg. COMLEX: Tibial shaft fracture is the most common cause of lower leg compartment syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q18","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the gold-standard definitive treatment for a displaced, closed tibial shaft fracture in a healthy adult, and why is this approach preferred over plating?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation using a medial plate applied to the anteromedial surface, which provides excellent stability but carries a high wound complication risk given the minimal soft tissue coverage over the tibial surface","B":"Long leg casting in a three-point mold with non-weight bearing for 12 weeks, which is appropriate for minimally displaced stable fractures but cannot control rotation or maintain length in significantly displaced tibial shaft fractures","C":"Intramedullary nailing, which restores length, alignment, and rotational stability while preserving the periosteal blood supply by avoiding open dissection of the fracture site, and allows early weight bearing","D":"External fixation with a unilateral frame spanning the fracture site, which is the preferred definitive treatment for open tibial fractures with contaminated wounds but results in higher malunion rates than nailing for closed injuries","E":"Functional bracing after initial casting for 4 weeks to allow early soft tissue healing, which has been used historically but produces unacceptable angular malunion rates compared to intramedullary nailing for fractures with displacement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intramedullary nailing is the gold standard for displaced tibial shaft fractures. The nail is inserted without exposing the fracture, preserving the periosteal blood supply and fracture hematoma. It controls length, alignment, and rotation through interlocking screws. Medial plating is avoided because the anteromedial tibial surface has minimal soft tissue coverage. External fixation is used for temporizing open fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial shaft fracture definitive treatment = intramedullary nailing. Preserves periosteal blood supply. Controls rotation via interlocking screws. COMLEX: Nail is inserted through patellar tendon approach. Compartment syndrome can still develop after nailing — monitor post-op.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q19","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An isolated mid-shaft fibular fracture from a direct blow is noted on X-ray with an intact ankle mortise. How is this fracture managed, and what anatomical rationale justifies this approach?","choices":{"A":"Weight bearing as tolerated in an air-stirrup brace or walking boot, since the fibula is not a weight-bearing bone and this isolated shaft fracture is mechanically stable","B":"Surgical fixation with a lateral plate and screws to prevent late valgus angulation of the ankle and fibular shortening, followed by protected weight bearing in a boot for 6 weeks","C":"Non-weight-bearing immobilization in a long leg cast for 8 weeks to protect the fibula until radiographic union, followed by progressive weight bearing and ankle range of motion","D":"Intramedullary nailing of the fibula to provide axial and rotational stability and allow immediate full weight bearing, with hardware removal after radiographic union","E":"Emergent orthopedic referral for operative fixation within 24 hours, since displacement of the fibular shaft can progress and widen the ankle mortise if fixation is delayed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fibula does not bear or transfer body weight (Moore Ch 7). The interosseous membrane and intact tibia provide stability in isolated mid-shaft fibular fractures. As long as the ankle mortise is intact, conservative treatment with weight bearing as tolerated provides excellent outcomes. Plating, intramedullary nailing, long-leg casting, and urgent operative referral add risk or delay recovery without benefit for this stable injury; fixation is reserved for fractures with syndesmotic or mortise disruption. The critical clinical concern is always ensuring the ankle mortise is intact, as syndesmotic disruption or deltoid ligament injury changes management entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated fibular shaft fracture with intact ankle mortise = conservative management. The fibula is not weight-bearing (Moore Ch 7). COMLEX: Always assess full ankle views — widened mortise changes fracture to unstable Weber B or C, requiring surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q20","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old twists his ankle and is found to have no fracture on ankle radiographs, but he has proximal fibular tenderness. Full-length tibia-fibula films reveal a fracture at the fibular neck. What is the diagnosis and which associated ankle structure must be assessed for integrity?","choices":{"A":"An isolated stress fracture of the fibular neck from repetitive loading, which develops insidiously over weeks to months and is typically seen in runners and military recruits","B":"A peroneal nerve traction injury at the fibular head from the twisting mechanism, producing tenderness over the nerve with foot drop and dorsal foot numbness","C":"A Maisonneuve fracture: a pronation-external rotation injury that disrupts the syndesmosis and interosseous membrane, so the syndesmosis and deltoid ligament must be assessed","D":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from the twisting injury, which presents with proximal lateral knee pain, a prominent fibular head, and peroneal nerve symptoms","E":"A fracture of the fibular head from a direct varus knee mechanism, which is associated with lateral collateral ligament injury and peroneal nerve palsy at the knee"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation force at the ankle. The medial ankle structures fail first (deltoid ligament or medial malleolus), then the interosseous membrane tears proximally, finally producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle films appear nearly normal, but medial clear space widening indicates ankle instability. Full-length fibular films must be obtained in any ankle injury with unexplained mortise widening. A stress fracture lacks an acute ankle mechanism, a nerve traction injury does not explain a fibular neck fracture, and proximal tibiofibular dislocation or a varus fibular head fracture are knee injuries that do not involve the distal syndesmosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibular fracture from ankle mechanism. Mechanism: pronation-external rotation. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture or medial malleolus fracture. COMLEX: If ankle mortise is widened without a visible distal fibular fracture, get full-length films — classic boards pitfall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q21","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Danis-Weber classification, a fibular fracture occurring above the level of the ankle mortise and above the tibiofibular syndesmosis is classified as Weber C. What is the clinical implication of this fracture level?","choices":{"A":"Weber C implies the fracture is extra-articular and mechanically stable, since the fibular fracture is remote from the ankle joint and the syndesmosis remains intact because the fracture is above the level of the tibiofibular ligaments","B":"Weber C implies isolated lateral ankle instability from disruption of the anterior talofibular ligament without syndesmotic injury, requiring only cast immobilization and weight-bearing restriction until soft tissue healing is complete","C":"Weber C implies the fracture occurred from an inversion mechanism, consistent with the most common ankle injury pattern, and is associated with lateral ligament complex injury rather than syndesmotic disruption","D":"Weber C implies a stable fracture pattern amenable to closed treatment in a short leg walking cast, since the fibular fracture above the syndesmosis relieves rather than transmits destabilizing forces to the ankle mortise","E":"Weber C implies likely disruption of the tibiofibular syndesmosis and interosseous membrane, creating an unstable ankle mortise that needs fibular fixation plus syndesmotic positional screw or suture-button stabilization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Weber C fractures occur above the tibiofibular syndesmosis and imply syndesmotic disruption because the fibula must fracture proximal to the intact syndesmosis for the fracture to occur at that level. The syndesmotic disruption destabilizes the ankle mortise, allowing lateral talar shift under loading. Surgical treatment includes fibular fixation and syndesmotic stabilization with a positional screw or suture-button device placed through the fibula and tibia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber A = below syndesmosis = stable. Weber B = at syndesmosis = variable. Weber C = above syndesmosis = unstable. Weber C always requires surgery. COMLEX: Test syndesmotic integrity with external rotation stress test and squeeze test. MRI or stress radiographs confirm syndesmotic disruption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q22","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A quality-improvement project reviews adult ankle fractures seen in an emergency department. According to the epidemiology presented in the course, which pattern would the team expect to find?","choices":{"A":"Most fractures are trimalleolar, and men and women are affected mainly after age 70, from osteoporotic falls in the home","B":"Most fractures are bimalleolar, and men are affected far more often than women in every age group because of sports","C":"Most fractures are unimalleolar, with similar rates by sex: men mostly as young adults and women mostly at 50 to 70","D":"Most fractures involve the talar dome, and women are affected mainly as young adults while men are affected after 70","E":"Most fractures are isolated posterior malleolar, with women affected four times more often than men at all ages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course gives an ankle fracture incidence of about 187 per 100,000 people per year. Most are malleolar: 60 to 70% unimalleolar, 15 to 20% bimalleolar, and 7 to 12% trimalleolar. Men and women are affected equally overall, with men affected mainly as young adults and women mainly between 50 and 70 years. Trimalleolar and bimalleolar fractures are the minority, so neither is most common. Men do not outnumber women in every age group. Talar dome injuries and isolated posterior malleolar fractures are not the common patterns described, and women are not affected four times more often.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle fractures: unimalleolar 60–70% > bimalleolar 15–20% > trimalleolar 7–12%; Ottawa ankle rules decide imaging; Danis-Weber level (A below, B at, C above the syndesmosis) guides stability. COMLEX: Bimodal by sex: young men (sport, trauma), women 50–70 (bone fragility).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Ottawa ankle rules and ankle sprain grading"},{"id":"sdl9_q23","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture involves fractures of which three bony structures of the ankle?","choices":{"A":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and the anterior tibial plafond, where the anterior rim fracture is produced by forced dorsiflexion with axial loading of the talus","B":"The medial malleolus, distal fibular shaft, and the posterior tibial shaft, with the fibular component typically located above the syndesmosis in a Weber C pattern","C":"The lateral malleolus, navicular, and posterior malleolus, produced by a high-energy axial load that drives the talus into the distal tibia and midfoot","D":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus, where the posterior malleolus is the posterior articular rim of the distal tibial plafond","E":"The medial malleolus, posterior malleolus, and the anterior process of the calcaneus, produced by forced inversion and plantarflexion of the hindfoot"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trimalleolar fractures involve the medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus. The posterior malleolus is the posterior articular rim of the distal tibia; the anterior plafond, tibial shaft, navicular, and calcaneus are not malleoli. When it is fractured, it removes the posterior osseous constraint on the talus, significantly increasing posterior talar subluxation risk. The larger the posterior malleolus fragment, the more instability is created. Fragments involving more than 25% to 33% of the articular surface have traditionally been fixed (a threshold the SDL 25 reading calls controversial).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trimalleolar fracture = medial + lateral + posterior malleolus. Posterior malleolus fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface has traditionally been fixed (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: Posterior malleolus fixation also reduces syndesmotic instability. CT scan is essential to measure posterior fragment size before planning surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q24","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture is considered more unstable than a bimalleolar fracture. Which anatomical principle best explains the additional instability conferred by the posterior malleolus fracture?","choices":{"A":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus disrupts the attachment of the posterior tibiofibular ligament at the tibia, eliminating the strongest component of the syndesmotic complex and causing complete lateral ankle instability","B":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus creates a posterior cortical stress riser in the distal tibia, increasing the risk of propagation to a pilon fracture pattern that would extend the articular injury to the central tibial plafond","C":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus exposes the Achilles tendon insertion to abnormal tensile loading, which pulls the calcaneus posteriorly and creates a secondary subtalar joint instability that compounds the ankle mortise disruption","D":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus interrupts the vascular supply to the distal tibia from the posterior tibial artery, increasing the risk of avascular necrosis of the tibial plafond and post-traumatic arthritis from ischemia","E":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus eliminates the posterior osseous buttress of the tibiotalar joint, removing the posterior constraint on the talus and allowing posterior subluxation of the talus under the tibia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior malleolus provides the posterior osseous buttress for the tibiotalar joint. When fractured, the talus can sublux posteriorly, which dramatically reduces the contact area of the tibiotalar joint and accelerates post-traumatic arthritis. Posterior malleolus fragments involving more than 25% to 33% of the tibial articular surface have traditionally been fixed to restore the posterior buttress. The posterior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, so its fixation also indirectly reduces syndesmotic instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior malleolus = posterior tibial rim = posterior buttress for talus. Fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface = traditional fixation threshold (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: Trimalleolar fractures require ORIF of all components when posterior malleolus is large enough to affect stability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q25","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male is injured in a dirt bike crash and sustains a fracture described by the trauma surgeon as involving the \"zone of bone with the richest blood supply, adjacent to the growth plate, where the medullary canal transitions from a narrow isthmus to the flared bony ends of the femur.\" Which anatomical region is being described, and which clinical complication does this region's vascularity predispose to in children?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, which is the most vascular zone of long bone due to its rich capillary sinusoidal network, predisposing children to hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding because the sluggish sinusoidal blood flow facilitates bacterial lodgment","B":"The epiphysis, which receives blood from terminal epiphyseal vessels that cross the physis and is the primary site of septic arthritis in neonates when bacterial infection spreads from adjacent metaphyseal vessels into the joint space","C":"The diaphysis, which contains the nutrient foramen through which the nutrient artery enters and branches centrifugally to supply the cortical bone, and is the primary site of Ewing sarcoma arising from intramedullary mesenchymal cells","D":"The periosteum, which contains a dense capillary network in its inner cambium layer that supplies the outer cortex and provides the osteoprogenitor cells responsible for periosteal callus formation during fracture healing","E":"The physis, which is a highly vascularized cartilaginous disc containing the proliferative and hypertrophic zones of chondrocytes and is the weakest structural point in pediatric long bones under mechanical loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis contains large, thin-walled sinusoids with sluggish blood flow and lacks phagocytic lining cells, making it the primary site for hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. Bacteria seed the metaphysis via the bloodstream. In neonates and infants, metaphyseal vessels cross the physis into the epiphysis, allowing spread to cause septic arthritis. Acute hematogenous osteomyelitis usually occurs in the metaphysis (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteomyelitis in children: metaphysis is the seeding site. Staph aureus is the most common organism overall; Salmonella in sickle cell disease. Neonates can have epiphyseal or joint extension. COMLEX: Always check for concurrent septic arthritis when diagnosing pediatric osteomyelitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q26","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks about the blood supply to cortical bone during a histology lab session. She wants to understand which vessels supply the deeper (endosteal) part of the compact bone of a long bone diaphysis and which supply its outermost part. What is the correct answer?","choices":{"A":"The periosteal arteries supply the deeper compact bone by penetrating through Volkmann canals to the endosteal surface, while the nutrient artery supplies only the medullary hematopoietic tissue and does not contribute to cortical bone perfusion","B":"The nutrient artery supplies the marrow, spongy bone and deeper compact bone through its medullary branches, while periosteal artery branches supply the outermost compact bone; blood moves from the marrow outward to periosteal veins","C":"The epiphyseal arteries supply the deeper compact bone through their metaphyseal branches, while the periosteal arteries supply the outermost part, and the nutrient artery is a vestigial structure in adults with no functional blood flow","D":"The Haversian canal arterioles supply the deeper compact bone entirely independently of the medullary circulation, receiving their blood from the periosteum without contribution from the nutrient artery or endosteum","E":"Both the inner and outer cortex receive equal contributions from the nutrient artery and periosteal arteries, which anastomose freely within the Volkmann canals such that no regional distinction in cortical blood supply can be identified"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The nutrient artery enters through the nutrient foramen and divides in the medullary cavity, \"supplying the bone marrow, spongy bone, and deeper portions of the compact bone\" (Moore Ch 1), while small branches of the periosteal arteries supply the outer compact bone. Pawlina Ch 8 describes the flow as essentially centrifugal — from the marrow cavity through the bone tissue and out via periosteal veins — with a lesser supply to the outermost compact bone from periosteal arteries. The two textbooks differ on which supply dominates: Pawlina calls the periosteal contribution lesser, whereas Moore says periosteal branches nourish most of the compact bone and that a bone stripped of its periosteum dies. Both agree on the regional pattern tested here. The nutrient artery is neither vestigial nor limited to the marrow, Haversian vessels are reached through Volkmann canals rather than running independently of the medullary circulation, and the supply is regional rather than equal throughout the cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortical blood supply — nutrient artery → marrow, spongy bone and deeper compact bone; periosteal arteries → outermost compact bone; flow is centrifugal (Pawlina Ch 8). Sources differ on dominance: Pawlina calls the periosteal supply lesser, Moore Ch 1 says periosteal branches nourish most of the compact bone. COMLEX: A bone stripped of its periosteum dies (Moore Ch 1), so periosteal stripping during fixation threatens the outer cortex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q27","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old cyclist sustains a segmental femoral shaft fracture with significant periosteal stripping noted intraoperatively. The orthopedic surgeon expresses concern about delayed union for the isolated middle fragment. Which characteristic of periosteum explains this clinical concern?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum contains dense nerve fibers that trigger the local inflammatory response critical for initiating osteoblast recruitment, and its stripping prevents the neurogenic pain signal that normally drives early bone repair","B":"The periosteum anchors ligaments and tendons to the bone through Sharpey fibers and provides the mechanical load transfer needed to stimulate piezoelectric osteoblast activation at the fracture site during early healing","C":"The periosteum acts as a mechanical barrier preventing hematoma dispersal from the fracture site, and its disruption allows the fracture hematoma to dissipate before organizing into a fibrocartilaginous bridging callus","D":"The periosteum's inner cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells and provides the majority of blood supply for periosteal callus formation, and its stripping deprives the middle segment of the cellular and vascular resources required for healing","E":"The periosteum provides the only route of venous drainage from the diaphyseal cortex through emissary veins, and its stripping causes venous congestion and cortical ischemia that independently delays osteoblast-mediated bone formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The periosteum has two layers: an outer fibrous layer and an inner cambium layer. The cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells that differentiate into osteoblasts during fracture healing and produce periosteal callus. It also contributes to the vascular supply through the periosteal capillary network. Periosteal stripping eliminates this cellular and vascular contribution to healing, explaining the high non-union risk in segmental fractures and open fractures with extensive stripping.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteum = outer fibrous + inner cambium layers. Cambium contains osteoprogenitor cells = periosteal callus. Periosteal stripping = delayed union, especially in segmental fractures. COMLEX: Open fracture debridement must preserve periosteum whenever possible to protect healing capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q28","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is hit by a car. AP and lateral radiographs of the thigh show the distal femoral shaft broken into two separate pieces that are widely separated from each other, with no additional fragments. A resident calls the consult a \"comminuted\" fracture. How should the fracture fragments be described?","choices":{"A":"Widely displaced but not comminuted, because comminution means three or more fragments and this fracture has two pieces","B":"Comminuted, because any fracture that separates the bone into unconnected pieces is comminuted regardless of how many fragments are present","C":"Highly comminuted, because wide separation of the fragments implies that small pieces too tiny to see on radiographs have broken free","D":"Compound, because wide displacement of a femoral fracture means the bone has penetrated the skin even if no wound is visible on exam","E":"Segmental, because a two-part shaft fracture is by definition segmental whenever the gap between the fragments exceeds the shaft width"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fracture management chapter for this SDL defines comminution by the number of fragments: three or more fragments is comminuted, and more than four or five is highly comminuted. It uses a two-piece, widely displaced distal femur fracture as its example of a fracture that is widely displaced but not comminuted. Displacement describes how far apart the fragments are, so it is separate from comminution. Wide separation does not imply hidden fragments, and the description should match the radiograph. Orthopedists avoid the term compound because it is used inconsistently; open versus closed depends on a skin wound communicating with the fracture. A segmental fracture has two fracture levels that isolate a middle fragment, which this two-piece fracture does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Describe fractures by open/closed, bone and location (diaphysis, metaphysis, epiphysis), pattern (transverse, oblique, spiral), comminution (≥3 fragments), displacement, angulation, shortening, rotation, intra- vs extra-articular. COMLEX: Always obtain two orthogonal views including the joints above and below a long-bone fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — describing and managing humerus fractures"},{"id":"sdl9_q29","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old is struck by a vehicle. Imaging shows a tibial fracture described as \"segmental with a 7 cm isolated middle fragment and 50% cortical displacement at both fracture levels.\" Which specific characteristic of this fracture type explains the increased risk of non-union compared to a simple two-fragment tibial fracture?","choices":{"A":"The fracture occurs at two separate levels, doubling the total fracture surface area requiring callus bridging and therefore requiring twice as long a healing time as a single-level fracture with the same amount of fracture displacement","B":"The fracture pattern indicates a torsional mechanism at the proximal level and a bending mechanism at the distal level, creating two biomechanically distinct fracture interfaces that respond differently to the same fixation device","C":"The middle segment retains the nutrient foramen in most tibial segmental fractures, which paradoxically impairs healing by competing with periosteal callus formation and reducing the centrifugal blood flow through the Haversian system","D":"The middle segment has lost both its endosteal and periosteal blood supply at both fracture ends, creating an avascular fragment that must be reperfused through new vessel ingrowth before bone healing can proceed at both fracture sites","E":"The fracture is classified as open by definition due to the extent of displacement, requiring more aggressive antibiotic prophylaxis and debridement than a single-level closed fracture of similar displacement and mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The isolated middle segment in a segmental fracture loses its blood supply at both its proximal and distal fracture interfaces. Without an intact periosteal or endosteal blood supply, the segment becomes relatively avascular and relies on neovascularization from surrounding soft tissue and from the remaining fibula. This dramatically increases non-union risk, particularly when periosteal stripping accompanies the injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental fracture = isolated middle segment = high non-union risk due to avascularity. Often requires bone grafting. COMLEX: Segmental tibial fractures have highest complications of any tibial fracture pattern. Intramedullary nail spans both fracture levels and provides stability while vascularization occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q30","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female military recruit reports progressive anterior shin pain over 4 weeks of training. The pain worsens with activity and is relieved by rest. X-ray shows no fracture. MRI demonstrates periosteal edema and a faint cortical lucency. Which imaging modality best characterizes this injury when radiographs are negative?","choices":{"A":"Repeat plain radiographs at 2 to 3 weeks, which show periosteal reaction and a cortical fracture line once healing begins and are the standard confirmatory test for tibial stress injury","B":"MRI is the gold-standard imaging for stress fractures when plain radiographs are negative, detecting periosteal edema and endosteal signal change weeks before the cortical fracture line becomes visible on X-ray","C":"CT scan is the preferred imaging modality for stress fractures because it best identifies the cortical stress reaction and can quantify the degree of cortical disruption, providing better sensitivity than MRI for early periosteal changes","D":"Bone scintigraphy with technetium-99m is the only validated imaging modality for stress fractures, providing high sensitivity for any osteoblastic activity and distinguishing stress fractures from shin splints based on the focal versus diffuse uptake pattern","E":"Clinical diagnosis alone using the triad of activity-related pain, point tenderness, and response to rest is sufficient to diagnose stress fractures without any imaging, since false-positive imaging findings are common in active patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MRI is the most sensitive imaging modality for stress fractures, detecting periosteal edema, endosteal edema, and cortical signal change weeks before X-ray changes appear. Bone scintigraphy is highly sensitive but non-specific. CT can show the fracture line but misses early marrow edema. Repeat radiographs may eventually show periosteal reaction, but they lag weeks behind the injury and are insensitive early. Stress fractures in the anterior tibial cortex are high-risk and may require activity modification or even surgical fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress fracture = repetitive loading exceeds repair. MRI is gold standard when X-ray negative. High-risk locations requiring surgery: anterior tibial cortex, femoral neck tension side, navicular, 5th metatarsal diaphysis. COMLEX: Female athlete triad increases stress fracture risk: low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone mineral density.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q31","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl presents after a fall from a swing. Radiograph shows the radial diaphysis is angulated 25 degrees with the tension cortex clearly fractured and the compression cortex intact. Her parents ask why the bone did not break all the way through. What explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum in children is thicker and more firmly attached than in adults, generating enough mechanical resistance to halt fracture propagation before it can reach the compression cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"The child has open physes that absorb a portion of the mechanical energy from the fall before it can fully propagate through the radial diaphysis, diverting stress away from the cortex and limiting the fracture to one side","C":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to rapidly repair early microfractures as the force is applied, preventing the accumulation of damage required for complete cortical failure","D":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio and greater plastic deformability than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to bend beyond its elastic limit without fracturing while the tension cortex fails first","E":"The nutrient foramen in pediatric bone is positioned on the tension cortex, creating a stress concentration point that causes the tension cortex to fail first while the compression cortex on the opposite side remains protected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The greenstick fracture pattern is unique to pediatric bone because of its high plasticity. The collagen-rich matrix allows considerable bending before failure. During a bending force, the tension side experiences tensile stress and fails first. The compression side bends beyond the elastic limit but does not fracture because the ductile matrix can absorb the compressive energy. This is analogous to bending a green twig — it snaps on the tension side but the fibers on the other side hold together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = tension side fractures, compression side bends. Unique to pediatric bone due to high collagen content. Treat with closed reduction and casting when angulation is unacceptable. COMLEX: Greenstick fractures may need to be completed to achieve adequate reduction in severely angulated cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q32","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old distance runner has a 6-week history of progressively worsening anterior tibial pain. MRI shows periosteal edema with a partial cortical fracture of the anterior tibial diaphysis. The sports medicine physician explains that this stress fracture type is classified as high-risk. What makes anterior tibial cortex stress fractures particularly dangerous compared to posteromedial stress reactions?","choices":{"A":"The anterior tibial cortex has a thinner periosteal covering than the posterior cortex, reducing the osteoprogenitor cell density available for healing and causing the fracture to progress to cortical nonunion more rapidly","B":"The anterior tibial cortex is more metabolically active than the posterior cortex due to its proximity to the tibial nutrient foramen, generating more osteoclastic activity that outpaces osteoblastic repair during periods of high training load","C":"The anterior tibial cortex is subjected to higher shear forces than the posteromedial cortex during running gait because the anterior tibialis muscle origin inserts directly into this cortex and generates repetitive tensile avulsion forces","D":"The anterior tibial cortex is the most vascular region of the tibia due to the periosteal network from the anterior tibial artery, and the resulting hyperemia paradoxically accelerates osteoclastic resorption faster than osteoblasts can repair","E":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tension during weight bearing, which inhibits fracture healing and predisposes to propagation to a complete transverse fracture known as the dreaded black line fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tensile stress during the loading phase of gait. Tension-side stress fractures heal poorly because the fracture gap is under opening rather than compressive forces. Healing requires the fracture to be under compression. The classic \"dreaded black line\" on lateral X-ray represents a chronic tension-side stress fracture. These fractures often require surgical fixation with an intramedullary nail rather than conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior tibial stress fracture = high risk = tension side = dreaded black line on lateral X-ray. Often needs IMN. Contrast with posteromedial tibial stress reaction from MTSS: low risk, responds to rest. COMLEX: Female athlete triad predisposes to stress fractures — screen with DEXA scan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q33","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An orthopedic surgeon tells residents that a 6-year-old child's femur fracture with 20-degree angular malunion can be observed rather than operatively corrected, because significant spontaneous remodeling is expected over the next 2 to 3 years. Which combination of factors is most predictive of successful remodeling in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"Young age with significant remaining growth, fracture near a physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent hip or knee joint — all three factors maximize remodeling potential","B":"Large periosteal callus formation at the fracture site combined with early mobilization, which generates mechanical stimulus for Wolff's law-driven remodeling, independently correcting angular deformities through adaptive bone resorption and deposition","C":"High serum levels of insulin-like growth factor 1 and growth hormone in young children, which drive systemic bone formation at all sites including the fracture and are sufficient alone to correct angular deformities through accelerated remodeling","D":"Adequate calcium and vitamin D intake during the healing period combined with early weight bearing, which stimulates piezoelectric osteoblast activation throughout the healing bone and provides the mineral substrate for remodeling the malunited callus","E":"The severity of the initial angular deformity exceeding 20 degrees, which paradoxically stimulates greater Wolff's law-mediated remodeling response than smaller deformities through the more pronounced asymmetric mechanical loading it creates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Three factors predict successful remodeling: young age with substantial remaining growth, fracture proximity to the physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. Physeal growth corrects deformity as the bone elongates. Rotational deformities do not remodel reliably. The further the fracture from the dominant physis, the less remodeling occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling predictors: young age, physeal proximity, plane of joint motion (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). Rotation does NOT remodel. Valgus/varus in the coronal plane does. COMLEX: Acceptable angulation varies by age and location — more angulation accepted in younger children, less near wrist than hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q34","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two 10-year-old boys have physeal fractures from separate falls: one through the distal femoral physis and one through the distal radial physis. Both are reduced and heal. When counseling families about the long-term risk of growth disturbance after a lower extremity physeal injury, which statement is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Upper and lower extremity physeal injuries carry the same risk, because growth arrest depends on Salter-Harris type and not on the physis involved","B":"The distal radial physis is the most common site of post-traumatic arrest, and it causes the largest functional limb length discrepancy","C":"Growth disturbance after physeal injury improves as abnormal growth continues, because the remaining physis remodels the bone toward normal","D":"Distal femoral physeal injuries have a high incidence of growth arrest and a greater effect on leg length, worsening as growth continues","E":"Lower limb length differences of any size require epiphysiodesis, because a shoe lift cannot compensate for discrepancy after physeal arrest"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pediatric orthopedics chapter for this SDL notes that injuries to the physis worsen as abnormal growth continues, especially near rapidly growing areas such as the knee, and that distal femoral physeal injuries have a high incidence of growth arrest and a greater effect on leg length. Post-traumatic physeal arrest occurs most commonly after injury in the distal medial tibia. Salter-Harris type matters, but location also matters, so the two injuries do not carry equal risk. Upper extremity length differences are usually only cosmetic. Remodeling corrects many malunions, but permanent physeal damage worsens with growth rather than improving. Lower extremity discrepancies greater than 3 cm may limit function and need treatment, while lesser discrepancies can be managed with a shoe lift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physis = growth plate between metaphysis and epiphysis; physeal damage → bony bar → growth arrest (leg length discrepancy or angular deformity). The knee physes (distal femur, proximal tibia) grow fastest. COMLEX: Follow physeal fractures until growth is shown to continue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification of pediatric physeal fractures"},{"id":"sdl9_q35","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male gymnast injures his wrist during vault training. Initial radiograph appears normal with only mild physeal widening. Six months later, the affected forearm is shorter than the contralateral side with progressive radial deviation deformity. What was the most likely initial injury, and why was it radiographically occult?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III, which produces a fracture line visible on initial radiograph extending from the physis into the epiphysis, and the deformity developed because the intra-articular reduction was inadequate despite operative fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, which produces a visible metaphyseal corner fragment at the time of injury, and the subsequent growth arrest indicates that the germinal layer was injured despite the favorable fracture pattern","C":"An occult torus fracture of the distal radial metaphysis, which buckles the dorsal cortex without a discrete fracture line, and the growth deformity resulted from inappropriate immobilization in a position that compressed the physis","D":"A ligamentous injury to the distal radioulnar joint causing chronic instability, which progressively transferred abnormal stress to the distal radial physis and caused asymmetric physeal closure from mechanical overload","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, an axial compression injury that crushes the germinal chondrocyte layer of the physis without producing a visible fracture line, causing physeal arrest and subsequent angular deformity as the ulna continues to grow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a crush injury to the physis. The compressive force damages the chondrocyte columns without producing a discrete fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is often seen in sports requiring repetitive axial loading of the physis, such as gymnastics. The only finding may be subtle physeal widening acutely. Growth arrest is confirmed months later when leg or arm length discrepancy or angular deformity becomes apparent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush = normal X-ray = delayed diagnosis = worst growth prognosis. Classic scenario: gymnast with wrist pain, normal X-ray, later develops premature physeal arrest. COMLEX: Treat with restriction from gymnastics and close follow-up with contralateral comparison films.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q36","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric orthopedic surgeon is teaching residents using the mnemonic SALTR for the Salter-Harris classification. She assigns each letter to a fracture type in ascending order of severity. What structure does the letter \"L\" represent, and which Salter-Harris type does it correspond to?","choices":{"A":"\"L\" represents the Ligamentous attachments to the periosteum, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type II, in which the periosteal ligaments are avulsed along with the metaphyseal Thurston-Holland corner fragment","B":"\"L\" represents Longitudinal crack propagation, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture propagates along the full length of the physis from one side of the bone to the other without entering metaphysis or epiphysis","C":"\"L\" represents the Lower epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture line passes through the physis and exits downward through the epiphysis into the joint space","D":"\"L\" represents the Loss of growth potential, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type V, in which the physis is directly crushed and all potential for further longitudinal growth is immediately eliminated at the time of injury","E":"\"L\" represents the Lateral displacement of the epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the epiphysis slides laterally off the physis without producing any bony fragment on either the metaphyseal or epiphyseal side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The SALTR mnemonic corresponds to: S = Slipped/through physis alone (Type I), A = Above the physis through the metaphysis (Type II), L = Lower through the epiphysis (Type III), T = Through both metaphysis and epiphysis (Type IV), R = Rammed/crushed physis (Type V). Each letter reflects the key structural involvement and direction of fracture propagation in ascending severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SALTR mnemonic: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Types I-II = favorable. Types III-IV = moderate, require anatomic reduction. Type V = worst prognosis. COMLEX: Use this mnemonic reliably — Salter-Harris classification is a boards staple for both USMLE and COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q37","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman is struck by a car bumper at knee level. CT scan reveals lateral tibial plateau fracture with 9 mm articular depression. The surgeon discusses the Schatzker classification. Which Schatzker type is a lateral split-depression fracture, and what is the surgical threshold for articular depression?","choices":{"A":"Schatzker Type I is a pure lateral tibial plateau split fracture without depression, and surgical fixation is indicated for this type when articular step-off exceeds 10 mm, with conservative management acceptable below that threshold","B":"Schatzker Type IV is a medial tibial plateau fracture from a varus mechanism, and surgical fixation is indicated for any displacement because medial plateau fractures are associated with higher-energy mechanisms and peroneal nerve injury","C":"Schatzker Type VI is a bicondylar tibial plateau fracture with metaphyseal-diaphyseal dissociation, and the articular depression threshold for surgery is 5 mm, with any amount of cortical comminution serving as an independent surgical indication","D":"Schatzker Type III is a pure lateral depression fracture without a split component, and conservative management is the standard approach regardless of the degree of articular depression given the intact cortical rim provides inherent stability","E":"Schatzker Type II is a combined split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, and surgical reduction is generally indicated when articular depression exceeds 3 mm to prevent progressive valgus deformity and lateral compartment arthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schatzker Type II is a split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, the most common pattern in older patients with osteoporotic bone. The articular surface is both split and depressed. The threshold for surgical intervention is depression greater than 3 mm, as this degree of articular incongruity leads to premature lateral compartment arthritis. Schatzker I is a pure split; Type III is pure depression; Type VI involves both plateaus with metaphyseal separation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzker II = split-depression = most common tibial plateau fracture overall. Surgical threshold: over 3 mm articular depression. COMLEX: Associated injuries: popliteal artery injury with high Schatzker types. Check ABI and pulses. Compartment syndrome risk is high with bicondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q38","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with osteoporosis and diabetes falls from a step and sustains a proximal tibial plateau fracture. She is placed in a splint pending surgical consultation. Which complication listed in the course presentation of this fracture requires close monitoring in the hours after injury?","choices":{"A":"Fat embolism syndrome, because marrow fat from the plateau enters the venous circulation and lodges in the lung within the first hour","B":"Acute compartment syndrome of the leg, which can develop after a proximal tibia fracture along with ligament and meniscal injuries","C":"Avascular necrosis of the tibial plateau, because the fracture interrupts the single nutrient vessel supplying the proximal epiphysis","D":"Common peroneal nerve palsy from the splint, because pressure at the medial tibial condyle compresses the nerve as it crosses","E":"Septic arthritis, because blood within the knee joint after a closed plateau fracture becomes infected in the first few hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists pain, swelling, ligamentous injury, meniscal injury, and acute compartment syndrome in the presentation of proximal tibial (plateau) fractures, which are more common than midshaft or distal tibial fractures and are more frequent in women over 51 with comorbidities and osteoporosis. Swelling in the leg compartments after a proximal tibia fracture can raise pressure enough to cause compartment syndrome, so pain out of proportion and pain with passive stretch must be watched for. Fat embolism is not among the complications the course lists for this fracture. Avascular necrosis of the plateau is not described. The common peroneal nerve runs at the lateral fibular neck, not the medial tibial condyle. A closed fracture with hemarthrosis is not septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch, tense compartment; late signs paresthesia, pallor, pulselessness; delta pressure (diastolic − compartment) ≤30 mm Hg → fasciotomy. COMLEX: Initial plateau fracture care = PRICE, immobilization, surgical consult.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — compartment syndrome after tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q39","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old osteoporotic woman falls from standing and sustains a lateral tibial plateau fracture with 10 mm of articular depression on CT scan. Orthopedic surgery plans for open reduction and internal fixation. What specific intraoperative challenge is unique to osteoporotic bone in this fracture type?","choices":{"A":"Osteoporotic bone cannot be imaged adequately with standard CT protocols at the time of surgery, requiring intraoperative fluoroscopy alone to guide reduction, which is less accurate than preoperative CT planning for achieving anatomic articular reduction","B":"Osteoporotic bone heals more slowly than normal bone due to reduced osteoblast density, requiring double the standard immobilization period after fixation and restricting weight bearing for 6 months rather than the usual 3 months","C":"Osteoporotic bone has insufficient structural strength to hold fixation screws and support a subchondral raft beneath the reduced articular surface, requiring bone graft or calcium phosphate substitute to fill the metaphyseal void after elevation","D":"Osteoporotic bone generates insufficient callus around implants due to low bone turnover, requiring prophylactic bisphosphonate therapy to be started immediately after surgery to prevent fixation failure and implant migration","E":"Osteoporotic bone cannot sustain the traction forces required for closed reduction of the articular surface, making open surgical elevation mandatory regardless of the degree of depression, even for fractures with less than 3 mm of articular depression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When a depressed tibial plateau fragment is elevated in osteoporotic bone, the metaphyseal void left beneath the elevated articular surface has insufficient strength to resist subsidence. This void must be filled with autograft, allograft, or calcium phosphate bone substitute to maintain reduction. Failure to address this void leads to loss of reduction and articular re-depression under weight bearing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau elevation creates metaphyseal void in osteoporotic bone — requires bone graft or substitute. Schatzker II in elderly = high risk for subsidence. COMLEX: Calcium phosphate cements are commonly used as synthetic bone substitutes in elderly osteoporotic plateau fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q40","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture. Four hours after splinting, the nurse reports that the patient is screaming in pain despite IV morphine. On examination, the anterior compartment is tense and the patient has paresthesias in the first dorsal web space. Compartment pressure is measured at 45 mmHg, and blood pressure is 130/72 mmHg. What is the delta-pressure and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"The delta-pressure is 85 mmHg (systolic minus compartment pressure), well above the 30 mmHg threshold, so the compartment is adequately perfused and repeat pressure monitoring every 30 minutes is appropriate","B":"The delta-pressure is about 45 mmHg (mean arterial minus compartment pressure), above the 30 mmHg threshold, confirming that fasciotomy is not yet indicated and that observation with pain control is appropriate","C":"The absolute compartment pressure of 45 mmHg is below the 50 mmHg cutoff, so fasciotomy is not indicated and the splint should be bivalved with reassessment of the leg in 2 hours","D":"The delta-pressure of 27 mmHg indicates adequate perfusion, so the severe pain is attributed to opioid tolerance requiring patient-controlled analgesia and adjunct non-opioid medications before surgery","E":"The delta-pressure is 72 − 45 = 27 mmHg, at or below the 30 mmHg threshold, which together with the clinical findings confirms compartment syndrome; emergent four-compartment fasciotomy is required"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta pressure = diastolic blood pressure minus compartment pressure: 72 − 45 = 27 mmHg. A delta-P at or below 30 mmHg supports fasciotomy, and this patient also has the clinical picture of compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion despite morphine, a tense anterior compartment, and first dorsal web space paresthesias (deep peroneal nerve). Emergent four-compartment fasciotomy is required, and when the clinical signs are unequivocal, pressure measurement should never delay surgery. Using systolic or mean arterial pressure overestimates perfusion, a delta-P of 27 mmHg is not adequate perfusion, an absolute-pressure cutoff of 50 mmHg is too high, and attributing the pain to opioid tolerance risks missing an evolving compartment syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Delta-P = diastolic BP minus compartment pressure. Fasciotomy threshold: delta-P at or below 30 mmHg. Clinical signs precede pressure changes — never delay fasciotomy for pressure measurement when the exam is diagnostic. COMLEX: Four leg compartments: anterior, lateral, deep posterior, superficial posterior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q41","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-month-old is brought to the emergency department by her parents after they noticed she stopped walking. X-ray shows a non-displaced spiral lucency of the mid-tibial diaphysis. Parents report no witnessed trauma. What is the diagnosis, and what is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"A non-accidental injury fracture pattern requiring mandatory child protective services report, because spiral tibial fractures in toddlers are pathognomonic for child abuse and cannot result from accidental mechanisms","B":"A pathologic fracture through a unicameral bone cyst requiring urgent orthopaedic referral and biopsy to rule out malignancy, since non-displaced spiral fractures in toddlers without witnessed trauma are suspicious for underlying bone disease","C":"A toddler's fracture, a non-displaced spiral tibial shaft fracture from a minor twisting mechanism in a young child just learning to walk, treated with simple immobilization with expected full recovery","D":"A Salter-Harris Type I physeal fracture of the distal tibia rather than a diaphyseal fracture, and it requires reduction followed by casting to prevent physeal arrest given the growth plate involvement in this age group","E":"A stress fracture from excessive parental pressure for early ambulation requiring behavioral counseling and reduced weight-bearing activity, since 18-month-old children should not yet be expected to walk and premature ambulation overloads the tibial cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Toddler's fracture is a classic diagnosis in young children just learning to walk (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12; Nelson notes it especially in children younger than 6 years) who sustain a minor twisting injury. The fracture is characteristically non-displaced and spiral, occurring in the tibial diaphysis. It presents with refusal to bear weight or limp. The mechanism is often minor and unwitnessed. Treatment is simple immobilization. It is NOT pathognomonic for abuse, though non-accidental injury must always be in the differential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Toddler's fracture = non-displaced spiral tibial fracture in a child learning to walk. Often unwitnessed, benign minor mechanism. Simple immobilization. Not pathognomonic for abuse — evaluate clinical context. COMLEX: Classic test question: distinguish toddler's fracture from abuse. Key factors: age, fracture pattern, multiple fractures, delayed presentation, inconsistent history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q42","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 36-year-old sustains a Gustilo Type IIIB open tibial shaft fracture in a motorcycle crash with a 5 cm wound and periosteal stripping. What is the most critical time-sensitive intervention, and what is the recommended sequence of management?","choices":{"A":"Emergency wound closure by the plastic surgery team is the most time-critical intervention, as the 6-hour window for primary closure determines whether the wound can be closed or requires a rotational flap with significantly higher complication risk","B":"Intravenous antibiotics begun immediately after injury are the most time-critical intervention, followed by emergent irrigation and debridement in the operating room, temporary external fixation, and delayed wound closure after serial debridement","C":"Definitive intramedullary nailing at the index procedure is the priority to achieve mechanical stability before wound colonization occurs, since the first 6 hours represent the golden window for definitive fixation in contaminated open fractures","D":"CT angiography of the affected extremity is the first priority to exclude popliteal artery injury before any other intervention, as vascular repair must precede both wound management and fracture stabilization in all high-energy tibial fractures","E":"Wound culture and sensitivity testing before antibiotic administration ensures that prophylaxis is targeted to the specific organisms present in the contaminating environment, avoiding unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotic use and resistance selection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most time-critical intervention in open fracture management is IV antibiotics begun immediately to prevent osteomyelitis (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Cefazolin covers gram-positive organisms for Gustilo Types I and II. Gram-negative coverage is added for Type III. Management sequence: antibiotics in ED, then emergent OR debridement, then temporary external fixation, then serial debridement every 48-72 hours until clean, then soft tissue reconstruction, then definitive fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture antibiotics begun immediately — the single most important intervention. Gustilo IIIB requires plastic surgery for soft tissue coverage. Do NOT close primarily. Serial debridements every 48-72 hours. COMLEX: Antibiotics for open fractures: cefazolin for Type I-II; add aminoglycoside for Type III; add penicillin G for farm contamination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: periostitis of the posteromedial tibia as the overuse-spectrum counterpart to tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q43","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old soccer player pronates his ankle forcefully. In the emergency department he has medial ankle tenderness and ecchymosis, but ankle X-rays show only mild mortise widening without a fibular fracture. On palpation, he has proximal fibular tenderness. What is the full injury diagnosis, and what additional imaging is mandatory?","choices":{"A":"A Maisonneuve fracture with syndesmotic disruption and deltoid ligament injury; full-length tibia-fibula radiographs are mandatory to identify the proximal fibular fracture that will be missed on ankle films alone","B":"An isolated deltoid ligament sprain with ankle mortise stress injury; no additional imaging beyond standard ankle views is needed since the mortise widening is mild and the patient should be treated with immobilization and gradual weight bearing","C":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from a torsional mechanism; knee radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the proximal tibiofibular articulation and rule out concurrent lateral compartment injury at the knee","D":"A stress fracture of the fibular shaft from chronic repetitive soccer training that was acutely displaced by the pronation mechanism; MRI of the entire fibula is mandatory to determine the extent of stress reaction before planning treatment","E":"A Lisfranc midfoot injury with tarsometatarsal disruption that referred pain proximally; weight-bearing foot radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the Lisfranc joint, and the proximal fibular tenderness represents a referred pain pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation mechanism. The medial ankle structures fail first, then the force transmits proximally along the interosseous membrane, producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle appears relatively normal on standard views, but mortise widening indicates instability. Full-length tibia-fibula films are mandatory. This injury requires surgical fixation including syndesmotic stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibula fracture from ankle mechanism. Mortise widening without distal fibular fracture = get full leg films. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture. Unstable ankle requiring syndesmotic screw fixation. COMLEX: Classic boards pitfall: treat the ankle, miss the proximal fibula.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q44","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female sustains a distal fibular fracture at the level of the ankle mortise. On clinical examination, the medial ankle is tender. Stress examination under fluoroscopy shows widening of the medial clear space with external rotation force. What is the Danis-Weber classification and what is the management implication of the stress finding?","choices":{"A":"Weber A fracture below the syndesmosis; the medial clear space widening indicates associated deltoid ligament sprain rather than rupture, and can be managed non-operatively since below-syndesmosis fractures are inherently stable regardless of ligamentous tenderness","B":"Weber C fracture above the syndesmosis; syndesmotic disruption is confirmed by the proximal fibular fracture level, and the stress examination finding of medial widening is expected for this fracture type and does not add additional management information","C":"Weber B fracture at the syndesmotic level; the medial clear space widening on stress examination indicates deltoid ligament rupture, converting this from a potentially stable to a definitively unstable ankle fracture requiring surgical fixation","D":"A bimalleolar equivalent fracture by definition; any fibular fracture with medial ankle tenderness must be treated as a bimalleolar fracture with immediate surgical fixation of the fibula and medial ankle reconstruction without further stress testing","E":"An unstable fracture-dislocation of the ankle; the mortise widening indicates complete dislocation of the talus, and emergent closed reduction with sedation must be performed immediately before any further diagnostic workup or imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A distal fibular fracture at the level of the joint line is Danis-Weber type B. The course reading (Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Orthopedics Ch 2, SDL 25) notes that with type B, if the medial malleolus is intact there can be a tear of the deltoid ligament, and that a lateral malleolus fracture with complete deltoid rupture is considered bimalleolar on a functional basis. Medial clear space widening under stress shows the talus shifting laterally because the deltoid no longer restrains it — a second break in the ankle ring (SDL 9 ring-scheme slide) — so the mortise is unstable; the same reading notes that a lateral talar shift of as little as 1 mm decreases tibiotalar contact by 40%, which is why the fracture is fixed surgically. Type A is below the joint line and type C above the syndesmosis; medial tenderness alone does not establish a functional bimalleolar injury without the stress finding; and stress widening is not a talar dislocation needing emergent reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber B = at the syndesmosis = variable stability; medial clear space widening on stress means deltoid rupture. COMLEX: Lateral malleolus fracture plus complete deltoid rupture is a functional bimalleolar fracture (course SDL 25 reading) and is unstable; 1 mm of lateral talar shift reduces tibiotalar contact by 40%.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q45","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male football player is struck on the lateral proximal leg just below the knee. He immediately develops foot drop with weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension, and reports numbness over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space. Which nerve is injured, at what specific anatomical location, and what is the expected prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The deep peroneal nerve at the anterior ankle retinaculum, where it passes under the extensor retinaculum and can be compressed by a tight boot or direct blow, producing dorsal foot numbness and toe extension weakness without complete foot drop","B":"The common peroneal nerve at the fibular neck, where it wraps around the posterolateral fibular head in a superficial location that makes it vulnerable to direct compression and traction injury, with variable recovery depending on severity of axonal injury","C":"The superficial peroneal nerve at the crural fascia in the distal lateral leg, where it perforates the deep fascia and is vulnerable to injury, producing only sensory loss over the dorsolateral foot without any motor weakness or foot drop","D":"The tibial nerve at the popliteal fossa, where it travels with the popliteal artery and can be injured by posterior knee dislocation, producing plantarflexion weakness and plantar foot numbness rather than the dorsiflexion weakness seen here","E":"The saphenous nerve at the medial knee, where it exits the adductor canal and descends along the medial leg, producing purely sensory deficits along the medial leg and foot without any motor involvement or foot drop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The common peroneal nerve wraps around the fibular neck just below the lateral knee and is covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat at that level. It is the most commonly injured nerve in the lower extremity, highly vulnerable to direct trauma, compression, or fracture at the fibular head. Injury produces foot drop from tibialis anterior paralysis and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot including the first web space. Recovery depends on nerve continuity — neuropraxia recovers in weeks to months; axonotmesis may take months to a year.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Common peroneal nerve at fibular neck = foot drop. Most commonly injured lower extremity nerve. Causes: direct blow, fibular head fracture, tight cast, prolonged leg crossing. First web space numbness is pathognomonic. COMLEX: Also injured during fibular head fracture reduction — protect the nerve during all proximal fibular procedures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q46","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a displaced intra-articular fracture of the distal tibia at the ankle, and a 40-year-old woman has a displaced intra-articular fracture of the acetabulum. Both heal with a small residual step in the joint surface. According to the course reading, why is the ankle more likely than the hip to develop post-traumatic arthritis from the same degree of incongruity?","choices":{"A":"The ankle is non-weight-bearing in normal gait, so its thin cartilage never adapts to load and wears out quickly once the surface is uneven","B":"The ankle cartilage has its own blood supply that is torn by the fracture, whereas hip cartilage is fed by synovial fluid and keeps living","C":"The ankle is weight-bearing with motion limited to one plane, whereas the hip is a universal joint that shifts to spread abnormal forces","D":"The ankle has no capsule or ligaments, so even a small step lets the talus subluxate freely while the hip is held by a thick capsule","E":"The ankle fractures always involve the physis, which stops growth and creates a progressive deformity that the adult hip does not develop"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fracture management reading explains that intra-articular fractures that change the shape of the joint surface accelerate cartilage wear, and that the ankle and knee are especially susceptible because they are weight-bearing joints with motion limited to a single flexion-extension plane. The hip is also weight-bearing, but it is a universal joint that can move in any plane, so it can shift its position and spread abnormal forces over its surface. The reading adds that fractures are the leading cause of arthritis in the ankle. The ankle is weight-bearing, not non-weight-bearing. Articular cartilage has no blood supply in either joint. The ankle has a capsule and strong ligaments. These are adult injuries, so physeal growth is not a factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fractures cause arthritis by chondrocyte death, angular malunion loading adjacent joints, intra-articular incongruity, and denser, less elastic healed bone. COMLEX: Displaced intra-articular ankle fractures need anatomic reduction; even 1 mm of talar shift sharply cuts contact area.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — talar shift and loss of tibiotalar contact area"},{"id":"sdl9_q47","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman slips on ice and sustains a bimalleolar ankle fracture. Radiograph shows 3 mm lateral talar shift within the ankle mortise. An orthopedic resident asks the attending why this small amount of displacement is clinically significant. What is the physiological explanation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral talar shift stretches the deltoid ligament beyond its recoverable elastic range, causing permanent elongation that will prevent the ankle from returning to its stable weight-bearing position after fracture healing is complete","B":"Even 1 mm of lateral talar shift reduces tibiotalar contact area by about 40 percent, and more shift reduces it further, sharply raising peak contact stress on the remaining cartilage and predicting early post-traumatic arthritis","C":"Lateral talar shift by 3 mm indicates that the fibula is shortened by the same amount, and fibular shortening of 3 mm is the threshold above which the ankle develops a fixed valgus deformity that cannot be corrected by cast immobilization alone","D":"Lateral talar shift represents subtalar joint subluxation rather than tibiotalar shift, and the subtalar joint is more sensitive to small displacements than the tibiotalar joint because of its complex three-dimensional motion requirements","E":"Lateral talar shift indicates complete rupture of all four ankle ligament complexes simultaneously, which is the threshold injury required to produce even 1 mm of talar displacement under physiologic loading conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tibiotalar joint has a small articular surface area compared to the hip or knee. Lateral talar shift dramatically reduces this already-limited contact area. One millimeter of lateral shift reduces tibiotalar contact by about 40% (course reading), and larger shifts reduce it further. This creates focal stress concentration on the remaining articular cartilage, accelerating chondral wear and post-traumatic arthritis. This biomechanical reality is the primary justification for anatomic surgical reduction of ankle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral talar shift 1 mm = ~40% contact area loss; more shift = more loss. Even subtle shift = dramatically increased arthritis risk. COMLEX: This is why ankle fractures with mortise disruption require anatomic ORIF — the tibiotalar joint cannot tolerate any incongruity without long-term consequences.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"},{"id":"sdl9_q48","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old sustains a trimalleolar ankle fracture. CT scan shows the posterior malleolus fragment involves 30 percent of the articular surface. The orthopedic surgeon decides to fix all three malleoli. What rationale justifies fixing the posterior malleolus specifically, beyond achieving articular reduction?","choices":{"A":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis because the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, and reducing the fragment anatomically restores syndesmotic integrity without a separate syndesmotic screw","B":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents Achilles tendon avulsion from its calcaneal insertion by reducing the posterior tibial cortex, which transmits tensile forces from the triceps surae through the posterior ankle capsule to the posterior malleolus","C":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is required in all trimalleolar fractures regardless of fragment size because any posterior bony fragment creates a mechanical block to ankle dorsiflexion that prevents return to normal gait without operative reduction","D":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents propagation of the fracture anteriorly into the tibial plafond under weight-bearing stress because the unfixed posterior fragment acts as a stress riser for pilon-type fracture extension in the early post-operative period","E":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is necessary to reattach the posterior tibiotalar ligament at its tibial origin, since ligamentous avulsions at the posterior malleolus heal inadequately without bony fixation due to the poor vascularity of the posterior ankle capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, a component of the syndesmotic complex, attaches to the posterior malleolus. When the posterior malleolus fragment is anatomically reduced and fixed, this ligament is restored to its normal length and tension, which substantially stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis. This is one of the main reasons to fix posterior malleolus fragments over 25% to 33% (a traditional threshold the SDL 25 reading calls controversial) — it reduces or eliminates the need for a separate syndesmotic screw.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to posterior malleolus. Fixing the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the syndesmosis. Fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface = traditional fixation threshold (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: This is a frequently tested syndesmotic anatomy point — the syndesmotic complex includes the AITFL, PITFL, and interosseous membrane.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"Pediatric Overuse Injuries & Apophysitis","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to urgent care after her father pulled her arm to keep her from running into traffic. She now holds her left arm adducted and refuses to move it. Which of the following best describes the mechanism that produces radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"Forced supination of the forearm causes the radial head to displace anteriorly against the capitellum, rupturing the annular ligament and producing a stable partial dislocation that requires reduction under procedural sedation","B":"A fall onto an outstretched hand transfers axial load up the radius, compressing the radial head against the capitellum and producing a buckle-type physeal injury at the proximal radial growth plate in young children","C":"Axial traction on a pronated, extended forearm causes the annular ligament to slip distally over the radial head and become interposed between the radial head and capitellum in the radiohumeral joint, producing the subluxation","D":"Valgus stress applied to the elbow during a throwing motion causes the radial head to rotate excessively within the annular ligament, producing microavulsions at the ligament proximal attachment to the lateral ulna","E":"Hyperextension of the elbow from a swinging lift causes the olecranon to lever against the posterior humerus, driving the radial head anteriorly through the annular ligament and producing a complete anterior radial head dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Radial head subluxation occurs when axial traction is applied to a pronated, extended forearm. The annular ligament, which holds the radial head in place, slips distally over the radial head and becomes interposed between the radial head and capitellum. This is the classic mechanism of nursemaid's elbow, typically occurring when a caregiver pulls or swings a young child by the arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic mechanism = axial traction on pronated extended forearm causes annular ligament to slip over radial head. Age 1-4, F > M, left > right. Reduction: supination with flexion produces a palpable click. COMLEX: No imaging needed unless age >5 or atypical mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q02","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric emergency physician notes that nursemaid's elbow accounts for a large volume of pediatric elbow injuries. Which of the following epidemiological statements about radial head subluxation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Radial head subluxation accounts for approximately 20,000 cases per year in the United States, occurs mostly between ages 1 and 4 with 80 percent of cases at ages 1 to 3, is more common in females, and more often affects the left arm","B":"Radial head subluxation occurs most commonly between ages 5 and 10, is more common in males than females, and is equally distributed between left and right arms because the mechanism of injury is symmetric and not influenced by caregiver handedness","C":"Radial head subluxation occurs almost exclusively in infants under age 12 months due to extreme ligamentous laxity present in early infancy, and it rarely recurs after spontaneous resolution because the annular ligament matures and thickens rapidly","D":"Radial head subluxation affects children up to age 12, occurs with equal frequency in males and females, and is most commonly bilateral because caregivers typically use both hands to lift children by their arms simultaneously","E":"Radial head subluxation primarily affects children between ages 1 and 4, is more common in males than females due to higher activity levels, and most commonly affects the right arm because most caregivers are right-handed and hold the child by the right arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Radial head subluxation affects approximately 20,000 children per year in the US. It is most common between ages 1 and 4, with 80% of cases in the 1-to-3 age range, reflecting the period when the annular ligament is lax relative to the radial head. It is more common in females and more often affects the left arm, likely because right-handed caregivers pull the child's left arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 1-4 peak; F > M; left > right. 20,000 cases/year US. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — no imaging unless atypical. Mechanism and demographics are high-yield for question stems.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q03","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to clinic after a yanking injury to his arm. He holds the arm adducted to the body with the elbow extended and the forearm slightly pronated. Which of the following additional findings is most consistent with radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"The child has visible swelling and ecchymosis over the lateral elbow, passive pronation reproduces pain more than supination, and he allows full elbow flexion but refuses any forearm rotation in either direction with marked resistance","B":"The child has a prominent posterior bulge at the olecranon from posterior displacement of the ulna, and the elbow is locked in slight flexion with complete refusal to allow any passive range of motion including supination, pronation, and flexion","C":"The child screams with lateral elbow palpation and cannot passively flex the elbow beyond 90 degrees due to guarding from protective muscle spasm of the biceps brachii, which prevents forearm rotation in any direction","D":"The child has no visible deformity, demonstrates pain specifically with attempted supination of the forearm, may have mild tenderness to palpation at the radial head, and otherwise has normal passive range of elbow motion","E":"The child holds the arm in a sling position with the elbow flexed to 90 degrees, has significant swelling over the antecubital fossa, and demonstrates specific point tenderness over the medial epicondyle with valgus stress testing of the elbow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial head subluxation presents with the arm adducted, the elbow extended or slightly flexed, and the forearm pronated. Pain occurs specifically with supination because this motion attempts to slide the annular ligament back over the radial head. There is no significant swelling, no visible deformity, and the child typically has normal passive range of motion in all planes except supination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key exam findings = arm adducted, elbow extended, forearm pronated, pain with supination, no swelling/deformity. Normal X-ray. COMLEX: Reduction by supination with flexion at elbow — palpable click = success, child uses arm within minutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q04","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male competitive pitcher presents with progressive right shoulder pain during and after pitching. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral growth plate on the right compared to the left. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathology?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy from repetitive overhead motion causes progressive inflammation of the supraspinatus tendon at its insertion on the greater tuberosity, resulting in impingement under the coracoacromial arch during the late acceleration phase of throwing","B":"Repetitive throwing stress produces a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate, with physeal widening and pain during or after throwing; this condition, most common in adolescent male pitchers, is little league shoulder","C":"Calcific tendinosis of the biceps long head tendon at its attachment to the superior labrum produces SLAP lesions that extend into the posterior labrum and cause clicking during the late cocking phase in overhead throwing athletes","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete causes capsular laxity and progressive superior migration of the distal clavicle relative to the acromion during pitching","E":"Repetitive microtrauma to the posterior glenoid labrum during the deceleration phase of pitching produces labral tears that destabilize the glenohumeral joint and cause superior humeral head migration during the follow-through phase of the throw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a stress reaction or Salter-Harris Type I physeal injury of the proximal humeral growth plate caused by repetitive rotational forces during throwing. It most commonly affects adolescent male pitchers aged 11-16, with a peak at age 13. X-ray may show physeal widening compared to the contralateral shoulder. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and PT, with pitch count programs for prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress reaction; age 11-16, peak 13, males. X-ray widening of growth plate. Treatment: rest up to 3 months. COMLEX: Prevention by pitch count limits is testable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q05","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow after presenting with medial elbow pain worse with pitching. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathomechanics of this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive extension forces during ball release place tensile stress on the lateral collateral ligament complex, producing chronic lateral elbow instability and progressive osteochondral damage to the capitellum in young throwing athletes","B":"Combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching produce repetitive traction stress on the medial epicondylar growth plate, causing apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondyle","C":"Overuse of the flexor-pronator mass from repetitive wrist flexion during follow-through causes tendinous degeneration at the medial epicondyle, producing a condition identical to adult medial epicondylitis but with greater recurrence due to open growth plates","D":"Repetitive compressive forces from the medial coronoid against the trochlea during the acceleration phase produce subchondral bone edema at the trochlea, progressing to articular cartilage fragmentation in severe or untreated cases","E":"Repetitive supination of the forearm during the pronation-to-supination transition in the late acceleration phase causes the annular ligament to become inflamed, entrapping the posterior interosseous nerve and producing lateral elbow pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Little league elbow is apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondylar growth plate caused by combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching. The medial epicondylar growth plate normally closes in the mid-teens, making it vulnerable during adolescence. It most commonly affects pitchers aged 9-14, with avulsion fractures more common in ages 12-14.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysitis or avulsion from valgus and traction forces during pitching. Age 9-14. Growth plate closes mid-teens. X-ray shows medial epicondyle widening or displacement. COMLEX: Treatment = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT; surgery rare but indicated for significant displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q06","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow. X-ray shows medial epicondyle apophyseal widening without fragment displacement. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Rest from throwing for 4 to 6 weeks, analgesic therapy for pain control, physical therapy for gradual rehabilitation, and return to sport guided by an age-appropriate pitch count program to prevent recurrence","B":"Immediate operative fixation with percutaneous screw placement across the medial epicondyle is indicated for all cases of little league elbow, because any delay in surgical stabilization results in permanent medial instability from growth plate damage","C":"A long arm cast applied with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full supination is standard conservative treatment, maintained for 8 to 12 weeks regardless of fragment size or degree of physeal widening on radiograph","D":"Corticosteroid injection at the medial epicondyle is first-line treatment for little league elbow, providing rapid anti-inflammatory relief that allows safe return to throwing within 1 to 2 weeks when combined with a counterforce brace","E":"Transition to batting-only practice with continued half-effort throwing is an acceptable alternative to full rest in young athletes, as partial activity maintains muscle conditioning while allowing gradual healing of the growth plate apophysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Non-displaced little league elbow is managed conservatively with 4-6 weeks of rest from throwing, analgesics, and physical therapy. Return to sport should be gradual and guided by pitch count programs, which are the primary preventive tool. Surgery is reserved for significantly displaced fragments or cases with valgus instability, which are uncommon at this age without a clear avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced little league elbow = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT. Surgery rare. Prevention = pitch counts. COMLEX: Distinguish from adult medial epicondylitis — in children, the growth plate is the weak link, not the UCL.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q07","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male athlete presents with anterior knee pain and a prominent, tender bump just below the kneecap that worsens with running, jumping, and kneeling. He is in a period of rapid growth. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Patellofemoral syndrome caused by maltracking of the patella in the trochlear groove, producing retropatellar cartilage friction and diffuse peripatellar pain with prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting in rapidly growing adolescent athletes","B":"Patellar tendinopathy from repetitive microtrauma at the inferior pole of the patella, producing degenerative changes within the tendon substance and localized swelling at the superior insertion of the patellar tendon on the patella itself","C":"Prepatellar bursitis from repetitive mechanical irritation of the superficial bursa anterior to the patella, producing soft tissue swelling that is fluctuant on palpation and localizes to the anterior patella rather than the tibial tuberosity","D":"Stress fracture of the proximal tibial metaphysis from repetitive compressive loading, producing point tenderness over the medial tibial flare and periosteal new bone formation visible on plain radiographs of the proximal tibia below the joint line","E":"Traction apophysitis at the insertion of the patellar tendon on the tibial tuberosity, caused by repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid skeletal growth, producing pain and bony prominence at the tibial tuberosity that worsens with knee flexion activities"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is traction apophysitis at the tibial tuberosity, where the patellar tendon inserts. Repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid growth applies repetitive traction stress to the tibial tuberosity apophysis, causing inflammation, pain, and eventual bony prominence. It is most common in active adolescents aged 9-14, predominantly males, and bilateral in 25-50% of cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = traction apophysitis at tibial tuberosity, patellar tendon insertion. Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Pain with knee flexion activities. COMLEX: No imaging required. Treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q08","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following most accurately describes the epidemiology of Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 5 and 9, is equally common in males and females, and almost always presents bilaterally because patellar tendon stress is symmetric across both knees during growth spurts in all active children","B":"Osgood-Schlatter disease most commonly affects children between ages 9 and 14, is more common in males than females, carries approximately double the risk in athletes compared to non-athletes, and presents bilaterally in 25 to 50 percent of cases","C":"Osgood-Schlatter disease primarily affects obese adolescents between ages 12 and 17 and is more common in females, since a high-riding patella in females increases patellar tendon length and amplifies tensile stress at the tibial tuberosity during running","D":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is equally common across all age groups from 8 to 18, shows no sex predilection, and is more prevalent in sedentary adolescents because inactivity leads to relative tightening of the quadriceps mechanism during growth","E":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 6 and 12, is more common in females due to earlier onset of growth spurts, and accounts for the majority of all anterior knee pain presentations seen in pediatric primary care visits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease peaks at ages 9-14, coinciding with the adolescent growth spurt. Males are more commonly affected. Athletes have approximately double the risk compared to non-athletes. Bilateral presentation occurs in 25-50% of cases. The incidence in adolescents aged 12-15 has been estimated at approximately 9.83%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Rapid growth is the key predisposing factor. COMLEX: The fact that imaging is not necessary is commonly tested — Osgood-Schlatter is a clinical diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q09","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old soccer player is diagnosed with Osgood-Schlatter disease. He trains 5 days per week. Which of the following best describes the correct treatment approach?","choices":{"A":"Treatment centers on rebalancing the ratio of physical stress to recovery time, supported by physical therapy for quadriceps flexibility and strengthening, analgesics for pain control, and activity modification rather than complete cessation of sport","B":"Complete immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 to 8 weeks is indicated to allow the apophysis to heal before resuming activity, as continued sport participation without immobilization consistently leads to avulsion fracture of the tibial tuberosity in growing athletes","C":"Surgical excision of the tibial tuberosity apophysis is the definitive treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease in athletes who fail 4 weeks of conservative management, as prolonged apophyseal inflammation leads to a painful ossicle requiring operative removal","D":"Intraarticular corticosteroid injection into the infrapatellar region is first-line treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease, providing immediate anti-inflammatory relief and allowing rapid return to sport when combined with a patellar tendon strap brace","E":"Complete cessation of all athletic activity for a minimum of 3 months is required, as continued sport participation accelerates apophyseal fragmentation and significantly increases the long-term risk of tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture in skeletally immature athletes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of Osgood-Schlatter disease focuses on rebalancing the ratio of stress to recovery rather than mandating complete rest. Physical therapy addresses quadriceps tightness and strengthening, analgesics manage pain, and activity modification reduces apophyseal load. Regenerative injection therapy such as prolotherapy is an option for refractory cases. Complete cessation of sport is generally not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter treatment = rebalance stress vs recovery, PT, analgesics, activity modification. Not immobilization or surgery in most cases. COMLEX: Prolotherapy is mentioned as a regenerative option for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q10","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with insidious onset left hip pain and limp. Hip X-ray shows femoral head flattening with increased density. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological sequence of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Chronic repetitive loading of the femoral head causes cartilage degeneration at the articular surface, exposing subchondral bone to synovial fluid, which triggers inflammatory degradation of trabecular bone progressing inward from the articular margin","B":"Inadequate blood flow to the femoral head causes ischemic necrosis of the epiphysis; new bone deposits on avascular trabeculae while calcification of necrotic marrow crushes them, distorting the femoral head and risking subluxation from the acetabulum","C":"Autoimmune synovitis causes inflammatory mediators to diffuse into the femoral head, producing reactive bone resorption at the subchondral plate and secondary avascular necrosis from cytokine-mediated vascular thrombosis in the epiphyseal vessels","D":"Repetitive microtrauma to the proximal femoral physis from running and jumping activities produces stress fractures through the growth plate, causing progressive physeal widening and subsequent collapse of the femoral head under axial weight-bearing load","E":"Slippage of the proximal femoral epiphysis through a weakened physis produces mechanical impingement of the femoral neck against the acetabulum, causing secondary avascular necrosis of the femoral head from repetitive vascular compression during weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children. The sequence is: inadequate blood flow causes ischemic epiphyseal death, new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae, calcification of necrotic marrow crushes the avascular scaffold, and femoral head distortion progresses to subluxation or dislocation. Initial X-rays may be negative; bone scan and MRI detect it earlier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP = AVN of femoral head in children age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%. Pathology: ischemia leads to femoral head collapse and distortion. Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI or bone scan detect earlier. COMLEX: Decreased abduction and IR on exam; can refer to knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q11","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A parent brings their 5-year-old son to clinic with a 2-week history of limp. He occasionally complains of knee pain. Hip X-ray is initially unremarkable. Which of the following best characterizes the typical presentation and epidemiology of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents acutely with severe hip pain, fever, and refusal to bear weight in children aged 10 to 15, with a male-to-female ratio of 1:4 and bilateral involvement in approximately 50 percent of cases at the time of initial diagnosis","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly presents between ages 8 and 12 with bilateral hip pain and a waddling gait, affects females more commonly than males at a ratio of 4:1, and is associated with rapid growth spurts causing relative vascular insufficiency","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents in overweight adolescents aged 12 to 15 with acute hip and groin pain after minor trauma, with equal sex distribution, bilateral involvement in 30 percent, and external rotation deformity on passive hip examination","D":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly affects children aged 4 to 9, with a male-to-female ratio of 4:1, and typically presents with insidious onset hip pain, antalgic limp, pain that may refer to the knee, and decreased hip abduction and internal rotation on examination","E":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents subacutely in children aged 2 to 6 with bilateral hip stiffness and a Trendelenburg gait, affects females more than males, and is strongly associated with a prior episode of septic hip arthritis in the majority of cases"},"correct":"D","explanation":"LCP occurs in children aged 4-9 with a 4:1 male predominance. Presentation is insidious onset hip pain, limp, and referred knee pain. Exam shows decreased hip abduction and internal rotation. Bilateral involvement occurs in only 10% of cases. Initial radiographs may be normal, requiring bone scan or MRI for early diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP demographics: age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%, insidious onset. Classic exam = decreased abduction and IR. Referred knee pain is a trap — always examine the hip in a limping child. COMLEX: MRI or bone scan when X-ray is negative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q12","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male with suspected Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has normal plain radiographs on initial presentation but persistent hip pain and limp. Which of the following is most accurate regarding imaging and management of this condition?","choices":{"A":"Because plain radiographs are normal, Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease can be excluded and the child should be reassured and observed; the condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are both interpreted as normal by a radiologist","B":"Ultrasound of the hip is the definitive imaging modality for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating increased echogenicity of the femoral head cartilage before X-ray changes appear, and treatment consists of ultrasound-guided aspiration of the joint effusion","C":"Bone scan and MRI can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease before radiographic changes appear; conservative management with NSAIDs, activity restriction, physical therapy, and bracing or casting for containment is first-line, with surgery reserved for cases failing conservative care","D":"CT scan of the pelvis with contrast is the most sensitive initial imaging study for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating contrast pooling in the femoral head, and treatment always requires immediate surgical core decompression to restore femoral head perfusion","E":"Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scanning of the hip is indicated when plain films are normal, demonstrating reduced bone mineral density of the femoral head before structural changes appear, and treatment is calcium and vitamin D supplementation to improve remineralization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in LCP, and MRI or bone scan should be obtained when clinical suspicion remains high. Conservative management includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain range of motion, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during the revascularization phase. Surgery is reserved for failure of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP imaging: Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI and bone scan detect earlier. Treatment: NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing/casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Maintaining containment of femoral head in acetabulum is the therapeutic goal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q13","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old obese male presents with 3 weeks of dull left hip pain and a limp. He denies trauma. On exam, the left leg is externally rotated. Hip radiographs are obtained and confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathology?","choices":{"A":"The proximal femoral epiphysis undergoes avascular necrosis due to disruption of the lateral epiphyseal arterial supply from within the hip joint capsule, causing the femoral head to collapse into the acetabulum in the absence of any significant shear force","B":"Repetitive impact loading causes a stress fracture through the femoral neck at the subcapital region, producing gradual anterior and superior displacement of the femoral head relative to the femoral neck in overweight adolescent males during rapid growth","C":"Acetabular labral hypertrophy from compensatory overload causes progressive anterior femoral head coverage deficit, producing cam-type femoroacetabular impingement and secondary physeal stress in overweight adolescent males during peak growth","D":"Inflammatory synovitis from a prior viral illness causes the proximal femoral growth plate to weaken through cytokine-mediated degradation of physeal cartilage, allowing the femoral neck to rotate anteriorly relative to the fixed femoral head during the preslip phase","E":"Shearing forces through a physiologically weakened proximal femoral physis cause the femoral head epiphysis to slip posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck, producing the characteristic external rotation deformity and antalgic gait seen in this presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE is a developmental condition in which the proximal femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck through a weakened physis. The physis is weakened by physiologic hormonal changes during puberty, exacerbated by obesity, metabolic disorders, and other factors. The result is the classic presentation of an obese adolescent with dull atraumatic hip pain, antalgic gait, and external rotation deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE = posterior and inferior epiphyseal slip through weakened proximal femoral physis. Classic = obese adolescent, dull atraumatic hip pain, external rotation deformity. Radiographs mandatory. COMLEX: Referred knee pain is a trap; always X-ray the hip.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q14","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female with SCFE is classified as unstable. Which of the following best describes how SCFE is staged and what determines stability classification?","choices":{"A":"SCFE is staged entirely by patient age: preslip in children under age 10, acute SCFE in those aged 10 to 12, and chronic SCFE in those over age 12; stability is determined by whether bilateral hip radiographs show symmetric or asymmetric epiphyseal height","B":"SCFE stability is based solely on the degree of epiphyseal slip angle on radiograph: mild slip below 30 degrees is stable, moderate slip 30 to 50 degrees is borderline, and severe slip above 50 degrees is unstable regardless of functional weight-bearing status","C":"SCFE is classified by duration into preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks; stable SCFE allows weight bearing without pain and unstable SCFE causes a painful gait, with a much higher AVN risk when unstable","D":"SCFE is classified only by duration, with acute lasting under 3 weeks and chronic lasting over 3 months; stability is determined by MRI perfusion imaging of the femoral head showing whether lateral epiphyseal vessels remain patent on gadolinium sequences","E":"SCFE staging uses the modified Loder classification exclusively based on slip width: stage 1 is less than one-third physis displacement, stage 2 is one-third to two-thirds, and stage 3 is greater than two-thirds; all stage 3 cases are automatically classified as unstable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SCFE is classified by duration: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks. Stability is determined clinically: stable SCFE allows weight-bearing without pain, while unstable SCFE presents with a painful gait. Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN because ongoing physeal shear disrupts the remaining blood supply to the femoral head.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE stages: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, chronic. Stable = bears weight without pain; unstable = painful gait. Unstable = high AVN risk. COMLEX: All SCFE = non-weight bearing + urgent/emergent operative fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q15","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male presents with stable SCFE confirmed on radiograph. He walks without crutches and bears weight without pain, with a mild limp. Which of the following best describes initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate open reduction with periacetabular osteotomy is indicated for all SCFE presentations to prevent progressive AVN, as conservative management consistently results in femoral head collapse in the majority of pediatric patients with any degree of physeal slip","B":"The patient should be made non-weight bearing and referred urgently for operative fixation, the standard of care for SCFE regardless of stability, because further slippage with weight bearing increases the risk of avascular necrosis","C":"A hip abduction brace applied in 30 degrees of abduction is first-line conservative treatment for stable SCFE, worn continuously for 8 to 12 weeks until radiographic evidence of physeal closure is confirmed on AP and frog-leg lateral projections","D":"NSAIDs and activity restriction for 6 to 8 weeks with repeat radiographs at 4-week intervals is standard conservative management for stable SCFE, reserving operative fixation for cases where the slip angle increases by more than 10 degrees on surveillance imaging","E":"Closed manipulation under anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 12 weeks is indicated for stable SCFE in adolescents under age 13, since surgical fixation carries an unacceptably high AVN risk from physeal instrumentation at this developmental stage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"All SCFE, regardless of stability, is managed with immediate non-weight bearing and urgent operative fixation. The standard surgical treatment is in situ fixation with a cannulated screw across the physis. Even in stable SCFE, continued weight-bearing risks further slip progression and AVN. Conservative management alone is insufficient for any confirmed SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE management = non-weight bearing immediately + operative fixation for all types. In situ screw fixation is standard. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction, as this increases AVN risk. Bilateral fixation may be considered for high-risk patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q16","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl rolls her ankle into inversion playing soccer. She has swelling and point tenderness directly over the distal fibular growth plate, about 1 to 2 cm above the tip of the lateral malleolus, and no tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament. Radiographs show no fracture line. Which interpretation best fits the course reading on pediatric ankle injuries?","choices":{"A":"A grade 2 ATFL sprain, because ligaments are the weakest link at this age and tear before bone or cartilage gives way","B":"A Salter-Harris type V crush of the distal tibial physis, because inversion axially compresses the medial tibial plate","C":"A torus fracture of the distal fibular metaphysis, because inversion buckles the lateral cortex without a visible line","D":"A Salter-Harris type I distal fibular injury, as the physis is the weakest link and slides apart before ligaments tear","E":"A normal examination with growth-plate tenderness, because physeal pain is expected after any sprain in a growing child"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson sports medicine chapter for this SDL notes that physeal injuries at the ankle, most commonly the distal fibula, typically follow an inversion injury and are predominantly Salter-Harris I fractures. In children younger than 12, the physeal plates are generally the weakest link and tend to slide or pull apart before the surrounding ligaments tear. A type I injury runs through the cartilaginous physis, which is not seen on radiographs, so point tenderness over the physis with normal films should be treated as a fracture. Calling it an ATFL sprain reverses the weak-link principle and ignores the site of tenderness. A type V crush involves axial compression, not an inversion pull on the fibula. A torus fracture is a visible cortical buckle at the metaphysis. Physeal tenderness is not an expected normal finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris (SALTR): I = straight across the physis (may look normal), II = above (metaphysis), III = lower (epiphysis), IV = through all, V = rammed/crushed. Type I has the lowest and type V the highest risk of growth disturbance. COMLEX: In a child, tenderness over the physis after a \"sprain\" = presumed SH I fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — lateral ankle sprain mechanism and grading in adults"},{"id":"sdl10_q17","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl falls onto an outstretched hand. X-ray shows localized bulging of the cortex at the distal radius metaphysis without a complete cortical break. Which of the following best explains this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"The greenstick pattern occurs in children because pediatric bone has higher mineral content than adult bone, causing the cortex to crack completely on the tension side while the compression side remains intact, producing an incomplete transverse fracture through the diaphysis","B":"The Salter-Harris Type I fracture produces physeal disruption without cortical injury when axial force is applied to an immature bone, causing the growth plate to fail in shear before the adjacent metaphyseal cortex accumulates enough strain to buckle","C":"The buckle fracture in adults occurs because cortical bone is more brittle than pediatric bone, causing complete fracture with axial load rather than the plastic deformation seen in younger patients with more porous and compliant metaphyseal cancellous bone","D":"The torus fracture represents an avulsion of the dorsal cortex at the distal radial metaphysis caused by tensile stress from dorsal radiocarpal ligaments during wrist hyperextension, producing a dorsal cortical flake fragment visible on lateral X-ray","E":"Pediatric bone is more porous and compliant than adult bone, so axial compressive force causes the cortex to buckle rather than fracture completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge of the torus fracture at the metaphysis without complete cortical disruption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Torus fractures occur because pediatric bone is more porous and mechanically compliant than adult bone. When axial compressive force is applied, the relatively porous metaphyseal cortex buckles without fracturing completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge. This is distinct from greenstick fractures, which result from bending forces and produce complete cortical failure on the tension side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus fracture = axial compression buckles cortex without complete fracture; pediatric bone is more porous/compliant. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks, other bends. COMLEX: Torus fractures are stable injuries treated conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q18","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a mid-shaft forearm injury after a fall. X-ray shows one cortex of the radius fractured completely while the opposite cortex is intact but angulated without a fracture line. Which of the following best characterizes this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture is a complete transverse fracture of both cortices of a long bone in a child that is identical to an adult fracture pattern, occurring when bending forces exceed the plastic deformation limit of pediatric bone at the diaphyseal-metaphyseal junction","B":"A greenstick fracture results from axial loading along the long axis of the bone, causing the periosteum to separate from the cortex on the compressive side without cortical fracture, producing a painful periosteal hematoma beneath the periosteal sleeve","C":"A greenstick fracture is an incomplete fracture unique to pediatric bone in which bending stress fractures one cortex completely while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, analogous to the behavior of a fresh green tree branch that breaks on one side when bent","D":"A greenstick fracture involves axial compression of the metaphyseal cortex without complete fracture, producing a localized cortical buckle at the point of maximum compressive stress, and is treated identically to torus fractures with a short arm cast for 3 weeks","E":"A greenstick fracture is a physeal fracture pattern in which the fracture line propagates along the zone of provisional calcification within the growth plate, producing a clean separation of the epiphysis from the metaphysis without cortical injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Greenstick fractures are incomplete fractures of pediatric long bones resulting from bending forces. One cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, because pediatric bone is more flexible and has a higher plastic deformation tolerance. The name derives from the analogy of bending a fresh green tree branch, which splits on one side before breaking completely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = bending force, one cortex fractures, other bends; pediatric only. Compare to torus = axial compression with cortical buckle, no complete fracture. COMLEX: Greenstick may require reduction to correct angulation before casting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q19","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old female is brought to the ED after her older sibling pulled her arm to help her up from the floor. She holds the left arm adducted, slightly pronated, and refuses to lift it. Elbow X-ray is normal. Vitals are stable. Which of the following describes the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Perform closed reduction by supinating the forearm while applying gentle pressure over the radial head and flexing the elbow; a palpable click indicates successful reduction, and most children regain normal arm function within minutes without imaging follow-up","B":"Apply a posterior long arm splint with the elbow at 90 degrees and the forearm in neutral rotation, and arrange orthopedic follow-up within 1 to 2 weeks for formal reduction under procedural sedation in a controlled surgical outpatient setting","C":"Obtain CT arthrography of the elbow to confirm annular ligament interposition and plan the optimal reduction approach, as blind closed reduction attempts can further displace the ligament and convert a subluxation to a complete elbow dislocation","D":"Administer oral ibuprofen for pain control and arrange return visit in 5 days to reassess, since nursemaid's elbow typically resolves spontaneously within 48 to 72 hours without formal reduction in children under age 3","E":"Refer to orthopedic surgery for arthroscopic removal of the interposed annular ligament, as closed reduction is contraindicated in children under age 3 due to the risk of physeal injury to the proximal radial growth plate during the supination maneuver"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nursemaid's elbow is treated with closed reduction using the supination-flexion or hyperpronation technique. In the supination-flexion method, the forearm is supinated while gentle pressure is applied over the radial head, followed by elbow flexion. A palpable or audible click indicates successful reduction. Most children recover full use of the arm within minutes. Imaging is not required after successful reduction in children under age 5 with a classic presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduction technique = supinate forearm with gentle pressure over radial head, then flex elbow. Click = success. Child should use arm within minutes. COMLEX: No imaging needed after successful reduction in classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q20","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old male presents with left arm holding after a pulling injury. History and exam are classic for nursemaid's elbow. The child is 3 years old, the mechanism is consistent, and the child has no swelling or bruising. Which of the following best describes the appropriate use of imaging in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral elbow radiographs in AP and lateral projections are mandatory for all suspected cases of radial head subluxation in order to exclude concurrent fractures and to document the baseline radiocapitellar relationship before reduction is attempted","B":"MRI of the elbow without contrast is the preferred diagnostic modality for radial head subluxation, providing direct visualization of the displaced annular ligament and confirming the diagnosis when the clinical presentation is classic but imaging is required for documentation","C":"Fluoroscopy during the reduction maneuver is required in children under age 4 to confirm that the radiocapitellar line is restored, as physical examination alone cannot reliably confirm successful reduction in preverbal and minimally verbal children","D":"Radiographs are required before and after every reduction attempt as a standard medical-legal practice, since failure to obtain pre-reduction imaging and document a normal baseline radiograph constitutes a deviation from the standard of care in pediatric emergency medicine","E":"Imaging is not necessary in children under age 5 with a classic history and presentation consistent with radial head subluxation; radiographs should be obtained when age is over 5, the mechanism is atypical, or the child fails to regain normal function after reduction as expected"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Radial head subluxation is a clinical diagnosis. Imaging is not necessary when the history and presentation are classic, the patient is under age 5, and the mechanism is consistent with annular ligament injury. Radiographs are indicated when age is over 5, when the mechanism is atypical suggesting possible fracture, or when the child fails to recover normal function following an attempted reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging needed for classic nursemaid's elbow in child under 5 with typical mechanism. Image when: age over 5, atypical mechanism, or fails reduction. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — unnecessary imaging exposes young children to radiation without benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q21","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy had a nursemaid's elbow reduced in clinic by supinating the forearm with pressure over the radial head; a click was felt and he is now reaching for toys with the arm. This is his second episode in 4 months. His parents ask about aftercare and the chance of it happening again. Which advice is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Place the arm in a long arm cast for 3 weeks, because the torn annular ligament must heal in a fixed position to prevent another episode","B":"Order elbow radiographs now, because any second episode means a radial head fracture is likely and must be excluded before discharge","C":"Refer for surgical repair of the annular ligament, because two episodes before age 3 show it is incompetent and will keep slipping","D":"No immobilization is needed; it can recur, so avoid pulling on the arms, parents can learn the reduction, and it is rare after age 5","E":"Keep the elbow in a sling in full extension for 2 weeks, because extension tightens the annular ligament around the radial head"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson upper limb chapter for this SDL states that after the annular ligament is reduced (supination with pressure over the radial head, often with a palpable click), immobilization is not required, but recurrence can happen. Parents should avoid activities that apply traction to the elbows, they can learn the reduction maneuver for recurrent episodes, and recurrence beyond 5 years of age is rare. Casting is not needed after a successful reduction. Imaging is reserved for children over 5, an atypical mechanism, or failure to regain function; a recurrence with a classic history does not require films. Surgical repair is not part of management. A sling in extension is not recommended, and extension is not the position of reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nursemaid's elbow = annular ligament subluxation from axial traction on a pronated, extended arm; ages 1–4, F > M, left > right. COMLEX: Reduce by supination (or hyperpronation) with pressure on the radial head; recheck that the child uses the arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — elbow dislocation presentation and reduction"},{"id":"sdl10_q22","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male pitcher presents with progressive right proximal shoulder pain after his third pitching appearance this week. He has no pitch count monitoring and pitches 5 days per week. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral physis compared to the contralateral shoulder. What is the diagnosis and the most important preventive measure?","choices":{"A":"Little league shoulder, a stress reaction of the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing; the most important preventive measure is implementing age-appropriate pitch count limits to restrict cumulative physeal stress across a week and across a season","B":"Rotator cuff impingement syndrome from subacromial narrowing in a skeletally immature athlete; the most important preventive measure is strengthening the rotator cuff muscles through internal and external rotation exercises with progressive elastic resistance band training","C":"Septic arthritis of the glenohumeral joint from bacteremic seeding of the shoulder synovium during intense training; the most important immediate step is joint aspiration under ultrasound guidance followed by IV antibiotics pending culture and sensitivity results","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete; the most important preventive measure is limiting horizontal adduction forces across the acromioclavicular joint during the follow-through phase of the pitch","E":"Bicipital tendinopathy from repetitive supination forces during the late cocking phase, producing inflammation at the biceps long head tendon origin at the superior labrum; the most important preventive measure is limiting curveball pitching in athletes under age 14"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing. The lack of pitch count monitoring in this case allowed cumulative physeal overload. Pitch counts are the most important preventive tool, as they limit cumulative stress on the open growth plate. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and physical therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress from overuse in male adolescent pitchers. X-ray shows physeal widening. Pitch counts = primary prevention. Treatment: rest up to 3 months, analgesics, PT. COMLEX: Distinguish from rotator cuff pathology by age and physeal widening on X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q23","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old right-handed pitcher presents to the ED after feeling a pop in his elbow during pitch release. He has medial elbow pain, swelling, and limited range of motion. X-ray shows a displaced medial epicondyle fragment. Valgus stress testing reveals elbow instability. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast application with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full pronation for 8 weeks is standard treatment; this position relaxes the flexor-pronator mass and restores attachment of the avulsed medial epicondyle through gradual fibrous union","B":"Immediate closed reduction of the medial epicondyle fragment under conscious sedation followed by a functional hinged elbow brace for 4 weeks with immediate initiation of range-of-motion exercises is appropriate for displaced fragments with valgus instability","C":"Conservative management with rest, ice, analgesics, and a sling for 4 to 6 weeks is appropriate because displaced medial epicondyle fragments in adolescents universally heal with fibrous union regardless of displacement magnitude or degree of instability","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the ulnar nerve groove to reduce traction neuropathy of the ulnar nerve, combined with a counterforce brace, is the first-line treatment when a pop is heard during pitching with associated valgus elbow instability","E":"Surgical fixation of the displaced medial epicondyle fragment is indicated given the significant displacement and valgus elbow instability; conservative management alone is insufficient when displacement and ligamentous instability are both present"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Significantly displaced medial epicondyle avulsion fractures with concurrent valgus elbow instability are indications for surgical fixation. The medial epicondyle is the attachment of the ulnar collateral ligament; displacement with instability means the UCL cannot be restored by conservative means alone. Rest alone for 4-6 weeks is appropriate for non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures without instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced medial epicondyle avulsion with valgus instability = surgical fixation indicated. Non-displaced without instability = conservative (rest 4-6 wk). COMLEX: Little league elbow peak avulsion risk at age 12-14; surgery reserved for displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q24","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluates two 13-year-old pitchers in the same clinic session. Patient A has proximal shoulder pain after throwing, with physeal widening at the proximal humerus on X-ray. Patient B has medial elbow pain worsened by valgus stress, with medial epicondyle apophyseal widening on X-ray. Which of the following correctly pairs each patient with their diagnosis and the growth plate involved?","choices":{"A":"Patient A has little league elbow affecting the medial epicondylar apophysis of the distal humerus, while patient B has little league shoulder affecting the greater tuberosity apophysis of the proximal humerus; both conditions are treated with surgical fixation regardless of severity","B":"Patient A has medial epicondyle apophysitis from repetitive valgus loading in the deceleration phase of pitching, while patient B has proximal humeral physeal stress reaction from excessive torque during the cocking phase; both require bilateral surgical referral","C":"Patient A has little league shoulder, a stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing, while patient B has little league elbow, which is apophysitis or avulsion at the medial epicondylar growth plate from repeated valgus and distracting forces during pitching","D":"Patient A has a proximal humeral stress fracture requiring immediate shoulder spica cast immobilization, while patient B has medial collateral ligament insufficiency requiring ulnar collateral ligament reconstruction due to valgus instability from medial apophyseal disruption","E":"Patient A has adhesive capsulitis triggered by repetitive overhead throwing in a skeletally immature shoulder, while patient B has ulnar collateral ligament sprain from the valgus torque of pitching; both are managed with a minimum 6-month rest period"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Little league shoulder and little league elbow are distinct overuse injuries in adolescent pitchers affecting different growth plates. Little league shoulder involves the proximal humeral physis from rotational stresses during throwing. Little league elbow involves the medial epicondylar apophysis from valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases. Both are prevented by pitch count programs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physis; age 11-16, rest up to 3 months. Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysis; age 9-14, rest 4-6 weeks. Both prevented by pitch counts. COMLEX: Know which growth plate each condition affects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q25","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old male soccer player presents with bilateral anterior knee pain for 2 months, worse with kneeling, jumping, and stair climbing. He has palpable, tender prominences below both knees. He is in a rapid growth phase and has no history of injury. X-ray is not obtained. Which of the following best justifies appropriately deferring imaging?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis based on tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in a growing athlete; X-ray does not change the initial conservative management, which centers on activity modification, physical therapy, and analgesics","B":"X-ray should be deferred because radiation exposure to the proximal tibial growth plate significantly increases the risk of physeal arrest and leg length discrepancy when cumulative X-ray dose reaches the 0.1 Gy threshold in skeletally immature adolescents","C":"X-ray is deferred because Osgood-Schlatter disease is caused by a metabolic disorder of calcium deposition at the tibial tuberosity, and radiographs cannot detect the early calcium dysregulation that precedes structural bony changes at the apophysis","D":"Radiographs are deferred in bilateral cases because bilateral tibial tuberosity pain eliminates the possibility of a fracture or neoplasm, which would present unilaterally, and bilateral symptoms confirm the diagnosis by exclusion from other pathology","E":"X-ray is deferred because the proximal tibial apophysis is always radiolucent in adolescents experiencing rapid skeletal growth, rendering radiographs unreliable for assessment of apophyseal injury during the active growth phase of the condition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis. The characteristic presentation — tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in an active adolescent during rapid growth — does not require X-ray confirmation. Imaging does not change initial management, which focuses on rebalancing stress and recovery through activity modification, PT, and analgesics. X-ray may be obtained to exclude other pathology when presentation is atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = clinical diagnosis; no imaging required. X-ray shows tibial tuberosity irregularity or avulsion only if obtained. COMLEX: Treat with activity modification, PT, analgesics, prolotherapy for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q26","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old female sprinter has Osgood-Schlatter disease with persistent tibial tuberosity pain despite 6 weeks of activity modification, physical therapy, and NSAIDs. Her coach asks whether there are any advanced treatment options. Which of the following is an advanced treatment option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the infrapatellar fat pad provides sustained relief for 6 to 12 months in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by reducing patellar tendon tension at its tibial insertion and decreasing local inflammatory cytokine concentrations","B":"Arthroscopic debridement of the tibial tuberosity apophysis provides definitive relief in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by removing the avulsed apophyseal ossicle and smoothing the tibial tuberosity prominence under direct arthroscopic visualization","C":"Prolotherapy, a form of regenerative injection therapy at the patellar tendon insertion on the tibial tuberosity, is an option for refractory cases of Osgood-Schlatter disease aimed at stimulating tissue repair at the tendon-bone junction","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy is contraindicated in Osgood-Schlatter disease because ultrasonic energy directed at an open growth plate in skeletally immature athletes produces physeal microhemorrhage and accelerates apophyseal avulsion","E":"Quadriceps tendon transfer to the tibial metaphysis is the surgical treatment of choice for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease in adolescents who have completed skeletal maturation and continue to experience pain with kneeling activities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course specifically cites prolotherapy as a regenerative injection option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease. Prolotherapy involves injection of an irritant solution at the tendon-bone junction to stimulate a healing response. Corticosteroid injections are generally avoided in tendon insertion sites in growing athletes due to risk of tendon weakening and physeal effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter refractory treatment = regenerative injection (prolotherapy). Standard treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q27","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with right anterior knee pain. He is actively growing. Examination reveals diffuse peripatellar tenderness worsened by prolonged sitting and stair climbing, without a palpable prominent or tender tibial tuberosity. Which of the following conditions is most consistent with this presentation, and how does it differ from Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendinopathy, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by involving the tendon substance at the inferior pole of the patella rather than the tibial tuberosity, and presents with pain worsening during jumping activities rather than prolonged sitting or stair climbing","B":"Prepatellar bursitis, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing fluctuant swelling directly anterior to the patella rather than at the tibial tuberosity, and is associated with repetitive kneeling occupations rather than sport-related rapid skeletal growth","C":"Patellofemoral syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing diffuse peripatellar and retropatellar pain with prolonged sitting and stairs and no tibial tuberosity prominence, from patellar maltracking rather than apophyseal traction","D":"Medial collateral ligament sprain, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by localizing pain to the medial knee joint line rather than the anterior tibial tuberosity, and is produced by a valgus mechanism of injury rather than repetitive patellar tendon traction","E":"Iliotibial band syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing lateral knee pain at the lateral femoral condyle rather than anterior tibial tuberosity pain, and is exacerbated by running and cycling rather than jumping and kneeling activities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patellofemoral syndrome causes diffuse peripatellar or retropatellar pain worsened by activities that increase patellofemoral contact stress, such as prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting. Unlike Osgood-Schlatter disease, there is no tibial tuberosity prominence or tenderness, and the mechanism is patellar maltracking rather than apophyseal traction stress. Both occur in adolescent athletes but are clinically distinguishable by location and associated findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = tender tibial tuberosity prominence, worse with kneeling and jumping. Patellofemoral syndrome = diffuse peripatellar pain, worse with prolonged sitting and stairs, no tuberosity prominence. COMLEX: Know both anterior knee pain differentials in growing athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q28","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 3 weeks of left hip pain and limp. Exam shows decreased internal rotation and abduction of the left hip. Initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are reported as normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the diagnostic workup?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the family that normal X-rays exclude Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease and schedule a follow-up appointment in 6 months if symptoms persist, because this condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the hip are both read as normal","B":"Perform hip joint aspiration to rule out transient synovitis or septic arthritis, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease cannot present at age 7 without radiographic changes visible on plain film when both AP and frog-leg lateral projections are obtained and interpreted as normal","C":"Prescribe NSAIDs and activity restriction for 4 weeks and repeat X-rays at that time, as Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is a self-limited condition that produces visible radiographic changes within 4 weeks if the diagnosis is correct","D":"Order MRI or bone scan of the hips, which can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease earlier than plain radiographs by identifying abnormal perfusion or bone marrow signal changes in the femoral head before bony structural changes are visible on X-ray","E":"Order a complete blood count, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and C-reactive protein panel, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents with elevated inflammatory markers that can confirm the diagnosis biochemically when radiographs are unrevealing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in early LCP. When clinical suspicion remains high — a child aged 4-9 with insidious hip pain, limp, decreased abduction and IR, and normal labs — MRI or bone scan should be obtained. MRI identifies abnormal marrow signal (ischemia) and bone scan shows reduced perfusion in the affected femoral head, both detectable before plain radiograph changes appear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP can have normal initial X-rays. Order MRI or bone scan when X-rays are normal but clinical suspicion is high. MRI = gold standard for early detection. COMLEX: Normal inflammatory markers help distinguish LCP from septic hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q29","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Three children are diagnosed with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease. A physician reviewing prognosis with the families notes that some children do well without treatment while others do poorly despite treatment. According to the course reading on long-term prognosis, which factor is the most important predictor of outcome?","choices":{"A":"Age at diagnosis, with children younger than 5 years doing well and children older than 8 to 9 years doing poorly","B":"Sex of the child, with girls doing well because they are affected less often and have fewer bilateral cases overall","C":"Duration of limp before diagnosis, with a limp under 2 weeks predicting full recovery whatever the age of the child","D":"Whether crutches are used, because relief of weight bearing prevents femoral head collapse during the revascularization phase","E":"Side involved, with left-sided disease doing better because the dominant leg receives more load during the collapse"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pediatric orthopedics chapter assigned in this block states that prognosis can often be predicted from certain factors, with age being the most important: age at diagnosis under 5 years is good and over 8 to 9 years is poor. Other factors are hip motion (abduction maintained above 30 degrees is good, under 15 degrees is poor), extent of involvement (under 50% of the femoral head is good), and the absence of subluxation or lateral calcification. Younger children have more cartilage and greater remodeling potential. Sex is not listed as the key predictor. Duration of limp is not a listed factor. The chapter notes there is no evidence that crutches or relief of weight bearing affect femoral head collapse. Side of involvement is not a prognostic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP = idiopathic AVN of the femoral head, ages 4–9, M:F 4:1, bilateral ~10%; MRI and bone scan detect it before X-ray. COMLEX: Containment (abduction bracing or casting) and maintaining hip abduction are the goals of treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — osteochondrosis as AVN in children"},{"id":"sdl10_q30","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has radiographic containment of the femoral head within the acetabulum and no subluxation on initial imaging. His parents ask what the treatment plan involves. Which of the following most accurately describes the principles of conservative management?","choices":{"A":"Because the child is under age 6, no treatment is required beyond parental reassurance; Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease in children under age 6 is universally self-limiting and produces no residual femoral head deformity when left completely untreated","B":"High-dose systemic corticosteroids are the standard treatment to suppress the inflammatory component of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, reducing synovial inflammation and preventing secondary capsular contracture that contributes to femoral head subluxation","C":"Conservative management with NSAIDs for pain, activity restriction or crutches to protect weight bearing, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, and bracing or casting to contain the femoral head in the acetabulum during revascularization","D":"Immediate varus derotational osteotomy is indicated for all children with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease because even brief delays in surgical containment produce spherical femoral head deformity that worsens long-term functional and radiographic outcomes","E":"Intraarticular bisphosphonate injection is first-line treatment for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, slowing osteoclastic resorption of avascular trabeculae and preventing femoral head collapse during the revascularization phase in children under age 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conservative management of LCP includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, crutches for weight-bearing protection, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during revascularization. Surgery is reserved for cases failing conservative management. The goal is to prevent femoral head deformity while the avascular bone undergoes slow revascularization and remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP conservative treatment = NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing or casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Femoral head containment in acetabulum is the central therapeutic principle throughout conservative and surgical management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q31","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old obese female presents with 4 weeks of right knee pain and a mild limp. Hip examination is not initially performed. Hip radiograph obtained later shows posterior and inferior displacement of the right femoral epiphysis. Which of the following best explains the referred knee pain and what key diagnostic lesson does this case illustrate?","choices":{"A":"The referred knee pain is caused by direct compression of the femoral nerve between the displaced femoral head and the inguinal ligament, producing dermatomal sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial knee in the distribution of the saphenous branch","B":"The referred knee pain arises from iliotibial band tightening secondary to the altered gait pattern in SCFE, as the external rotation deformity increases iliotibial band tension at Gerdy's tubercle on the lateral tibia and refers pain to the lateral knee","C":"The referred knee pain is produced by compression of the sciatic nerve between the posteriorly displaced femoral head and the posterior acetabular wall, causing posterior thigh and posterior knee pain exacerbated by hip extension and internal rotation","D":"The referred knee pain is caused by hip capsular distension from the effusion associated with SCFE, which stimulates obturator nerve articular branches supplying both the hip capsule and the medial knee, producing medial knee pain as the primary presenting symptom in all cases","E":"SCFE classically presents with anterior thigh or knee pain referred from the hip through the femoral and obturator nerve distributions, so knee pain in an obese adolescent should always prompt hip examination and radiographs to exclude SCFE"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE can present with knee pain rather than hip pain due to referred pain via the femoral and obturator nerve distributions, which supply both the hip and the anterior thigh and knee. This is a classic diagnostic trap. The key lesson is that any obese adolescent presenting with knee pain and a limp should have the hip examined and hip radiographs obtained to exclude SCFE, even when knee pain is the only complaint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE trap = knee pain without obvious hip pain. Referred pain via femoral and obturator nerves. Classic vignette: obese adolescent with knee pain, limp, and a normal knee exam — get hip X-ray. COMLEX: Radiographs mandatory for all suspected SCFE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q32","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male is diagnosed with an unstable SCFE. He is currently unable to bear weight due to severe hip pain. Which of the following best describes the management urgency and the key risk associated with the unstable classification?","choices":{"A":"Unstable SCFE can be managed with bed rest and traction for 24 to 48 hours before surgical scheduling, as a brief delay in operative fixation does not significantly increase the risk of avascular necrosis compared to immediate operative intervention","B":"Unstable SCFE requires urgent operative fixation because the inability to bear weight reflects ongoing physeal shear forces that sharply raise the risk of avascular necrosis compared with stable SCFE, where residual blood supply is more often preserved","C":"Unstable SCFE should be treated with emergent closed reduction under general anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 8 weeks, as surgical instrumentation of the unstable physis universally worsens AVN risk by disrupting residual periosteal blood supply","D":"Unstable SCFE has the same operative urgency as stable SCFE and can be scheduled electively within 2 weeks; the stability classification is used only to predict long-term functional outcomes and does not influence the timing of initial surgical management","E":"Unstable SCFE is initially managed with intravenous bisphosphonate therapy for 48 hours before surgical fixation, as the antiresorptive effect stabilizes the weakened physis and reduces the degree of further slip during transfer to the operating room"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN than stable SCFE because the ongoing inability to bear weight indicates continued physeal shear that progressively compromises the remaining blood supply to the femoral head. Urgent operative fixation in situ is required. Closed reduction is generally avoided as it can further disrupt residual blood supply. Stable SCFE also requires operative fixation but with less urgent timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unstable SCFE = painful gait, unable to bear weight, high AVN risk — requires urgent in situ fixation. Stable = weight-bearing without pain, lower AVN risk — still needs fixation but less emergent. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction of SCFE — increases AVN risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q33","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old female with hypothyroidism and obesity presents with bilateral hip pain and is found to have bilateral SCFE on radiographs. She is younger than the typical SCFE patient, and both hips have slipped at presentation. Which of the following best explains why this patient developed SCFE in this atypical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Obesity alone is sufficient to cause bilateral SCFE in females, as excess body weight increases shear forces at both femoral physes simultaneously; hypothyroidism has no independent effect on physeal integrity and is a coincidental finding in this patient","B":"Hypothyroidism produces premature physeal closure through TSH-mediated effects on growth plate chondrocytes, which paradoxically weakens the physis during the preslip phase by accelerating the transition from proliferative to hypertrophic chondrocytes","C":"Bilateral SCFE in a young female indicates an underlying genetic connective tissue disorder such as Marfan syndrome, where fibrillin deficiency weakens the extracellular matrix of the physis; thyroid disorders are not associated with bilateral SCFE presentation","D":"Metabolic disorders such as hypothyroidism weaken the proximal femoral physis by altering the hormonal environment that maintains physeal integrity; combined with obesity-related shear forces, they predispose to SCFE at a younger age and in both hips","E":"Bilateral SCFE is always caused by exogenous corticosteroid therapy, which suppresses IGF-1 at the growth plate and prevents normal physeal mineralization; the presence of hypothyroidism suggests this patient was treated with corticosteroids for an autoimmune thyroiditis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral SCFE at younger than typical age in a female should prompt evaluation for an underlying metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is one of the metabolic causes of SCFE listed in the course slides, along with obesity, inflammation, and familial predisposition. Hormonal alterations from hypothyroidism weaken the physis, while obesity increases shear stress, creating a synergistic risk for bilateral and early-onset SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE causes: physiologic hormonal changes, obesity, trauma, inflammation, metabolic disorders, familial. Atypical age or bilateral SCFE = evaluate for metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is the classic metabolic association. COMLEX: Always screen for metabolic disorders in atypical SCFE presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q34","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old competitive female gymnast has months of dorsal radial-sided wrist pain that worsens with handstands and vaulting and settles with rest. Radiographs compared with the other wrist show widening of the distal radial physis with cystic metaphyseal changes. Which mechanism best explains this condition?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive axial loading causes temporary metaphyseal ischemia that blocks cartilage calcification, so the physis widens","B":"A single fall on an outstretched hand crushes the germinal layer, producing a Salter-Harris type V injury with early arrest","C":"Repetitive gripping causes traction on the extensor tendons at the radial styloid, producing an avulsion of the apophysis","D":"Axial loading buckles the dorsal metaphyseal cortex, producing a torus fracture that progresses to physeal widening","E":"Repetitive wrist flexion compresses the median nerve, which denervates the physis and leads to cystic change and widening"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Nelson growth plate injury section for this SDL describes gymnast's wrist: chronic wrist pain in up to 79% of young gymnasts, particularly girls aged 12 to 14. Repetitive axial loading and dorsiflexion through the distal radius can induce temporary metaphyseal ischemia, preventing cartilage calcification and causing the physis to widen, with microfractures possible. Radiographs compared with the other side show physeal widening with cystic metaphyseal change and, later, positive ulnar variance; MRI helps when films are inconclusive. A type V crush is a single acute compression injury that is often radiographically occult. The radial styloid is not a traction apophysis for the wrist extensors. A torus fracture is an acute buckle from one fall and does not cause chronic physeal widening. Median nerve compression does not affect the physis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physis = pressure growth plate (longitudinal growth); apophysis = traction growth plate at tendon attachments. Physeal fractures peak at peak height velocity and are about twice as common in males. COMLEX: Gymnast's wrist: PRICE, rest from loading; SH I and II fractures are treated with closed reduction and immobilization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — distal radius and other hand and wrist fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q35","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two 8-year-old children present to the ED after falls. Child 1 has an X-ray showing a cortical bulge at the distal radius metaphysis without complete cortical disruption. Child 2 has an X-ray showing complete fracture of one cortex with the opposite cortex intact and angulated but not broken. Which of the following correctly identifies both fracture types and their distinct mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Child 1 has a greenstick fracture caused by bending force that fractures one cortex while the other bends without breaking; child 2 has a torus fracture caused by axial compressive force that buckles the cortex without producing a complete fracture on either side","B":"Both children have torus fractures, as the distal radius is the most common site for both fracture types in pediatric patients; the difference in X-ray appearance reflects only the projection angle rather than a true difference in fracture mechanism or pattern","C":"Child 1 has a Salter-Harris Type I fracture of the distal radial physis caused by tensile stress at the growth plate; child 2 has a Salter-Harris Type II fracture with a metaphyseal fragment on the compressive side of the angulated fracture","D":"Child 1 has a torus fracture from axial compression, where the porous pediatric cortex buckles without fracturing through; child 2 has a greenstick fracture from bending force, where one cortex fractures while the other bends but stays intact","E":"Child 1 has a complete distal radius fracture with periosteal hinging on the compressive side preventing complete displacement; child 2 has plastic deformity of the radius where no cortical fracture occurs but the bone curves under sustained bending force without breaking"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Torus fractures result from axial compressive forces — the porous pediatric metaphyseal cortex buckles without completely fracturing, producing a cortical bulge. Greenstick fractures result from bending forces — one cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing on the compression side. Both are pediatric-specific fracture patterns resulting from the unique mechanical properties of immature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus = axial compression, cortical buckle without complete fracture. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks completely, other bends. Both unique to pediatric bone. COMLEX: Both are stable fracture patterns managed conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q36","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a high-energy distal femur injury. CT confirms a fracture that passes through the metaphysis, growth plate, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. The orthopedic surgeon counsels the family about growth arrest risk. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and why does it carry higher risk than Types I and II?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because intraarticular extension of the fracture through the epiphysis exposes the germinal layer to joint fluid and synovial metalloproteinases that degrade growth plate cartilage and prevent normal longitudinal growth","B":"Salter-Harris Type IV; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because the fracture crosses all three bone zones at once, so precise physeal alignment is hard to restore and a bony bridge can form, causing angular deformity or leg length discrepancy","C":"Salter-Harris Type V; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because axial compressive crushing of the physis destroys the germinal cell layer and the extent of damage is frequently underestimated on initial imaging because the physis appears only narrowed rather than displaced","D":"Salter-Harris Type II; it carries higher risk than Type I because the metaphyseal fragment creates a stress riser at the physis that concentrates shear stress during weight-bearing, propagating the fracture line into the germinal layer of the growth plate during the healing period","E":"Salter-Harris Type I; it carries higher risk than expected because high-energy mechanisms produce concurrent vascular disruption to the periosteal blood supply, which is the dominant source of nutrition to the germinal layer of the physis in children under age 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type IV fractures traverse the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. This creates a uniquely difficult reduction challenge because the fracture must be anatomically reduced across all three bone zones simultaneously. Failure to restore perfect alignment risks the formation of a bony bridge across the growth plate, which can produce angular deformity or leg length discrepancy as the physis continues to grow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type IV = fracture through metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis; crosses all three zones; requires anatomic reduction and often internal fixation. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk: Type I least, Type V worst. Types III and IV require anatomic reduction due to intraarticular extension or cross-zone disruption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"Joint Anatomy & Connective Tissue Physiology","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which layer of the synovial joint capsule is responsible for secreting synovial fluid into the joint cavity?","choices":{"A":"Outer fibrous membrane, which blends with the periosteum of adjacent bones and provides the primary structural stability to the joint capsule","B":"Articular hyaline cartilage, which covers the opposing bone surfaces and distributes compressive forces but does not produce synovial fluid","C":"Periosteum, which forms the outermost connective tissue covering of bone and provides attachment points for tendons and ligaments near the joint","D":"Inner serous synovial membrane, which lines the capsule and secretes synovial fluid that lubricates and nourishes avascular articular cartilage","E":"Periarticular fibrous bursa, which is a separate fluid-filled sac adjacent to the joint that reduces friction between tendons and bony prominences"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Synovial joints have a two-layered capsule: the outer fibrous membrane blends with periosteum for structural support, and the inner serous synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid. The cavity is a potential space; articular cartilage covers bone surfaces but is avascular and relies on synovial fluid for nutrition. Bursae are separate from the joint cavity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the two capsule layers and that synovial membrane (not cartilage) secretes fluid. COMLEX: Synovial joint anatomy underpins OMM concepts of joint play and somatic dysfunction — fluid dynamics are affected by fascial restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q02","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fibrous joint in which two bones are connected by an interosseous membrane and remain partially mobile is best classified as which subtype of fibrous joint?","choices":{"A":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which bones either overlap or interlock along irregular edges, allowing no meaningful movement and found exclusively between skull bones","B":"Gomphosis, a fibrous joint in which a peg-like process inserts into a bony socket via periodontal ligament fibers, as seen between tooth roots and alveolar bone","C":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and compliance of the connecting fibers","D":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage, typically a temporary union that permits growth and is later replaced by bone","E":"Amphiarthrosis, a functional classification describing joints with slight mobility that encompasses both fibrous and cartilaginous structural types rather than a specific structural subtype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fibrous joints are subdivided by the nature of the connecting tissue: sutures (interlocking bone edges, skull), syndesmoses (interosseous ligament/membrane, partially mobile), and gomphosis (tooth-in-socket). The distal tibiofibular joint and the interosseous membrane of the forearm are classic syndesmosis examples. Synchondrosis is cartilaginous, not fibrous.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (immobile, skull), syndesmosis (partially mobile, interosseous membrane/SI joint), and gomphosis (tooth socket). COMLEX: Syndesmosis disruption in high ankle sprains creates instability relevant to OMM evaluation of the ankle and tibiofibular relationship.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q03","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The epiphyseal growth plate in a skeletally immature child is best classified as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis), in which bones are united by fibrocartilage providing permanent slight mobility such as that found at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral discs","B":"Primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis), in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage forming a temporary union that permits growth and is eventually ossified and replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","C":"Fibrous syndesmosis, in which bones are connected by an interosseous membrane or ligament permitting partial mobility that depends on the length of the fibrous connecting fibers","D":"Synovial hinge joint, in which articulating surfaces are separated by a fluid-filled cavity lined by a secretory synovial membrane and permitting only flexion and extension","E":"Specialized elastic fibrocartilaginous joint, in which elastin fibers embedded in a fibrocartilage matrix allow bending and recoil during repetitive loading cycles in development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary cartilaginous joints (synchondroses) are united by hyaline cartilage and are typically temporary unions present during development. The epiphyseal plate is the classic example; it permits longitudinal bone growth and is replaced by bone (synostosis) at skeletal maturity. Secondary cartilaginous joints (symphyses) use fibrocartilage and are permanent, as seen at the pubic symphysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epiphyseal plate = synchondrosis (hyaline cartilage, temporary). Pubic symphysis = symphysis (fibrocartilage, permanent). COMLEX: Growth plate vulnerability underlies Salter-Harris fracture classification and the rationale for cautious OMM in skeletally immature patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q04","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The connective tissue found in tendons and ligaments is classified as which specific type, and what histological feature confers its high tensile strength?","choices":{"A":"Dense irregular connective tissue, characterized by collagen bundles arranged in multiple directions simultaneously to resist forces applied from various angles, as found in the deep dermis","B":"Loose areolar connective tissue, characterized by sparse collagen fibers in abundant ground substance with numerous resident and transient cell types facilitating rapid nutrient diffusion","C":"Specialized cartilaginous connective tissue, characterized by chondrocytes embedded in a matrix of collagen and proteoglycans without direct blood supply at the articular surface","D":"Dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen bundles aligned along the axis of stress, few fibroblasts, and minimal ground substance, giving unidirectional tensile strength","E":"Reticular connective tissue, characterized by a delicate meshwork of type III collagen fibers supporting lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, spleen, and adrenal cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ligaments and tendons are composed of dense regular connective tissue: parallel dense bundles of collagen, few fibroblasts, few blood vessels, high tensile strength, and very little ground substance. The outer surface has dense irregular CT continuous with adjacent muscle or bone. Dense irregular CT resists multidirectional forces, while dense regular CT excels along one axis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dense regular CT = tendons/ligaments (parallel collagen bundles). Dense irregular CT = dermis, joint capsule (multidirectional). COMLEX: Tendon and ligament composition explains why chronic overuse injuries heal slowly due to poor vascularity and why fascial OMM targets the surrounding dense irregular layer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q05","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Collagen type IV is uniquely distinguished from collagen types I, II, and III by which structural property?","choices":{"A":"Collagen type IV forms large fibrils arranged in parallel bundles providing the high tensile strength characteristic of tendons, organ capsules, and dermal connective tissue","B":"Collagen type IV forms sheets or network arrangements rather than fibrils, making it the primary structural protein of epithelial and endothelial basal laminae throughout the body","C":"Collagen type IV forms cross-linked elastin scaffolds that confer recoil properties to structures subject to repeated stretching such as arterial walls and lung parenchyma","D":"Collagen type IV forms short fibrillar linking structures that anchor extracellular matrix components to one another and maintain the overall integrity of the connective tissue scaffold","E":"Collagen type IV forms the reticular network of thin delicate fibers that support lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, adrenal cortex, and bone marrow stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen types I, II, and III form large fibrils (tendons, cartilage, skin). Type IV is unique in forming sheets or networks rather than fibrils, serving as the structural backbone of epithelial basal laminae. Elastic fibers contain elastin (not collagen) plus fibrillin. Reticular fibers are predominantly type III collagen in a thin meshwork.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY collagen types: I = bone/tendon/skin, II = cartilage, III = reticular/healing/fetal skin, IV = basement membrane. Type IV defect = Alport syndrome (renal basement membrane). COMLEX: Collagen type distribution explains tissue properties relevant to OMM — type I dominates fascial planes targeted by myofascial release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q06","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibroblasts are the predominant cell type of connective tissue. Which of the following correctly identifies their embryologic origin and primary function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblasts originate from bone marrow precursors similar to macrophages and plasma cells, circulating in the bloodstream before homing to connective tissue sites of injury","B":"Fibroblasts derive exclusively from cranial neural crest cells in all body regions, explaining their capacity for both structural ECM synthesis and local immune modulation","C":"Fibroblasts are ectodermally derived cells that differentiate locally within each tissue during organogenesis and rarely contribute to ECM synthesis under normal physiologic conditions","D":"Fibroblasts originate from adipose precursor cells in the deep dermis that retain pluripotency and can regenerate the full range of connective tissue components after injury","E":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme of mesodermal origin and produce the majority of extracellular matrix components including collagen fibers, reticular fibers, and ground substance macromolecules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme (mesodermal origin) and are the primary ECM-producing cells, secreting collagen subunits, proteoglycans, and other matrix proteins. Other CT cells include macrophages and plasma cells (bone marrow origin) and adipocytes (mesenchymal origin). Tendon and ligament fibroblast origin depends on body position: head = neural crest; limbs = lateral plate mesoderm; trunk = somites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibroblast = mesenchymal origin, makes ECM. Distinguish from macrophages (bone marrow), mast cells (bone marrow), and adipocytes (mesenchymal). COMLEX: Fibroblast activity in fascial connective tissue is the target of myofascial OMM — mechanical stimulation upregulates collagen synthesis and remodeling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q07","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The atlantoaxial joint allows the head to rotate from side to side. Which category of synovial joint movement does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Angular movement, defined as motion that changes the angle between two articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at joints such as the elbow and knee","B":"Gliding movement, in which two flat articular surfaces slide past each other without significant angular change, as seen at the carpal bones of the wrist and facet joints of the spine","C":"Rotational movement, in which one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, exemplified by the dens of C2 rotating within the ligamentous ring of C1 during head turning","D":"Special movement, such as inversion or eversion, which occurs only at anatomically specific sites such as the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints","E":"Circumduction, a composite movement that combines flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence to trace a cone-shaped arc, seen at multiaxial joints such as the hip and shoulder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Synovial joint movements are classified as gliding (flat surfaces translate past each other), angular (change of angle, e.g., elbow flexion), rotational (one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, e.g., atlantoaxial joint, radio-ulnar pivot), or special movements (inversion, eversion, pronation, etc.). The atlantoaxial joint is a pivot joint permitting uniaxial rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atlantoaxial = pivot = rotational movement. Elbow = hinge = angular. Wrist carpals = plane = gliding. COMLEX: Atlantoaxial motion restriction is a common finding in cervical somatic dysfunction; understanding rotation vs. side-bending mechanics is essential for HVLA and muscle energy technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q08","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly defines flexion as a type of angular joint movement?","choices":{"A":"A straightening motion that results in an increase in the angle between two articulating bones, returning a flexed joint toward anatomic neutral position or beyond it into hyperextension","B":"A bending motion that results in a decrease in the angle between two articulating bones, moving the distal segment toward the proximal segment and reducing the inter-segmental angle","C":"Movement of an appendage away from the body midline in the frontal plane, increasing the lateral distance between the limb and the central axis of the body","D":"Rotation of a limb segment toward the body midline along a vertical axis, reducing the anterior-facing angle of the limb relative to the sagittal plane","E":"A special movement unique to the ankle in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves toward the anterior surface of the tibia, reducing the dorsal foot-to-shin angle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angular movements change the angle between bones. Flexion decreases this angle (bending); extension increases it (straightening). Abduction moves away from midline; adduction moves toward midline. Internal rotation moves toward midline along a vertical axis; external rotation moves away. Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are special movements at the ankle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic traps — extension is increasing angle (not straightening past neutral); abduction vs. adduction confusion on anatomy questions. COMLEX: Flexion and extension terminology directly maps to OMM somatic dysfunction naming conventions (e.g., FRSL vs. ERSL in spinal mechanics).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q09","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Inversion of the foot and eversion of the foot are best categorized under which classification of synovial joint movement?","choices":{"A":"Angular movements, which are defined by changes in the inter-segmental angle between articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at synovial joints","B":"Rotational movements, which involve one bone rotating around its longitudinal axis as seen at the atlantoaxial pivot joint during head rotation from side to side","C":"Gliding movements, in which two flat articular surfaces translate past one another without meaningful angular change, as seen between carpal bones of the wrist","D":"Special movements, a category that includes inversion, eversion, protraction, retraction, elevation, depression, dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, and opposition at specific sites","E":"Circumduction movements, which trace a cone-shaped arc by combining flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence at multiaxial ball-and-socket joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Special movements do not fit neatly into gliding, angular, or rotational categories and are named for the specific anatomical site where they occur. Inversion (plantar surface medially) and eversion (plantar surface laterally) occur at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints. Other special movements include dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, pronation/supination, protraction/retraction, and elevation/depression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all special movements and their locations; inversion/eversion at subtalar joint, pronation/supination at radio-ulnar joint, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion at talocrural joint. COMLEX: Inversion/eversion restriction at the subtalar joint is a common somatic dysfunction after ankle sprain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q10","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized direct structural consequence of normal joint aging?","choices":{"A":"Increased production of hyaluronic acid within the synovial fluid, leading to paradoxically improved joint lubrication despite reduced overall synovial fluid volume","B":"Increased joint stiffness from capsular thickening and shortening of periarticular ligaments, which together reduce range of motion and functional mobility with age","C":"Hypertrophy of periarticular muscles due to chronic loading stimulus, partially compensating for cartilage loss by improving active stabilization of the aging joint","D":"Progressive replacement of hyaline articular cartilage by fibrocartilage, which provides improved shock absorption at the cost of reduced articular surface congruity","E":"Expansion of the synovial membrane lining and increased synovial fluid volume, improving nutrient delivery to avascular cartilage as its own intrinsic nutrition declines"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aging produces a constellation of joint changes: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening causing stiffness, hyaline cartilage degeneration and thinning, reduction in synovial fluid volume and quality, and decreased periarticular muscle mass. These changes cumulatively impair joint function and predispose to OA and fall risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know aging effects on joints — cartilage thins, synovial fluid decreases, ligaments shorten, muscle mass decreases. COMLEX: Aging-related joint stiffness creates palpable somatic dysfunction patterns; OMM aims to restore joint mobility and soft tissue pliability that aging reduces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q11","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Regular physical exercise improves joint health primarily through which of the following mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating chondrocyte proliferation to directly regenerate lost articular cartilage, histologically reversing the degenerative changes of osteoarthritis at the joint surface","B":"Inducing hypertrophy of the synovial membrane and increasing its surface area, thereby raising the total protein and hyaluronic acid content of synovial fluid","C":"Improving joint lubrication through synovial fluid production and distribution, reducing soft tissue stiffness, and strengthening periarticular stabilizing muscles","D":"Promoting calcification of periarticular tendons and ligaments, increasing passive joint stability and reducing the risk of traumatic ligamentous injury","E":"Decreasing bone density through controlled loading cycles, redistributing mechanical stress away from the articular surface and toward the cortical diaphysis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exercise benefits for joint health include improved bone density, improved balance, reduction in pain, improved soft tissue pliability and reduced stiffness, improved joint lubrication, and strengthening of supportive muscles. Exercise does not directly regenerate cartilage or cause beneficial calcification of soft tissues. Synovial membrane hypertrophy can be pathological.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exercise improves lubrication, reduces stiffness, strengthens periarticular muscles. Does NOT reverse cartilage loss histologically. COMLEX: Exercise prescription is central to osteopathic management of musculoskeletal conditions; combining OMM with graded exercise maximizes functional restoration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q12","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which age-related change to articular cartilage most directly explains the onset of joint pain and loss of function in elderly patients with osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage, which reduces shock absorption and exposes subchondral bone to increased mechanical stress and erosion","B":"Overgrowth of the synovial membrane filling the joint cavity, mechanically obstructing joint movement and generating a reactive inflammatory response that destroys articular cartilage","C":"Increased collagen cross-linking within the cartilage matrix, paradoxically stiffening it to the point where it can no longer distribute compressive forces across the articular surface","D":"Proliferation of chondrocytes at the articular margin, forming osteophytes that impinge on adjacent soft tissues and restrict range of motion without affecting articular cartilage itself","E":"Accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the cartilage matrix, triggering recurrent pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage through crystal-induced inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging causes progressive thinning and degeneration of hyaline articular cartilage, reducing its shock absorption and load distribution capacity. Once the cartilage is lost, subchondral bone is exposed, causing pain and reactive bone changes (sclerosis, osteophyte formation). These are consequences of cartilage loss rather than primary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = cartilage degeneration and thinning → subchondral bone exposure → pain and loss of function. Pseudogout involves CPPD crystals but is episodic, not the primary mechanism of OA. COMLEX: OMM reduces the joint loading that accelerates cartilage degradation; muscle strengthening offloads the articular surface.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q13","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man presents with bilateral knee pain worsening over 6 months, aggravated by stairs and prolonged standing. BMI is 31. Vitals are normal. Exam reveals bilateral crepitus, mild bony enlargement, and no warmth or effusion. X-ray shows symmetric joint space narrowing and subchondral sclerosis. Which structural component of the synovial joint is most directly compromised in this patient, explaining his loss of smooth pain-free movement?","choices":{"A":"The outer fibrous membrane of the joint capsule, which blends with periosteum and maintains structural alignment of articulating bones but does not directly contribute to the smoothness of articular movement","B":"The inner serous synovial membrane lining the joint capsule, which produces synovial fluid for lubrication and nutrition but whose primary dysfunction causes effusion rather than crepitus or joint space narrowing","C":"The periarticular fibrous bursae adjacent to the joint, which reduce friction between tendons and bony prominences outside the joint cavity rather than at the articular surfaces within it","D":"The fibrous outer layer of the joint capsule at its attachment to the periosteum, which stiffens with capsular fibrosis but does not explain the symmetric joint space narrowing seen on radiograph","E":"Articular hyaline cartilage covering the bone surfaces within the synovial joint cavity, which normally distributes compressive forces evenly across the joint and provides a nearly frictionless surface for movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Joint space narrowing on X-ray reflects cartilage loss. Articular hyaline cartilage distributes compressive forces, absorbs mechanical pressure, and provides a smooth surface (with synovial fluid) for low-friction movement. Its degeneration produces crepitus and pain. Synovial membrane dysfunction causes effusion, not narrowing. Bursae are extracapsular. Capsular fibrosis causes stiffness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA pathology = cartilage loss → subchondral sclerosis + osteophytes + joint space narrowing on X-ray. Know each component of the synovial joint and what its specific failure causes clinically. COMLEX: OMM for knee OA targets the periarticular soft tissue and capsular restriction, indirectly protecting remaining cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q14","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is reviewing a histology slide from a 50-year-old cadaver specimen. The joint shown connects two skull bones via interlocking bony projections with no intervening cartilage, fibrous tissue, or joint space. Movement across this structure is zero. The student correctly classifies this as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage that permits slight bending early in life, typically a temporary union replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","B":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and tension of the connecting fibers","C":"Symphysis, a secondary cartilaginous joint united by thick fibrocartilage providing strength and slight permanent mobility, as seen at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral disc junctions","D":"Synostosis, a synarthrosis in which bone is fused directly to bone with no intervening material, permitting no movement and representing the normal adult fate of previously separate skull bones","E":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which cranial bone edges overlap or interlock with fibrous tissue interposed between them, found in young skulls before complete bony fusion has occurred"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In adult skulls, sutures progressively ossify into synostoses where bone is directly fused to bone with zero movement. Sutures in children still have fibrous tissue interposed. Synchondroses use hyaline cartilage. Syndesmoses have ligamentous tissue. Symphyses use fibrocartilage. The absence of any intervening tissue and complete immobility specifies synostosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (fibrous, skull, children) from synostosis (fused bone, adults, skull after ossification). Know all three structural categories and their subtypes. COMLEX: Cranial OMM techniques are predicated on the concept of slight motion at cranial sutures — understanding that sutures exist as fibrous joints before synostosis informs this OMM approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q15","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old soccer player presents after landing awkwardly on an outstretched arm. MRI confirms anterior glenohumeral dislocation. The orthopedic consultant explains that the shoulder's extreme mobility — the greatest of any joint in the body — is a direct consequence of its synovial joint subtype. Which subtype correctly describes the glenohumeral joint and accounts for its multiplanar motion capability?","choices":{"A":"Ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design in which a rounded head fits into a concavity, permitting flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, and circumduction","B":"Condyloid joint, a biaxial design in which an oval condyle articulates with an elliptical fossa permitting flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction but restricting pure axial rotation","C":"Saddle joint, a biaxial design in which reciprocal saddle-shaped articular surfaces allow movement in two orthogonal planes, best exemplified by the first carpometacarpal joint of the thumb","D":"Hinge joint, a uniaxial design in which a spool-shaped surface articulates with a concave surface permitting only flexion and extension along a single fixed transverse axis","E":"Pivot joint, a uniaxial design in which a rounded bony process fits within a ligamentous ring permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis of the bone involved"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ball-and-socket joints are multiaxial, allowing movement in all three planes. The glenohumeral joint trades stability for mobility due to the shallow glenoid fossa. Condyloid joints allow two axes (no pure rotation); saddle joints allow two axes at the CMC thumb joint; hinge joints (elbow) allow only one axis (flex/ext); pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 6 synovial subtypes: plane, hinge, pivot, condyloid, saddle, ball-and-socket — and their uniaxial/biaxial/multiaxial classification. Hip and shoulder = ball-and-socket. COMLEX: Glenohumeral instability predisposes to dislocation; OMM for shoulder conditions addresses capsular restriction and rotator cuff muscle imbalance affecting all planes of motion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q16","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A developmental biology lecture traces where tendons and ligaments come from. The lecturer notes that the embryologic source differs along the rostrocaudal axis of the body. From which embryologic source do the tendons and ligaments of the limbs arise?","choices":{"A":"Cranial neural crest cells, which migrate into the limb buds and form tendons and ligaments throughout the upper and lower limbs","B":"The somites, whose sclerotome cells migrate into the limb buds with the myotome and form tendons alongside the limb muscles","C":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which also forms the limb skeleton, while somites form trunk tendons and neural crest forms head tendons","D":"The surface ectoderm of the apical ectodermal ridge, which folds inward to lay down the collagen of limb tendons and ligaments","E":"The intermediate mesoderm, which extends into the limb buds next to the developing kidneys and gives rise to the dense regular tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide on tendon and ligament origin states that the source depends on position along the rostrocaudal axis: in the head they arise from cranial neural crest cells; in the neck and shoulder girdle from lateral plate mesoderm, cranial neural crest, and paraxial mesoderm; in the limbs from lateral plate mesoderm; and in the trunk from the somites. Cranial neural crest supplies head tendons, not limb tendons. Somites form trunk tendons, and the somite cells that enter the limb become muscle (myotome), not limb tendons. The AER is a signaling ectoderm that drives limb outgrowth and does not form connective tissue. Intermediate mesoderm forms urogenital structures and does not contribute to the musculoskeletal system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Connective tissue derives from mesenchyme (mesoderm); limb skeleton and limb tendons/ligaments come from lateral plate mesoderm, while limb muscle comes from migrating somite (myotome) cells. COMLEX: Tendons join muscle to periosteum; ligaments join the periosteum of adjacent bones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — mesodermal origins of musculoskeletal tissues"},{"id":"sdl11_q17","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is studying for a histology practical. The review sheet describes a protein that constitutes approximately 30% of human dry weight, can form fibers, sheets, and networks, and is highly resistant to tearing forces. Type I forms large fibrils found in tendons and dermis. Type II forms fibrils in cartilage. Type IV forms sheets in basal laminae. What protein is being described?","choices":{"A":"Elastin, a structural protein embedded within fibrillin microfibrils forming cross-linked elastic networks that confer recoil properties to arterial walls, skin, and lung parenchyma","B":"Fibrillin, a glycoprotein that forms the microfibrillar scaffold into which elastin is deposited, creating elastic fibers throughout connective tissue in skin, vasculature, and ligaments","C":"Proteoglycan, a ground substance macromolecule composed of a protein core with attached glycosaminoglycan chains that retains water within the extracellular matrix of cartilage and connective tissue","D":"Laminin, a glycoprotein found exclusively in basement membranes that anchors epithelial cells to type IV collagen networks via integrin receptor binding at the cell surface","E":"Collagen, a family of structural proteins produced by fibroblasts that polymerize into elongated structures and represent the most abundant protein in the human body by dry weight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body (30% of dry weight). Types I/II/III form large fibrils; type IV forms sheets for basal laminae. Elastin (with fibrillin) forms elastic fibers with recoil properties. Proteoglycans are ground substance components. Laminins are basement membrane glycoproteins that anchor epithelium to collagen via integrins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagen = 30% dry weight, most abundant protein. Key pathology: type I defect in OI, type V (classic) or type III (vascular subtype) in Ehlers-Danlos, type IV in Alport syndrome. COMLEX: The elastic and collagen fiber composition of fascia determines its mechanical properties and explains why sustained OMM pressure induces tissue creep and eventual release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q18","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with progressive knee pain is told her articular cartilage is thinning. Her orthopedist explains that hyaline cartilage covering the articular surface uniquely lacks a perichondrium at that location. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of this anatomical feature for normal joint physiology?","choices":{"A":"Absence of perichondrium allows water and nutrients to exchange between the cartilage matrix and synovial fluid during loading cycles, nourishing avascular articular cartilage","B":"Absence of perichondrium reduces friction at the articular surface by preventing a fibrous layer from being interposed between opposing cartilage surfaces during joint loading","C":"Absence of perichondrium allows chondrocytes direct vascular access from synovial membrane capillaries, supporting aerobic metabolism within the cartilage during loading","D":"Absence of perichondrium permits collagen fibers to orient themselves parallel to the articular surface throughout the full depth of the cartilage matrix to resist shear forces","E":"Absence of perichondrium exposes the cartilage surface to direct innervation from capsular nerve branches, explaining the severe pain experienced when cartilage itself is damaged"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Articular hyaline cartilage lacks perichondrium at the exposed surface, allowing pressure-dependent water exchange with synovial fluid — the mechanism by which avascular chondrocytes receive nutrients. Loading compresses the matrix and expels water; decompression draws in synovial fluid. Cartilage itself lacks nerves and blood vessels; pain in OA comes from subchondral bone and capsular innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Articular cartilage is avascular, alymphatic, and aneural — nutrition comes from synovial fluid via pressure-dependent exchange. This explains why immobilization harms cartilage and loading protocols help. COMLEX: Joint mobilization in OMM promotes synovial fluid circulation, providing trophic support to avascular articular cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q19","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist documents shoulder range of motion. When the patient raises their arm forward in the sagittal plane from anatomic position and then returns it to anatomic position, which pair of terms correctly labels these two movements?","choices":{"A":"The forward arm raise is shoulder abduction (moving away from midline in the frontal plane) and returning to neutral is shoulder adduction (moving toward the body midline)","B":"The forward arm raise is shoulder internal rotation (moving the limb toward midline along a vertical axis) and returning to neutral is shoulder external rotation (moving away from midline)","C":"The forward arm raise is shoulder flexion (movement forward in the sagittal plane) and returning to neutral is shoulder extension (movement back toward anatomic position)","D":"The forward arm raise is shoulder circumduction and returning to neutral is shoulder elevation, with both being composite or special movements at the glenohumeral joint","E":"The forward arm raise is shoulder protraction (moving the shoulder anteriorly) and returning to neutral is shoulder retraction (moving the shoulder posteriorly) at the scapulothoracic joint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angular movements are defined by inter-segmental angle changes. Flexion decreases the angle (arm forward); extension increases it (arm back to neutral or beyond). Abduction/adduction occur in the frontal plane. Internal/external rotation occur around a vertical axis. Circumduction is a composite movement tracing a cone. Protraction/retraction are special movements of the scapula, not the glenohumeral joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Flexion = decreasing angle. Extension = increasing angle. These definitions must be applied consistently across all joints. COMLEX: Correct motion terminology is mandatory for documenting SOAP notes in OMM — imprecise terminology (e.g., calling flexion \"elevation\") causes diagnostic errors in somatic dysfunction assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q20","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old basketball player is evaluated after sustaining a lateral ankle sprain. Physical examination reveals that the plantar surface of his foot is turned to face medially and he is unable to actively return it to neutral. Which term correctly names this foot position, and to which category of joint movement does it belong?","choices":{"A":"Inversion, a special movement in which the plantar surface of the foot faces medially, occurring at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints rather than as angular or rotational motion","B":"Adduction, an angular movement in which the foot moves toward the body midline in the frontal plane, reducing the lateral distance between the foot and the central axis of the lower limb","C":"Internal rotation, a movement in which the entire lower extremity rotates medially along a vertical axis at the hip joint, rotating the foot passively but not representing a foot-specific movement","D":"Plantarflexion, a special movement in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves away from the anterior tibia, pointing the toes downward as occurs when standing on the toes","E":"Pronation, a forearm-specific special movement rotating the palm posteriorly that when incorrectly applied to the foot refers to a combined eversion, dorsiflexion, and abduction pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inversion (plantar surface faces medially) and eversion (plantar surface faces laterally) are special movements at the subtalar/transverse tarsal joints. They do not fit angular or rotational categories. Lateral ankle sprains most commonly injure the anterior talofibular ligament through inversion mechanism. Plantarflexion and dorsiflexion are distinct special movements at the talocrural joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inversion = subtalar joint, plantar surface medial, lateral ankle sprain mechanism. Eversion = opposite. Know which ligaments are stressed in inversion (ATFL, CFL) vs. eversion (deltoid) sprains. COMLEX: The subtalar joint is a common site of somatic dysfunction after ankle sprains; OMM addresses the restricted motion at the talocalcaneal joint to restore inversion-eversion balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q21","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical medicine resident is comparing range of motion between the elbow and hip joint during a clinical encounter. The elbow allows only flexion and extension along a single fixed axis, while the hip allows movement in all three planes of space. Which pair of synovial joint subtypes correctly corresponds to the elbow and hip, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The elbow is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis, while the hip is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and abduction but restricting pure axial rotation","B":"The elbow is a hinge joint, a uniaxial design permitting only flexion and extension, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting movement in all three planes including flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation","C":"The elbow is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and circumduction, while the hip is a saddle joint, a biaxial design permitting movement in two orthogonal planes only","D":"The elbow is a plane joint, a uniaxial gliding design where flat surfaces translate past each other without angular change, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting three-plane movement","E":"The elbow is a saddle joint, a biaxial design with reciprocal concave and convex articular surfaces, while the hip is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design allowing only rotation around its longitudinal axis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hinge joints (elbow, knee) are uniaxial, permitting only flexion/extension. Ball-and-socket joints (hip, shoulder) are multiaxial, permitting movement in all three planes. Pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation. Condyloid joints (MCP, wrist) allow two axes. Plane joints (carpals, facets) allow gliding. Saddle joints (CMC thumb) allow two axes with reciprocal surfaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 6 synovial joint types and their representative locations. Elbow = hinge (humeroulnar); the proximal radio-ulnar is the pivot within the elbow that allows forearm rotation. COMLEX: Classifying joints by their axes of motion underpins OMM diagnosis — restriction of a normally multiaxial joint in a specific plane localizes the dysfunction being treated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q22","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with mild knee osteoarthritis and a recent fall asks whether a regular exercise program will do anything beyond easing her knee pain. Which additional benefit of exercise on the musculoskeletal system is listed in the course?","choices":{"A":"Improved bone density and improved balance, which together lower her risk of falls and fractures","B":"Regrowth of the thinned articular cartilage to its original thickness within a few months of training","C":"Reversal of osteophytes already present at the joint margins once body weight has been reduced","D":"Conversion of the fibrocartilage in her menisci into hyaline cartilage that absorbs load better","E":"Lengthening of the joint capsule beyond its youthful size, so that range of motion exceeds normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists the role of exercise in joint health as improved bone density, improved balance, reduced pain, more pliable joint soft tissue with less stiffness, better joint lubrication, and stronger supportive muscles. For an older woman who has fallen, better balance and bone density lower the chance of a fall and of a fracture if she does fall. Articular cartilage has no capacity to heal or regenerate, so exercise does not regrow it. Osteophytes are not reversed by exercise or weight loss. Meniscal fibrocartilage does not convert to hyaline cartilage. Exercise reduces stiffness but does not lengthen the capsule beyond normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aging joints: stiffer capsule, shorter ligaments, thinner hyaline cartilage, less synovial fluid, less muscle mass. Exercise counters these and also improves bone density and balance. COMLEX: Weight-bearing exercise is part of osteoporosis and fall prevention in older adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — mechanical loading and bone remodeling"},{"id":"sdl11_q23","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 77-year-old man presents with bilateral hip stiffness and aching worse in the morning and after prolonged sitting. He denies trauma. Radiographs show symmetric joint space narrowing, marginal osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis. Physical examination reveals markedly reduced internal rotation bilaterally. Which combination of age-related joint changes most directly explains both the radiographic findings and the restricted internal rotation on examination?","choices":{"A":"Increased synovial fluid volume with elevated protein content causing joint distension, which mechanically restricts capsular compliance and produces both the radiographic narrowing and the motion restriction","B":"Progressive replacement of dense regular connective tissue in the hip ligaments by loose areolar tissue, paradoxically increasing joint laxity and producing instability rather than the restricted motion seen here","C":"Hypertrophy of the periarticular muscles from compensatory overloading, generating passive stiffness that mimics capsular restriction but that would resolve completely with targeted strengthening exercises","D":"Focal accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the articular cartilage causing pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage and produce episodic rather than chronic symmetric restriction","E":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage causing joint space narrowing, combined with capsular thickening and ligament shortening that restrict motion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of joint space narrowing on X-ray and restricted internal rotation on exam reflects the two dominant mechanisms of joint aging: cartilage degeneration (structural, causes narrowing and crepitus) and capsular thickening with ligament shortening (functional, causes stiffness and range of motion restriction). Synovial hypertrophy causes effusion, not narrowing. Pseudogout is episodic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms = structural (cartilage loss) + functional (capsular/ligament stiffness). Know how to distinguish OA from inflammatory arthritis and crystal arthropathies clinically. COMLEX: Restricted internal rotation is a hallmark of hip somatic dysfunction and OA; OMM targeting the posterior hip capsule can restore some motion in early-to-moderate disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q24","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A physical therapist is counseling a healthy 48-year-old recreational athlete about maintaining joint health as she ages. The therapist explains that aging and regular exercise have opposing effects on the same joint tissues. Which of the following best contrasts the effects of aging versus exercise on the joint and its surrounding structures?","choices":{"A":"Aging uniformly increases bone density through age-related osteoblast stimulation, while excessive aerobic exercise paradoxically reduces density through mechanical unloading of the axial skeleton and limbs","B":"Aging promotes chondrocyte proliferation as a compensatory response to cartilage wear, while high-impact exercise suppresses this regenerative capacity and accelerates irreversible articular degeneration","C":"Aging selectively shortens all periarticular ligaments through gradual scarring and contracture, while targeted stretching exercise selectively elongates ligaments beyond their normal anatomic resting length","D":"Aging reduces synovial fluid, thickens the capsule, shortens ligaments, and reduces muscle mass, whereas exercise improves lubrication, keeps soft tissue pliable, and strengthens stabilizing muscles","E":"Aging converts all diarthrodial synovial joints into synarthroses through progressive ossification, while regular aerobic exercise completely prevents this conversion by maintaining synovial fluid flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Aging effects on joints: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening (stiffness), hyaline cartilage thinning, reduced synovial fluid volume and quality, decreased muscle mass. Exercise counteracts these: improves lubrication, soft tissue pliability, joint lubrication, bone density, balance, pain reduction, and muscle strengthening. Aging does not ossify all diarthroses or universally increase bone density.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the complete list of aging joint changes and how exercise addresses each one. COMLEX: This bidirectional contrast underpins the osteopathic philosophy of maintaining health through activity — OMM facilitates motion restoration while exercise prescription prevents recurrence of dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"Integrative Medicine","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term describes the use of complementary health practices as a substitute for conventional standard care rather than alongside it?","choices":{"A":"Complementary medicine, which refers specifically to practices used together with conventional therapies rather than as a substitute for them","B":"Integrative health care, which refers to coordinated use of both conventional and complementary practices with emphasis on whole-person care across social, psychological, and physical domains","C":"Whole person health, which is a framework focusing on restoring health, promoting resilience, and preventing disease across the full lifespan rather than treating individual organs or systems","D":"Alternative medicine, which denotes practices used in place of conventional standard care and is increasingly less preferred than the term \"complementary,\" which implies use alongside conventional therapy","E":"Salutogenesis, which describes the process by which health is actively restored when recovering from injury, acute illness, or exacerbation of chronic disease through behavioral rather than pharmacologic change"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alternative medicine means used instead of conventional care; complementary means used alongside it. \"Complementary\" is increasingly preferred over \"alternative.\" Integrative health care combines both in a coordinated way with a whole-person focus. Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse — restoring health through behavioral change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the distinction: complementary = with, alternative = instead of. COMLEX: Osteopathic medicine is classified as integrative (mainstream + OMM), not alternative. Understanding this framework is directly tested in COMLEX clinical presentation scenarios.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q02","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under which primary therapeutic input category would meditation and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) be correctly classified?","choices":{"A":"Physical input, which includes modalities delivered manually such as massage, by device such as acupuncture, or generated by the patient such as exercise","B":"Dietary input, which encompasses herbs, vitamins, minerals, and dietary supplements consumed to influence biological function through nutritional pathways","C":"Multicomponent system input, which describes whole health systems such as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, naturopathy, and chiropractic that combine multiple therapeutic approaches","D":"Combined physical and psychological input, which characterizes practices such as yoga and tai chi that integrate movement with meditative elements into a single intervention","E":"Psychological input, as both target mental processes as their primary therapeutic mechanism, alongside mindfulness-based stress reduction, hypnosis, and biofeedback-assisted relaxation techniques"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Complementary therapies are classified by their primary therapeutic input: dietary (herbs, supplements), psychological (CBT, MBSR, meditation, hypnosis, relaxation), physical (massage, acupuncture, exercise), or combined (yoga, tai chi). CBT and meditation have predominantly mental/psychological mechanisms, making them primary psychological inputs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 4 input categories and their representative modalities — this framework organizes an otherwise unwieldy list. COMLEX: OMM and spinal manipulation are classified as physical integrative medicine; understanding this helps frame osteopathic practice within the broader integrative health landscape.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q03","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A health system launches a program built on the concept of whole person health. Which description best matches how the course reading defines whole person health?","choices":{"A":"Treating each diagnosed disease with the single most effective therapy, whether conventional or complementary, organ by organ","B":"Viewing the person across biological, behavioral, social, and environmental areas to restore health and resilience","C":"Replacing conventional care with complementary practices so that patients avoid the adverse effects of prescription drugs","D":"Using complementary practices as self-care without physician involvement, paid out of pocket rather than through insurance","E":"Limiting care to mind and body practices that combine physical movement with meditation, such as yoga and tai chi"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course reading defines whole person health as looking at the whole person rather than separate organs or body systems, considering multiple interconnected biological, behavioral, social, and environmental factors. Instead of treating a specific disease, it focuses on restoring health, promoting resilience, and preventing disease across the lifespan. Treating each disease organ by organ is the opposite of this approach. Replacing conventional care describes alternative medicine. Self-care paid out of pocket describes how many complementary practices are used, not the definition of whole person health. Yoga and tai chi are examples of combined psychological and physical input, not a definition of whole person health.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complementary = alongside conventional care; alternative = instead of it; integrative = coordinated use of both; whole person health = multiple interconnected domains, restoring health and resilience. COMLEX: Osteopathic tenets (body as a unit of body, mind, and spirit) align with whole person health.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — psychological versus physical integrative inputs"},{"id":"sdl12_q04","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the American College of Physicians (ACP) clinical practice guidelines, which integrative therapy is strongly recommended as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia before pharmacologic agents?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which the ACP strongly recommends as the initial treatment, often with relaxation techniques as components","B":"Melatonin supplementation, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia because it reduces sleep-onset latency and daytime sleepiness","C":"Acupuncture, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia on the basis of its benefit in chronic pain and osteoarthritis","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which the ACP recommends as a standalone first-line treatment for chronic insomnia because it lowers cortisol and anxiety","E":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia given its benefits for pain and anxiety"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 2016 ACP practice guidelines strongly recommend CBT-I as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia. CBT-I often includes relaxation techniques but the full multi-component CBT-I program carries the guideline endorsement. Melatonin has evidence but is not ACP first-line; it also has safety concerns in children and mislabeling issues. Acupuncture is ACP-recommended for low-back pain, not insomnia, and it is not clear that relaxation alone (such as progressive muscle relaxation) is beneficial; MBSR helps pain and anxiety but is not the ACP initial treatment for insomnia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT-I = ACP first-line for chronic insomnia (before sleep aids). COMLEX: Insomnia is a common clinical presentation where osteopathic physicians are expected to recommend non-pharmacologic interventions first; CBT-I is the evidence-based answer before considering hypnotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q05","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The American College of Rheumatology and the Arthritis Foundation issue which level of recommendation for tai chi as a treatment for knee and hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Conditional recommendation for tai chi as a second-line option for OA after failure of NSAIDs and corticosteroid injections, reflecting limited evidence compared to acupuncture","B":"Strong recommendation for tai chi alongside other nondrug approaches for knee and/or hip OA, given its benefits for motor function, balance, stability, sleep, and quality of life","C":"No formal recommendation exists for tai chi in OA; the ACR and Arthritis Foundation only recognize acupuncture and massage as having sufficient evidence for guideline inclusion","D":"Weak recommendation against tai chi in elderly OA patients due to fall risk, with guidance instead favoring low-impact aquatic exercise programs for patients with severe hip OA","E":"Moderate recommendation pending ongoing randomized trials, with current use suggested only as an adjunct to pharmacologic therapy rather than as a standalone intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ACR and Arthritis Foundation strongly recommend tai chi for knee/hip OA as a nondrug approach. Acupuncture receives a conditional (not strong) recommendation for knee/hip/hand OA. Tai chi also improves balance, stability in elderly, sleep quality in mild insomnia, and quality of life in heart disease and cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the guideline levels — tai chi STRONG recommendation for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture CONDITIONAL for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture RECOMMENDED for low-back pain (ACP 2017). COMLEX: Tai chi exemplifies combined physical-psychological integrative medicine specifically endorsed for the musculoskeletal conditions most common in COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q06","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Clinical trials have shown that omega-3 fatty acid supplementation is beneficial for which of the following musculoskeletal conditions by reducing tender joints, morning stiffness, and the daily NSAID requirement?","choices":{"A":"Knee osteoarthritis, where fish oil reverses cartilage loss on MRI and lowers the daily NSAID requirement in randomized trials","B":"Fibromyalgia, where omega-3 supplementation is the guideline-endorsed first-line integrative therapy for tender points and morning stiffness","C":"Ankylosing spondylitis, where fish oil halts progression of spinal fusion and lowers inflammatory back pain scores in clinical trials","D":"Gout, where omega-3 fatty acids lower serum uric acid enough to replace allopurinol and prevent acute flares in clinical trials","E":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), where fish oil reduces tender joints, morning stiffness, and daily NSAID requirements in clinical trials"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Omega-3 fatty acids have the strongest integrative evidence in RA: reduced tender joints, reduced morning stiffness, reduced NSAID requirement. Omega-3 supplementation has not been shown to reverse cartilage loss in OA, to be first-line for fibromyalgia, to halt spinal fusion in ankylosing spondylitis, or to replace urate-lowering therapy in gout. For depression, omega-3 data are inconclusive as monotherapy. High-dose fish oil is associated with increased atrial fibrillation risk. For AMD, AREDS2 antioxidants are the relevant supplement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Omega-3 = RA (reduces NSAID need, tender joints, morning stiffness). Not a replacement for DMARDs. High-dose fish oil → increased AF risk. COMLEX: Omega-3 supplementation is a dietary integrative medicine strategy; knowing its evidence base versus areas where evidence is lacking is a classic COMLEX pharmacology-nutrition integration question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q07","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 establishes which specific regulatory limitation on claims that dietary supplement manufacturers may make on their product labels?","choices":{"A":"Supplements must demonstrate clinical safety and efficacy through FDA premarket approval similar to pharmaceutical drugs before being sold to consumers in the United States","B":"Manufacturers may claim a supplement maintains \"normal structure and function\" of body systems but may not claim it prevents, treats, or cures any disease; false claims fall under FTC enforcement","C":"Dietary supplements are prohibited from listing any health benefits on their labels and may only display ingredient lists, serving sizes, and recommended daily values without any wellness claims","D":"All dietary supplement advertising claims must be reviewed and approved by the FDA before they appear on product labels, in online marketing, or in any print or broadcast media","E":"Supplement manufacturers must disclose all known herb-drug interactions on product labels, similar to the drug interaction warning requirements imposed on pharmaceutical manufacturers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under DSHEA (1994), dietary supplements do NOT require FDA premarket approval for efficacy. Manufacturers can claim to \"maintain normal joint health\" or \"support normal structure and function\" but cannot claim to \"treat arthritis\" or \"prevent cancer.\" The FTC (not FDA) regulates advertising claims. The FDA regulates labeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA (1994) = structure/function claims OK; disease claims NOT OK. The burden of proving harm falls on the FDA, not the manufacturer. FTC regulates advertising. Classic board trap: supplements do not require proof of efficacy before sale. COMLEX: This regulatory framework creates risk — patients may use unproven supplements believing they are equivalent to evidence-based medicines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q08","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The FDA banned dietary supplements containing Ephedra sinica (ma huang) in the United States in 2004. Which pharmacologic property of this plant directly led to that regulatory action?","choices":{"A":"Ephedra sinica was found to contain aristolochic acid alkaloids that were specifically associated with genitourinary malignancies and interstitial nephritis in consumers using herbal weight-loss preparations","B":"Ephedra sinica caused the eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome through contamination of amino acid supplements during manufacturing, resulting in multisystem inflammation affecting thousands of users","C":"Ephedra sinica supplements were found to contain 1,3-dimethylamylamine, a synthetic stimulant frequently adulterated into herbal products that caused cardiovascular problems and heart attacks in users","D":"Ephedra sinica was withdrawn after being found to cause anosmia in users of a homeopathic nasal spray product, later determined to result from significant zinc content rather than the herb itself","E":"Ephedra sinica contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine alkaloids; supplements sold for weight loss and athletic performance caused severe and fatal cardiovascular adverse events including heart attacks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ephedra sinica (ma huang) contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine. Used for weight loss and athletic performance, it caused severe cardiovascular adverse events. FDA banned it in 2004. Aristolochia = genitourinary malignancies and nephritis. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome. DMAA = cardiovascular problems in its own right. Zicam = anosmia from zinc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY toxicology: ephedra/ma huang = cardiovascular events (ephedrine/pseudoephedrine) → FDA banned 2004. Aristolochia = nephritis + GU cancer. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia. Keep each toxin matched to its specific supplement and outcome. COMLEX: Counseling patients about natural product risks includes knowing these specific examples to cite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q09","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"St. John's Wort is known to reduce the bioavailability of many co-administered drugs. Which molecular mechanism is responsible for this herb-drug interaction?","choices":{"A":"Upregulation of the pregnane X receptor (PXR) by hyperforin in St. John's Wort, which increases expression of hepatic oxidative, conjugative, and efflux enzymes involved in drug metabolism and transport","B":"Direct competitive inhibition of cytochrome P450 isoforms at the catalytic active site, permanently reducing the capacity of these enzymes to metabolize co-administered drugs to their active or inactive metabolites","C":"Chelation of divalent cations required as cofactors for intestinal drug transport proteins, reducing the bioavailability of drugs that require metal-dependent active transport across the gastrointestinal epithelium","D":"Displacement of protein-bound drugs from albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein binding sites, dramatically increasing the unbound free fraction of narrow therapeutic index drugs and raising toxicity risk","E":"Inhibition of renal tubular secretion through competitive blockade of the organic anion transporter system, extending the half-life of antibiotics and chemotherapeutic agents dependent on renal clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperforin in St. John's Wort activates PXR (pregnane X receptor), a promiscuous nuclear regulatory factor that upregulates hepatic oxidative (CYP enzymes), conjugative, and efflux enzymes. This was first shown with indinavir (HIV protease inhibitor), then cyclosporine, irinotecan, and many others. Particularly dangerous with narrow therapeutic index drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: St. John's Wort → PXR activation → CYP induction → reduced drug levels. Classic examples: indinavir (HIV drug — first documented 2000), cyclosporine (transplant rejection risk), irinotecan (chemo). COMLEX: Herb-drug interactions are a real clinical concern in integrative medicine; asking patients about natural product use is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q10","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following integrative practices is correctly categorized as having a primary PHYSICAL therapeutic input?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy, which targets maladaptive thought patterns and behaviors through a structured, time-limited series of therapy sessions","B":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which cultivates present-moment awareness through guided meditation practice in a structured group program","C":"Acupuncture, which delivers therapeutic input by stimulating defined anatomic points with solid metallic needles or electrical stimulation","D":"Yoga, which integrates physical postures and breathing with meditative elements and improves back-related function in chronic low-back pain","E":"Tai chi, which combines slow, gentle movements originating in China with meditative practice and improves balance and stability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Physical input modalities are delivered manually (massage), by device (acupuncture), or generated by the patient (exercise). Psychological inputs target mental processes (CBT, MBSR, hypnosis). Combined inputs integrate both (yoga, tai chi). Acupuncture is purely physical — the therapeutic mechanism is stimulation of anatomic points, not a mental/psychological process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the input category for each major integrative modality: acupuncture = physical; CBT/MBSR = psychological; yoga/tai chi = combined. COMLEX: OMM, spinal manipulation, and massage are all physical integrative medicine — understanding this classification helps frame osteopathic medicine within the Harrison's integrative medicine framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q11","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 2017 American College of Physicians clinical guidelines recommend which integrative therapy as one of the initial treatment options for patients with acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Melatonin supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because poor sleep amplifies the perception of pain","B":"Omega-3 fatty acid supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because of its anti-inflammatory effects","C":"Acupuncture, which the ACP recommends as one initial option for acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain, amid opioid concerns","D":"Glucosamine and chondroitin supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain","E":"Hypnotherapy, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because it reduces the need for sedatives and analgesics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The 2017 ACP guidelines recommend nonpharmacologic first-line treatments for low-back pain including acupuncture (all stages: acute, subacute, chronic), spinal manipulation, and massage, reflecting the opioid crisis. Melatonin, omega-3 fatty acids, hypnotherapy, and glucosamine-chondroitin are not among the ACP's recommended initial treatments for low-back pain. Acupuncture has been shown superior to both usual care and sham acupuncture for musculoskeletal pain with effects lasting up to 12 months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACP 2017 low-back pain guideline: nonpharmacologic first (acupuncture, spinal manipulation, massage, exercise, yoga, tai chi) before NSAIDs, before opioids. This reflects the opioid epidemic response. COMLEX: Spinal manipulation is also in these guidelines — osteopathic physicians are uniquely positioned to offer multiple ACP-recommended nonpharmacologic options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q12","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Self-hypnosis training has been shown to significantly reduce the need for sedatives and analgesia during which type of medical procedure?","choices":{"A":"Interventional radiologic procedures, where self-hypnosis training reduced the need for sedatives and analgesia; hypnotherapy also helps anxiety and quality of life in IBS","B":"Orthopedic joint replacement surgery, where preoperative hypnosis training has been shown to reduce postoperative opioid consumption more effectively than any other mind-body intervention","C":"Cardiac catheterization, where hypnosis is the recommended primary sedation modality in patients with contrast allergy who cannot receive standard intravenous sedation protocols","D":"Colonoscopy, where hypnosis has been shown to reduce both procedural discomfort and polyp detection rates by altering bowel motility through autonomic nervous system mechanisms","E":"Radiation therapy for prostate cancer, where hypnosis is recommended by American Society for Radiation Oncology guidelines to reduce both anxiety and radiation-induced tissue injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Studies showed self-hypnosis significantly reduced need for sedatives and analgesia during interventional radiologic procedures. Hypnotherapy also benefits anxiety and quality of life in IBS and may help smoking cessation. These are specific evidence-based indications. No comparable hypnosis evidence is described for joint replacement, cardiac catheterization, colonoscopy, or prostate radiation therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypnosis clinical evidence: interventional radiology (sedation/analgesia reduction), IBS (anxiety and QoL), smoking cessation (some evidence, not definitive). COMLEX: Hypnosis is classified as a primary psychological integrative modality; distinguishing its evidence-based applications from unsupported claims is a clinical reasoning competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q13","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which regulatory requirement does the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 explicitly NOT impose on dietary supplement manufacturers?","choices":{"A":"Prohibition against claiming to prevent, treat, or cure specific diseases such as arthritis or cancer on product labels, with violators subject to FTC enforcement actions and FDA warning letters","B":"Labeling requirements identifying the product as a dietary supplement with a list of ingredients, serving size, and a disclosure that the FDA has not evaluated any structure or function claims","C":"Proof of efficacy before marketing, as DSHEA allows supplements to be sold without demonstrating clinical effectiveness, unlike prescription drugs, which require FDA premarket approval","D":"Compliance with Current Good Manufacturing Practices establishing minimum quality control standards for supplement production, including identity, purity, strength, and composition of ingredients","E":"Prohibition of fraudulent or deceptive advertising claims in digital, print, and broadcast media, enforced through FTC authority to take legal action against promoters of false supplement claims"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSHEA does NOT require pre-market proof of efficacy — the burden of proving harm lies with the FDA, not the manufacturer. DSHEA does require: no disease claims, structure/function label disclosures, Good Manufacturing Practice compliance. Advertising is regulated by FTC, not FDA. This asymmetry from pharma regulations creates significant consumer safety risks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key DSHEA distinction: supplements ≠ drugs. No premarket efficacy proof required. Manufacturer makes safety-responsible, FDA proves harm after the fact. This is why supplements can reach market with limited evidence. COMLEX: Counseling patients about supplement risks requires understanding this regulatory gap — \"sold in stores\" does not mean \"FDA approved.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q14","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Classic randomized controlled trial (RCT) design faces which specific challenge when applied to studying multicomponent complementary systems such as naturopathy or Ayurvedic medicine?","choices":{"A":"Ethical review boards prohibit the use of any control arm in studies of ancient healing traditions because denying patients access to culturally meaningful care constitutes a violation of research ethics principles","B":"The FDA requires a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product contained in a multicomponent naturopathic or Ayurvedic treatment regimen, making the regulatory pathway prohibitive","C":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for study visits using complementary interventions, making participant retention impossible in trials where patients must pay out of pocket for study procedures","D":"Their individualized, dynamic nature makes treatment hard to standardize, so pragmatic comparative effectiveness studies with usual care comparators are used, though they cannot isolate the active component","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in studies of physical integrative therapies because practitioners and participants can always identify the active treatment, rendering all such research inherently unblinded"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multicomponent systems like naturopathy and Ayurveda are inherently individualized. Classic RCTs require standardized interventions, but these systems adapt to each patient. Pragmatic designs with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate active components. \"Manualized\" treatment protocols with some flexibility are being developed to address this. Blinding challenges exist but are not the primary issue cited.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT limitations for integrative research: individualization prevents standardization; cannot isolate which component works; pragmatic designs used instead. COMLEX: OMM research faces similar challenges — individualized treatment plans, practitioner variability, and difficulty blinding make it methodologically complex to study.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q15","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following represents a specific research challenge unique to studying natural products compared to psychological or physical integrative interventions?","choices":{"A":"The complexity and variability of natural products, including unstable active components, impurities, conflicting low-powered studies, and no standardized dosing for over-the-counter products","B":"The impossibility of blinding any natural product study because participants can always distinguish an active herbal supplement from a placebo by taste, smell, or physical appearance","C":"The complete absence of federal funding for natural product research from agencies such as the NIH National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, requiring industry sponsorship for all studies","D":"The requirement that all natural product researchers hold dual degrees in pharmacology and botany, creating a severe workforce shortage that restricts the number of clinical trials that can be conducted","E":"The ethical prohibition against studying natural products alongside conventional drugs, which prevents investigation of the real-world usage patterns most commonly observed in clinical practice"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Natural product research challenges: complexity and variability; possible active component instability; impurities; conflicting literature; low statistical power; paucity of safety data in modern contexts. \"More is not necessarily better\" — excessive doses of vitamins can be harmful. These are distinct from the multicomponent/individualization challenges of systems like naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the natural product research challenges: dose variability, instability, impurities, low statistical power. The NCCIH exists specifically to fund this research (so federal funding is NOT absent). COMLEX: When patients ask about natural products, acknowledging the research limitations and directing them to NCCIH resources is appropriate clinical guidance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q16","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis tells his physician that, in addition to his DMARD, he takes fish oil capsules and a turmeric supplement and follows an anti-inflammatory diet. Using the course scheme that classifies complementary practices by their primary therapeutic input, how are these practices classified?","choices":{"A":"Primary physical input, because the supplements act on the joints directly in the way that massage acts on soft tissue","B":"Primary psychological input, because the benefit of supplements comes mainly from expectation and a sense of self-care","C":"Combined physical and psychological input, because diet changes involve both behavior and the body, as in tai chi","D":"Primary dietary input, the category that includes diet changes, herbs, and other natural products such as fish oil","E":"Alternative medicine, because any supplement taken with a disease-modifying drug replaces part of conventional care"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course reading classifies complementary practices by primary therapeutic input: dietary (diet, herbs, and other natural products), psychological (for example meditation), physical (for example massage and acupuncture), or combined psychological and physical (yoga, tai chi). Fish oil, turmeric, and an anti-inflammatory diet are dietary inputs. The reading also notes that fish oil can reduce tender joints, morning stiffness, and NSAID needs in RA. Physical input is delivered manually, by a device, or by the patient's own movement. Psychological input targets mental processes. Combined input describes movement plus meditation. Because he uses these alongside his DMARD, they are complementary, not alternative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Natural products are complex mixtures; risks include toxicity, contamination, adulteration, and herb-drug interactions (St. John's wort induces PXR). COMLEX: Ask every patient about supplements and check interaction resources for narrow-therapeutic-index drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — toxicity, regulation, and herb-drug interactions of natural products"},{"id":"sdl12_q17","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis asks his rheumatologist about yoga and tai chi as options. The rheumatologist explains that these two practices share a classification that makes them distinct from acupuncture alone or meditation alone. Which classification do yoga and tai chi share?","choices":{"A":"Primary physical input, as both yoga and tai chi involve predominantly physical movement and exercise, with any meditative components considered secondary and clinically insignificant for classification purposes","B":"Combined physical and psychological input, as both integrate physical movement and postures with meditative elements, unlike purely physical acupuncture or purely psychological meditation","C":"Primary psychological input, as both yoga and tai chi originate from Eastern meditation traditions and their therapeutic benefits arise primarily from psychological mechanisms rather than from physical movement","D":"Multicomponent system input, as both originate from traditional Asian medical systems and therefore belong to the same category as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, and naturopathy","E":"Dietary input, as both yoga and tai chi traditions emphasize dietary practices alongside movement, making nutrition the primary therapeutic input category for these ancient health systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Yoga and tai chi combine physical postures/movements with meditative elements, classifying them uniquely as combined physical and psychological inputs. This distinguishes them from: acupuncture (primary physical), massage (primary physical), CBT/MBSR (primary psychological), traditional Chinese medicine (multicomponent system). The combination gives them a broader therapeutic target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined physical + psychological = yoga, tai chi. Physical only = acupuncture, massage, exercise. Psychological only = CBT, MBSR, hypnosis, meditation. This classification has appeared in exam questions asking to categorize specific modalities. COMLEX: The combined nature of yoga/tai chi mirrors the holistic osteopathic approach, explaining why they are endorsed in multiple ACR and ACP guidelines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q18","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An integrative medicine specialist explains that their practice incorporates \"salutogenesis\" as a guiding principle. A medical student asks what this term means. Which of the following best defines salutogenesis in the context of integrative health?","choices":{"A":"Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse: the process by which health is actively restored after injury, acute illness, or a chronic disease flare, or when prediabetes is reversed by behavioral change","B":"Salutogenesis describes the simultaneous development of multiple chronic comorbidities as a result of shared metabolic risk factors, representing the intersection of pathological pathways in complex patients","C":"Salutogenesis refers to the prevention of disease through prophylactic pharmacologic treatment before symptoms arise, as distinguished from reactive treatment given only after a diagnosis has been established","D":"Salutogenesis is the systematic review of a patient's social determinants of health to identify upstream factors that increase disease risk, forming the basis for population-level public health interventions","E":"Salutogenesis refers to the spontaneous remission of disease that occurs in some patients without treatment, which integrative medicine attempts to harness and amplify through mind-body interventions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salutogenesis = pathogenesis in reverse. It emphasizes the process of restoring health rather than solely preventing or treating disease. Integrative health incorporates salutogenesis by addressing recovery from injury/illness and reversing predisease states (e.g., prediabetes, prehypertension) through behavioral interventions. It is not about spontaneous remission or social determinants specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salutogenesis = health restoration process (vs. pathogenesis = disease development). Integrative medicine frames health as a continuum, with salutogenesis as the return direction. COMLEX: The osteopathic philosophy of treating the whole person and supporting the body's self-healing mechanisms aligns directly with salutogenesis as a guiding principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q19","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with fibromyalgia and chronic low-back pain asks about a single integrative intervention that has evidence for both conditions. She prefers something she can do independently after initial instruction. Which integrative practice has evidence supporting benefit for BOTH fibromyalgia AND chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has evidence for chronic low-back pain, headache, and osteoarthritis, with treatment effects persisting for up to 12 months","B":"Biofeedback, which has evidence for tension headaches and for reducing pediatric and adult migraine frequency over about 17 months","C":"Hypnotherapy, which has evidence for reducing sedative use during interventional radiology and for anxiety and quality of life in IBS","D":"Yoga, which can benefit fibromyalgia and gives small to moderate gains in back-related function at 3 and 6 months in chronic low-back pain","E":"Omega-3 supplementation, which reduces tender joints, morning stiffness, and NSAID requirements in rheumatoid arthritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yoga has specific evidence for both fibromyalgia (beneficial) and chronic low-back pain (small-moderate improvement at 3 and 6 months vs. nonexercise controls). MBSR also benefits fibromyalgia and low-back pain. Acupuncture, biofeedback, hypnotherapy, and omega-3 fatty acids have evidence for other conditions, but not for both fibromyalgia and chronic low-back pain. Yoga has additional benefits for anxiety, depression, smoking cessation, and quality of life in chronic diseases. The American Cancer Society recommends yoga for anxiety in breast cancer patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yoga evidence base: fibromyalgia, chronic LBP, anxiety, smoking cessation, cancer QoL. Also recommended by American Cancer Society for breast cancer-related anxiety. COMLEX: As a combined physical-psychological modality, yoga exemplifies whole-person integrative care — its multimodal benefits reflect the osteopathic principle of treating the whole patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q20","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman undergoing cycle 3 of platinum-based chemotherapy for ovarian cancer reports refractory nausea and vomiting not controlled by standard antiemetics. Her oncologist mentions an integrative option that can be added to drug treatment. Which integrative practice has specific evidence for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting in cancer patients?","choices":{"A":"Tai chi, which improves overall motor function, balance, stability, sleep quality, and quality of life in patients with chronic illness","B":"Yoga, which the American Cancer Society recommends for anxiety and stress reduction during and after breast cancer treatment","C":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain through multiple non-opioidergic mechanisms and benefits anxiety","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which may reduce occupational stress and help anxiety in patients undergoing medical procedures","E":"Acupuncture or electroacupuncture, which may be an appropriate addition to drug treatment for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acupuncture or electroacupuncture may be an appropriate addition to drug treatment for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting. Separately, 2022 Society for Integrative Oncology/ASCO guidelines found intermediate-level evidence that acupuncture may relieve pain in people with cancer, and acupuncture may also relieve allergic rhinitis symptoms. Yoga is recommended for anxiety/stress in cancer but not specifically nausea, and tai chi, MBSR, and progressive muscle relaxation have evidence for function, pain, anxiety, or stress rather than chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acupuncture indications include: chronic MSK pain, headache, OA (ACR/AF, conditional), low-back pain (ACP), chemo-induced nausea (add-on to antiemetics), pain in cancer (SIO/ASCO 2022), depression (modest), allergic rhinitis. This broad evidence base makes it the most guideline-supported physical integrative modality. COMLEX: When asked about nonpharmacologic management of chemo-related symptoms, acupuncture is a testable answer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q21","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a 5-year history of chronic migraines asks about integrative options. She has tried two preventive medications without success. Which integrative intervention has the strongest evidence for reducing headache frequency in both pediatric and adult migraine patients, with effects documented over a follow-up period of approximately 17 months?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has evidence for chronic musculoskeletal pain, headache, and osteoarthritis, with effects lasting up to 12 months","B":"Yoga, which has evidence for reducing anxiety, improving quality of life in chronic diseases, and helping with smoking cessation","C":"Biofeedback, which decreases the frequency of pediatric and adult migraines, with some studies showing an effect over an average 17-month follow-up","D":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain through multiple non-opioidergic mechanisms and benefits low-back pain and fibromyalgia","E":"Melatonin, which has evidence for reducing sleep-onset latency and improving daytime sleepiness in shift work sleep disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biofeedback has the most specific evidence for migraine: decreased frequency in pediatric and adult populations, with effects lasting ~17 months. Relaxation techniques more broadly also benefit migraines. Acupuncture has broad pain evidence including headache, but the specific 17-month migraine frequency data cited in Harrison's is for biofeedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biofeedback = tension headaches and migraines (strongest evidence, ~17-month durability). Relaxation techniques broadly help anxiety and stress conditions. Acupuncture helps headache but biofeedback has the specific pediatric and adult migraine-frequency evidence. COMLEX: Biofeedback is a psychological integrative modality that trains patients to control autonomic functions — an important distinction from physical manipulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q22","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is hospitalized with acute hepatic failure. His family reports he had been consuming wild mushrooms he foraged himself, believing them to have medicinal properties based on information from a wellness website. Which category of integrative medicine risk does this case exemplify?","choices":{"A":"Inherent toxicity of natural products, as misidentification of medicinal mushrooms can cause liver failure, a reminder that \"natural\" does not equate to \"safe\" when counseling patients","B":"Herb-drug interaction, as the hepatotoxicity resulted from the wild mushrooms upregulating pregnane X receptor expression in hepatic tissue, reducing the bioavailability of his prescribed medications","C":"Regulatory failure under DSHEA, as foraging websites making medicinal claims about wild mushrooms violate DSHEA structure-function claim restrictions and fall under FTC false advertising jurisdiction","D":"Adulteration of a dietary supplement product, as the mushrooms he consumed were contaminated with anabolic steroids and synthetic stimulants commonly found in products marketed for immune support","E":"Homeopathic product toxicity, as the diluted mushroom extract he consumed in homeopathic teething tablets contained significant amounts of undeclared pharmacologically active belladonna compounds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Misidentification of medicinal mushrooms → liver failure is a specific example of inherent natural product toxicity. Other examples: tryptophan contamination → eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome; Aristolochia → GU malignancies and interstitial nephritis; ephedra → cardiovascular events. \"Natural\" ≠ \"safe\" is the core principle. This is NOT an herb-drug interaction, adulteration, or homeopathic scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Natural product toxicity examples: mushrooms → liver failure; tryptophan → eosinophilia-myalgia; Aristolochia → GU cancer + nephritis; ephedra → cardiac events; DMAA → cardiovascular problems. Keep each example matched to its specific toxidrome. COMLEX: Counseling patients that natural products require the same safety scrutiny as medications is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q23","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman develops progressive renal insufficiency, and biopsy shows interstitial nephritis. She has been taking an imported herbal weight-loss product for 2 years. Months later she is also found to have a urothelial malignancy. Which natural product toxicity does this course reading describe that best fits her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated tryptophan supplements, which caused eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome with severe muscle pain and eosinophilia","B":"Misidentified medicinal mushrooms, which cause acute liver failure within days after a single foraged meal","C":"Ephedra-containing supplements, which caused heart attacks and death and were banned by the FDA in 2004","D":"DMAA stimulant supplements, which were touted as natural in 2013 and caused cardiovascular problems","E":"Herbal products with certain Aristolochia species, linked to interstitial nephritis and genitourinary malignancies"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course reading's section on inherent toxicity lists several harms from natural products: herbal products containing particular species of Aristolochia were associated with genitourinary malignancies and interstitial nephritis, which matches her kidney disease and urothelial cancer. Contaminated tryptophan caused eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome, a muscle and blood eosinophil syndrome rather than nephritis and cancer. Misidentified mushrooms caused liver failure. Ephedra and DMAA caused cardiovascular events, including heart attacks. The reading uses these examples to show that natural does not mean safe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aristolochic acid → interstitial nephritis (\"Chinese herb nephropathy\") and urothelial carcinoma. Other natural-product harms: ephedra (CV events, banned 2004), contaminated tryptophan (eosinophilia-myalgia). COMLEX: Supplements under DSHEA need no premarket proof of safety or efficacy, so a supplement history is essential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 5 — research challenges with variable, impure natural products"},{"id":"sdl12_q24","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient shows you a dietary supplement label that states: \"This product treats arthritis and reverses joint degeneration.\" You explain this is an illegal disease claim. Which claim on a supplement label WOULD be permissible under DSHEA?","choices":{"A":"A claim that the supplement is FDA-approved as safe and effective for reduction of joint inflammation based on Phase III clinical trial data submitted for premarket review","B":"A claim that the supplement prevents the development of osteoarthritis in high-risk patients, as prevention of a named disease would constitute a legally permissible prophylactic health claim","C":"A claim that the supplement cures rheumatoid arthritis by modulating immune function and has been shown in head-to-head trials to be equivalent to approved disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs","D":"A claim that the supplement \"supports normal structure and function\" of joints, since DSHEA permits structure/function claims but bans claims to prevent, treat, or cure a disease","E":"A claim that the supplement treats the pain and inflammation of specific disease states including osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, provided the manufacturer discloses the absence of FDA evaluation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSHEA structure/function claims examples: \"maintains normal joint health,\" \"supports immune function,\" \"promotes bone health.\" Disease claims examples (prohibited): \"treats arthritis,\" \"prevents osteoporosis,\" \"cures RA.\" Disclosure of no FDA evaluation does NOT make disease claims permissible. FDA does NOT grant premarket approval for supplements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA permitted = structure/function claims with FDA-disclaimer. DSHEA prohibited = disease claims (treat, prevent, cure). Classic example: can say \"maintains normal joint health\" but not \"treats arthritis.\" FTC acts against deceptive advertising; FDA acts against false label claims. COMLEX: Patients often show physicians supplement labels — recognizing illegal disease claims is a practical clinical skill.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q25","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The course reading notes that a physical therapeutic input can be delivered manually, delivered with a device, or generated by the patient. Which practice is an example of a physical input that is generated by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Massage, in which a therapist manipulates muscle and connective tissue","B":"Exercise, in which the patient's own movement provides the physical input","C":"Acupuncture, in which thin metallic needles stimulate defined anatomic points","D":"Biofeedback, in which a device helps the patient learn to control body functions","E":"Spinal manipulation, in which a practitioner applies a controlled thrust to a joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reading states that a physical input can be delivered manually (for example massage), with a device (for example acupuncture), or generated by the patient (for example exercise). Exercise is the patient-generated example. Massage and spinal manipulation are delivered manually by a practitioner. Acupuncture is delivered with a device. Biofeedback uses electronic devices, but it belongs with the relaxation techniques under primary psychological input, because the patient learns to consciously control functions such as breathing or heart rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physical inputs: manual (massage, spinal manipulation), device (acupuncture), patient-generated (exercise). Psychological inputs: CBT, meditation/MBSR, hypnosis, relaxation, biofeedback. Combined: yoga, tai chi. COMLEX: OMT is a manually delivered physical input with ACP support for low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — therapeutic exercise types in physical therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q26","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with fibromyalgia enrolls in a mindfulness-based stress reduction course and reports less pain. She asks whether the effect is just a placebo response or works through the same pathway as her prescribed opioid. According to the course reading, which statement best describes how mindfulness meditation reduces pain?","choices":{"A":"It reduces pain through endogenous opioid release, so naloxone blocks the effect in the same way it blocks morphine","B":"It produces no measurable change in pain beyond placebo, so any benefit reflects expectation and the course's attention","C":"It engages several unique nonopioidergic mechanisms that are distinct from placebo and vary with meditative training","D":"It works by stretching connective tissue during seated postures, which classifies it as a primary physical input","E":"It acts through the same peripheral COX inhibition as NSAIDs, lowering prostaglandin release in tender points"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The reading states that mindfulness meditation significantly reduces pain in experimental and clinical settings, including low back pain and fibromyalgia, and that neuroimaging and randomized trials show it reduces pain by engaging multiple unique nonopioidergic mechanisms that are distinct from placebo and vary across meditative training level. An opioid-mediated mechanism blocked by naloxone contradicts the nonopioidergic finding. A placebo-only effect contradicts the trials cited. Meditation is a primary psychological input; it does not act by stretching tissue. COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism, not the mechanism of meditation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Opioid analgesia = mu receptor agonism, reversed by naloxone; mindfulness analgesia is nonopioidergic. Psychological inputs (CBT-I first-line for chronic insomnia, MBSR, hypnosis, biofeedback for migraine). COMLEX: Nonpharmacologic pain care reduces reliance on chronic opioids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 7 — mechanism of action of opioid medications"},{"id":"sdl12_q27","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic low-back pain is treated with osteopathic spinal manipulative therapy. His wife asks what evidence supports this. According to ACP guidelines and current evidence, which statement best describes the evidence base for spinal manipulation in low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Spinal manipulation is recommended against by ACP guidelines for low-back pain due to the risk of vascular injury during cervical manipulation, which outweighs any documented functional benefit in the lumbar spine","B":"Spinal manipulation has high-quality evidence demonstrating clear superiority to acupuncture, yoga, and massage therapy for both acute and chronic low-back pain across all patient age groups","C":"Spinal manipulation carries only a weak ACP recommendation for low-back pain because the evidence quality is rated high but the benefit magnitude is considered too small to justify its routine use clinically","D":"Spinal manipulation has a small effect on function and pain versus sham or inert control, and nonpharmacologic treatment including it carries a strong ACP recommendation amid opioid concerns","E":"Spinal manipulation is classified as alternative rather than complementary medicine by ACP guidelines and therefore falls outside the scope of its nonpharmacologic treatment recommendations for low-back pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Evidence for spinal manipulation: small effect on function and pain vs. control. ACP rates nonpharmacologic treatment (including spinal manipulation) as a STRONG recommendation for low-back pain, driven by opioid crisis concerns. Evidence quality is LOW (not high), but the strong recommendation reflects the importance of nonpharmacologic alternatives. Cervical manipulation has vascular risk concerns but this applies to the neck, not as a blanket recommendation against lumbar SMT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spinal manipulation = small effect, strong ACP recommendation (nonpharmacologic treatment category). Low-quality evidence overall but strong recommendation context = opioid avoidance. Cervical SMT has vascular risk (vertebral artery dissection) — important distinction from lumbar. COMLEX: OMM is the osteopathic version of spinal manipulation; understanding this evidence base prepares you to discuss it with patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low-level laser therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q28","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The first large national survey documenting that more than 30% of Americans use complementary health products and practices surprised the medical community upon its publication. Who conducted this survey and in what year?","choices":{"A":"David Eisenberg and associates in 1993, whose survey revealed that more than 30% of Americans used complementary health products and practices, a finding that surprised the medical community and launched follow-up research","B":"The CDC National Center for Health Statistics in 1985, which performed the first National Health Interview Survey (NHIS) component specifically tracking complementary and alternative medicine use in U.S. households","C":"The National Institutes of Health NCCIH in 2002, which funded the first large-scale systematic review to establish prevalence estimates and annual out-of-pocket expenditures on complementary health practices","D":"The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality in 1998, which established the first federal tracking system specifically documenting expenditures on complementary health practices as a proportion of total health spending","E":"The American Medical Association in 1975, which conducted the original national survey on patient self-referral to complementary health practitioners that prompted early professional interest in this phenomenon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eisenberg et al. 1993 = the landmark survey. Found >30% of Americans use complementary practices, surprising the medical community. The NHIS (conducted by CDC/NCHS) has since expanded this surveillance, surveying in 2002, 2007, 2012, 2017, and 2022. The 2022 NHIS found 36.7% use, up from 19.2% in 2002 for the seven tracked approaches.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eisenberg 1993 = landmark survey showing >30% of Americans used complementary medicine. 2022 NHIS = 36.7% (vs. 19.2% in 2002). Out-of-pocket: $30.2 billion in 2012 (9.2% of total OOP costs). Pain = most common reason for complementary health use. COMLEX: Understanding the prevalence data helps osteopathic physicians recognize why integrative counseling is a routine clinical need.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q29","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team designs a study to evaluate a naturopathic treatment protocol for metabolic syndrome. They find that applying a classic parallel-group RCT design is problematic. Which feature of naturopathic medicine most directly creates this methodologic challenge?","choices":{"A":"Naturopathic practitioners lack licensure in most U.S. states, making study enrollment problematic because only licensed health care providers may legally serve as study investigators in federally funded clinical research","B":"The multicomponent, individualized nature of naturopathic protocols makes interventions hard to standardize, and pragmatic usual-care comparisons cannot identify which component produces the benefit","C":"FDA regulations require a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product included in a naturopathic protocol, creating an insurmountable regulatory barrier that prevents most such trials from being initiated","D":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for naturopathic study visits, making participation financially prohibitive for patients with metabolic syndrome who face disproportionately high baseline health care costs","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in all naturopathic research because both practitioners and participants can always identify whether active naturopathic treatment or a sham protocol is being administered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic RCT requires a standardized, reproducible intervention. Naturopathy individualizes treatment — a given patient might receive different herbal, dietary, physical, and lifestyle combinations. Pragmatic trials with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate which component helped. \"Manualized\" protocols with patient-level flexibility are being developed. Blinding challenges exist but are not unique to naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT challenge in multicomponent integrative systems = individualization prevents standardization + cannot isolate active components. Solution = pragmatic designs + manualized protocols. The NCCIH funds much of this research. COMLEX: OMM research faces the same individualization challenge — treatment plans are patient-specific, making RCT design in osteopathic research similarly complex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q30","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A health policy researcher asks you about U.S. out-of-pocket spending on complementary health practices based on the 2012 National Health Interview Survey data. Which statement accurately represents these expenditure findings?","choices":{"A":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, with the majority spent on yoga and meditation classes; natural product spending accounted for less than 5% of this total","B":"Americans spent approximately $5 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, primarily on acupuncture and chiropractic visits, representing a minor fraction of total out-of-pocket expenditures nationally","C":"Americans spent approximately $75 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, exceeding their total spending on conventional physician visits and making complementary care the single largest category of personal health expenditure","D":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, which exceeded total out-of-pocket spending on prescription drugs ($54.1 billion), making supplements the dominant out-of-pocket health cost","E":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion out of pocket in 2012 (9.2% of out-of-pocket health spending), including $12.8 billion on natural products, about one-quarter of what they spent on prescription drugs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Exact 2012 NHIS data: $30.2 billion total OOP ($28.3B adults + $1.9B children). $14.7B on practitioner visits (vs. $49.6B conventional physicians). $12.8B on natural products (vs. $54.1B prescription drugs = ~24%). Represents 9.2% of total OOP costs and 1.1% of total health expenditures. Pain is the most common driver of spending.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 2012 expenditure data: $30.2B total; $14.7B practitioner visits; $12.8B natural products = ~24% of RX drug spending. This puts integrative medicine use in economic perspective. COMLEX: The high out-of-pocket cost burden reflects patient demand that is not covered by insurance — osteopathic physicians should be prepared to counsel on cost-effective integrative options.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"Shoulder Pain & Pathologies","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of a shoulder pain presentation most strongly suggests an EXTRINSIC rather than intrinsic cause?","choices":{"A":"Anterior shoulder pain aggravated by glenohumeral abduction and external rotation, with focal anterior joint line tenderness and a directional loss of motion corresponding to those same planes on examination","B":"Sharp, shooting, or electric pain radiating from the shoulder to the neck or down the arm, accompanied by dyspnea or diaphoresis, with symmetric shoulder appearance and glenohumeral motion unaffected on examination","C":"Gradually worsening anterior shoulder pain over several years with morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, crepitus during motion, and age greater than 45 years suggesting glenohumeral osteoarthritis","D":"Lateral shoulder pain referred to the deltoid insertion on the humerus, worse at night and when sleeping on the affected side, associated with overhead activities and weakness if a significant rotator cuff tear is present","E":"Superior shoulder pain worsening with adduction and forward flexion, with localized tenderness directly over the acromioclavicular joint and a corresponding loss of motion in those specific loading planes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrinsic shoulder pain clues from the diagnostic workflow include: vague or poorly localized pain, pain associated with other symptoms such as dyspnea, abdominal pain, or diaphoresis, sharp or electric pain radiating to the neck or down the arm, a history concerning for extrinsic cause such as recent MVC or cardiac disease, glenohumeral motion does NOT reproduce the pain, and symmetric shoulder appearance, motion, and strength. All other choices describe intrinsic patterns (GH OA, rotator cuff, ACJ OA). The \"shooting with dyspnea\" combination is a classic red flag for referred or visceral pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the extrinsic shoulder pain red flags: dyspnea + shoulder pain = rule out cardiac/pulmonary; diaphoresis = autonomic activation from visceral source. Glenohumeral motion should NOT reproduce extrinsic pain. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use thorough somatic and visceral assessment — Chapman reflex for lung/heart can produce extrinsic shoulder pain in osteopathic context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q02","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man presents with shoulder pain after a fall on an outstretched hand. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, what is the FIRST branching point that determines the direction of the workup?","choices":{"A":"Determine whether the pattern of motion loss is directional (suggesting arthritis) or global (suggesting frozen shoulder) before proceeding to radiographs, since this classification avoids unnecessary imaging costs","B":"Determine whether the pain is primarily glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal based on whether gentle passive glenohumeral motion reproduces the pain during the physical examination portion of the visit","C":"Order plain radiographs as the first action in all shoulder pain presentations regardless of mechanism, since imaging is required before any classification of the shoulder pain source can be made","D":"Classify the shoulder complaint as intrinsic versus extrinsic based on pain character, associated symptoms, and the symmetry of shoulder appearance, motion, and strength on bilateral comparison examination","E":"Determine whether a significant history of trauma is present, since trauma versus no trauma is the first branch, routing toward imaging for fracture or dislocation versus assessment for intrinsic or extrinsic cause"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The UpToDate shoulder pain diagnostic workflow begins with a single gate: \"Is there a history of trauma?\" This is the primary bifurcation. Trauma-positive routes to history, physical exam, and plain radiographs to assess for fracture, glenohumeral dislocation, AC sprain, or sternoclavicular subluxation. Trauma-negative routes to the intrinsic versus extrinsic distinction. The intrinsic versus extrinsic differentiation and the glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal distinction occur downstream from the trauma gate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trauma gate is intuitive but often tested in the context of missed fractures — always image a traumatized shoulder. COMLEX: Osteopathic evaluation adds palpatory assessment of the shoulder girdle (acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular, glenohumeral) and may identify somatic dysfunction at T2-T4 as a viscerosomatic source of shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q03","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding on physical examination best localizes a shoulder complaint to an EXTRAGLENOHUMEREAL (rather than glenohumeral intra-articular) intrinsic source?","choices":{"A":"Focal pain and tenderness outside the glenohumeral joint capsule, with little or no pain on gentle passive glenohumeral motion, pointing toward biceps tendinopathy, AC arthritis, subscapular bursitis, or distal clavicle osteolysis","B":"Anterior glenohumeral joint line tenderness with pain and loss of motion in abduction and external rotation that worsens with active resistance testing, consistent with a glenohumeral intra-articular source such as GH osteoarthritis","C":"Global loss of active and passive motion in multiple planes with preserved strength and absence of crepitus, consistent with a capsular process such as adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint interior","D":"Weakness of the supraspinatus on resisted abduction testing with a positive drop arm sign and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during overhead activity or active abduction","E":"Pain reproduced by gentle passive glenohumeral rotation, with anterior joint line tenderness and crepitus felt during passive motion, consistent with a glenohumeral intra-articular source"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Per the diagnostic workflow, extraglenohumereal causes are identified by two criteria: (1) focal pain and tenderness outside the GH joint, and (2) gentle passive motion of the glenohumeral joint generally does NOT cause pain. Extraglenohumereal conditions include biceps tendinopathy, biceps tendon tear, AC arthritis, scapulothoracic weakness, subscapular bursitis, and distal clavicle osteolysis. The other options describe glenohumeral patterns (GH OA, adhesive capsulitis, full-thickness cuff tear, and pain on passive GH motion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The \"passive GH motion does not hurt\" rule is the key clinical divider. Biceps tendinopathy is the most classic extraglenohumereal diagnosis — Speed test and Yergason test assess it specifically. COMLEX: Biceps tendinopathy in the context of shoulder somatic dysfunction is a common COMLEX scenario where OMM to restore GH motion may reduce impingement on the biceps tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q04","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has 3 years of gradually worsening anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, morning stiffness lasting 20 minutes, increased stiffness after prolonged rest, and loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness and mild crepitus. Which diagnosis and mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint, characterized by idiopathic global loss of motion in all planes, preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern, and stronger association with diabetes and thyroid disease than with age-related degeneration","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, with degenerative cartilage loss at the glenohumeral articulation producing usage-related anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is directionally worst in abduction and external rotation, consistent with OARSI clinical criteria","C":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis, with degenerative changes at the superior shoulder articulation producing superior (not anterior) shoulder pain that is characteristically worst with adduction and forward flexion rather than abduction and external rotation","D":"Rotator cuff impingement with subacromial tendinosis, producing supraspinatus compression in the extra-articular subacromial space with pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus rather than diffusely to the upper arm","E":"Subacromial bursitis with fluid accumulation in the subdeltoid bursa causing anterior shoulder pain, but distinguished from OA by absence of crepitus, morning stiffness, and usage-related progressive course over years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GH OA: most common type of arthritis at the GH joint; most patients >70 (women > men); anterior shoulder and upper arm pain (GH location); morning stiffness; stiffer with inactivity; loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation; anterior joint line tenderness and crepitus. ACJ OA produces SUPERIOR shoulder pain worst with adduction/forward flexion. Adhesive capsulitis produces GLOBAL motion loss in all planes. Rotator cuff pain refers to the lateral humerus. These location and plane distinctions are high-yield board discriminators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA = anterior shoulder + upper arm, worst abduction/ER. ACJ OA = superior shoulder, worst adduction/forward flexion. COMLEX: GH OA has a COMLEX bump because shoulder somatic dysfunction and OMM are tested in the context of glenohumeral pathology and post-arthroplasty rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q05","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately distinguishes the clinical presentation of acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis from glenohumeral osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Acromioclavicular OA primarily affects patients under age 50 with prior overhead sports exposure, while glenohumeral OA primarily affects women over age 70 with prior dislocation, fracture, or massive rotator cuff tear history","B":"Acromioclavicular OA produces posterior shoulder pain worsening with internal rotation and extension, while glenohumeral OA produces lateral deltoid pain worsening with overhead motion due to subacromial compression","C":"Acromioclavicular OA produces superior shoulder pain that is worst with adduction and forward flexion, while glenohumeral OA produces anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is worst with abduction and external rotation","D":"Acromioclavicular OA always requires radiographic joint space narrowing for clinical diagnosis, while glenohumeral OA is exclusively a clinical diagnosis that does not require or benefit from any radiographic assessment","E":"Acromioclavicular OA is managed with joint replacement as first-line therapy, while glenohumeral OA requires a stepwise conservative approach with pharmacologic management and physical therapy before surgical options"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most tested distinction: ACJ OA = superior shoulder pain, worst with ADDUCTION and FORWARD FLEXION (planes that load the AC joint — cross-body adduction test is positive). GH OA = anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, worst with ABDUCTION and EXTERNAL ROTATION (planes that load the glenohumeral joint — GH joint line tenderness anteriorly). Both share similar diagnostic criteria (age ≥45, usage-related pain, morning stiffness ≤30 min) and both are managed with a similar OARSI-adapted framework. Joint replacement is a last resort for both.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remember — ACJ = ADDUCTION worsens (cross-body maneuver). GH = ABDUCTION/ER worsens. These opposing plane findings appear frequently on USMLE. COMLEX: Both joints can develop somatic dysfunction and may respond to OMM — AC joint manipulation is a tested COMLEX technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q06","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the course, which statement best describes the epidemiology of glenohumeral and acromioclavicular osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Most patients are older than 70 and women outnumber men; true prevalence is unclear because many patients are asymptomatic","B":"Most patients are younger than 50 and men outnumber women; prevalence is well defined because most patients have symptoms","C":"Rheumatoid arthritis, not osteoarthritis, is the most common type of arthritis in both joints, especially in older women","D":"Shoulder joints are the most common sites of osteoarthritis in the body, ahead of the knee and hip in every age group","E":"Most patients are teenage overhead athletes, because repeated throwing wears the glenoid cartilage before skeletal maturity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that glenohumeral and acromioclavicular OA are less common than OA of other joints, but that osteoarthritis is the most common type of arthritis seen in the GH and AC joints. Most patients are older than 70, women outnumber men, and prevalence is very difficult to determine because many patients are asymptomatic. A younger, male-predominant pattern with well-defined prevalence reverses these points. Rheumatoid arthritis is not the most common type in these joints. Shoulder joints are less commonly affected than other joints, not more. Shoulder OA is not a disease of adolescent throwers; risk factors are direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, and (for GH) severe rotator cuff tears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shoulder OA is a clinical diagnosis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, morning stiffness ≤30 min); GH OA = anterior pain worst in abduction/ER; AC OA = superior pain worst in adduction/forward flexion. COMLEX: \"The rotator cuff is not a joint\": cuff disease is a separate diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee osteoarthritis criteria and OARSI management"},{"id":"sdl13_q07","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rotator cuff impingement occurs in the subacromial space. Which anatomical fact about this space is most clinically important?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial space is an intra-articular compartment within the glenohumeral joint capsule, so glenohumeral corticosteroid injection simultaneously treats both impingement and GH osteoarthritis with a single injection procedure","B":"The subacromial space is extra-articular (outside the glenohumeral joint capsule), so impingement is distinct from glenohumeral joint disease and needs a targeted subacromial injection rather than an intra-articular injection","C":"The subacromial space is narrowed exclusively by edema of the supraspinatus tendon, while chronic tendinosis causing tendon thinning actually widens the space and reduces impingement because the tendon occupies less volume","D":"The subacromial space is a diarthrodial synovial joint lined by synovium, and inflammation of this space constitutes a septic bursitis that requires emergent aspiration, culture, and intravenous antibiotic therapy in all cases","E":"The subacromial space contains the infraspinatus tendon as its primary occupant, and infraspinatus impingement is the most common rotator cuff pathology because the posterior cuff is most exposed to mechanical stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The subacromial space is EXTRA-articular — outside the GH joint capsule. This is a key clinical distinction: subacromial injection targets the bursa and rotator cuff tendons directly, while GH intra-articular injection targets joint cartilage and synovium. The supraspinatus (not infraspinatus) occupies the subacromial space's \"critical zone.\" Two impingement mechanisms: (1) edematous supraspinatus too large to glide through the space; (2) thin/degenerated supraspinatus allows superior humeral migration, narrowing the space from the humeral side. Patients cycle between these states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extra-articular subacromial space = supraspinatus, subacromial-subdeltoid bursa. Intra-articular = GH joint capsule and cartilage. Injection target depends on pathology location. COMLEX: Subacromial injection is a common osteopathic procedural topic. Glenohumeral somatic dysfunction can reduce subacromial clearance — an OMM rationale for treating impingement conservatively.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q08","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The pathologic progression of rotator cuff tendinosis is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"An acute inflammatory infiltration of the supraspinatus tendon that permanently resolves with anti-inflammatory treatment within 6 weeks, leaving no residual structural vulnerability or predisposition to re-injury in the affected tendon","B":"A process beginning with bursal-sided full-thickness tearing that propagates to the articular surface over time due to repetitive eccentric loading, and anti-inflammatory medications can halt propagation if initiated within the first 3 months","C":"Calcification of the supraspinatus tendon from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition that mechanically narrows the subacromial space, producing calcific tendinitis rather than true tendinosis with a distinct treatment algorithm","D":"Progressive glenohumeral joint capsule fibrosis triggered by rotator cuff pain inhibition, producing global loss of motion in all planes that is indistinguishable from primary adhesive capsulitis on clinical examination alone","E":"After about 3 months of ongoing cuff injury, the tendon becomes chronically degenerative with no inflammatory cells, showing disorganization and degeneration that can periodically re-inflame and perpetuate the impingement cycle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tendinosis vs. tendinitis: it is only a true \"tendinitis\" if acutely inflamed. Once chronic (approximately 3 months), no inflammatory cells are present — the tendon is in a state of disorganization and degeneration. This chronic state can periodically re-inflame, driving the rotator cuff impingement cycle. This distinction matters therapeutically: anti-inflammatories and corticosteroids address the acute inflammatory phase but do not reverse chronic degeneration. Surgery is reserved for recalcitrant cases and full or massive tears. PT is the mainstay of management throughout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinosis (3+ months, no inflammation, disorganized collagen) vs. tendinitis (acute, inflammatory cells, responds to NSAIDs). Classic board trap: calling a chronic rotator cuff condition \"tendinitis\" and treating only with NSAIDs. COMLEX: Osteopathic management of tendinosis includes prolotherapy and PRP (pro-inflammatory regenerative injections to stimulate repair) — listed explicitly in the course treatment options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q09","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test specifically assesses rotator cuff tendon INTEGRITY (presence/absence of a significant tear) rather than the presence of impingement?","choices":{"A":"The Neer impingement sign, performed by stabilizing the scapula and passively forward flexing the internally rotated arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the anterior acromion and is positive in subacromial impingement syndrome","B":"The Hawkins impingement test, performed by forward flexing the shoulder to 90 degrees and then passively internally rotating the arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the coracoacromial ligament and is positive in impingement","C":"The cross-body adduction test, performed by passively adducting the arm horizontally across the chest at 90 degrees, which compresses the acromioclavicular joint and is positive in AC joint arthritis rather than rotator cuff pathology","D":"The Drop Arm Test, performed by passively abducting the patient's arm to 90 degrees and asking them to slowly lower it — inability to control the lowering indicates a significant full-thickness rotator cuff tear affecting active tendon function","E":"The Speed test, performed by forward flexing the arm against resistance with the elbow extended and forearm supinated, which loads the proximal biceps tendon and is positive in biceps tendinopathy rather than rotator cuff tear"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Per the course slides, tendon strength and integrity testing includes resisted abduction, external rotation, and internal rotation — PLUS the Drop Arm Test. The Drop Arm Test specifically tests whether the patient can actively control the lowered arm (requires intact supraspinatus function); inability = significant full-thickness tear. Neer's sign and Hawkins test assess for IMPINGEMENT. The distinction matters: impingement can occur with an intact tendon, while a positive Drop Arm suggests the tendon has lost functional integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drop Arm = full-thickness tear (supraspinatus). Neer = impingement (supraspinatus against anterior acromion). Hawkins = impingement (supraspinatus against coracoacromial ligament). Empty Can test = supraspinatus strength. These physical exam tests are classic Step 1 discriminators. COMLEX: All of these tests are tested in COMLEX clinical skills and are part of the osteopathic shoulder structural examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q10","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with 6 months of gradually worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had pain that has now lessened; she has difficulty reaching overhead, behind her back, and across her body equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, producing directional loss of motion specifically worst in abduction and external rotation with anterior joint line tenderness, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and crepitus in a woman over age 45","B":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy with impingement, presenting with overhead pain, subacromial tenderness, a positive Neer or Hawkins sign, and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during resisted abduction testing","C":"Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), presenting with global loss of motion in multiple planes without significant strength loss or pain on resistance testing, in a patient at peak age (mid-50s) with a well-established risk factor (type 2 diabetes)","D":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis with secondary bursitis, producing superior shoulder stiffness and multiple planes of motion restriction due to AC joint osteophyte formation causing mechanical compression of the subacromial space","E":"Cervical radiculopathy at C5-C6, producing referred shoulder stiffness and apparent global motion loss due to chronic pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and secondary periarticular contracture in a patient with diabetic neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adhesive capsulitis hallmarks: global loss of motion in ALL planes (not directional), no other structural explanation, gradual onset, pain then stiffness pattern (pain often lessens as stiffness progresses), preserved strength, peak mid-50s, and strong association with diabetes (most important metabolic risk factor), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease. Lifetime prevalence 2-5%; affects 8.2% of males and 10.1% of females (working age). GH OA would have directional loss (worst abduction/ER). Rotator cuff impingement preserves most motion. Imaging is NOT helpful for diagnosing frozen shoulder — it may identify other conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = diabetes association (strongest), bilateral in 6-17% of patients, global motion loss in all planes. Classic board presentation is a middle-aged diabetic woman with progressively restricted shoulder motion in all planes. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis management includes OMM to restore GH motion — glenohumeral articulation with high-velocity low-amplitude technique or muscle energy is a tested COMLEX treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q11","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized SECONDARY cause of adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)?","choices":{"A":"Cervical C5-C6 radiculopathy in a manual laborer, which refers sharp pain into the shoulder and limits active elevation while gentle passive glenohumeral motion remains full and painless on examination","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis with severe cartilage erosion, which progresses to capsular contracture when osteophytes mechanically block abduction and external rotation until the joint develops secondary adhesive changes","C":"Acromioclavicular joint arthritis with superior osteophyte formation that bridges the AC joint space, causing progressive mechanical impingement of the rotator cuff that leads to secondary GH capsular fibrosis over years","D":"Prolonged immobilization following a humerus fracture or a post-operative shoulder procedure, during which capsular contracture and adhesions form without adequate range of motion exercise and joint mobilization therapy","E":"Subacromial bursitis with marked edema of the subdeltoid bursa compressing the glenohumeral capsule externally, causing secondary fibrosis and reduced joint volume indistinguishable from primary idiopathic frozen shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary causes of adhesive capsulitis listed in the course slides: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, and post-operative procedures. Immobilization prevents the normal joint motion that maintains capsular compliance, allowing contracture and adhesion formation. Cervical radiculopathy is an extrinsic source of shoulder pain and does not contract the glenohumeral capsule; glenohumeral OA, AC arthritis, and subacromial bursitis restrict motion directionally or not at all. Idiopathic (primary) frozen shoulder is associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease — these are NOT secondary causes. This distinction matters because prevention after humerus fracture or shoulder surgery requires aggressive early ROM, and failure to do so produces iatrogenic frozen shoulder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary frozen shoulder triggers: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, post-op. Primary triggers: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia. The course slides explicitly list these categories. COMLEX: Post-surgical frozen shoulder is a preventable complication when OMM-guided early shoulder mobilization is incorporated into rehabilitation — an osteopathic perioperative care concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q12","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the standard first-line treatment approach for adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), and what is the escalation step if initial management fails?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) combined with physical therapy to restore range of motion as soon as possible, escalating to manipulation under anesthesia if this approach fails to restore adequate ROM","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection combined with NSAIDs and activity modification, targeting the subacromial bursa to reduce impingement-related inflammation and allow passive stretching to gradually restore capsular compliance over weeks","C":"Immediate surgical arthroscopic capsular release as first-line treatment due to the irreversible fibrotic nature of adhesive capsulitis, with glenohumeral injection reserved only for patients who are not surgical candidates due to comorbidities","D":"Physical therapy stretching alone for 12 to 18 months without any corticosteroid injection, since evidence shows corticosteroids worsen long-term outcomes by masking pain and reducing the patient's motivation to perform home exercises","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint combined with hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation, which outperforms corticosteroids for the capsular fibrosis of frozen shoulder in meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of adhesive capsulitis from the course slides: (1) PARAMOUNT — restore ROM as soon as possible; (2) FIRST LINE — glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection OR oral glucocorticoids + physical therapy; (3) ESCALATION — manipulation under anesthesia if the above fails. The injection target is GLENOHUMERAL (intra-articular) because the pathology is capsular, not subacromial. Arthroscopic capsular release exists but is not first-line. The emphasis on early ROM reflects the pathophysiology: continued immobility worsens capsular fibrosis and adhesion formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder Rx = GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. NOT subacromial injection (that targets rotator cuff/bursa). NOT immediate surgery. The intra-articular versus subacromial distinction is a classic board discriminator. COMLEX: OMM (articulatory/muscle energy) may be used as an adjunct to restore GH motion in adhesive capsulitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q13","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the emergency department after a motor vehicle collision. She has right shoulder pain. Examination reveals diffuse, poorly localized shoulder pain without focal joint line tenderness. Glenohumeral range of motion is symmetric to the left and does not reproduce her pain. She also reports mild shortness of breath and diaphoresis. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform supraspinatus strength testing in resisted abduction and external rotation followed by Neer and Hawkins impingement maneuvers to assess for a traumatic rotator cuff tear associated with the motor vehicle collision","B":"Order MRI of the right shoulder with contrast to evaluate for labral tear, glenoid rim fracture, or glenohumeral instability, given the history of high-energy trauma from the motor vehicle collision mechanism of injury","C":"Order plain radiographs of the right shoulder to assess subacromial space dimension and acromial morphology, since plain films are the first imaging modality for all traumatically injured shoulders in the emergency setting","D":"Evaluate for an extrinsic or referred cause of her shoulder complaint, given poorly localized pain, unaffected glenohumeral motion, symmetric shoulder appearance, dyspnea, and diaphoresis suggesting a visceral source","E":"Perform glenohumeral joint aspiration to rule out post-traumatic hemarthrosis, since bloody effusion within the GH joint capsule is the most common cause of diffuse shoulder pain following a motor vehicle collision"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Despite the trauma history, this patient has multiple extrinsic pain markers: diffuse poorly localized pain, GH motion does NOT reproduce pain, symmetric shoulder appearance and motion, AND associated dyspnea and diaphoresis. These autonomic and respiratory symptoms alongside a trauma mechanism (MVC) raise concern for pneumothorax, cardiac contusion, aortic injury, or other thoracic pathology producing referred shoulder pain. The shoulder workflow directs evaluation for extrinsic cause when these features are present, regardless of trauma history. Proceeding directly to shoulder-specific imaging without considering the broader clinical picture would miss a potentially life-threatening cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diaphoresis + dyspnea + diffuse shoulder pain after MVC = rule out thoracic/cardiac cause first. Referred pain to the left shoulder (Kehr sign) from diaphragm irritation (spleen rupture) is a classic Step 1 scenario. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians trained in viscerosomatic reflexes recognize that T1-T5 somatic dysfunction can represent referred visceral pain — thoracic inlet assessment is always part of the OMM shoulder evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q14","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old man falls off a bicycle onto his right shoulder. He has shoulder pain, and his history and examination are documented. Plain radiographs show no fracture of the clavicle or proximal humerus, no glenohumeral dislocation, no AC sprain, and no sternoclavicular subluxation. According to the shoulder pain workflow taught in the course, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Close the trauma pathway and evaluate for an extrinsic cause, such as cardiac or abdominal disease referring pain to the shoulder","B":"Order an MRI arthrogram before any further examination, because normal radiographs after trauma mean a labral tear is likely","C":"Evaluate for intrinsic soft tissue injury and decide whether the findings point to a glenohumeral or extraglenohumeral cause","D":"Refer directly to orthopedic surgery, because any shoulder trauma with pain and normal radiographs needs operative exploration","E":"Repeat the plain radiographs in 2 weeks, because occult fractures of the humerus become visible as resorption widens the line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the UpToDate shoulder pain workflow on the course slide, the trauma branch starts with history, examination (which may include ultrasound), and plain radiographs. If imaging shows a fracture, glenohumeral dislocation, AC sprain, or sternoclavicular injury, management is started and orthopedics is consulted as indicated. If none is found, the workflow moves to evaluating for intrinsic soft tissue injury and deciding between a glenohumeral cause (pain, weakness, or abnormal motion on glenohumeral exam, such as rotator cuff disease, labral tear, or instability) and an extraglenohumeral cause (focal tenderness outside the joint with painless gentle passive motion). Extrinsic causes are considered in the no-trauma branch. An MRI arthrogram is not the next step before the clinical examination sorts the cause. Orthopedic referral is for an identified structural injury. Repeat radiographs are not the workflow step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shoulder pain algorithm: trauma? → imaging for fracture/dislocation/AC/SC injury → if negative, intrinsic soft tissue injury (glenohumeral vs extraglenohumeral). No trauma → intrinsic vs extrinsic (referred) cause. COMLEX: Glenohumeral causes include rotator cuff disease, adhesive capsulitis, instability, labral tear, GH arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — clavicle fracture presentation and imaging"},{"id":"sdl13_q15","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male recreational rock climber presents with 6 weeks of right shoulder pain after a bouldering fall. He reports diffuse anterior shoulder pain. On exam, the glenohumeral joint produces pain and restricted motion with passive testing. Bilateral shoulder comparison reveals asymmetric shoulder contour and motion. Radiographs show no fracture but confirm the humeral head is displaced anteriorly relative to the glenoid. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, which pathway does this patient follow?","choices":{"A":"Trauma-negative pathway: assess whether the pain pattern suggests intrinsic versus extrinsic cause, then determine if examination findings are consistent with a glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal etiology before ordering any imaging studies","B":"Trauma-positive pathway: obtain the history of trauma, perform a physical exam, obtain plain radiographs, and identify the specific structural finding (here, glenohumeral dislocation), then manage and refer to orthopedics as indicated","C":"Trauma-positive pathway: first determine whether the cause is glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal by assessing whether passive GH motion produces pain, then order MRI for soft tissue characterization before plain radiographs given the high-energy mechanism","D":"Trauma-negative intrinsic pathway: classify as a glenohumeral cause and order MRI with contrast to evaluate for labral pathology associated with the traumatic mechanism, reserving plain radiographs for elderly patients with concern for osteoporosis","E":"Extrinsic cause pathway: the diffuse nature of the pain and the history of trauma from a fall suggest referred visceral pain and a cardiac or pulmonary source should be excluded before any shoulder-specific imaging or intervention is initiated"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trauma is present → trauma-positive pathway: history + exam + plain radiographs + identify specific pathology. This patient has glenohumeral dislocation (anterior displacement of humeral head, asymmetric contour, restricted motion). Per the workflow: workup and imaging reveal a specific diagnosis (fracture, GH dislocation, AC sprain, SCJ subluxation) → initiate appropriate management → refer to orthopedics as indicated. The workflow explicitly lists glenohumeral dislocation as a trauma-positive finding. The extraglenohumereal and passive motion assessment come AFTER the trauma gate routes to \"no\" (trauma-negative).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior GH dislocation (most common) presents with arm held in slight abduction and external rotation, humeral head palpable anteriorly. Axillary nerve injury (deltoid weakness, lateral arm numbness) is the classic associated nerve injury. COMLEX: Post-dislocation shoulder somatic dysfunction is a COMLEX scenario — glenohumeral restriction (especially external rotation) often persists after reduction and may respond to OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q16","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a 5-year history of right anterior shoulder and upper arm pain progressively worsening with activity. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness, pain and reduced motion worst in abduction and external rotation, mild crepitus, and no weakness. She has a history of right glenohumeral dislocation at age 45. Which management approach is MOST consistent with the OARSI-adapted framework taught for this condition?","choices":{"A":"Immediate referral for total shoulder arthroplasty given her 5-year disease duration and prior dislocation history, since surgical joint replacement is indicated for glenohumeral OA once conservative management has exceeded 3 years","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection as the primary pharmacologic intervention, targeting the subacromial space and rotator cuff rather than the glenohumeral joint space, since reactive bursitis from cartilage degeneration causes most of the pain","C":"A stepwise approach beginning with pharmacologic management, physical therapy, and exercise, escalating to intra-articular corticosteroid injections, and reserving regenerative injections, acupuncture, and arthroplasty for refractory cases","D":"Long-term daily oral glucocorticoid therapy as the pharmacologic anchor of GH OA management combined with passive physical therapy modalities, avoiding exercise due to concerns that loading an arthritic joint accelerates cartilage degradation","E":"Avoidance of physical therapy and exercise because evidence shows resistance training accelerates cartilage loss in glenohumeral osteoarthritis, making all weight-bearing and load-bearing shoulder activities contraindicated in elderly women"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The OARSI-adapted framework for shoulder OA management (from the course slides): pharmacologic management → physical therapy and exercise → intra-articular corticosteroid injections → regenerative injections (prolotherapy, PRP, stem cells) → acupuncture → joint replacement. It is escalating and stepwise. PT/exercise is a core component (NOT contraindicated). The injection for GH OA is INTRA-ARTICULAR (not subacromial — that targets rotator cuff/bursa). Daily oral glucocorticoids are NOT the anchor. Prior dislocation is a known risk factor for GH OA but does not accelerate the timing for surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA treatment is conservative-first: analgesics, PT, intra-articular injection, then surgery. The injection goes IN the joint (intra-articular), not above it (subacromial). This distinction is tested. COMLEX: Acupuncture is listed as a treatment option in the OARSI framework for OA — integrative medicine approaches for musculoskeletal OA are COMLEX-tested, connecting to SDL 12 content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q17","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has 3 years of gradually worsening anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, stiffness that is worse after inactivity, and loss of abduction and external rotation. Radiographs show glenohumeral joint space narrowing with osteophytes. Which item in her history is listed in the course as a risk factor for this condition?","choices":{"A":"A displaced proximal humerus fracture treated 20 years ago after a fall onto the shoulder","B":"A history of lateral epicondylitis on the same side treated with a counterforce brace","C":"Years of daily swimming laps, which the course lists as the leading cause of shoulder OA","D":"Long-term use of an oral bisphosphonate started after a vertebral compression fracture","E":"A prior episode of adhesive capsulitis on the opposite shoulder that resolved with therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists risk factors for glenohumeral and AC osteoarthritis as direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, and, for the glenohumeral joint, severe rotator cuff tears. A remote proximal humerus fracture is a prior fracture involving the joint region. Lateral epicondylitis is an elbow tendinopathy and is not a shoulder OA risk factor. Swimming is not listed as a risk factor; overhead activity is listed for rotator cuff injury instead. Bisphosphonate use is not a listed risk factor. Adhesive capsulitis on the opposite side raises the chance of contralateral frozen shoulder (6 to 17%), not of osteoarthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-traumatic arthritis follows intra-articular injury, malunion, and chondrocyte death. Shoulder OA risk: trauma, prior dislocation or fracture, massive cuff tear (cuff tear arthropathy). COMLEX: Treatment follows an OARSI-adapted ladder: analgesics → PT/exercise → IA steroid → regenerative injection/acupuncture → arthroplasty.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — proximal humerus fractures in older adults"},{"id":"sdl13_q18","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a known massive rotator cuff tear managed conservatively for 4 years presents with new progressive anterior shoulder pain and loss of motion. Radiographs now show superior migration of the humeral head toward the acromion. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam: anterior joint line tenderness, pain and loss of abduction and external rotation, crepitus. Strength testing shows weakness. Which complication best explains his new presentation?","choices":{"A":"Progressive extension of the rotator cuff tear into the subscapularis tendon, producing new internal rotation weakness and anterior shoulder pain at the lesser tuberosity rather than anterior joint line tenderness indicating cartilage involvement","B":"Development of isolated subacromial bursitis due to mechanical impingement by the superiorly migrated humeral head, producing anterior and lateral shoulder pain that is distinct from the progressive articular cartilage degeneration of OA","C":"Glenohumeral instability secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, with the humeral head subluxing anteriorly during abduction attempts and causing anterior joint pain that mimics OA without true articular cartilage degeneration","D":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, since severe cuff tears are a recognized risk factor for GH OA through superior humeral migration and resulting cartilage damage (rotator cuff tear arthropathy)","E":"Adhesive capsulitis triggered by pain-mediated disuse of the shoulder, producing secondary capsular contracture with global motion loss in all planes in a patient whose rotator cuff deficiency impairs normal glenohumeral mechanics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"From the course slides: GH OA risk factors include direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, AND severe (massive) rotator cuff tears. Massive cuff tears permit superior migration of the humeral head (visible as \"high-riding humerus\" on radiograph), which shears articular cartilage and produces GH OA — called cuff tear arthropathy. Clinical features: anterior joint line tenderness, directional loss (worst abduction/ER), morning stiffness ≤30 min, crepitus, and in advanced cases weakness from combined articular and tendon dysfunction. Adhesive capsulitis would show GLOBAL, not directional, loss of motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Massive rotator cuff tear → superior humeral migration (\"high-riding humerus\" on X-ray) → cuff tear arthropathy (secondary GH OA). This is a tested progression. The subacromial space becomes so narrow it is obliterated. COMLEX: Rotator cuff tear arthropathy is managed with shoulder arthroplasty (reverse total shoulder arthroplasty) in surgical candidates — a COMLEX management escalation point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q19","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female physical therapist has 14 months of right shoulder pain worst with overhead activity and when sleeping on the right side. Pain refers to the lateral deltoid region. Abduction is limited to 110 degrees. Neer and Hawkins tests are both positive. Drop Arm Test is negative. MRI shows supraspinatus edema without tear. Which mechanism BEST explains her impingement?","choices":{"A":"The supraspinatus has undergone full-thickness articular-sided tearing, allowing the humeral head to migrate superiorly and mechanically narrow the subacromial space from the humeral side, consistent with the thinning mechanism of impingement","B":"The subdeltoid bursa has ruptured into the glenohumeral joint capsule, creating a reactive synovitis that directly compresses the supraspinatus tendon at the musculotendinous junction from within the intra-articular compartment","C":"The supraspinatus has calcified from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition in the critical zone, creating a fixed mechanical block in the subacromial space that prevents normal tendon gliding regardless of the inflammation state","D":"The supraspinatus has undergone chronic tendinosis with complete loss of inflammatory cells and collagen disorganization, causing tendon thinning and superior humeral migration that narrows the subacromial space from the humeral side as a separate process","E":"The supraspinatus tendon is acutely edematous and too large to glide smoothly through the subacromial space, representing the edematous-tendon mechanism of impingement rather than the degenerative thinning mechanism that allows superior humeral migration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course slides describe two impingement mechanisms: (1) Edematous supraspinatus — tendon becomes too large to glide through subacromial space → mechanical impingement from the tendon side; (2) Thin supraspinatus from chronic tendinosis — humerus shifts superiorly → space narrows from the humeral side. The negative Drop Arm Test and MRI showing edema without tear exclude a significant full-thickness tear. 14 months is chronic but patients cycle between states. The current state is edema-driven impingement (positive Neer/Hawkins, edema on MRI, no tear). The thinning/tendinosis mechanism would be the other part of the cycle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms of subacromial impingement — edema (tendon too big) vs. thinning (humerus migrates up). Both produce positive impingement tests. MRI differentiates edema (no tear) from tendinosis/tear. Drop Arm negative = no significant full-thickness tear. COMLEX: Impingement cycle (edema → thinning → re-inflammation) is the pathophysiologic rationale for PT, injections, and avoiding cortisone repeatedly (degrades collagen in already-diseased tendon).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q20","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male contractor has 3 weeks of acute right shoulder pain triggered by overhead work. Exam: positive Hawkins test, tenderness over the greater tuberosity, painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees of abduction. Radiograph shows superior humeral migration with subacromial space narrowing. Which imaging modality would BEST characterize the SOFT TISSUE pathology of his rotator cuff?","choices":{"A":"CT arthrogram of the right shoulder with intravenous contrast, which provides superior visualization of bony architecture and articular cartilage compared to MRI or ultrasound and is the preferred modality for subacromial space assessment","B":"Repeat plain radiographs of the right shoulder in three additional projections, since multiple-view radiography is the most sensitive modality for diagnosing partial-thickness rotator cuff tears and quantifying the degree of tendon involvement","C":"MRI or musculoskeletal ultrasound of the right shoulder, as both offer equivalent sensitivity for soft tissue pathology including rotator cuff tendinosis, partial-thickness tears, full-thickness tears, and subacromial bursitis","D":"Technetium bone scan of the right shoulder, which identifies areas of increased metabolic activity in the supraspinatus insertion and acromion corresponding to mechanical stress and is more sensitive than MRI for subacromial impingement","E":"Arthrographic fluoroscopy of the glenohumeral joint with contrast injection, which is the gold standard for detecting full-thickness rotator cuff tears by demonstrating contrast communication between the joint space and the subacromial bursa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Per the course slides: (1) Radiographs assess subacromial space dimension and acromion shape — good for bony architecture and space measurement. (2) MRI is sensitive and specific for soft tissue pathology, especially with contrast for cartilage and labral pathology; NOT as helpful for spacing/arthritis as radiographs. (3) Musculoskeletal ultrasound has EQUIVALENT sensitivity to MRI for soft tissue pathology. This equivalence is the high-yield teaching point — ultrasound is increasingly preferred in clinical practice for its availability, lower cost, and dynamic imaging capability. CT arthrogram and bone scans are not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for soft tissue shoulder pathology. MSK ultrasound = equivalent sensitivity. X-ray = bony architecture + subacromial space. Know when to use each. COMLEX: Musculoskeletal ultrasound is used by osteopathic sports medicine physicians for real-time guided injection procedures (subacromial, glenohumeral) — a COMLEX procedural competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q21","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old male has had rotator cuff tendinosis for 8 months. He has taken NSAIDs and completed 16 weeks of physical therapy without significant improvement. MRI shows a partial-thickness bursal-sided rotator cuff tear with intact articular surface. He continues to have significant pain with overhead activity. Which of the following represents the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection targeting the subacromial subdeltoid bursa to address the inflammatory component, which is the injection step in the rotator cuff treatment hierarchy after analgesics and physical therapy fail","B":"Immediate referral for arthroscopic surgical repair, since any bursal-sided partial-thickness tear is a mandatory indication for surgical intervention after 8 months of conservative management regardless of tear thickness or percentage","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint as the standard evidence-based second-line treatment after physical therapy failure, given its Level I evidence for partial-thickness rotator cuff tears in systematic reviews","D":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection targeting the joint space rather than the subacromial space, since the bursal-sided tear involves the bursal compartment which communicates directly with the GH joint interior","E":"MRI arthrography with gadolinium contrast to reclassify the tear size and compartment involvement before any further intervention, since precise tear classification is required to determine the surgical versus conservative management threshold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rotator cuff treatment hierarchy: analgesics → physical therapy (mainstay) → injections (subacromial corticosteroid for anti-inflammatory, OR regenerative options: prolotherapy/PRP/stem cells) → surgery (recalcitrant cases, full-thickness tears, massive tears). After 8 months of analgesics + PT failure, injections are next. For bursal-sided pathology, SUBACROMIAL injection directly targets the subacromial-subdeltoid bursa and the tendon surface. Surgery (arthroscopic repair) is indicated for FULL-THICKNESS and MASSIVE tears, not routinely for partial-thickness. PRP is a regenerative (pro-inflammatory) option, not the standard first injection choice, and goes into the subacromial space not the GH joint. The bursa does NOT communicate with the GH joint in a partial tear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Partial tear → conservative management including injection. Full or massive tear → surgery. The treatment escalation ladder is a classic board sequence. Bursal-sided tear = subacromial space injection. COMLEX: PRP and prolotherapy (pro-inflammatory, regenerative injections) are explicitly listed in the course slides as treatment options — COMLEX tests knowledge of regenerative injection indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q22","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypothyroidism presents with 9 months of progressively worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had significant pain that has partially resolved; she now has difficulty with forward flexion, abduction, internal rotation, and external rotation equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. Radiographs are unremarkable. Which mechanism best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Progressive supraspinatus tendinosis with eccentric overload producing bursal-sided tendon thinning and superior humeral migration, creating mechanical restriction of glenohumeral abduction and external rotation in a patient with diabetes-impaired tendon healing","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis accelerated by diabetes-related metabolic syndrome, with articular cartilage degradation producing directional loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation and progressive joint space narrowing visible on plain radiography","C":"Massive rotator cuff tear arthropathy with superior humeral migration, producing weakness on manual testing and combined articular and tendon dysfunction in a patient whose hypothyroidism predisposes to tendon collagen fragility and multidirectional tear extension","D":"Cervical spondylosis at C5-C6 producing referred shoulder stiffness with pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and sympathetically mediated capsular contracture in a patient with diabetic autonomic neuropathy affecting proprioceptive feedback","E":"Idiopathic adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) of poorly understood pathology, producing global loss of motion in all planes with no structural radiographic explanation, and strongly associated with diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease as metabolic risk factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Classic adhesive capsulitis: global loss of motion in ALL planes (forward flexion + abduction + IR + ER — this multiplanar global pattern is the key distinguishing feature), no structural radiographic explanation (radiographs NOT helpful for diagnosis), preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern (pain may lessen as stiffness progresses), peak mid-50s. Strong associations: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, autoimmune disease. Pathology is poorly understood (idiopathic). GH OA would show directional loss (worst abduction/ER) and likely radiographic changes. Rotator cuff tear arthropathy would show weakness and radiographic superior migration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = global motion loss in all planes + diabetes association + unremarkable imaging + peak mid-50s. Bilateral in 6-17%. Preserved strength distinguishes it from massive rotator cuff tears. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use glenohumeral articulation and muscle energy techniques to restore joint motion in adhesive capsulitis — OMM is a non-pharmacologic intervention that directly addresses the ROM loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q23","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man underwent open rotator cuff repair 4 months ago and was immobilized in a sling for 8 weeks per surgical protocol. He now presents with severe restriction of right shoulder motion in all planes equally. His surgeon confirms there are no hardware complications, no re-tear on ultrasound, and no incisional scar tissue limiting motion. What is the most likely diagnosis and most important treatment principle?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff re-tear at the surgical repair site causing protective guarding and apparent global motion limitation, requiring repeat MRI before initiating any physical therapy to confirm tendon integrity before applying passive mobilization forces","B":"Secondary adhesive capsulitis from prolonged post-operative immobilization, for which the paramount principle is restoring range of motion quickly with glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection plus physical therapy","C":"Post-operative hemarthrosis of the glenohumeral joint causing secondary capsular fibrosis from blood-derived fibrin deposition, requiring ultrasound-guided joint aspiration to remove residual hemarthrosis before physical therapy can be effective","D":"Complex regional pain syndrome type II developing after open shoulder surgery, producing diffuse allodynia, autonomic changes, and apparent motion limitation that requires a multidisciplinary pain management approach rather than joint mobilization","E":"Normal expected post-operative stiffness after open rotator cuff repair, which is expected to persist for 3 to 6 months after surgery and requires only reassurance, analgesics, and continuation of the sling for another 4 weeks before exercises begin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prolonged post-operative immobilization is an explicit secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis in the course slides. The hallmark remains global loss of motion in all planes with no other structural explanation. The cardinal treatment principle from the course slides is: \"It is paramount to restore ROM as soon as possible.\" First-line treatment: glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) + PT. If this fails: manipulation under anesthesia. This case illustrates an iatrogenic frozen shoulder from excessive immobilization after rotator cuff repair — early ROM protocols after shoulder surgery exist specifically to prevent this complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-op shoulder immobilization → frozen shoulder is a preventable surgical complication. Early ROM exercises after rotator cuff repair are the prevention strategy. Treatment is GH injection (intra-articular, NOT subacromial) + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if refractory. COMLEX: Osteopathic perioperative rehabilitation incorporates early OMM-guided joint mobilization to prevent post-surgical adhesive capsulitis, a COMLEX-relevant complication prevention concept.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q24","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has 11 months of left shoulder stiffness. Her primary care physician treated her with NSAIDs and a series of subacromial corticosteroid injections without improvement. Current exam shows globally restricted shoulder motion: forward flexion 80 degrees, external rotation 10 degrees, internal rotation to L4. Strength is intact. Which statement best identifies the error in prior management and the correct next step?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial injection series was underdosed — the correct next step is a higher-concentration subacromial triamcinolone injection (80 mg rather than 40 mg) targeting the same subacromial compartment with prolonged post-injection physical therapy","B":"Referral for total shoulder arthroplasty is indicated since she has failed one course of subacromial corticosteroid therapy, because capsular fibrosis in frozen shoulder is irreversible without surgical joint replacement in patients with 11 months of stiffness","C":"Long-term oral NSAID therapy combined with supervised home stretching is the correct next step, since this approach avoids the systemic effects of glucocorticoids and the procedural risks of manipulation under anesthesia in a middle-aged woman","D":"The subacromial injections were incorrectly targeted for a capsular (intra-articular) condition; the correct treatment is glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection plus physical therapy, then manipulation under anesthesia if it fails","E":"Initiation of biologic therapy with an interleukin-6 receptor antagonist targeting the fibroinflammatory capsular process, which has Level I evidence supporting its use in refractory idiopathic adhesive capsulitis before surgical intervention is attempted"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The critical teaching point: adhesive capsulitis is a CAPSULAR (intra-articular) condition — the contracted joint capsule is the pathological structure. The correct injection target is the GLENOHUMERAL joint space (intra-articular corticosteroid or oral glucocorticoids). Subacromial injection targets the subacromial bursa and rotator cuff — an entirely different compartment for a different pathology (rotator cuff impingement/tendinosis). The patient has never received the correct treatment. The correct sequence: GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. Total shoulder arthroplasty is for GH OA or cuff tear arthropathy, not adhesive capsulitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder injection = INTRA-ARTICULAR (GH joint). Rotator cuff injection = SUBACROMIAL. This is a high-yield board discriminator — wrong injection site is a common clinical error. COMLEX: OMM is an alternative to corticosteroid injection for early adhesive capsulitis — glenohumeral muscle energy and articulation techniques directly address the capsular restriction and are a COMLEX-preferred non-pharmacologic intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"Elbow, Wrist & Hand Pathologies (Obj 1-6)","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which muscle-tendon unit is most commonly injured in lateral epicondylitis?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi ulnaris at its origin on the posteromedial epicondyle of the humerus","B":"Flexor carpi radialis at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","C":"Common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","D":"Extensor pollicis longus at its origin on the radial shaft distal to the elbow","E":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis at its origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral epicondylitis involves the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB), which originates at the lateral epicondyle and is the most commonly affected tendon due to its eccentric loading during wrist extension and forearm activities. The extensor carpi ulnaris originates on the lateral epicondyle as well but is less commonly the primary lesion. The flexor carpi radialis and common flexor tendon originate at the medial epicondyle, placing them in the territory of medial epicondylitis. The extensor pollicis longus arises from the ulnar shaft in the third dorsal compartment, not the epicondyle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA 2025: lateral (tennis) elbow tendinopathy = repetitive wrist extension; the course slides name the ECRB as the most commonly injured tendon; expect a vignette with a tennis player or office worker with lateral elbow pain on wrist extension. COMLEX: Counterforce brace placement and the eccentric loading mechanism map directly to COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q02","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term correctly describes the pathological state of lateral epicondylitis that has become a chronic, degenerative condition?","choices":{"A":"Epicondylitis, characterized by acute neutrophilic infiltrate and classical inflammatory mediators within the ECRB tendon","B":"Epicondylosis, with degenerative angiofibroblastic change and disorganized collagen but no inflammatory cells","C":"Epicondyloma, characterized by fibroblastic hyperplasia with lymphocytic infiltration at the tendon-bone junction","D":"Enthesopathy, characterized by calcific deposits forming at the tendon-bone interface from chronic calcium influx","E":"Bursitis, characterized by inflammatory fluid accumulation within the lateral epicondylar bursa sac"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic lateral epicondylitis is more accurately termed epicondylosis (tendinosis), in which the dominant pathology is angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with disorganized collagen and neovascularization rather than classical acute inflammation. Epicondylitis (tendinitis) correctly applies only to the acute phase with neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate. Epicondyloma and enthesopathy are not standard diagnostic categories for this condition. Bursitis implies a distinct bursal structure, which is not the primary pathology in lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The -itis vs -osis distinction is a high-yield conceptual pair for all tendon overuse injuries; tendinosis involves degeneration rather than inflammation, which changes treatment from anti-inflammatory to regenerative approaches. COMLEX: Treatment algorithm differences between acute and chronic presentations (corticosteroids for acute, PRP/OT for chronic) align with COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q03","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with confirmed acute lateral epicondylitis fails a 3-week course of NSAIDs and rest. Which non-surgical intervention is most appropriate as the next step?","choices":{"A":"Surgical debridement and open release of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle given NSAID failure","B":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the ECRB tendon to stimulate regenerative tendon healing","C":"Corticosteroid injection at the lateral epicondyle with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy applied weekly over the common extensor origin","E":"Nitroglycerin patch applied daily over the lateral epicondyle to promote tendon repair"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After NSAID failure in acute lateral epicondylitis, corticosteroid injection combined with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace represents the appropriate next non-surgical step. Surgical debridement is reserved for chronic cases failing prolonged conservative management (course slide p5 lists percutaneous tendon procedures under chronic treatment). Platelet-rich plasma, nitroglycerin patches, and extracorporeal shock wave therapy are interventions for the chronic tendinosis phase, not the acute phase following NSAID failure. The counterforce brace (unloader strap) reduces the force transmitted through the ECRB origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral epicondylitis treatment follows an escalating stepwise algorithm; boards test recognition of when to escalate from NSAIDs to injection versus when surgery is appropriate. COMLEX: The distinction between acute and chronic treatment is explicitly tested, with PRP representing the regenerative approach to the degenerative chronic phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q04","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendon is primarily injured in medial epicondylitis, and where does it originate?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis originating at the lateral epicondyle of the humerus during backhand motions","B":"Common extensor tendon originating at the lateral epicondyle and stressed by repetitive wrist extension","C":"Biceps brachii tendon at its distal insertion on the radial tuberosity from repetitive forearm supination","D":"Triceps brachii tendon at its olecranon insertion stressed by repetitive elbow extension overhead","E":"Common flexor tendon originating at the medial epicondyle of the humerus, stressing the flexor-pronator mass"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medial epicondylitis, known as \"golfer's elbow,\" involves the common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus. The flexor-pronator mass includes the flexor carpi radialis, pronator teres, flexor carpi ulnaris, and palmaris longus. The ECRB at the lateral epicondyle is the target in lateral epicondylitis. The biceps tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity and is unrelated to epicondylitis. The triceps olecranon insertion is associated with olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy, not medial epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial vs lateral epicondylitis is a classic boards distinction; \"golfer's elbow\" = medial/common flexor, \"tennis elbow\" = lateral/ECRB. FA 2025: tennis elbow = repetitive wrist extension; golfer's elbow = repetitive wrist flexion, so the sport or activity is the contextual clue. COMLEX: The proximity of the ulnar nerve to the medial epicondyle is a testable complication, especially in overhead athletes with medial elbow symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q05","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which movement pattern most reliably provokes pain in medial epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Resisted wrist extension with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the ECRB tendon","B":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the common flexor origin","C":"Passive elbow flexion beyond 90 degrees with the wrist in neutral and forearm supinated maximally","D":"Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance with the elbow extended at the patient's side","E":"Maximum elbow flexion held for 60 seconds to reproduce ulnar nerve paresthesias at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow extended and forearm supinated directly loads the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, reproducing pain in medial epicondylitis. Resisted wrist extension loads the ECRB and provokes lateral epicondylitis (Cozen's test). Passive elbow flexion is used in the maximum elbow flexion test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance tests the rotator cuff. Maximum elbow flexion for 60 seconds evaluates the ulnar nerve, not the common flexor tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The examination finding of medial elbow pain with resisted wrist flexion versus resisted wrist extension reliably separates medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: The clinical presentation of medial elbow pain in a throwing or golf athlete is a prototypical COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q06","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pitcher with medial epicondylitis reports new ring and small finger tingling during overhead throwing. Which anatomical structure is most likely responsible for this additional symptom?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, which passes anterior to the lateral epicondyle and is stretched during elbow valgus stress","B":"Median nerve, which traverses the cubital fossa anterior to the medial epicondyle during overhead motion","C":"Posterior interosseous nerve, a radial nerve branch passing through the radial tunnel at the elbow","D":"Ulnar nerve, which courses posterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched during overhead throwing","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, which supplies the lateral forearm and is stretched during overhead abduction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ulnar nerve runs in a groove posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel. In overhead athletes with medial epicondylitis, the valgus stress and repetitive elbow flexion can compress or traction the ulnar nerve, producing ring and small finger paresthesias in addition to medial elbow pain. The radial nerve is lateral and not associated with medial elbow pathology. The median nerve does not course posterior to the medial epicondyle. The posterior interosseous and musculocutaneous nerves serve different anatomical regions not involving ring and small finger sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve vulnerability at the medial epicondyle is high-yield; a vignette combining medial elbow pain with ring/small finger tingling in a throwing athlete points to concomitant cubital tunnel involvement. COMLEX: The combination of medial epicondylitis and ulnar nerve compression at the same anatomical region is a classic COMLEX multiple-pathology clinical scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q07","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which provocative test is most specific for reproducing symptoms in cubital tunnel syndrome during the physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds to compress the carpal tunnel","B":"Maximum elbow flexion test, in which the patient holds the elbow in full flexion with wrist extension for 60 seconds","C":"Reverse Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full extension at 90 degrees for 60 seconds","D":"Cozen's test, in which the examiner resists wrist extension while the elbow is held in full extension","E":"Finkelstein maneuver, in which the patient grasps the thumb in the fist and the examiner ulnar deviates"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The maximum elbow flexion test, with the elbow in full flexion and wrist in extension held for 60 seconds, stretches and compresses the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel and is the most specific provocative test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Phalen's test evaluates the carpal tunnel for median nerve compression. Reverse Phalen's also assesses the carpal tunnel. Cozen's test loads the ECRB and is used for lateral epicondylitis. The Finkelstein maneuver specifically evaluates the first dorsal compartment tendons in De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cubital tunnel syndrome is tested as the 2nd most common upper extremity nerve entrapment; knowing the maximum elbow flexion test (analogous to Phalen's for CTS) is key for the exam. COMLEX: Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow and the maximum flexion test are both expected on COMLEX clinical examination questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q08","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cubital tunnel syndrome has weakness of grip and an inability to separate the fingers against resistance. Which finding correctly identifies the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Weakness of the ulnar-innervated interossei and hypothenar muscles, causing loss of finger abduction and adduction","B":"Weakness of the thenar muscles innervated by the median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and palmar abduction","C":"Weakness of the extensor digitorum communis innervated by the radial nerve at the posterior interosseous branch","D":"Weakness of the flexor carpi radialis innervated by the median nerve proximal to the wrist at the forearm","E":"Weakness of the brachioradialis innervated by the radial nerve in the lateral compartment of the arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ulnar nerve innervates the intrinsic hand muscles including the interossei (which abduct and adduct the fingers), the hypothenar muscles (abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi, opponens digiti minimi), and the medial two lumbricals. Weakness of these structures produces loss of finger abduction and adduction, and contributes to claw hand deformity. The thenar muscles are median nerve territory. Extensor digitorum communis is radial nerve territory. Flexor carpi radialis and brachioradialis are median and radial nerve territories, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve motor deficit (interossei weakness, claw hand) versus median nerve deficit (thenar wasting, loss of thumb opposition) is a classic boards distinction. COMLEX: The claw hand deformity specifically affects the ring and small fingers due to loss of ulnar intrinsic muscles and is a named COMLEX musculoskeletal physical finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q09","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which initial conservative management is most appropriate for a patient with mild cubital tunnel syndrome and no intrinsic (interosseous or hypothenar) wasting or weakness?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve anterior regardless of severity or conservative trial","B":"Immobilization in a full arm cast at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce nerve traction","C":"Activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion plus a padded elbow brace to limit cubital tunnel compression","D":"Immediate NCS/EMG testing followed by percutaneous cubital tunnel release regardless of symptom severity","E":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel adjacent to the ulnar nerve at the medial elbow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild cubital tunnel syndrome without motor deficits is initially managed conservatively with activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion, padding of the elbow, and bracing that limits full flexion. Surgical ulnar nerve transposition is reserved for cases failing conservative management or with significant motor deficits. A full arm cast in flexion would worsen cubital tunnel compression, not relieve it. Immediate surgery before a conservative trial is not indicated for mild cases. Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel risks nerve injury and is not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The boards test the step-wise approach to cubital tunnel syndrome: conservative first (activity mod, brace), then electrodiagnostics if uncertain, then surgery for failures or motor deficits. COMLEX: Padding and ergonomic modification align with COMLEX's emphasis on functional and non-pharmacological interventions before procedural management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q10","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In carpal tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed, and through which anatomical structure does the compression occur?","choices":{"A":"Ulnar nerve, compressed between the pisiform and hook of hamate within Guyon's canal at the volar wrist","B":"Radial nerve, compressed beneath the first dorsal compartment retinaculum at the radial styloid","C":"Median nerve, compressed between the two heads of pronator teres muscle in the proximal forearm","D":"Ulnar nerve, compressed under the flexor carpi ulnaris at the distal two-thirds of the forearm","E":"Median nerve, compressed under the transverse carpal ligament at the roof of the carpal canal of the wrist"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involves compression of the median nerve under the transverse carpal ligament, which forms the roof of the carpal canal at the wrist. Any condition that increases pressure within the canal can compress the median nerve. The ulnar nerve is compressed in Guyon's canal between the pisiform and hamate. Radial nerve compression under the first dorsal compartment retinaculum is not a recognized entrapment syndrome. Median nerve compression between pronator teres heads is pronator teres syndrome, a separate condition. Flexor carpi ulnaris compression of the ulnar nerve is a distinct entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS is the highest-yield nerve entrapment syndrome on Step 1; FA 2025 emphasizes thenar eminence wasting (APB weakness) from chronic median nerve compression as the classic motor finding. COMLEX: CTS is a named COMLEX CP8 condition; wrist OMM and somatic dysfunction at C5-T1 contributing to median nerve tension are COMLEX-specific clinical angles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q11","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with suspected carpal tunnel syndrome undergoes provocative testing. Which combination of physical examination tests is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Maximum elbow flexion test combined with Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow cubital tunnel","B":"Finkelstein maneuver combined with tenderness palpation over the first dorsal compartment radial styloid","C":"Cozen's test combined with passive wrist extension and resisted forearm supination at the elbow","D":"Phalen's sign combined with Tinel's sign at the volar wrist, both targeting the median nerve distribution","E":"Drop arm test combined with Neer's and Hawkins-Kennedy impingement signs at the shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phalen's sign (wrist flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds reproducing median distribution paresthesias) and Tinel's sign at the volar wrist (percussion over the carpal tunnel) are the two classic provocative tests for CTS. The maximum elbow flexion test and cubital tunnel Tinel's are for cubital tunnel syndrome. The Finkelstein maneuver with first dorsal compartment tenderness evaluates De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Cozen's test and wrist extension/forearm supination combination targets lateral epicondylitis. The drop arm test and shoulder impingement signs evaluate rotator cuff and subacromial pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phalen's test is more sensitive and Tinel's more specific for CTS; boards may ask which is more sensitive or appropriate as a first test. COMLEX: Both tests are expected clinical knowledge for COMLEX, with the additional consideration of NCS/EMG for confirmation when clinical findings are equivocal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q12","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic pattern is most consistent with the epidemiology of carpal tunnel syndrome in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Female-predominant, with an incidence approximately three times higher in women than in men across all age groups","B":"Male-predominant, with a 2:1 male-to-female ratio most pronounced in adults over 60 who perform heavy manual labor","C":"Equal sex distribution, with rates driven entirely by occupation regardless of biological sex","D":"Pediatric-predominant, with most cases arising in adolescents during periods of rapid skeletal growth","E":"Elderly-predominant, with first presentation occurring typically after age 70 in both males and females"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is 3 times more common in females than males, with female incidence reported at 2.2-5.4 per 1,000 persons per year versus 1.1-3 per 1,000 for males. CTS affects 1-5% of the general population and is not pediatric-predominant or elderly-specific in onset. While occupational exposure increases risk, sex remains a significant independent risk factor likely related to hormonal influences and anatomical differences in carpal canal dimensions. The condition is not equally distributed across sexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS demographics are testable; women 3:1 over men, peak incidence in working-age adults. FA 2025 lists pregnancy as a major risk factor due to edema, which connects CTS to the obstetrics section. COMLEX: Postpartum and pregnancy-related CTS are classically tested COMLEX clinical presentations connecting OB and musculoskeletal pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q13","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomical boundaries correctly define Guyon's canal, the site of ulnar nerve compression at the wrist?","choices":{"A":"Bounded by the flexor carpi radialis medially and the median nerve laterally at the proximal carpal row","B":"Bounded by the extensor carpi ulnaris dorsally and flexor carpi ulnaris volarly at the ulnar wrist","C":"Bounded by the pisiform medially, the hook of hamate laterally, and the palmar carpal ligament above","D":"Bounded by the transverse carpal ligament roofing the carpal canal and the carpal bones at the floor","E":"Bounded by the first dorsal compartment laterally and the radial styloid medially at the radial wrist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyon's canal is a fibro-osseous tunnel at the ulnar wrist bounded by the pisiform bone on the medial side, the hook of the hamate on the lateral side, and the palmar carpal ligament forming the roof. The ulnar nerve and artery pass through this tunnel into the hand. This is distinct from the carpal tunnel (transverse carpal ligament roof, carpal bones floor), the radial styloid region (De Quervain's), and the extensor compartments on the dorsum of the wrist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyon's canal anatomy is tested in comparison to the carpal tunnel; knowing the pisiform (medial) and hook of hamate (lateral) as the side walls is key. COMLEX: Cyclists with hypothenar wasting from handlebar pressure on Guyon's canal is a classic COMLEX clinical vignette linking biomechanics to peripheral nerve entrapment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q14","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which occupational or recreational activity is classically associated with ulnar nerve entrapment at Guyon's canal?","choices":{"A":"Overhead throwing motions that repetitively stress the medial elbow flexor-pronator mass with valgus force","B":"Repetitive wrist extension activities such as prolonged keyboard typing producing carpal tunnel compression","C":"Postpartum infant care with repeated pinch-grip lifting creating first dorsal compartment friction stress","D":"Prolonged handlebar pressure during cycling that compresses the ulnar nerve at the hypothenar eminence","E":"Backhand tennis strokes that repetitively load the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle with wrist extension"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cyclists applying prolonged pressure on handlebars directly compress the hypothenar eminence over Guyon's canal, making this the classic occupational/recreational association. The resulting ulnar neuropathy is sometimes called \"cyclist's palsy\" or \"handlebar palsy.\" Overhead throwing stresses the medial elbow and ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel, not Guyon's canal. Keyboard typing is associated with carpal tunnel syndrome. Postpartum lifting is associated with De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Tennis backhand strokes cause lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cycling-Guyon's canal association is a classic boards vignette; the key distinguishing feature from cubital tunnel is preserved dorsal ulnar sensation since the dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch exits proximal to Guyon's canal. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling and padded gloves as management represent COMLEX's non-pharmacological first-line approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q15","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with Guyon's canal syndrome has normal sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand. How does this finding help distinguish Guyon's canal from cubital tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch joins the median nerve proximally, so it is never affected by ulnar compression","B":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch leaves the ulnar nerve proximal to Guyon's canal, so wrist compression spares it","C":"Guyon's canal compression always affects dorsal sensation because the branch enters the canal distal to the pisiform","D":"Cubital tunnel syndrome selectively spares the dorsal ulnar hand because it only involves the terminal palmar branch","E":"Loss of dorsal ulnar sensation is pathognomonic of Guyon's canal and cannot occur with cubital tunnel compression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve arises from the ulnar nerve in the distal half of the forearm (Moore Ch 3), well before Guyon's canal. This means that compression within Guyon's canal at the wrist will spare this branch, leaving dorsal ulnar hand sensation intact. In contrast, cubital tunnel syndrome at the elbow occurs proximal to this branch point, so dorsal ulnar hand sensation IS affected. The preserved dorsal ulnar sensation in a patient with hypothenar weakness and ring/small finger palmar paresthesias helps localize the lesion to the wrist rather than the elbow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The preserved dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch in Guyon's canal compression is the classic localizing feature that distinguishes it from cubital tunnel syndrome. COMLEX: Localization of nerve lesions by sensory territory is a core COMLEX neurological examination skill applied to upper extremity entrapment syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q16","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendons are specifically affected in De Quervain's tenosynovitis, and in which anatomical compartment do they reside?","choices":{"A":"Flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus within the carpal canal at the volar wrist crease","B":"Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis in the third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle","C":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid","D":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis in the second dorsal compartment of the wrist","E":"Flexor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis within the carpal tunnel at the thenar eminence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis affects the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendons, which share the first dorsal compartment of the wrist at the level of the radial styloid. Repetitive or overuse activities cause friction-based inflammation within this fibro-osseous tunnel. The flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus are volar structures in the carpal canal. The third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle contains the extensor pollicis longus. The second dorsal compartment contains the ECR longus and brevis. Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar intrinsic muscle, not a dorsal compartment structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: De Quervain's is a classic FA 2025 vignette: APL and EPB in the first dorsal compartment, Finkelstein test, and postpartum women. COMLEX: The first dorsal compartment as a fibro-osseous tunnel fits COMLEX's anatomical entrapment framework; somatic dysfunction at the wrist affecting the radial styloid region is a COMLEX-specific management angle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q17","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination maneuver is diagnostic for De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test: the patient holds both wrists in full flexion at 90 degrees sustained for 60 seconds","B":"Finkelstein maneuver: the patient makes a fist over the thumb and the examiner ulnar-deviates the wrist","C":"Maximum elbow flexion test: the patient holds the elbow in full flexion for 60 seconds with wrist extended","D":"Cozen's test: the examiner resists wrist extension while the patient holds the elbow fully extended","E":"Tinel's sign: the examiner percusses over the volar wrist crease to reproduce thumb paresthesias"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Finkelstein maneuver is performed by having the patient place the thumb across the palm, flex the fingers over the thumb, and then the examiner passively ulnar-deviates the wrist. This stretches the APL and EPB tendons over the radial styloid and reproduces the patient's pain, confirming De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Phalen's test evaluates carpal tunnel syndrome. The maximum elbow flexion test evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Cozen's test evaluates lateral epicondylitis. Tinel's sign at the wrist evaluates for carpal tunnel syndrome, not De Quervain's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Finkelstein maneuver is high-yield for De Quervain's; the course mechanics are thumb in palm, then ulnar deviation reproduces radial wrist pain (FA 2025: pain at the radial styloid with stretch of the thumb tendons). COMLEX: Boards expect you to distinguish Finkelstein (De Quervain's) from Phalen's (CTS) from the maximum flexion test (cubital tunnel) as three different conditions with three different diagnostic maneuvers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q18","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic group has the highest epidemiological risk for developing De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Young male contact sport athletes aged 18 to 30 with high-velocity wrist flexion demands from sport","B":"Elderly men over age 65 performing heavy manual labor with repetitive gripping and lifting activities","C":"Adolescent females during growth spurts who have increased susceptibility to wrist tendon overuse","D":"Postmenopausal women over age 65 who develop concurrent wrist and hand osteoarthritis with synovitis","E":"Women aged 30 to 50, with a notably elevated risk specifically during the 4 to 6 weeks following childbirth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis predominantly affects women aged 30-50, with a particularly high incidence in the 4-6 weeks postpartum period. The combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist ulnar deviation), and breastfeeding-related wrist positions creates significant stress on the first dorsal compartment tendons. Young male athletes, elderly men, adolescent females, and postmenopausal women with OA are not the primary demographic for this condition, though any repetitive radial wrist deviation activity can trigger it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum woman with radial wrist pain and positive Finkelstein is a classic Step 1 clinical vignette connecting obstetrics to musculoskeletal pathology. COMLEX: COMLEX tests the management (thumb spica bracing, corticosteroid injection) and the postpartum timing as a clinical trigger for the condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q19","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman and recreational tennis player has an 8-week history of right lateral elbow pain worsened by gripping and backhand strokes. No prior trauma or neurological symptoms. Exam: point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle, pain with resisted wrist extension. X-ray is normal. Which of the following most accurately describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Acute neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate invading the ECRB tendon and surrounding periosteal tissue","B":"Angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with degeneration and disorganized collagen within the ECRB tendon origin","C":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystal deposition within the common extensor tendon producing calcific tendinitis","D":"Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia of the common extensor tendon from repetitive valgus stress at the elbow","E":"Synovial hypertrophy with pannus formation eroding the articular cartilage of the radiocapitellar joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With persistent symptoms, this presentation reflects the transition from the acute tendinitis phase into chronic tendinosis, characterized by angiofibroblastic hyperplasia, disorganized collagen, and neovascularization rather than classic acute neutrophilic inflammation. This histopathological distinction has treatment implications: anti-inflammatory approaches are less effective in the chronic phase compared with regenerative or eccentric loading approaches. Calcific tendinitis involves hydroxyapatite deposits and presents distinctly on imaging. Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia and synovial pannus are not characteristic of lateral epicondylitis histopathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The chronicity of lateral epicondylitis shifts histopathology from neutrophilic inflammation to angiofibroblastic degeneration; this distinction drives the treatment shift from NSAIDs to OT and regenerative injections. COMLEX: The chronic versus acute management algorithm is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q20","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male electrician has 4 months of left lateral elbow pain. He completed 6 weeks of NSAIDs and activity modification without adequate improvement. No weakness or neurological deficits. Exam confirms lateral epicondylitis. Which intervention is most appropriate as the next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening and a counterforce forearm brace for chronic tendinosis","B":"Immediate surgical ECRB tendon debridement given failure of initial conservative pharmaceutical management","C":"Full elbow immobilization in a long arm cast for 6 weeks to allow ECRB tendon collagen remodeling","D":"Monthly corticosteroid injection repeated for 3 consecutive months regardless of initial injection response","E":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy combined with ultrasound-guided open surgical release of the tendon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After NSAIDs and activity modification fail, occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening and a counterforce brace is the appropriate next step for chronic lateral epicondylitis. Eccentric loading programs are strongly supported for chronic tendinosis by promoting controlled collagen remodeling. Surgical debridement is not indicated this early; it is reserved for prolonged failure of conservative care. Full elbow casting is counterproductive and may promote stiffness. Repeated corticosteroid injections are not recommended as they may further weaken the tendon. ESWT is not combined with surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boards test the stepwise treatment ladder for lateral epicondylitis; OT with eccentric loading is the key second-line intervention after NSAIDs in the chronic phase. COMLEX: The counterforce brace and occupational therapy are considered conservative non-pharmacological interventions preferred by COMLEX before escalating to injection or surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q21","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female chef has bilateral lateral elbow pain for 3 months, worse on the dominant right side. She reports pain with repetitive gripping and forearm activities during work. Exam shows point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle bilaterally, with a positive Cozen's test. X-ray is normal. Which of the following best explains why her occupation exposes her to this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive wrist flexion generates excessive valgus stress at the medial epicondyle during food preparation","B":"Overhead lifting generates axial loading that compresses the radiocapitellar articular cartilage directly","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading of the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle during gripping causes tendon microtrauma","D":"Sustained elbow flexion during preparation work compresses the ulnar nerve at the posteromedial elbow","E":"Repetitive forearm supination creates traction stress at the biceps tendon insertion on the radial tuberosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repetitive eccentric loading during gripping and forearm activities generates tensile stress at the ECRB origin on the lateral epicondyle, causing cumulative microtrauma that over time produces tendinosis. Chefs perform constant gripping, lifting, and cutting motions that repeatedly load this tendon eccentrically. Wrist flexion and medial epicondyle stress describes medial epicondylitis. Radiocapitellar compression suggests intra-articular pathology. Sustained elbow flexion and ulnar nerve compression describes cubital tunnel syndrome. Forearm supination and biceps traction describe a separate entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding the biomechanical mechanism of eccentric loading at the ECRB origin is fundamental to explaining lateral epicondylitis in any occupation, not just tennis. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling about grip technique and tool modification is a COMLEX-favored management component for occupational lateral epicondylitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q22","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old amateur golfer presents with a 6-week history of right medial elbow pain that worsens progressively during his round. No numbness or tingling. Exam shows tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation. Grip strength is mildly reduced. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Medial epicondylitis from overuse degeneration of the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle","B":"Lateral epicondylitis caused by eccentric overuse of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle","C":"Cubital tunnel syndrome caused by ulnar nerve compression at the posteromedial elbow during swinging","D":"UCL sprain caused by repetitive valgus stress on the ulnar collateral ligament of the medial elbow","E":"Pronator teres syndrome caused by median nerve compression between the two heads of pronator teres"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The combination of medial epicondyle tenderness, pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation, and a golf history establishes medial epicondylitis. The flexor-pronator mass generates high valgus stress during the golf swing, causing eccentric overload at the medial epicondyle origin. Lateral epicondylitis presents with lateral tenderness and pain on wrist extension, not flexion. Cubital tunnel syndrome would include ring/small finger paresthesias. UCL injury presents with valgus instability rather than resisted wrist flexion pain. Pronator teres syndrome includes median nerve distribution paresthesias in the forearm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Golfer's elbow\" = medial epicondylitis = common flexor tendon at medial epicondyle; pain with resisted wrist flexion is the key differentiating exam finding from lateral epicondylitis. COMLEX: The medial elbow anatomy including ulnar nerve proximity is a testable COMLEX clinical pearl within the medial epicondylitis vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q23","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 51-year-old office worker has had medial elbow pain for 10 months. An acute-phase course of NSAIDs, an unloader strap, and occupational therapy helped only briefly. Pain is reproduced with resisted wrist flexion and pronation, and there are no ulnar nerve symptoms. Ultrasound shows degeneration of the common flexor tendon without a tear. Which option does the course list for this chronic stage?","choices":{"A":"A series of three corticosteroid injections a month apart, since injections are the chronic-stage option for tendinosis","B":"Anterior transposition of the ulnar nerve, because chronic medial elbow pain reflects traction on the nerve behind the epicondyle","C":"Regenerative injection or a percutaneous tendon procedure, which the course lists with nitroglycerin patches for chronic disease","D":"Rigid long arm cast immobilization for 6 weeks, because complete rest lets the degenerated tendon remodel back to normal","E":"Open release of the transverse carpal ligament, because flexor tendon overload at the elbow begins with wrist compression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides epicondylitis treatment by stage. Acute (itis) care uses NSAIDs, corticosteroid injection, an unloader strap, and occupational therapy. Chronic (osis) disease is degenerative, and the listed options are occupational therapy, nitroglycerin patches, regenerative injections, and percutaneous tendon procedures. The proposed pathology is eccentric overload without matching muscular endurance. Corticosteroid injection is an acute-phase option, and repeated injections do not treat degeneration. Ulnar nerve transposition is a cubital tunnel procedure, and this patient has no nerve symptoms. Rigid casting is not a listed treatment and causes stiffness. Carpal tunnel release treats median nerve compression at the wrist, not the common flexor origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) = common flexor-pronator origin; lateral (tennis elbow) = ECRB. Acute = inflammation (itis); chronic = degeneration (osis), no inflammatory cells. COMLEX: Check the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle in every medial elbow pain patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — eccentric exercise for tendinopathy rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl14_q24","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male pitcher with chronic medial elbow pain has pain with wrist flexion and forearm pronation but no pain with wrist extension. X-ray shows no fracture or valgus instability. Which of the following most reliably distinguishes medial from lateral epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Pain with passive elbow flexion above 90 degrees reliably distinguishes medial epicondylitis from lateral epicondylitis","B":"Tenderness directly over the olecranon process rather than the epicondyle is pathognomonic for medial pathology","C":"The presence of neurological symptoms is required to distinguish medial from lateral epicondylitis clinically","D":"Pain with resisted wrist flexion and pronation marks medial epicondylitis; resisted extension pain marks lateral","E":"Bilateral symptom involvement is characteristic of medial epicondylitis and absent in lateral epicondylitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key examination distinction is the movement that reproduces pain: resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation provoke medial epicondylitis by loading the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, while resisted wrist extension (Cozen's test) provokes lateral epicondylitis by loading the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle. Passive elbow flexion evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Olecranon tenderness suggests olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy. Neurological symptoms may coexist but are not required for the distinction. Both conditions can be bilateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The resisted wrist flexion vs extension distinction is the single most important physical exam finding to separate medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: Boards expect efficient clinical differentiation using one or two targeted examination maneuvers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q25","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old long-haul truck driver presents with 4 months of right ring and small finger numbness and tingling that is worst during long drives. He holds the wheel with his right elbow flexed for hours. Exam: positive Tinel's sign at the right posteromedial elbow, symptoms reproduced with maximum elbow flexion test, mildly reduced grip strength. Which of the following most likely explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the wrist","B":"Lateral epicondylitis from ECRB overuse at the lateral epicondyle during steering wheel gripping","C":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis from APL and EPB tendon inflammation at the first dorsal compartment","D":"Guyon's canal syndrome from ulnar nerve compression at the wrist between the pisiform and hamate","E":"Cubital tunnel syndrome from ulnar compression at the elbow due to sustained flexion while driving"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sustained elbow flexion while driving compresses and stretches the ulnar nerve within the cubital tunnel, producing classic cubital tunnel syndrome. Ring and small finger distribution paresthesias, positive Tinel's at the posteromedial elbow, and reproduction of symptoms with the maximum elbow flexion test all confirm the diagnosis. Carpal tunnel syndrome involves median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle) and positive Phalen's/Tinel's at the wrist. Lateral epicondylitis and De Quervain's are tendon pathologies without neurological symptoms. Guyon's canal syndrome would be positive at the wrist, not the elbow, and preserves dorsal ulnar sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolonged elbow flexion as the mechanism for cubital tunnel is a classic board vignette; the driving truck driver or the reader who leans on the elbow are prototypical presentations. COMLEX: The Tinel's sign location (posteromedial elbow vs volar wrist) differentiates cubital tunnel from CTS and is a COMLEX examination must-know.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q26","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male programmer develops cubital tunnel syndrome confirmed by NCS/EMG. After 3 months of activity modification, elbow padding, and NSAIDs, symptoms have not improved and NCS shows progressive axonal changes. Which surgical intervention is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel release by dividing the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist to decompress the median nerve","B":"Lateral epicondyle ECRB debridement with tendon release to address the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle","C":"First dorsal compartment release by opening the fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid of the wrist","D":"Ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve to an anterior position at the elbow, relieving tension and compression","E":"Total elbow arthroplasty to decompress the cubital tunnel by replacing the elbow joint articular surfaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ulnar nerve transposition surgically moves the ulnar nerve from its posterior position behind the medial epicondyle to an anterior location (subcutaneous, intramuscular, or submuscular), shortening the path the nerve must travel and relieving tension and compression during elbow flexion. This is the appropriate surgical intervention for cubital tunnel syndrome failing conservative management with progressive NCS changes. Carpal tunnel release is for CTS, not cubital tunnel. ECRB debridement is for lateral epicondylitis. First dorsal compartment release is for De Quervain's. Total elbow arthroplasty is for end-stage arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve transposition is the definitive surgical procedure for refractory cubital tunnel syndrome. COMLEX: Boards expect knowledge of when conservative management has failed and surgery is warranted based on NCS evidence of axonal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q27","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with cubital tunnel syndrome has a classic claw-hand deformity involving the ring and small fingers at rest, along with inability to spread her fingers apart. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the finger deformity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of ulnar innervation to the interossei and ulnar lumbricals leaves ring and small finger MCP extension unopposed","B":"Loss of median nerve innervation to the flexor digitorum superficialis causes weakness of ring and small finger flexion","C":"Loss of radial nerve innervation to the extensor digitorum communis causes inability to extend the ring and small fingers","D":"Loss of musculocutaneous nerve innervation to the biceps causes loss of forearm flexion and supination strength","E":"Compression of the anterior interosseous nerve causes isolated weakness of the flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The claw-hand deformity in ulnar nerve palsy results from loss of the interossei and ulnar lumbricals, which normally flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints. Without these muscles, the extrinsic extensors (radial nerve) produce unopposed MCP extension, while the extrinsic flexors (FDP) produce IP flexion, creating the claw posture. The ring and small fingers are most affected because the median-innervated lumbricals maintain normal posture in the index and middle fingers. Median nerve loss affects different muscles. Radial nerve loss produces wrist drop, not claw hand. Musculocutaneous and AIN losses produce entirely different deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The intrinsic minus hand, claw hand, and the ulnar nerve innervation pattern (interossei, hypothenar, medial two lumbricals) is very high-yield; FA 2025 has a detailed nerve injury table for upper extremity entrapments. COMLEX: The functional consequences of ulnar nerve injury at the elbow are expected COMLEX clinical knowledge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q28","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman in her third trimester of pregnancy presents with bilateral hand numbness and tingling worse at night, predominantly in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Phalen's test is positive bilaterally at 30 seconds. Which mechanism best explains why pregnancy predisposes to carpal tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Hormonal changes cause direct peripheral axonal degeneration of the median nerve throughout the carpal tunnel","B":"Uterine enlargement displaces the brachial plexus superiorly and compresses the median nerve proximally","C":"Elevated estrogen produces direct cytotoxic injury to the transverse carpal ligament and canal walls","D":"Cervical disc herniation at C6 compresses the root whose sensory distribution overlaps the median nerve","E":"Edema from fluid retention raises carpal canal pressure, compressing the median nerve beneath the ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-related fluid retention and edema increase the pressure within the rigid carpal canal, compressing the median nerve against the transverse carpal ligament. This is the primary mechanism for the high prevalence of CTS in the third trimester and early postpartum period. The symptoms are typically bilateral and often resolve after delivery as edema subsides. Hormonal changes do not cause direct axonal degeneration. Uterine size does not affect the brachial plexus. Estrogen is not directly cytotoxic to the transverse carpal ligament. Cervical radiculopathy at C6 is a separate condition with a different clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy-associated CTS due to edema is a high-yield board pearl connecting obstetrics and musculoskeletal pathology. FA 2025 lists pregnancy (due to edema) among CTS associations. COMLEX: Postpartum management (bracing, reassurance, expected resolution) is testable on COMLEX for pregnancy-related CTS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q29","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old female secretary has a 6-month history of right thumb, index, and middle finger paresthesias that wake her at night. She has tried wrist splinting with partial relief. Exam shows right thenar muscle wasting and positive Phalen's and Tinel's signs. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"An additional 3-month trial of nighttime splinting with strict activity modification before considering any procedure","B":"Referral for carpal tunnel release to decompress the median nerve before further irreversible motor function is lost","C":"Oral prednisone taper for 2 weeks to reduce intracanal inflammation and relieve nerve compression","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the carpal tunnel as definitive treatment to avoid the need for surgical release","E":"NCS/EMG testing only, with no further treatment until electrodiagnostic severity grading is obtained"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thenar muscle wasting indicates chronic severe median nerve compression with motor axonal loss. At this stage, further delay risks irreversible motor deficit; surgical carpal tunnel release (division of the transverse carpal ligament) is the most appropriate intervention to decompress the nerve and halt progression. Additional conservative management trials are not appropriate given objective motor deficits. Oral prednisone is not indicated for CTS. Corticosteroid injection may provide temporary relief but is not definitive treatment when thenar atrophy is present. NCS/EMG is useful for confirmation but should not delay surgery when clinical findings already show motor involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thenar wasting from CTS is the signal that surgery is urgently needed; boards test the threshold between conservative management and surgical referral, with motor deficit being the key trigger. COMLEX: Timely recognition of motor involvement and referral timing is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q30","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old male presents 3 days after a displaced distal radius fracture with acute onset of severe median nerve distribution paresthesias and thumb weakness. He had no prior symptoms. X-ray confirms the fracture. Which of the following most likely explains the acute neurological findings?","choices":{"A":"Median nerve laceration from the sharp fracture fragment directly severing the nerve at the fracture site","B":"Brachial plexus traction injury at C6 and C7 nerve roots from the force of impact during the fall","C":"Radial nerve palsy at the anatomical spiral groove of the humerus from the fracture mechanism","D":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome from fracture edema and hematoma raising pressure within the carpal canal","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve compression causing isolated flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome is a well-recognized complication of distal radius fractures, caused by post-fracture edema, hematoma, and fragment displacement acutely increasing pressure within the rigid carpal canal. This compresses the median nerve and produces rapid-onset sensory and motor symptoms. A direct nerve laceration is possible but less common than compression. Brachial plexus injury would produce a much more widespread deficit. Radial nerve palsy at the spiral groove produces wrist drop, not thumb weakness or palmar paresthesias. Anterior interosseous syndrome causes isolated deep flexor weakness without sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal radius fractures causing acute CTS is high-yield for boards; increased canal pressure from fracture hematoma can require emergent carpal tunnel release. COMLEX: Recognizing acute compartment syndrome or acute CTS as a fracture complication requiring urgent intervention is a COMLEX emergency management question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q31","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two patients have ulnar neuropathy. One has compression at Guyon's canal from a hook of hamate fracture; the other has a lesion at the elbow above the branches to the forearm flexors. On attempted finger extension, the clawing of the ring and small fingers is clearly more pronounced in the patient with the wrist lesion. Which explanation best accounts for this difference?","choices":{"A":"The wrist lesion also compresses the median nerve in the carpal tunnel, adding index and middle finger lumbrical loss to the claw","B":"The elbow lesion spares the interossei, which are supplied by a branch that leaves the ulnar nerve above the elbow","C":"With the wrist lesion, the ulnar half of flexor digitorum profundus still works and exaggerates flexion at the IP joints","D":"The wrist lesion denervates the extensor digitorum, so the fingers cannot extend at the MCP joints and stay flexed","E":"The elbow lesion paralyzes the hypothenar muscles, whose loss pulls the fingers straight and cancels the claw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid describes the ulnar claw as best seen with distal lesions: loss of the ulnar lumbricals and interossei lets the fingers extend at the MCP joints, and the remaining extrinsic digital flexors exaggerate flexion at the PIP and DIP joints. With a Guyon canal lesion, the ulnar half of FDP is still innervated, so clawing is marked; a proximal lesion also paralyzes the ulnar FDP, so the deficit is less pronounced. First Aid also lists hook of hamate fracture as a cause of Guyon canal syndrome. A Guyon canal lesion does not compress the median nerve. The interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve in the hand, not by a branch above the elbow. The extensor digitorum is radial (posterior interosseous) nerve territory. Hypothenar paralysis occurs with both lesions and does not cancel clawing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar claw = distal ulnar lesion (FDP to digits 4–5 intact); proximal lesion → less clawing plus radial deviation of the wrist on flexion (FCU loss). Guyon canal: cyclists' handlebar pressure, hook of hamate fracture. COMLEX: Dorsal ulnar sensation is spared in Guyon canal lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — cubital tunnel syndrome at the elbow"},{"id":"sdl14_q32","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old female secretary presents with right hypothenar muscle wasting and weakness of finger abduction without any sensory loss in the hand. Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel is negative. Which statement best explains why this presentation is consistent with a distal Guyon's canal lesion?","choices":{"A":"Guyon's canal compression always produces both motor and sensory deficits in all patients with this condition","B":"A proximal lesion at Guyon's canal selectively damages motor fibers before sensory fibers in all cases","C":"Sensory fibers in the ulnar nerve always recover faster than motor fibers following any peripheral nerve injury","D":"The proximal ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal carries only sensory information and no motor components at all","E":"A distal Guyon's canal lesion can affect only the deep motor branch of the ulnar nerve, sparing sensation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Within Guyon's canal, the ulnar nerve divides into a superficial sensory branch and a deep motor branch. A lesion at the distal aspect of Guyon's canal, or at the level of the hamate hook, can selectively affect the deep motor branch while sparing the superficial sensory branch, producing pure motor deficits without sensory loss. This is one of the localizing features that distinguishes distal from proximal ulnar nerve lesions. Guyon's canal compression does not always produce combined motor and sensory deficits; the anatomy of the bifurcation within the canal determines the clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure motor ulnar hand weakness with preserved sensation localizes a lesion to the deep branch of the ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal, a testable anatomical distinction. COMLEX: Sensory vs motor branch involvement within Guyon's canal is a clinically relevant distinction for treatment decision-making.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q33","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with confirmed Guyon's canal syndrome fails 4 months of conservative management. MSK ultrasound reveals a ganglion cyst compressing the ulnar nerve within the canal. Which next step in management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Surgical exploration and decompression of Guyon's canal with excision of the compressing ganglion cyst","B":"Repeat corticosteroid injection at 4-week intervals for a total of 3 injections before considering any surgery","C":"MRI of the cervical spine to rule out C8 radiculopathy as the source of symptoms before proceeding","D":"Botulinum toxin injection into the hypothenar muscles to reduce intrinsic muscle tension in the canal","E":"Long arm cast immobilization at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce canal pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A structural lesion (ganglion cyst) confirmed on ultrasound causing nerve compression in Guyon's canal is an indication for surgical decompression and excision. Conservative management is unlikely to be effective when a physical mass is compressing the nerve. Additional corticosteroid injections will not address the structural problem. Cervical MRI is not needed when imaging has already identified the specific compressive lesion. Botulinum toxin and casting are not indicated and would not address the underlying cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Structural causes of nerve compression (ganglion cysts, hamate fractures) require surgical management; boards test the recognition of when imaging findings change the management algorithm from conservative to surgical. COMLEX: MSK ultrasound as a diagnostic tool for peripheral nerve compression is a COMLEX-testable point-of-care imaging modality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q34","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman who is 5 weeks postpartum presents with right wrist pain at the radial aspect while breastfeeding and lifting her infant. Exam shows tenderness over the radial styloid and a positive Finkelstein maneuver. What is the most likely diagnosis and which tendons are involved?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involving median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","B":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis involving the APL and EPB tendons in the first dorsal compartment at the wrist","C":"Lateral epicondylitis involving ECRB tendon overuse at the lateral epicondyle from repetitive infant lifting","D":"Ganglion cyst arising from the scapholunate ligament on the dorsal radial aspect of the wrist","E":"Radial tunnel syndrome from posterior interosseous nerve compression at the level of the radial head"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Postpartum De Quervain's tenosynovitis is a classic presentation: the combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist deviation), and breastfeeding positions stresses the APL and EPB tendons within the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid. The positive Finkelstein maneuver confirms the diagnosis. CTS presents with median nerve distribution paresthesias and does not cause radial styloid tenderness. Lateral epicondylitis presents at the elbow, not the wrist. A ganglion cyst would be a palpable mass. Radial tunnel syndrome presents with pain distal to the lateral epicondyle and a different examination pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum radial wrist pain presentation is a classic boards scenario (FA 2025: ↑ risk in new mothers); the APL and EPB tendon involvement with positive Finkelstein is the diagnostic combination to memorize. COMLEX: Treatment includes thumb spica bracing and corticosteroid injection; postpartum management considerations (breastfeeding, safe medications) add COMLEX clinical depth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q35","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female seamstress with De Quervain's tenosynovitis has tried 3 months of thumb spica bracing and NSAIDs without adequate relief. She cannot take time off work. Finkelstein maneuver remains positive. MSK ultrasound confirms first dorsal compartment tenosynovitis with peritendinous fluid. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Percutaneous first dorsal compartment release in the operating room given failure of all conservative options","B":"Extensor pollicis longus debridement via open surgery at Lister's tubercle to address the third compartment","C":"Carpal tunnel corticosteroid injection targeting the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath, targeting the APL and EPB","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the radial collateral ligament to reduce compartment inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After failing NSAIDs and bracing, corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath is the appropriate next step for De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Ultrasound-confirmed peritendinous fluid confirms the clinical diagnosis and guides injection targeting. Surgery (first dorsal compartment release) is reserved for cases failing at least one corticosteroid injection after adequate conservative management. EPL debridement at Lister's tubercle addresses the third compartment, not the first. Carpal tunnel injection treats CTS, not De Quervain's. PRP is used for chronic tendinosis, not acute tenosynovitis with fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The De Quervain's treatment ladder is boards-tested: bracing/NSAIDs first, then corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment, then surgical release for refractory cases. COMLEX: Injection site accuracy (first dorsal compartment vs carpal tunnel) is a COMLEX procedural anatomy question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q36","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old avid cross-country skier presents with bilateral radial wrist pain that began during ski season. She repetitively grasps poles with forceful thumb extension and radial deviation motions. Finkelstein maneuvers are positive bilaterally. No systemic symptoms. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive thumb abduction and radial deviation causing friction tenosynovitis in the first dorsal compartment","B":"Repetitive ulnar deviation causing triangular fibrocartilage complex impaction at the distal radioulnar joint","C":"Sustained wrist extension from pole gripping increasing carpal canal pressure and compressing the median nerve","D":"Valgus stress on the wrist from pole planting causing ulnar collateral ligament injury at the wrist joint","E":"Sustained elbow extension during the pole push-off phase compressing the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cross-country skiing requires repetitive forceful thumb abduction (poling) and wrist radial deviation, which generate friction and tensile stress on the APL and EPB tendons as they pass through the first dorsal compartment fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid. This repetitive mechanical friction produces stenosing tenosynovitis. TFCC impaction involves the ulnar wrist and a different mechanism. Carpal canal pressure from wrist extension causes CTS, not De Quervain's. UCL injury of the wrist is a separate ligamentous condition. Cubital tunnel syndrome involves the elbow, not the wrist, and affects the ulnar nerve rather than the thumb tendons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any activity requiring repetitive thumb abduction or wrist radial deviation (skiing, hammering, lifting infants) is a risk factor for De Quervain's; the mechanism of friction within the fibro-osseous tunnel is the key pathophysiological concept. COMLEX: Identifying repetitive motion mechanics and recommending activity modification as the cornerstone of management aligns with COMLEX's functional medicine approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"Elbow, Wrist & Hand Pathologies (Obj 7-12)","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the most common anatomical location of a ganglion cyst?","choices":{"A":"Palmar surface of the wrist, arising from the flexor carpi radialis tendon sheath","B":"Volar aspect of the distal interphalangeal joint, adjacent to the nail bed","C":"Lateral border of the index finger MCP joint, arising from the A1 pulley region","D":"Dorsal surface of the ankle, arising from the peroneal tendon sheath","E":"Dorsal wrist, arising from the scapholunate ligament or the dorsal joint capsule"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ganglion cysts are the most common soft tissue tumors of the hand and wrist. Approximately 70% occur on the dorsal wrist, typically arising from the scapholunate ligament or dorsal joint capsule. About 20% are on the palmar wrist, arising from the scaphotrapezoid joint, and the remaining 10% arise from other locations such as the finger sheaths. The cyst contains thick mucinous fluid derived from joint or tendon sheath tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cysts are the most common wrist mass — dorsal location (70%) from scapholunate ligament is classic. COMLEX: Transillumination distinguishes fluid-filled ganglion from solid tumor; OMT is not first-line.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q02","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A ganglion cyst is brought to transillumination testing during physical examination. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"The cyst does not transmit light, indicating a solid lipomatous composition","B":"The cyst partially transmits light, consistent with a mixed solid-cystic pattern","C":"The cyst transmits light, confirming its fluid-filled mucinous content","D":"The cyst fluoresces under ultraviolet light due to mucinous protein content","E":"Transillumination is unreliable for ganglion cysts and should not be performed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transillumination is a key clinical maneuver for ganglion cysts. Because the cyst is filled with thick, gelatinous mucinous fluid, it transmits light when a light source is held against it — confirming the cystic, fluid-filled nature. This distinguishes ganglion cysts from solid tumors such as lipomas or giant cell tumors of the tendon sheath, which do not transilluminate. The test is simple and requires no equipment beyond a small flashlight.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transillumination positive = fluid-filled cyst; negative = solid tumor. COMLEX: Ganglion cysts are benign; biopsy is unnecessary if transillumination confirms diagnosis. Aspiration can be both diagnostic and therapeutic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q03","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman presents with a 2 cm dorsal wrist mass that has been present for 6 months. It transilluminates and causes mild discomfort with wrist extension. Which of the following is an appropriate management option?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration of the cyst contents, which is both diagnostic and may be therapeutic","B":"Immediate surgical excision is required because recurrence after aspiration exceeds 90%","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the cyst is the preferred first-line treatment","D":"MRI of the wrist is mandatory prior to any intervention to rule out malignancy","E":"No treatment is needed and the patient should be told the cyst will never recur"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglion cysts may be managed conservatively, with aspiration, or with surgical excision. Aspiration is both diagnostic and therapeutic — removal of mucinous fluid can decompress the cyst and relieve symptoms. However, recurrence after aspiration is about 59%, compared to 21% after open surgical excision (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Observation is appropriate for asymptomatic cysts. Surgery is reserved for persistent, symptomatic, or recurrent cysts. Corticosteroid injection is reserved for recalcitrant cysts, not first-line, and MRI is not mandatory when clinical diagnosis is clear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst management — observation, aspiration (59% recurrence), or surgical excision (21% recurrence, the lowest). COMLEX: Aspiration is first-line for symptomatic cysts before surgical referral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q04","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following bony prominences is a hallmark of osteoarthritis at the distal interphalangeal joints of the hand?","choices":{"A":"Bouchard nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the proximal interphalangeal joints","B":"Haygarth nodes, representing periarticular soft tissue swelling at the MCP joints","C":"Garrod pads, representing fibrous tissue deposition over the DIP joint dorsum","D":"Swan neck deformity nodes, representing hyperextension contractures at the PIP joints","E":"Heberden nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the DIP joint margins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heberden nodes are bony osteophytes that develop at the distal interphalangeal joints and are a classic feature of hand osteoarthritis. They represent marginal osteophyte formation secondary to cartilage degeneration and subchondral bone remodeling. Bouchard nodes are the analogous finding at the proximal interphalangeal joints. Together, Heberden and Bouchard nodes distinguish osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis, which characteristically spares the DIP joints and produces soft, boggy MCP and PIP swelling rather than hard bony prominences.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heberden nodes = DIP osteophytes (OA); Bouchard nodes = PIP osteophytes (OA); rheumatoid arthritis spares DIP and involves MCP/PIP with soft swelling. COMLEX: OA nodes are hard and non-tender in chronic disease; RA nodes are soft, warm, and tender.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q05","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 65-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with pain and stiffness at the base of her thumb that worsens with pinching and gripping. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes. Which joint is most commonly affected in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"First carpometacarpal joint, where OA is particularly prevalent in postmenopausal women","B":"Metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb, which bears the greatest compressive force during pinch grip","C":"Scapholunate joint, which undergoes progressive instability from repetitive grip loading","D":"Trapezioscaphoid joint, which is the primary articulation stressed during lateral pinch","E":"Interphalangeal joint of the thumb, which loses cartilage from repeated flexion under load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoarthritis of the first carpometacarpal joint is the most common form of wrist and hand OA and is especially prevalent in postmenopausal women. The joint is subjected to high compressive and shear forces during pinch and grip activities. Patients present with pain and tenderness at the base of the thumb, a positive grind test, and radiographic evidence of joint space narrowing. The increased prevalence in postmenopausal women is attributed to the loss of estrogen, which affects collagen synthesis and cartilage integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb base pain, postmenopausal women, grind test positive. COMLEX: Distinguish from De Quervain tenosynovitis (radial styloid, Finkelstein positive) — both cause radial-sided wrist pain but different structures involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q06","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Erosive osteoarthritis of the hand predominantly affects which joints and is distinguished from classic OA by which additional histological feature?","choices":{"A":"Primarily the IP joints, with subchondral bone erosions and inflammatory synovitis superimposed on degenerative changes","B":"Primarily the wrist radiocarpal joints, with calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition as the hallmark feature","C":"Primarily the MCP joints, with fibrinoid necrosis and pannus formation distinguishing it from typical OA","D":"Primarily the 1st CMC joint, with diffuse cartilage thinning and absence of osteophyte formation","E":"Primarily all hand joints symmetrically, with anti-CCP antibody positivity distinguishing it from classic OA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Erosive osteoarthritis is a more aggressive inflammatory variant of hand OA that primarily involves the interphalangeal joints — both DIP and PIP. Unlike classic OA, erosive OA features central subchondral bone erosions visible on radiograph, giving a \"seagull wing\" or \"gull wing\" appearance, along with superimposed inflammatory synovitis. Anti-CCP antibodies are negative, distinguishing it from rheumatoid arthritis. MCP joint involvement and pannus formation are features of rheumatoid arthritis, not erosive OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Erosive OA = IP joints, inflammatory synovitis, subchondral erosions, anti-CCP negative. COMLEX: Radiographic \"gull wing\" erosion pattern at IP joints distinguishes erosive OA from RA (MCP predominant, anti-CCP positive).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q07","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Trigger finger results from stenosis of which structure, causing the flexor tendon to catch or lock during finger motion?","choices":{"A":"A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx, which is the strongest annular pulley in the finger sheath","B":"A1 pulley at the base of the finger, which becomes thickened and narrows the tendon sheath","C":"C1 cruciate pulley between the A1 and A2 pulleys, which frays under repetitive flexion loads","D":"Retinacular ligament of the DIP joint, which prevents bowstringing of the terminal extensor","E":"Transverse carpal ligament at the wrist, which compresses the median nerve and adjacent tendons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trigger finger, or stenosing flexor tenosynovitis, results from thickening and stenosis of the A1 pulley, located at the level of the MCP joint at the base of the finger. The flexor tendon develops a nodule or thickening that catches against the narrowed A1 pulley as the finger flexes and extends, producing the characteristic catching, triggering, or locking. The A2 pulley is the longest and strongest annular pulley but is not the primary site of stenosis in trigger finger. The transverse carpal ligament is involved in carpal tunnel syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger = A1 pulley stenosis at MCP joint base. The nodule is on the flexor tendon, not the pulley. COMLEX: Most common in middle finger, ring finger, and thumb; also associated with diabetes mellitus and rheumatoid arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q08","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with a ring finger that locks in flexion and must be passively extended. She has point tenderness at the MCP joint base. What is the first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia in the operating room","B":"Splinting of the MCP joint in extension for 6 weeks to allow pulley remodeling","C":"Oral NSAIDs and activity modification as definitive management for the first episode","D":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration of the tendon nodule with a large-bore needle","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley level"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley is the first-line treatment for trigger finger. A single injection resolves symptoms in approximately 60-90% of cases, with higher success rates in patients without diabetes. Patients with diabetes tend to have lower response rates and shorter durations of benefit. If symptoms recur after two injections, surgical release of the A1 pulley is indicated. Splinting alone has limited efficacy in established trigger finger. NSAIDs and activity modification are adjuncts but are insufficient as sole therapy for a locking trigger finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger first-line = corticosteroid injection into tendon sheath at A1 pulley; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Diabetes is a risk factor for trigger finger and reduces injection success rates — important COMLEX clinical application.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q09","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Trigger finger affects about 2% of the population and is most common in women in their 40s and 50s. Which group of systemic conditions does the course list as raising the rate of trigger finger?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism, chronic kidney disease, and sarcoidosis","B":"Osteoporosis, vitamin D deficiency, and hyperparathyroidism","C":"Diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, and deposition diseases","D":"Multiple sclerosis, Parkinson disease, and prior stroke","E":"Hypertension, coronary disease, and hyperlipidemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists trigger finger as affecting about 2% of the population, most commonly women in their 40s and 50s, sometimes in multiple fingers or both hands, with higher rates in patients with systemic conditions: diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, and deposition diseases. These conditions thicken or inflame the flexor tendon sheath and the A1 pulley. The other groups are not listed: hypothyroidism, kidney disease, and sarcoidosis; bone-mineral disorders; neurologic diseases; and cardiovascular risk factors are not named as trigger finger risks in the course.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger = stenosing flexor tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley over the MCP joint; ring finger and thumb most often involved. COMLEX: Diabetes predicts multiple digits and a poorer response to corticosteroid injection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — carpal tunnel syndrome, another diabetes-associated hand condition"},{"id":"sdl15_q10","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A complete ulnar collateral ligament tear of the thumb MCP joint is sometimes complicated by a Stener lesion. What is a Stener lesion?","choices":{"A":"The flexor pollicis longus tendon displaces into the MCP joint space and blocks reduction","B":"The adductor pollicis aponeurosis is interposed between the torn UCL ends, blocking healing","C":"A bony avulsion fragment from the ulnar base of the proximal phalanx rotates and blocks the joint","D":"Synovial tissue from the MCP joint capsule herniates between the torn ligament ends","E":"The extensor pollicis brevis tendon dislocates medially and tethers the torn UCL in malposition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Stener lesion occurs when the torn UCL end flips proximally and the adductor pollicis aponeurosis — a fascial layer overlying the MCP joint — becomes interposed between the two torn ends of the UCL. This prevents the torn ligament ends from healing in proper apposition, making conservative management inadequate. A Stener lesion is a primary indication for surgical repair. MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion preoperatively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = adductor pollicis aponeurosis interposed between torn UCL ends; prevents healing; requires surgery. COMLEX: Complete UCL tears need repair within 1 week (Atlas of Emergency Medicine, SDL 14/15 reading); Tintinalli recommends hand surgery referral within 7 days.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q11","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old skier falls and lands with their thumb hyperabducted against a ski pole. They present with thumb MCP joint pain, swelling, and instability with lateral stress testing. What mechanism produces this injury?","choices":{"A":"Forced ulnar deviation of the thumb at the MCP joint tears the radial collateral ligament on the lateral side","B":"Hyperflexion of the thumb IP joint avulses the flexor pollicis longus from its distal phalangeal insertion","C":"Axial loading through the thumb compresses the sesamoid bones into the MCP joint, rupturing the volar plate","D":"Forced extension of the MCP joint beyond neutral tears the dorsal capsule and extensor mechanism","E":"Forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint overstresses and tears the ulnar collateral ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"UCL injury of the thumb — classically called Gamekeeper's thumb when chronic and Skier's thumb when acute — results from forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint. This valgus stress mechanism overstresses the UCL on the ulnar aspect of the joint. In skiing, this occurs when the ski pole catches the thumb during a fall, leveraging the thumb radially. The UCL resists this radial abduction force; a sudden excessive force tears the ligament, which can be partial or complete. The RCL is on the opposite, radial side, and is injured by forced ulnar deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL thumb injury = forced radial abduction (valgus stress at MCP) = Skier's thumb (acute) or Gamekeeper's thumb (chronic). COMLEX: Clinical valgus stress test under local anesthesia assesses stability; more than 30-35 degrees of radial angulation, or 10-15 degrees more than the other thumb, suggests complete tear (Tintinalli, SDL 25 reading); the Atlas (SDL 14/15 reading) gives 30-40 degrees.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q12","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the gold-standard imaging modality for diagnosing a Stener lesion in a patient with suspected complete UCL tear of the thumb?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the thumb, which directly visualizes the displaced UCL and interposed adductor aponeurosis","B":"Stress radiograph of the thumb in abduction, which quantifies degrees of instability at the MCP joint","C":"Ultrasound of the ulnar aspect of the MCP joint performed dynamically during valgus stress loading","D":"CT arthrogram of the thumb, which outlines the joint capsule disruption and intraarticular hematoma","E":"Plain radiograph of the thumb, which identifies the avulsion fracture fragment at the UCL insertion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion because it directly visualizes the torn UCL and the interposed adductor pollicis aponeurosis. This distinction is critical because a Stener lesion requires surgical repair, whereas a partial UCL tear or complete tear without Stener lesion may be managed conservatively with thumb spica casting. Stress radiographs quantify instability but cannot differentiate Stener from non-Stener injuries. Ultrasound can be used by experienced operators but is operator-dependent. Plain radiographs identify bony avulsion fractures but cannot assess soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for Stener lesion detection. COMLEX: Surgical indication: Stener lesion, or complete-tear laxity (more than 30-35 degrees, or 10-15 degrees more than the other thumb, per Tintinalli; 30-40 degrees per the Atlas). Complete tears need repair within 1 week (Atlas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q13","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury is most consistent with mallet finger?","choices":{"A":"Sudden forced extension of a passively flexed DIP joint, tearing the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion","B":"Axial compression of the fingertip against a hard surface, driving the distal phalanx into the middle phalanx","C":"Sudden forced flexion of an actively extended fingertip, avulsing the terminal extensor tendon from the distal phalanx","D":"Hyperextension of the PIP joint beyond 90 degrees, disrupting the central slip of the extensor mechanism","E":"Chronic repetitive microtrauma to the DIP joint from gripping activities, leading to gradual extensor attrition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mallet finger, also called baseball finger, occurs when the terminal extensor tendon at the DIP joint is avulsed — either as a soft-tissue tear or as a bony avulsion fracture — by a sudden forced flexion force applied to an actively extended fingertip. The classic mechanism is a ball striking the end of the outstretched finger, abruptly flexing the DIP joint. This ruptures or avulses the terminal extensor tendon from its insertion on the dorsal distal phalanx, causing the DIP joint to drop into a flexion posture. The injury to the FDP causing jersey finger involves the volar tendon and forced extension, not flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = forced DIP flexion → terminal extensor rupture → DIP cannot extend. COMLEX: If less than 1/3 of articular surface involved, treat with extension splinting; greater than 1/3 or volar subluxation may require surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q14","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient presents with a flexion deformity of the DIP joint after being struck on the fingertip by a basketball. The DIP joint cannot be actively extended but passive extension is preserved. Radiograph shows no fracture. What is the appropriate treatment?","choices":{"A":"Surgical repair of the terminal extensor tendon is required for all acute mallet finger injuries within 24 hours","B":"Buddy taping to the adjacent finger and activity restriction for 3 weeks is sufficient for soft-tissue mallet","C":"Splinting the PIP joint in flexion and the DIP joint in extension simultaneously for 4 weeks restores function","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the DIP joint reduces inflammation and allows tendon reattachment within 2 weeks","E":"Continuous splinting of the DIP joint in full extension for 6-8 weeks without interruption, even during bathing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure soft-tissue mallet finger is managed with continuous DIP extension splinting for 6-8 weeks (CDT Orthopedics, SDL 25 reading; the course slide and the Atlas give at least 6 weeks). The splint must be worn continuously — including during bathing and sleep — because any inadvertent DIP flexion during the healing period restarts the 6-8 week clock. The splint maintains the terminal extensor tendon in apposition with its distal phalanx insertion, allowing scar tissue healing. Buddy taping alone is insufficient. Surgical repair is generally reserved for open injuries, bony avulsion fractures involving greater than 1/3 of the articular surface, or cases with volar subluxation of the distal phalanx.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Soft-tissue mallet finger = DIP extension splint continuously for 6-8 weeks; any DIP flexion during splinting restarts the clock. COMLEX: Bony mallet with less than 1/3 articular surface involvement is still treated conservatively with splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q15","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A bony mallet finger involves an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx. Which radiographic finding in a bony mallet finger is most likely to require surgical intervention rather than splinting?","choices":{"A":"The avulsion fragment is displaced more than 1 mm from the distal phalanx articular surface on lateral view","B":"The avulsion fracture involves the dorsal cortex without extension into the subchondral bone surface","C":"The patient presents more than 48 hours after the injury occurred due to delayed medical attention","D":"The avulsion fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface of the DIP joint","E":"The finger involved is the middle or ring finger, which bear greater functional pinch grip demands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bony mallet finger is typically managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting when the avulsion fragment involves less than one-third of the articular surface and the distal phalanx remains reduced without volar subluxation. When the bony fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface, joint stability may be compromised and the risk of articular incongruity is higher, making surgical fixation with a pin or screw the preferred approach. Volar subluxation of the distal phalanx with a large dorsal fragment is an absolute indication for surgery. The duration of delay and finger involved do not independently determine surgical need.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications for bony mallet finger: articular fragment > 1/3 of DIP surface, OR volar subluxation of distal phalanx. COMLEX: Less than 1/3 fragment = extension splint 6-8 weeks regardless of fragment displacement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q16","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Jersey finger most frequently involves which digit, and what tendon is avulsed in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The index finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum superficialis from its insertion on the middle phalanx","B":"The middle finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from the A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx","C":"The ring finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx","D":"The small finger, with avulsion of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon from the pisiform bony attachment","E":"The thumb, with avulsion of the flexor pollicis longus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Jersey finger is avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx, most commonly affecting the ring finger in approximately 75% of cases. The ring finger is vulnerable because it has the least independent flexion compared to adjacent fingers and is held in a more extended position during gripping, making it the last to release when the grip is forcefully opened. The injury occurs when a player grabs a jersey and the opponent pulls away, forcibly extending the DIP joint against active FDP contraction. The FDS inserts on the middle phalanx and is not typically avulsed in jersey finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion, ring finger 75%, inability to flex DIP against resistance. COMLEX: Mechanism = forced DIP extension against resisted FDP contraction; contrast with mallet finger (forced DIP flexion, extensor injury).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q17","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger, a Type I injury carries the most urgent surgical timeline. Why is surgery most urgent for Type I?","choices":{"A":"The FDP tendon remains at the PIP joint level, where it compresses the digital nerve and requires urgent decompression","B":"A large bony avulsion fragment obstructs the A4 pulley, causing vascular compromise of the entire finger","C":"The tendon avulses with the volar plate of the DIP joint, making anatomical reduction technically time-sensitive","D":"The FDP retracts into the palm, losing its vincular blood supply, so repair is needed within days to prevent necrosis","E":"Both vincula are intact in Type I, preserving blood supply and allowing delayed repair up to 3 months later"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type I jersey finger involves the FDP tendon retracting all the way into the palm, rupturing both the long and short vincula — the mesotenon attachments that supply blood to the tendon. Without vascular supply, the tendon undergoes rapid fibrosis and becomes unsuitable for repair. Therefore, surgical repair must be performed within days (typically 7-10 days) of injury. Type II tendons retract to the PIP joint level, maintaining the short vinculum and blood supply, allowing reinsertion within several weeks (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment that catches at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing a less urgent surgical timeline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer Type I = tendon into palm, both vincula ruptured, surgery within days. Type II = PIP level, short vinculum intact, repair within several weeks. Type III = bony fragment at A4. COMLEX: Delay in Type I = irreparable tendon necrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q18","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding best distinguishes jersey finger from other finger injuries?","choices":{"A":"Loss of active DIP flexion with preserved passive DIP flexion and intact PIP flexion against resistance","B":"Loss of active DIP extension with the DIP joint held in a flexion posture that cannot be voluntarily corrected","C":"Loss of both active DIP and PIP flexion with the finger held in extension by the intact extensor mechanism","D":"Triggering and catching of the finger with active flexion due to a nodule catching on the A1 pulley","E":"Point tenderness at the MCP joint with palpable swelling and clicking on passive range of motion testing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jersey finger presents with inability to actively flex the DIP joint because the FDP — the only tendon that flexes the DIP — has been avulsed from its insertion. Passive DIP flexion is preserved because the joint itself is intact. PIP flexion is maintained because the FDS, which inserts on the middle phalanx, is uninjured. This pattern — lost active DIP flexion with preserved active PIP flexion — is pathognomonic for FDP avulsion. Mallet finger, in contrast, presents with lost active DIP extension (the finger droops in flexion). Trigger finger involves catching on active flexion at the MCP joint level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = cannot actively flex DIP; FDS intact so PIP flexion preserved. Mallet finger = cannot actively extend DIP. COMLEX: Distinguish on exam — jersey finger \"flexion loss at DIP,\" mallet finger \"drop at DIP.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q19","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 26-year-old right-hand-dominant office worker presents with a 2-year history of a slowly enlarging lump on the dorsum of her wrist. She reports mild aching that worsens with prolonged typing. On exam, a 1.5 cm firm, smooth, non-tender mass is found over the dorsal radiocarpal joint. It transilluminates; the overlying skin moves freely over it, but the mass is fixed to deeper structures. Radiographs are normal. What is the most likely origin of this mass?","choices":{"A":"Reactive hypertrophy of the dorsal extensor retinaculum from repetitive friction against the underlying carpal bones","B":"Lipomatous degeneration of the dorsal wrist fat pad secondary to chronic compression from keyboard use","C":"Herniation of synovial fluid through a one-way valve defect in the dorsal scapholunate ligament or joint capsule","D":"Proliferation of synovial lining cells within the joint, forming an exophytic synovial cyst into the dorsal soft tissue","E":"Hematoma organization after an unrecognized minor wrist sprain that was not treated acutely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglion cysts of the dorsal wrist arise through a one-way valve or pedicle defect in the joint capsule or scapholunate ligament that allows synovial fluid to herniate outward. The fluid collects into a fibrous-walled cyst and becomes gelatinous over time. The \"one-way valve\" mechanism means that joint movement can pump more fluid into the cyst but not back into the joint, which accounts for the cyst's fluctuating size with activity. Synovial proliferation produces villous synovitis or rice bodies in inflammatory arthritis, not discrete cysts. Fixation to deep structures with freely mobile overlying skin reflects its capsular/ligamentous origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst arises via one-way valve defect in joint capsule — synovial fluid herniates out and becomes gelatinous. COMLEX: Wrist ganglia fluctuate in size with wrist motion/activity — this history clue supports ganglion over lipoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: mucinous synovial cyst arising from posterior knee joint communication via one-way valve — compare mechanism to dorsal wrist ganglion"},{"id":"sdl15_q20","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with a 3 cm mass on the palmar radial aspect of his wrist near the base of the thenar eminence. It has been present for 8 months and causes mild discomfort with wrist flexion. Transillumination is positive. He asks about treatment options and their recurrence rates. Which statement about recurrence is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration has approximately 10% recurrence rate and is therefore preferred over surgical excision in most patients","B":"Aspiration has approximately 59% recurrence rate, while open surgical excision has about a 21% recurrence rate","C":"Surgical excision has a 50-60% recurrence rate because the cyst pedicle commonly regrows from residual capsular tissue","D":"Observation leads to spontaneous resolution in less than 5% of cases, making active treatment always necessary","E":"Recurrence rates are identical regardless of treatment modality and depend solely on continued wrist overuse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Management of ganglion cysts must be discussed with the patient in the context of recurrence rates. Aspiration carries about 59% recurrence, while open surgical excision — which removes the cyst wall and its pedicle from the joint capsule or ligament — has about 21% recurrence (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Observation is also appropriate; in children under 10, up to 80% resolve within a year (Nelson). The choice of management should be individualized based on symptom severity, cyst size, patient preference, and hand dominance. Palmar wrist cysts are in proximity to the radial artery and require careful technique for aspiration or excision.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion recurrence — aspiration ~59%, open excision ~21% (CDT Orthopedics); observation is an option. COMLEX: Palmar cysts near radial artery require caution with aspiration; surgical excision is preferred when intervention is needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: conservative observation vs aspiration vs surgical excision decision-making parallels ganglion cyst management"},{"id":"sdl15_q21","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 19-year-old male collegiate baseball pitcher presents with a small lump on the volar radial wrist for 4 months. It is 1 cm, non-tender, smooth, and non-fluctuant. He is concerned it might be cancer. Transillumination is positive. There is no change in size with compression. Radiographs are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the patient that ganglion cysts are benign, and offer observation with activity modification as an initial option","B":"Order an MRI with contrast to rule out low-grade synovial sarcoma before any further management is planned","C":"Perform immediate needle aspiration in the office to confirm diagnosis and provide therapeutic decompression","D":"Refer to hand surgery for urgent excision given the patient's athletic demands and occupational requirements","E":"Obtain an ultrasound-guided core biopsy of the mass because transillumination is not specific enough for diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A small, non-tender, transilluminating mass on the wrist in a young patient with a typical clinical presentation is reliably a ganglion cyst. Reassurance and observation are appropriate as a first step, given that the mass is benign and the course lists observation as first-line (SDL 14/15 slide p28). MRI or biopsy is not indicated when the clinical diagnosis is clear and there are no atypical features such as rapid growth, firmness, or systemic symptoms suggesting malignancy. Aspiration or referral to hand surgery can be offered if the patient desires intervention, but immediate escalation is not warranted for a small, asymptomatic, and uncomplicated presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transilluminating wrist mass with typical clinical features = ganglion cyst; observation is first-line. COMLEX: Avoid overinvestigation — clinical diagnosis of ganglion does not require biopsy or advanced imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: reassurance and observation appropriate for asymptomatic synovial cysts regardless of anatomical location"},{"id":"sdl15_q22","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old retired seamstress presents with 5 years of bilateral hand pain and stiffness. She notes bony enlargement of the DIP and PIP joints of multiple fingers bilaterally. Radiographs reveal joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes at the DIP and PIP joints. Lab work including ESR, CRP, RF, and anti-CCP antibodies are all within normal limits. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis with seronegative presentation affecting the DIP and PIP joints symmetrically","B":"Psoriatic arthritis with DIP predominant involvement and enthesopathy at the extensor tendon insertions","C":"Gout with monosodium urate crystal deposition causing recurrent periarticular bone erosion at multiple joints","D":"Primary hand osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints","E":"Reactive arthritis triggered by prior infection, causing polyarticular DIP and PIP joint inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary hand osteoarthritis is characterized by Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints, with radiographic findings of joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes. Normal inflammatory markers and seronegative labs distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis, which is seropositive in most cases and characteristically spares the DIP joints. Psoriatic arthritis can involve the DIP joints but typically shows \"pencil in cup\" erosions on X-ray and is associated with psoriatic skin or nail changes. Gout shows tophi and negatively birefringent urate crystals on joint aspiration. A retired seamstress with bilateral symmetric DIP and PIP bony enlargement and normal labs is classic for primary OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hand OA = Heberden nodes (DIP) + Bouchard nodes (PIP); OA spares MCP; RA spares DIP. COMLEX: Normal ESR/CRP and seronegative labs confirm OA over inflammatory arthritis in this presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: cartilage degeneration, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation pathophysiology underlying all OA including hand OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q23","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 60-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with a 2-year history of pain and weakness at the base of her right thumb. She reports difficulty opening jars and turning a key. Exam reveals tenderness at the thenar eminence proximal to the MCP joint, a palpable bony ridge at the 1st CMC joint, and a positive grind test. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes at the 1st CMC joint. Which of the following best describes the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is the recommended initial treatment given radiographic OA","B":"Thumb spica splinting combined with NSAIDs and activity modification as the first-line conservative approach","C":"Systemic corticosteroids for 2 weeks followed by hand therapy to reduce acute flare and restore grip strength","D":"Intraarticular methotrexate injection into the 1st CMC joint to slow cartilage degeneration over time","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the 1st CMC joint should be offered before any splinting or NSAID use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First CMC joint OA is initially managed conservatively with thumb spica splinting to immobilize the joint and reduce mechanical stress, combined with NSAIDs for pain control and activity modification. Occupational therapy with hand exercises is also beneficial. Corticosteroid injections into the 1st CMC joint are useful for acute flares. Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is reserved for patients who fail conservative management over several months and have functional limitations. Systemic corticosteroids are not appropriate for focal OA. Intraarticular methotrexate and PRP are not standard evidence-based treatments for 1st CMC OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb spica, NSAIDs, activity modification; corticosteroid injection for flares; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Grind test positive (axial compression + rotation of 1st metacarpal) = 1st CMC OA; Finkelstein positive = De Quervain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: conservative management principles including splinting, NSAIDs, and activity modification applicable to 1st CMC OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q24","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Two women aged 64 have symptomatic hand osteoarthritis with similar radiographic changes. Patient 1 has two involved DIP joints and still does all her own housework. Patient 2 has eleven involved joints across both hands and already needs help with buttons and jars. According to the course, which statement about their outlook is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Both have the same outlook, because radiographic severity is the main predictor of how hand osteoarthritis will progress","B":"Patient 1 has the worse outlook, because disease limited to a few DIP joints tends to become erosive and spread faster","C":"Patient 2 has the worse outlook, because lower baseline function and more involved joints predict a worse prognosis","D":"Patient 2 has the better outlook, because widespread involvement spreads load and slows progression in any one joint","E":"Neither outlook can be judged until inflammatory markers are measured, because hand OA progression tracks the ESR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that hand osteoarthritis is usually bilateral and symmetric with variable progression, that radiographic severity does not necessarily correlate with the clinical presentation, and that a worse prognosis is linked to lower baseline function and an increasing number of involved joints. Patient 2 has both. Equal radiographs do not mean an equal outlook, since films do not track clinical course closely. Limited DIP disease is not described as a predictor of erosive spread. More involved joints worsen, not improve, the outlook. Hand OA is not monitored by ESR; inflammatory markers are typically normal in primary hand OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hand OA: DIP (Heberden) and PIP (Bouchard) nodes, first CMC in postmenopausal women; erosive OA targets IP joints with central erosions. RA spares DIPs. COMLEX: Management follows a modified OARSI approach with orthoses; surgery for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — OARSI framework for osteoarthritis management"},{"id":"sdl15_q25","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has 3 weeks of painful catching of the right thumb when she bends and straightens it, relieved by a snap. It never locks, and there is a tender nodule at the base of the thumb over the MCP joint. She wants to avoid injections and surgery for now. Which treatment is part of the conservative care described in the course readings?","choices":{"A":"Excision of the palpable nodule under local anesthesia, since removing it stops catching without opening the A1 pulley","B":"Long arm casting with the elbow flexed for 6 weeks, since resting the forearm flexor muscles unloads the flexor sheath","C":"Daily forceful passive stretching into full extension through the catch, since repeated snapping wears the nodule down","D":"Oral corticosteroids for 3 months, since a systemic taper reaches the tendon sheath as well as a local injection does","E":"Rest, anti-inflammatory medication, and immobilizing the digit with a finger splint or buddy tape to settle the sheath"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Tintinalli hand chapter assigned with this SDL lists conservative treatment of trigger finger as rest, anti-inflammatory medication, and immobilization with buddy tape or a finger splint to reduce inflammation and swelling of the flexor sheath; the course slide likewise lists NSAIDs and splinting with corticosteroid injection, and surgery is rare. Corticosteroid injection treats early triggering, and A1 pulley release is curative when conservative care and injection fail. Excising the nodule alone is not a described treatment. A long arm cast does not target the digital sheath. Repeated forceful snapping irritates the sheath rather than treating it. A prolonged oral steroid course is not part of the course plan.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger: nodular flexor tendon catches at the A1 pulley; ring finger and thumb most common. Ladder: rest/NSAIDs/splint → sheath corticosteroid injection → A1 pulley release (>90% cure). COMLEX: Diabetes and RA increase risk and recurrence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — De Quervain tenosynovitis, another stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl15_q26","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with trigger finger of the right long finger that has failed two corticosteroid injections over 6 months. She now has a finger that locks fully in flexion and must be manually extended. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"A third corticosteroid injection combined with formal hand therapy for 8 weeks before considering surgery","B":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia, which has a greater than 90% cure rate","C":"Splinting the MCP joint in full extension for 6 weeks to allow A1 pulley remodeling and tendon decompression","D":"Oral methotrexate for 3 months to address underlying inflammatory tenosynovitis contributing to pulley stenosis","E":"Excision of the flexor tendon nodule alone, leaving the thickened A1 pulley intact to preserve finger flexion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After two failed corticosteroid injections, surgical A1 pulley release is the appropriate next step. The procedure involves dividing the A1 pulley longitudinally under local anesthesia, either through an open incision or percutaneously, which decompresses the tendon and resolves triggering. Surgical release is curative in more than 90% of digits refractory to steroid injection (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), and complications are rare. A third injection provides minimal additional benefit after two failures, especially in a locking trigger finger. Splinting alone does not decompress a stenotic pulley. Methotrexate is used for inflammatory arthritis, not idiopathic trigger finger. Excising the tendon nodule while leaving the stenotic A1 pulley intact does not decompress the tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Refractory trigger finger after 2 injections = A1 pulley surgical release; greater than 90% cure rate (CDT Orthopedics). COMLEX: Open vs percutaneous A1 release both effective; percutaneous avoided near neurovascular structures in thumb and index finger.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: A1 pulley surgical division relieves mechanical obstruction to tendon gliding through fibro-osseous canal"},{"id":"sdl15_q27","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with no significant medical history develops trigger finger of the right middle finger after 6 weeks of intensive grip training at a new warehouse job. He describes a painful snap when bending the finger, with the finger occasionally locking in a bent position. The MCP base is tender. Which comorbid condition, if present, would most significantly reduce the expected response rate to a single corticosteroid injection?","choices":{"A":"Diabetes mellitus, which promotes fibrosis of the A1 pulley and impairs tendon sheath response to corticosteroids","B":"Hypertension, which impairs microvascular supply to the tendon sheath and prolongs inflammatory resolution","C":"Osteoporosis, which results in subchondral softening of the MCP joint that amplifies compressive forces on the A1 pulley","D":"Hypothyroidism, which causes diffuse mucin deposition in the tenosynovium and is not responsive to steroids","E":"Obesity, which increases grip force demand on the A1 pulley and accelerates pulley stenosis after any injection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus is the most significant comorbidity that reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy in trigger finger. Diabetes promotes glycation of collagen and fibrosis of the tendon sheath and pulleys, creating a more fibrotic and less responsive pathological substrate. Additionally, corticosteroid injections in diabetic patients can transiently elevate blood glucose. In the general population, a single corticosteroid injection resolves trigger finger in 60-90% of patients, but diabetics are less likely to respond (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics) and recurrence is more common. Hypothyroidism is associated with trigger finger due to mucinous tenosynovial deposits but does respond to treatment. Hypertension and obesity are not directly associated with reduced injection response rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes mellitus reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy for trigger finger and increases recurrence — plan for earlier surgical referral. COMLEX: Also note hypothyroidism as a risk factor for trigger finger (mucinous tenosynovitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: collagen glycation in diabetes promotes A1 pulley fibrosis, reducing responsiveness to steroid-mediated inflammation modulation"},{"id":"sdl15_q28","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old skier presents after a fall in which her right thumb was caught on a ski pole and forced radially. She reports MCP joint pain and difficulty pinching. Exam reveals swelling and ecchymosis over the ulnar MCP joint, tenderness at the ulnar collateral ligament, and laxity with valgus stress at 30 degrees of MCP flexion but not at 0 degrees. What does the pattern of laxity suggest?","choices":{"A":"Complete UCL rupture with Stener lesion, because laxity at 30 degrees confirms total ligament disruption","B":"Volar plate rupture of the MCP joint, which produces laxity at 30 degrees flexion and 0 degrees equally","C":"Radial collateral ligament sprain, because the radial side is stressed when the thumb is forced into radial deviation","D":"Physeal fracture at the base of the proximal phalanx, which must be ruled out by radiograph before stress testing","E":"Proper UCL injury with an intact accessory collateral ligament and volar plate, since laxity appears only in flexion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In thumb MCP stress testing, laxity at 30 degrees of flexion indicates injury to the proper UCL, as the proper UCL is the primary stabilizer in flexion. Laxity at both 0 degrees and 30 degrees of MCP flexion indicates a more complete injury involving both the proper and accessory UCL. In this vignette, laxity only at 30 degrees with stability at 0 degrees localizes the injury to the proper UCL with an intact accessory UCL and volar plate; the degree of opening and comparison with the other thumb determine whether the tear is partial or complete. Complete tears show laxity at both angles. Volar plate injury would not isolate laxity to 30 degrees flexion only. A Stener lesion cannot be confirmed or excluded by clinical examination alone — MRI is required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL stress test — laxity at 30 degrees = proper UCL; laxity at 0 degrees = accessory UCL also torn. More than 30-35 degrees or 10-15 degrees more than contralateral = complete tear (Tintinalli, SDL 25 reading); the Atlas (SDL 14/15 reading) gives 30-40 degrees. COMLEX: Always radiograph first before stress testing to rule out avulsion fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: valgus and varus stress testing principles for hand joint ligament integrity assessment"},{"id":"sdl15_q29","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old rugby player sustains a UCL injury of the right thumb. MRI confirms a complete UCL tear with a Stener lesion. The injury occurred 4 days ago. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because Stener lesions can resolve spontaneously with immobilization alone","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the MCP joint to reduce inflammation followed by functional bracing for 4 weeks","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection at the UCL insertion to promote tissue regeneration and avoid surgery","D":"Surgical repair of the UCL with reapproximation of the torn ends within about 1 week, before fibrosis occurs","E":"Dynamic splinting of the MCP joint in ulnar deviation for 4 weeks to allow UCL scar tissue approximation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Stener lesion — interposition of the adductor pollicis aponeurosis between the torn UCL ends — prevents anatomic healing of the torn ligament with conservative treatment. Surgical repair is required and should be performed within 1 week (Atlas of Emergency Medicine, SDL 14/15 reading; Tintinalli advises hand surgery referral within 7 days), as fibrosis and scar tissue formation after that time make anatomic repair technically more difficult. The surgeon excises the interposed aponeurosis, reapproximates the UCL ends, and may augment with a suture anchor. Conservative management with thumb spica casting is appropriate only for partial tears without Stener lesion. Corticosteroid injection and PRP are not indicated for acute ligamentous injuries requiring operative repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = surgery required; repair within 1 week (Atlas). COMLEX: Failure to diagnose Stener lesion leads to chronic MCP instability — clinical pearl for COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: surgical vs conservative management decision-making based on ligamentous integrity and interposed tissue"},{"id":"sdl15_q30","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old gamekeeper presents with chronic right thumb weakness for 2 years. He reports difficulty with pinch grip and a history of repetitive thumb hyperabduction while wringing the necks of game birds. Exam reveals MCP joint laxity with valgus stress and weak key pinch. Radiographs show mild 1st MCP OA. What term describes his injury, and what is its key distinguishing feature from acute Skier's thumb?","choices":{"A":"Skier's thumb — an acute UCL rupture with Stener lesion that presents identically in chronic form with pinch weakness","B":"De Quervain tenosynovitis — chronic radial-sided wrist pain from repetitive thumb loading that mimics UCL pathology","C":"Gamekeeper's thumb — chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma, rather than one acute complete tear","D":"Trigger thumb — chronic A1 pulley stenosis from repetitive thumb gripping that secondarily loosens the UCL over time","E":"First CMC OA — degenerative joint disease of the thumb base from grip overuse that does not affect the UCL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gamekeeper's thumb describes chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma — originally described in Scottish gamekeepers who routinely wrung the necks of game, stressing the thumb UCL thousands of times. Over time, the UCL stretches and becomes insufficient, producing progressive MCP instability, pinch weakness, and secondary OA. This is distinguished from Skier's thumb, which is an acute complete UCL tear from a single traumatic event. Both involve the UCL, but gamekeeper's thumb represents cumulative attenuation rather than acute rupture, and Stener lesions are less common in chronic cases. Treatment for chronic gamekeeper's thumb may require ligament reconstruction rather than primary repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gamekeeper's thumb = chronic UCL laxity from repetitive stress; Skier's thumb = acute UCL rupture from single event. COMLEX: Both cause MCP valgus instability and difficulty pinching — distinguish by mechanism and chronicity on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: chronic ligamentous insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma compared to acute dislocation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl15_q31","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has a closed soft-tissue mallet finger of the left middle finger with no fracture on radiographs. In urgent care, the finger was placed in an aluminum splint that holds both the PIP and DIP joints straight. According to the course reading, how should the splinting be corrected?","choices":{"A":"Splint the DIP joint by itself in extension and leave the PIP free, wearing it continuously for at least 6 weeks","B":"Splint the PIP joint by itself in flexion and leave the DIP free, so the lateral bands relax and pull the tendon back","C":"Splint the wrist and all fingers in a resting cast, since the extensor tendon must be unloaded at the forearm","D":"Remove the splint and begin active DIP flexion exercises now, so that the joint does not become stiff in extension","E":"Splint the DIP joint in 45 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, so the tendon heals at its resting length first"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Emergency Medicine Atlas reading for this SDL states that a closed mallet finger without joint involvement is treated by splinting the DIP joint in extension, that the PIP joint should not be splinted, and that the splint is worn continuously for at least 6 weeks; operative treatment is usually not required unless more than one-third of the articular surface is avulsed. The course slide gives full extension for 6 weeks. Splinting the PIP alone leaves the DIP to droop and does not oppose the injury. A forearm and hand cast immobilizes joints that do not need it. Early DIP flexion would pull the healing tendon ends apart. Splinting the DIP in flexion places the torn extensor at its longest and prevents healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = terminal extensor tendon avulsion (forced flexion of an extended DIP); most common finger tendon injury, more common in males. COMLEX: Any DIP flexion during the 6 weeks restarts the clock; untreated mallet → swan neck deformity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — interphalangeal joint dislocation management"},{"id":"sdl15_q32","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents 10 days after sustaining a mallet finger of the right long finger while playing basketball. He has been using buddy tape since the injury. Radiographs show a dorsal avulsion fracture involving approximately 15% of the DIP articular surface without volar subluxation. The DIP joint rests in 40 degrees of flexion. What is the most appropriate management at this time?","choices":{"A":"The 10-day delay makes conservative splinting ineffective; immediate surgical pin fixation is now required","B":"The avulsion fracture size mandates surgical ORIF regardless of delay to restore articular congruence","C":"Obtain MRI to assess soft tissue terminal extensor integrity before committing to surgical vs conservative care","D":"Apply a DIP extension splint now and treat conservatively with continuous splinting for 8 weeks from this visit","E":"Apply a short arm cast with the wrist in extension and DIP in neutral for 6 weeks to off-load the extensor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mallet finger presenting 10 days after injury can still be managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting, provided the fracture involves less than one-third of the articular surface and there is no volar subluxation of the distal phalanx. A 15% articular surface avulsion fragment without subluxation meets these criteria. The splinting period is calculated from the time splinting begins — so 8 weeks of continuous splinting from this visit is appropriate. Surgical indications are: fragment > 1/3 articular surface, volar subluxation, or open injury. A short delay in presentation does not automatically mandate surgery. MRI is not needed when radiographs provide sufficient information for surgical decision-making.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conservative mallet finger criteria — fragment less than 1/3 articular surface AND no volar subluxation, regardless of delay. Splinting duration starts from when splinting begins. COMLEX: Counsel patient that any DIP flexion during the 8 weeks restarts the clock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: articular surface involvement criteria determine conservative vs operative management for intraarticular avulsion fractures"},{"id":"sdl15_q33","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A chronic mallet finger deformity that is left untreated for more than 3 months can lead to a secondary deformity affecting the PIP joint. What is this secondary deformity and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Boutonniere deformity, in which the central slip tears at the PIP joint and causes the PIP to flex and DIP to extend","B":"Swan neck deformity, in which the lateral bands migrate dorsally and hyperextend the PIP joint as the volar plate becomes lax","C":"Trigger finger deformity, in which the lateral bands tighten the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley and cause PIP locking","D":"Mallet deformity extension proximally, in which the extensor tendon attrition spreads from the DIP to the PIP level","E":"Z-deformity of the finger, in which the extensor mechanism imbalance causes alternating flexion and extension at each joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic untreated mallet finger can lead to swan neck deformity. As the DIP rests in flexion chronically, the extensor mechanism proximal to the DIP becomes slack. The lateral bands — normally held in position by the triangular ligament — can migrate dorsally at the PIP joint level, creating a tethering force that hyperextends the PIP joint. The volar plate, which normally prevents PIP hyperextension, stretches and becomes lax over time. The resulting swan neck deformity has DIP flexion and PIP hyperextension. Boutonniere deformity involves central slip disruption causing PIP flexion and DIP extension — the opposite pattern. Recognizing swan neck as the secondary complication of untreated mallet finger is a board-tested correlation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Untreated mallet finger → swan neck deformity (DIP flexion + PIP hyperextension) from dorsal lateral band migration. Boutonniere = central slip rupture → PIP flexion + DIP extension. COMLEX: Swan neck deformity is also seen in RA; distinguish by history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: extensor mechanism anatomy at the DIP and PIP levels is foundational to understanding secondary deformities from untreated bony and soft-tissue injuries"},{"id":"sdl15_q34","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old rugby player cannot bend the tip of his ring finger after grabbing an opponent's jersey. With the MCP and PIP joints held in extension, he cannot flex the DIP joint; passive motion is full. Radiographs show no bony fragment. What is the most appropriate management in the emergency department?","choices":{"A":"Splint the DIP joint in full extension for 6 weeks without interruption and recheck in clinic, as for a mallet finger","B":"Buddy tape the ring finger to the long finger and start active flexion exercises, since most cases heal with motion","C":"Order MRI before any splinting, because radiographs are not useful for this injury and must be skipped","D":"Splint the PIP and DIP joints in mild flexion and refer to orthopedics, since every jersey finger needs referral","E":"Inject corticosteroid into the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley to reduce swelling and restore active DIP flexion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course slide lists acute management of jersey finger as a splint with the PIP and DIP in mild flexion, with all cases referred to orthopedics; the Atlas reading notes that jersey finger often needs surgical repair, that early referral is recommended, and that prognosis worsens with delay or severe tendon retraction. Isolating the DIP by holding the MCP and PIP in extension tests FDP function. Splinting the DIP in full extension is mallet finger treatment and would stretch the injured flexor. Buddy taping with active motion does not repair an avulsed tendon. Radiographs are indicated to look for a bony avulsion, with MRI if radiographs are unclear. Steroid injection at the A1 pulley treats trigger finger, not tendon avulsion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion from forced extension of a flexed DIP; ring finger ~75%; test FDP by isolating the DIP. COMLEX: Leddy-Packer type I (retraction into the palm) needs repair within days because the vincular supply is lost.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — mallet finger, the extensor counterpart at the DIP"},{"id":"sdl15_q35","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old wrestler is diagnosed with a Leddy-Packer Type II jersey finger of the right ring finger, 8 days after the injury. On radiographs, no bony fragment is seen. Which statement about this injury type and its timing of repair is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Type II involves FDP retraction into the palm with both vincula ruptured, requiring surgery within 3-5 days to prevent necrosis","B":"Type II involves a bony avulsion fragment at the A4 pulley level, and repair can be delayed up to 3 months without tendon necrosis","C":"Type II involves FDP avulsion with intact A2 pulley that prevents any proximal tendon retraction beyond the midshaft","D":"Type II is the rarest subtype and involves simultaneous FDS and FDP avulsion at the DIP joint level","E":"Type II involves FDP retraction to the PIP joint level with intact short vinculum, allowing repair within several weeks of injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type II jersey finger occurs when the FDP tendon retracts to the PIP joint level, where it is held by the intact short vinculum (long vinculum is ruptured). Because the short vinculum maintains some blood supply to the tendon, the tendon is viable for a longer window — the tendon may be reinserted within several weeks of injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Type I (retraction into palm) disrupts both vincula and requires surgery within days. Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment large enough to catch at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing surgical repair even after a longer delay. The absence of a radiographic bony fragment in this case makes Type III less likely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer — Type I: palm retraction, both vincula gone, urgent surgery within days. Type II: PIP level, short vinculum intact, repair within several weeks. Type III: bony fragment at A4, delayed repair possible. COMLEX: Timing of repair determines tendon viability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: vascular supply to avulsed tendons determines surgical urgency — same principle applies to Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q36","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old rugby player presents 3 weeks after sustaining a jersey finger of the right ring finger that was never diagnosed. He now has a small firm mass at the proximal palm and cannot flex his DIP at all. MRI confirms the FDP tendon has retracted into the palm with disruption of both vincula. What is the expected finding at surgery, and what does this mean for repair options?","choices":{"A":"The tendon is supple and can be pulled back through the intact sheath for primary repair despite the 3-week delay","B":"Tendon fibrosis and retraction make primary repair impossible; tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction is required","C":"The tendon has healed onto the lumbrical origin in the palm and can be re-routed through the digital canal primarily","D":"The A4 pulley has remodeled and widened in the absence of the tendon, allowing graft passage without pulley reconstruction","E":"Spontaneous healing of the FDP tendon to the A2 pulley has occurred, and DIP arthrodesis is therefore unnecessary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a Type I jersey finger (FDP retracted into palm with both vincula disrupted) is diagnosed after 3 weeks, the tendon has undergone significant fibrosis, shortening, and loss of elasticity. The tendon cannot be retrieved and primarily repaired through the intact digital flexor sheath without prohibitive tension and risk of sheath rupture. Surgical options at this stage include tendon grafting (one-stage if sheath is supple) or two-stage reconstruction (Hunter rod placement first, then graft). In some patients, DIP arthrodesis in a functional position is preferred for its reliability. The firm palmar mass corresponds to the fibrosed, retracted FDP tendon end. Primary repair is not feasible after this delay in Type I injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neglected Type I jersey finger (past the 7-10 day repair window, CDT Orthopedics) = tendon fibrosis; primary repair not possible; requires tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction. COMLEX: Delayed jersey finger is a common board scenario — know the time-sensitive Type I window.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: delayed avulsion repair leads to fibrosis and requires staged reconstruction — principle applies to neglected jersey finger"}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"Upper Extremity Fractures","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A midshaft humerus fracture in the spiral groove is most commonly associated with injury to which nerve, and what is the classic clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve, causing loss of deltoid function and shoulder abduction deficit","B":"Median nerve, causing loss of thumb opposition and thenar muscle wasting","C":"Radial nerve, causing wrist drop from loss of wrist and finger extension","D":"Ulnar nerve, causing claw hand deformity and loss of intrinsic hand muscle function","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, causing loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The radial nerve travels through the spiral groove of the midshaft humerus and is the most commonly injured nerve with midshaft humerus fractures. Injury produces wrist drop — inability to extend the wrist and fingers — due to paralysis of the extensor muscles of the forearm. The axillary nerve, in contrast, is injured with surgical neck fractures of the proximal humerus, producing deltoid weakness and loss of shoulder abduction. Median nerve injury at the elbow level is associated with supracondylar fractures. Ulnar nerve injury occurs with medial epicondyle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus = radial nerve = wrist drop. Surgical neck = axillary nerve = deltoid weakness. Supracondylar = anterior interosseous nerve (median branch). COMLEX: Radial nerve runs in the spiral groove; Holstein-Lewis fracture is a distal third spiral fracture with especially high radial nerve injury risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q02","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A proximal humerus fracture at the surgical neck is most likely to injure which nerve, and what is the primary clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, producing wrist drop and loss of finger extension distal to the elbow","B":"Suprascapular nerve, producing weakness of both supraspinatus and infraspinatus with external rotation loss","C":"Long thoracic nerve, producing winging of the scapula due to serratus anterior paralysis","D":"Axillary nerve, producing weakness of deltoid contraction and loss of active shoulder abduction","E":"Median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and flexor pollicis longus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve runs around the surgical neck of the humerus in the quadrilateral space, making it vulnerable to injury with proximal humerus fractures at this location. Axillary nerve injury causes weakness or paralysis of the deltoid muscle, with loss of active shoulder abduction. There is also loss of sensation over the lateral deltoid region (regimental badge area). Proximal humerus fractures are especially common in older women, accounting for 4-5% of all fractures. Radial nerve injury is associated with midshaft fractures, not surgical neck. Suprascapular nerve injury occurs with shoulder dislocations or space-occupying lesions at the spinoglenoid notch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical neck humerus fracture = axillary nerve injury = deltoid weakness + lateral shoulder numbness (regimental badge area). COMLEX: Also test for axillary nerve injury after anterior shoulder dislocation, which can also stretch the nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q03","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the initial management of a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture in a 75-year-old woman?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation is indicated because osteoporosis prevents adequate fracture stability","B":"Shoulder arthroplasty is the preferred initial treatment for all proximal humerus fractures in older adults","C":"Percutaneous pinning under fluoroscopy is the standard of care for all proximal humerus fractures","D":"Immediate surgical repair is required for all proximal humerus fractures to prevent axillary nerve injury","E":"Non-surgical management with a sling or coaptation splint and early gentle range-of-motion exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The majority of proximal humerus fractures are non-displaced and can be managed non-surgically with a sling or coaptation splint, followed by early supervised gentle range-of-motion exercises to prevent shoulder stiffness. This is especially true in older adults where surgical risks may outweigh benefits. Displaced and/or comminuted fractures may require surgical intervention — including ORIF or shoulder arthroplasty — depending on patient factors and fracture pattern. Routine surgery for all proximal humerus fractures regardless of displacement is not indicated and may lead to unnecessary morbidity in the elderly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal and midshaft humerus fractures → conservative management with sling. Displaced or comminuted → consider surgery. COMLEX: Sling with early range of motion starting about 2 weeks after injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3), then progressive PT; early motion prevents frozen shoulder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q04","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture in a 5-year-old is defined by which radiographic feature, and what is the standard treatment?","choices":{"A":"Non-displaced fracture with effusion and normal alignment; treated with long arm splinting alone","B":"Fracture displaced greater than 2 mm with anterior humeral line disruption; treated with closed reduction only","C":"Fracture with medial cortical comminution; treated with open reduction and internal fixation immediately","D":"Complete displacement of the distal fragment; treated with urgent closed reduction and percutaneous pinning","E":"Stress fracture of the distal humerus without cortical disruption; treated with observation and activity restriction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gartland Type III supracondylar fractures are completely displaced with no cortical contact between fracture fragments. They require urgent orthopedic consultation for closed reduction and percutaneous Kirschner wire fixation (CRPP) under general anesthesia. The urgency is driven by the risk of neurovascular injury — particularly to the brachial artery and anterior interosseous nerve — and the risk of compartment syndrome. Type I fractures are minimally displaced and treated with splinting; Type II fractures are displaced more than 2 mm with intact posterior cortex and may be treated with casting or CRPP depending on stability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland I = splint; Type II = cast vs CRPP; Type III = CRPP urgently. COMLEX: AIN (anterior interosseous nerve, branch of median) is the most commonly injured nerve; \"OK sign\" inability tests AIN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q05","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in a pediatric supracondylar humerus fracture, and what is the classic clinical test for its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, tested by asking the child to extend the wrist and fingers against resistance","B":"Ulnar nerve, tested by asking the child to abduct and adduct the fingers against resistance","C":"Anterior interosseous nerve, tested by having the child make an \"OK\" sign with thumb and index finger","D":"Axillary nerve, tested by assessing sensation over the lateral deltoid and deltoid contraction strength","E":"Median nerve proper, tested by asking the child to oppose the thumb to the little finger"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a purely motor branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in supracondylar humerus fractures. AIN injury produces inability to flex the terminal phalanx of the thumb and index finger, so the child cannot make a perfect \"OK\" sign — instead producing a pinch pattern with the interphalangeal joints extended rather than flexed. The AIN also innervates the flexor pollicis longus, the flexor digitorum profundus to the index/middle fingers, and pronator quadratus. Radial nerve and ulnar nerve injuries occur less frequently but should also be assessed. Brachial artery injury is the feared vascular complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture — AIN (median branch) most commonly injured; cannot make \"OK\" sign. COMLEX: Also assess radial pulse and brachial artery perfusion; \"pink pulseless hand\" after reduction may be observed if perfused via collaterals.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q06","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Supracondylar fractures account for what proportion of pediatric elbow fractures, and what is the most common mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 20% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a direct blow to the posterior elbow during contact sports","B":"Approximately 80% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a throwing motion with sudden valgus overload at the elbow","C":"Approximately 40% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from axial loading during falls in a flexed elbow position","D":"Approximately 10% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from forced hyperextension during gymnastics tumbling","E":"Approximately 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Supracondylar humerus fractures represent approximately 60% of all pediatric elbow fractures, making them the most common elbow fracture in children. They are most prevalent between ages 2 and 7 and are rare in adults. The predominant mechanism is a fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) with the elbow extended, which occurs in about 70% of cases. The non-dominant extremity is more commonly affected. The hyperextension force drives the olecranon into the olecranon fossa, levering the distal humerus and fracturing the thin supracondylar region of the bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture = 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; peak age 2-7; FOOSH with elbow extended. COMLEX: Supracondylar fracture is rare in adults because the bone remodels and becomes stronger past the pediatric growth phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q07","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which segment of the clavicle is fractured in the majority of clavicle fractures, and what proportion does it represent?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the clavicle near the AC joint, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","B":"Proximal third of the clavicle near the sternoclavicular joint, representing approximately 50% of all fractures","C":"Junction of the middle and distal thirds, representing approximately 40% of all clavicle fractures","D":"Middle third of the clavicle, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","E":"Entire clavicle with comminution, representing the majority of fractures in high-energy trauma patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clavicle fractures occur most commonly in the middle third of the clavicle, accounting for approximately 69% of all cases. The distal third accounts for about 28% and the proximal third for only 2.8%. The middle third is vulnerable because it is the thinnest part of the clavicle and lacks the support of the acromioclavicular and sternoclavicular ligaments that stabilize the distal and proximal thirds, respectively. Middle-third fractures result from direct blows or indirect forces transmitted through the shoulder. Clavicle fractures represent about 2.6% of all fractures and peak in children and young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture location — middle 1/3 = 69%, distal 1/3 = 28%, proximal 1/3 = 2.8%. COMLEX: Middle-third is the most common because it lacks ligamentous support that stabilizes the other thirds.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q08","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for a non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fracture in a 25-year-old with no neurovascular compromise?","choices":{"A":"A sling for comfort and pain control, with progressive return to activity as healing occurs","B":"Surgical ORIF with plate fixation because clavicle fractures have high non-union rates without surgery","C":"Figure-of-eight bandage applied in the ER, which is superior to sling for anatomic reduction","D":"Rigid shoulder immobilizer combined with intravenous antibiotics to prevent periosteal infection","E":"Immediate orthopedic referral for closed reduction under fluoroscopy before beginning conservative care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vast majority of non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fractures are managed conservatively with a sling for comfort and analgesics. A sling is preferred over a figure-of-eight bandage, which does not improve outcomes and causes more patient discomfort. Surgery is not routinely required for non-displaced fractures. Indications for orthopedic referral include open fractures, skin tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening, comminution, or patients who are athletes concerned about cosmetic deformity. Most mid-shaft fractures heal well with conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture = sling; figure-of-eight bandage is not superior to sling. Surgery for open, displaced, comminuted, neurovascular compromise, or shortening. COMLEX: Sling preferred over figure-of-eight — boards commonly test this distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q09","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding in a clavicle fracture is an indication for urgent orthopedic referral rather than conservative management with a sling?","choices":{"A":"Tenderness at the fracture site on palpation, which is expected in all clavicle fractures regardless of severity","B":"Radiographic evidence of a distal third fracture, which is always managed with surgery due to AC joint proximity","C":"A patient age greater than 60 years, because osteoporosis prevents adequate healing without internal fixation","D":"Fracture occurring during a recreational sport activity, which indicates higher energy and mandates surgical evaluation","E":"Tenting of the skin overlying the fracture site, indicating risk of skin breakdown and imminent open fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Skin tenting — where the sharp fracture end pushes against the overlying skin from inside — indicates imminent risk of skin penetration and conversion to an open fracture, which carries infection risk and requires urgent orthopedic consultation. Other indications for referral include open fractures, neurovascular compromise (including brachial plexus and subclavian vessel injury), complete displacement, shortening, comminution, and in athletes where cosmetic outcome or return-to-sport timeline matters. Routine mid-shaft tenderness, patient age, and recreational mechanism do not automatically mandate surgery. Distal-third fractures with AC ligament disruption may require surgery but are not automatically referred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle ortho referral = open fractures, tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening, comminution. COMLEX: Neurovascular structures at risk include the brachial plexus and subclavian artery/vein deep to the clavicle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q10","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, representing what proportion of all childhood fractures?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10-15% of all childhood fractures, with the proximal third being the most frequent location","B":"Approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures, with the distal third being the most frequent location","C":"Approximately 20-25% of all childhood fractures, with the midshaft being the most frequent location","D":"Approximately 60-70% of all childhood fractures, with the entire forearm equally distributed by region","E":"Approximately 5% of all childhood fractures, with forearm fractures less common than femur or tibia fractures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, accounting for approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures. The distal third of the forearm is the most commonly fractured segment, responsible for approximately 75% of all forearm fractures in children and 20-25% of all pediatric fractures. The peak age is 10-14 years, when a relative mismatch between rapidly increasing body mass and bone strength creates vulnerability during the adolescent growth spurt. Falls on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) are the predominant mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fractures = most common childhood fracture (40-50%); distal 1/3 most common (75% of forearm); peak age 10-14. COMLEX: Without plausible mechanism in a child, forearm fracture warrants consideration of non-accidental trauma (child abuse).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q11","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures of childhood and peak between ages 10 and 14. According to the course, how does the typical fracture pattern change with age in children?","choices":{"A":"Under age 10, physeal separations predominate; over age 10, buckle and greenstick fractures predominate","B":"Under age 10, complete transverse fractures predominate; over age 10, plastic bowing without fracture predominates","C":"Fracture pattern does not change with age, because the distal third is involved in 75% of cases at every age","D":"Under age 10, buckle and greenstick fractures predominate; over age 10, physeal separations become typical","E":"Under age 10, Monteggia injuries predominate; over age 10, Galeazzi injuries predominate as the bone matures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that forearm fractures make up 40 to 50% of childhood fractures, that the distal third accounts for 75% of forearm fractures, and that the peak age is 10 to 14 because body mass outpaces bone strength. Under age 10 the typical patterns are buckle (torus) and greenstick fractures of the more compliant bone; over age 10 physeal separations appear, and adolescent fractures are linked to the growth spurt. Reversing the age groups contradicts the course. Complete transverse fractures and plastic bowing are not the age patterns listed. The distal-third predominance is true but does not mean the pattern is fixed. Monteggia and Galeazzi injuries are not described as age-defined patterns. A fracture without a plausible history warrants consideration of child abuse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric bone (more collagen, thicker periosteum) → torus and greenstick fractures; growth plate is the weak link near maturity → physeal (Salter-Harris) injuries. COMLEX: Most forearm fractures follow a FOOSH; consider abuse when the history does not fit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Salter-Harris, greenstick, and torus fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q12","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Monteggia fracture involves which combination of injuries, and what distinguishes it from a Galeazzi fracture?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia = proximal ulna fracture with radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal radius fracture with distal radioulnar joint dislocation","B":"Monteggia = distal radius fracture with volar angulation; Galeazzi = distal ulna fracture with radial head subluxation","C":"Monteggia = midshaft radius fracture with interosseous membrane rupture; Galeazzi = ulna fracture with olecranon avulsion","D":"Monteggia = both-bone forearm fracture with posterior interosseous nerve injury; Galeazzi = isolated radius fracture with DRUJ stability","E":"Monteggia = radial head fracture with ulnar styloid avulsion; Galeazzi = scaphoid fracture with radial styloid impaction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head at the radiocapitellar joint. A Galeazzi fracture is a fracture of the distal radius combined with disruption of the distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ). A useful mnemonic is \"MUGR\" — Monteggia = Ulna fracture, Galeazzi = Radius fracture. Both are considered unstable injury patterns that typically require surgical fixation. Monteggia fractures are associated with radial nerve (posterior interosseous nerve) injury due to the radial head dislocation. Both injuries can occur in children and adults and should not be confused with isolated radial or ulnar shaft fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna + radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. Mnemonic: MUG (Monteggia-Ulna) + GR (Galeazzi-Radius). COMLEX: Both require surgery; isolated ulna or radius shaft fractures in adults also usually require fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q13","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of the scaphoid bone makes it uniquely prone to avascular necrosis (AVN) following a fracture?","choices":{"A":"The scaphoid is the densest carpal bone, requiring greater forces to fracture and causing more vascular disruption","B":"The scaphoid spans two carpal rows, making it subject to greater shear forces that disrupt all entering blood vessels simultaneously","C":"The scaphoid is entirely covered by articular cartilage except at the tubercle, leaving minimal periosteal vascularity","D":"The scaphoid waist is avascular in all individuals due to developmental absence of nutrient vessels in this region","E":"The scaphoid receives a retrograde blood supply from distal to proximal, so the proximal pole depends on distal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The scaphoid receives its primary blood supply through branches of the radial artery that enter distally at the dorsal ridge and waist, traveling proximally in a retrograde fashion to supply the proximal pole. A fracture through the scaphoid waist or proximal pole disrupts this blood supply to the proximal fragment, which has no independent arterial supply. This makes the proximal pole susceptible to avascular necrosis if blood flow is not restored. This also explains why proximal pole fractures carry the highest AVN risk and require the longest immobilization. Initial radiographs are often negative because the fracture line is subtle, and MRI is the most sensitive early imaging modality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = retrograde blood supply → proximal pole AVN risk after waist/proximal fracture. Initial X-rays often negative — MRI is gold standard. COMLEX: Anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH = presumed scaphoid fracture; treat as fracture even with negative X-ray.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q14","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Boxer's fracture involves a fracture of which bone and at which location?","choices":{"A":"The neck of the fifth metacarpal, typically resulting from striking a hard surface with a clenched fist","B":"The shaft of the fourth metacarpal, resulting from direct axial loading during a punch with the ring finger knuckle","C":"The head of the second metacarpal, resulting from a hyperextension injury of the index MCP joint","D":"The base of the fifth metacarpal, resulting from an inversion injury of the ulnar wrist with associated hamate impaction","E":"The proximal phalanx of the fifth finger, resulting from a valgus stress fracture at the small finger MCP joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture is a fracture of the neck of the fifth metacarpal, which occurs when a clenched fist strikes a hard surface. The fracture produces volar angulation of the metacarpal head. It is one of the most common metacarpal fractures in adults. In clinical practice, the acceptable angulation for the small finger metacarpal is 50 to 60 degrees (30 to 40 degrees for the ring finger; SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), given the fifth metacarpal's mobility at the carpometacarpal joint, which compensates for some residual angulation. Neurovascular assessment and rotational alignment must be checked. Malrotation, unlike angulation, is not compensated and requires reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck, volar angulation; conservative if angulation is within 50-60 degrees (CDT Orthopedics). COMLEX: Always assess rotational alignment — malrotation (scissors deformity with fingers overlapping on flexion) requires reduction regardless of angulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q15","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A young athlete presents with wrist pain after a FOOSH. Radiographs are negative but anatomic snuffbox tenderness is present. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Discharge with a wrist brace and return precautions because negative radiographs effectively exclude scaphoid fracture","B":"Treat as a presumed scaphoid fracture with thumb spica splinting and arrange MRI or CT to confirm the diagnosis","C":"Immobilize and repeat radiographs in 5-7 days as the fracture line will invariably become visible by then on plain film","D":"Refer immediately to hand surgery for urgent ORIF because scaphoid fractures always require surgical fixation","E":"Perform immediate wrist aspiration to evaluate for hemarthrosis, which confirms intraarticular carpal bone injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Scaphoid fractures are notoriously missed on initial radiographs — the fracture line can be occult on the first films, which is why repeat X-rays 7-10 days after injury were the traditional approach (Practical Office Orthopedics, SDL 9 reading). However, anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH is the classic clinical sign of a scaphoid fracture and should prompt immediate empiric treatment. The standard approach is to apply a thumb spica splint and obtain advanced imaging — MRI is the gold standard for early diagnosis, with CT as an alternative. Simply discharging the patient or waiting for repeat films risks delayed diagnosis, AVN, and nonunion. Not all scaphoid fractures require surgery — undisplaced waist fractures can often be managed conservatively with casting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH; X-ray often negative; MRI gold standard. Treat empirically with thumb spica even if X-ray negative. COMLEX: Delayed diagnosis leads to nonunion and AVN of proximal pole — key complication tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q16","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman falls onto her right shoulder and holds the arm against her side. There is swelling and tenderness over the proximal humerus, and neurovascular examination is intact. Which radiographic series does the course recommend for this suspected fracture?","choices":{"A":"A shoulder AP and an axillary view, with examination and imaging above and below the suspected fracture site","B":"A humerus AP, lateral, and oblique series, the views listed for midshaft and supracondylar humerus fractures","C":"A single chest radiograph, since the proximal humerus is visible and further views add radiation without benefit","D":"No radiographs before MRI, because MRI is the first-line test for proximal humerus fractures in older adults","E":"Bilateral stress views with weights in both hands, to compare the injured humerus with the uninjured side"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists the radiographs by fracture location: proximal humerus fractures need a shoulder AP and an axillary view, while midshaft and supracondylar fractures need humerus AP, lateral, and oblique views. It adds that, out of caution, the area above and below the suspected fracture should always be examined and imaged. Proximal humerus fractures are most prevalent at ages 73 to 78 and in women. The humerus AP, lateral, and oblique series is the set for midshaft and supracondylar injuries. A chest film is not an adequate shoulder series. MRI is not the first test. Weighted stress views are used for AC joint questions, not for a suspected humerus fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proximal humerus fracture = 3rd most common fracture in older adults (osteoporosis, FOOSH); surgical neck → axillary nerve. COMLEX: Always obtain two orthogonal views (for the shoulder, AP + axillary) and image the joints above and below.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — axillary view in glenohumeral dislocation"},{"id":"sdl16_q17","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man falls while snowboarding and has a closed, nondisplaced transverse fracture of the midshaft of the left humerus. Wrist and finger extension are intact, sensation is normal, and the skin is intact. According to the course, how are most fractures like this managed?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation within 24 hours, since every midshaft humerus fracture in a young adult is unstable","B":"Nonsurgical management, since most midshaft fractures are nondisplaced; surgery is considered if displaced or comminuted","C":"Exploration of the radial nerve at the spiral groove before splinting, since nerve injury must be excluded surgically","D":"Immediate intramedullary nailing so that early weight bearing can begin, since prolonged splinting causes nonunion","E":"No immobilization, since the humerus is non-weight-bearing and nondisplaced shaft fractures heal without support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that the majority of proximal and midshaft humerus fractures are nondisplaced and can be managed nonsurgically, with surgery considered for displaced or comminuted fractures. Midshaft fractures make up about 2% of all fractures and are bimodal (men in their 20s, women in their 60s). Routine ORIF for every young adult is not the course approach. With normal wrist and finger extension, there is no radial nerve deficit to explore. Immediate nailing is not indicated for a nondisplaced fracture. Fractures still need protection from displacement until they heal, so leaving the arm unsupported is not appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus fracture → radial nerve in the spiral groove (wrist drop, dorsal hand numbness). Nondisplaced → splint/brace; displaced or comminuted → surgical consult. COMLEX: Document radial nerve function before and after any manipulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — fracture nomenclature: displacement and comminution"},{"id":"sdl16_q18","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture presents for a 2-week follow-up. He has been in a sling and is asking when he should start moving the shoulder. He has no neurovascular deficits. Which of the following best describes the role of early motion in management?","choices":{"A":"All shoulder motion must be restricted until radiographic healing is complete at 8-12 weeks to prevent displacement","B":"Active range-of-motion exercises against resistance should begin immediately to prevent rotator cuff atrophy","C":"Gentle pendulum exercises begun about 2 weeks after injury reduce shoulder stiffness without harming healing","D":"Surgery should be performed within 2 weeks for any proximal humerus fracture in patients over 65 to ensure optimal recovery","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the glenohumeral joint at 2 weeks reduces inflammation and accelerates return to function"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For non-displaced proximal humerus fractures managed conservatively, early supervised gentle range of motion such as pendulum exercises starts about 2 weeks after the injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3). This is critical to prevent shoulder stiffness and adhesive capsulitis, which are significant complications that can cause long-term functional impairment. Pendulum exercises use gravity to provide gentle traction and rotation of the humeral head within the glenoid. Active assisted range-of-motion exercises are added progressively over the following weeks. Prolonged complete immobilization until radiographic healing predictably results in stiffness. Surgical intervention is not indicated for non-displaced fractures in otherwise healthy patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal humerus fracture → sling + early pendulum exercises starting about 2 weeks after injury → progressive PT. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis is a known complication of prolonged immobilization of the shoulder — early motion is key to prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis): prolonged immobilization of the shoulder after proximal humerus fracture is a recognized precipitating cause of secondary adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl16_q19","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to the ED after falling from a playground structure. She is guarding her right elbow, which is swollen and markedly bruised. Radiographs show a Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture with complete displacement. Her radial pulse is absent but the hand appears pink and warm with capillary refill of 2 seconds. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Immediate vascular surgery consultation and brachial artery exploration before any fracture reduction is attempted","B":"Urgent orthopedic closed reduction and percutaneous pinning; a perfused but pulseless hand may then be observed","C":"Apply a long arm cast in flexion immediately and reassess vascular status after 6 hours of observation","D":"Discharge home with sling and urgent outpatient orthopedic follow-up within 48 hours for reduction planning","E":"Perform bedside closed reduction under procedural sedation and reassess pulse before orthopedic consultation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A \"pink pulseless hand\" after a supracondylar fracture — where the hand is perfused via collateral circulation (anterior interosseous and ulnar collateral vessels) despite absent radial pulse — is a recognized entity that does not require emergency vascular surgery before fracture reduction. The standard approach is urgent orthopedic consultation for CRPP, after which the radial pulse typically returns as the brachial artery kinking from fracture displacement is relieved. If the hand is cold, pale, and pulseless after reduction, then vascular surgery exploration is indicated for brachial artery injury. This is an emergency; casting without reduction or discharge is inappropriate for a Type III fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pink pulseless hand in supracondylar fracture = urgent CRPP; pulse often returns after reduction. Cold, pale, pulseless hand post-reduction = vascular surgery. COMLEX: Brachial artery is at risk in Type III; compartment syndrome is the feared complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: neurovascular anatomy of the elbow is critical for understanding nerve and vessel injury patterns in pediatric supracondylar fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q20","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy with a Gartland Type I supracondylar fracture has a visible elbow effusion on X-ray but no displacement and normal anterior humeral line. He is otherwise neurovascularly intact. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Closed reduction and percutaneous pinning because all supracondylar fractures in children require operative fixation","B":"Velpeau bandage alone for 6 weeks with no follow-up radiographs because Type I fractures never displace","C":"Observation only with no immobilization because joint effusion without cortical disruption does not require treatment","D":"Open reduction and internal fixation to restore the anterior humeral line and prevent future elbow varus deformity","E":"Long arm posterior splint or cast for about 4 weeks, with close radiographic follow-up to confirm alignment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gartland Type I supracondylar fractures are non-displaced (displaced less than 2 mm) with an elbow effusion and normal elbow alignment. They are managed conservatively with a long arm posterior splint or cast; most surgeons immobilize pediatric elbow fractures for about 4 weeks (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). Surgery is not indicated for Type I fractures in the absence of neurovascular compromise or instability. Close radiographic follow-up is appropriate to ensure the fracture does not displace during healing. Observation without immobilization is inadequate. Cubitus varus (gunstock deformity) is a late complication of malunion, most commonly seen after Type III fractures managed with inadequate reduction, not Type I fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland Type I (non-displaced) = long arm cast about 4 weeks; no surgery. Type II = CRPP vs cast depending on stability; Type III = urgent CRPP. COMLEX: Cubitus varus is the most common late complication of supracondylar fractures treated with inadequate reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy and cubital tunnel are directly involved in elbow fracture deformity complications including progressive ulnar nerve neuropathy from cubitus varus"},{"id":"sdl16_q21","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy fell from his bicycle 2 hours ago and was found to have a supracondylar fracture. He is now developing increasing elbow pain out of proportion to the injury, forearm tenseness, and paresthesias in the fingers. Passive finger extension markedly worsens his pain. What is the most likely developing complication?","choices":{"A":"Reflex sympathetic dystrophy of the forearm, which presents with burning pain and is managed with nerve blocks","B":"Heterotopic ossification of the elbow, which presents with progressive stiffness and is managed with NSAIDs","C":"Acute compartment syndrome of the forearm, requiring emergent fasciotomy to prevent permanent ischemic contracture","D":"Osteomyelitis of the distal humerus from a hairline open fracture, managed with IV antibiotics and debridement","E":"Volkmann ischemic contracture that has already occurred and is irreversible without tendon lengthening surgery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The scenario describes the classic presentation of developing acute compartment syndrome in a child with a supracondylar fracture: pain out of proportion, forearm tenseness, and pain with passive stretch of the fingers (the most reliable early sign). Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture most commonly affects the volar forearm compartment and is a surgical emergency requiring fasciotomy. If untreated, ischemia progresses to Volkmann ischemic contracture — a devastating permanent flexion deformity of the wrist and fingers from irreversible muscle fibrosis. This is why supracondylar fractures with neurovascular compromise require emergency consultation rather than elective management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture = pain with passive stretch, tenseness, paresthesias → emergent fasciotomy. Delayed = Volkmann contracture. COMLEX: Compartment syndrome and neurovascular compromise are the two emergency ortho consult indications for supracondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: compartment pressure in the forearm can simultaneously compress ulnar, median, and radial nerves — forearm fasciotomy must release all compartments"},{"id":"sdl16_q22","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male cyclist is thrown over his handlebars and lands on his right shoulder. He presents with right shoulder pain, a visible step-off deformity at the mid-clavicle, and crepitus on palpation. Radiographs reveal a displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture. His radial pulse is intact and he has no sensory deficits. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a figure-of-eight bandage immediately in the ER to reduce the fracture and hold the shoulder in retraction","B":"Sling for comfort with analgesics and orthopedic referral for evaluation of surgical candidacy given displacement","C":"Immediate ORIF with plate fixation is required for all displaced clavicle fractures in active young males","D":"Admit for IV antibiotics because the crepitus indicates an occult open fracture requiring operative debridement","E":"Perform closed reduction under procedural sedation in the emergency department and cast the shoulder for 6 weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Displaced mid-shaft clavicle fractures in young active patients may benefit from surgical fixation compared to conservative management, with evidence suggesting lower nonunion rates and faster return to function. However, the initial management is a sling for comfort and pain control, followed by orthopedic evaluation to determine whether surgical fixation (ORIF with plate or intramedullary pin) is appropriate given patient factors such as fracture displacement, shortening, activity level, and occupation. A figure-of-eight bandage does not effectively reduce displaced clavicle fractures and adds patient discomfort without benefit. IV antibiotics are not indicated for a closed fracture. Closed reduction cannot be maintained for clavicle fractures in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced mid-shaft clavicle in young active patient = sling + orthopedic referral for surgical consideration; not all displaced fractures require surgery. COMLEX: Referral indications include shortening, complete displacement, comminution, neurovascular compromise, or athlete/cosmetic concern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures are distinguished from AC joint separation and shoulder dislocation by palpation at the clavicular shaft rather than AC joint or glenohumeral joint"},{"id":"sdl16_q23","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old soccer player sustains a clavicle fracture during a collision. On exam, a sharp fracture fragment is visibly tenting the overlying skin, and the patient has paresthesias along the medial forearm and ulnar two fingers. What neurovascular structures are most at risk in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The axillary nerve and circumflex humeral vessels, which are at risk because of their proximity to the shoulder joint capsule","B":"The radial nerve and radial artery, which pass through the radial groove of the humerus at risk from the clavicular spike","C":"The brachial plexus and subclavian vessels, which run deep to the clavicle between it and the first rib","D":"The phrenic nerve and internal thoracic artery, which run on the anterior surface of the scalene muscles medially","E":"The suprascapular nerve and artery, which cross the superior border of the scapula through the suprascapular notch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The brachial plexus and subclavian artery and vein run in the costoclavicular space, passing between the clavicle superiorly and the first rib inferiorly. Severely displaced clavicle fractures with skin tenting can impale or compress these structures. Medial forearm and ulnar two-finger paresthesias suggest involvement of the lower trunk or medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8-T1 distribution). Subclavian vein injury can produce significant hemorrhage. These findings represent emergency indications for orthopedic consultation and vascular surgery involvement. The axillary nerve is at risk in proximal humerus fractures and shoulder dislocations, not mid-shaft clavicle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture neurovascular risk = brachial plexus and subclavian vessels in costoclavicular space. Skin tenting = imminent open fracture = emergent ortho referral. COMLEX: Lower trunk brachial plexus (C8-T1) = medial forearm and hand paresthesias.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle and shoulder anatomy is foundational to identifying neurovascular structures at risk with proximal upper extremity injuries"},{"id":"sdl16_q24","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male baseball player falls and fractures his left clavicle. He is right-hand dominant, his left arm is in a sling, and the fracture is not displaced. His parents ask when he can return to baseball. Which factor is most important in determining return-to-sport timeline?","choices":{"A":"His dominant hand is unaffected, so he may return to batting and pitching within 2 weeks of the injury","B":"He must wait exactly 12 months before any upper extremity sport because clavicle fractures have very high reinjury rates","C":"Pain resolution alone at 3 weeks is sufficient to clear him for full return to pitching and throwing sports","D":"Radiographic and clinical evidence of union on follow-up X-rays, with full pain-free motion and restored strength","E":"Surgical fixation is mandatory before return to any contact or overhead sport regardless of fracture displacement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic evidence of healing combined with full pain-free range of motion and restoration of strength. A typical plan is 4 weeks in a sling and 4-6 weeks out of it before X-rays confirm healing; weight training may start at 3 months, and contact sports wait until the fracture has healed and 3-6 months have passed (Practical Office Orthopedics, SDL 9 reading). Pain resolution alone does not indicate sufficient structural healing. The non-dominant arm being injured does not permit early return for sports requiring two-arm function or physical contact that could displace the fracture. While some athletes with displaced fractures in the dominant arm may be candidates for surgical fixation to expedite return to sport, this is an individualized decision and not mandatory for all athletes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic union and full painless ROM; healing usually confirmed on X-ray 8-10 weeks after injury; weight training at 3 months; contact sport after healing and 3-6 months (Practical Office Orthopedics). COMLEX: Athletes may request surgery to shorten return-to-sport timeline — this is an acceptable indication for consideration of ORIF in high-demand athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: sport-specific return-to-play protocols for shoulder girdle injuries require clinical and radiographic assessment before full clearance"},{"id":"sdl16_q25","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl falls off a trampoline onto her outstretched left hand. Radiographs show a buckle (torus) fracture of the distal radius without physeal involvement. She has full neurovascular function and minimal swelling. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast with the elbow at 90 degrees for 6-8 weeks because buckle fractures involve the physis and risk growth arrest","B":"Immediate orthopedic referral for percutaneous pinning because all forearm fractures in children under 10 require fixation","C":"Short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks, with activity restriction and return precautions","D":"No immobilization is needed; buckle fractures are radiographic findings only and do not require treatment","E":"Open reduction under general anesthesia because the periosteum in children is too thick for closed treatment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Buckle (torus) fractures of the distal radius are inherently stable injuries — the compressed cortex provides structural integrity and the fracture does not significantly displace. They are treated with a short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks. Long arm casting with elbow flexion is unnecessary as the fracture is stable and the elbow is uninvolved. Surgery is not indicated. Importantly, buckle fractures do not involve the physis (growth plate) and therefore do not carry growth arrest risk — that concern applies to Salter-Harris fractures. A removable splint is increasingly preferred over a cast in stable buckle fractures because it improves patient comfort and hygiene without compromising outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buckle fracture = stable; short arm splint 3-4 weeks. Greenstick = incomplete cortical disruption, may require gentle manipulation if angulated. Salter-Harris = physeal fracture, growth arrest risk. COMLEX: Distinguish buckle (stable, no physis) from Salter-Harris (physis involved) on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle fractures are unique to children due to the relative plasticity and porosity of pediatric cortical bone under compressive loads"},{"id":"sdl16_q26","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents after a FOOSH. Radiographs show a fracture through the proximal ulna shaft with anterior dislocation of the radial head at the elbow. Which of the following best describes this injury and its required management?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia fracture; requires ORIF of the ulna fracture, which reliably reduces the radial head if performed anatomically","B":"Galeazzi fracture; requires ORIF of the radius with closed reduction of the distal radioulnar joint under fluoroscopy","C":"Essex-Lopresti injury; requires reduction of the radial head and forearm interosseous membrane repair under general anesthesia","D":"Isolated radial head dislocation without associated fracture; requires closed reduction and posterior interosseous nerve exploration","E":"Both-bone forearm fracture with secondary radial head subluxation; requires ORIF of both radius and ulna simultaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is defined as a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head. In children, it is often managed with closed reduction under general anesthesia; in adults, ORIF of the ulna is typically required. The key principle is that anatomic fixation of the ulna fracture is the primary treatment — once the ulna is restored to proper length and alignment, the radial head typically reduces spontaneously. Failure to recognize the radial head dislocation and treating it as an isolated ulna fracture is a classic missed diagnosis. The posterior interosseous nerve (branch of radial nerve) can be injured with the radial head dislocation. A Galeazzi fracture involves the distal radius and DRUJ, not the proximal ulna.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna fracture + radial head dislocation; ORIF ulna reduces radial head. Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. COMLEX: Posterior interosseous nerve at risk with Monteggia; always image the elbow with forearm fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: Monteggia fractures can be treated with closed reduction in children but typically require ORIF in adults because of differences in bone healing capacity"},{"id":"sdl16_q27","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with a distal forearm fracture after falling during basketball. His parents ask why this age group is so prone to forearm fractures. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Teenagers participate in higher-risk sports than younger children, so their fracture rate reflects behavioral rather than biological factors","B":"The distal radius physis closes at age 12-14 in males, leaving a biomechanically weak zone that is uniquely vulnerable until full fusion","C":"Bone mineral density reaches its lifetime peak at age 10-14, making the cortex stiff and brittle so that ordinary falls fracture it","D":"The cartilaginous epiphysis at age 13 is softer than adult bone and more susceptible to shear fractures during torsional loading","E":"During the growth spurt, body mass and bone length rise faster than bone strength and mineral content, causing relative skeletal fragility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The peak incidence of forearm fractures at age 10-14 coincides with the adolescent growth spurt (course slides: \"Body mass vs bone strength\"), during which bone length increases rapidly through endochondral ossification at the growth plates. This rapid longitudinal growth temporarily outpaces the accumulation of bone mineral density, creating a period of relative skeletal fragility sometimes called the \"calcium deficit\" of adolescence. The distal radius metaphysis is particularly vulnerable. While behavioral factors (increased athletic activity) contribute, the biological timing of the peak fracture incidence — tracking closely with peak height velocity — demonstrates a true skeletal vulnerability rather than purely behavioral causation. The physis in this age group is still open and is a site of susceptibility for Salter-Harris fractures. Peak bone mass is not reached until early adulthood, so the cortex is not at its stiffest at this age.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fracture peak age 10-14 = growth spurt with bone mass lagging bone length growth. COMLEX: Body mass vs bone strength imbalance during puberty explains the peak; the growth plate also remains open and is a Salter-Harris fracture risk zone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: the adolescent growth spurt creates a transient period of reduced bone mineral density relative to body mass, increasing forearm fracture risk"},{"id":"sdl16_q28","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man treated in a cast for a distal radius fracture 8 months ago now notices a firm nodule in the palm at the base of the ring finger, and the finger is starting to resist full extension. There is no triggering. He asks whether the fracture caused it. Which statement best reflects the course?","choices":{"A":"This is a flexor tendon rupture caused by fracture fragments, so the finger cannot extend and needs urgent repair","B":"This is Dupuytren contracture, a fibrosis of the palmar fascia; a direct link to the fracture is not established","C":"This is complex regional pain syndrome, which always follows wrist fractures and causes palmar nodules","D":"This is a ganglion cyst from the healing fracture, which transilluminates and resolves after aspiration","E":"This is trigger finger at the A1 pulley, caused by the cast pressing on the flexor sheath during healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists Dupuytren contracture among complications discussed with hand and wrist fractures: its pathology is fibrosis of the palmar fascia, there is no direct link to prior wrist or hand fracture, it is suspected in a small fraction of fracture patients, and some fracture patients develop a nodule. A palmar nodule with a ring finger that resists full extension and no triggering fits Dupuytren contracture. A flexor tendon rupture would cause loss of active flexion, not a contracture. CRPS presents with pain, swelling, and vasomotor changes, not a palmar cord, and does not always follow fractures. A ganglion is a fluid-filled cyst, not a fascial contracture. Trigger finger causes catching and locking at the A1 pulley.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dupuytren contracture = fibroblastic proliferation and thickening of the superficial palmar fascia, typically at the base of the ring and little fingers; most often men over 50 of Northern European descent (FA). COMLEX: Distinguish from trigger finger (catching at the A1 pulley) and tendon rupture (no active motion).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — trigger finger and A1 pulley stenosis"},{"id":"sdl16_q29","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with osteoporosis falls on her outstretched right hand. She presents with wrist pain, dorsal swelling, and a visible \"dinner fork\" deformity. Radiographs show a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement. What is the name of this fracture pattern and what is the deformity caused by?","choices":{"A":"Colles fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates and displaces dorsally, creating a dorsal prominence resembling a dinner fork","B":"Smith fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates volarly, creating a volar prominence resembling a reversed dinner fork","C":"Barton fracture, in which an intraarticular rim of the distal radius fractures and displaces with the carpus into the wrist joint","D":"Monteggia fracture, in which the distal radius fractures with concomitant disruption of the proximal radioulnar joint","E":"Chauffeur fracture, in which the radial styloid avulses from a direct blow transmitted through the scaphoid into the radial styloid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Colles fracture is a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement of the distal fragment, producing the classic \"dinner fork\" deformity — a dorsal prominence just proximal to the wrist on lateral profile. It is the most common fracture in osteoporotic women who fall on an outstretched hand. Management depends on displacement and patient factors. Non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures can be treated with a short arm cast; displaced fractures require closed reduction with or without surgical fixation depending on stability. A Smith fracture is the reverse — volar angulation of the distal radius fragment after a fall onto a flexed wrist. A Barton fracture is intraarticular and associated with carpal subluxation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colles = dorsal angulation = dinner fork = FOOSH in osteoporotic patient. Smith = volar angulation = \"garden spade\" = fall on flexed wrist. COMLEX: Most common fracture in osteoporotic women; always assess for median nerve injury (acute CTS complication).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: distal radius fractures (Colles) can acutely compress the median nerve within the carpal tunnel, presenting with acute carpal tunnel syndrome requiring urgent decompression"},{"id":"sdl16_q30","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old man punches a wall during an argument. He presents with pain, swelling, and tenderness at the ulnar dorsum of the right hand. Radiographs show a fifth metacarpal neck fracture with 35 degrees of volar angulation. He has full extension of all fingers and no rotational deformity on exam. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ORIF because any angulation greater than 30 degrees in a metacarpal neck fracture mandates fixation","B":"No reduction needed; immobilize in an ulnar gutter splint, since 35 degrees is acceptable for this fracture","C":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because the fifth metacarpal neck fracture is treated identically to a scaphoid fracture","D":"Closed reduction to anatomic (0 degree) alignment, which is mandatory before any splinting of a boxer's fracture","E":"Buddy taping alone is sufficient because metacarpal neck fractures heal without immobilization in young adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture (5th metacarpal neck fracture with volar angulation) at 35 degrees may be treated conservatively — the acceptable angulation for the small finger is 50 to 60 degrees (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), because the CMC joint of the small finger provides compensatory mobility. At 35 degrees with no rotational deformity and full MCP extension, this fracture falls within the acceptable range and can be managed with splinting in an ulnar gutter splint. Rotational deformity (scissoring of the fingers with flexion) and pseudoclawing are not acceptable and mandate reduction regardless of angulation. ORIF is reserved for fractures with significant rotational malrotation, irreducible angulation, or intraarticular extension. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute displaced fractures, and closed reduction to anatomic alignment is unnecessary when angulation is already within the acceptable range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck; acceptable angulation up to 50-60 degrees (CDT Orthopedics); rotational deformity is never acceptable. COMLEX: Ulnar gutter splint with the MCP joints flexed (intrinsic plus position) prevents flexion contracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: hand anatomy and the metacarpal/phalangeal relationship is foundational to understanding safe splinting positions that prevent intrinsic muscle contracture during immobilization"}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"Running Footwear & Biomechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which individual is credited with introducing spikes to running footwear in 1852, a design innovation that dramatically improved athletic traction on natural surfaces?","choices":{"A":"Adolf Dassler, who developed differentiated sprint and endurance shoe designs for competitive athletes in the 1920s","B":"Wait Webster, whose vulcanized rubber and leather sole design in 1832 laid the foundation for modern athletic footwear","C":"Bill Bowerman, who collaborated with Phil Knight in the early 1960s to develop what became the Nike brand","D":"Joseph William Foster, whose spiked shoes became the predecessor to what eventually became the Reebok brand","E":"Kihachiro Onitsuka, who founded the post-WWII Japanese athletic shoe company that eventually became Asics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Joseph William Foster introduced spiked running shoes in 1852, providing athletes with enhanced traction on grass and natural running surfaces. Foster's innovation was a key milestone in the evolution of performance footwear. Adolf Dassler (1920) later differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe designs and famously supplied shoes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Wait Webster (1832) introduced rubber soles. Onitsuka (1949) founded what became Asics in post-WWII Japan. Bowerman and Knight (early 1960s) founded Nike.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield — footwear history is contextual background. COMLEX: Understand how the athletic footwear industry grew from biomechanical innovation rather than evidence-based medicine, setting the stage for marketing-science gaps explored in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q02","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the history of running footwear, which company first marketed its shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" representing the earliest attempt to use clinical/scientific language to sell athletic footwear?","choices":{"A":"Nike, founded in the early 1960s by Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight, whose early waffle-sole design was derived from Bowerman's biomechanical experiments at the University of Oregon","B":"Adidas, developed by Adolf Dassler, who differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe construction and provided shoes to athletes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics","C":"Asics, originally Onitsuka Tiger, which incorporated research from Japanese sports scientists after its founding in 1949 in post-WWII Japan","D":"Reebok, whose predecessor company Joseph William Foster pioneered spiked running shoes in 1852 and later adopted modern materials science in their manufacturing processes","E":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first to market running shoes with a \"scientifically tested\" claim, pioneering the commercial use of science-based language in footwear marketing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first company to market running shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" introducing science-based language to athletic footwear marketing. This milestone is historically significant because it marked the beginning of the conflation of commercial claims with scientific validation — a tension that the course explores at length. Nike (early 1960s) used biomechanical inspiration (waffle sole) but later marketing. Adidas/Dassler and Asics/Onitsuka preceded this with performance designs but not explicit scientific testing claims.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical context. COMLEX: The 1962 New Balance claim represents the beginning of a long-standing gap between marketing language and evidence-based footwear science, which directly informs the critical appraisal framework in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q03","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 contribution to athletic footwear was notable because he was the first to do which of the following in footwear design?","choices":{"A":"Introduce vulcanized rubber outsoles to replace leather soles, dramatically improving durability and traction on wet playing surfaces","B":"Incorporate spikes into running shoes for use on natural grass and track surfaces, improving athlete traction during competitive events","C":"Differentiate shoe construction based on athletic event type, designing separate shoe models optimized for sprinting versus endurance running","D":"Market shoes using claims of scientific testing, setting the commercial precedent for evidence-based language in athletic footwear","E":"Develop the first canvas and rubber shoe marketed for general athletic use, eventually evolving into what became the Keds brand"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 innovation was the differentiation of shoe design by athletic event — creating distinct construction features for sprinting versus endurance running. This was a fundamental departure from the one-shoe-fits-all approach. Dassler's shoes were famously worn at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Vulcanized rubber (Goodyear, 1892), spikes (Foster, 1852), \"scientifically tested\" claims (New Balance, 1962), and canvas/rubber shoes (Goodyear/Keds, 1892) were all separate milestones by other companies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Dassler differentiation principle (sprint vs endurance) foreshadows modern footwear biomechanics debates about whether any shoe design truly optimizes performance for a given runner or running style.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q04","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A patient who runs 25 miles per week asks whether switching to a more heavily cushioned \"traditional\" running shoe will reduce the impact forces on their joints. Based on the biomechanical evidence, which response is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Heavily cushioned traditional running shoes actually INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length and decreasing stride frequency, producing greater ground reaction forces","B":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce ground reaction forces by absorbing impact energy through the midsole, directly lowering the mechanical load transmitted to the tibia and knee","C":"Heavily cushioned shoes have a neutral effect on impact forces because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any shoe type, maintaining a constant ground reaction force regardless of cushion","D":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces specifically on hard surfaces like asphalt, though they have no effect on softer surfaces where ground reaction forces are inherently lower","E":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces only in runners over age 50, because natural shock-absorbing capacity declines with age and supplemental cushioning compensates for this deficit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Counterintuitively, modern \"traditional\" heavily cushioned running shoes INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length, increasing contact times, and decreasing stride frequency — all of which elevate ground reaction forces (GRF). Hard surfaces do NOT increase GRF independently, and \"absorption\" within the shoe does NOT decrease GRF. Furthermore, more cushioning leads to less tactile sensation from the ground, which reduces the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes (Robbins phenomenon), paradoxically increasing injury risk. Natural shock-absorbing capacity does decrease with age, but cushioned shoes do not reliably compensate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a conceptually important counterintuitive finding. COMLEX: The Robbins phenomenon (cushioning → reduced tactile feedback → larger actual impact) is clinically relevant when counseling runners; more cushion ≠ less injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q05","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A runner with a history of recurrent tibial stress fractures asks whether wearing rigid orthotics will help prevent future fractures. Based on the evidence, which statement most accurately reflects what the research shows?","choices":{"A":"Custom rigid orthotics significantly reduce tibial stress fracture recurrence by redistributing ground reaction forces away from the medial tibial cortex during running","B":"Orthotics reduce tibial stress fracture risk primarily in runners with pes planus by correcting excessive pronation that otherwise transmits greater torsional force to the tibial shaft","C":"Orthotics provide equivalent tibial stress protection compared to barefoot running, making them a cost-effective alternative to gait retraining for stress fracture prevention","D":"Orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures; some evidence suggests semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain rather than reduce it","E":"Semirigid orthotics are protective against tibial stress fractures in military recruits but have no demonstrated benefit in recreational runners with lower training volumes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Based on Ekenman 2002, orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures, and there is some evidence that semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain during running — the opposite of their intended effect. Butler 2003 similarly showed no difference in eversion excursion, peak eversion, eversion velocity, loading rate, or leg stiffness between no orthotic, multifunctional orthotic, and rigid orthotic conditions. The evidence for orthotics in injury prevention is mixed, with many trials examining military walking populations rather than runners.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The negative data on orthotics for tibial stress fracture prevention is clinically important for sports medicine practitioners; orthotics may improve subjective comfort but should not be prescribed with expectations of biomechanical injury prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q06","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Compared to shod running, non-shod (barefoot) running consistently produces which biomechanical pattern according to published studies?","choices":{"A":"Barefoot running reduces knee joint torques but increases ankle and metatarsophalangeal joint loading due to forefoot strike mechanics, resulting in an equivalent overall injury risk profile","B":"Barefoot running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers impact peak, reduces loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to running in conventional footwear","C":"Barefoot running reduces ground reaction forces on soft surfaces but produces higher impact forces than cushioned shoes on hard surfaces due to the absence of shock-absorbing midsole material","D":"Barefoot running reduces loading rate and impact peak during initial contact but increases stance time and ground reaction forces during push-off, producing no net biomechanical advantage","E":"Barefoot running produces identical ground reaction forces to cushioned shoes because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any running surface, maintaining homeostatic loading across all footwear conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Studies (Morley 2010, Lieberman 2010, Kerrigan 2009, Squadrone 2009) consistently show that non-shod (barefoot) running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers the impact peak, reduces the loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to conventional shod running. This is mechanistically explained by the shorter stride length and anterior foot strike pattern induced by barefoot conditions. However, it is the change in RUNNING STYLE (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the absence of the shoe itself that drives these benefits — as demonstrated by Connors 2023.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that barefoot running benefits derive from gait changes, not shoe absence, is clinically useful when counseling patients — running style modification can be achieved in any shoe.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: one of the most extensively studied running injuries in the footwear biomechanics literature, yet no shoe type has proven preventive"},{"id":"sdl17_q07","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study by Marti (1989) examined the relationship between running shoe price and lower extremity injury rates after correcting for previous injuries and training load. Which finding most accurately summarizes the results?","choices":{"A":"Runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 shoe group, suggesting that higher shoe price does not protect against injury","B":"Runners spending between $65 and $95 on shoes had the lowest injury rates, supporting a moderate price range as optimal for injury prevention in recreational distance runners","C":"No significant difference in injury rates was found between high-priced and low-priced shoe groups when training volume and prior injury history were controlled, confirming price as a neutral variable","D":"Higher-priced shoes reduced soft-tissue injuries such as Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis but showed no protective effect on bony stress fractures of the tibia or metatarsals","E":"The >$95 shoe group showed lower injury rates overall but higher rates of metatarsal stress fractures, suggesting that premium cushioning shifts injury risk from soft-tissue to bone pathology"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Marti (1989) found that runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 group, after correcting for prior injuries and training regimen. This finding is striking and counterintuitive — more expensive shoes, which often carry greater marketing claims, are associated with MORE injuries, not fewer. This likely relates to the Robbins \"impact moderating behavior\" phenomenon: expensive, heavily cushioned shoes reduce tactile feedback, blunt natural protective reflexes, and thereby increase impact forces and injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a high-impact teaching point. COMLEX: This is the key data point for counseling runners: expensive shoes are NOT protective, and clinical footwear prescriptions should not be driven by cost. The evidence base for shoe marketing claims is generally weak.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q08","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The traditional shoe prescription paradigm matches arch type to shoe type: overpronation (pes planus) receives motion control shoes, underpronation (pes cavus) receives cushioned shoes, and neutral motion receives stability shoes. Multiple systematic reviews have concluded that this approach is:","choices":{"A":"Supported by moderate-quality evidence for injury prevention in civilian runners but not validated in military populations, where rapid training volume increases confound shoe prescription outcomes","B":"Simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury rates — prescribing footwear based on arch type is not evidence-based according to available systematic reviews","C":"Effective for reducing knee and hip pain in runners with pes planus through motion control correction, though evidence for pes cavus cushioning and neutral stability shoes remains limited","D":"Validated in the short term for pain reduction during training, but not effective for long-term injury prevention beyond the first 12 weeks of a new running program","E":"Evidence-based for runners with BMI greater than 25, in whom arch instability and excess body weight compound to increase injury risk, but not in normal-weight recreational runners"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multiple systematic reviews (Ryan 2009, van Gent 2007, Knapik 2010) have concluded that prescribing footwear based on arch type is simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury. Richards 2008 specifically states: \"Prescription of this shoe type is not evidence based.\" Relph 2022 further confirmed: \"There is no evidence that prescribing footwear based on foot type reduces running-related lower-limb injuries in adults.\" This represents one of the most important marketing-versus-science disconnects in sports medicine — a widely practiced paradigm that lacks supporting evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that the arch-type → shoe-type paradigm is not evidence-based is directly applicable to clinical practice; physicians should not simply match arch type to shoe type when counseling runners.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q09","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Hoffmann (1905) conducted anatomical observations comparing barefoot populations to shoe-wearing populations. Which finding from his work directly supports the concept that conventional shoe design negatively alters foot structure and function?","choices":{"A":"Shoe-wearing populations develop stronger plantar intrinsic muscles compared to barefoot populations, as the mechanical resistance of the shoe sole provides passive resistance training during the gait cycle","B":"Shoe-wearing populations demonstrate lower rates of hallux valgus deformity because the medial counter of the shoe provides structural support for the great toe during push-off mechanics","C":"Shoe wearing does not alter foot anatomy in populations that begin wearing shoes after age five, as the foot skeleton has completed the majority of its ossification and remodeling by this age","D":"Shoe-wearing populations in both industrial and non-industrial societies develop identical foot anatomy, suggesting that footwear style rather than shoe wearing per se determines anatomical outcome","E":"Shoe wearing after the first year of life causes progressive narrowing of the anterior foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints compared to barefoot populations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hoffmann (1905) observed that barefoot and shoe-wearing populations have similar foot anatomy until shoes that compress and splint the foot are introduced — typically around the end of the first year of life. After this, shoe wearers develop progressive narrowing of the anterior portion of the foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints. He also noted that the great toe maintains foot adduction and provides leverage — functions that are compromised by narrow-toed shoes. This historical data supports the broader SDL theme that conventional shoe design may harm rather than help natural foot function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical data. COMLEX: Hoffmann's findings support recommending toe-box-wide shoes for patients, particularly children, to avoid acquired foot deformities. Great toe function (adduction, push-off leverage) is mechanically important and worth preserving.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q10","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"When counseling a competitive runner about shoe selection, the evidence supports using shoe WEIGHT as a meaningful prescription variable. Which relationship correctly describes the physiological impact of shoe weight on running economy?","choices":{"A":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption by approximately 3 to 5%, making shoe weight the single most important determinant of running performance and economy","B":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption (VO2) by approximately 0.7 to 1%, directly reducing running economy and metabolic efficiency","C":"Shoe weight does not independently affect oxygen consumption; only midsole stiffness and heel-to-toe drop alter metabolic efficiency, with heavier shoes being offset by greater energy return per stride","D":"Shoe weight increases oxygen consumption only when carried at the foot distal to the ankle; weight distributed proximally in the midsole and heel has no measurable effect on metabolic cost","E":"The relationship between shoe weight and oxygen consumption is nonlinear, with shoes under 200 grams having no metabolic effect and the cost increasing exponentially only above this threshold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Shoe weight is one of the evidence-based prescription variables in footwear selection. Each 100-gram increase in shoe weight results in approximately a 0.7 to 1% increase in oxygen consumption (VO2), meaningfully reducing running economy. This is why lighter shoes — all else being equal — are metabolically advantageous. The other evidence-based prescription variables include stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. These are contrasted with non-evidence-based marketing features (price, brand claims, motion control systems) that do not reliably correlate with performance or injury prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The 100 gm / 0.7-1% VO2 relationship is the only specific quantitative fact worth memorizing from the prescription paradigm section — relevant when advising competitive runners or when evaluating claims about racing flats versus training shoes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q11","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"According to the lever arm phenomena described by Hamill (1992), a running shoe with a softer midsole compared to one with a firmer midsole produces which biomechanical consequence for the foot and ankle?","choices":{"A":"A softer midsole produces greater pronation magnitude and a shorter time to reach maximum pronation compared to a firmer midsole, so cushioning can increase rather than control rearfoot motion","B":"A softer midsole reduces pronation magnitude by absorbing rearfoot eversion forces before they can be transmitted to the subtalar joint, producing a more neutral midfoot position at heel strike","C":"A softer midsole produces identical pronation magnitude to a firm midsole but extends the time to maximum pronation, allowing muscles more time to eccentrically decelerate subtalar motion","D":"A softer midsole shifts loading from the rearfoot to the forefoot, decreasing heel strike pronation while increasing metatarsophalangeal dorsiflexion torque during push-off","E":"A softer midsole increases pronation only in runners with pes planus; runners with neutral arch mechanics demonstrate no difference in pronation between soft and firm midsole conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hamill (1992) described the lever arm phenomenon: a softer midsole collapses under load more readily, allowing greater rearfoot eversion (pronation) and reducing the time to reach maximum pronation. Additionally, a wider sole base creates a longer lever arm, increasing the demand on the foot to overcome pronation forces. Counterintuitively, motion control shoes with dense thermoplastic medial posts often have WIDER soles — which actually increases the pronation-inducing lever arm rather than reducing it. This is another example of a shoe feature that is mechanistically opposite to its marketing claim.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The lever arm phenomenon explains why wide, soft-soled motion control shoes may worsen rather than correct pronation — a mechanistic argument against the arch-type prescription paradigm covered in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q12","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The evidence-based \"take home points\" for running shoe prescription recommend that patients prioritize which set of shoe characteristics when selecting footwear?","choices":{"A":"Motion control features, arch support, and cushioned heel collar — selected based on static arch height assessment using the wet footprint test or a commercially available pressure plate system","B":"Heel-to-toe drop greater than 10 mm, a wide toe box, and a reinforced medial post — prioritizing rearfoot stability for runners who log more than 30 miles per week","C":"Maximum cushioning with a thick midsole, a neutral motion category designation, and replacement on a fixed schedule of every 250 to 500 miles regardless of shoe condition","D":"Light weight, flexibility, and a thin profile — chosen for immediate comfort upon fitting while ignoring cost, brand features, and the marketing claims made about the shoe","E":"High cost within the runner's budget, a brand with independent biomechanics laboratory testing, and a heel cup width matched to the runner's measured calcaneal width"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The evidence-based shoe-buying recommendations from the course emphasize selecting shoes that are light, flexible, and thin, with the most important practical criterion being that the shoe is immediately comfortable when tried on. Patients should get properly sized (feet change with age and weight), try multiple pairs, and buy a new shoe when the current one is no longer comfortable — not on a fixed mileage or time schedule. The features to IGNORE include cost, brand marketing, motion control elements, bells, and whistles. This directly contradicts most commercial shoe retail advice and represents the core clinical take-away of OBJ 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The prescription framework (light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable) is the answer to \"how do you prescribe running shoes?\" on clinical vignettes. Price, brand, and arch type are explicitly NOT evidence-based selection criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q13","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine lecture traces how several modern running shoe brands began. The lecturer describes a company founded in post-World War II Japan in 1949. Which modern brand did this company become?","choices":{"A":"Nike, founded from Onitsuka's Japanese factory after Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight bought it in 1949","B":"Converse, whose Chuck Taylor shoes were first made in Japan and worn by the 1936 US basketball team","C":"Asics, which grew out of the Onitsuka company founded in post-World War II Japan in the year 1949","D":"Reebok, which grew from Joseph William Foster's Japanese spike factory opened after the war","E":"Keds, which Goodyear launched in Japan in 1949 after developing rubber vulcanization there"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course timeline of running shoes lists 1949 (Onitsuka) in post-World War II Japan as the origin of Asics. The same timeline places Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight's Nike in the early 1960s in the United States, not in 1949 Japan. Converse Chuck Taylors were worn by the US basketball team in 1936, and they did not come from Japan. Joseph William Foster introduced spikes in 1852 in England, the line that later became Reebok. Goodyear's vulcanization and Keds date to 1892, not post-war Japan.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Running shoe timeline: 1832 Webster rubber soles; 1852 Foster spikes; 1892 Goodyear vulcanization (Keds); 1920 Dassler sprint vs endurance shoes; 1936 Berlin Olympics; 1949 Onitsuka (Asics); 1962 New Balance \"scientifically tested\"; early 1960s Nike. COMLEX: Marketing language entered footwear long before outcome evidence did.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — commercial marketing versus the science of footwear design"},{"id":"sdl17_q14","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine attending notes that Adolf Dassler's shoes were worn at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Dassler's company was the direct predecessor to which modern athletic brand?","choices":{"A":"Adidas — Adolf Dassler's company, operating under the Dassler Brothers brand before the split, evolved into Adidas (from \"Adi Dassler\") after a post-WWII family dispute","B":"Nike — because Phil Knight and Bill Bowerman initially imported Dassler shoes for distribution in the United States before founding their own brand in the early 1960s","C":"New Balance — because the German engineering approach developed by Dassler was adopted by New Balance when it introduced its \"scientifically tested\" shoe line in 1962","D":"Puma — which was the sole successor company to the original Dassler Brothers Shoe Factory, retaining the full patent portfolio and manufacturing facilities after the family split","E":"Asics — because Kihachiro Onitsuka licensed the Dassler spiked shoe design when founding his Japanese athletic company in 1949 after World War II"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's company evolved into Adidas (named from his nickname \"Adi\" and surname \"Dassler\"). After a post-WWII family dispute, his brother Rudolf founded Puma — so both Adidas and Puma share the Dassler Brothers lineage. For this course, Dassler's 1920 innovation (sprint vs endurance shoe differentiation) and his presence at the 1936 Berlin Olympics are the testable historical facts. Nike was founded independently by Bowerman and Knight. New Balance, Asics, and the others have separate founding histories with no direct Dassler connection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — brand history. COMLEX: The Dassler → Adidas/Puma origin story is a contextual detail. The clinically relevant lesson is that the earliest \"performance differentiation\" in footwear was driven by athletic intuition, not randomized evidence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Dassler's sprint vs endurance differentiation foreshadows modern heel-to-toe drop debates, as sprint shoes are zero-drop and endurance shoes historically had a raised heel — each influencing Achilles tendon loading differently"},{"id":"sdl17_q15","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports history question on an exam presents the following timeline of footwear milestones. Which item in this list is placed in the INCORRECT year?","choices":{"A":"1852 — Joseph William Foster introduced spikes to running footwear, improving traction for competitive runners on natural grass and cinder track surfaces","B":"1949 — Kihachiro Onitsuka founded the company in post-WWII Japan that eventually became Asics, producing athletic footwear for Japanese Olympic development programs","C":"1920 — Adolf Dassler began differentiating shoe construction by athletic event type, creating separate designs for sprint and endurance running athletes","D":"1962 — New Balance became the first athletic shoe company to explicitly market its products using the phrase \"scientifically tested\" in its commercial advertising","E":"1852 — Wait Webster introduced rubber soles combined with leather uppers, giving athletic footwear a more flexible and durable sole for running and walking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wait Webster's rubber-and-leather sole dates to 1832, not 1852; 1852 is Foster's spikes. The 1852 milestone belongs to Joseph William Foster and the introduction of spikes. Goodyear's contribution in 1892 was vulcanization, which was used in the production of Keds. All other dates in the answer choices are correct as stated. This question requires distinguishing between the 1832 rubber sole milestone (Webster) and the 1852 spike milestone (Foster), which are commonly confused.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Historical chronology for footwear is background context. Remember the key dates: 1832 (rubber soles/Webster), 1852 (spikes/Foster), 1892 (vulcanization/Goodyear/Keds), 1920 (Dassler/sprint vs endurance), 1962 (New Balance/scientifically tested).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the 1892 vulcanization milestone enabled mass-produced shoe soles, beginning the era of standardized heel heights that altered Achilles tendon resting length across populations who adopted footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q16","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old recreational runner presents with medial tibial pain after increasing her mileage. She recently purchased expensive motion control shoes with maximum cushioning on the advice of a running store specialist. Which statement from the published evidence most directly challenges the assumption that her shoe choice will reduce her injury risk?","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes with rigid thermoplastic medial posts increase eversion control at the subtalar joint, reducing pronation-related tibial torsional forces and thereby lowering medial tibial stress","B":"More cushioning reduces tactile ground sensation, which blunts the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes, paradoxically increasing actual impact forces despite the perception of softer contact","C":"Highly cushioned shoes improve injury outcomes in runners with pes cavus who underpronate, but offer no additional protection for neutral runners or those with pes planus foot type","D":"Maximum cushioning reduces the vertical loading rate during heel strike, which is the primary biomechanical determinant of medial tibial stress syndrome and tibial stress fracture risk","E":"Cushioned shoes provide equivalent impact protection to barefoot running on soft surfaces, making them the preferred option for runners transitioning from high-mileage training blocks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Robbins phenomenon (1987-1997) is the key mechanism: more cushioning → less tactile sensation from the plantar surface → reduced natural absorption reflexes → larger actual impact forces. The runner's body typically modulates impact based on sensory feedback from the foot; when this feedback is muffled by thick cushioning, the brain cannot appropriately trigger protective muscle co-contraction, and ground reaction forces increase. Additionally, Marti 1989 showed that shoes costing >$95 were associated with TWICE the lower extremity injury rate compared to shoes under $40, challenging the assumption that premium cushioned shoes reduce injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Robbins impact-moderating behavior concept is the core mechanistic explanation for why more cushion ≠ less injury. This is the most clinically applicable concept from OBJ 2 when counseling runners who present with overuse injuries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: MTSS involves repetitive tibial loading; the evidence that cushioned shoes do not reduce impact forces directly explains why shoe type has failed to demonstrate preventive efficacy for this condition"},{"id":"sdl17_q17","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 38-year-old recreational runner with knee pain wants to move all of his training from asphalt roads to a soft dirt trail because he believes hard surfaces raise the ground reaction forces that reach his joints. Which statement from the course best addresses his belief?","choices":{"A":"Hard surfaces raise ground reaction forces in direct proportion to their stiffness, so a softer trail will lower joint loading","B":"Soft surfaces absorb impact and lower ground reaction force, which is why cushioned shoes also lower joint loading","C":"Ground reaction forces depend on the running surface exclusively, so his stride length and shoes will not affect impact","D":"Hard surfaces do not increase ground reaction force, because the runner's kinematics adapt to the surface on landing","E":"Hard surfaces increase muscle damage and hemolysis, so his knee pain reflects microtrauma from road running"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course's review of cushioning and impact states that hard surfaces do not increase ground reaction force, that absorption does not decrease it, and that to some degree a runner's kinematics adapt to accommodate a change in running surface. It also notes that increased stride length on its own raises ground reaction force, and that more cushioning reduces tactile sensation and leads to larger impact force. Saying hard surfaces raise force in proportion to stiffness, or that soft surfaces and cushioned shoes lower force by absorption, contradicts these points. Surface is not the only determinant; stride length and footwear matter. The course notes no difference in muscle damage or hemolysis with cushioning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ground reaction force rises with longer stride and lower cadence; runners stiffen or soften the leg to match the surface. Natural shock-absorbing capacity decreases with age. COMLEX: Address training load and running mechanics before surface or shoe changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — shoe factors that are useful in prescription (weight, stack, drop, flexibility)"},{"id":"sdl17_q18","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team studies the biomechanics of minimalist footwear use and finds that runners transitioning to minimalist shoes demonstrate lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less energy absorption at the knee, and more energy absorption at the ankle. The researchers conclude that these changes are most directly attributable to:","choices":{"A":"The reduction in shoe weight associated with minimalist footwear, which decreases the metabolic cost of swing phase and reflexively produces a shorter stride and more anterior foot contact angle","B":"The elimination of heel cushioning in minimalist shoes, which prevents heel strike by making heel contact painful, thereby mechanically forcing an anterior foot strike pattern in all runners","C":"The change in running style induced by minimalist shoes (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the physical properties of the shoe itself, such as its weight, cushioning, or drop","D":"The zero or near-zero heel-to-toe drop of minimalist shoes, which anatomically positions the foot in plantarflexion at heel contact and biomechanically requires a forefoot or midfoot strike pattern","E":"The increased plantar tactile feedback from minimalist shoes, which amplifies afferent signals from ground contact and triggers spinal reflex arcs that automatically shorten stride length"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Connors (2023) explicitly concluded that improvements seen with minimalist footwear — lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less knee energy absorption, more ankle energy absorption, and improvement in prior injuries — result from the CHANGE IN RUNNING STYLE (anterior/forefoot foot strike, higher cadence, shorter stride) rather than any inherent property of the shoe itself. This is a clinically critical distinction: running gait retraining, regardless of shoe type, can replicate these benefits. Minimalist shoes also increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes (Sun 2020), so they carry trade-offs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Connors 2023 conclusion is the capstone finding for OBJ 2 — it unifies the data by showing that footwear effects on biomechanics are mediated through gait changes. Gait retraining is therefore more fundamental than shoe selection for injury prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the increased ankle energy absorption with anterior foot strike induced by minimalist shoes increases eccentric Achilles loading, which explains the higher rates of Achilles tendinopathy reported in runners who transition too rapidly to minimalist footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q19","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 45-year-old marathon runner visits a sports medicine clinic and reports that a running store employee recommended replacing his shoes every 6 months or after 250-500 miles, citing \"complete cushioning breakdown.\" When asked if this recommendation is evidence-based, the physician should respond that:","choices":{"A":"The 250-500 mile replacement guideline is not outcome-based; EVA midsole cushioning loses about 40% of its properties after 400 miles, but replacement timing should be guided by comfort rather than a fixed mileage schedule","B":"The 250-500 mile guideline is supported by prospective trials showing that runners who replace shoes on this schedule have significantly lower rates of tibial stress fractures and Achilles tendinopathy","C":"The 250-500 mile guideline is appropriate for runners over age 40 because natural shock-absorbing capacity decreases with age, making midsole cushioning degradation more biomechanically consequential in older runners","D":"The guideline is evidence-based for heavy runners (BMI greater than 27) in whom midsole compression is accelerated, but lacks evidence for normal-weight runners who can use shoes until sole tread wear is visible","E":"The 500-mile upper limit is based on controlled wear-testing data showing complete EVA foam failure at this threshold, making continued use unsafe due to uneven surface contact and increased lateral ankle sprain risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 250-500 mile replacement recommendation is NOT outcome-based. The course notes that EVA foam cushioning loses approximately 40% of its cushioning properties after 400 miles — but this has not been translated into a clinical trial demonstrating that replacement at this threshold reduces injury. Moreover, the \"right\" replacement interval is highly variable based on runner weight, frequency of use, running conditions, and shoe storage. The evidence-based recommendation is simply: replace shoes when they are no longer comfortable. This is another example of a commercially convenient guideline that lacks scientific support.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The 40% EVA cushioning loss figure at 400 miles is the only specific number worth knowing. Clinical pearl: advise patients to replace based on comfort, not mileage — a simple, evidence-consistent, and cost-conscious recommendation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: fixed-interval shoe replacement guidelines parallel unproven integrative medicine protocols — both are commercially promoted practices that lack patient-oriented outcome evidence but persist due to industry interests"},{"id":"sdl17_q20","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study (Stacoff 2001) examined whether shoe type — specifically motion control versus cushioning versus stability designs — could reliably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running. The most accurate summary of this study's finding is:","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes significantly reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in overpronators when compared to neutral shoes, supporting their prescription in runners with pes planus and rearfoot valgus alignment","B":"Stability shoes reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in neutral runners but have no effect in overpronators, suggesting that motion control features only benefit runners who already demonstrate moderate arch integrity","C":"Shoe type reliably controls tibiocalcaneal kinematics in runners who weigh less than 75 kg, but not in heavier runners in whom foot mass and ground reaction forces overwhelm the shoe's mechanical resistance","D":"Tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique and not reliably controlled by shoe type, undermining the biomechanical rationale for motion control shoe prescription","E":"Motion control shoes reduce the loading rate of tibiocalcaneal eversion but do not alter the total range of pronation, providing partial biomechanical benefit without complete kinematics correction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stacoff (2001) found that tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique — i.e., each runner's pronation pattern is specific to them and NOT predictably controlled by shoe type. This finding is foundational to the marketing-versus-science argument: the entire motion control shoe category is built on the premise that shoe design can reliably correct rearfoot mechanics, but Stacoff's data shows this is not the case. Combined with the Ryan/Richards/Knapik systematic review data showing no injury reduction from arch-based prescription, this study represents a mechanistic basis for skepticism about motion control shoes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Stacoff 2001 provides the mechanistic explanation for why motion control shoes don't work as marketed — individual kinematics variability means no shoe type can reliably control rearfoot motion for all runners. This supports a comfort-first prescription approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the failure of shoe design to predictably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics parallels the failure of many physical integrative therapies to produce consistent biomechanical changes — individualized responses undermine group-based intervention protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q21","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study of 2,300 children aged 4-13 years compared the rate of static anatomic changes of the foot by footwear type. Closed-toe shoes were associated with a 13.2% rate, sandals 6.0%, slippers 8.2%, and unshod children 2.8%. Which conclusion does this data most directly support regarding footwear in children?","choices":{"A":"All footwear types produce equivalent rates of static anatomic change compared to the unshod condition, suggesting that walking itself, rather than shoe type, is the primary driver of pediatric foot anatomy changes","B":"Sandals and slippers produce similar rates of anatomic change because they provide insufficient medial arch support, whereas closed-toe shoes with built-in arch structures paradoxically reduce change in older children","C":"Closed-toe shoes are associated with the highest rate of static anatomic change and going unshod with the lowest, suggesting that wearing shoes, not going without them, may contribute to anatomic changes of the foot","D":"The 13.2% rate of anatomic change in closed-toe shoe wearers reflects selection bias, as children with pre-existing foot problems are more likely to be prescribed closed-toe orthopedic footwear by their physicians","E":"Children who wear sandals have lower rates of anatomic change than slipper wearers because sandal straps provide dynamic lateral containment of the foot, reducing hallux valgus drift during the push-off phase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide (SDL 17 p12, Rao 1992) asks whether bulky shoes cause static anatomic changes in children and answers \"Maybe\": in 2,300 children aged 4-13, the rate was 13.2% with closed-toe shoes, 8.2% with slippers, 6.0% with sandals and 2.8% unshod. The gradient fits a possible contribution of shoe wearing, which is why the slide stops at \"maybe\" rather than proving cause. The slides that follow (p13-p14, Hoffmann 1905) show the same direction: foot impressions of children after a few weeks of shoe-wearing show narrowing of the front of the foot and a change in the long axis of the great toe, and once footwear that compresses and splints the foot is used, there is progressive narrowing of the anterior foot and reduced range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal and ankle joints. The data shows no equal rates across footwear, does not test arch support or sandal straps, and gives no information on prescribing patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Rao 1992 (course slide p12): static anatomic foot changes in children were most common with closed-toe shoes (13.2%) and least common unshod (2.8%); the course answer to \"do bulky shoes cause them?\" is \"maybe\". Hoffmann 1905: shoe-wearing narrows the forefoot and reduces range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal and ankle joints.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the pediatric foot deformity data illustrates how a cultural norm (children wearing closed-toe shoes) persists despite evidence of harm — the same uncritical acceptance seen with many unregulated integrative health products"},{"id":"sdl17_q22","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine physician is developing an individualized running shoe prescription for a 28-year-old male recreational runner with no prior injuries. He uses an evidence-based shoe prescription framework. Which combination of shoe characteristics does the evidence-based framework identify as the most clinically relevant for this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Medial post stiffness, arch height index, rearfoot flare angle, and pronation control category — selected based on a dynamic foot pressure analysis and static arch height measurement in the clinic","B":"Shoe weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width — with comfort upon immediate fitting as the overriding clinical criterion for the final selection","C":"Brand reputation, independent biomechanical testing certification, outsole durometer rating, and midsole foam density — as these are the construction variables most strongly correlated with injury prevention","D":"Price point within the runner's budget, outsole tread depth, upper breathability rating, and motion control category — prioritizing long-term durability and cost-effectiveness over immediate comfort","E":"Sole stiffness in the sagittal plane, heel counter height, toe spring angle, and midsole thickness at the metatarsal heads — selected based on the runner's habitual foot strike pattern as assessed by treadmill video analysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The evidence-based prescription framework from the course identifies six useful shoe characteristics: weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. Immediate comfort upon fitting is the most practical overriding criterion. The framework explicitly rejects arch-type matching, price-based selection, and brand-specific claims as non-evidence-based prescription methods. The paradigm also considers the interaction between congenital and acquired foot anatomy, foot function, and running gait — all of which are individually unique and cannot be addressed by a standardized shoe prescription protocol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Memorize the six evidence-based shoe variables (weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, heel cup width) and the take-home rule: light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable — ignore cost and marketing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: the shoe prescription framework parallels PT prescription — both require individualized goal setting, specific measurable parameters (drop, weight, flexibility), and patient-centered comfort outcomes rather than generic protocol adherence"},{"id":"sdl17_q23","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During a footwear consultation, a physician lists heel-to-toe drop among the useful factors when choosing a running shoe. A medical student asks for clarification. Which description correctly defines heel-to-toe drop?","choices":{"A":"Heel-to-toe drop is the total midsole stack height at the midfoot, measured in millimeters at the navicular level; higher drop values indicate greater overall cushioning and are associated with more posterior foot strike patterns","B":"Heel-to-toe drop refers to the degree of heel counter rigidity relative to toe box flexibility; a high-drop shoe has a stiff heel and a compliant forefoot, promoting rearfoot stability during heel strike","C":"Heel-to-toe drop measures the vertical distance between the ground and the ball of the foot during midstance; higher drop values indicate greater arch height and are used to classify shoe type as motion control, stability, or neutral","D":"Heel-to-toe drop is the rearfoot-to-forefoot weight distribution ratio measured by force plate analysis; a 12 mm drop corresponds to approximately 60% rearfoot loading and 40% forefoot loading during heel strike","E":"Heel-to-toe drop is the difference between rearfoot stack height and forefoot stack height; a shoe with a 24 mm rearfoot and a 12 mm forefoot has a 12 mm drop, and it is listed with weight and stack height as useful shoe factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heel-to-toe drop (the \"drop\" of a shoe) is the difference between the rearfoot and forefoot stack heights. The course slide (SDL 17 p40) gives the example: 24 mm rearfoot minus 12 mm forefoot equals a 12 mm drop. The course lists weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop and flexibility as the factors that are useful in choosing a running shoe (p41). Drop is not a midfoot stack height, a measure of heel counter rigidity, an arch height or a force-plate weight distribution. Related course points on minimalist shoes: they are associated with an anterior foot strike, and the effect comes from the change in running style rather than the shoe itself (p34, Connors 2023); they can increase Achilles tendon cross-sectional area and stiffness but raise metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared with conventional shoes (p33, Sun 2020).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Drop = rearfoot stack height − forefoot stack height (24 mm − 12 mm = 12 mm; course slide p40). Useful shoe factors: weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility (p41). Minimalist shoes: anterior foot strike, a running-style effect (Connors 2023); thicker, stiffer Achilles but more MTP and ankle loading (Sun 2020).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: like heel-to-toe drop in a shoe, each PT prescription element is a specific, stated variable"},{"id":"sdl17_q24","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A Sun (2020) systematic review of 63 studies on footwear construction and running biomechanics identified four key conclusions. Which set of findings most accurately reflects the systematic review's conclusions?","choices":{"A":"Higher midsole cushioning uniformly reduces injury risk; motion control features reliably decrease pronation in overpronators; minimalist shoes produce equivalent joint loading to conventional shoes when worn by experienced barefoot runners","B":"No shoe construction variable demonstrated consistent biomechanical effects across subjects; individual kinematic variability negated population-level findings; only gait retraining produced statistically significant reductions in running-related injury rates","C":"Optimal midsole stiffness benefits performance; softer midsoles reduce impact forces and loading rates; thicker midsoles cushion but reduce plantar sensation; minimalist shoes improve running economy and Achilles tendon stiffness but increase MTP and ankle joint loading","D":"Maximalist cushioned shoes reduce GRF more than any other design; rigid carbon-plate shoes improve running economy equally across all runner types; heel-to-toe drop does not independently affect foot strike pattern or Achilles tendon loading","E":"Thicker midsoles reduce injury risk in all runners; stiffer midsoles increase injury risk in runners over age 40; minimalist shoes should only be prescribed to runners with pes cavus who already demonstrate an anterior foot strike pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sun (2020) identified four key conclusions from 63 studies: (1) Increasing midsole stiffness at the optimal range can benefit performance-related variables; (2) Softer midsoles can reduce impact forces and loading rates; (3) Thicker midsoles provide better cushioning but may decrease plantar sensations — reducing the natural impact-moderating reflexes; (4) Minimalist shoes can improve running economy and increase cross-sectional area and stiffness of the Achilles tendon, but would increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes. This four-point summary represents the most evidence-consolidated framework for understanding the trade-offs in shoe construction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The Sun 2020 four conclusions are the most board-testable framework from OBJ 4 — they synthesize the minimalist shoe trade-offs (better Achilles/economy but more MTP and ankle load) and explain why no single shoe design is universally superior across all runners.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: Sun 2020's four-conclusion framework mirrors a PT prescription in its acknowledgment of trade-offs — every shoe construction variable that benefits one outcome worsens another, requiring individualized clinical judgment rather than universal protocols"}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"Bone Histology, Physiology & Remodeling","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cell type constitutes approximately 90–95% of all cells found in mature bone tissue and serves as the primary sensor of mechanical strain and regulator of local mineral chemistry?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which synthesize and secrete osteoid and remain the dominant cell population in cortical bone throughout adulthood","B":"Osteoclasts, which are large multinucleated resorptive cells that account for the majority of cellular volume in cortical bone","C":"Periosteal stem cells, which reside in the inner cambium layer and represent the most abundant progenitor population in mature bone","D":"Chondroblasts, which persist within cortical bone after endochondral ossification and maintain the calcified matrix in adult life","E":"Osteocytes, former osteoblasts embedded within the mineralized matrix and linked to neighboring cells by canalicular processes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osteocytes make up 90–95% of all bone cells. They derive from osteoblasts that become trapped within the matrix they secrete and differentiate into long-lived mechanosensors connected by canalicular processes. Osteoblasts represent only 4–6% of bone cells and reside on active formation surfaces. Osteoclasts are a small fraction derived from hematopoietic precursors. Periosteal stem cells and chondroblasts are not significant resident populations in mature cortical bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteocytes are the HY \"most abundant bone cell\" — classic single-best-answer. Also know that osteocyte death (lacunar necrosis) is the hallmark of avascular necrosis. COMLEX: Osteocytes respond to Wolff's Law (bone remodels in response to mechanical loading); loss of loading leads to disuse osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q02","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An osteoblast synthesizes and secretes osteoid prior to its mineralization into mature bone matrix. Which organic component constitutes the primary structural protein within this pre-mineralized scaffold?","choices":{"A":"Type I collagen, which provides the tensile framework upon which hydroxyapatite crystals are subsequently deposited to form mineralized bone","B":"Type II collagen, which is the principal structural protein of hyaline cartilage and is degraded before bone replaces the cartilaginous template","C":"Type III collagen, which is the dominant structural protein of soft connective tissues such as skin and arterial walls in the body","D":"Elastin, which provides elastic recoil and flexibility to connective tissues and is a minor component of ligaments throughout the skeleton","E":"Fibronectin, which anchors cells to the extracellular matrix and facilitates cell migration during bone formation and fracture repair"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoid — the pre-mineralized organic matrix secreted by osteoblasts — is composed predominantly of Type I collagen along with non-collagenous proteins such as osteocalcin and osteopontin. Type II collagen is specific to cartilage. Type III collagen is found in soft tissues. Elastin and fibronectin are minor matrix components that do not form the primary structural scaffold of bone. Hydroxyapatite subsequently crystallizes on and between the collagen fibers to create the composite mineral-organic structure of mature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in Type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2) — directly linking collagen type to bone disease. COMLEX: \"Blue sclerae\" in OI occurs because the thin collagen allows underlying choroid to show through — a classic clinical pearl often paired with bone fragility questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q03","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During bone resorption, osteoclasts must first dissolve the inorganic mineral phase of bone matrix before the organic matrix can be degraded. Which mechanism accounts for demineralization of the hydroxyapatite crystals?","choices":{"A":"Release of alkaline phosphatase, which catalyzes hydrolysis of phosphate ester bonds within the hydroxyapatite lattice to release calcium and phosphate ions","B":"Secretion of osteocalcin, which chelates calcium ions from the crystal surface through high-affinity gamma-carboxyglutamate residues in the acidic resorption lacuna","C":"Secretion of collagenase and gelatinase enzymes at the ruffled border, which directly degrade the hydroxyapatite mineral phase before the underlying organic matrix is lysed","D":"Secretion of hydrogen ions via an apical proton pump, which creates an acidic microenvironment beneath the ruffled border that dissolves hydroxyapatite crystals","E":"Production of reactive oxygen species by the osteoclast NADPH oxidase, which oxidatively fragment the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice to release mineral ions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteoclasts seal a resorption lacuna against the bone surface using an actin ring, then deploy a ruffled border that secretes H+ via a vacuolar-type H+-ATPase (proton pump). The resulting acidic pH dissolves hydroxyapatite, releasing Ca2+ and PO43−. After demineralization, cathepsins and matrix metalloproteinases degrade the organic matrix. Alkaline phosphatase is an osteoblast marker, not an osteoclast mechanism. Osteocalcin and reactive oxygen species are not the primary demineralization mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proton pump inhibitors and carbonic anhydrase II inhibitors can impair osteoclast function — carbonic anhydrase II deficiency causes osteopetrosis. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite and are ingested by osteoclasts, inducing apoptosis — clinically relevant for osteoporosis and Paget disease management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q04","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the RANKL-mediated pathway of bone remodeling, which cell type produces receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand in response to mechanical and hormonal stimuli, thereby driving osteoclastogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which express RANKL that binds RANK on monocyte precursors and drives their fusion into mature multinucleated osteoclasts","B":"Osteoclasts, which produce RANKL in an autocrine fashion to sustain their own activation and amplify bone resorption at sites of high turnover","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid, which release RANKL in response to elevated serum calcium as part of the calcitonin regulatory feedback loop","D":"T lymphocytes, which are the exclusive systemic source of RANKL and drive osteoclastogenesis only under conditions of inflammatory joint disease","E":"Osteocytes, which are solely responsible for RANKL secretion due to their embedded position and direct exposure to mechanical strain signals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoblasts are the principal producers of RANKL in physiologic bone remodeling. RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, initiating a signaling cascade that drives their differentiation into mature bone-resorbing osteoclasts. While T lymphocytes can produce RANKL in inflammatory conditions (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis), osteoblasts are the physiologic source. Parafollicular cells produce calcitonin, not RANKL. Osteoclasts and osteocytes are not primary RANKL producers under normal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The RANKL/RANK/OPG axis is a classic high-yield pathway — denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL used to treat osteoporosis. COMLEX: Understand that PTH paradoxically promotes bone resorption (via RANKL upregulation) with chronic elevation but stimulates formation with intermittent dosing — teriparatide exploits this.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q05","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Osteoprotegerin is a key regulatory glycoprotein in the control of bone remodeling. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which osteoprotegerin limits bone resorption?","choices":{"A":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoclasts and directly inhibits calcitonin binding at its receptor on the osteoclast surface, reducing the anti-resorptive hormonal response","B":"Osteoprotegerin is secreted by osteoblasts and acts as a soluble decoy receptor that binds RANKL, keeping it from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors","C":"Osteoprotegerin is a transmembrane enzyme on osteocytes that phosphorylates and inactivates RANKL before it can reach circulation and bind distant osteoclast precursors","D":"Osteoprotegerin is a circulating protease derived from hepatocytes that cleaves PTH in the bloodstream, reducing upstream stimulation of the RANKL-dependent resorption cycle","E":"Osteoprotegerin is produced by T lymphocytes and blocks vitamin D receptors on intestinal epithelium to suppress calcium absorption and reduce systemic calcium-driven remodeling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG) is a member of the TNF receptor superfamily secreted by osteoblasts. It acts as a decoy receptor by binding RANKL with high affinity, preventing RANKL from engaging its functional receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. This OPG/RANKL ratio is the key determinant of osteoclastogenesis — high OPG favors bone formation, low OPG favors resorption. It is not produced by osteoclasts, T lymphocytes, or hepatocytes, and it has no direct effect on calcitonin or vitamin D receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The OPG/RANKL ratio is a critical concept — estrogen increases OPG production, which is why postmenopausal estrogen loss leads to decreased OPG, increased RANKL-driven resorption, and osteoporosis. COMLEX: Denosumab mimics OPG by targeting RANKL; contrast with bisphosphonates (induce osteoclast apoptosis directly).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q06","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient has an elevated serum calcium level following excessive vitamin D supplementation. Which cell type responds to this hypercalcemia and what hormone do they release to restore mineral homeostasis?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells of the parathyroid gland, which detect elevated serum calcium and release parathyroid hormone to stimulate osteoblast-mediated bone formation and restore balance","B":"Osteoblasts on endosteal surfaces, which detect local hypercalcemia and secrete an autocrine calcitonin-like peptide that suppresses adjacent osteoclast activity","C":"Parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland, which secrete calcitonin in response to elevated serum calcium to directly inhibit osteoclast activity and reduce bone resorption","D":"Alpha cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, which respond to elevated serum calcium by releasing calcitonin alongside glucagon to promote calcium deposition in bone","E":"Enterochromaffin cells of the duodenal mucosa, which release calcitonin in response to dietary calcium load to limit further intestinal absorption and prevent systemic hypercalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Calcitonin is produced exclusively by the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid gland in response to elevated serum calcium. Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. Chief cells of the parathyroid produce PTH (released in response to hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia). Pancreatic alpha cells produce glucagon; osteoblasts and enterochromaffin cells do not produce calcitonin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from C cells and produces excess calcitonin — serum calcitonin is used as a tumor marker. MEN2A/MEN2B involve RET mutation and include medullary thyroid carcinoma. COMLEX: In clinical practice, calcitonin is used therapeutically for Paget disease and hypercalcemia of malignancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q07","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In a mature long bone, which statement best describes the direction of blood flow through the bone tissue and the main route by which vessels cross the compact bone?","choices":{"A":"Flow is centrifugal, from the marrow cavity out through the cortex to periosteal veins, entering mainly via Volkmann canals","B":"Flow is centripetal, from periosteal arteries inward to the marrow, with Haversian canals as the main route of entry across the cortex","C":"Flow is longitudinal, from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis along the marrow cavity, crossing the cortex through the growth plate","D":"Flow is circular within each osteon, with blood passing from canaliculi into the lacunae and back without leaving the cortex","E":"Flow is bidirectional and equal, with half of cortical blood entering from the marrow and half from the periosteum at all ages"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pawlina describes blood flow in bone as essentially centrifugal: blood moves from the marrow cavity into and through the bone tissue and leaves through periosteal veins. Volkmann canals provide the major route for vessels to pass through compact bone, and smaller vessels enter the Haversian canals, each holding an arteriole and venule or a single capillary. Periosteal arteries give a lesser supply to the outermost compact bone. A centripetal pattern reverses this. Flow does not run along the bone through the growth plate. Canaliculi carry tissue fluid and osteocyte processes, not blood. The marrow-derived supply dominates, so the contribution is not an equal split. Bone tissue also lacks lymphatic vessels; lymphatic drainage occurs from the periosteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Long bone supply: nutrient artery (marrow, inner cortex), metaphyseal and epiphyseal arteries, periosteal arteries (outer cortex); flow is centrifugal. COMLEX: Periosteal stripping and intramedullary reaming each injure part of this supply, which matters for fracture healing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — regions of long bones and their blood supply"},{"id":"sdl18_q08","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a tibial shaft stress fracture reports severe, well-localized point tenderness over the bone. Which anatomical structure is primarily responsible for the exquisite pain sensitivity of bone to injury and periosteal irritation?","choices":{"A":"The inner cellular layer of the periosteum, which contains osteoprogenitor stem cells and is primarily responsible for nociceptive signaling via its dense afferent innervation","B":"The Haversian canals within cortical bone, which contain abundant sensory nerve fibers running parallel to the osteon and serve as the principal pain-sensing compartment","C":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum, which is densely invested with sensory nerve fibers and sympathetic afferents that transmit pain signals upon mechanical deformation or injury","D":"The trabecular surfaces of cancellous bone, which are densely innervated with free nerve endings and are the primary source of nociception in long bone injuries","E":"The endosteum lining the medullary cavity, which contains the highest concentration of pain-sensitive nerve endings and is predominantly responsible for fracture pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The outer fibrous layer of the periosteum is richly innervated with sensory (nociceptive) and sympathetic nerve fibers, making it the most pain-sensitive component of bone. This explains why periosteal elevation, tumors invading the periosteum, or stress fractures cause exquisite localized pain. The inner cellular (cambium) layer contains progenitor cells but sparse innervation. Haversian canals carry small vessels and nerves, but they are not the primary pain-sensing structure. The endosteum and cancellous surfaces have relatively sparse innervation compared to the periosteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteal pain is why bone metastases are so painful — tumor expansion stretches the periosteum. NSAIDs and bisphosphonates help reduce periosteal nociception. COMLEX: OMM consideration — the periosteum transmits viscerosomatic reflexes; periosteal tenderness at Chapman reflex points reflects visceral dysfunction and is used diagnostically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q09","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the weeks following a long bone fracture, a fibrocartilaginous bridge spans the fracture gap and stabilizes the fragments. What is this structure called, and what process ultimately converts it into bone?","choices":{"A":"The hard callus, which is a directly mineralized woven bone collar that forms within the first 72 hours after fracture by periosteal osteoblast activation at the injury site","B":"The primary union bridge, which is a direct lamellar bone-to-bone junction that forms when fracture ends are held in rigid contact without any intermediate cartilaginous phase","C":"The remodeling zone, which is the mature lamellar bone structure that replaces disorganized woven bone and is classified as the bridging callus on post-fracture imaging","D":"The soft callus, a fibrocartilaginous scaffold that stabilizes the fracture ends and is later replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification by invading osteoblasts","E":"The periosteal sleeve, which is an avascular fibrous collar that encases and immobilizes the fracture gap and is later resorbed entirely before new bone can be laid down"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bone repair proceeds through: (1) hematoma formation, (2) soft callus formation — a fibrocartilaginous bridge that stabilizes the fracture, (3) hard callus — the soft callus is replaced via endochondral ossification by woven bone, and (4) remodeling — woven bone is replaced by organized lamellar bone over months to years. The hard callus appears weeks later, not within 72 hours. Primary union (no callus) occurs only with rigid fixation and direct cortical contact. The remodeling zone is the final stage, not the bridging callus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fracture repair stages are classic HY: hematoma → fibrocartilaginous callus → bony callus → remodeling. Know that NSAIDs impair early inflammatory phase (hematoma/soft callus), potentially slowing healing. COMLEX: Smoking and diabetes impair bone repair — smoking reduces osteoblast activity and impairs angiogenesis needed for callus vascularization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q10","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following bones is formed via intramembranous ossification, in which mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts without requiring a cartilaginous intermediate template?","choices":{"A":"The femur, which forms via endochondral ossification in which a hyaline cartilage model is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity","B":"The skull, in which mesenchymal cells condense into nodules and differentiate directly into osteoblasts that deposit a collagen-proteoglycan matrix that then calcifies","C":"The tibia, which undergoes endochondral ossification from a primary center in the diaphysis that extends toward epiphyseal growth plates at both ends of the bone","D":"The humerus, which develops through endochondral ossification identical to other long bones of the appendicular skeleton, requiring a cartilaginous template at all stages","E":"The vertebral bodies, which are formed through endochondral ossification from somite-derived cartilaginous precursors during embryonic vertebral column development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intramembranous ossification forms flat bones including the skull (calvaria), mandible, clavicle, and some facial bones. In this process, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage template is needed. Endochondral ossification forms long bones (femur, tibia, humerus) and vertebral bodies, which require a cartilage scaffold that is progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cleidocranial dysostosis involves defective intramembranous ossification of the clavicle and cranial vault (RUNX2 mutation) — can present with absent clavicles allowing shoulder hyperflexion. COMLEX: Fontanelles represent areas of delayed intramembranous ossification — the anterior fontanelle closes by 18 months; delayed closure suggests hypothyroidism or rickets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q11","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"During endochondral ossification of a long bone, where does the primary ossification center first appear, and which event triggers the initial wave of bone matrix deposition at that location?","choices":{"A":"The epiphysis, where hypertrophied chondrocytes first calcify the surrounding matrix and attract osteoblast precursors to initiate the primary ossification sequence","B":"The growth plate, where chondrocyte proliferation produces the initial calcified template that osteoblasts later colonize to establish the primary ossification center","C":"The periosteum, where mesenchymal progenitors first differentiate into osteoblasts and lay down a periosteal collar that anchors the primary ossification process within the diaphysis","D":"The diaphysis, where vascular connective tissue invades the calcified cartilage model and introduces osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the cartilaginous scaffold","E":"The metaphysis, where blood vessel invasion first occurs near the flared ends of the bone and establishes the ossification center before extending into the diaphyseal shaft"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis (shaft) of the long bone. Mesodermal mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes that form the cartilage model. Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete a matrix that calcifies. The perichondrium then becomes periosteum as a bone collar forms. Vascular connective tissue subsequently invades from the periosteal region, bringing osteoblasts that deposit bone matrix onto the calcified cartilage scaffold. Secondary ossification centers later appear in the epiphyses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function mutation) disrupts endochondral ossification at the growth plate, causing the characteristic short-limb dwarfism — long bones are short but membranous bones of the skull are normal. COMLEX: Ossification centers appear at predictable developmental ages — important for reading pediatric radiographs and assessing bone age.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q12","sdlNumber":18,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Early in endochondral ossification of a long bone, a thin layer of bone is laid down around the middle of the cartilage model. What happens to the connective tissue layer that covers the diaphysis at this stage?","choices":{"A":"It becomes the endosteum lining the marrow cavity, after the cartilage beneath it is resorbed by osteoclasts","B":"It becomes the epiphyseal growth plate, which migrates toward the ends of the bone as the diaphysis lengthens","C":"The perichondrium becomes the periosteum once bone is deposited beneath it around the diaphysis of the model","D":"It becomes articular cartilage, which lacks a perichondrium so that it can exchange water with synovial fluid","E":"It degenerates by apoptosis, because a covering layer is no longer needed once the matrix of the model calcifies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide on endochondral ossification describes the sequence: mesenchymal cells become chondrocytes that proliferate, hypertrophy, and secrete matrix; the matrix calcifies; a thin layer of bone is deposited under the perichondrium surrounding the diaphysis, and the perichondrium becomes the periosteum. Vascular connective tissue then invades from the periosteum, bringing hemopoietic cells and osteoblasts that deposit bone on the calcified cartilage. The endosteum lines the marrow cavity but does not come from this covering layer. Growth plates are the epiphyseal cartilage plates at the diaphyseal-epiphyseal junctions. Articular cartilage covers joint surfaces and is not formed from the diaphyseal covering. The covering layer persists as periosteum and supplies osteoprogenitor cells throughout life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Endochondral ossification (long bones, vertebrae): cartilage model → bone collar under perichondrium (→ periosteum) → vascular invasion → primary center in diaphysis → secondary centers in epiphyses → growth at physes. Intramembranous: flat bones of skull, direct from mesenchyme. COMLEX: Achondroplasia (FGFR3) affects endochondral growth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — achondroplasia and endochondral growth plate failure"},{"id":"sdl18_q13","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with known osteogenesis imperfecta presents with his third long bone fracture in two years. Bone biopsy reveals a reduced ratio of osteoid to mineralized matrix and an abundance of large multinucleated cells actively excavating the bone surface. These multinucleated cells are derived from which embryological lineage?","choices":{"A":"Mesenchymal stromal cells of the inner periosteum, which differentiate sequentially into osteoblasts and subsequently fuse into multinucleated giant cells when stimulated by inflammatory cytokines","B":"Hematopoietic stem cells of the granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit lineage, which give rise to monocyte precursors that fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL stimulation","C":"Neural crest cells migrating from the dorsal neural tube, which provide the progenitor population for all bone-resorbing cells in the craniofacial and axial skeletal compartments","D":"Lateral plate mesodermal cells that differentiate into myoblasts and subsequently transdifferentiate into osteoclast precursors in response to mechanical unloading signals","E":"Endothelial progenitor cells derived from the aorta-gonad-mesonephros region, which give rise to the monocyte-macrophage lineage that fuses into multinucleated bone-resorbing cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteoclasts are derived from the hematopoietic granulocyte-macrophage colony-forming unit (GM-CFU) lineage — the same lineage that gives rise to monocytes and macrophages. Monocyte precursors are recruited to bone surfaces and fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts under RANKL and M-CSF stimulation. In osteogenesis imperfecta, defective Type I collagen is produced by osteoblasts (mesenchymal origin), while osteoclasts are hematopoietic-derived and function normally or are even hyperactive. Mesenchymal stromal cells give rise to osteoblasts, chondrocytes, and adipocytes — not osteoclasts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know both lineages — osteoblasts from mesenchymal stromal cells, osteoclasts from GM-CFU/monocyte lineage. Paget disease involves dysregulated osteoclast activity (measles virus-triggered). COMLEX: In multiple myeloma, plasma cells secrete RANKL and inhibit OPG, driving osteoclast activity and the punched-out lytic lesions on skull X-rays.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q14","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An astronaut returns from 6 months aboard the International Space Station and undergoes DEXA scanning, which shows a 1.5% decrease in lumbar spine bone mineral density. The loss of mechanical loading in microgravity primarily disrupts the function of which bone cell type, leading to the observed reduction in bone formation signaling?","choices":{"A":"Osteoblasts, which are the primary mechanosensors of bone and directly reduce their collagen secretion when gravity-mediated compressive forces are absent from the bone surface","B":"Periosteal stem cells, which rely on repetitive impact loading to maintain their proliferative state and undergo apoptosis during prolonged microgravity exposure","C":"Osteoclasts, which sense the lack of mechanical deformation through integrins on their ruffled border and downregulate their resorptive activity in a microgravity environment","D":"Osteocytes, which sense strain through their canalicular network; with unloading they increase sclerostin, suppressing Wnt-driven bone formation and favoring net resorption","E":"Haversian canal endothelial cells, which detect fluid shear stress changes caused by reduced loading and decrease vascular growth factor secretion required for bone cell maintenance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteocytes are the primary mechanosensors of bone. Embedded within the canalicular network, they detect fluid shear stress caused by mechanical loading and regulate bone remodeling accordingly. Under normal loading, osteocytes suppress sclerostin (which inhibits Wnt/bone formation signaling). In microgravity, absent mechanical stimulus leads to increased sclerostin production, reduced Wnt signaling, decreased osteoblast activity, and net bone loss. Osteoblasts produce matrix but are not the primary sensors. Osteoclasts increase activity in disuse osteoporosis but are not the primary sensing cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sclerostin is produced by osteocytes and inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, suppressing bone formation — romosozumab is a sclerostin inhibitor approved for severe osteoporosis. COMLEX: Wolff's Law states that bone remodels in response to mechanical stress — disuse osteoporosis from bed rest or paralysis is a common clinical manifestation tested on COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q15","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the histological structure and cellular composition of bone, focusing on the roles of osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and osteocytes.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A histology laboratory slide of cortical bone shows a central canal surrounded by concentric rings of mineralized lamellae with osteocytes in lacunae connected by fine radiating channels. A student is asked to identify the contents of the central canal of this structural unit. Which of the following is correct?","choices":{"A":"The central Haversian canal contains a marrow cavity with hematopoietic cells and osteoclast precursors that replenish the bone-resorbing cell population throughout adult life","B":"The central Haversian canal contains lymphatic channels exclusively, which drain interstitial fluid from the bone lacunae and prevent accumulation of metabolic waste products","C":"The central Haversian canal contains unmineralized osteoid and osteoblasts that continuously deposit new matrix in a centrifugal direction, expanding the osteon over time","D":"The central Haversian canal is an empty conduit that allows transmission of mechanical strain waves between adjacent osteons in the cortical bone structure","E":"The central Haversian canal contains blood vessels and nerve fibers that supply the osteocytes within the surrounding concentric lamellae via diffusion through the canalicular network"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon (Haversian system) is the basic structural unit of cortical bone, consisting of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central Haversian canal. The canal contains one or two small blood vessels (capillaries/arterioles) and nerve fibers. Nutrients and metabolic products diffuse to and from osteocytes through the canalicular network connecting lacunae. Volkmann's canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals, connecting them to the periosteal surface. The marrow cavity is a separate medullary compartment, not within individual osteons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the osteon hierarchy — Haversian canal (vessels/nerves) → lamellae → lacunae (osteocytes) → canaliculi (connections). Paget disease shows disorganized \"mosaic\" lamellae replacing normal osteons on histology. COMLEX: Osteoporosis thins cortical bone and reduces the number of intact osteons — this explains the increased fracture risk despite apparently normal-looking radiographs until significant mass is lost.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific MSK tissue types"},{"id":"sdl18_q16","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with stage 3 chronic kidney disease has a serum calcium of 7.1 mg/dL and an elevated PTH of 320 pg/mL on routine labs. Her provider explains that the elevated PTH is acting on bone to raise serum calcium. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which PTH increases serum calcium through bone?","choices":{"A":"PTH stimulates osteoblasts to raise RANKL and lower OPG, shifting the balance toward osteoclast activation and resorption that releases calcium and phosphate into circulation","B":"PTH directly activates osteoclasts by binding PTH receptors on their surface, causing immediate secretion of hydrogen ions and collagenase without requiring osteoblast intermediary signaling","C":"PTH stimulates osteocytes to release sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt signaling in osteoblasts and thereby promotes unopposed osteoclast-mediated bone resorption over time","D":"PTH induces osteoblasts to produce calcitonin locally, which paradoxically stimulates osteoclast activity through a non-canonical intracrine signaling pathway within bone","E":"PTH suppresses OPG expression in T lymphocytes, reducing the circulating anti-resorptive signal and allowing systemic RANKL levels to drive osteoclast differentiation throughout the skeleton"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH acts on PTH/PTHrP receptors expressed on osteoblasts (not directly on osteoclasts). PTH upregulates RANKL expression and downregulates OPG on osteoblasts, creating a pro-resorptive environment that activates osteoclast precursors. The resulting osteoclast activity releases Ca2+ and PO43− from hydroxyapatite. This is the dominant mechanism of PTH-mediated hypercalcemia. PTH does not directly activate osteoclasts (they lack PTH receptors in significant numbers). Sclerostin and calcitonin effects described are incorrect in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperparathyroidism (primary = parathyroid adenoma) causes hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, increased PTH — classic \"bones, groans, moans, stones.\" Renal osteodystrophy (secondary hyperparathyroidism) results from low vitamin D + hyperphosphatemia → low Ca → high PTH. COMLEX: Distinguish primary, secondary, and tertiary hyperparathyroidism — CKD drives secondary; autonomous gland develops into tertiary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q17","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a T-score of −2.7 at the femoral neck is started on alendronate for osteoporosis. Her physician explains that this medication directly targets the osteoclast to reduce bone resorption. Which of the following correctly describes the cellular mechanism by which bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast function?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates bind RANKL and function as decoy receptors, preventing RANKL from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors in a mechanism identical to that of osteoprotegerin","B":"Bisphosphonates are incorporated into the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice and increase mineral resistance to acid dissolution, physically preventing osteoclasts from creating resorption lacunae","C":"Bisphosphonates directly bind the PTH receptor on osteoblasts, preventing RANKL upregulation and thereby reducing downstream osteoclast activation throughout the remodeling cycle","D":"Bisphosphonates enter osteocytes through canalicular channels and inhibit sclerostin secretion, indirectly increasing Wnt-mediated osteoblast formation to outpace osteoclast resorption","E":"Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite at resorption sites, are taken up by osteoclasts, block farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, and trigger osteoclast apoptosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate, risedronate) are pyrophosphate analogs that bind avidly to hydroxyapatite at sites of active bone resorption. When osteoclasts resorb the bone surface, they ingest the bisphosphonate. Inside the cell, nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, impairing prenylation of signaling proteins required for osteoclast cytoskeletal function and survival, ultimately triggering apoptosis. Denosumab — not bisphosphonates — binds RANKL. Bisphosphonates have no direct action on PTH receptors, osteocytes, or sclerostin pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway) — same pathway inhibited by statins. Side effects include esophageal irritation, osteonecrosis of the jaw, and atypical femoral fractures with long-term use. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates are also used in Paget disease and osteogenesis imperfecta to reduce excess osteoclast activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q18","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the physiological mechanisms and hormones of bone remodeling and mineral homeostasis, emphasizing the balance between bone formation and resorption.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is found to have a serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D of 12 ng/mL and a serum calcium of 8.0 mg/dL. He is started on vitamin D supplementation. At which anatomical site does vitamin D primarily exert its calcium-raising effect, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The proximal renal tubule, where activated vitamin D binds to receptors on tubular cells and directly promotes calcium reabsorption from the tubular filtrate into the peritubular capillaries","B":"The osteoclast surface, where activated vitamin D binds receptors and directly stimulates RANKL-independent bone resorption to mobilize calcium stores into the circulation","C":"The small intestine, where activated vitamin D binds nuclear receptors in enterocytes to upregulate calbindin and calcium transport proteins, increasing active transcellular calcium absorption","D":"The parathyroid gland, where activated vitamin D binds chief cell receptors and directly stimulates PTH synthesis and secretion to drive downstream calcium mobilization from bone","E":"The large intestine, where activated vitamin D stimulates passive paracellular calcium absorption through the colonic epithelium without requiring active transport proteins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary site of vitamin D action to raise serum calcium is the small intestine (duodenum and jejunum). Active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, calcitriol) binds vitamin D receptors in enterocytes and upregulates calbindin and other calcium transport proteins, dramatically increasing transcellular calcium absorption. Vitamin D also increases renal calcium reabsorption and enhances PTH-induced bone resorption as secondary mechanisms, but the gut effect is primary. Vitamin D actually suppresses PTH synthesis (feedback inhibition) rather than stimulating it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → rickets in children (bowed legs, rachitic rosary) and osteomalacia in adults (bone pain, pseudo-fractures). Know the activation pathway: liver (25-hydroxylation) → kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylation to calcitriol). COMLEX: Granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis, TB) cause hypercalcemia by producing 1-alpha-hydroxylase in macrophages, creating excess calcitriol.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis pathophysiology involving bone remodeling imbalance"},{"id":"sdl18_q19","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player sustains a closed mid-shaft tibial fracture. Six weeks later, plain radiographs show a calcified bridge spanning the fracture gap. The orthopedic surgeon explains that this represents normal progression of fracture healing. Which phase of bone repair does this radiographic finding represent?","choices":{"A":"The hematoma phase, in which organized clot at the fracture site has begun to mineralize, forming a calcified scaffold that will eventually be remodeled into compact bone","B":"The soft callus phase, in which fibrocartilaginous tissue spans the fracture gap and is visible on radiograph due to early calcium deposition within the cartilaginous matrix","C":"The remodeling phase, in which lamellar bone has replaced disorganized woven bone and the medullary canal has been restored to its normal architecture throughout the fracture zone","D":"The hard callus phase, in which the fibrocartilaginous soft callus has been replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification, producing the calcified bridge visible on radiograph","E":"The primary union phase, in which direct bone-to-bone contact between rigidly immobilized fracture ends has allowed osteons to bridge the gap without any callus formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fracture repair proceeds through four overlapping stages: (1) hematoma formation and inflammation (up to 3-7 days, not visible on radiograph), (2) soft callus formation (about 2 weeks, fibrocartilaginous, radiolucent on X-ray), (3) hard callus formation (6+ weeks, woven bone replaces fibrocartilage through endochondral ossification — this is the calcified bridging callus visible on radiograph), and (4) remodeling (months to years, woven bone replaced by lamellar bone) (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Primary union occurs only with rigid fixation and compressed cortical contact — no callus forms in that scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 4 stages of fracture repair are high-yield: hematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodeling. Know that smoking, steroids, and NSAIDs (especially in the early phases) impair healing. COMLEX: Delayed union vs. non-union — non-union occurs when the fracture fails to heal by expected time, often requiring surgical intervention; pseudarthrosis is a fibrous non-union that creates a false joint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Primary regions of long bones and their anatomical organization"},{"id":"sdl18_q20","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has a closed tibial shaft fracture. Tissue taken from the fracture gap 5 days after injury is examined under the microscope. Which description best fits the repair stage expected at this time?","choices":{"A":"Woven bone trabeculae lined by osteoblasts bridging the fragments, forming a hard callus that allows full weight bearing","B":"Hyaline cartilage undergoing calcification and endochondral replacement across the whole gap, forming a mature soft callus","C":"Lamellar compact bone with a restored marrow cavity, with osteoclasts removing the last remnants of hard callus","D":"Dense regular collagen bundles aligned along the bone axis, laid down by tenocytes that migrate from nearby tendons","E":"A fracture hematoma with neutrophils and macrophages, necrotic bone ends, and early granulation tissue replacing the clot"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pawlina describes the first stage of indirect fracture healing as an acute inflammatory phase lasting about 1 week: a fracture hematoma forms around the bone ends, the ends of the fragments undergo necrosis, cytokines draw in neutrophils followed by macrophages, fibroblasts and capillaries proliferate, and the hematoma is gradually replaced by granulation tissue. At day 5 this is the expected picture. The fibrocartilaginous soft callus forms at about 2 to 3 weeks, and the bony hard callus needs about 3 to 4 months. Remodeling into lamellar bone with a restored marrow cavity takes months to years. Fracture repair does not use tendon-type dense regular collagen from tenocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fracture healing: hematoma and inflammation (~1 week) → granulation tissue → fibrocartilaginous soft callus (2–3 weeks) → bony hard callus (3–4 months) → remodeling (months–years). COMLEX: Inflammation is required for healing; stable fixation speeds union.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — NSAID inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis"},{"id":"sdl18_q21","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify and discuss the blood supply and innervation of bones and explain the mechanisms of bone repair and regeneration.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman had a femoral shaft fracture that united 14 months ago. Serial radiographs over the past year show the bulky callus shrinking, the cortex becoming smoother and denser, and the marrow cavity reappearing across the old fracture site. Which process explains these changes?","choices":{"A":"Formation of a fibrocartilaginous soft callus that is just now stabilizing the fragments enough to allow weight bearing","B":"Delayed union with resorption of the callus by infection, because callus should never shrink after union has occurred","C":"Remodeling, in which osteoclasts and osteoblasts convert woven callus into lamellar bone and restore the original shape","D":"Intramembranous ossification of a new cartilage model, which replaces the hard callus with a second, larger callus","E":"Disuse osteoporosis of the healed segment, in which cortical bone is lost without replacement after immobilization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pawlina explains that the hard callus gives mechanical stability but does not restore the properties of normal bone. Remodeling converts the woven bone of the callus into mature lamellar bone; osteoclasts remove the remnants of the hard callus, the marrow cavity is restored, and the bone gradually returns to its original shape over months to years. Shrinking callus with a denser, smoother cortex and a reappearing marrow cavity after union is remodeling. The soft callus forms at about 2 to 3 weeks, not after a year. Callus shrinkage after union is expected and does not signal infection. Intramembranous ossification does not use a cartilage model. Disuse loss would thin the cortex, not smooth and densify it while restoring the canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Woven (immature) bone → lamellar (mature) bone through coupled osteoclast resorption and osteoblast formation; bone remodels along lines of stress (Wolff's law). COMLEX: Remodeling continues for months to years after clinical union.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — remodeling potential of pediatric bone"},{"id":"sdl18_q22","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student studying embryology traces the sequence of events in intramembranous ossification of the calvaria. After osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts begin to bind and solidify the matrix, which structural formation is the next observable event in this process?","choices":{"A":"A hyaline cartilage template forms around the calcified matrix to provide mechanical stability before vascular invasion occurs and osteoblasts replace the cartilage with lamellar bone","B":"Osteoclasts are recruited to the calcified region to resorb the initial disorganized spicules, and osteoblasts then deposit organized lamellar bone in a centripetal direction","C":"Chondrocytes hypertrophy and secrete alkaline phosphatase into the calcified matrix, creating additional mineralization fronts that accelerate spicule formation and bone collar development","D":"Growth plate chondrocytes proliferate at the margin of each calcified center and drive longitudinal expansion of the flat bone anlage through endochondral ossification","E":"Calcified spicules radiate outward from ossification centers, and trapped osteoblasts within the solidifying matrix differentiate into osteocytes as the region becomes enclosed by the periosteum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In intramembranous ossification, after osteoblasts deposit collagen-proteoglycan matrix and calcium salts bind and solidify it: (1) calcified spicules radiate outward from the ossification center, (2) osteoblasts trapped within the solidifying matrix become osteocytes, and (3) the entire region — consisting of centers with radiating spicules — becomes enclosed within the periosteum. No cartilage template forms at any stage. Osteoclasts participate later in remodeling but are not the next immediate event. Growth plates and chondrocyte proliferation are features of endochondral, not intramembranous, ossification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intramembranous ossification produces woven bone initially (rapidly deposited, disorganized collagen) that is later replaced by lamellar bone during remodeling — the same sequence occurs in fracture repair (soft callus → hard callus → remodeling). COMLEX: Fontanelles — the soft spots on a newborn's skull — are areas where intramembranous ossification is incomplete; the anterior fontanelle is the last to close at approximately 18 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q23","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy falls from his bicycle and sustains a Salter-Harris Type II fracture of the distal radius. The pediatric orthopedist explains to his parents that this injury is concerning because it involves the growth plate. Which feature of endochondral ossification makes growth plate injury particularly significant in skeletally immature patients?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal growth occurs by chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy at the growth plate, so disruption here can cause premature physeal closure with limb length discrepancy or angular deformity","B":"The growth plate is the site where intramembranous ossification transitions to endochondral ossification, and injury to this zone eliminates the only mechanism by which flat bones can increase their surface area","C":"All blood supply to the epiphysis travels exclusively through the growth plate cartilage, and fracture at this level causes immediate avascular necrosis of the entire epiphyseal ossification center","D":"The growth plate is the primary location of osteoclast activity in growing bone, and its disruption leads to unchecked osteoblast activity and paradoxical overgrowth of the affected limb","E":"Periosteal stem cells are exclusively located within the growth plate zone, and injury to this region eliminates all appositional growth capacity throughout the remainder of skeletal development"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In endochondral ossification, longitudinal bone growth is driven by chondrocyte proliferation in the zone of proliferation and hypertrophy within the epiphyseal growth plate. Ossification of these cartilage columns at the metaphysis progressively lengthens the bone. The Salter-Harris classification describes fractures involving the physis — injuries to the germinal zone of the growth plate (Salter-Harris III, IV, V) carry the highest risk of premature physeal closure, which can result in limb length discrepancy or angular deformity. Blood supply to the epiphysis comes from epiphyseal arteries that are separate from the growth plate vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris classification is HY: Type I (through physis, \"SALTER\" mnemonic — Straight), II (through physis and metaphysis — most common, best prognosis), III (physis and epiphysis), IV (all three), V (crush — worst prognosis). COMLEX: Always obtain comparison X-rays for pediatric wrist injuries — the physis can be confused with a fracture line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"},{"id":"sdl18_q24","sdlNumber":18,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the processes of intramembranous and endochondral ossification in bone development and their relevance to skeletal formation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is reviewing fetal bone development. She notes that the femur and the skull each begin as collections of mesenchymal cells but develop through entirely different ossification mechanisms. Which single feature most fundamentally distinguishes endochondral ossification of the femur from intramembranous ossification of the calvaria?","choices":{"A":"Endochondral ossification produces only cancellous bone whereas intramembranous ossification exclusively generates cortical bone, reflecting their distinct structural requirements in the adult skeleton","B":"Endochondral ossification requires a hyaline cartilage intermediate replaced by bone after vascular invasion, whereas intramembranous bone forms directly from mesenchymal cells with no cartilage phase","C":"Endochondral ossification is driven entirely by neural crest cell-derived progenitors, whereas intramembranous ossification depends exclusively on lateral plate mesodermal cells for bone formation","D":"Endochondral ossification forms bones that contain only red marrow throughout life, whereas intramembranous ossification forms bones that contain exclusively yellow marrow after puberty","E":"Endochondral ossification proceeds without periosteal formation, relying solely on the perichondrium, whereas intramembranous ossification forms a periosteum that is the exclusive source of appositional growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The defining difference between the two mechanisms is the presence or absence of a cartilage template. In endochondral ossification, mesenchymal cells first differentiate into chondrocytes, forming a hyaline cartilage model that is then progressively replaced by bone through vascular invasion and osteoblast activity. In intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells differentiate directly into osteoblasts — no cartilage is produced at any stage. Both processes can produce cortical and cancellous bone; marrow composition and periosteum formation are not the distinguishing features. The femur is mesodermal and the skull has both mesodermal and neural crest contributions, but germ-layer origin is not the defining feature of the ossification mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia (FGFR3 gain-of-function) specifically impairs endochondral ossification at the growth plate — long bones are short, but the vault of the skull (intramembranous) is of normal size, producing the characteristic macrocephaly appearance. This distinction is a frequent NBME question. COMLEX: Achondroplasia is the most common form of short-limb dwarfism; foramen magnum stenosis causing myelopathy is a serious complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation via mesenchymal differentiation"}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"Spine & Back Pain","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary pathophysiologic mechanism of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Progressive depletion of proteoglycan from articular cartilage reduces its viscoelastic properties, leading to fibrillation, chondrocyte apoptosis, and reactive subchondral bone changes","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium drives pannus formation, which progressively erodes articular cartilage and underlying subchondral bone via matrix metalloproteinase secretion","C":"Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes that produce progressive joint space narrowing and fibrillation","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha and IL-1beta drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and periarticular bone remodeling across multiple joints","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular soft tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade that produces chronic joint space narrowing and cartilage surface erosion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OA begins with chondrocyte dysfunction and loss of proteoglycans from the extracellular matrix, reducing cartilage resilience. This leads to fibrillation, fissuring, and eventual full-thickness cartilage loss. Secondary reactive changes — subchondral sclerosis, cyst formation, and osteophyte formation — follow. Pannus, CPPD, TNF-alpha, and uric acid describe RA, pseudogout, systemic inflammatory arthritis, and gout, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = loss of proteoglycans → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis (no periarticular osteopenia). COMLEX: OA is the most common musculoskeletal complaint in primary care; OMM can address somatic dysfunction and muscle imbalance contributing to joint loading.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q02","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements best describes the epidemiology of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis is the most prevalent joint disease globally, affecting 10 to 15% of the adult population, with peak onset in adults between the ages of 40 and 60 years","B":"Gout is the most common form of inflammatory arthritis and the most prevalent joint disease in men over age 50, with lifetime prevalence exceeding 50% as serum uric acid rises","C":"Psoriatic arthritis is the most frequently diagnosed joint disorder in middle-aged adults, accounting for the largest proportion of primary care musculoskeletal visits annually in the United States","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis is the most prevalent joint disease affecting the axial skeleton, with a radiographic prevalence of 20 to 25% in adults over age 40 in large population studies","E":"Osteoarthritis is the most common form of arthritis worldwide, with prevalence increasing sharply after age 50 and radiographic changes present in the majority of adults over age 65"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA is the most common arthritis globally. It affects about 70% of the general population over 65 (SDL 5 reading, Symptom to Diagnosis), though symptoms do not always correlate with imaging. Risk factors include age, obesity, prior joint injury, and female sex. RA is far less common (about 2% of persons older than 60 in the same source). Gout is more common than RA but far less than OA, and AS is less common still.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know OA epidemiology — age is the strongest risk factor; obesity drives knee OA specifically. COMLEX: OA is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8 (musculoskeletal) condition; OMM + weight loss + exercise are first-line osteopathic management principles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q03","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines, which of the following best describes the first-line approach to managing symptomatic osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Opioid analgesics are preferred over NSAIDs as first-line pharmacologic therapy for OA pain, particularly in elderly patients with elevated cardiovascular risk who cannot tolerate COX inhibition","B":"Exercise therapy and weight loss are the foundational first-line interventions, with NSAIDs added for pain control when nonpharmacologic measures alone provide insufficient symptomatic relief","C":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injections are recommended as the initial intervention for all patients with symptomatic knee OA before attempting any pharmacologic or nonpharmacologic trial","D":"Surgical joint replacement is appropriate as the primary management strategy for patients with radiographic OA and pain, regardless of the duration or response to conservative measures","E":"Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs targeting synovial inflammation, such as methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine, are the first-line pharmacologic treatment for all symptomatic OA patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OARSI and ACR guidelines place exercise and weight loss at the top of OA management — they reduce pain and improve function with minimal risk. NSAIDs (topical or oral) are the preferred analgesics when pharmacotherapy is needed. Intra-articular injections are reserved for inadequate response to first-line therapy. Opioids and DMARDs have no role in standard OA management. Surgery is last resort.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA management ladder — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical agents → intra-articular injections → joint replacement. COMLEX: OMM addresses muscle imbalance and somatic dysfunction that contribute to abnormal joint loading; integrative approaches (acupuncture, OMM) are COMLEX-favored first steps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging all impact joint health; OA is the prototypical age-driven joint failure syndrome"},{"id":"sdl19_q04","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the preferred imaging modality when neurological symptoms accompany cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Plain radiographs with flexion-extension views are the gold standard because they detect dynamic instability and cord compression during movement that static MRI cannot assess at rest","B":"CT myelography is the first-line study for all cases of cervical spondylosis with neurological symptoms because it provides superior visualization of bony osteophytes and foraminal stenosis","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes disc morphology, spinal cord signal changes, nerve root compression within the foramina, and canal dimensions without radiation exposure","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the preferred study for cervical spondylosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at degenerated levels that radiographs and MRI cannot adequately detect","E":"Ultrasound of the cervical spine is the recommended initial imaging for cervical spondylosis because it provides dynamic visualization with low cost and absence of ionizing radiation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the imaging of choice when cervical spondylosis produces neurological symptoms, as it shows cord signal (T2 hyperintensity = myelomalacia), disc morphology, and nerve root compression. Plain radiographs diagnose and monitor spondylosis (narrowing, osteophytes) but cannot visualize cord or soft tissue. CT myelography is reserved for MRI-contraindicated patients. Bone scan and ultrasound have no role in standard evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc + osteophyte + facet arthropathy. X-ray shows it; MRI defines cord/root involvement. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 is a common OMM indication in cervical spondylosis patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q05","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate prevalence of radiographic cervical spondylosis by age 65, and how does this correlate with symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spondylosis is radiographically present in approximately 40% of individuals by age 65, with clinical symptoms developing in roughly half of those with positive imaging findings","B":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis is present in approximately 95% of individuals by age 65, but the degree of radiographic change correlates poorly with clinical symptoms","C":"By age 65, radiographic cervical spondylosis is detectable in fewer than 60% of adults, and the presence of osteophytes strongly predicts future development of radiculopathy or myelopathy","D":"Cervical spondylosis develops radiographically in approximately 75% of individuals by age 65, with the C2-C3 level most commonly affected due to its high segmental mobility","E":"Cervical spondylosis affects approximately 50% of adults over age 65, with symptomatic disease developing in the majority of those with radiographic disc space narrowing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Radiographic cervical spondylosis — disc space narrowing, osteophytes, endplate sclerosis — is nearly universal by age 65 (~95%). However, there is a marked disconnect between imaging and symptoms; many patients with severe radiographic changes are asymptomatic. This makes imaging findings alone insufficient to guide management decisions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key concept — radiographic spondylosis ≠ clinical disease. Do not treat the image. COMLEX: Cervical spondylosis is a COMLEX-recognized condition where somatic dysfunction contributes to symptom amplification; OMM targets the functional component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q06","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the typical clinical presentation of cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute onset of bilateral arm weakness and bilateral loss of upper extremity reflexes, reflecting simultaneous multilevel nerve root compression across all cervical segments bilaterally","B":"Severe, localized neck pain occurring in discrete episodic attacks separated by completely pain-free intervals of weeks to months, without any associated stiffness or range of motion restriction","C":"Prominent lower extremity signs including bilateral leg weakness and gait difficulty as the dominant presenting features, reflecting early cervical myelopathy before any axial neck pain","D":"Axial neck pain, periscapular aching, and shoulder girdle stiffness with reduced cervical range of motion, often with intermittent upper extremity radiation but without fixed neurological deficits","E":"Cranial nerve deficits including facial numbness and diplopia caused by vertebrobasilar insufficiency from upper cervical disc disease at C1-C2 as the primary presenting complaint"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis most often presents with chronic axial neck pain, stiffness, and periscapular discomfort. Upper extremity radiation may occur. Fixed neurological deficits (weakness, reflex loss) suggest radiculopathy; bilateral long-tract signs (hyperreflexia, gait instability) suggest myelopathy — both are complications rather than the typical spondylosis presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spondylosis = neck pain + stiffness ± referred shoulder pain. Radiculopathy = adds dermatomal arm pain/weakness/reflex loss. Myelopathy = adds UMN signs + gait disturbance. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction with restricted sidebending and paraspinal hypertonicity is the OMM target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and RANKL/OPG balance govern vertebral osteophyte formation, the structural hallmark of cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q07","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the diagnostic properties of Spurling's test for cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% sensitivity and 45% specificity, meaning a negative result effectively rules out cervical nerve root compression in most clinical settings","B":"Spurling's test has approximately 90% specificity and 45% sensitivity, meaning a positive result strongly suggests nerve root compression but a negative result does not reliably exclude it","C":"Spurling's test has approximately 70% sensitivity and 70% specificity, representing a balanced trade-off that makes it equally useful as both a rule-in and rule-out test for radiculopathy","D":"Spurling's test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 80%, and a positive result combined with MRI findings confirms the need for immediate surgical foraminotomy","E":"Spurling's test has approximately 45% specificity for cervical radiculopathy, which limits its diagnostic utility due to high rates of false-positive results in patients with axial neck pain alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spurling's test (pushing down on top of the head with the neck extended and the head leaning toward the symptomatic side) is a specific but insensitive test: 90% specific, 45% sensitive (Tintinalli Table 279-2, the SDL 19 course reading). This means a positive test is highly meaningful (rules in radiculopathy), but a negative test does not rule it out. It is best used in conjunction with history, dermatomal examination, and MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spurling = 90% specific, 45% sensitive — use it to RULE IN cervical radiculopathy. COMLEX: Cervical radiculopathy is a named COMLEX Clinical Presentation; OMM including cervical muscle energy and HVLA (with caution in stenosis) addresses associated somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q08","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cervical radiculopathy has numbness in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger along with weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"C7 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm, ring finger, and little finger with weakness of elbow extension and a diminished triceps reflex at the C7 level","B":"C5 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the lateral arm and deltoid region with weakness of shoulder abduction and a diminished biceps reflex at the C5 root level","C":"C8 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the medial forearm with intrinsic hand muscle weakness and diminished grip strength without a reliable deep tendon reflex loss","D":"C6 radiculopathy produces sensory loss in the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger with weakness of elbow flexion and supination and a diminished brachioradialis reflex","E":"T1 radiculopathy produces sensory loss over the medial arm and axilla with interosseous muscle weakness and bilateral reflex changes from bilateral nerve root involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"C6 is compressed at C5-C6 and covers the lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger. Motor: elbow flexion, forearm supination/pronation (course root table). Reflex: brachioradialis. C5 = lateral arm/deltoid, shoulder abduction, biceps reflex. C7 = middle finger, triceps, triceps reflex. C8 = ring/little finger, finger flexion, no reliable reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY nerve root table: C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex; C6 = elbow flexion and forearm supination/BR (or biceps) reflex/thumb-index; C7 = triceps/triceps reflex/middle finger; C8 = hand intrinsics/ring-little. COMLEX: Cervical somatic dysfunction at C5-C6 commonly accompanies C6 radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q09","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes appropriate initial management of acute cervical radiculopathy without progressive neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical discectomy is indicated when Spurling's test is positive, as nerve root inflammation from disc herniation will not resolve within an acceptable timeframe without decompression","B":"Emergent cervical traction as the primary intervention, applied continuously at 10 to 15 pounds for 24-hour periods until the radicular arm pain has fully subsided","C":"Activity modification, analgesics, a short course of oral glucocorticoids for severe pain, and a soft cervical collar for comfort, with physical therapy and OMM added as symptoms allow","D":"Epidural steroid injection within the first 48 hours is mandatory, as early neural blockade is the only intervention proven to prevent permanent neurological deficits from nerve root inflammation","E":"High-dose intravenous methylprednisolone for 3 days followed by surgical foraminotomy, as conservative measures are uniformly ineffective for acute nerve root inflammation in cervical radiculopathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The majority of acute cervical radiculopathy resolves with conservative management. First-line includes activity modification, NSAIDs/analgesics, a short collar for comfort, and a brief glucocorticoid taper for severe pain. PT, cervical traction, and OMM are added as tolerated. Epidural injections are reserved for persistent symptoms. Surgery is indicated only for progressive neurological deficits or failure of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most cervical radiculopathy resolves conservatively. Surgery only for motor deficits or failed conservative therapy. COMLEX: OMM is a first-line adjunct — cervical HVLA is relatively contraindicated with significant stenosis, but muscle energy and soft tissue techniques are appropriate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow and cervical radiculopathy share a foraminal compression mechanism producing dermatomal sensory loss"},{"id":"sdl19_q10","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following neurological findings distinguishes cervical myelopathy from uncomplicated cervical radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron signs exclusively, including flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and fasciculations at all levels below the stenotic segment, characterize cervical myelopathy and distinguish it from radiculopathy","B":"Loss of all deep tendon reflexes bilaterally is the hallmark of cervical myelopathy, reflecting global axon degeneration within the central spinal cord at the level of the stenotic segment","C":"Autonomic instability including orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, and bowel and bladder incontinence is the earliest and most distinguishing finding of cervical myelopathy from cervical radiculopathy","D":"Strictly unilateral upper and lower extremity weakness with contralateral sensory loss is the classical presentation of cervical myelopathy, reflecting a Brown-Sequard cord hemisection pattern","E":"Upper motor neuron signs — including hyperreflexia below the level of cord compression, spastic gait, positive Hoffman's sign, and positive Babinski sign — indicate spinal cord involvement characteristic of myelopathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Myelopathy = spinal cord compression → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's sign (finger flexion on flicking middle finger), Babinski, clonus, and gait disturbance. Radiculopathy = nerve root compression → LMN signs at the affected root level (weakness, hyporeflexia, dermatomal sensory loss) without long-tract involvement. These are fundamentally distinct clinical syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy = UMN signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman's, Babinski, spastic gait) + hand clumsiness. Hoffman's sign = flicking the middle finger nail produces involuntary flexion of thumb/index. COMLEX: OMM (HVLA) is CONTRAINDICATED in cervical myelopathy due to cord vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q11","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes Lhermitte's sign and its clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"Lhermitte's sign is an electric shock-like sensation down the spine and into the limbs with neck flexion, indicating posterior column dysfunction from cord compression or demyelination","B":"Lhermitte's sign is a pain response elicited by axial compression of the cervical spine in neutral position, indicating facet joint arthropathy at the symptomatic cervical level","C":"Lhermitte's sign refers to reproduction of radicular arm pain with simultaneous ipsilateral neck rotation and extension, equivalent to Spurling's maneuver performed in the supine position","D":"Lhermitte's sign is defined as reproduction of radicular symptoms by tapping the posterior cervical spinous processes, indicating foraminal nerve root impingement at the level tapped","E":"Lhermitte's sign is involuntary finger flexion elicited by flicking the middle finger downward, indicating upper motor neuron dysfunction in the corticospinal tract at or above C5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lhermitte's sign (neck flexion → electric shock down spine into limbs) indicates posterior column pathology — compressed or demyelinated dorsal column axons generate ectopic impulses when mechanically deformed by flexion. It occurs in cervical myelopathy from stenosis but is also classic for MS. Note: involuntary finger flexion on middle-finger flick = Hoffman's sign, not Lhermitte's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lhermitte's = neck flexion → electric shock down spine. Seen in: MS, cervical myelopathy, B12 deficiency. Hoffman's = flick middle finger → thumb/index flex = UMN sign. Both appear on boards — do not confuse. COMLEX: Both findings contraindicate cervical HVLA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q12","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the treatment approach for cervical spinal stenosis compared to uncomplicated cervical spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated exclusively with immediate surgical laminectomy, as conservative measures uniformly fail and delays increase the risk of permanent spinal cord injury","B":"Cervical spinal stenosis is managed with epidural corticosteroid injections as the primary intervention, providing durable cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in most patients","C":"Initial management mirrors cervical spondylosis — NSAIDs, physical therapy, and OMM — but surgical decompression becomes indicated when myelopathic signs develop or progressive neurological deficits occur","D":"Cervical spinal stenosis requires 12 weeks of immobilization in a rigid cervical orthosis as the primary treatment, after which most patients achieve complete neurological recovery without surgery","E":"Cervical spinal stenosis is treated with long-term oral corticosteroids to reduce cord edema, combined with muscle relaxants for associated paraspinal spasm and cervicogenic headache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without myelopathy, cervical stenosis is managed like spondylosis: activity modification, NSAIDs, PT, OMM (with caution — no HVLA in significant stenosis). Surgical indication: myelopathy, progressive neurological deficits, or failure of adequate conservative therapy. Epidural injections may help radiculopathy but do not decompress the cord. Immobilization alone is not definitive treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical decompression (laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion) for myelopathy. Conservative management for asymptomatic or radiculopathy-only stenosis. COMLEX: Soft tissue OMM and muscle energy are safe in stenosis; HVLA at the level of stenosis is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — Structural features of synovial and fibrous joints explain how facet arthropathy and ligamentum flavum hypertrophy narrow the cervical canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q13","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lifetime prevalence of low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Up to 80% of people experience at least one significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, making it one of the most prevalent and economically burdensome musculoskeletal conditions","B":"Approximately 30% of adults experience at least one clinically significant episode of low back pain during their lifetime, with the majority recovering fully without any medical intervention","C":"Lifetime prevalence of low back pain is approximately 50%, with the highest incidence in adults aged 25 to 45 and prevalence declining significantly after age 60 as activity levels decrease","D":"Low back pain affects approximately 20% of adults at any given time, and the majority of primary care musculoskeletal visits involve lumbar spine disease as the chief complaint","E":"Approximately 65% of adults experience at least one episode of disabling low back pain, with chronic pain developing in one-third and representing the leading cause of opioid prescribing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LBP lifetime prevalence is ~80%. It is the leading cause of disability worldwide and a top reason for primary care visits. Most acute episodes resolve within 4-6 weeks with conservative management (80% to 90% of patients, Tintinalli Ch 279). Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) are stronger predictors of chronification than structural findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80% lifetime prevalence; most acute LBP resolves without imaging. Red flags: age >50, fever, weight loss, cancer history, IV drug use, neurological deficit, bladder/bowel dysfunction — warrant further workup. COMLEX: LBP is the most common indication for OMM referral; somatic dysfunction is a recognized contributor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q14","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical presentation of cauda equina syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral sciatica with an absent Achilles reflex and foot drop from single-level L5-S1 nerve root compression that resolves fully with epidural steroid injection within 2 to 3 weeks","B":"Saddle area anesthesia affecting the perineum, inner thighs, and genitalia, combined with bilateral lower extremity weakness and bladder or bowel dysfunction, representing a surgical emergency","C":"Fever, elevated ESR, and bilateral radicular leg pain from infectious epidural abscess that requires IV antibiotics and not urgent surgical decompression in most immunocompetent patients","D":"Bilateral Hoffman's signs and spastic paraplegia from upper motor neuron involvement caused by thoracic spinal cord compression rather than a lumbosacral cauda equina process","E":"Midline low back pain, bilateral hamstring tightness, and positive bilateral straight leg raise without any neurological deficits or sphincter involvement requiring emergent intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) is caused by massive central disc herniation or other mass compressing the cauda equina at or below the conus. Classic triad: saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction (the course slide lists usually incontinence; retention also occurs), bilateral leg weakness/pain. This is a surgical emergency — emergent MRI and urgent decompression within 48 hours to prevent permanent sphincter loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cauda equina = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness = EMERGENCY. Next step = emergent MRI lumbar spine. COMLEX: CES is an absolute contraindication to OMM; immediate surgical referral is required.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q15","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes first-line management of acute mechanical low back pain without red flags or neurological deficits?","choices":{"A":"Complete bed rest for 2 to 3 weeks combined with opioid analgesics, as early mobilization risks converting an acute injury to chronic pain syndrome through sensitization of spinal pain generators","B":"Immediate lumbar MRI to exclude disc herniation, followed by epidural steroid injection within 72 hours to prevent progression of mechanical pain to chronic radiculopathy","C":"Oral corticosteroids for 10 to 14 days with strict activity restriction, as prostaglandin-mediated inflammation is the primary driver of acute mechanical low back pain tissue damage","D":"Urgent referral to spine surgery within the first week, as structural instability from acute muscle and ligament injury requires evaluation for possible surgical stabilization","E":"Relative rest with continuation of tolerated activities, NSAIDs for analgesia, superficial heat application, and early physical therapy; prolonged bed rest is discouraged as it worsens outcomes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP management: stay active (bed rest worsens outcomes), NSAIDs/acetaminophen, heat, and early PT. Muscle relaxants may be added short-term for spasm. OMM is appropriate and COMLEX-favored. Opioids are not first-line. Imaging is not indicated in the first 4-6 weeks without red flags. Most cases resolve within 6 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. NSAIDs > acetaminophen for LBP. Avoid prolonged bed rest. COMLEX: OMM (soft tissue, muscle energy, counterstrain) for lumbar somatic dysfunction is a high-yield first-line intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — Low back pain is a primary indication for integrative medicine; OMM, acupuncture, and exercise are COMLEX-recognized management strategies"},{"id":"sdl19_q16","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best characterizes the pain pattern of lumbosacral spondylosis?","choices":{"A":"Pain and stiffness are worst upon waking and improve gradually with movement and activity through the day, reflecting joint and disc stiffness that loosens with use","B":"Pain is worst at the end of the workday after sustained activity and loading, and is completely relieved within minutes of lying down flat in the supine position","C":"Pain is intermittent and colicky in character, radiating to the groin, and associated with nausea, distinguishing it from mechanical causes of low back pain","D":"Pain is constant and unrelenting, waking the patient from sleep at night and unresponsive to any positional change, consistent with an underlying destructive or malignant process","E":"Pain occurs exclusively with lumbar flexion and is absent with extension, reflecting anterior disc pathology and differentiating it from posterior element or facet-mediated pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"LS spondylosis (age-related DDD and facet OA) causes morning stiffness that improves with activity — the classic \"gelling\" phenomenon of degenerative joint disease. Pain may be localized or refer to the buttocks. Night pain, constitutional symptoms, or pain unresponsive to position should raise concern for malignancy or infection (red flags). Colicky radiation to groin suggests renal/urological etiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Morning stiffness improving with activity = OA/spondylosis pattern. Night pain + weight loss + fever = red flags for malignancy or infection. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis commonly presents with restricted spinal motion and somatic dysfunction — OMM is integral to management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q17","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred when lumbosacral spondylosis presents with neurological symptoms such as leg pain or weakness?","choices":{"A":"Plain lumbar radiographs with oblique views are the gold standard when neurological symptoms accompany spondylosis, as they directly visualize foraminal narrowing at each lumbar level","B":"CT myelography is the first-line imaging for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and neurological symptoms, surpassing MRI in detecting foraminal stenosis and arachnoid adhesions","C":"Nuclear medicine bone scan is indicated for all patients with lumbosacral spondylosis and radicular symptoms, identifying active metabolic changes at degenerated levels better than cross-sectional imaging","D":"MRI is the preferred study because it simultaneously assesses disc integrity, nerve root compression, vertebral endplate Modic changes, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and spinal canal dimensions","E":"Discography is the preferred initial imaging for lumbosacral spondylosis with neurological symptoms, identifying the pain-generating disc level through provocative contrast injection testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MRI is the imaging workhorse for lumbar spondylosis with neurological symptoms: it shows disc degeneration (T2 signal loss), herniation, end plate Modic changes, facet hypertrophy, ligamentum flavum thickening, and foraminal/central stenosis. Plain X-rays show alignment, disc height, and osteophytes but cannot visualize soft tissue or nerve roots. CT myelography is used when MRI is contraindicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI for neurological symptoms with lumbar spondylosis. X-ray for initial spondylosis workup (alignment, disc height, osteophytes). CT if MRI contraindicated. COMLEX: MRI guides surgical planning; OMM is appropriate while awaiting imaging or during conservative management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q18","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the prevalence of lumbosacral spondylosis in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects fewer than 20% of adults over age 50, with most lumbar degenerative changes representing clinically insignificant incidental imaging findings","B":"Lumbosacral spondylosis has a uniform prevalence of approximately 30% across all adult age groups with no significant increase in prevalence associated with advancing age","C":"Prevalence ranges from 38% to 85% across studied populations, varying with age, imaging modality, and the specific radiographic criteria applied for diagnosis","D":"Lumbosacral spondylosis affects nearly 95% of adults over age 60, mirroring cervical spondylosis prevalence, with most cases being symptomatic and requiring clinical management","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis is present in fewer than 10% of adults aged 40 to 60 on MRI, increasing only modestly to 25% in those over age 70 in large epidemiologic studies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LS spondylosis prevalence is wide — 38-85% depending on the population and criteria used — and increases with age. This wide range reflects the spectrum from mild disc height loss to severe multilevel disease. Like cervical spondylosis, radiographic findings often do not correlate with symptoms. Over 50% of adults without low back pain have bulging or degenerative discs on imaging (Foundations Ch 42).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative changes are common and often asymptomatic — do not over-image or over-treat based on MRI findings alone. Treat the patient, not the picture. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction at lumbar levels is a distinct finding from spondylosis and should be addressed with OMM regardless of imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates drives the disc space narrowing and sclerosis characteristic of lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q19","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which deep tendon reflex is diminished or absent in L4 nerve root compression, and what motor deficit accompanies it?","choices":{"A":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the Achilles reflex and produces weakness of plantar flexion from S1 myotome involvement, with sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe","B":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the brachioradialis reflex and produces weakness of wrist extension, reflecting a mid-cervical level nerve root rather than a lumbar radiculopathy","C":"L4 radiculopathy produces no reliable reflex change because the L4 root is assessed only by sensory testing over the medial calf without any associated deep tendon reflex","D":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the patellar reflex and produces weakness of knee extension from quadriceps involvement, with sensory loss over the medial leg and ankle","E":"L4 radiculopathy diminishes the biceps reflex and produces weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination, reflecting musculocutaneous nerve involvement at the C5-C6 level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"L4 myotome: quadriceps (knee extension). L4 dermatome: medial leg and ankle. L4 reflex: patellar tendon reflex. Compressed at L3-L4 disc. Clinically: difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs. L5 = dorsiflexion, no reflex. S1 = plantar flexion, Achilles reflex. Mastering the lumbar myotome-reflex-dermatome triad is essential for localization.","boardPrep":"Step 1 HY lumbar root table: L4 = knee extension/patellar reflex/medial leg; L5 = dorsiflexion + great toe extension/no reflex/dorsum foot; S1 = plantar flexion/Achilles reflex/lateral foot. COMLEX: Can't rise from chair = L2/L3/L4; dragging toe = L5; buckling knee = L3/L4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q20","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the motor deficit and reflex finding in L5 nerve root compression?","choices":{"A":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of plantar flexion with loss of the Achilles reflex and sensory loss over the lateral foot, reflecting involvement of the S1 myotome and triceps surae","B":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of knee extension and hip flexion with loss of the patellar reflex, reflecting femoral nerve territory and the L4 myotome rather than dorsiflexion weakness","C":"L5 radiculopathy causes proximal lower extremity weakness of hip abduction and knee flexion with preserved distal function and no foot drop on neurological examination","D":"L5 radiculopathy produces isolated ankle eversion weakness with sensory loss limited to the lateral calf, representing common peroneal nerve palsy at the fibular head rather than root pathology","E":"L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of dorsiflexion and great toe extension causing foot drop, with sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot; no reliable deep tendon reflex is associated with the L5 root"},"correct":"E","explanation":"L5 is compressed at L4-L5. Myotome: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion) and extensor hallucis longus (great toe extension) — foot drop. Dermatome: dorsum of foot and lateral calf. Reflex: none reliably attributed to L5, making motor and sensory testing critical for localization. Peroneal nerve palsy mimics L5 but is distinguished by normal hip abduction and no low back pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 = foot drop (dorsiflexion weak) + dorsal foot numbness + NO reflex. Key distinguisher: peroneal nerve palsy vs. L5 radiculopathy — check hip abduction (preserved in peroneal palsy). COMLEX: \"Dragging toe\" = L5 root; assess with single-leg heel walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q21","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the approximate sensitivity of the straight leg raise test for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise test has 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity for lumbosacral radiculopathy, making it the single most reliable clinical test for identifying disc herniation at any level","B":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 40 to 45% sensitivity for lumbar disc herniation, limiting its diagnostic utility and making MRI mandatory for all patients with radicular symptoms","C":"The straight leg raise test has approximately 68 to 80% sensitivity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, making it useful for ruling out lower lumbar disc pathology when negative","D":"The straight leg raise test has 80 to 90% specificity for L4-L5 and L5-S1 disc herniation, meaning a positive result at less than 60 degrees is nearly diagnostic without further imaging","E":"The straight leg raise test has equal sensitivity and specificity of approximately 50%, and the crossed straight leg raise is far more sensitive for identifying lumbar disc herniation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A positive straight leg raise test is 68% to 80% sensitive for an L4-L5 or L5-S1 herniated disk (Tintinalli Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading), so a negative test makes lower lumbar disc herniation less likely. The reading gives no specificity figure for the ipsilateral test; Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine (Ch 42) quotes 91% sensitivity and 26% specificity for the supine test, which likewise makes it better for ruling out than for ruling in. A positive test is radicular pain radiating below the knee; back, gluteal, or hamstring pain alone is not a positive result (Tintinalli), and the osteopathic texts place a positive test typically between 30 and 70 degrees of hip flexion. The crossed straight leg raise is highly specific but insensitive for nerve root compression by a herniated disk. No course source gives 95% sensitivity and specificity, a 40 to 45% sensitivity, or equal 50% values, and the reading does not make MRI mandatory for every patient with radicular symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR = 68-80% sensitive for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation (Tintinalli); positive = radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees. Crossed SLR = highly specific but insensitive. COMLEX: Foundations Ch 42 quotes 91% sensitivity and 26% specificity for the supine SLR, so a negative test helps exclude lumbar radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injury patterns and myotomal mapping are directly applied to lumbosacral radiculopathy localization"},{"id":"sdl19_q22","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark clinical feature of neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral calf pain that occurs after a fixed walking distance and is relieved only by cessation of walking, indistinguishable from vascular claudication without Doppler imaging studies","B":"Bilateral leg pain and heaviness with walking that is relieved by sitting, resting, or leaning forward, reflecting increased spinal canal diameter with lumbar flexion","C":"Leg pain with standing but not walking, relieved by lumbar extension and worsened by forward flexion, opposite to the typical positional pattern of lumbar spinal stenosis","D":"Acute onset bilateral lower extremity weakness occurring at rest without any relationship to positional change or walking distance, indicating an acute cord compression emergency","E":"Burning bilateral foot pain at rest that worsens with leg elevation, distinguishing it from vascular claudication where elevation relieves ischemic symptoms in the lower limbs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neurogenic claudication from lumbar stenosis: bilateral leg pain, heaviness, or numbness that builds with walking and is relieved by forward flexion (sitting, stooping, leaning on a cart). Mechanism: flexion widens the lumbar canal; extension narrows it. Distinguishing from vascular claudication: neurogenic is relieved by flexion even while standing; vascular is relieved simply by stopping walking regardless of posture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication = bilateral leg pain with walking relieved by flexion. Spinal canal narrows in extension, widens in flexion. Shopping cart sign = leans forward for relief. COMLEX: Lumbar somatic dysfunction in extension restriction pattern is common; OMM targets the flexion-relief mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q23","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A course slide on lumbosacral stenosis labels a defect at L5 as \"spondylolysis\" and a forward slip of L5 on the sacrum as \"spondylolisthesis.\" Which statement correctly defines spondylolysis?","choices":{"A":"A fracture of the pars interarticularis, which can let the vertebral body slip forward and narrow the canal","B":"Forward slippage of one vertebral body on the one below, which then fractures the pars as a later complication","C":"Hypertrophy of the facet joints from osteoarthritis, which narrows the intervertebral foramen of the exiting root","D":"Thickening of the ligamentum flavum, which buckles into the posterior canal when the spine is held in extension","E":"Posterior herniation of the nucleus pulposus through a tear in the annulus fibrosus into the spinal canal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On the course's lumbosacral stenosis figure, spondylolysis is labeled as a fracture in the pars interarticularis, and spondylolisthesis is the forward slip of the vertebra, drawn as L5 on the sacrum; the figure shows these with a thickened ligamentum flavum, hypertrophied facets, and a herniated disc as causes of canal narrowing. The Tintinalli chapter adds that patients under 18 have a higher incidence of spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis. Forward slippage is spondylolisthesis, not spondylolysis, and the pars defect comes first. Facet hypertrophy, ligamentum flavum thickening, and disc herniation are separate contributors to stenosis shown in the same figure, not definitions of spondylolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spondylolysis = pars interarticularis defect (stress fracture, adolescent athletes with repetitive extension); spondylolisthesis = anterior slip, most often L5 on S1. COMLEX: Lumbar stenosis sits on a spectrum with spondylosis and radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 6 — lumbosacral spondylosis pathology"},{"id":"sdl19_q24","sdlNumber":19,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is preferred for diagnosis and surgical planning in lumbar spinal stenosis?","choices":{"A":"CT myelography is the required first-line imaging for all patients with lumbar spinal stenosis, providing superior anatomic resolution of bony stenosis and dynamic nerve root compression versus MRI","B":"Plain radiographs with dynamic flexion-extension views are the gold standard for evaluating lumbar spinal stenosis, detecting instability and degenerative spondylolisthesis that static imaging misses","C":"MRI is preferred because it directly visualizes central and foraminal canal dimensions, disc pathology, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, and facet joint changes without ionizing radiation","D":"Nuclear bone scan is the recommended first-line study for lumbar spinal stenosis because it identifies active metabolic bone turnover at stenotic levels, guiding targeted injection therapy","E":"Electromyography and nerve conduction studies are the preferred initial diagnostic modality, quantifying the degree of nerve root compromise before committing to cross-sectional imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for lumbar spinal stenosis: it shows the cross-sectional canal area, ligamentum flavum buckling, facet hypertrophy, and disc herniation. CT myelography is reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI. Plain X-rays may show spondylolisthesis and disc height loss but cannot directly assess canal compromise. EMG is useful to confirm radiculopathy but does not image stenosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = diagnostic standard for lumbar stenosis. CT myelography if MRI contraindicated. Look for \"trefoil\" canal shape on axial MRI — classic for severe stenosis. COMLEX: Neurogenic claudication with confirmed stenosis — conservative management first including OMM; surgery for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip fracture and vascular disease must be systematically excluded when evaluating bilateral leg pain; neurogenic claudication is distinguished by positional relief"},{"id":"sdl19_q25","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 3 years of worsening bilateral knee pain, morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, and bony DIP joint enlargement. X-ray shows asymmetric joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes bilaterally. ESR and CRP are normal. Which of the following best explains the primary underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Progressive proteoglycan depletion from articular cartilage reduces compressive stiffness, causing fibrillation and chondrocyte apoptosis with reactive subchondral sclerosis and marginal osteophytes","B":"Autoimmune CD4+ T-cell infiltration of the synovium with pannus formation progressively erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone, producing symmetric joint destruction and systemic inflammation","C":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within articular cartilage triggers recurrent acute inflammatory episodes, producing joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation indistinguishable from primary OA","D":"Systemic elevation of TNF-alpha drives neutrophil influx into the joint space, producing enzymatic cartilage degradation and the reactive periarticular bone changes observed on plain radiography","E":"Uric acid crystal deposition in the periarticular tissues and synovium triggers a local inflammatory cascade, producing the chronic joint destruction and DIP joint enlargement in this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation — chronic knee pain, short morning stiffness, DIP Heberden's nodes, normal inflammatory markers, and asymmetric joint space narrowing with osteophytes — is classic OA. Mechanism: chondrocyte dysfunction → proteoglycan loss → decreased cartilage resilience → mechanical fibrillation → subchondral reactive changes. Pannus (RA), CPPD, TNF-alpha (systemic inflammatory arthritis), and uric acid (gout) each describe different pathophysiology with different clinical profiles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = proteoglycan loss → cartilage fibrillation → joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis, WITHOUT periarticular osteopenia (distinguish from RA). DIP Heberden's nodes = OA. COMLEX: Short morning stiffness <30 min = OA; inflammatory arthritis lasts > 1 hour per FA 2025 (Bootcamp gives ~>30 min for RA).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact cartilage integrity and joint health through mechanisms directly relevant to OA progression"},{"id":"sdl19_q26","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man presents with progressive right hip pain and difficulty with prolonged walking. Plain radiograph of the right hip shows medial joint space narrowing, subchondral cyst formation at the femoral head, and osteophytes at the acetabular rim. BMI is 31. Which combination of radiographic findings is most characteristic of osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Uniform joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions at the joint edges, and bilateral symmetric involvement is the hallmark pattern that distinguishes OA from this patient's diagnosis","B":"Periarticular osteopenia with central marginal erosions and bilateral symmetric joint space narrowing define the underlying radiographic pattern causing this patient's progressive hip pain","C":"Chondrocalcinosis with calcification of the fibrocartilaginous labrum and periarticular soft tissues is the defining radiographic feature that confirms the diagnosis in this patient","D":"Rapid progressive joint space loss over weeks to months with normal bone density and no osteophyte formation is the radiographic pattern most consistent with this patient's diagnosis","E":"Non-uniform joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis and cyst formation, and marginal osteophyte formation without periarticular osteopenia is the classic radiographic tetrad of osteoarthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OA radiographic findings: (1) non-uniform (asymmetric, weight-bearing surface) joint space narrowing; (2) subchondral sclerosis; (3) subchondral cyst formation (synovial fluid forced into bone under pressure); (4) marginal osteophytes; (5) NO periarticular osteopenia. Contrast: RA = symmetric, periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions, no osteophytes early. CPPD = chondrocalcinosis. Rapid progression = septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray mnemonic LOSS — Loss of joint space (non-uniform), Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts. No osteopenia. COMLEX: Hip OA commonly produces somatic dysfunction with restricted internal rotation and extension at the hip — OMM addresses this component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Aging and physical conditioning alter subchondral bone density and cartilage hydration, directly influencing OA radiographic progression"},{"id":"sdl19_q27","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteoarthritis in general.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with confirmed bilateral knee osteoarthritis has failed 3 months of exercise therapy, a 10-pound weight loss, and scheduled acetaminophen. She rates her daily pain 7/10 with significant functional limitation. She has stage 3b CKD and a prior upper GI bleed, so oral NSAIDs are contraindicated, and 6 weeks of topical diclofenac gave no benefit. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Initiating long-term opioid therapy with extended-release oxycodone is the recommended next step for moderate OA pain that has not responded adequately to acetaminophen and weight management","B":"Referring for total knee arthroplasty is appropriate at this stage, as failure of one analgesic agent constitutes sufficient evidence that conservative management has been adequately exhausted","C":"Initiating hydroxychloroquine and methotrexate is the recommended escalation step for symptomatic knee OA after failure of first-line analgesic and nonpharmacologic therapy over 3 months","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection, which provides short-term pain relief and functional improvement when oral NSAIDs are contraindicated and topical NSAIDs have failed","E":"Prescribing scheduled maximum-dose oral naproxen is the most appropriate next step before considering any procedural intervention in this patient with bilateral knee OA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With oral NSAIDs contraindicated (stage 3b CKD, prior GI bleed) and topical NSAIDs already failed, intra-articular corticosteroid injection is the appropriate next step, so scheduled oral naproxen is not an option. Intra-articular corticosteroids provide meaningful short-term (4-6 week) benefit and are a reasonable step before proceeding to surgery. Opioids are not recommended for OA. DMARDs have no role in OA. Arthroplasty is indicated after failure of prolonged conservative management, not after a 3-month trial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA escalation — exercise/weight loss → NSAIDs/topical → intra-articular injection (corticosteroid or hyaluronic acid) → joint replacement. Opioids are not guideline-recommended for OA. COMLEX: OMM improves joint mechanics; document conservative trial before procedural escalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise conditioning reduces joint loading forces and slows OA progression; weight loss has the strongest evidence for knee OA outcomes"},{"id":"sdl19_q28","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man presents with 1 year of bilateral neck pain, morning cervical stiffness, and intermittent periscapular aching. MRI shows disc space narrowing at C5-C6 and C6-C7, vertebral endplate osteophytes, and facet joint hypertrophy. He has full upper extremity strength and intact reflexes. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory cytokine production by the synovial lining of the cervical facet joints drives progressive cartilage erosion and bone destruction, producing disc space collapse and endplate irregularity","B":"Hematogenous seeding of the cervical disc space by bacteria causes disc space collapse and adjacent vertebral endplate destruction, producing the MRI findings and bilateral neck pain","C":"Autoimmune attack targeting type II collagen within the nucleus pulposus triggers progressive disc destruction and bilateral facet involvement in this patient with cervical spondylosis","D":"Microcrystalline calcium pyrophosphate deposition within the posterior longitudinal ligament and annulus fibrosus drives disc space narrowing and the osteophyte formation seen on MRI","E":"Age-related nucleus pulposus dehydration lowers disc height and loads the endplates and facets, driving osteophytes, facet arthropathy, and PLL hypertrophy that narrow the canal and foramina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cervical spondylosis pathophysiology: disc nucleus pulposus desiccates with age → reduced disc height → increased annular fissuring → increased load on facet joints → facet arthropathy → osteophyte formation at endplates and facet margins → PLL and ligamentum flavum thickening → canal and foraminal narrowing. This is a mechanical degenerative process, not inflammatory (as in RA or infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc degeneration + facet arthropathy + osteophyte formation. Disc height loss is seen on X-ray; cord/root compression seen on MRI. COMLEX: Restriction of cervical sidebending and rotation = somatic dysfunction — address with OMM as adjunct to spondylosis management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — RANKL/OPG balance and bone remodeling hormones govern osteophyte formation at vertebral endplates in cervical spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q29","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with cervical spondylosis has persistent cervical and periscapular pain despite 3 months of NSAIDs. She has increased bilateral upper trapezius tension and restricted C4-C5 sidebending on osteopathic examination, consistent with somatic dysfunction. Which of the following interventions most directly addresses the somatic dysfunction component of her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing a COX-2 selective NSAID from a different chemical class is the most direct intervention for her somatic dysfunction, as segmental hypertonicity is driven primarily by prostaglandin-mediated inflammation","B":"Referring for cervical epidural steroid injection is the most direct next step for the restricted C4-C5 sidebending and upper trapezius tension documented on osteopathic examination","C":"Ordering repeat cervical MRI is the most direct intervention for the documented somatic dysfunction, as imaging must confirm structural pathology before any manual medicine intervention","D":"Prescribing a benzodiazepine muscle relaxant for 4 weeks is the most direct treatment for somatic dysfunction with paraspinal hypertonicity in cervical spondylosis","E":"Osteopathic manipulative medicine targeting the cervical somatic dysfunction can restore segmental motion, reduce paraspinal hypertonicity, and decrease pain without relying on additional pharmacotherapy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Somatic dysfunction — defined as impaired or altered function of related components of the somatic system — is a distinct clinical entity from spondylosis. OMM (muscle energy, soft tissue, counterstrain, high-velocity low-amplitude with appropriate screening) directly addresses restricted joint motion and paraspinal muscle hypertonicity. NSAIDs address inflammation but not the mechanical dysfunction. Epidural injection targets nerve root, not somatic dysfunction. MRI does not treat dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis + restricted motion = somatic dysfunction as comorbid finding. COMLEX: OMM is the primary intervention for somatic dysfunction in cervical spondylosis; document restricted sidebending, rotation, or flexion-extension as the dysfunction findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — Bone remodeling and osteophyte formation in spondylosis create structural constraints on segmental motion that contribute to somatic dysfunction"},{"id":"sdl19_q30","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has cervical spondylosis with chronic neck and periscapular pain and stiffness, no myelopathic signs, and only partial relief from an NSAID. Her physician is following the course's adaptation of OARSI recommendations for the cervical spine. Which additional pharmacologic classes appear in that course plan?","choices":{"A":"Scheduled long-acting opioids and oral bisphosphonates, used together to treat pain and slow osteophyte growth","B":"Muscle relaxants and antidepressants, listed alongside NSAIDs in the pharmacologic arm of the management plan","C":"Colchicine and allopurinol, since crystal deposition in the facet joints drives most cervical spondylosis pain","D":"Methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine, since the degenerative disc changes reflect an autoimmune synovitis","E":"Oral antibiotics and antifungals, since low-grade disc space infection is the usual cause of chronic neck pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course's management slide for cervical spondylosis adapts OARSI recommendations to the cervical spine: brace or collar, avoidance of high-risk activities, pharmacologic treatment (NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, antidepressants), physical therapy, osteopathic manual medicine, acupuncture, injections, and surgical decompression. Muscle relaxants and antidepressants are the listed drug classes beyond NSAIDs. Long-acting opioids and bisphosphonates are not part of the plan, and bisphosphonates do not slow osteophytes. Colchicine and allopurinol treat gout, which is not the mechanism of spondylosis. DMARDs treat inflammatory arthritis, not degenerative disease. Infection is not the cause of cervical spondylosis, which is age-related wear of discs, facets, and ligaments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylosis = disc degeneration, facet arthropathy, osteophytes, PLL/ligamentum flavum hypertrophy; 95% by age 65, poor symptom correlation. COMLEX: OMM addresses associated somatic dysfunction; surgery is reserved for progressive neurologic deficit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects that limit long-term use"},{"id":"sdl19_q31","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has 3 weeks of neck pain radiating down the medial forearm to the ring and little fingers, with numbness of the medial forearm, medial hand, and fourth and fifth digits. He has weakness of finger abduction and of flexion at the thumb tip. Biceps, brachioradialis, and triceps reflexes are normal. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"C8, which gives medial forearm and fourth and fifth digit numbness, intrinsic and distal thumb flexion weakness, and no reflex change","B":"C6, which gives lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger numbness, elbow flexion and supination weakness, and a reduced brachioradialis reflex","C":"C7, which gives index and middle finger numbness, elbow and wrist extension weakness, and a reduced triceps reflex on the affected side","D":"C5, which gives lateral arm numbness over the deltoid, shoulder abduction and external rotation weakness, and a reduced biceps reflex","E":"The ulnar nerve at the elbow, which gives fourth and fifth digit numbness with intrinsic weakness, including weakness of distal thumb flexion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course's cervical nerve root table lists C8 as pain in the neck, shoulder, medial forearm, fourth and fifth digits, and medial hand; numbness of the medial forearm, medial hand, and fourth and fifth digits; weakness of finger extension, ulnar wrist extension, distal finger flexion, finger abduction and adduction, and distal thumb flexion; and no reflex affected. Normal reflexes fit C8. C6 produces lateral forearm, thumb, and index numbness with a reduced brachioradialis reflex. C7 reduces the triceps reflex. C5 affects shoulder abduction and the biceps reflex. An ulnar nerve lesion does not explain medial forearm numbness or weakness of distal thumb flexion, which depends on flexor pollicis longus outside the ulnar nerve's territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical roots: C5 biceps/deltoid; C6 brachioradialis, thumb/index; C7 triceps, middle finger; C8 no reflex, ring/little finger and medial forearm, intrinsics; T1 thumb abduction, medial arm. COMLEX: Spurling test is specific (~90%) but insensitive for radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow as a mimic of C8 radiculopathy"},{"id":"sdl19_q32","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old carpenter with a right C6 radiculopathy is being managed as an outpatient with activity modification and oral analgesics. Which new finding at a follow-up visit would be the clearest indication for hospital admission?","choices":{"A":"A positive right Spurling sign that still reproduces his lateral forearm pain, while his arm strength, reflexes, and gait have not changed","B":"Persistent mild numbness of the right thumb and index finger two weeks after onset, with arm strength and reflexes the same as at the first visit","C":"Neck and trapezius pain that worsens with overhead work and eases with rest, with focal muscle tenderness and a normal neurological examination","D":"Right arm weakness that has progressed over successive visits, most notably in the C7 distribution, with worsening elbow extension strength","E":"An MRI showing a C5-C6 disc-osteophyte complex that contacts the right C6 root and matches his symptoms, while his examination stays stable"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tintinalli (Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading, p7) lists the indications for hospital admission in cervical radiculopathy: progressive upper extremity weakness, especially in the C7 distribution; acute or progressive symptoms or signs of myelopathy; and, in a small subset of patients, intractable radicular pain unresponsive to treatment. The course slides list conservative treatment first (activity modification, collar, analgesics, oral glucocorticoids, PT, OMM, traction) and note that severe cases show upper extremity weakness. A positive Spurling sign (90% specific, 45% sensitive, Tintinalli Table 279-2) supports the diagnosis but is not a reason to admit. Sensory loss in cervical radiculopathy is usually absent or mild (course slide), so stable mild numbness does not change disposition. Activity-related neck and trapezius pain with focal tenderness and a normal neurological examination favors uncomplicated musculoskeletal neck pain (Table 279-2). Concordant imaging becomes relevant when symptoms fail conservative treatment or recur, when surgery may be recommended, but with a stable examination it is not an admission criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Admit cervical radiculopathy for progressive arm weakness (especially C7), acute or progressive myelopathy, or intractable radicular pain (Tintinalli). COMLEX: Most cases are managed as outpatients with activity modification, analgesics, PT, and OMM; surgery is considered when symptoms fail conservative care or recur and imaging is concordant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — myelopathy signs (hyperreflexia, gait change) also warrant admission or surgical referral"},{"id":"sdl19_q33","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman presents with right neck and posterior arm pain radiating to the dorsal forearm and middle finger. She notes weakness with pushing movements and a reduced triceps deep tendon reflex on the right. Which of the following best localizes this radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"Biceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow flexion and sensory loss in the lateral forearm and thumb localizes the lesion to C5-C6 nerve root involvement rather than the presentation described","B":"Triceps reflex loss with weakness of elbow extension and sensory loss in the dorsal forearm and middle finger localizes the lesion to the C7 nerve root, most commonly compressed at C6-C7","C":"Brachioradialis reflex loss with weakness of wrist extension and sensory loss in the lateral forearm localizes the lesion to the C6 nerve root rather than the posterior arm and middle finger distribution","D":"Patellar reflex loss with weakness of knee extension and sensory loss over the medial leg localizes the lesion to the L4 nerve root at the lumbar level, not the cervical spine","E":"Achilles reflex loss with weakness of plantar flexion and sensory loss over the lateral foot localizes the lesion to the S1 nerve root at the lumbosacral level, not the cervical spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"C7 root exits at C6-C7. Sensory: dorsal forearm and middle finger. Motor: triceps (elbow extension) and wrist flexors. Reflex: triceps deep tendon reflex. C7 is the most commonly affected cervical root. Weakness \"with pushing\" corresponds to triceps/elbow extension. C6 = lateral forearm/thumb/BR reflex. C5 = deltoid/biceps reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C7 = middle finger numbness + triceps weakness + triceps reflex loss. Most common cervical root affected. Associated with C6-C7 disc herniation. COMLEX: C7 somatic dysfunction with tenderness at C6-C7 interspinous space — palpatory diagnosis supports level localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Nerve root localization by dermatomal and myotomal mapping is the same principle applied in upper extremity entrapment neuropathies at the elbow and wrist"},{"id":"sdl19_q34","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with 4 months of progressive bilateral hand weakness and difficulty with fine motor tasks, along with gait unsteadiness. Neurological exam shows hyperreflexia at biceps and triceps bilaterally and bilateral positive Hoffman's signs. MRI reveals a central disc herniation at C4-C5 with T2 cord signal change. Which of the following best explains her deficits?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral central canal compression at C4-C5 damages the corticospinal tracts, producing hyperreflexia and spastic gait, while the T2 cord signal reflects compressive myelomalacia or ischemia","B":"Bilateral C5 foraminal compression produces simultaneous bilateral lower motor neuron deficits including hyperreflexia, spastic gait, and hand weakness through axonal degeneration at both foramina","C":"Vertebral artery compression at C4-C5 produces posterior circulation ischemia, causing bilateral upper extremity weakness, hyperreflexia, and gait unsteadiness through cerebellar and brainstem dysfunction","D":"Inflammatory demyelination of the cervical cord produces bilateral upper motor neuron signs; however, the absence of optic nerve involvement makes this the mechanism underlying this patient's deficits","E":"Epidural hematoma compressing the cervical cord at C4-C5 produces the bilateral upper motor neuron deficits; the gradual 4-month progression and absence of trauma are consistent with this mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central disc herniation compresses the cervical cord → corticospinal tract damage → UMN signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Hoffman's, gait ataxia, hand clumsiness. T2 signal change in the cord = myelomalacia (irreversible ischemic/compressive change) — a poor prognostic indicator requiring urgent surgical decompression. Foraminal (LMN) compression causes hyporeflexia, not hyperreflexia. Vertebral artery compression would cause brainstem/posterior fossa symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2 cord signal change on MRI = myelomalacia = surgical emergency. Cervical myelopathy = bilateral UMN signs + hand dysfunction + gait ataxia (central cord syndrome is a distinct, usually traumatic, hyperextension injury with arms weaker than legs). Hoffman's + hyperreflexia = UMN. COMLEX: HVLA absolutely contraindicated; refer for neurosurgical decompression urgently.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — The structural features of the cervical spinal canal — facet joints, PLL, ligamentum flavum — directly determine the space available for the cord when central stenosis develops"},{"id":"sdl19_q35","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis has MRI findings described in two reports. In one, degenerative change narrows the central canal at C5-C6; in the other, osteophytes narrow the right C6-C7 intervertebral foramen. According to the course, which structure is compressed in each type of stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Central stenosis compresses a single nerve root, and foraminal stenosis compresses the spinal cord at that level","B":"Both types compress the vertebral artery, which explains why cervical stenosis presents with dizziness","C":"Central stenosis compresses the spinal cord, and foraminal stenosis compresses the exiting cervical nerve root","D":"Both types compress the spinal cord, so foraminal narrowing produces the same signs as central narrowing","E":"Central stenosis compresses the dorsal root ganglion, and foraminal stenosis compresses the dura by itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course defines cervical spinal stenosis as degeneration that leads to central or foraminal stenosis: central stenosis compresses the spinal cord, and foraminal stenosis compresses the nerve root. Stenosis can be congenital or acquired, prevalence is 14 to 24.2% and rises after age 65, and it presents and is treated like cervical spondylosis. Central cord compression produces myelopathic (upper motor neuron) findings, while foraminal compression produces radiculopathy in the distribution of the exiting root. Reversing the two structures is incorrect. The vertebral artery is not the structure the course describes as compressed. Foraminal narrowing affects the root, not the cord. The dorsal root ganglion lies in the foramen, not the central canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical central stenosis → myelopathy (hyperreflexia, Hoffmann, spastic gait); foraminal stenosis → radiculopathy (dermatomal pain, root weakness, reduced reflex). COMLEX: MRI is preferred when neurologic signs are present; surgery when myelopathy progresses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — cervical radiculopathy localization by nerve root"},{"id":"sdl19_q36","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of cervical spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis has been managed with NSAIDs and physical therapy for 8 weeks. He now has progressive bilateral hand weakness and new urinary urgency. Repeat MRI shows worsening T2 cord signal at C5-C6. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continue conservative management for an additional 12 weeks with a rigid cervical orthosis, as natural history data show spontaneous resolution of cord signal change in most patients","B":"Prescribe high-dose oral corticosteroid taper, as cord edema is the primary driver of progressive neurological deterioration and can be reversed with systemic steroid therapy","C":"Cervical epidural steroid injection is the most appropriate next step, as it provides cord decompression equivalent to surgical outcomes in patients with T2 signal change and sphincter symptoms","D":"Progressive myelopathy with worsening cord signal and new sphincter dysfunction despite conservative care warrants urgent surgical decompression (laminoplasty or laminectomy with fusion)","E":"Repeat MRI in 3 months to monitor progression before considering surgery, as cervical myelopathy uniformly plateaus without intervention and surgical outcomes worsen with delay"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indications for surgical decompression in cervical myelopathy: progressive neurological deficits despite adequate conservative management, or moderate-to-severe myelopathy at presentation. Worsening T2 cord signal = myelomalacia (irreversible change if persistent) + new sphincter involvement = urgent surgical referral. Laminoplasty (cord-expansive) or laminectomy with fusion are standard approaches. Epidural injections address radiculopathy, not cord compression. Corticosteroids are not a standard treatment for compressive myelopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelopathy + progressive deficits = surgical emergency. T2 cord signal = myelomalacia = operate before permanent injury. Surgical options: anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF), laminoplasty, or laminectomy with fusion. COMLEX: OMM is supportive only — does not substitute for surgery in myelopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — Understanding the structural anatomy of the cervical spinal canal is essential to understanding why canal compromise from posterior ligamentum flavum and anterior osteophytes produces bilateral cord dysfunction"},{"id":"sdl19_q37","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old teacher presents with 3 weeks of acute, non-radiating central low back pain rated 6/10, without leg pain, neurological deficits, fever, weight loss, or bowel or bladder symptoms. Examination shows diffuse paraspinal tenderness with full lower extremity strength and reflexes. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent lumbar MRI is indicated because 3 weeks of unresolved low back pain represents failure of natural history and requires imaging to exclude structural pathology before initiating treatment","B":"Lumbar radiographs should be obtained immediately in this patient because plain films are a low-risk screening tool that excludes compression fracture, spondylolysis, and serious structural disease","C":"EMG and nerve conduction studies should be performed to quantify subclinical nerve root involvement before physical therapy is initiated, as radiculopathy may precede overt clinical symptoms","D":"CT of the lumbar spine is indicated in this patient to evaluate the posterior elements and intervertebral discs at higher resolution than radiography, guiding early management decisions","E":"Without red flag symptoms, routine imaging is not indicated in the first 4 to 6 weeks of acute mechanical low back pain; NSAIDs, relative rest, heat, and early physical therapy come first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute mechanical LBP without red flags does not require imaging in the first 4-6 weeks. Red flags that DO warrant imaging: age >50, unexplained weight loss, fever, history of cancer, IV drug use, prolonged steroid use, major trauma, neurological deficits, bowel/bladder dysfunction. This patient has none. Early imaging does not improve outcomes and may identify incidental findings that lead to unnecessary intervention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging for acute LBP without red flags in first 4-6 weeks. This is a high-yield clinical decision point on USMLE. Red flags = cancer, infection, CES, fracture. COMLEX: OMM for lumbar somatic dysfunction is appropriate and recommended; do not wait for imaging to begin manual therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Low back pain without red flags is a primary indication for integrative and osteopathic medicine referral before escalating to pharmacologic or procedural interventions"},{"id":"sdl19_q38","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with 2 days of severe low back pain now reports new perianal numbness and difficulty initiating urination. He has bilateral leg pain below the knee. Examination reveals decreased perianal sensation, 3/5 bilateral hip flexor strength, and reduced anal sphincter tone. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Administer IV methylprednisolone and arrange outpatient lumbar MRI within 48 hours, as corticosteroids can partially reverse cauda equina syndrome and avoid the risks of urgent surgery","B":"Perform lumbar puncture for CSF analysis to exclude infectious meningitis before obtaining lumbar MRI, as infectious etiologies must be ruled out before attributing symptoms to disc herniation","C":"This presentation is consistent with cauda equina syndrome and requires emergent lumbar MRI with immediate neurosurgical consultation for urgent decompression","D":"Order EMG of the bilateral lower extremities as the initial step to confirm nerve root involvement before committing to cross-sectional imaging or surgical consultation","E":"Prescribe oral NSAIDs and a muscle relaxant with close outpatient follow-up in one week, as bilateral radicular symptoms with urinary complaints often resolve with conservative management"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) = saddle anesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness. This is a surgical emergency. Next step: emergent MRI lumbar spine, then urgent neurosurgical decompression (within 24-48 hours to preserve bladder function). Delay = permanent incontinence. IV steroids are not standard treatment for CES. LP, EMG, and conservative management all inappropriately delay necessary surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CES = emergent MRI → urgent surgery. Saddle anesthesia is pathognomonic. Bladder/bowel dysfunction: the course slide lists usually incontinence; retention also occurs. COMLEX: CES is an absolute OMM contraindication — surgical emergency first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Integrative and conservative care is appropriate for mechanical LBP; recognizing when red flags mandate emergent workup is essential to safe practice"},{"id":"sdl19_q39","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of mechanical back pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old construction worker on workers' compensation has had low back pain for 7 months. MRI shows mild L4-L5 disc narrowing without herniation or significant stenosis. He has fear-avoidance beliefs, has stopped all physical activity, scores high on the Pain Catastrophizing Scale, and reports job dissatisfaction. Which of the following is the strongest independent predictor of chronic disability in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Psychosocial yellow flags (fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, workers' compensation, low job satisfaction, depression) are the strongest predictors of chronic disability, outweighing imaging findings","B":"The presence of L4-L5 disc space narrowing on MRI is the strongest predictor of chronic disability in this patient, as structural degeneration at this level directly correlates with long-term functional impairment","C":"Duration of pain beyond 6 months alone is the strongest predictor of poor recovery, as chronification is driven purely by the peripheral pain generator and is unrelated to psychological factors","D":"Workers' compensation status alone, independent of all other psychosocial factors, completely accounts for the disability reported and is the strongest single predictor of poor recovery in LBP","E":"BMI and physical deconditioning are the strongest predictors of chronic disability in this patient, as adiposity drives systemic inflammation that amplifies the structural disc degeneration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Psychosocial yellow flags — fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, depression, anxiety, low job satisfaction, workers' comp — are stronger predictors of chronification and disability than structural imaging findings. The biopsychosocial model is the evidence-based framework for chronic LBP. MRI findings do not predict disability. Addressing yellow flags through cognitive behavioral therapy, graded exercise, and psychosocial support is as important as physical treatments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychosocial factors (yellow flags) predict chronic LBP disability more than imaging. Biopsychosocial model is the framework for chronic LBP management. COMLEX: OMM combined with patient education and addressing yellow flags reflects the osteopathic whole-person approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — LBP is the primary indication for integrative medicine; psychosocial yellow flags are a recognized indication for mind-body and behavioral approaches alongside manual therapy"},{"id":"sdl19_q40","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old retired teacher presents with 2 years of low back pain and stiffness, worst upon waking and improving after 1 hour of morning activity. She reports mild bilateral buttock pain without leg radiation. X-ray shows disc space narrowing at L4-L5 and L5-S1 with endplate sclerosis and marginal osteophytes. ESR and CRP are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Ankylosing spondylitis presents with morning low back stiffness that improves with activity, along with sacroiliitis on imaging, elevated inflammatory markers, and HLA-B27","B":"Lumbar disc herniation presents with pain radiating below the knee in a dermatomal pattern that worsens with sitting, coughing, and Valsalva maneuvers","C":"Vertebral compression fracture presents with acute-onset localized back pain in an osteoporotic patient and anterior vertebral height loss on radiograph","D":"Lumbar spinal stenosis presents with bilateral leg pain on walking that is relieved by forward flexion, sitting down, or leaning on a shopping cart","E":"Lumbosacral spondylosis presents with morning stiffness that eases with activity, nondermatomal buttock pain, and disc narrowing, osteophytes, and endplate sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LS spondylosis (lumbar DDD + OA): morning pain and stiffness that improve through the day (course slide SDL 19 p24), localized or referred buttock/posterior thigh pain (not dermatomal), normal inflammatory markers, X-ray with disc space narrowing + osteophytes + endplate sclerosis. Ankylosing spondylitis: similar morning pattern BUT sacroiliitis + elevated CRP/ESR + HLA-B27. Herniation = dermatomal radiation. Stenosis = neurogenic claudication. Compression fracture = acute localized pain with vertebral height loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LS spondylosis vs. ankylosing spondylitis — key differentiators: age, sacroiliitis, HLA-B27, elevated ESR/CRP. Both improve with activity. COMLEX: Lumbar spondylosis with restricted motion = somatic dysfunction at L4-L5 or L5-S1; document restriction pattern for OMM treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteocyte and chondrocyte dysfunction at vertebral endplates parallels the bone histology of degenerative disc disease in lumbosacral spondylosis"},{"id":"sdl19_q41","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with chronic lumbosacral spondylosis undergoes lumbar MRI. The radiologist reports Modic type I changes at the L3-L4 vertebral endplates. Which of the following MRI signal characteristics corresponds to Modic type I changes?","choices":{"A":"Modic type I changes are hyperintense on T1 and hypointense on T2 sequences, representing fatty marrow replacement at the vertebral endplate indicating chronic, stable degeneration without active inflammation","B":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on both T1 and T2 sequences, representing vertebral endplate sclerosis indicating end-stage degeneration with dense bone mineralization at the disc-vertebra interface","C":"Modic type I changes represent disc material herniating through the endplate, producing a T2-hyperintense defect with cortical endplate disruption on sagittal MRI sequences at L3-L4","D":"Modic type I changes are hypointense on T1 and hyperintense on T2 sequences, representing bone marrow edema and hypervascularity at the vertebral endplate from an active inflammatory or degenerative process","E":"Modic type I changes represent the vacuum phenomenon of advanced disc degeneration, appearing as signal void on both T1 and T2 sequences at the intervertebral disc-endplate junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modic endplate changes: Type I = active inflammation/edema = T1 hypo, T2 hyper. Type II = fatty replacement (most common, stable) = T1 hyper, T2 iso/slightly hyper. Type III = sclerosis = T1 hypo, T2 hypo. Type I correlates with active pain. The mnemonic: Type I = Inflammatory (T1 dark, T2 bright). Type II = Fat (T1 bright). Type III = sclerosis (both dark).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modic type I = active, painful — T1 low, T2 high. Type II = fatty, stable — T1 high. Type III = sclerotic — both low. COMLEX: Modic type I at a level with somatic dysfunction supports targeting that level with OMM, but inflammation may require medical co-management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Osteoblast and osteocyte activity at vertebral endplates generates the Modic change pattern; Type I reflects active bone marrow edema from disrupted endplate homeostasis"},{"id":"sdl19_q42","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spondylosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with lumbosacral spondylosis has taken scheduled naproxen for 4 months without adequate pain relief. She has hypertension and mild CKD limiting continued NSAID use. Physical therapy provided partial benefit. She has no neurological deficits. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar fusion surgery is the most appropriate next step because failure of two conservative modalities meets the threshold for surgical intervention in lumbosacral spondylosis without neurological deficits","B":"Epidural steroid injection is an appropriate next-step procedural intervention for refractory lumbosacral spondylosis pain when NSAIDs are limited by comorbidities and physical therapy has provided only partial benefit","C":"Long-term opioid therapy with extended-release morphine is the most appropriate next step for this patient, as her renal and cardiovascular comorbidities make all further NSAID use contraindicated","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI and referring to rheumatology is the most appropriate next step, as age, chronic pain, and NSAID failure suggest an inflammatory arthropathy requiring DMARD therapy","E":"Prescribing long-term low-dose oral prednisone is the most appropriate next step for lumbosacral spondylosis refractory to NSAIDs, as chronic corticosteroids reduce the underlying degenerative process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When NSAIDs are contraindicated and PT provides partial benefit, epidural steroid injections (ESI) offer a procedural option for spondylosis with radicular or axial pain. ESI is not curative but can provide months of relief and facilitate PT engagement. Lumbar fusion requires more exhaustive conservative failure AND structural instability or deformity. Opioids have minimal evidence for chronic LBP and significant risks. Long-term corticosteroids worsen osteoporosis and are not indicated for degenerative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epidural steroid injection = next step when conservative measures fail in LBP with radiculopathy or refractory spondylosis. Surgery = last resort. Opioids = poor evidence for chronic LBP, high risk in elderly. COMLEX: OMM continues alongside procedural management and is not mutually exclusive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Endplate and osteocyte dysfunction in lumbosacral spondylosis drives the structural changes that lead to clinical pain refractory to conservative management"},{"id":"sdl19_q43","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has 5 weeks of right anterior thigh pain after lifting a heavy box. She says her right knee suddenly buckles when she walks down stairs. The patellar reflex is reduced on the right. Using the course's functional clues for lumbosacral radiculopathy, which interpretation of the knee buckling is best?","choices":{"A":"Toe dragging from tibialis anterior weakness, which points to the L5 root and would come with a reduced Achilles reflex","B":"Hip extensor weakness from gluteus maximus involvement, which points to the S1 root and causes the knee to collapse","C":"Saddle anesthesia with sphincter weakness, which points to the S2 to S4 roots and requires emergent decompression","D":"Quadriceps weakness, which the course ties to the L3 and L4 roots, matching her anterior thigh pain and patellar reflex","E":"Hamstring weakness from tibial nerve involvement, which points to the L5 root and destabilizes the knee in flexion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists bedside functional clues for lumbosacral radiculopathy: inability to get up from a chair suggests iliopsoas or quadriceps weakness (L2, L3, L4); buckling of the knee is consistent with quadriceps weakness (L3, L4); dragging of the toe points to tibialis anterior weakness (L5); and urinary or fecal incontinence may reflect S2 to S4 sphincter weakness. Knee buckling with anterior thigh pain and a reduced patellar reflex fits L3 or L4. Toe dragging is the L5 clue, and L5 has no reliable reflex. Gluteus maximus weakness does not produce knee buckling on stairs as described. She has no saddle anesthesia or sphincter symptoms. Hamstring weakness would not cause the knee to buckle into flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lumbar roots: L4 patellar reflex, quadriceps, medial leg; L5 dorsiflexion/EHL, dorsum of foot, no reliable reflex; S1 Achilles reflex, plantarflexion, lateral foot. COMLEX: Initial care = analgesics/NSAIDs or oral steroids + activity modification; later PT, OMM, epidural steroid injection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — closed kinetic chain quadriceps strengthening"},{"id":"sdl19_q44","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman presents with right leg pain radiating from the buttock to the posterior thigh, calf, and plantar surface of the foot. She has difficulty standing on her tiptoes. Examination reveals 4/5 plantar flexion on the right, absent right Achilles reflex, and reduced pinprick sensation over the right lateral foot and little toe. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion weakness, foot drop, and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot with intact Achilles reflex are the classic triad of L5 nerve root compression rather than the S1 presentation described","B":"Knee extension weakness, diminished patellar reflex, and sensory loss over the medial calf and ankle define L4 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior leg and plantar foot distribution","C":"Plantar flexion weakness, absent Achilles reflex, and sensory loss over the lateral foot and little toe are the classic triad of S1 nerve root compression at the L5-S1 disc level","D":"Hip abductor weakness, preserved lower extremity reflexes, and lateral thigh numbness define L2 to L3 nerve root involvement rather than the posterior chain sensory and reflex pattern described","E":"Bilateral leg weakness with bowel or bladder dysfunction and saddle anesthesia defines cauda equina syndrome rather than the unilateral radicular pattern with isolated S1 reflex loss described"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S1 myotome: gastrocnemius/soleus (plantar flexion — heel raises). S1 dermatome: lateral foot, little toe, posterior calf. S1 reflex: Achilles. Compressed at L5-S1. Clinical test: inability to stand on tiptoes = S1 weakness. Distinguish from S1 from L5: L5 = dorsiflexion + dorsal foot; S1 = plantar flexion + lateral/plantar foot + Achilles loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S1 = can't do heel raises (plantar flexion weak) + Achilles reflex loss + lateral foot/little toe numbness. Compressed by L5-S1 disc. COMLEX: \"Can't walk on heels\" = L4/L5 (dorsiflexion); \"Can't walk on toes\" = S1 (plantar flexion).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injury patterns and the Achilles reflex arc are the same mechanisms evaluated in lumbosacral radiculopathy and peripheral nerve injury"},{"id":"sdl19_q45","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral radiculopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a 4-week history of left leg pain. Straight leg raise on the left reproduces radicular pain below the knee at 35 degrees of elevation. A crossed straight leg raise on the right also reproduces left leg pain. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of the straight leg raise test?","choices":{"A":"The straight leg raise compresses the sacroiliac joint by externally rotating the hip in the supine position, reproducing sacroiliac joint pain that radiates to the buttock and posterior thigh","B":"The straight leg raise measures sciatic nerve mobility at the popliteal fossa, and a positive test at any angle confirms a tibial neuropathy","C":"The straight leg raise stretches the hamstring muscles, and pain at less than 60 degrees indicates hamstring tightness or proximal hamstring tendinopathy","D":"The straight leg raise loads the L4-L5 facet joints by extending the lumbar spine in the supine position, reproducing facet-mediated pain that radiates to the lateral thigh in facet arthropathy","E":"The straight leg raise tensions the L4, L5, and S1 roots; radicular pain radiating below the knee indicates an inflamed or compressed lower lumbar root"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLR mechanism: hip flexion with knee extension stretches the sciatic nerve and its contributing roots (L4, L5, S1). When a root is inflamed or compressed by a disc herniation, this tension reproduces radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees of hip flexion (Foundations Ch 93). A positive crossed SLR (lifting the asymptomatic leg reproduces pain down the affected leg) is highly specific but insensitive for nerve root compression by a herniated disk (Tintinalli Ch 279, the SDL 19 course reading). SLR does not test the sacroiliac joint, hamstrings, or facets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLR positive = radicular pain below the knee, typically between 30 and 70 degrees = inflamed or compressed nerve root. Sensitivity 68-80% for L4-L5/L5-S1 herniation (Tintinalli). Crossed SLR = highly specific but insensitive. COMLEX: SLR is part of standard lumbar OMM neurological screening before any high-velocity technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — The straight leg raise test tensioning L4-S1 nerve roots is the same principle used in lower extremity peripheral nerve tension tests for sciatic nerve mobility"},{"id":"sdl19_q46","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 69-year-old man has 1 year of low back pain and bilateral buttock and leg pain that comes on with walking and prolonged standing and eases with sitting. His strength, sensation, reflexes, and pulses are normal. His daughter asks how stenosis can be the diagnosis when the examination is normal. Which response best reflects the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Examination findings are often absent in lumbar stenosis; the diagnosis rests mainly on history and is confirmed by MRI or CT","B":"A normal examination excludes lumbar stenosis, because canal narrowing always reduces the Achilles reflexes on both sides","C":"A normal examination means the pain is psychosomatic, so imaging should be avoided and counseling arranged instead","D":"A normal examination points to vascular claudication, so ankle-brachial indices must be abnormal for stenosis to be considered","E":"A normal examination requires electrodiagnostic testing, because stenosis is confirmed exclusively by nerve conduction studies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Tintinalli chapter assigned for this SDL describes lumbar spinal stenosis as narrowing of the spinal canal, nerve root canal, or foramina from degenerative disease, with symptoms usually beginning in the sixth decade: back and leg pain aggravated by standing and extension and relieved by rest and forward flexion. It states that physical examination findings are often absent and that the diagnosis is made principally by history and confirmed by CT or MRI. A normal exam therefore does not exclude stenosis. Reflex loss is not required. Calling the pain psychosomatic ignores a classic history. Normal pulses do not point to vascular disease. Nerve conduction studies are not the confirmatory test.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurogenic claudication: worse with standing/extension, better with sitting/flexion; pulses normal. Vascular claudication: relieved by standing still, abnormal pulses/ABI. COMLEX: Symptomatic treatment matches chronic back pain; surgery after failed conservative care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — red flags and imaging rules in mechanical low back pain"},{"id":"sdl19_q47","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with achondroplasia has 6 months of bilateral leg pain and heaviness when walking that improves when he sits. MRI shows a narrow lumbar canal along several levels with a narrow interpedicular distance and little degenerative change. How is his spinal stenosis best classified?","choices":{"A":"Acquired degenerative stenosis, since lumbar stenosis in any patient arises from disc and facet wear after age 50","B":"Foraminal stenosis at one level, since a narrow canal compresses just the single exiting root at the tightest foramen","C":"Spondylolisthesis, since a narrow interpedicular distance in achondroplasia is by definition a forward vertebral slip","D":"Vascular claudication, since achondroplasia narrows the iliac arteries and causes leg pain relieved by sitting","E":"Congenital stenosis, since FGFR3-related growth plate failure leaves a small canal that can compress neural tissue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that lumbosacral spinal stenosis can be congenital or acquired. The SDL 1 reading explains that achondroplasia, a gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation that impairs chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, is commonly associated with spinal stenosis that may cause signs of neural compression. A young adult with achondroplasia, a multilevel narrow canal with a narrow interpedicular distance, and little degeneration has congenital stenosis. Acquired degenerative stenosis typically begins in the sixth decade with ligamentum flavum, facet, and disc changes. A multilevel narrow canal is central, not a single-level foraminal lesion. A narrow interpedicular distance is not spondylolisthesis. Vascular claudication is relieved by stopping, regardless of posture, and is not a feature of achondroplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia = AD gain-of-function FGFR3 → impaired endochondral growth; short limbs, normal trunk, large head, narrow interpedicular distance, spinal stenosis risk. COMLEX: Congenitally narrow canals become symptomatic earlier and with less degeneration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — achondroplasia and the small spinal canal"},{"id":"sdl19_q48","sdlNumber":19,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lumbosacral spinal stenosis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with MRI-confirmed severe central lumbar spinal stenosis at L3-L4 and L4-L5 presents after 6 months of physical therapy, NSAIDs, and two epidural steroid injections. She continues to have neurogenic claudication limiting walking to less than half a block. She has no significant surgical comorbidities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Continuing epidural steroid injections indefinitely at 3-month intervals is the appropriate management for lumbar stenosis that has failed conservative therapy, as surgical outcomes are equivalent to injection therapy at 5 years","B":"Surgical decompression via lumbar laminectomy with or without fusion is indicated for lumbar spinal stenosis with persistent neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management including epidural injections","C":"Intrathecal morphine via implanted pump is the next appropriate step for lumbar stenosis refractory to two epidural steroid injections, providing analgesia without the risks of open surgical decompression","D":"Repeating lumbar MRI in 6 months to reassess stenosis severity before considering any procedural intervention is the most appropriate next step, as neurogenic claudication uniformly stabilizes without surgery","E":"Prescribing a walker and permanent activity restriction is the appropriate management for elderly patients with lumbar stenosis who fail conservative therapy, as surgical decompression is contraindicated over age 65"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indication for lumbar stenosis: persistent, functionally limiting neurogenic claudication after failure of adequate conservative management including PT and ESI. Standard procedure: lumbar laminectomy (decompression), with fusion if instability or spondylolisthesis coexists. In the SPORT trial, surgically treated patients with spinal stenosis and degenerative spondylolisthesis improved more than nonsurgical patients over 2 and 4 years (Foundations Ch 93). Age alone is not a contraindication. Intrathecal pumps are not standard for stenosis. Indefinite ESI is not appropriate after two failures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lumbar stenosis surgical indication = persistent neurogenic claudication after failed conservative management (PT + ESI). SPORT trial demonstrated surgical benefit at 2-4 years. Laminectomy = standard decompression. Fusion added if spondylolisthesis present. COMLEX: OMM is adjunctive; does not substitute for surgery when indicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip and vascular causes of leg pain with ambulation are excluded by workup before surgical planning for lumbar stenosis, ensuring the correct etiology is targeted"}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"NSAIDs, Analgesics & Opioids","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis asks why her naproxen must be taken twice daily while her friend's piroxicam is dosed only once daily. Which pharmacokinetic property most directly determines NSAID dosing frequency?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic first-pass extraction rate, which determines the fraction of an oral dose that reaches systemic circulation before entering the portal circulation and undergoing presystemic metabolism","B":"Renal clearance rate, which governs the daily volume of plasma cleared of drug and determines the risk of toxicity accumulation in patients with impaired kidney function","C":"Elimination half-life, which ranges from approximately 2 hours for ibuprofen to approximately 50 hours for piroxicam, governs how often a dose must be administered to maintain therapeutic concentrations","D":"Volume of distribution, which determines how extensively a drug distributes into peripheral tissues and fat depots relative to the plasma compartment","E":"Oral bioavailability, which determines the maximum achievable plasma concentration after a single dose and sets the upper bound for therapeutic exposure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs vary widely in half-life: ibuprofen ~2 h, naproxen ~12-17 h, piroxicam ~50 h. Half-life determines the dosing interval needed to maintain therapeutic plasma and synovial fluid concentrations. Bioavailability, volume of distribution, and clearance all influence pharmacokinetics but it is half-life specifically that dictates how frequently a drug must be redosed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Piroxicam (t1/2 ~50 h) = once daily; naproxen (t1/2 ~12-17 h) = twice daily; ibuprofen (t1/2 ~2 h) = three to four times daily. COMLEX: NSAID dosing frequency reflects half-life; synovial fluid concentrations can outlast serum concentrations for short-t1/2 agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis management includes NSAIDs; kinetic properties determine dosing interval and joint-level drug accumulation"},{"id":"sdl22_q02","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pharmacology student is asked to describe the primary mechanism by which NSAIDs reduce inflammation, pain, and fever. Which of the following best describes this mechanism?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs activate lipocortin, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and prevents the release of arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids, thereby blocking all downstream eicosanoid synthesis upstream of cyclooxygenase","B":"NSAIDs block nuclear factor kappa-B nuclear translocation, reducing transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 in activated macrophages and synoviocytes","C":"NSAIDs directly antagonize bradykinin B2 and histamine H1 receptors on sensory nerve fibers, preventing peripheral nociceptor activation without affecting prostaglandin biosynthesis","D":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase enzymes, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin and thereby reducing pain sensitization, vasodilation, and fever","E":"NSAIDs upregulate heme oxygenase-1 expression in endothelial cells, increasing production of anti-inflammatory carbon monoxide and biliverdin that suppress leukocyte adhesion and activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary mechanism of all NSAIDs is COX (cyclooxygenase) inhibition. COX-1 and COX-2 convert arachidonic acid to PGH2, the precursor to prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and prostacyclin. Prostaglandins sensitize nociceptors, promote vasodilation, and act on the hypothalamus to raise body temperature. Blocking COX reduces all these effects. The other choices describe mechanisms of corticosteroids, biologics, or secondary NSAID actions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-1 = constitutive (gastric protection, platelet aggregation); COX-2 = inducible (inflammation, fever). NSAIDs block both (nonselective) or COX-2 only (celecoxib). COMLEX: Prostaglandins mediate inflammation and sensitize nociceptors; COX inhibition reduces PGE2-dependent fever set point in the hypothalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Bone remodeling is partially regulated by prostaglandins; COX-derived PGs modulate osteoclast and osteoblast activity"},{"id":"sdl22_q03","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An attending explains that indomethacin's anti-inflammatory potency exceeds what would be expected from COX inhibition alone. Which of the following best describes indomethacin's secondary mechanisms of action?","choices":{"A":"Indomethacin inhibits phospholipase A2 and C, reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and adhesion, suppresses free radical production, inhibits lymphocyte proliferation, and lowers IL-1 synthesis independently of COX","B":"Indomethacin activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma in macrophages, shifting polarization toward an anti-inflammatory phenotype and suppressing NF-kB target gene transcription in synovial tissue","C":"Indomethacin competitively blocks bradykinin B2 receptors and histamine H1 receptors at peripheral nociceptors, reducing afferent pain signal transduction independently of prostaglandin synthesis inhibition","D":"Indomethacin inhibits 5-lipoxygenase in mast cells and eosinophils, blocking leukotriene C4 and D4 synthesis and preventing bronchoconstriction and vascular permeability changes at sites of inflammation","E":"Indomethacin stabilizes lysosomal membranes in neutrophils and macrophages, preventing release of proteolytic enzymes and reactive oxygen species that would otherwise amplify tissue inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Indomethacin and several other NSAIDs have secondary anti-inflammatory mechanisms including inhibition of phospholipase A2/C (reducing arachidonic acid availability upstream of COX), inhibition of neutrophil migration and adhesion, suppression of free radical production, and reduction of T- and B-cell proliferation. These account for its clinical potency beyond COX inhibition alone and also contribute to its toxicity profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include phospholipase inhibition, anti-chemotaxis, and lymphocyte suppression — these are lower-yield but distinguish indomethacin as a particularly potent anti-inflammatory. COMLEX: Indomethacin is used for ankylosing spondylitis and gout; its broad mechanisms underlie both efficacy and toxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs exert secondary effects on connective tissue and joint physiology beyond COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q04","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen 600 mg three times daily. After six months he develops epigastric pain and is found to have a gastric ulcer on endoscopy. Which mechanism best explains NSAID-induced gastric mucosal injury?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs undergo extensive enterohepatic recirculation, directly damaging the colonic and gastric mucosa through repeated high-concentration biliary re-exposure to the chemically irritant parent drug compound","B":"COX-1 inhibition reduces prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin in gastric mucosa, impairing mucus and bicarbonate secretion, decreasing mucosal blood flow, and leaving the epithelium vulnerable to acid-peptic injury","C":"NSAIDs block parietal cell histamine H2 receptors, paradoxically increasing gastrin-stimulated basal acid output and reducing the efficacy of endogenous mucosal protection mechanisms","D":"COX-2 inhibition in gastric epithelial stem cells impairs mucosal regeneration and prevents healing of micro-erosions caused by normal mechanical and chemical stress from gastric contents","E":"NSAIDs directly activate mast cells in the gastric submucosa, triggering histamine release that increases acid secretion through H2 receptors while simultaneously causing local vascular permeability changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"COX-1 is constitutively expressed in gastric mucosa and produces PGE2 and prostacyclin that stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion, promote mucosal blood flow, and inhibit acid secretion. NSAID-mediated COX-1 inhibition removes this protection, exposing the epithelium to acid-peptic damage. Selective COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1 and have significantly lower GI ulcer rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gastric protection by COX-1 prostaglandins is the key concept — inhibiting COX-1 removes the mucus/bicarbonate/blood flow triad. Add a PPI or use celecoxib to reduce risk. COMLEX: GI adverse effects are the most common reason NSAIDs are discontinued; H. pylori co-infection multiplies the risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — Long-term NSAID adverse effects (GI, renal, CV) are central considerations in the knee arthritis diagnostic and management workflow"},{"id":"sdl22_q05","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A hematologist is counseling an orthopedic patient about NSAID selection. She explains that one class carries a critical antiplatelet effect that can persist for over a week. Which statement best distinguishes aspirin from nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate regarding platelet effects?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1, blocking thromboxane A2 synthesis for the 8 to 10 day platelet lifespan; nonacetylated salicylates cannot acetylate COX and do not inhibit aggregation","B":"Nonacetylated salicylates selectively inhibit COX-2 in platelets, reducing prostacyclin production while leaving thromboxane A2 intact, thereby paradoxically promoting platelet aggregation compared to aspirin","C":"Aspirin causes immune-mediated thrombocytopenia through hapten formation, reducing platelet counts and prolonging bleeding time; nonacetylated salicylates prolong aggregation time through reversible COX-1 binding only","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates cause greater platelet inhibition than aspirin because their longer plasma half-lives allow more sustained exposure of platelet COX-1 to the active salicylate moiety","E":"Aspirin activates fibrinolysis through tissue plasminogen activator upregulation in endothelial cells; nonacetylated salicylates inhibit fibrinolysis through plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 induction in hepatocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin is unique among salicylates in permanently acetylating serine-530 on COX-1. Because platelets are anucleate, they cannot synthesize new COX-1; therefore, the antiplatelet effect lasts the entire ~8-10 day platelet lifespan. Nonacetylated salicylates (salsalate, choline magnesium trisalicylate) do not acetylate COX and thus do not inhibit platelet aggregation, making them suitable for patients with bleeding risk or aspirin-exacerbated asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = only NSAID that covalently (irreversibly) modifies COX. All others = reversible competitive inhibitors. COMLEX: Nonacetylated salicylates tolerated in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (no COX-1 platelet effect, no leukotriene shunting).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — Hip osteoarthritis management requires selecting NSAIDs by category based on individual patient risk profile (GI, CV, renal)"},{"id":"sdl22_q06","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with moderate musculoskeletal low back pain is prescribed tramadol. His provider explains that tramadol works differently from both traditional NSAIDs and pure opioids. Which statement best describes tramadol's mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a selective COX-2 inhibitor with no opioid receptor activity, providing anti-inflammatory analgesia through prostaglandin inhibition without the respiratory depression or abuse potential of opioids","B":"Tramadol is a full mu-opioid agonist with additional anticholinergic properties that reduce smooth muscle spasm contributing to musculoskeletal pain without affecting reuptake transporters","C":"Tramadol inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 while activating alpha-2 adrenoceptors in the dorsal horn, reducing substance P release through a presynaptic mechanism independent of opioid receptor engagement","D":"Tramadol is a selective delta-opioid receptor agonist that modulates limbic system pain processing through hippocampal circuits without affecting spinal cord neurotransmission or reuptake transporters","E":"Tramadol acts as a weak mu-opioid agonist while also inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake transporters, modulating descending pain inhibitory pathways in the spinal cord and brainstem"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tramadol combines two distinct mechanisms: weak mu-opioid receptor agonism (via the active O-desmethyltramadol metabolite) and inhibition of both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, similar to SNRIs. This dual action modulates descending inhibitory pain pathways. The SNRI component creates significant risk for serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs, MAOIs, or other serotonergic drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tramadol = weak mu agonist + SNRI. Risk: serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/MAOIs; lower seizure threshold. Not a first-line analgesic for patients on serotonergic drugs. COMLEX: Tramadol serotonin syndrome is a tested clinical presentation; also note it requires CYP2D6 for active metabolite formation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain management employs acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol as non-opioid analgesic options"},{"id":"sdl22_q07","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is studying opioid receptor pharmacology for Step 1. Which of the following best describes the receptor type, signal transduction mechanism, and net physiological effect shared by mu, delta, and kappa opioid receptors?","choices":{"A":"Opioid receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels that directly hyperpolarize neurons upon agonist binding, bypassing second-messenger cascades and producing instantaneous inhibition of action potential generation","B":"Mu receptors couple exclusively to Gs proteins, increasing cAMP and activating protein kinase A to open calcium channels and facilitate ascending pain signal transmission in the dorsal horn","C":"Delta and kappa receptors couple to Gq proteins, activating phospholipase C to increase IP3 and diacylglycerol, triggering protein kinase C-mediated excitatory neuronal effects that paradoxically reduce pain perception","D":"Opioid receptors activate receptor tyrosine kinase cascades similar to growth factor receptors, producing long-term synaptic potentiation in pain processing circuits through ERK and Akt phosphorylation","E":"Mu, delta, and kappa receptors are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs that lower cAMP, close presynaptic calcium channels, and open postsynaptic potassium channels, reducing neuronal excitability and pain transmission"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All three classical opioid receptors (mu, delta, kappa) are Gi/o-coupled GPCRs. Gi/o activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase (decreasing cAMP), closes N-type presynaptic Ca2+ channels (reducing neurotransmitter release), and opens GIRK (inwardly rectifying K+) channels postsynaptically (causing hyperpolarization). The net result is reduced neuronal excitability and pain signal propagation at all levels of the pain pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mu/delta/kappa = Gi/o GPCRs. Endogenous ligands: beta-endorphin (mu preferred), enkephalin (delta preferred), dynorphin (kappa preferred). COMLEX: Opioid receptor classification is core pharmacology; Gi/o coupling is the unifying mechanism across all three subtypes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — Opioid receptor pharmacology is foundational to understanding acute pain management in femoroacetabular dislocations"},{"id":"sdl22_q08","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A primary care physician is reviewing the current opioid prescribing framework with a third-year student. Which of the following represents an acceptable and guideline-supported use of opioid medications?","choices":{"A":"First-line therapy for mild-to-moderate chronic low back pain in patients who have not yet tried non-opioid analgesics such as NSAIDs, acetaminophen, physical therapy, or cognitive behavioral therapy","B":"Routine long-term maintenance therapy for osteoarthritis of the knee or hip when patients report inadequate response to acetaminophen after only two weeks of therapy","C":"Management of acute severe pain (postoperative, trauma-related), cancer-related pain at any stage, and palliative or end-of-life care where comfort is the primary treatment goal","D":"Preferred initial treatment for all neuropathic pain syndromes over gabapentin, pregabalin, tricyclic antidepressants, or SNRIs in adult outpatient settings with documented chronic pain","E":"Long-term monotherapy for fibromyalgia syndrome when physical therapy and antidepressant medications have been trialed for fewer than four weeks without achieving complete symptom resolution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Opioids are appropriate for acute severe pain (surgery, trauma), cancer pain at any stage, and palliative/end-of-life care. They are not first-line for chronic non-cancer musculoskeletal pain, where non-opioid agents and physical therapy should be exhausted first. Long-term opioid use for OA or fibromyalgia is generally not guideline-supported as first- or second-line therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three main acceptable opioid indications. For chronic pain, CDC guidelines emphasize non-opioid alternatives first. COMLEX: Acceptable opioid use is a tested clinical presentation; addiction medicine and responsible prescribing are COMLEX-emphasized topics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Opioid prescribing guidelines apply to Achilles tendinopathy and musculoskeletal pain management"},{"id":"sdl22_q09","sdlNumber":22,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with chronic cancer pain has taken oral morphine at stable doses for two years. He requires the same dose for adequate analgesia as when he started. He continues to have severe constipation despite daily laxative use. Which explanation is most accurate for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory depression does not develop tolerance because brainstem pre-Botzinger complex mu receptors remain constitutively sensitized throughout chronic opioid therapy regardless of dose or duration","B":"Constipation and miosis persist throughout chronic opioid therapy because enteric mu receptors and pupillary sphincter neurons do not undergo the receptor desensitization seen in CNS pain-processing circuits","C":"Euphoria does not develop tolerance because mesolimbic dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens is mediated by delta receptors, which are not subject to the Gi-mediated desensitization that affects mu receptors","D":"Sedation is uniquely resistant to tolerance because cortical arousal pathways in the ascending reticular activating system are constitutively protected from receptor internalization by scaffold proteins","E":"Nausea is the opioid adverse effect most resistant to tolerance because vestibular system chemoreceptor trigger zone neurons have constitutively high baseline mu receptor expression that resists downregulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tolerance develops to most CNS-mediated opioid effects (analgesia, euphoria, sedation, respiratory depression) through receptor desensitization, downregulation, and uncoupling from Gi/o proteins. However, constipation and miosis do not develop tolerance because peripheral myenteric plexus mu receptors (governing GI motility) and iris sphincter neurons do not undergo the same adaptive changes. This is why laxatives are always co-prescribed with chronic opioids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tolerance does NOT develop to constipation or miosis — classic board point. COMLEX: Always prescribe prophylactic laxative with chronic opioids; stimulant laxatives (senna) preferred over osmotic agents for opioid-induced constipation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Opioid adverse effects inform chronic shoulder pathology pain management decisions"},{"id":"sdl22_q10","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the common properties of the kinetics of NSAIDs.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 59-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen three times daily. He reads that ibuprofen has a serum half-life of about 2 hours and worries that his knee is unprotected for most of each dosing interval. According to the pharmacology reading for this SDL, which statement about NSAID distribution into joints best answers his concern?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs do not enter synovial fluid, so joint benefit comes from central COX inhibition and lasts as long as serum levels","B":"Short half-life NSAIDs remain in synovial fluid longer than their serum half-lives predict after repeated dosing","C":"Long half-life NSAIDs leave synovial fluid much faster than they leave serum, so short-acting drugs are always preferred","D":"NSAIDs reach the joint exclusively after hepatic conversion to active metabolites, which have half-lives of several days","E":"Synovial NSAID levels depend on albumin in joint fluid, so levels fall to zero as soon as serum albumin binding drops"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Katzung's NSAID chapter assigned for this SDL states that all NSAIDs can be found in synovial fluid after repeated dosing, and that drugs with short half-lives remain in the joints longer than their serum half-lives would predict, while drugs with longer half-lives leave synovial fluid at a rate proportional to their serum half-lives. NSAIDs do reach synovial fluid. Long half-life drugs track their serum half-lives rather than clearing faster. Most NSAIDs are active parent compounds; prodrugs such as nabumetone and sulindac are exceptions, not the rule. The chapter notes most NSAIDs are about 98% bound to albumin in plasma, but it does not describe synovial levels vanishing when binding changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID kinetics: well absorbed, ~98% albumin bound, hepatic CYP2C/CYP3A metabolism and glucuronidation, renal elimination, enterohepatic circulation (more circulation → more lower GI irritation); half-life ranges ~2 h (ibuprofen) to ~50 h (piroxicam). COMLEX: Dosing interval follows serum half-life, but joint levels persist longer for short-acting agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects by body system"},{"id":"sdl22_q11","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the common source of mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction takes low-dose aspirin 81 mg daily for secondary prevention. His cardiologist explains that aspirin's antiplatelet effect is irreversible, unlike other NSAIDs. Which mechanism best explains why aspirin's COX inhibition permanently eliminates thromboxane A2 synthesis in platelets for 8 to 10 days?","choices":{"A":"Aspirin is the only NSAID small enough to cross the platelet plasma membrane and reach intracellular COX-1 stores; other NSAIDs are excluded by the platelet membrane lipid bilayer and can only inhibit surface-associated COX","B":"Aspirin selectively inhibits COX-2 at low doses while simultaneously acetylating a different serine residue on COX-1 that produces 15-epi-lipoxin A4, an endogenous anti-aggregatory mediator that suppresses platelet function for days","C":"Aspirin covalently acetylates serine-530 on the COX-1 active site, permanently inactivating the enzyme; since platelets are anucleate and cannot transcribe or translate new COX-1 protein, the inhibition lasts the entire platelet lifespan","D":"Aspirin is metabolized to salicylate in the liver, and it is the salicylate metabolite that permanently binds the ADP receptor P2Y12 on platelet surfaces, preventing aggregation signal transduction independently of the COX pathway","E":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 with equal potency at any dose; other NSAIDs selectively target COX-2 at clinical doses and therefore preserve thromboxane A2 synthesis from platelet COX-1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin uniquely acetylates serine-530 on COX-1 (and serine-516 on COX-2), forming a covalent bond that permanently inactivates the enzyme. In nucleated cells, new COX protein can be synthesized within hours, restoring function. Platelets, being anucleate, cannot replace inactivated COX-1 for the rest of their 8-10 day lifespan. All other NSAIDs are reversible competitive inhibitors, so their antiplatelet effect resolves as drug is cleared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin = irreversible (covalent) COX acetylation; all other NSAIDs = reversible competitive inhibition. Platelets anucleate = no new COX synthesis = lasting antiplatelet effect. COMLEX: Preoperative aspirin cessation timing is based on platelet lifespan (~10 days for full recovery).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — Knee ligament injuries: perioperative antiplatelet management requires understanding aspirin irreversible COX-1 acetylation and the timeline for platelet recovery"},{"id":"sdl22_q12","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe other secondary mechanisms of action common for NSAIDs.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with osteoarthritis of both knees asks his rheumatologist whether long-term NSAID use might offer any cancer prevention benefits. The rheumatologist mentions epidemiological data suggesting reduced colorectal cancer incidence with regular NSAID use. Which secondary mechanism best explains this potential protective effect?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs upregulate p53 tumor suppressor expression in colonic epithelial cells by reducing prostaglandin E2-mediated ubiquitination of p53 protein, enhancing apoptotic elimination of genetically damaged pre-malignant cells","B":"NSAIDs reduce vascular endothelial growth factor expression in colonic stroma by blocking COX-2-derived 12-hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid signaling, impairing angiogenesis in early adenoma microenvironments","C":"NSAIDs stimulate Toll-like receptor 4 signaling in colonic macrophages, increasing local interferon-gamma production that activates cytotoxic T-lymphocytes against dysplastic epithelial cells expressing altered surface antigens","D":"COX-2 inhibition in colonic epithelium reduces PGE2-driven proliferation and apoptosis resistance in pre-malignant cells; epidemiological studies show up to 50 percent lower colorectal cancer risk with 5 or more years of use","E":"NSAIDs inhibit the Wnt signaling pathway in intestinal stem cells by reducing beta-catenin phosphorylation downstream of prostaglandin receptor activation, decreasing stem cell renewal in the colon crypt epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A well-documented secondary benefit of NSAIDs — particularly aspirin and indomethacin — is colorectal cancer risk reduction. COX-2 is overexpressed in colonic adenomas and carcinomas; its product PGE2 promotes cell proliferation and suppresses apoptosis. Regular NSAID use inhibits this pathway, with epidemiological data showing ~50% reduction in colorectal cancer incidence with long-term use. This is distinct from COX primary anti-inflammatory mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 overexpressed in colorectal adenomas/carcinomas — NSAIDs reduce cancer risk via PGE2 reduction. COMLEX: Secondary NSAID mechanisms include chemopreventive properties; indomethacin is particularly well-studied in this regard.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — Exercise and joint aging: secondary NSAID mechanisms reduce oxidative and proliferative tissue injury in chronically inflamed tissues beyond primary COX inhibition"},{"id":"sdl22_q13","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with NYHA Class II heart failure and knee osteoarthritis is prescribed ibuprofen by his orthopedic surgeon. One week later, his serum creatinine rises from 1.1 to 2.4 mg/dL. His urine output has decreased. Which mechanism best explains this NSAID-induced acute kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Renal prostaglandins normally maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation to preserve GFR when renal perfusion is reduced; NSAID COX inhibition removes this protection, precipitating acute prerenal azotemia in heart failure","B":"Ibuprofen directly inhibits aquaporin-2 water channels in collecting duct principal cells, reducing free water reabsorption, causing medullary washout, and impairing the concentrating ability needed to maintain adequate GFR","C":"NSAID metabolites compete with creatinine for proximal tubular secretion transporters, pharmacologically blocking creatinine excretion and artificially elevating serum creatinine without truly reducing the glomerular filtration rate","D":"Ibuprofen activates renal thromboxane A2 receptors on afferent arterioles, directly causing vasoconstriction and reducing single-nephron GFR through a prostaglandin-independent mechanism that is not reversed by stopping the drug","E":"Chronic ibuprofen use triggers autoimmune tubulointerstitial nephritis through T-cell sensitization to NSAID-protein adducts formed in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells during phase I hepatic biotransformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In states of reduced effective circulating volume (heart failure, cirrhosis, hypovolemia), the kidney becomes prostaglandin-dependent to maintain afferent arteriolar tone and GFR. COX inhibition removes this compensatory prostaglandin vasodilation, leading to afferent arteriolar constriction and acute reduction in GFR. This is a hemodynamically mediated, reversible form of acute kidney injury that resolves with NSAID discontinuation. NSAIDs are contraindicated in patients with decompensated heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostaglandins maintain RBF/GFR when perfusion is compromised (heart failure, cirrhosis, dehydration) — NSAIDs most dangerous in these settings. COMLEX: NSAIDs contraindicated in decompensated heart failure; renal PG-dependence underlies the mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID pharmacokinetics and renal hemodynamic adverse effects are critical in elderly patients with underlying cardiovascular and renal comorbidities"},{"id":"sdl22_q14","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss adverse effects of NSAIDs by category (salicylates, COX-2 selective inhibitors, nonselective COX inhibitors).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis requires long-term NSAID therapy. She has a documented history of peptic ulcer disease but no cardiovascular disease history and a normal echocardiogram. She needs adequate anti-inflammatory coverage. Which NSAID class best minimizes her GI complication risk?","choices":{"A":"High-dose nonselective COX inhibitors such as ibuprofen are preferred because their potent COX-2 inhibition reduces joint inflammation more rapidly than selective agents and their GI risk can be neutralized with routine proton pump inhibitor co-prescription","B":"High-dose aspirin is preferred because irreversible COX-1 inhibition paradoxically protects the gastric mucosa by suppressing thromboxane A2-mediated mast cell degranulation and reducing local histamine-driven acid secretion","C":"Selective COX-2 inhibitors such as celecoxib are preferred because sparing gastric COX-1 preserves prostaglandin E2-mediated mucus and bicarbonate secretion, significantly reducing peptic ulcer risk compared to nonselective NSAIDs","D":"Nonacetylated salicylates such as salsalate are the best choice because they provide full anti-inflammatory efficacy equivalent to celecoxib without any COX inhibition and therefore carry no GI ulcer risk","E":"Nonselective COX inhibitors combined with misoprostol fully eliminate peptic ulcer risk by replacing the gastroprotective prostaglandins lost to COX-1 inhibition and should be preferred over selective agents for their superior anti-inflammatory potency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"COX-2 selective inhibitors (celecoxib) spare gastric COX-1, preserving PGE2-mediated mucosal protection (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow). This results in significantly fewer gastric ulcers compared to nonselective NSAIDs. In this patient with no CV history, the increased CV risk of celecoxib is less concerning than her peptic ulcer history. Nonacetylated salicylates have reduced but not absent GI risk and may have less anti-inflammatory potency for RA. Misoprostol co-prescription reduces but does not eliminate risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COX-2 selective = fewer GI ulcers (spares COX-1 gastric protection) but greater CV risk (reduces endothelial prostacyclin). Weigh GI vs. CV risk per patient profile. COMLEX: Patient-specific risk stratification determines NSAID category selection in the clinical management framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Hip OA: NSAID category selection balances GI and cardiovascular risk in elderly patients with competing comorbidities requiring chronic anti-inflammatory therapy"},{"id":"sdl22_q15","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the mechanism of action for other analgesics including acetaminophen, ketorolac, and tramadol.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with chronic low back pain and major depressive disorder is taking sertraline 100 mg daily. Her orthopedic specialist prescribes tramadol 50 mg every 6 hours. Three days later she presents with agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, and hyperthermia. Which mechanism best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tramadol is a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6, blocking sertraline metabolism and increasing sertraline plasma concentrations to toxic levels that directly stimulate 5-HT2A receptors in the brainstem independent of reuptake inhibition","B":"Tramadol activates mu-opioid receptors in the dorsal raphe nucleus, paradoxically increasing serotonin synthesis and release into synaptic clefts where sertraline prevents reuptake, creating a purely pharmacodynamic interaction","C":"Tramadol is metabolized to O-desmethyltramadol, which acts as a potent direct 5-HT2C receptor agonist and produces serotonergic toxicity independently of reuptake inhibition or pharmacokinetic interactions with sertraline","D":"Tramadol inhibits both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake; combined with sertraline's serotonin reuptake inhibition, synaptic serotonin accumulates and overstimulates 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors, causing serotonin syndrome","E":"Tramadol and sertraline both inhibit dopamine reuptake in striatal circuits, producing dopaminergic overstimulation that mimics serotonin syndrome clinically but is mechanistically mediated through D2 receptor hyperstimulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tramadol has dual mechanisms: weak mu-opioid agonism and inhibition of both serotonin (SERT) and norepinephrine (NET) reuptake transporters. Sertraline independently blocks SERT. The combination creates marked excess synaptic serotonin, activating 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors and producing serotonin syndrome (agitation, clonus, diaphoresis, hyperthermia, tachycardia). Tramadol is contraindicated with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, and other serotonergic medications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serotonin syndrome triad = altered mental status + autonomic instability + neuromuscular abnormalities (clonus). Tramadol + SSRI is the classic precipitant. COMLEX: Serotonin syndrome is distinguished from NMS by clonus and rapid onset; treatment = cyproheptadine, stop offending agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: tramadol serotonin syndrome risk mandates careful screening for concurrent serotonergic medications before prescribing"},{"id":"sdl22_q16","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the mechanism of action of opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks their neuroscience tutor to explain how opioids reduce pain transmission at the level of the spinal dorsal horn. The tutor describes both presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms. Which of the following correctly describes both components of opioid-mediated pain inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Presynaptically, opioids activate adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, which phosphorylates potassium channels and hyperpolarizes the presynaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids stimulate IP3 production to sequester intracellular calcium and reduce AMPA receptor sensitivity","B":"Presynaptically, opioids block voltage-gated sodium channels to prevent action potential propagation into the synaptic terminal; postsynaptically, opioids activate chloride channels to hyperpolarize the secondary neuron and suppress ascending pain signal transmission","C":"Presynaptically, opioids activate phospholipase C to produce diacylglycerol that directly closes N-type calcium channels; postsynaptically, opioids increase cyclic GMP through nitric oxide synthase activation, reducing glutamate receptor phosphorylation and sensitivity","D":"Presynaptically, opioids stimulate large-conductance potassium channels to prolong repolarization and reduce firing frequency; postsynaptically, opioids block NMDA receptors through an open-channel mechanism to prevent calcium influx and central sensitization","E":"Presynaptically, opioids close voltage-gated calcium channels on primary afferent terminals, reducing Ca2+ influx and release of glutamate and substance P; postsynaptically, they open inwardly rectifying potassium channels and hyperpolarize dorsal horn neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mu (and other opioid) receptor activation via Gi/o coupling produces two key synaptic effects: (1) presynaptically — closure of N-type (and P/Q-type) voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reduces intracellular Ca2+ and decreases vesicular release of glutamate and substance P from primary afferent C-fiber and A-delta terminals; (2) postsynaptically — opening of GIRK (G-protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K+) channels hyperpolarizes the secondary neuron, raising its threshold for firing. Together these reduce ascending pain transmission in the dorsal horn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pre = close Ca2+ channels (less neurotransmitter release); Post = open K+ channels (hyperpolarization). Both mediated by Gi/o. COMLEX: Same mechanisms operate in supraspinal sites (PAG, RVM) to activate descending inhibitory pathways.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: opioid presynaptic and postsynaptic mechanisms explain the rapid analgesic onset needed for procedural pain control in acute joint reductions"},{"id":"sdl22_q17","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Outline acceptable uses for opioid medications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with opioid use disorder and a history of intravenous heroin use is started on buprenorphine plus naloxone (Suboxone) for maintenance therapy. His provider explains the specific pharmacological rationale for this combination. Which statement best explains the therapeutic design of this regimen?","choices":{"A":"Buprenorphine is a full mu-opioid agonist with a very long plasma half-life exceeding 72 hours; naloxone is added as a competitive antagonist that partially occupies mu receptors and creates a pharmacological ceiling preventing dose escalation-related euphoria","B":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with very high receptor affinity and slow dissociation, producing a ceiling on respiratory depression; naloxone deters IV misuse, since it is poorly absorbed sublingually but precipitates withdrawal if injected","C":"Buprenorphine is a kappa-opioid receptor antagonist that blocks the dysphoria and paranoia produced by endogenous dynorphin signaling; naloxone functions as a partial mu agonist to suppress opioid withdrawal symptoms without producing significant euphoria","D":"Buprenorphine undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism that converts it to norbuprenorphine, a full mu-opioid agonist; naloxone competitively blocks this active metabolite from binding mu receptors in mesolimbic reward circuits to eliminate the addiction potential","E":"Buprenorphine and naloxone target different opioid receptor populations in the same brain region; buprenorphine selectively activates spinal cord mu receptors for analgesia while naloxone selectively blocks limbic mu receptors to eliminate the rewarding properties of opioids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Buprenorphine is a partial mu-opioid agonist with exceptionally high receptor affinity (displaces full agonists) and slow receptor dissociation. Its partial agonism produces analgesia and suppresses withdrawal while creating a ceiling on euphoria and respiratory depression. Sublingual naloxone is poorly absorbed (first-pass destroyed), contributing minimally to the clinical effect when taken properly. However, if the tablet is dissolved and injected, naloxone reaches systemic circulation and precipitates withdrawal, deterring intravenous misuse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buprenorphine = partial mu agonist (ceiling effect on respiratory depression); high affinity displaces other opioids. Naloxone in Suboxone = abuse deterrent (poor sublingual bioavailability but active IV). COMLEX: Opioid use disorder treatment with buprenorphine is a COMLEX-emphasized clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — Hip dislocations: buprenorphine partial agonism and naloxone deterrence inform management decisions when opioid-maintained patients require acute pain control"},{"id":"sdl22_q18","sdlNumber":22,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Discuss adverse effects of opioid medication.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 79-year-old man with stage 3 chronic kidney disease (GFR 35 mL/min) undergoes elective total hip arthroplasty. The anesthesiologist orders meperidine 50 mg IV every 4 hours for postoperative pain. On postoperative day 3, the patient develops myoclonus and then a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Naloxone administration fails to terminate the seizure. Which mechanism is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Meperidine is metabolized to normeperidine, a renally excreted active metabolite; in renal insufficiency it accumulates and lowers the seizure threshold, which naloxone cannot reverse because normeperidine excites the CNS through non-opioid receptor mechanisms","B":"Meperidine itself directly activates NMDA glutamate receptors in the hippocampal CA3 region after accumulating to toxic plasma concentrations in renal failure, triggering excitotoxic calcium influx and seizure activity independent of opioid receptor binding","C":"Chronic meperidine administration upregulates mu-opioid receptor expression in motor cortex interneurons, paradoxically increasing cortical excitability and producing myoclonus through receptor sensitization and increased Gi-mediated disinhibition of glutamatergic circuits","D":"Meperidine undergoes O-demethylation to meperidinic acid in hepatic microsomes, and this metabolite competes with GABA at ionotropic GABA-A receptors, reducing inhibitory chloride conductance in cortical motor networks and triggering focal seizure activity","E":"Renal failure impairs meperidine excretion and forces accumulation of the parent compound in central nervous system tissue where it directly blocks glycine receptors in spinal cord and brainstem interneurons, causing disinhibitory myoclonus through loss of glycinergic inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meperidine is demethylated in the liver to normeperidine, an active metabolite that is proconvulsant. Normeperidine accumulates in patients with decreased renal function or high doses and may cause seizures (Katzung Ch 31, SDL 22 reading). Normeperidine lowers the seizure threshold through non-opioid excitatory CNS mechanisms and therefore does not respond to naloxone. Meperidine is absolutely contraindicated in renal insufficiency and in elderly patients. Preferred alternatives in renal insufficiency include hydromorphone or fentanyl; morphine is less suitable because its glucuronide metabolites (M3G, M6G) also accumulate in renal failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meperidine contraindicated in renal failure (normeperidine accumulation) and MAOIs (serotonin syndrome/hyperpyrexic crisis). Seizures from normeperidine are naloxone-unresponsive — classic board trap. COMLEX: Meperidine is also contraindicated with MAOIs; alternative opioids must be selected in elderly patients with CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — Hip fracture: normeperidine accumulation in renal-impaired elderly patients produces non-naloxone-reversible seizures, mandating alternative opioid analgesic selection in the perioperative setting"}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type fixes the distal segment so the proximal segment moves, and is specifically recommended for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability?","choices":{"A":"Open kinetic chain exercise fixes the proximal segment while the distal segment moves freely, as in long arc quadriceps extension and most arm weight-lifting, making it ideal for functional improvement in activities of daily living","B":"Concentric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed during muscle shortening, generating compression forces that make it specifically suited for early post-operative knee and shoulder rehabilitation programs","C":"Eccentric exercise braces the distal segment against a fixed surface while lengthening contraction produces controlled deceleration forces, which is why it is prescribed for shoulder and knee sport-specific strengthening protocols","D":"Isometric exercise involves the distal segment being fixed against an immovable surface while the muscle contracts without joint movement, and is specifically prescribed for shoulder and knee rehabilitation after joint replacement surgery","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise fixes the distal segment while the proximal segment moves, as in leg presses, squats, and elliptical walking, making it ideal for shoulder and knee rehabilitation and dynamic stability training"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercise, the distal segment is fixed and the proximal segment moves — e.g., leg press, squat, elliptical walker. This creates multi-joint co-contraction and is ideal for shoulder and knee rehab and dynamic stability. Open kinetic chain (OKC) is the opposite: distal segment moves freely (e.g., long arc quad extension), used for ADL functional improvement. Concentric = shortening contraction; eccentric = lengthening contraction; isometric = no joint movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know CKC vs OKC — CKC has distal segment fixed (leg press, squat), OKC has distal segment free (quad extension). COMLEX: CKC is used in ACL/post-op knee rehab due to joint stability from co-contraction; OMM complements CKC by addressing somatic dysfunction that limits kinetic chain mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q02","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A sports medicine physician prescribes eccentric heel drops for a patient with Achilles tendinopathy. Which statement correctly defines eccentric exercise and its primary therapeutic application?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously shortening, generating maximum force at the shortest fiber length, which is why it is the primary mechanism for increasing muscle mass and strength","B":"Eccentric exercise occurs when a muscle contracts while simultaneously lengthening, such as during the extension phase of a biceps or hamstring curl, and is prescribed for sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention","C":"Eccentric exercise maintains constant muscle length during contraction against an immovable surface, making it ideal for joint-protective strengthening when joint mobility is restricted after injury or surgery","D":"Eccentric exercise applies constant resistance through the complete joint range of motion in both directions using free weights or cable machines, and is primarily prescribed for general muscle conditioning","E":"Eccentric exercise specifically targets deep trunk and abdominal stabilizers through whole-body lengthening movements such as Pilates and back extension, making it the preferred choice for lumbar spine rehabilitation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric contraction = muscle contracts while lengthening (e.g., extension phase of a biceps curl, lowering phase of a squat). Key therapeutic use: sport-specific strengthening and injury prevention — especially Achilles and patellar tendinopathy. Concentric (shortening) = increases mass/strength. Isometric = no length change. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM. Core stability targets low back/trunk/abdomen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercises are first-line for tendinopathy rehabilitation — Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is the classic example for Achilles tendinopathy. COMLEX: Eccentric loading promotes collagen remodeling in tendons; failure to prescribe appropriately leads to chronic tendinopathy that OMM alone cannot address.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q03","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which therapeutic exercise type is most specifically indicated for a patient with patellofemoral pain syndrome when joint range of motion is limited or contraindicated?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic exercise using constant resistance through full range of motion is most appropriate when joint mobility must be preserved and the goal is general muscle conditioning for the entire lower extremity","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise is most appropriate when joint mobility is limited because the free distal segment reduces compressive forces at the patellofemoral interface and allows strengthening without joint stress","C":"Isometric exercise contracts the muscle at constant length without joint movement, making it ideal when joint mobility is restricted; quadriceps isometrics are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome","D":"Core stability exercise is most appropriate for patients with patellofemoral joint mobility limitations because abdominal bracing reduces compressive forces on peripheral joints and enables safe supervised exercise","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is specifically recommended for patellofemoral syndrome because weight-bearing joint co-contraction reduces shear forces and maintains mobility through progressive loading of the articular surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isometric exercise = muscle contracts without changing length or moving the joint (e.g., holding a weight in a stationary position, quad sets). Specifically indicated when joint mobility is contraindicated. Quadriceps isometrics are first-line for patellofemoral pain syndrome. Isotonic = constant resistance through ROM (general conditioning). OKC = distal segment free (ADL function). CKC = dynamic stability for shoulder/knee rehab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isometric quad exercises are specifically recommended for patellofemoral pain syndrome — the lack of joint movement avoids provocative arc loading. COMLEX: When prescribing PT for patellofemoral syndrome, isometrics bridge the gap between immobility and full loading; OMM addressing patellar tracking and hip somatic dysfunction complements isometric strengthening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: therapeutic exercise types are central to conservative management and structured rehabilitation protocols"},{"id":"sdl23_q04","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary mechanism by which pulsed (nonthermal) therapeutic ultrasound promotes tissue healing, as distinguished from the thermal effects of continuous ultrasound?","choices":{"A":"Pulsed ultrasound heats superficial soft tissues more efficiently than continuous ultrasound because intermittent energy delivery allows deeper penetration before thermal energy dissipates into surrounding structures","B":"Pulsed ultrasound stimulates type I collagen synthesis by directly activating fibroblast gene expression through nuclear membrane deformation, a mechanism unique to intermittent acoustic radiation not seen with continuous delivery","C":"Pulsed ultrasound generates stable cavitation bubbles that mechanically disrupt collagen cross-links in scar tissue, enabling remodeling that continuous ultrasound cannot achieve due to its lower average power output","D":"Pulsed ultrasound activates the gate control mechanism in peripheral nociceptors by creating rhythmic pressure waves that override pain signal transmission to the dorsal horn, providing analgesia without tissue healing effects","E":"Pulsed ultrasound produces nonthermal effects by altering membrane permeability, ionic concentration gradients, and cellular biochemical activity at the cellular level, accelerating tissue healing without generating significant heat"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Continuous ultrasound = thermal effect (heats tissue). Pulsed ultrasound = nonthermal effect (membrane permeability, ionic gradients, cellular biochemical activity) → accelerates healing at the cellular level. High-frequency US treats superficial tissue (patellar tendons); lower frequencies penetrate deeper (up to 5 cm) for deep muscle bruising/spasms/strains. Can cause burns or endothelial damage if applied incorrectly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know thermal vs nonthermal US — continuous = heat, pulsed = cellular healing. Ultrasound CI include pacemakers, malignant tumors, epiphyseal plates, thrombophlebitis, acute inflammation, and reproductive organs. COMLEX: Ultrasound is an adjunctive modality; it does not replace OMM or therapeutic exercise but complements soft tissue treatment in PT protocols.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q05","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound to deliver topical medications through the skin to deeper tissues. Which preparation is typically used with this modality, and for which conditions?","choices":{"A":"A corticosteroid or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, usually mixed in an aqueous base at 10 percent concentration and applied with ultrasound at 1 to 2 watts per cm2, for inflammatory conditions such as tendonitis, arthritis, and bursitis","B":"Methotrexate 2.5% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into inflamed synovium to suppress local lymphocyte proliferation and provide disease-modifying effects in inflammatory arthritis","C":"Botulinum toxin type A solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, driven into skeletal muscle to block acetylcholine release and relieve spasm and myofascial trigger point pain in chronic conditions","D":"Hyaluronic acid 0.5% solution is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered into periarticular tissues to augment synovial fluid viscosity and provide viscoelastic cushioning comparable to intra-articular hyaluronate injection","E":"Capsaicin 0.025% cream is the most commonly used medication in phonophoresis, delivered through the dermis to deplete substance P in peripheral nociceptors and provide long-lasting analgesia for chronic musculoskeletal pain syndromes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound to drive topical medication through the skin to deeper tissues. Per the course reading (Rand et al., Am Fam Physician 2007), a corticosteroid or NSAID is usually mixed in an aqueous base at a 10 percent concentration and applied with ultrasound at 1 to 2 W/cm2, for inflammatory conditions such as tendonitis, arthritis, and bursitis; ketoprofen tissue levels rise with phonophoresis compared with topical application alone. Dexamethasone 0.4% solution is the most commonly prescribed iontophoresis drug, not the phonophoresis preparation. Methotrexate, botulinum toxin, hyaluronic acid, and capsaicin are not the described phonophoresis preparations. The risk of thermal injury increases with the energy applied, and the contraindications are the same as for ultrasound.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + corticosteroid/NSAID (10% in an aqueous base, 1-2 W/cm2) for tendonitis, arthritis, bursitis. Iontophoresis = electric current + ionic drug (dexamethasone 0.4% most common). Contraindications for phonophoresis are the same as for ultrasound. COMLEX: Phonophoresis is a common adjunct in PT for inflammatory tendinopathies that may also benefit from OMM soft-tissue techniques targeting the same regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q06","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In which patient group is electrical stimulation absolutely contraindicated, and what is the physiological rationale for this restriction?","choices":{"A":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with any metal implant anywhere in the body because electrical current concentrates at all metallic surfaces and causes resistive heating that leads to thermal injury of surrounding bone and soft tissue","B":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with cardiac pacemakers and known cardiac arrhythmias because the external current can interfere with pacemaker sensing circuits, trigger arrhythmias, or compete with paced impulses","C":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with peripheral neuropathy because absent protective sensation prevents patients from reporting excessive current intensity, leading to inadvertent burns and electrode-related skin breakdown","D":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with osteoporosis because the muscle contractions generated by neuromuscular electrical stimulation create excessive compressive loads on fragile trabecular bone and may cause pathologic fractures","E":"Electrical stimulation is contraindicated in patients with inflammatory arthritis because the electrical current activates synovial mast cells and fibroblasts, increasing prostaglandin and cytokine production and worsening joint inflammation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Electrical stimulation absolute CI: cardiac pacemakers, known cardiac arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, and abdomen/pelvis of pregnant patients. Near metallic implants is a precaution (not absolute CI for all metals — CI specifically near metallic implants/wires/staples per iontophoresis; electrical stimulation is used with caution in patients with cardiac disease). Mechanism of ES analgesia: gate control + endogenous opioid/cortisol release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Electrical stimulation CI = cardiac pacemakers, arrhythmias, thrombophlebitis, pregnant abdomen. TENS mechanism: gate control theory + endogenous opioid release. COMLEX: TENS for LBP and neuropathic pain is commonly tested; OMM is often combined with TENS in multimodal pain management protocols for chronic low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: PT modalities including ultrasound, TENS, and iontophoresis are widely applied in LBP management"},{"id":"sdl23_q07","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a required component of a valid physical therapy prescription according to standard prescribing guidelines?","choices":{"A":"A completed insurance pre-authorization form signed by both the ordering physician and the physical therapist is required before initiating any PT intervention because payers mandate prospective coverage review for all outpatient rehabilitation services","B":"An objective functional assessment score such as the Oswestry Disability Index or DASH questionnaire is required on the PT prescription to establish a documented baseline for measuring therapeutic progress and justifying continuation of care","C":"A list of all current medications including dosage, frequency, and prescribing physician is required on the PT prescription so the therapist can screen for pharmacological agents that may alter pain perception, muscle tone, or cardiovascular response","D":"A radiology report confirming musculoskeletal pathology via X-ray or MRI is required before a PT prescription is valid, ensuring the therapist treats a documented structural abnormality rather than subjective symptoms without imaging confirmation","E":"A physician signature and date are required for a physical therapist to perform requested services; the prescription must also include the diagnosis, frequency and duration, specific protocols or treatments, and any safety precautions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Required components of a PT prescription (Table 3 from SDL): 1. Diagnosis (with proper coding for insurance billing), 2. Frequency and duration of therapy, 3. Specific protocols or treatments, 4. Safety precautions (ROM limitations, weight-bearing limits, comorbidities), 5. Physician signature and date (required for therapist to perform services). Goals are also a standard part. Functional scores, pre-auth forms, imaging, and medication lists are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription components = Diagnosis, Goals, Frequency/Duration, Specific treatments, Safety precautions, Physician signature/date. Missing any required component invalidates the prescription. COMLEX: Prescribing PT is a core competency — COMLEX tests whether students can write complete PT orders that include all mandated elements for musculoskeletal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q08","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician writes a PT prescription for knee osteoarthritis that includes the diagnosis, frequency, specific exercises, and physician signature. The physical therapist returns it as incomplete. Which required element is most likely missing?","choices":{"A":"Safety precautions are a required prescription component, including weight-bearing limitations, range-of-motion restrictions, and comorbidities affecting therapy; without them the therapist cannot safely set exercise intensity","B":"The patient's insurance policy number and prior authorization code are the missing required elements, without which the physical therapy clinic cannot bill for services or begin treatment under federal outpatient therapy billing regulations","C":"Documentation of prior conservative treatment attempts is the missing required element because payers require evidence that home-based exercise was tried and failed before approving supervised physical therapy for knee osteoarthritis management","D":"The specific brand name of exercise equipment to be used is the missing required element because physical therapists are legally required to use physician-specified equipment for all therapeutic exercise prescriptions for musculoskeletal conditions","E":"The expected discharge functional status stated in measurable terms such as anticipated walking distance or stair-climbing ability is the missing required element required by federal guidelines for establishing an end-point for care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A complete PT prescription includes: diagnosis, frequency/duration, specific protocols/treatments, safety precautions, and physician signature/date. In this case the prescription includes diagnosis, frequency, exercises, and signature — so the most likely missing element is safety precautions (weight-bearing limits, ROM restrictions, comorbidities affecting therapy decisions). These are critical for post-surgical, elderly, or high-risk patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Safety precautions are a required PT prescription element — especially critical for post-operative and high-risk patients. They inform the therapist about weight-bearing status, ROM restrictions, and clinical conditions affecting exercise prescription. COMLEX: Incomplete prescriptions are a legal liability; learn all required components to write a valid, actionable PT order.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q09","sdlNumber":23,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Based on the standard algorithm for selecting physical therapy interventions by phase of injury, which combination of modality, exercise, and frequency is most appropriate for a subacute musculoskeletal injury at 10 days post-injury?","choices":{"A":"In the subacute phase, cold application, pulsed ultrasound, and electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with isometric and gentle active range-of-motion exercises with gentle massage, prescribed daily for five days","B":"In the subacute phase, transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation and continuous ultrasound are the preferred modalities combined with progressive strengthening and stabilization exercises with myofascial release, prescribed twice per week for four weeks","C":"In the subacute phase, heat application, low-level laser therapy, and iontophoresis are preferred modalities combined with isotonic and active range-of-motion exercises and joint mobilization, prescribed three times per week for two weeks","D":"In the subacute phase, cryotherapy and compression with neuromuscular electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with eccentric loading and concentric strengthening with soft tissue mobilization, prescribed four times per week for one week","E":"In the subacute phase, phonophoresis and interferential electrical stimulation are the preferred modalities combined with core stability and closed kinetic chain exercises with passive stretching only, prescribed five times per week for two weeks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Injury phase algorithm: Acute (<72h) = cold, electrical stimulation, pulsed ultrasound; isometric, gentle ROM; gentle massage; daily x5 days. Subacute (3-14 days) = heat, electrical stimulation, low-level laser, iontophoresis; isotonic, active ROM, stretching; massage, joint mobilization; 3x/week x2 weeks. Chronic (>2 weeks) = TENS, continuous ultrasound; strengthening, stabilization; myofascial release; 2x/week x4 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the three injury phases and their corresponding modalities, exercises, and dosing: acute = cold/isometric/daily; subacute = heat/isotonic/3x/week; chronic = TENS/strengthening/2x/week. COMLEX: Prescribing PT by injury phase is a core clinical skill — COMLEX tests appropriate modality and exercise selection based on the timeline of musculoskeletal injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Shoulder diagnostic workflow: PT prescription is a core management step integrating diagnosis, frequency, and safety precautions"},{"id":"sdl23_q10","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old female competitive runner presents with 4 months of posterior heel pain. Imaging confirms Achilles tendinopathy without tear. She has failed rest and passive stretching. Which type of therapeutic exercise has the strongest evidence for promoting tendon collagen remodeling and should anchor her rehabilitation program?","choices":{"A":"Isometric exercise, in which muscle contraction occurs without any change in length, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because static loading at therapeutic angles reduces cortical pain inhibition without creating the shear forces associated with dynamic loading","B":"Open kinetic chain exercise, in which the distal segment is free, has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because the absence of weight-bearing compressive forces on the inflamed tendon allows strengthening while maintaining cardiovascular fitness","C":"Core stability exercise targeting the lumbopelvic region has the strongest evidence for Achilles tendinopathy because improving proximal control reduces excessive ground reaction force transmission and indirectly offloads the distal tendon","D":"Eccentric exercise, in which the muscle contracts while lengthening, has the strongest evidence for treating tendinopathy; eccentric heel drops load the Achilles tendon during the lengthening phase, stimulating collagen synthesis and tendon remodeling as first-line exercise therapy","E":"Isotonic exercise applying constant resistance through full range of motion in both concentric and eccentric phases has the strongest evidence for tendinopathy because it stimulates the highest absolute rate of tendon collagen cross-linking per session"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eccentric exercise is the exercise type with the strongest evidence for tendinopathy rehabilitation. The controlled lengthening contraction (e.g., Alfredson protocol: eccentric heel drops for Achilles) stimulates collagen synthesis and remodeling. Isometric exercise may reduce acute pain but does not drive tendon structural change as effectively. OKC reduces load but lacks the mechanotransduction signal needed for tendon healing. Core stability is complementary, not primary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eccentric exercise = first-line for tendinopathy; Alfredson protocol (eccentric heel drops) is classic for Achilles. COMLEX: Tendinopathy rehabilitation relies on controlled progressive loading — eccentric protocols are complemented by OMM soft-tissue techniques to address fascial restrictions contributing to mechanical load on the tendon.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: eccentric heel drops are first-line exercise therapy and the cornerstone of conservative management"},{"id":"sdl23_q11","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes ACL reconstruction with a patellar tendon autograft. The physical therapist plans to use closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercises preferentially over open kinetic chain (OKC) exercises in the early post-operative period. Which biomechanical feature of CKC exercise justifies this preference?","choices":{"A":"In closed kinetic chain exercise, the fixed distal segment promotes co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, enhancing joint stability and reducing anterior tibial shear on the healing graft compared with quadriceps-isolating open chain exercise","B":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it isolates the quadriceps without engaging the hamstrings, which reduces compressive load from antagonist co-contraction and prevents anterior drawer forces at the tibiofemoral joint","C":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it allows high-velocity ballistic movements that accelerate neuromuscular re-education and restore proprioception faster than the slower controlled movements characteristic of open kinetic chain protocols","D":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because it loads the graft exclusively through concentric contraction, avoiding eccentric lengthening forces that cause repetitive microtrauma and graft elongation during early healing","E":"Closed kinetic chain exercise is preferred post-ACL because the distal segment moves freely about the proximal segment, which reduces the bending moment at the knee and distributes load evenly across the healing graft and native structures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CKC = distal segment fixed, proximal moves (leg press, squat, lunges). Co-contraction of quads + hamstrings in CKC reduces anterior tibial shear (and therefore graft stress) compared to OKC quad-isolated exercises (e.g., leg extension). This is why CKC is the foundation of early ACL rehab. OKC exercises are reintroduced later in rehab when the graft is more mature. The option describing CKC with a free distal segment is incorrect — that defines OKC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKC = distal fixed, proximal moves; co-contraction protects ACL graft. OKC = distal free (leg extension) — delayed in ACL rehab due to anterior tibial shear. COMLEX: Post-surgical PT prescriptions should specify CKC vs OKC progression — COMLEX tests appropriate exercise selection in post-operative rehabilitation scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL injuries: CKC exercises are the foundation of post-operative ACL rehabilitation due to co-contraction-mediated joint stability"},{"id":"sdl23_q12","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe common therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old sedentary office worker presents with chronic non-specific low back pain without neurological deficits or red flags. Her physician wants to prescribe a therapeutic exercise category specifically shown to reduce low back pain symptoms. Which exercise classification is most directly targeted at her primary needs?","choices":{"A":"Closed kinetic chain exercises in weight-bearing positions such as squats and leg presses strengthen the paraspinal muscles that support the lumbar vertebral column and are most specifically indicated for non-specific low back pain in sedentary adults","B":"Open kinetic chain exercises activating free trunk movement in space are most specifically indicated because they engage the deep multifidus stabilizers more effectively than weight-bearing exercises that primarily recruit superficial global movers of the spine","C":"Eccentric exercises involving controlled lengthening contractions of the erector spinae and multifidus during forward bending are the most efficient mechanism for achieving lumbar stabilization in deconditioned sedentary patients with chronic low back pain","D":"Isometric paraspinal contractions without joint movement are the safest approach for deconditioned sedentary adults and produce the highest absolute strength gains in the lumbar extensors per session without risk of exacerbation","E":"Core stability exercises targeting the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles through sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunches, and Pilates are specifically indicated for the relief of low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Core stability exercises (sit-ups, back extension, abdominal crunch, Pilates) specifically target the low back, trunk, and abdominal muscles and are the exercise category indicated for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain per Table 2. CKC is for shoulder/knee rehab and dynamic stability, not specifically LBP. Eccentric is for tendinopathy/sport strengthening. Isometric is for when joint mobility is restricted. OKC is for ADL functional improvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Core stability = specific indication for low back pain and pregnancy-related pelvic pain (sit-up, back extension, Pilates). CKC = shoulder/knee rehab. Isometric = patellofemoral or restricted mobility. COMLEX: Core stability exercises are prescribed alongside OMM for low back pain — both address the deep stabilizing musculature that is often inhibited in chronic LBP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — Mechanical back pain: core stability exercises are the primary therapeutic exercise intervention for non-specific low back pain management"},{"id":"sdl23_q13","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist plans to treat a patient's calcific shoulder tendinopathy with iontophoresis using acetic acid. A medical student asks how iontophoresis differs from phonophoresis as a drug delivery mechanism. Which comparison is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Phonophoresis uses a direct electric current to deliver charged anti-inflammatory molecules into the joint, while iontophoresis uses high-frequency sound waves to heat the medication and increase its thermal diffusion rate into periarticular tissues","B":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis use identical delivery mechanisms based on sonic vibration, differing only in the frequency range: phonophoresis operates at audible frequencies while iontophoresis operates at ultrasonic frequencies above 20 kHz","C":"Phonophoresis uses ultrasound waves to push topical medications through the skin into deeper tissue, while iontophoresis uses direct current to drive ionically charged substances, with cathodes repelling negative drugs and anodes repelling positive drugs","D":"Phonophoresis delivers medications exclusively into the superficial epidermis, while iontophoresis penetrates all the way to bone and joint capsule because the electrical gradient overcomes resistance of all tissue layers simultaneously and without limitation","E":"Phonophoresis and iontophoresis are equally effective for any musculoskeletal condition, and the clinical distinction is only whether the medication is formulated as a topical gel or as an aqueous ionic solution compatible with the delivery device"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Phonophoresis = ultrasound used to drive topical corticosteroid/NSAID through skin. Iontophoresis = direct electric current used to drive ionically charged substances through skin to deeper tissue. Cathode delivers negatively charged substances; anode delivers positively charged substances. Iontophoresis uses: calcific tendinopathy, inflammatory conditions, hyperhidrosis. CI for iontophoresis: allergy to applied substance, open wounds, decreased sensation, near metallic implants/wires/staples.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phonophoresis = ultrasound + corticosteroid/NSAID (10%). Iontophoresis = current + ionic drug (dexamethasone 0.4% most common; acetic acid for calcific deposits). Cathode = negatively charged; anode = positively charged. COMLEX: Both modalities deliver anti-inflammatory agents; iontophoresis is specifically indicated for calcific tendinopathy and hyperhidrosis in addition to general inflammatory conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — De Quervain's tenosynovitis: phonophoresis and iontophoresis are commonly applied PT modalities for tenosynovitis and inflammatory tendon conditions"},{"id":"sdl23_q14","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with chronic low back pain reports significant pain relief from transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) during his PT sessions. His physician asks the therapist to explain the mechanism of TENS analgesia. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"TENS reduces pain by generating microcurrents that directly reverse depolarization of peripheral nociceptor membranes, restoring resting membrane potential and preventing action potential generation in C-fibers and A-delta fibers","B":"TENS reduces pain by delivering an electrical field that disrupts substance P binding to NK-1 receptors in the dorsal horn, preventing central sensitization without engaging the descending inhibitory system or any endogenous analgesic pathway","C":"TENS reduces pain by activating sympathetic efferents that produce local vasoconstriction, reducing edema and lowering the concentration of algogenic chemicals such as bradykinin and prostaglandins in the interstitial fluid around nociceptors","D":"TENS reduces pain by activating the gate control mechanism, in which large-diameter sensory fiber stimulation inhibits pain transmission from small C and A-delta fibers at the dorsal horn, and by releasing endogenous opioids and cortisol","E":"TENS reduces pain by generating a therapeutic electrical field that directly inactivates COX-2 in injured tissue, reducing local prostaglandin E2 concentration through the same pathway as NSAIDs but without systemic adverse effects"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TENS analgesia mechanisms: 1) Gate control theory — large A-beta fiber stimulation inhibits C-fiber and A-delta nociceptive transmission at the dorsal horn interneurons; 2) Stimulates endogenous opioid and cortisol release. Electrical stimulation also reduces inflammation and edema and facilitates chronic wound healing. This is distinct from phonophoresis (drug delivery) or ultrasound (tissue heating/healing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TENS mechanism = gate control (large fiber inhibits small fiber at dorsal horn) + endogenous opioid/cortisol release. TENS is used for chronic LBP, neuropathic pain, and TENS is applied in the chronic phase of injury. COMLEX: TENS is used alongside OMM for chronic musculoskeletal pain; understanding the gate control mechanism is testable for both Step 1 and COMLEX-1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: electrical stimulation including TENS is a standard PT modality for managing chronic lumbar pain and neurogenic claudication"},{"id":"sdl23_q15","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe commonly used physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low level laser therapy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with bilateral hand numbness and weakness, consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome, asks her physician whether low-level laser therapy is an option for her condition. Which statement most accurately characterizes the evidence base and regulatory status of LLLT?","choices":{"A":"LLLT is FDA approved for all musculoskeletal conditions including tendinopathy, osteoarthritis, and rheumatoid arthritis based on consistent level-1 randomized controlled trial evidence demonstrating clinically significant improvements in pain and joint function","B":"LLLT is FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor musculoskeletal pain; Cochrane reviews show limited but inconsistent benefit for osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, and LLLT has not been shown to cause adverse effects in standard use","C":"LLLT has no FDA approval for any musculoskeletal condition because randomized controlled trials have consistently demonstrated no benefit beyond sham therapy, and its use in clinical practice is considered experimental and generally discouraged","D":"LLLT is FDA approved specifically for rheumatoid arthritis because its mechanism of decreasing prostaglandin E2 production mimics the anti-inflammatory effect of NSAIDs without the associated gastrointestinal and cardiovascular adverse effects","E":"LLLT is contraindicated in all patients with musculoskeletal pain because photon radiation causes cumulative DNA damage in connective tissue cells, accelerating cartilage degeneration and ultimately worsening the conditions it is intended to treat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"LLLT mechanism: absorption of photon radiation → alters cellular oxidative metabolism (adenosine triphosphate production via cytochrome oxidase) → decreases prostaglandin E2 concentration in peritendinous fluid. FDA approved for carpal tunnel syndrome and minor MSK pain. Evidence for OA and RA is limited and conflicting per Cochrane reviews. Safety goggles are required; caution in patients with malignant tumors, anticoagulants, corticosteroids, immunosuppressives, and pregnant patients (not on uterus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LLLT FDA approved for CTS and minor MSK pain. Mechanism: cytochrome oxidase activation → increased ATP, decreased PGE2. Evidence for OA/RA is limited and inconsistent. COMLEX: LLLT is a low-risk modality used in chronic MSK pain management — cautions include malignant tumors and anticoagulant therapy; safety goggles protect therapists and patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: low-level laser therapy is FDA approved for CTS management and is one of the PT modalities applied for this condition"},{"id":"sdl23_q16","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician writes a physical therapy referral for a 52-year-old woman: \"PT 3 times per week for 4 weeks; strengthening and stabilization exercises; no lifting over 10 pounds; goals: improve function and reduce pain. Signed and dated.\" The clinic's billing office returns it before the first visit. Which required component is missing, and why does it matter?","choices":{"A":"The therapist's own treatment plan, because a prescription is invalid until the therapist cosigns the physician's orders","B":"A list of every modality to be used, because the therapist may not choose interventions unless each one is named","C":"The diagnosis to be treated, with proper coding, which is needed for accurate insurance billing and reimbursement","D":"The patient's imaging results, because therapy cannot begin until radiographs or MRI are attached to the referral","E":"A follow-up date with the physician, because the prescription expires unless the next office visit is scheduled"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AAFP prescription article assigned for this SDL lists the components of a PT prescription: the diagnosis to be treated with proper coding for accurate insurance billing and reimbursement; frequency and duration; specific protocols or treatments; safety precautions; and the physician's signature and date. This referral includes frequency and duration, specific exercises, a safety restriction, goals, and a signature and date, but no diagnosis, so the billing office cannot code it. A therapist cosignature is not a listed component. Naming every modality is not required; forms often include an evaluate-and-treat option that lets the therapist use discretion. Imaging results and a scheduled follow-up date are not listed components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription = diagnosis (coded), frequency and duration, specific protocols, safety precautions, signature and date; \"evaluate and treat\" lets the therapist choose interventions. COMLEX: Acute strains often get daily therapy for a short period; chronic problems get less frequent therapy over longer periods.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — physical therapy modalities chosen by phase of injury"},{"id":"sdl23_q17","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A family physician writes the following PT referral: \"Dx: right shoulder rotator cuff tendinopathy. Tx: therapeutic exercises and ultrasound. Signed, Dr. Smith, 4/28/26.\" The physical therapist calls requesting a critical addition before scheduling can begin. What required element is most likely missing from this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Frequency and duration of therapy are missing; without specifying how many sessions per week and the total treatment length the therapist cannot create a schedule, and the clinic cannot obtain payer authorization for a defined episode of care","B":"The patient's insurance co-payment information and referral number are missing; without prior verification of coverage details the physical therapy clinic cannot register the patient or confirm that the payer will reimburse for the prescribed services","C":"An MRI report confirming rotator cuff tendinopathy is missing; physical therapists are legally required to verify structural pathology via imaging before initiating therapeutic exercise for suspected rotator cuff conditions in any patient over 40","D":"Specific exercise names and repetition counts for each planned therapeutic intervention are missing; physical therapists are required to obtain physician approval for exact exercises rather than using clinical judgment to select appropriate interventions","E":"The patient's numeric pain rating at rest and with overhead activity is missing; federal billing guidelines require an objective pain score to be documented on every PT prescription before payers will authorize outpatient musculoskeletal rehabilitation services"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This prescription has: diagnosis (rotator cuff tendinopathy), specific treatments (exercises, ultrasound), and physician signature/date. The missing required component is frequency and duration of therapy (e.g., \"3x/week for 6 weeks\"). Without this, the therapist cannot schedule sessions appropriately or submit for insurance authorization. The other prescription elements present are adequate; insurance details, imaging, exact exercise names, and pain scores are not required prescription components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription must include frequency and duration — this is distinct from just specifying the type of treatment. A complete prescription enables scheduling, insurance authorization, and a defined treatment episode. COMLEX: A common COMLEX stem presents an incomplete PT order and asks what is missing — frequency/duration and safety precautions are the most commonly omitted elements.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Shoulder rotator cuff injuries: PT prescription with complete frequency, duration, and safety precautions is the standard management step following rotator cuff tendinopathy diagnosis"},{"id":"sdl23_q18","sdlNumber":23,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the components of a physical therapy prescription to include diagnosis, goal, frequency and duration, specific protocols, exercises or treatments, safety requirements or limitations, and a signature and date.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A family physician refers a 47-year-old man with chronic low back pain to physical therapy on the clinic's standard referral form. Instead of listing individual modalities, he checks the box marked \"evaluate and treat.\" Which statement best describes this option and the follow-up the physician should expect?","choices":{"A":"It hands prescribing to the therapist, so the referral no longer needs a diagnosis or the physician's signature and date before therapy begins","B":"It authorizes passive modalities such as heat and ultrasound, while any exercise program still has to be listed item by item on the form","C":"It covers one evaluation visit, after which the physician must write a second prescription that states the frequency and duration of therapy","D":"It lets the therapist choose the interventions, and the therapist reports progress to the physician, typically every 30 days or before follow-up","E":"It lets the therapist set the treatment plan, and progress is reported to the physician once, at discharge, unless the pain worsens during therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rand et al. (Am Fam Physician 2007, the SDL 23 course reading) note that physical therapy clinics often provide standard forms, which usually include an \"evaluate and treat\" option that allows the therapist to use discretion in applying therapeutic interventions. The therapist documents the patient's progress so that the physician can modify the care plan if needed, and this documentation is typically given to the physician every 30 days or before the patient's follow-up visit. Checking the option does not remove the other components in Table 3 of the reading: the diagnosis (with proper coding) is still needed, and a physician signature and date are required for a therapist to perform the requested services. The reading does not limit the option to passive modalities or to a single evaluation visit, and progress reporting is ongoing rather than deferred to discharge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PT prescription (Rand 2007, Table 3) = diagnosis with coding, frequency and duration, specific protocols or treatments, safety precautions, physician signature and date; \"evaluate and treat\" lets the therapist choose the interventions. COMLEX: Expect therapist progress documentation about every 30 days or before follow-up, and use it to adjust the care plan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — components of a PT prescription (Table 3)"}]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"Joint Dislocations","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is primarily disrupted in a dorsal proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint dislocation, producing posterior displacement of the middle phalanx on the proximal phalanx?","choices":{"A":"Collateral ligament rupture alone without disruption of the palmar restraint structures, producing lateral instability rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx","B":"Central extensor slip avulsion from the base of the middle phalanx, producing volar rather than dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx at the PIP joint","C":"Volar plate rupture from its distal attachment, allowing dorsal translation of the middle phalanx through the resulting gap in the palmar restraint system","D":"Flexor digitorum profundus tendon rupture from its distal phalanx insertion, producing DIP extension deformity rather than PIP joint displacement","E":"Dorsal capsule rupture and posterior ligamentous disruption, which would produce anterior rather than posterior displacement of the middle phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dorsal PIP dislocation is the most common finger dislocation. The mechanism is axial load combined with hyperextension, which ruptures the volar plate from its distal attachment to the middle phalanx, allowing dorsal translation. Collateral ligament rupture alone causes lateral instability. Central slip injury causes volar PIP dislocation (rare) and boutonnière risk. FDP rupture creates a jersey finger deformity at the DIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar plate rupture = dorsal PIP dislocation (hyperextension); central slip injury = volar PIP dislocation → boutonnière if untreated. COMLEX: Post-reduction splint at 30° flexion for 3 weeks; somatic dysfunction of the hand may accompany high-energy digit injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q02","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After closed reduction of a dorsal PIP joint dislocation with a confirmed volar plate avulsion on post-reduction X-ray, what is the appropriate immobilization position and duration?","choices":{"A":"Splint the PIP joint in full extension for 6 weeks, which places maximum tension on the healing volar plate and is appropriate only for central slip injuries requiring boutonnière prevention","B":"Splint the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, which maintains reduction while allowing the volar plate to heal in a shortened position and prevents swan neck deformity","C":"Buddy tape only to the adjacent finger with immediate active range of motion exercises, which does not adequately immobilize the ruptured volar plate during the critical healing period","D":"Immobilize in 90 degrees of PIP flexion for 4 weeks, which over-shortens the volar plate and produces a flexion contracture of the PIP joint as the primary complication","E":"Apply a dynamic extension splint immediately to restore full range of motion within 48 hours, bypassing the initial immobilization phase required for volar plate healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Post-reduction management of dorsal PIP dislocation requires splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks to allow volar plate healing. Full extension splinting is reserved for central slip injuries (boutonnière prevention). Immediate motion without immobilization risks chronic volar plate laxity. 90° flexion causes a flexion contracture. The goal is healing without deformity — inadequate treatment leads to swan neck deformity from chronic volar plate laxity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal PIP → volar plate → splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; volar PIP → central slip → splint in full extension. COMLEX: Untreated volar plate laxity → swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion); somatic dysfunction of MCP/PIP may accompany ligamentous injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q03","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with a previously untreated dorsal PIP dislocation presents 6 weeks later with PIP joint hyperextension and DIP joint flexion. Which deformity has developed from the untreated volar plate tear?","choices":{"A":"Swan neck deformity — chronic volar plate laxity at the PIP allows hyperextension, which increases tension on the oblique retinacular ligament and produces reciprocal DIP flexion","B":"Boutonnière deformity — central slip rupture at the PIP joint allows lateral bands to migrate volarly, producing PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension as the characteristic pattern","C":"Mallet finger deformity — terminal extensor tendon rupture at the DIP joint produces DIP flexion and cannot be extended actively, with no associated PIP deformity","D":"Trigger finger deformity — stenosing tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley produces a catching or locking finger in flexion that snaps into extension with passive manipulation","E":"Dupuytren contracture — palmar fascia fibrosis producing progressive flexion deformity at the MCP and PIP joints bilaterally, unrelated to acute ligamentous injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion) develops from chronic volar plate laxity after untreated dorsal PIP dislocation. Boutonnière is the opposite pattern (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension) from central slip rupture. Mallet finger is isolated DIP flexion from terminal extensor tendon avulsion. Trigger finger is A1 pulley stenosis. Dupuytren is palmar fascia fibrosis — not post-traumatic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swan neck = PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion (volar plate laxity or FDS rupture); boutonnière = PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (central slip rupture). COMLEX: Both deformities benefit from orthotic management and hand rehabilitation; OMM of the wrist/finger may address secondary somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Joint stabilizers: volar plate and collateral ligament integrity at interphalangeal joints underlies dislocation pattern and deformity risk"},{"id":"sdl25_q04","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Elbow dislocations are the second most common major joint dislocation in adults. Which direction of dislocation is most common, and how is the elbow typically positioned by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Anterior dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in full extension with the arm appearing elongated and the antecubital fossa showing a palpable humeral mass anteriorly","B":"Medial dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow at 90 degrees with the forearm deviated medially and the radial head palpable laterally through the skin","C":"Posterior or posterolateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in partial flexion with the forearm appearing shortened and the olecranon prominent posteriorly","D":"Lateral dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in supination with the forearm deviated laterally and the medial epicondyle palpable on the medial arm","E":"Rotatory dislocation is most common; the patient holds the elbow in a neutral rotation with clicking and locking that prevents full flexion or extension of the forearm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior and posterolateral dislocations account for the majority of elbow dislocations. The mechanism is a FOOSH (fall on outstretched hand) with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress. The olecranon is prominent posteriorly and the elbow is held in partial flexion. Anterior dislocations are rare and result from a direct posterior blow. Medial and lateral dislocations are uncommon isolated injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Elbow dislocation = second most common major joint dislocation (shoulder is first); posterior/posterolateral most common; terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture. COMLEX: Neurovascular check before and after reduction; somatic dysfunction of the elbow and forearm common after dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q05","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The \"terrible triad\" of the elbow describes a combination of injuries that produces severe instability. Which combination of findings defines this injury pattern?","choices":{"A":"Elbow dislocation combined with olecranon fracture and avulsion of the medial epicondyle, creating a pattern that is best managed with immediate cast immobilization in 90 degrees of flexion","B":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture, creating a triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation to restore elbow stability","C":"Elbow dislocation combined with distal humerus fracture and median nerve laceration, representing the classic high-energy pattern seen in pediatric supracondylar fractures with dislocation","D":"Elbow dislocation combined with radial shaft fracture and complete ulnar nerve transection, creating the typical low-energy injury seen in elderly patients with osteoporosis","E":"Elbow dislocation combined with lateral epicondyle avulsion and biceps tendon rupture, producing an anterolateral rotatory instability pattern requiring ligament reconstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The terrible triad consists of elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid process fracture. The coronoid provides the anterior buttress against posterior ulnar translation, and the radial head provides lateral column support. Loss of both creates triplanar instability that typically requires surgical fixation of both the coronoid and radial head, plus ligamentous repair. This combination is notoriously unstable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid is the key stabilizer against posterior translation — even small fragments matter. COMLEX: Ulnar nerve monitoring essential post-reduction; elbow somatic dysfunction and restricted ROM are common sequelae requiring OMM and PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q06","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism most commonly produces a posterior elbow dislocation in an adult?","choices":{"A":"Direct medial blow to the flexed elbow with the forearm in pronation, which applies a varus moment that drives the radial head medially and the olecranon anteriorly under the trochlea","B":"Forced hyperflexion of the elbow against an axial compressive load, which fails the posterior capsule and drives the humeral trochlea anteriorly through the coronoid fossa into the brachialis","C":"Repetitive valgus stress from overhead throwing mechanics, which gradually attenuates the ulnar collateral ligament and eventually allows posterior subluxation of the elbow with throwing","D":"Posterior blow to the olecranon with the elbow in 90 degrees of flexion, which is the typical mechanism of anterior elbow dislocation rather than the posterior pattern","E":"Fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension and a valgus stress, which drives the olecranon posteriorly over the distal humerus as the anterior capsule fails under traction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior elbow dislocation results from a FOOSH with the elbow in extension plus valgus stress. This combination axially loads the elbow while forcing the olecranon posteriorly, failing the anterior capsule and lateral collateral ligament complex. Hyperflexion with axial load would more likely fracture the olecranon. Repetitive valgus stress causes UCL attenuation (thrower's elbow) without dislocation. A direct posterior blow to the olecranon produces anterior dislocation (rare).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FOOSH + extension + valgus → posterior elbow dislocation; reduction by longitudinal traction on forearm with supination, then flexion under conscious sedation. COMLEX: Post-reduction early mobilization to prevent elbow stiffness (most common complication); assess for heterotopic ossification in high-energy injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle course makes this nerve vulnerable in elbow dislocations and the terrible triad"},{"id":"sdl25_q07","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old athlete sustains recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocations. MRI shows an impaction fracture on the posterolateral humeral head. What is this lesion, and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head created by the sharp anteroinferior glenoid rim as the humeral head dislocates anteriorly over it during dislocation","B":"Bankart lesion — an anteroinferior capsular tear with avulsion of the glenoid labrum from the anteroinferior glenoid rim, representing the soft-tissue restraint failure rather than a bony humeral lesion","C":"Reverse Hill-Sachs lesion — a compression fracture of the anterior humeral head created in posterior dislocations when the posterior glenoid rim impacts the humeral head from behind","D":"Greater tuberosity avulsion — traction failure of the supraspinatus and infraspinatus insertions from the greater tuberosity during reduction maneuvers in elderly patients over 40 years of age","E":"SLAP lesion — a tear of the superior labrum extending from anterior to posterior at the biceps anchor, resulting from repetitive overhead traction rather than acute traumatic dislocation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Hill-Sachs lesion is a posterolateral humeral head impaction fracture caused by the anterior glenoid rim during anterior dislocation. The Bankart lesion is the glenoid labrum tear — the soft-tissue equivalent. Reverse Hill-Sachs (McLaughlin lesion) occurs in posterior dislocations — anterior humeral head impaction on the posterior glenoid. Greater tuberosity avulsion occurs in patients >40 years from rotator cuff traction. SLAP lesions are overhead athlete injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hill-Sachs = posterolateral humeral head (anterior dislocation); reverse Hill-Sachs = anterior humeral head (posterior dislocation); both Hill-Sachs and Bankart predispose to recurrent dislocation when large. COMLEX: Apprehension test detects anterior instability; relocation test (posterior pressure) relieves symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q08","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in anterior glenohumeral dislocation, and what is the classic sensory finding used to assess its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand with wrist drop confirms injury; the radial nerve is the most vulnerable structure during reduction maneuvers at the elbow","B":"Musculocutaneous nerve — sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous) with loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination confirms injury after dislocation","C":"Median nerve — sensory loss over the thenar eminence and radial 3.5 digits with hand of benediction deformity on attempted fist closure confirms median nerve injury at the shoulder","D":"Axillary nerve — sensory loss over the proximal lateral aspect of the arm (regimental badge area) confirms injury; the deltoid extension lag sign assesses motor function","E":"Suprascapular nerve — motor loss of supraspinatus and infraspinatus with empty can test weakness and external rotation deficit confirms suprascapular nerve injury after dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve courses around the surgical neck of the humerus and through the quadrilateral space, making it the nerve most vulnerable in anterior glenohumeral dislocation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) is the classic finding. The deltoid extension lag test assesses motor function — the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position if the axillary nerve is injured. Radial nerve injury occurs with midshaft humerus fractures, not shoulder dislocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = most common nerve injured in anterior shoulder dislocation; sensory = lateral deltoid (regimental badge); motor = deltoid (abduction) and teres minor (external rotation). COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve BEFORE and AFTER reduction; dead arm syndrome is a transient variant from brief axillary nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q09","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient sustains a posterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. In what position should the shoulder be immobilized, and why?","choices":{"A":"Internal rotation in a standard sling for 2 to 6 weeks, which is appropriate for anterior dislocations where external rotation is the apprehension position but would recreate the vulnerable position for posterior dislocations","B":"Full abduction at 90 degrees in a shoulder spica cast for 4 weeks, which is an outdated approach previously used for all shoulder dislocations but not specific to directional instability patterns","C":"External rotation with a small amount of abduction for 2 to 6 weeks, because this keeps the humeral head seated against the posterior glenoid and the arm out of the internally rotated position that caused the dislocation","D":"Neutral rotation with the elbow at 90 degrees in a sling for 3 weeks, which provides no directional preference and fails to tighten the posterior capsule against re-dislocation in the posterior direction","E":"Flexion at 90 degrees with internal rotation in a body harness for 6 weeks, which is the position used for acromioclavicular separations and is inappropriate for posterior glenohumeral instability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Posterior dislocation is immobilized in external rotation (+ slight abduction) for 2-6 weeks. This is opposite to anterior dislocation management (sling in internal rotation). External rotation keeps the humeral head seated against the posterior glenoid while the torn posterior capsule heals, and keeps the arm out of the vulnerable position of internal rotation and forward flexion (it is internal rotation that tensions the posterior capsule). Immobilizing in internal rotation recreates the injury position. Posterior dislocation is classically associated with seizures, lightning strike, and electrocution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation → arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP x-ray may look normal; axillary lateral view confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head) is the bony lesion; suspect posterior dislocation in any patient after seizure with shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff integrity: disrupted in anterior shoulder dislocation in patients over 40, contributing to glenohumeral instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q10","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Traumatic femoroacetabular dislocations most commonly occur in which direction, and in what characteristic position is the limb held?","choices":{"A":"Posterior dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases; the hip is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation with apparent limb shortening","B":"Anterior dislocation is most common at 65% of cases; the hip is held in extension, abduction, and external rotation with the femoral head palpable in the inguinal region or over the obturator foramen","C":"Central dislocation is the most common pattern; the femoral head is driven medially through the acetabular floor by axial loading, with no characteristic limb position as the femur remains in neutral","D":"Superior dislocation occurs most commonly in high-energy trauma; the limb is held in abduction with the greater trochanter riding superiorly above the acetabular rim on plain radiograph","E":"Inferior dislocation is most common in lower extremity compression injuries; the limb is held in abduction with the femoral head palpable in the perineum and the sciatic nerve stretched superiorly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation accounts for approximately 85% of cases. The classic mechanism is a posteriorly directed force on the knee with the hip flexed — the \"dashboard injury\" in MVCs. The limb is held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Anterior dislocation (15%) produces the opposite — extension, abduction, external rotation — with the femoral head near the obturator foramen or femoral triangle. Central dislocation involves acetabular fracture with medial femoral head displacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = flexion/adduction/internal rotation; MVC dashboard mechanism; reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, to minimize AVN risk. COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; sciatic nerve injury (10-20%); somatic dysfunction of the pelvis and sacrum common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q11","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which factor most significantly increases the risk of avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head following posterior hip dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Anterior direction of hip dislocation increases AVN risk more than posterior direction, because the medial circumflex femoral artery is more directly compressed during anterior displacement of the femoral head","B":"Patient age under 30 years is the strongest predictor of AVN because the femoral head blood supply is more dependent on the ligamentum teres in skeletally immature patients with open physes","C":"Delay in reduction — hips reduced more than 12 hours after injury have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN compared with those reduced within 12 hours, from prolonged ischemia of the retinacular vessels","D":"Use of closed rather than open reduction increases AVN risk significantly, because open reduction with direct visualization prevents additional vascular traction injury during the reduction maneuver","E":"Absence of an associated acetabular fracture paradoxically increases AVN risk because pure ligamentous dislocations require greater force to dislocate and cause more vascular stretch injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Time to reduction is the most critical modifiable factor for AVN prevention after hip dislocation. Hips reduced more than 12 hours after injury have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN compared with those reduced within 12 hours (course text), reflecting prolonged ischemia of the retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) supplying the femoral head. Posterior dislocation has higher AVN risk than anterior. Closed reduction is preferred and does not increase AVN risk. Age and fracture pattern affect prognosis but are not the primary AVN determinant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery injury; reduce as soon as possible (odds of AVN 5.6× higher after 12 hours); AVN may not manifest clinically for 2 years post-injury — follow with serial MRI. COMLEX: Hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; post-reduction PT and protected weight-bearing protocols are essential rehabilitation components.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — Hip dislocation: femoroacetabular instability, AVN risk, and sciatic nerve proximity established in hip trauma framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q12","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is injured when her extended, externally rotated hip is forced further outward in a high-speed fall while waterskiing. Which presentation is characteristic of the less common anterior type of hip dislocation?","choices":{"A":"The hip held flexed, adducted, and internally rotated with the limb shortened and the femoral head palpable in the buttock","B":"The hip held flexed and externally rotated with the leg lengthened and the greater trochanter displaced posteriorly","C":"The leg shortened and externally rotated with groin pain and an intact capsule, the pattern of a femoral neck fracture","D":"A normal resting position with pain on internal rotation, since anterior dislocations reduce spontaneously","E":"The hip held extended and externally rotated, with the femoral head palpable anteriorly below the inguinal crease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter for this SDL states that anterior hip dislocations account for 10 to 15% of hip dislocations and occur when the hip is extended and externally rotated at impact. The hip is classically held extended and externally rotated, and the femoral head is palpable anteriorly below the inguinal flexion crease. The head may lie beneath the obturator externus (obturator dislocation) or under the iliopsoas against the superior pubic ramus (pubic dislocation). Flexion, adduction, internal rotation, and shortening describe the 85% posterior type. A flexed, externally rotated, lengthened limb is not the described pattern. Shortening with external rotation and groin pain after a fall describes a femoral neck fracture. Anterior dislocations do not reduce spontaneously; closed reduction, usually under general anesthesia, is required, followed by radiographs to confirm a concentric reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip dislocation: posterior (85%; flexion, adduction, internal rotation; sciatic nerve; dashboard injury) vs anterior (10–15%; extension, external rotation; head palpable anteriorly). COMLEX: Reduce promptly; AVN odds rise about 5.6-fold when reduction is delayed beyond 12 hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — hip fracture and dislocation presentation"},{"id":"sdl25_q13","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"After reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, which ankle-brachial index (ABI) threshold mandates CT angiography to evaluate for popliteal artery injury?","choices":{"A":"An ABI below 0.5 is the only threshold requiring CT angiography, because ABIs between 0.5 and 0.9 represent mild flow reduction that can be managed with serial pulse checks every 4 hours","B":"An ABI below 1.2 compared to the contralateral limb requires CT angiography, because any reduction from the expected normal value of 1.0 to 1.2 indicates arterial injury requiring investigation","C":"An ABI below 0.9 is considered abnormal and mandates CT angiography, as even hard signs of vascular injury may be absent despite significant popliteal artery damage requiring surgical repair","D":"An ABI below 0.7 requires CT angiography, while ABIs between 0.7 and 0.9 can be managed conservatively with Doppler monitoring and repeat examination at 24 and 48 hours post-reduction","E":"Any ABI below 1.0 in the absolute sense requires CT angiography regardless of the degree of reduction, even though ABIs of 0.9 to 1.0 are commonly seen in normal limbs without vascular injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After knee dislocation, vascular assessment is critical because popliteal artery injury occurs in a significant proportion of cases. An ABI below 0.9 is abnormal and mandates CT angiography. Vascular status should be monitored up to 72 hours post-reduction because intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis even with initially normal pulses. Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor outcomes and amputation risk requiring urgent fasciotomy plus vascular repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = popliteal artery injury (most important vascular complication); ABI <0.9 → CTA; monitor vascular status for 72 hours. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; fasciotomy + vascular repair for ischemia; multiligamentous injury requires surgical reconstruction in young active patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q14","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"How are traumatic tibiofemoral dislocations classified, and why is this classification clinically important?","choices":{"A":"Classified by the direction of femoral displacement relative to the tibia, which determines whether the knee extensors or flexors are primarily involved and whether weight-bearing can begin immediately","B":"Classified by the degree of ligamentous tear on MRI (grade I through III), which directly determines surgical versus conservative management independent of the direction of dislocation","C":"Classified by the mechanism of injury (contact versus non-contact), which predicts the pattern of associated neurovascular injury and determines timing of surgical ligamentous reconstruction","D":"Classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur (anterior, posterior, lateral, medial, or rotatory), which guides ligamentous injury pattern and management strategy","E":"Classified by the associated meniscal injury pattern on arthroscopic examination, which is the primary determinant of long-term functional outcome and return to sport timeline after knee dislocation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Knee dislocations are classified by the direction of tibial displacement relative to the femur. This is clinically important because the direction helps anticipate which ligament complexes and neurovascular structures are injured and guides reduction and management. Complete dislocation can occur only after extensive tearing of the supporting ligaments and soft tissues (both cruciates are usually torn, with collateral involvement in lateral, medial, and rotatory patterns). Rotatory dislocations may be irreducible by closed means due to soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = classified by tibial displacement direction; all knee dislocations injure multiple ligaments; spontaneous reduction can occur before ED arrival — maintain high suspicion in multiligamentous instability. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; popliteal artery and common peroneal nerve at greatest risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q15","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Following reduction of a traumatic knee dislocation, at what threshold of ischemia time is prognosis considered poor and amputation risk high?","choices":{"A":"Ischemia lasting more than 12 hours is the threshold for poor prognosis; the first 12 hours represent a safe window during which vascular repair consistently restores limb viability without fasciotomy","B":"Ischemia lasting more than 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis; irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates after this threshold, requiring urgent vascular repair with fasciotomy","C":"Ischemia lasting more than 8 hours is the definitive threshold; outcomes remain acceptable within 8 hours of injury regardless of the degree of vascular occlusion in the popliteal artery","D":"Any period of ischemia beyond 30 minutes universally results in amputation regardless of subsequent vascular repair, making fasciotomy and immediate OR the only acceptable management","E":"Ischemia lasting more than 24 hours is the clinical threshold used in current guidelines, as modern vascular and microsurgical techniques reliably restore perfusion within this window after knee dislocation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The 4-hour ischemia threshold is critical for knee dislocations. After 4 hours of ischemia, irreversible muscle and nerve damage accelerates sharply, dramatically worsening amputation risk. Management requires immediate surgical vascular repair plus fasciotomy to address compartment syndrome from reperfusion. The 4-hour threshold emphasizes the urgency of vascular evaluation after knee dislocation — even spontaneously reduced dislocations require emergent vascular assessment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Popliteal artery injury after knee dislocation + ischemia >4 hours = poor prognosis; vascular repair + fasciotomy are emergent; monitor vascular status for 72 hours post-reduction for delayed intimal tear thrombosis. COMLEX: Physical exam findings of progressive pallor, pulselessness, paresthesias, and pain with passive stretch signal compartment syndrome post-repair.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — Knee ligamentous injury: ACL/PCL/collateral disruption all occur in traumatic knee dislocations requiring extensive soft-tissue tearing"},{"id":"sdl25_q16","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellar dislocations almost always occur in which direction, and which anatomical feature explains this directional predilection?","choices":{"A":"Medial dislocation is most common because the lateral retinaculum is thicker and stronger than the medial retinaculum, making medial displacement the path of least resistance under forceful contraction","B":"Superior dislocation is most common from quadriceps tendon avulsion injuries, because the superior pole of the patella loses its restraining structure during forced extension against resistance","C":"Inferior dislocation is most common from patellar tendon rupture, because inferior pole displacement follows loss of the patellar tendon anchor during sudden deceleration or landing injuries","D":"Lateral dislocation is nearly universal because the Q-angle creates a net lateral force on the patella, and the medial patellofemoral ligament is the primary soft-tissue restraint resisting that force","E":"Posterior dislocation into the intercondylar notch is most common in young athletes, because the thin posterior patellar cartilage fails under compressive loading from direct impact during pivoting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral patellar dislocation is almost universal due to the lateral force vector of the quadriceps pull angle (Q-angle). The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary restraint against lateral displacement, and it tears in >50% of acute dislocations, most often at its femoral attachment. Predisposing factors include trochlear dysplasia (reduced bony constraint), patella alta, and excessive femoral anteversion. Spontaneous reduction occurs as the knee is extended, tightening the quadriceps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation = almost always lateral; MPFL is primary restraint; predisposing factors = trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, increased Q-angle. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; first-time dislocation → brace in extension 2-3 weeks + VMO strengthening; recurrent → MPFL reconstruction ± tibial tubercle transfer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q17","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which ligament is torn in more than 50% of acute patellar dislocations, and at which attachment site does failure most commonly occur?","choices":{"A":"The lateral retinaculum is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the patellar attachment and is the primary structure addressed surgically during lateral retinacular release procedures","B":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is torn in more than 50% of cases; failure most commonly occurs at the femoral attachment on the medial femoral condyle near the adductor tubercle","C":"The medial collateral ligament of the knee is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations; failure occurs at the tibial attachment and produces combined valgus and patellar instability","D":"The anterior cruciate ligament is torn in more than 50% of patellar dislocations because the same valgus mechanism that dislocates the patella also applies an anterior tibial shear force to the ACL","E":"The patellar tendon is disrupted in more than 50% of cases; failure occurs at the tibial tubercle insertion and produces superior patellar migration rather than lateral displacement at the trochlea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The MPFL is the primary restraint against lateral patellar displacement and is torn in >50% of acute lateral patellar dislocations. The femoral attachment near the adductor tubercle is the most common failure site. MPFL reconstruction targeting this anatomical footprint is the basis of surgical treatment for recurrent instability. Lateral retinaculum release alone is no longer recommended as an isolated procedure. MCL and ACL injuries are associated with different mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint of the patella; femoral attachment most commonly torn; MRI confirms injury location prior to reconstruction planning. COMLEX: Hemarthrosis after patellar dislocation suggests osteochondral injury; apprehension test (lateral patellar force) + medial patellar tenderness = classic exam findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q18","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old athlete sustains a first-time lateral patellar dislocation that reduces spontaneously. X-ray shows no fracture. What is the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Brace or cylinder cast with the knee in extension for 2 to 3 weeks, followed by quadriceps strengthening focusing on the VMO, with operative management reserved for chronic or recurrent instability","B":"Immediate surgical MPFL repair is indicated for all first-time patellar dislocations in athletes to prevent recurrence, as conservative management has unacceptably high re-dislocation rates in young patients","C":"Closed reduction with splinting in 90 degrees of knee flexion for 6 weeks, which relaxes the quadriceps and allows the MPFL to heal in a shortened position suitable for return to sport","D":"Arthroscopic washout alone without immobilization, because hemarthrosis drainage is the primary treatment goal and early range of motion prevents arthrofibrosis after patellar dislocation","E":"Open patellar realignment surgery with tibial tubercle osteotomy for all first-time dislocations in patients with patella alta, regardless of TT-TG distance or degree of trochlear dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"First-time lateral patellar dislocation without fracture is managed conservatively: brace/cylinder cast in extension for 2-3 weeks, then quadriceps and VMO strengthening, and gradual return to activity. Operative management (MPFL reconstruction, tibial tubercle transfer) is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when structural risk factors (elevated TT-TG distance, severe trochlear dysplasia) warrant early surgical correction. Immobilization in flexion is incorrect — extension maintains patellar alignment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-time patellar dislocation → conservative management (brace in extension, PT); recurrent → MPFL reconstruction; elevated TT-TG → consider tibial tubercle anteromedialization. COMLEX: VMO strengthening is the cornerstone of patellar rehabilitation; assess for somatic dysfunction at the knee and hip affecting quadriceps mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral mechanics: trochlear dysplasia and quadriceps vector abnormalities predispose to lateral patellar instability and dislocation"},{"id":"sdl25_q19","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The distal tibiofibular syndesmosis is made up of four ligaments that hold the fibula against the tibia above the ankle joint. According to the course reading, which of these is the thickest and strongest?","choices":{"A":"The anterior talofibular ligament, which runs from the fibula to the talar neck","B":"The deltoid ligament, which fans from the medial malleolus to the talus","C":"The calcaneofibular ligament, which crosses the subtalar joint laterally","D":"The anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, at the front of the ankle","E":"The posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, at the level of the plafond"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics ankle section states that the syndesmotic ligament is composed of four ligaments and that the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament is the thickest and strongest, connecting the tibia to the fibula at the level of the tibial plafond. The anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament is also syndesmotic but is not the strongest. The anterior talofibular and calcaneofibular ligaments are lateral collateral ligaments of the ankle, not syndesmotic ligaments. The deltoid ligament is the medial collateral complex. Syndesmotic injury follows external rotation and hyperdorsiflexion, a tibiofibular clear space over 6 mm is the most reliable radiographic sign, and anatomic reduction is the most important determinant of outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmosis = four ligaments, with the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament the strongest; injury from external rotation + dorsiflexion (high ankle sprain); tibiofibular clear space >6 mm. COMLEX: Squeeze and external rotation tests screen for syndesmotic injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — syndesmotic (high ankle) sprain mechanism and examination"},{"id":"sdl25_q20","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"On a post-reduction mortise view following ankle dislocation, which radiographic finding is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring fixation?","choices":{"A":"Medial clear space greater than 4 mm indicates isolated deltoid ligament rupture and is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic injury requiring operative repair of the medial ankle structures","B":"Tibiofibular clear space greater than 6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption and indicates instability requiring syndesmotic fixation","C":"Fibular shortening greater than 2 mm on the AP view is the primary indicator of syndesmotic injury because fibular length is always maintained when the syndesmotic ligaments are intact","D":"Loss of talar dome cartilage on MRI is the most reliable indicator of syndesmotic disruption, as osteochondral injury consistently accompanies complete syndesmotic ligament failure in ankle dislocations","E":"Tibiofibular overlap less than 10 mm on the AP view is the standard threshold for syndesmotic injury, while tibiofibular overlap measurement on the mortise view is unreliable due to projection variation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tibiofibular clear space >6 mm on the mortise view is the most reliable radiographic indicator of syndesmotic injury. Medial clear space widening indicates deltoid ligament disruption, which is a separate finding. Fibular shortening suggests a Maisonneuve or Weber C pattern. When the syndesmosis is disrupted, the mortise widens and the talus laterally shifts, causing the characteristic contact area reduction. Syndesmotic fixation (screws or flexible suture-button device) restores mortise anatomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndesmotic injury = tibiofibular clear space >6 mm (mortise view); medial clear space widening = deltoid rupture; assess both views after ankle dislocation reduction. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation never left unreduced — skin necrosis risk; subtalar dislocation (talocalcaneal + talonavicular) is a separate entity from tibiotalar dislocation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q21","sdlNumber":25,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the highest-priority management step immediately upon recognizing an ankle dislocation in the emergency setting, before advanced imaging is obtained?","choices":{"A":"Obtain a full MRI of the ankle before any reduction attempt to characterize all associated soft-tissue injuries and plan the definitive surgical approach for ligamentous reconstruction","B":"Apply a posterior splint in the dislocated position and admit for observation, because premature reduction attempts without orthopedic consultation risk conversion to an open dislocation","C":"Administer IV antibiotics prophylactically before any reduction attempt, because all ankle dislocations are considered contaminated injuries requiring antibiotic coverage prior to manipulation","D":"Prompt closed reduction must be performed immediately, because the ankle should never be left dislocated — skin tenting risks pressure necrosis, and neurovascular compromise worsens with delay","E":"Obtain CT angiography of the foot and ankle before reduction to document vascular anatomy, as reduction without vascular imaging risks embolism from disrupted ankle vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ankle dislocations must be reduced immediately — the ankle should never be left in a dislocated position. Skin tenting risks pressure necrosis and conversion to an open injury. Neurovascular compromise worsens with prolonged dislocation. Reduction is performed with adequate analgesia/sedation, followed by splinting, elevation, and post-reduction imaging. MRI is not a pre-reduction study. Antibiotics are indicated for open fractures/dislocations but not routinely before closed reduction. The reduction priority parallels hip and knee dislocation urgency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; post-reduction: splint, elevate, neurovascular check, AP/mortise/lateral x-rays; persistent instability → CRIF or ORIF. COMLEX: Subtalar dislocation (medial 85%, lateral 15%) is distinct — inversion produces medial subtalar dislocation; reduction under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: lateral ligamentous laxity and mortise stability principles bridge ankle sprain to talar dislocation pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl25_q22","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male basketball player catches a fast-pass awkwardly, jamming the ring finger. He presents with swelling, point tenderness at the PIP joint, and dorsal displacement of the middle phalanx. Post-reduction X-ray shows a small volar plate avulsion fragment at the base of the middle phalanx with concentric joint reduction. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Digital nerve block followed by splinting the PIP joint at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, protecting the volar plate during healing while allowing early DIP and MCP range of motion","B":"Closed reduction under general anesthesia in the operating room, because all avulsion fractures at the base of the middle phalanx require intraoperative fluoroscopy to confirm fragment position before immobilization","C":"Immediate open reduction and internal fixation of the volar plate avulsion fragment regardless of fragment size, because any bony avulsion at the PIP joint destabilizes the flexor tendon sheath mechanism","D":"Hyperextend the PIP joint and apply a dorsal blocking splint in 90 degrees of flexion for 6 weeks, which is the correct technique for dorsal DIP dislocations but not for PIP volar plate avulsions","E":"Buddy tape the ring finger to the middle finger and allow immediate full active range of motion, because concentric reduction confirmed on X-ray means the joint is inherently stable without immobilization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A concentric dorsal PIP dislocation with a small volar plate avulsion is managed with digital block, reduction, and splinting at 30° flexion for 3 weeks. This protects the healing volar plate while allowing adjacent joint motion. Surgical fixation is reserved for unstable fracture-dislocations with more than 40% of the middle phalanx base involved (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11) or irreducible dislocations. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute volar plate injuries. 90° flexion immobilization would cause flexion contracture. The avulsion fragment does not destabilize the flexor mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small volar plate avulsion + concentric PIP reduction = splint at 30° flexion 3 weeks; fragment >40% of the middle phalanx base (CDT Orthopedics) or irreducible = operative. COMLEX: Irreducible PIP dislocation = interposed volar plate or FDP tendon blocking reduction; assess for associated osteochondral injury with X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: volar plate composition and attachment determine IP joint dislocation pattern and healing capacity after avulsion injury"},{"id":"sdl25_q23","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old volleyball player jams her right ring finger blocking a spike. The PIP joint is dislocated sideways, and the finger points toward the ulnar side. After reduction under a digital block, the joint is tender along one side. According to the course reading, which ligament most likely ruptured, and why is the finger deviated ulnarly?","choices":{"A":"The ulnar collateral ligament, which is the more commonly torn PIP collateral, letting the finger drift toward the torn side","B":"The central slip of the extensor tendon, which lets the lateral bands slide ulnarly and pull the middle phalanx with them","C":"The flexor digitorum profundus tendon, which avulses from the distal phalanx and pulls the finger toward the ulnar side","D":"The radial collateral ligament, about six times more likely than the ulnar collateral ligament to rupture at the PIP joint","E":"The A1 pulley, which tears with the volar plate and lets the flexor tendons bowstring toward the ulnar side of the digit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Tintinalli hand chapter assigned with this SDL states that lateral PIP dislocations occur when one collateral ligament ruptures, with at least a partial avulsion of the volar plate from the middle phalanx, and that the digit is usually ulnarly deviated because the radial collateral ligament is six times more likely than the ulnar collateral ligament to rupture. The ulnar collateral is the less commonly torn ligament. Central slip rupture is associated with volar PIP dislocations and causes a boutonniere pattern, not a lateral dislocation. FDP avulsion is a jersey finger at the DIP. The A1 pulley sits at the MCP level and is not the restraint for lateral PIP stability. After reduction, active motion and strength are tested; normal findings allow splinting at 30 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, while an irreducible joint or complete ligament disruption needs operative repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PIP dislocations: dorsal (volar plate rupture, most common), lateral (collateral + partial volar plate, RCL > UCL), volar (central slip risk; splint PIP in extension). COMLEX: Test active motion after reduction; untreated volar plate injury → swan neck.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb MCP"},{"id":"sdl25_q24","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the presentation and management of interphalangeal joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old male construction worker sustains a volar PIP dislocation — the middle phalanx is displaced volarly on the proximal phalanx. This injury is rare and requires careful evaluation. Which additional structure injury must be specifically assessed, and what deformity results if it is missed?","choices":{"A":"Flexor digitorum profundus avulsion must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is caused by FDP rupture at the distal phalanx, and if missed leads to jersey finger deformity with DIP flexion deficit and a palpable mass in the palm","B":"Volar plate rupture must be assessed as the primary associated injury — in volar PIP dislocations the volar plate avulses volarly, and if untreated leads to swan neck deformity from the resulting PIP hyperextension","C":"Central extensor slip rupture must be assessed — volar PIP dislocation is associated with central slip injury, and if missed and left unsplinted in extension, progressive boutonnière deformity develops as the lateral bands migrate volarly","D":"Radial collateral ligament rupture must be assessed — radial collateral ligament is six times more likely to rupture than the ulnar collateral, and if missed leads to permanent lateral instability at the PIP joint","E":"Terminal extensor tendon avulsion must be assessed — volar displacement of the middle phalanx stretches the terminal tendon at the DIP, and if missed leads to mallet finger deformity with isolated DIP flexion deficit"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volar PIP dislocation (middle phalanx displaced volarly) is the rare counterpart to the common dorsal dislocation. It is associated with central slip rupture — the extensor mechanism's middle slip that inserts on the dorsal base of the middle phalanx. If the central slip is disrupted and not splinted in full extension (6 weeks for acute closed central slip injuries, SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), the lateral bands migrate volarly past the PIP axis, producing boutonnière deformity (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension). This distinction from dorsal PIP dislocation management is critical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Volar PIP dislocation → central slip rupture → boutonnière if untreated; splint PIP in full EXTENSION for 6 weeks (CDT Orthopedics) (opposite of dorsal PIP); allow active DIP flexion during immobilization to prevent DIP stiffness. COMLEX: Elson test (DIP extension with PIP held at 90° flexion) detects central slip integrity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue physiology: central slip extensor mechanism integrity at PIP joint determines whether volar dislocation produces boutonnière deformity on healing"},{"id":"sdl25_q25","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male falls from a ladder, landing on an outstretched right hand. He presents with elbow pain, deformity, and a shortened forearm with the olecranon prominent posteriorly. X-ray confirms a posterior elbow dislocation without fracture. After successful closed reduction under conscious sedation, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate post-reduction management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a long arm cast in full extension for 6 weeks to allow complete capsuloligamentous healing before initiating any range of motion, which is the standard protocol for all elbow dislocations without fracture","B":"Perform immediate ORIF of the collateral ligaments and anterior capsule under general anesthesia, because all elbow dislocations result in complete ligamentous disruption requiring operative repair","C":"Obtain MRI arthrogram before allowing any range of motion to document the completeness of capsuloligamentous healing and determine if any occult osteochondral injuries require arthroscopic treatment","D":"Apply a circular cast in 30 degrees of elbow flexion for 8 weeks, because immobilization in flexion prevents posterior subluxation but is maintained at less than 90 degrees to avoid neurovascular compromise","E":"Assess neurovascular status and range of motion after reduction, then splint in 45 to 90 degrees of flexion and begin motion within 5 to 10 days to prevent stiffness, the most common complication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After reduction of a simple posterior elbow dislocation (no fracture), the priority is neurovascular reassessment and range of motion testing to confirm stability. The elbow is splinted in 45 to 90 degrees of flexion, and motion is started as soon as possible, generally within 5 to 10 days (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3), because elbow stiffness is the most common complication of elbow dislocation, and prolonged immobilization dramatically worsens outcomes. Long arm casting in extension is contraindicated. ORIF is reserved for unstable reductions or terrible triad injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple elbow dislocation → closed reduction → splint in 45-90° → motion within 5-10 days (CDT Orthopedics); stiffness = most common complication. COMLEX: Assess brachial artery and median/ulnar/radial nerves pre- and post-reduction; heterotopic ossification is a delayed complication of high-energy elbow dislocations treated with prolonged immobilization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: posterior medial epicondyle anatomy makes the ulnar nerve vulnerable during elbow dislocation and a priority for post-reduction neurologic assessment"},{"id":"sdl25_q26","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female rugby player sustains a posterior elbow dislocation during a match. After closed reduction, she reports paresthesias along the medial aspect of the forearm and the medial one and a half digits, along with weakness of her grip and finger abduction. Which nerve is most likely injured, and what is the anatomic explanation?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve — it spirals around the radial groove of the humerus and is injured during dislocation, producing wrist drop and loss of dorsal forearm and hand sensation from the posterior interosseous nerve branch","B":"Ulnar nerve — it courses in a superficial groove posterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched or contused during dislocation, producing medial forearm and ring/small finger paresthesias with intrinsic hand weakness","C":"Median nerve — it runs anteromedially through the cubital fossa and is the most commonly injured nerve in elbow dislocation, producing thenar atrophy and radial 3.5 digit sensory loss as the primary deficit","D":"Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve — it provides sensory innervation to the medial forearm and is the most commonly injured structure during elbow reduction maneuvers rather than the dislocation itself","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve — it branches from the median nerve just below the elbow and produces an isolated motor deficit of the FPL and index FDP without any sensory loss or intrinsic hand weakness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ulnar nerve lies in the cubital tunnel posterior to the medial epicondyle and is the nerve most vulnerable during elbow dislocation due to this superficial medial position. Injury produces medial forearm paresthesias (dorsal and palmar medial forearm) and medial 1.5 digit numbness (ring and small finger), plus intrinsic hand weakness (interossei — finger abduction/adduction; hypothenar muscles). The radial nerve injury causes wrist drop. Median nerve injury produces thenar and lateral finger sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve = medial epicondyle groove; elbow dislocation → ulnar nerve stretch; medial 1.5 digits + medial forearm sensory loss + intrinsic weakness. COMLEX: Cubital tunnel syndrome (SDL 14 Obj 3) involves same nerve at same location; elbow dislocation acutely recreates this anatomy with traction rather than compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at elbow: post-medial epicondyle course creates identical vulnerability in both chronic cubital tunnel syndrome and acute elbow dislocation traction injury"},{"id":"sdl25_q27","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the presentation and management of elbow joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male sustains a terrible triad elbow injury — posterior dislocation with radial head fracture and coronoid process fracture confirmed on CT. During surgical planning, the surgeon emphasizes that a specific fracture component is the most critical destabilizer of the elbow joint, even when the fragment appears small on imaging. Which structure is this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral epicondyle fracture — lateral epicondyle avulsion disrupts the common extensor origin and lateral ulnar collateral ligament origin, which is the primary restraint against posterolateral rotatory instability of the elbow","B":"Capitellum fracture — an associated capitellum fracture indicates a high-energy pattern that disrupts the radiocapitellar articulation, which is the primary lateral column stabilizer against valgus and axial loading","C":"Olecranon fracture — olecranon comminution is the defining feature of terrible triad injury and always requires plate fixation first because the triceps pull directly destabilizes the ulnohumeral articulation","D":"Coronoid process fracture — the coronoid is the primary anterior buttress preventing posterior ulnar translation, and even a small coronoid fragment removal destabilizes the elbow against posterior dislocation forces","E":"Radial styloid fracture — the radial styloid is the key lateral stabilizer of the forearm and its fracture predisposes to proximal radioulnar joint instability rather than distal radius shortening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The coronoid process is the critical anterior buttress of the ulna that prevents posterior translation of the ulnohumeral joint. In the terrible triad, even a small (Regan-Morrey Type I or II) coronoid fragment, if excised or not repaired, allows recurrent posterior elbow instability. Surgical management prioritizes coronoid fixation or reconstruction first, then radial head fixation or arthroplasty, then lateral collateral ligament repair. The radial head provides lateral column support but the coronoid is the key to preventing re-dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Terrible triad = dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid fracture; coronoid = anterior buttress; surgical priority = coronoid → radial head → LCL repair. COMLEX: Even small coronoid fragments (Regan-Morrey Type I) have clinical significance in the setting of elbow instability; stiffness remains the most common post-operative complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve at elbow: the medial anatomy of the elbow including the coronoid and medial epicondyle shares surgical planes with the ulnar nerve requiring careful protection during terrible triad repair"},{"id":"sdl25_q28","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old male college football player sustains his first anterior glenohumeral dislocation and undergoes successful closed reduction. He is immobilized in a sling for 4 weeks and completes physical therapy. Over the next year he has two more dislocations with progressively less force. MRI confirms a Bankart lesion and a Hill-Sachs lesion. What is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Repeat conservative management with a longer immobilization period of 8 to 12 weeks in internal rotation to allow the Bankart lesion to heal without surgery, as recurrence after two dislocations does not yet meet operative criteria","B":"Surgical stabilization including Bankart repair with labral reattachment to the anteroinferior glenoid rim and anteroinferior capsular tightening, given failure of conservative treatment and high recurrence risk in young active patients","C":"Physical therapy exclusively focusing on posterior capsule and internal rotator strengthening for 6 additional months before considering surgery, because strengthening the dynamic stabilizers eliminates most recurrent dislocation risk","D":"Glenohumeral corticosteroid injection to reduce capsular inflammation and restore normal joint mechanics, which is the recommended intervention for recurrent instability in young athletes before surgical consultation","E":"Reverse total shoulder arthroplasty to replace the damaged glenohumeral joint surfaces, which is the definitive surgical treatment for young patients with recurrent anterior instability and associated Hill-Sachs lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young patients (<25 years) with recurrent anterior glenohumeral dislocation have a high risk of re-dislocation if treated conservatively (SDL 25 reading) and should be counseled about surgical stabilization after failed conservative management. Arthroscopic Bankart repair (labral reattachment + anteroinferior capsular tightening) is the standard surgical approach. Large Hill-Sachs lesions may require remplissage or Latarjet procedure. Corticosteroids and additional PT are not appropriate for recurrent instability. Reverse shoulder arthroplasty is for rotator cuff arthropathy in elderly patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Young patient + recurrent anterior dislocation → Bankart repair; Hill-Sachs + Bankart lesions = classic pair in recurrent anterior instability; glenoid bone loss >20% → Latarjet coracoid transfer. COMLEX: TUBS = Traumatic, Unidirectional, Bankart lesion, Surgical treatment; AMBRI = Atraumatic, Multidirectional, Bilateral, Rehabilitation, Inferior capsular shift.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: rotator cuff tear occurs in >40% of patients over 40 with anterior shoulder dislocation, requiring MRI evaluation concurrent with instability workup before definitive surgical planning"},{"id":"sdl25_q29","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with a history of alcohol-use disorder is brought to the emergency department after a witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure. He complains of right shoulder pain and cannot lift his arm. His arm is held with the hand on his abdomen and he is unable to externally rotate the shoulder. The AP radiograph appears nearly normal. What is the diagnosis and what imaging confirms it?","choices":{"A":"Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) — the insidious onset and limited external rotation in this older patient with a comorbid condition is most consistent with adhesive capsulitis, confirmed by MRI arthrogram showing capsular thickening","B":"Anterior glenohumeral dislocation — the mechanism of seizure causes anterior capsule failure, and the AP radiograph typically confirms anterior displacement clearly without need for additional imaging views","C":"Acromioclavicular joint separation — the seizure causes a direct downward force on the shoulder girdle, and the Zanca view best demonstrates the degree of coracoclavicular ligament disruption and clavicle displacement","D":"Rotator cuff tear with pseudoparalysis — seizure-related muscle contractions avulse the supraspinatus from the greater tuberosity, and ultrasound of the subacromial space confirms the full-thickness tear causing apparent weakness","E":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation — classic after seizures and electrocution; the AP view may look nearly normal, and an axillary lateral or Velpeau view confirms it and shows a reverse Hill-Sachs lesion anteriorly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior glenohumeral dislocation is classically missed because the AP radiograph may appear nearly normal or show only a \"light bulb sign\" (internal rotation of the humeral head). The arm is held in internal rotation (hand on belly) and external rotation is impossible — the classic locked shoulder of posterior dislocation. Seizures, lightning strikes, and electrocution are the classic atraumatic mechanisms (bilateral symmetric muscle contractions). The axillary lateral view confirms the diagnosis and shows the reverse Hill-Sachs (anterior humeral head compression). Reduction requires longitudinal traction + anterior-directed force, then immobilize in external rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior dislocation = seizure, lightning, electrocution; arm in internal rotation, cannot externally rotate; AP may look normal (lightbulb sign); axillary lateral confirms; immobilize in EXTERNAL rotation. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation is the \"great masquerader\"; always obtain axillary lateral view in any shoulder with locked internal rotation after seizure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: posterior dislocation can avulse the lesser tuberosity via subscapularis traction, just as anterior dislocation avulses the greater tuberosity via supraspinatus/infraspinatus traction in patients over 40"},{"id":"sdl25_q30","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the presentation and management of glenohumeral joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old female volleyball player sustains a first-time anterior shoulder dislocation reduced in the emergency department. After reduction, she develops numbness over her lateral deltoid and cannot maintain her arm in extension when the shoulder is passively extended and released. Which nerve is injured and what specific test confirms the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve injury — the deltoid extension lag test confirms motor deficit: the shoulder is passively extended and the patient cannot hold the position because the deltoid (primary extensor at this range) is denervated","B":"Suprascapular nerve injury — confirmed by the empty can test showing weakness of supraspinatus-mediated abduction and the internal rotation lag sign showing infraspinatus deficit in external rotation arc","C":"Radial nerve injury — confirmed by wrist drop and loss of active extension at the wrist, thumb, and fingers, with sensory loss over the dorsal forearm and hand rather than the lateral deltoid region","D":"Musculocutaneous nerve injury — confirmed by loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination with sensory loss over the lateral forearm (lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve distribution) rather than the shoulder","E":"Long thoracic nerve injury — confirmed by scapular winging on the wall push-up test due to serratus anterior denervation, which presents with lateral winging when the arm is forward flexed against resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Axillary nerve injury is confirmed by the deltoid extension lag sign: the shoulder is passively extended to end range and the patient cannot actively hold the position. This tests deltoid function (specifically the posterior deltoid for extension) while avoiding supraspinatus compensation. Sensory loss over the lateral deltoid (regimental badge area) confirms the diagnosis. The suprascapular nerve supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus (not deltoid). The radial nerve injury produces wrist drop. The long thoracic nerve serves serratus anterior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Axillary nerve = surgical neck humerus/shoulder dislocation; deltoid extension lag test = most specific motor test; sensory = lateral deltoid. COMLEX: Assess axillary nerve before AND after reduction; dead arm syndrome = transient axillary nerve compression resolving within minutes from brief subluxation during athletic activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries: axillary nerve injury post-dislocation must be distinguished from rotator cuff tear as a cause of deltoid weakness, as both present with limited shoulder function but require different rehabilitation approaches"},{"id":"sdl25_q31","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male unrestrained front-seat passenger is brought in after a head-on MVC. He has right hip pain with the limb held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. X-ray confirms a posterior hip dislocation without associated fracture. He is hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent open reduction in the operating room for all posterior hip dislocations regardless of associated fractures, because closed reduction risks sciatic nerve traction injury and inadequate acetabular visualization","B":"CT scan of the pelvis before any reduction attempt to rule out associated acetabular fractures, because unrecognized intra-articular fragments during closed reduction cause iatrogenic femoral head cartilage damage","C":"Apply femoral skeletal traction overnight and schedule reduction in the morning operating room to allow muscle relaxation, which reduces the force required for reduction and minimizes sciatic nerve traction risk","D":"Urgent closed reduction under conscious sedation as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, using the Allis method, as the odds of AVN rise about 5.6-fold when reduction is delayed beyond 12 hours","E":"Discharge with non-weight-bearing crutches and outpatient orthopedic referral within 3 to 5 days, because isolated posterior hip dislocation without fracture can be safely managed as an elective reduction procedure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation requires urgent closed reduction, preferably within the first few hours (SDL 25 reading); time to reduction is the most critical determinant of AVN risk, and hips reduced after 12 hours have about 5.6 times the odds of AVN (course text). The Allis method: patient supine, assistant stabilizes the pelvis, surgeon applies axial traction with hip and knee at 90° flexion, then gentle internal→external rotation to reduce. A post-reduction CT is obtained after reduction (not before, unless the patient is going directly to OR) to check for intra-articular fragments and associated fractures. Delaying for CT or OR scheduling dramatically increases AVN risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip dislocation → reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours (Allis or Stimson methods); post-reduction CT to assess for intra-articular fragments; AVN follow-up with MRI (AVN can appear as late as 2 years). COMLEX: Posterior hip dislocation = CP8 named condition; 10-20% sciatic nerve injury rate; assess foot drop pre- and post-reduction; pelvis/sacral somatic dysfunction common after high-energy hip trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: medial circumflex femoral artery disruption in posterior hip dislocation directly bridges the mechanism of AVN and explains the 6-hour reduction urgency window"},{"id":"sdl25_q32","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male undergoes successful closed reduction of a posterior hip dislocation sustained in an MVC. Post-reduction CT confirms concentric reduction with a small posterior acetabular wall fragment and no intra-articular loose bodies. Hip stability is confirmed through full range of motion. Over the next 2 years, which complication remains the greatest concern for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head — disruption of the posterior capsule and retinacular vessels (branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery) during dislocation can cause ischemia that manifests as AVN months to 2 years after injury","B":"Sciatic nerve palsy that is permanent and will not recover — sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation uniformly progress to complete motor and sensory loss that fails to recover regardless of reduction timing or decompression","C":"Heterotopic ossification at the posterior acetabulum requiring prophylaxis — indomethacin is required for all posterior hip dislocations because heterotopic ossification predictably limits hip flexion to less than 90 degrees within 6 months","D":"Post-traumatic hip osteoarthritis developing within 6 months — cartilage damage from the dislocation produces symptomatic OA within 6 months requiring total hip arthroplasty in all patients under 35 years of age","E":"Deep vein thrombosis from enforced bed rest — immobility after hip dislocation causes DVT in more than 80% of patients, making anticoagulation with therapeutic heparin the most urgent management priority post-reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AVN of the femoral head is the most feared long-term complication after hip dislocation. The medial circumflex femoral artery supplies the femoral head retinacular vessels, which are disrupted during posterior dislocation. AVN may not manifest clinically or radiographically for months to 2 years, requiring serial MRI surveillance. Sciatic nerve injuries from hip dislocation can recover partially or fully. Heterotopic ossification occurs but is not universal. Post-traumatic OA develops over years, not months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN after hip dislocation = medial circumflex femoral artery (retinacular branches); onset 1-2 years after injury; follow with MRI; core decompression or THA if symptomatic. COMLEX: Protected weight-bearing post-reduction; serial follow-up for 2 years; hip rehabilitation OMM can address surrounding soft-tissue dysfunction without loading the compromised femoral head.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: femoral head blood supply via medial circumflex femoral artery is the shared anatomic basis for AVN in both hip dislocation and femoral neck fracture contexts"},{"id":"sdl25_q33","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the presentation and management of femoroacetabular joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has a posterior hip dislocation closed-reduced under general anesthesia. The hip is stable through the range of motion, but the post-reduction AP pelvis shows the right hip joint space wider than the left. Which interpretation and next step best match the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Expected capsular swelling after reduction; begin full weight bearing without restriction and repeat films in 6 weeks","B":"Recurrent posterior dislocation; repeat the Allis maneuver at the bedside without imaging and then place an abduction brace","C":"Nonconcentric reduction from soft tissue or bone fragment interposition; obtain CT and plan arthroscopy or arthrotomy","D":"Early avascular necrosis of the femoral head; obtain an MRI now, since joint widening is the first sign of collapse","E":"A normal variant of hip anatomy; compare with old films and discharge if the patient can walk without pain today"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter states that after reduction an AP pelvis is obtained, and that soft tissue or bone fragment interposition shows as widening of the joint space compared with the opposite side. Indications for surgery include irreducible dislocations, nonconcentric reductions, open dislocations, ipsilateral femoral neck fractures, and an unstable hip, especially with a posterior wall fracture. CT can identify loose bodies or other fractures, and hip arthroscopy may be a safer alternative to arthrotomy for removing loose bodies. Asymmetric widening is not expected swelling, and unrestricted weight bearing would be inappropriate. A widened but located joint is not a redislocation. AVN can appear as late as 2 years after injury and is not shown by acute joint space widening. Asymmetry should not be dismissed as a normal variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-reduction hip checklist: concentric reduction on AP pelvis, CT for loose bodies and acetabular/femoral head fractures, sciatic nerve exam before and after reduction. COMLEX: A new nerve deficit after reduction warrants exploration for nerve entrapment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — avascular necrosis after hip injury"},{"id":"sdl25_q34","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old obese male (BMI 44) trips stepping off a curb and presents with a grossly unstable knee. X-ray confirms a tibiofemoral dislocation. He is hemodynamically stable and the dislocation is reduced. Post-reduction ABI on the affected limb is 0.71. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Close observation with serial pulse checks every 4 hours for 72 hours, because an ABI between 0.7 and 0.9 represents mild flow reduction from post-reduction edema and does not require immediate vascular imaging","B":"Immediate surgical exploration of the popliteal fossa without vascular imaging, because any ABI below 1.0 mandates emergent operative vascular repair to prevent limb-threatening ischemia after knee dislocation","C":"Apply a knee immobilizer and obtain MRI of the knee ligaments before vascular evaluation, because multiligamentous injury documentation is required for surgical planning before addressing vascular status","D":"Repeat the ABI measurement in 6 hours after the swelling resolves from initial reduction, because edema-related ABI reductions routinely normalize within 6 hours and do not require immediate CTA","E":"CT angiography of the lower extremity to evaluate for popliteal artery injury, because an ABI below 0.9 is the threshold mandating vascular imaging regardless of the presence or absence of distal pulses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An ABI below 0.9 after knee dislocation mandates CT angiography regardless of pulse presence. The popliteal artery is tethered at the knee and commonly injured in knee dislocations, sometimes with initially preserved distal pulses from collateral flow. Intimal injuries can cause delayed thrombosis. Vascular status must be reassessed for 72 hours post-reduction. Low-energy dislocations in obese patients are increasingly recognized, and the same vascular urgency applies. MRI evaluation waits until vascular status is confirmed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation + ABI <0.9 → CT angiography; popliteal artery injury can present with normal pulses initially (intimal tear); monitor vascular status 72 hours. COMLEX: Low-energy knee dislocation in morbidly obese patients = emerging clinical pattern; same ABI threshold and vascular urgency as high-energy dislocations applies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: multiligamentous knee instability after dislocation involves the same ligamentous structures as isolated ACL/PCL injuries but with greater severity and obligate vascular risk assessment requirement"},{"id":"sdl25_q35","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed crash with a traumatic knee dislocation that spontaneously reduced before EMS arrival. CTA is normal. Physical examination reveals gross anteroposterior and mediolateral instability consistent with multiligamentous disruption including the ACL, PCL, and posterolateral complex. He is a competitive cyclist. What is the appropriate surgical management recommendation?","choices":{"A":"Conservative management with hinged knee bracing and physical therapy is equivalent to surgery for all knee dislocations and is the preferred approach because surgical complication rates exceed re-dislocation risk in braced patients","B":"Surgical reconstruction of all injured ligamentous structures is recommended for young, active patients, because a complete knee dislocation tears multiple supporting ligaments and bracing alone cannot restore stability","C":"Only the ACL and PCL require reconstruction after complete knee dislocation, because the collateral ligaments predictably heal with conservative management and do not contribute meaningfully to long-term instability","D":"Surgical reconstruction should be deferred for at least 6 months to allow spontaneous ligamentous healing and scar formation, which often restores sufficient stability without requiring operative intervention in young patients","E":"Only elderly or sedentary patients benefit from surgical reconstruction of knee dislocation; young athletes universally recover full function with an accelerated physical therapy protocol without operative intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Young, active patients with complete knee dislocation and multiligamentous injury should undergo surgical reconstruction of all injured structures. The knee cannot be stabilized adequately by bracing alone when the ACL, PCL, and lateral complex are all disrupted. Early surgical reconstruction (typically staged with initial spanning external fixation if needed) optimizes functional outcomes and return to sport. The spontaneous reduction before ED arrival does not diminish the severity — multiligamentous instability defines the injury pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee dislocation = multiligamentous injury requiring reconstruction in young active patients; staged approach common; neurovascular assessment before any surgical intervention. COMLEX: Knee dislocation = CP8 named condition; peroneal nerve injury (foot drop) assessed separately from vascular injury; CPM and early PT post-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: isolated ligament reconstruction techniques from ACL/PCL repair are combined in knee dislocation surgery, which requires multiligamentous reconstruction in a single or staged operative setting"},{"id":"sdl25_q36","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the presentation and management of knee joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman sustains a traumatic knee dislocation in a motor vehicle collision. The dislocation is reduced and her initial ABI is 0.95. Four hours after reduction, her leg becomes progressively pale, cool, and painful with passive stretch of her toes. What do these findings indicate and what is the immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Progressive pallor and coolness are expected post-reduction findings from reactive edema compressing superficial veins, and the appropriate management is elevation and compression dressing with reassessment in 24 hours","B":"Cold extremity and pain with passive stretch after knee dislocation indicate peroneal nerve injury causing loss of normal pain signaling, requiring only splinting in neutral ankle position to prevent foot drop contracture","C":"These findings indicate normal post-reduction inflammation and should be observed for 72 hours without intervention, as current guidelines recommend conservative vascular monitoring before any surgical exploration after knee dislocation","D":"Ischemia time exceeding 4 hours is associated with poor prognosis and amputation risk; these findings indicate evolving vascular occlusion or compartment syndrome requiring emergent vascular repair plus fasciotomy to prevent irreversible limb ischemia","E":"Pale and cool extremity after knee dislocation reduction indicates venous obstruction from hemarthrosis, which responds to aspiration of the joint effusion and repeat Doppler ultrasound to confirm venous patency"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive pallor, coolness, and pain with passive stretch 4 hours after reduction represent a vascular emergency. Ischemia beyond 4 hours after knee dislocation dramatically worsens prognosis and amputation risk. Pain with passive stretch of the toes is also an early compartment syndrome sign. This pattern requires immediate surgical vascular repair (typically popliteal artery bypass or interposition graft) plus four-compartment fasciotomy. An initial ABI of 0.95 can normalize transiently due to vasospasm, then worsen from intimal tear thrombosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ischemia >4 hours after knee dislocation = poor prognosis; emergent vascular repair + fasciotomy; pain with passive stretch = compartment syndrome sign. COMLEX: Monitor for 72 hours even with initially normal vascular exam; compartment syndrome of the leg is a named complication of knee dislocation vascular repair requiring clinical vigilance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL injuries: the popliteal fossa neurovascular bundle passes posterior to both the ACL and PCL attachment regions, explaining the proximity that makes it vulnerable in complete knee dislocation with multiligamentous disruption"},{"id":"sdl25_q37","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old female gymnast sustains a lateral patellar dislocation that spontaneously reduces when she extends her knee. MRI confirms a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment, severe trochlear dysplasia (flat trochlear groove), and patella alta. Which factor most significantly increases her risk of recurrent dislocation?","choices":{"A":"Patient age over 40 years is the strongest predictor of recurrent patellar dislocation, because older patients have greater quadriceps atrophy and VMO weakness that reduces dynamic patellar stabilization during activity","B":"Prior ACL reconstruction is a major risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation because the graft harvest creates a lateral retinacular imbalance that shifts the patellar tracking vector toward the lateral compartment","C":"Obesity with BMI over 30 is the primary structural risk factor for recurrent lateral patellar dislocation because increased weight increases the Q-angle and the lateral force vector on the patella with every step","D":"Isolated lateral retinaculum tightness without any bony dysplasia is the primary anatomic risk factor for recurrence, because lateral retinacular tightness is the single most important structure preventing medial patellar reduction","E":"Trochlear dysplasia — a flat or convex trochlear groove provides minimal bony constraint to lateral displacement, leaving the patella dependent on soft-tissue restraints that are already disrupted by the injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Trochlear dysplasia is the strongest anatomic risk factor for recurrent patellar dislocation. A flat or convex trochlear groove fails to provide the bony constraint that normally stabilizes the patella during flexion. Combined with MPFL disruption and patella alta, trochlear dysplasia creates the highest-risk triad for recurrence. Surgical management in this patient may include MPFL reconstruction and trochleoplasty. Young age (not older age) is actually a risk factor for recurrence. Prior ACL reconstruction and obesity are not classic risk factors for patellar instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar instability risk triad = trochlear dysplasia + patella alta + MPFL tear; elevated TT-TG distance = indication for tibial tubercle medialization. COMLEX: CP8 named condition; Dejour classification grades trochlear dysplasia A-D; Grade B-D dysplasia most likely to require trochleoplasty in addition to MPFL reconstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: trochlear morphology is the key bony determinant of both patellofemoral syndrome (contact stress) and patellar dislocation (bony constraint deficit), sharing the same anatomic framework"},{"id":"sdl25_q38","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male soccer player plants his foot and pivots, feeling his patella \"pop out\" and then immediately reduce. On examination he has medial patellar pole tenderness, a moderate hemarthrosis, and positive apprehension sign with lateral patellar pressure. What is the biomechanical explanation for why the patella spontaneously reduced when he extended his knee?","choices":{"A":"Extension tightens the quadriceps mechanism and pulls the patella proximally and centrally into the trochlear groove; as the knee extends, rising patellar tendon tension realigns the patella medially within the groove","B":"Medial retinaculum contraction actively generates sufficient force to pull the displaced patella back into the trochlear groove during knee extension, providing the primary active stabilization of the medially displaced patella","C":"Gravity acts on the laterally displaced patella when the patient stands upright, producing a medially directed force vector that passively returns the patella to the midline position of the trochlear groove on weight-bearing","D":"Hamstring relaxation during knee extension releases the posterolateral tightening that maintained the lateral dislocation, allowing the medial retinaculum tension alone to centralize the patella into the trochlear groove","E":"VMO spasm following lateral dislocation generates an acute medially directed contractile force that pulls the patella rapidly back across the trochlear groove, explaining the audible clunk heard at the moment of spontaneous reduction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spontaneous patellar reduction with knee extension occurs because quadriceps tension increases as the knee extends. This proximal and central traction pulls the patella back into the trochlear groove. As the knee approaches full extension, the tightened quadriceps vector becomes increasingly perpendicular to the patella, providing the centralization force. This is why lateral patellar dislocations almost always reduce spontaneously with extension — the quadriceps mechanism acts as the reduction force without manual assistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar dislocation spontaneously reduces with knee extension via quadriceps centralization; hemarthrosis + medial patellar pole tenderness + positive apprehension = classic post-dislocation triad; X-ray after spontaneous reduction confirms osteochondral fragment risk. COMLEX: Medial patellar pole tenderness = MPFL femoral attachment site; check sunrise view for osteochondral injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: quadriceps force vector and Q-angle mechanics that produce patellofemoral stress in PFS are the same biomechanical framework that drives lateral patellar dislocation and spontaneous reduction with extension"},{"id":"sdl25_q39","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the presentation and management of patellar dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male has sustained three lateral patellar dislocations over 18 months despite completing a structured physical therapy program including VMO strengthening, taping, and bracing. MRI shows a complete MPFL tear at the femoral attachment. Imaging shows a laterally positioned tibial tubercle that pulls the patella laterally. What is the most appropriate surgical intervention?","choices":{"A":"Isolated lateral retinacular release alone, which reduces the lateral tethering force on the patella and corrects the imbalanced Q-angle effect without requiring MPFL reconstruction or bony realignment surgery","B":"MPFL reconstruction combined with a tibial tubercle transfer that moves the patellar tendon insertion medially, correcting both the soft-tissue restraint deficit and the lateral quadriceps pull on the patella","C":"Total knee replacement for end-stage patellofemoral arthritis, which is the definitive treatment for recurrent patellar instability in young patients who have failed conservative management and MPFL reconstruction","D":"Patellectomy to eliminate the unstable patella entirely, which resolves recurrent lateral dislocation by removing the dislocating structure and has acceptable long-term functional outcomes in young active patients","E":"Quadriceps tendon repair with medial retinaculum reefing alone, which provides sufficient medial stabilization in all patients regardless of tibial tubercle position or the number of prior dislocations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course reading for SDL 25 (Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Orthopedics) notes that MPFL tears are complete in over 50% of patellar dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment, and that operative repair is best for chronic, recurrent dislocation. The SDL 4 reading (Practical Office Orthopedics) states that an MPFL reconstruction can help stabilize patellae prone to lateral tracking, as can a tibial tubercle transfer that moves the patellar tendon insertion medially to change the angle of tracking. With a torn MPFL and a laterally placed tubercle, combining the two addresses both problems. A lateral release alone loosens only the lateral retinaculum and leaves the torn MPFL and the lateral pull untreated; total knee replacement is not a treatment for instability in a young patient; excision of the patella costs strength and causes pain; and quadriceps tendon repair treats a different injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recurrent lateral patellar dislocation after failed rehabilitation → operative repair (course SDL 25 reading); MPFL reconstruction restores the medial restraint, and a tibial tubercle transfer moves the patellar tendon insertion medially to correct lateral tracking (SDL 4 reading). COMLEX: MPFL tears are complete in over 50% of dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: TT-TG distance is the same measurement used to evaluate patellofemoral syndrome malalignment, bridging PFS diagnosis to surgical planning for recurrent lateral patellar instability"},{"id":"sdl25_q40","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old male basketball player lands awkwardly from a jump, sustaining obvious ankle deformity. X-ray confirms talar dislocation from the mortise. The skin over the lateral malleolus is tented and beginning to blanch. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Obtain MRI before any reduction attempt to fully characterize all soft-tissue and osteochondral injuries prior to deciding between closed and open reduction techniques in the operating room","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation in the operating room is required before any closed reduction is attempted, because tented skin indicates soft-tissue interposition that will block closed reduction","C":"Immediate closed reduction — tented, blanching skin signals imminent pressure necrosis; reduce under procedural sedation, splint, elevate, and confirm mortise congruency on post-reduction imaging","D":"Apply a compression dressing and elevate the ankle overnight while awaiting orthopedic consultation in the morning, because the overlying skin blanching reflects normal post-injury inflammatory vasospasm rather than ischemia","E":"Perform bedside reduction only after general anesthesia is established in the operating room, because adequate muscle relaxation is required for all ankle dislocations and procedural sedation is insufficient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ankle dislocations are orthopedic emergencies requiring immediate reduction. The ankle should never be left in a dislocated position — skin tenting with blanching signals imminent pressure necrosis and risk of skin breakdown/open dislocation. Procedural sedation is appropriate for ED reduction. MRI is not a pre-reduction study in this emergency. The post-reduction priority is confirming mortise congruency on X-ray, assessing neurovascular status, and determining if persistent instability requires operative stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle dislocation = immediate reduction; never leave dislocated; skin tenting = emergency; post-reduction: splint, elevate, mortise/AP/lateral views, neurovascular check. COMLEX: Ankle dislocation = CP8 named condition; persistent instability after reduction → CRIF or ORIF; assess for associated distal fibula fracture on post-reduction films.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: the same anterolateral ligamentous complex (ATFL, CFL) that fails in lateral ankle sprains forms the primary restraint against talar dislocation, with mortise stability dependent on intact syndesmosis and medial structures"},{"id":"sdl25_q41","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman has a trimalleolar ankle fracture-dislocation. It is reduced promptly in the emergency department, splinted, and elevated. Post-reduction radiographs show that the talus will not stay centered in the mortise, and the soft tissues are badly swollen with fracture blisters. Which management does the course reading describe for this situation?","choices":{"A":"Closed reduction with external fixation, since the ankle is unstable after reduction and the skin does not yet allow ORIF","B":"Immediate open reduction and internal fixation through the blistered skin, since any delay increases the risk of arthritis","C":"Leave the ankle in its current position in a splint for 6 weeks, since repeated manipulation further damages the soft tissue","D":"Below-knee amputation, since a trimalleolar fracture-dislocation with blisters cannot be salvaged once swelling develops","E":"Weight bearing as tolerated in a walking boot, since loading the ankle pushes the talus back into the center of the mortise"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ankle section of the Current Diagnosis and Treatment in Orthopedics chapter states that a dislocated ankle should first be reduced, splinted, elevated, and imaged after reduction. If the ankle is still unstable after reduction, either closed reduction with external fixation or ORIF (if the soft tissues allow) should be performed, and an ankle should never be left dislocated. With severe swelling and fracture blisters, external fixation holds the reduction until the soft tissues recover. ORIF through compromised skin ignores the soft tissue condition. Leaving the talus out of position for 6 weeks would allow malunion and arthritis; a lateral talar shift of 1 mm cuts tibiotalar contact by 40%. Amputation is not indicated. Weight bearing will not hold an unstable mortise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle fracture goals: reduce dislocations promptly, restore joint surfaces, hold reduction until healed, mobilize early; in trimalleolar ORIF the lateral malleolus is usually fixed first. COMLEX: Open fracture-dislocation → IV antibiotics and urgent irrigation and debridement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — trimalleolar ankle fractures and the posterior malleolus"},{"id":"sdl25_q42","sdlNumber":25,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the presentation and management of ankle joint dislocations.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male sustains a severe ankle inversion injury in a trail-running fall. He presents with the foot inverted and internally rotated in a locked position that will not correct with gentle manipulation. X-ray shows simultaneous dislocation of both the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints without a talar neck fracture. What is the most likely diagnosis and predominant direction?","choices":{"A":"Lateral subtalar dislocation from an eversion mechanism is the most common pattern, accounting for 70% of subtalar dislocations, where the calcaneus and forefoot displace laterally while the talus remains in the ankle mortise","B":"Superior subtalar dislocation from an axial compression mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus is driven superiorly against the talar body during a fall from height onto the heel with foot in neutral position","C":"Anterior subtalar dislocation from a plantarflexion mechanism is the most frequent presentation, where the calcaneus displaces anteriorly on the talus while the talonavicular joint simultaneously subluxes dorsally","D":"Rotatory subtalar dislocation from a combined axial load and twisting mechanism is the most common pattern, where the calcaneus rotates on the talar axis producing a spiral dissociation of both subtalar and midtarsal joints simultaneously","E":"Medial subtalar dislocation — the talocalcaneal and talonavicular joints dislocate without talar neck fracture; an inversion mechanism displaces the calcaneus and forefoot medially in approximately 85% of subtalar dislocations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Subtalar dislocation involves simultaneous dislocation of the talocalcaneal (subtalar) and talonavicular joints with an intact talar neck — distinct from tibiotalar dislocation. Medial subtalar dislocation (foot inverted and internally rotated) accounts for approximately 85% of cases from inversion injury. Lateral subtalar dislocation (eversion mechanism) accounts for 15% and has worse prognosis due to associated open injury risk. Management is closed reduction under sedation, NWB short leg cast for 6 weeks. Open subtalar dislocations have high infection risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Subtalar dislocation = talocalcaneal + talonavicular simultaneous dislocation with intact talar neck; medial 85% (inversion), lateral 15% (eversion); reduce under sedation, NWB cast 6 weeks. COMLEX: Distinguish from tibiotalar dislocation on X-ray by noting talar position within mortise; subtalar joint is a CP8-adjacent structure in foot and ankle OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: severe inversion mechanism from ankle sprain can progress to subtalar dislocation when ligamentous disruption extends from the lateral ankle ligaments to involve the talocalcaneal interosseous ligament and talonavicular capsule"}]}]}]};
